window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"CV-1":{"unit":"CV-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01","title":"Cardiovascular Embryology","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the heart from paired endothelial tubes in week 3 to a four-chambered pump with separate outflow vessels, then explains the fetal circulation, the changes at birth, and the pharyngeal arch arteries that build the great vessels and set the paths of the recurrent laryngeal nerves. Part I questions usually connect three things: an embryonic structure, the adult structure it becomes, and the defect that appears when one step fails. If you understand why each step happens, you can predict the anatomy of a congenital lesion, the direction of its shunt, and its physical and ECG findings instead of memorizing each one.","prerequisites":["Germ layers, gastrulation, and the somatic and splanchnic layers of lateral plate mesoderm","Cephalocaudal and lateral folding of the embryo in week 4","Gross anatomy of the adult heart chambers, septa, valves, and great vessels (CV-3)","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships in the circulation (CV-11)","The oxygen hemoglobin dissociation curve and factors that shift it"],"connections":[{"to":"Heart anatomy (CV-3)","how":"Adult features such as the fossa ovalis and the ligamentum arteriosum are remnants of the developmental structures described here."},{"to":"Congenital heart conditions (CV-18-G)","how":"Each congenital lesion in that chapter is the failure of one step of tube formation, septation, or outflow tract division explained here."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Septal defects load or displace the conduction system, which is why primum and secundum atrial septal defects produce different QRS axes."},{"to":"Cardiovascular genetics (CV-14)","how":"Trisomy 21, the 22q11.2 deletion, TBX5 mutation, and ciliary dynein mutations each disrupt a specific step and so produce a predictable defect."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy","how":"Pharyngeal arch remodeling explains the different loops of the two recurrent laryngeal nerves and the vulnerability of the left nerve to thoracic disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cardiac looping","def":"The rightward (dextral or D-looping) bending of the straight heart tube during week 4 that places the future chambers in their adult positions."},{"term":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia","def":"An inherited, usually autosomal recessive, defect of the dynein arms or other parts of motile cilia; Kartagener syndrome is the form with situs inversus."},{"term":"Endocardial cushions","def":"Mesenchymal swellings in the atrioventricular canal and outflow tract formed when endocardial cells undergo epithelial-to-mesenchymal transformation into the cardiac jelly."},{"term":"Septum primum","def":"The thin first atrial septum that grows from the atrial roof toward the endocardial cushions and later forms the flap valve of the foramen ovale."},{"term":"Septum secundum","def":"The thick, incomplete second atrial septum that forms to the right of the septum primum and whose free edge bounds the foramen ovale."},{"term":"Foramen ovale","def":"The opening left by the septum secundum through which blood passes from the right to the left atrium before birth."},{"term":"Cardiac neural crest","def":"Neural crest cells from the hindbrain that migrate through pharyngeal arches 3, 4, and 6 into the outflow tract and help build the aorticopulmonary septum."},{"term":"Aorticopulmonary septum","def":"The spiral septum formed by fusion of the truncal ridges that divides the truncus arteriosus into the aorta and pulmonary trunk."},{"term":"Ductus venosus","def":"The fetal channel that carries umbilical venous blood through the liver to the inferior vena cava, bypassing most of the hepatic sinusoids."},{"term":"Ductus arteriosus","def":"The fetal vessel, derived from the left sixth arch artery, that carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta."},{"term":"Fetal hemoglobin (HbF)","def":"Hemoglobin of two alpha and two gamma chains that binds 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate weakly and so has a higher oxygen affinity than adult hemoglobin."},{"term":"Pharyngeal arch arteries","def":"Paired arteries (aortic arches) running through each pharyngeal arch from the aortic sac to the dorsal aortae, later remodeled into the great vessels."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C01","obj":"CV-1.1","title":"Formation of the heart tube, looping, and left-right asymmetry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"By week 3 the embryo is too large for diffusion, so the heart is the first organ to function. Cardiac progenitors pass through the cranial primitive streak into splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm and gather cranial to the neural plate as a horseshoe-shaped cardiogenic field (days 16 to 18). Endoderm signals (BMP, FGF, and Wnt inhibition) induce NKX2-5, committing them to a heart fate."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary heart field forms the initial tube, mainly the left ventricle and parts of the atria. The secondary heart field, in pharyngeal mesoderm, later adds most of the right ventricle and the outflow tract (conus cordis and truncus arteriosus) and part of the atria and sinus venosus. Too few secondary field cells leave the outflow tract too short to rotate and align, a cause of many outflow defects."},{"type":"p","text":"Angioblastic cords canalize into paired endocardial tubes, which lateral folding brings to the ventral midline to fuse into one tube by the end of week 3; cephalocaudal folding carries it into the future thorax. The tube has an inner endocardium, cardiac jelly (the future cushions), and an outer myocardium; the proepicardium later supplies the epicardium, coronary vessels, and many cardiac fibroblasts. Loss of the dorsal mesocardium leaves the transverse pericardial sinus. The heart beats at about day 22, and from caudal to cranial the tube is sinus venosus, primitive atrium, primitive ventricle, bulbus cordis, and truncus arteriosus opening into the aortic sac."},{"type":"p","text":"From about day 23 to 28 the tube outgrows the pericardial cavity and loops: the bulboventricular part bends ventrally, caudally, and right, and the atrial part dorsally, cranially, and left. The atria end up above and behind the ventricles, with the primitive ventricle (future left ventricle) on the left and the bulbus cordis (future right ventricle) on the right."},{"type":"table","title":"Segments of the heart tube and their adult derivatives","cols":["Embryonic segment","Main adult derivative"],"rows":[["Truncus arteriosus","Proximal ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"],["Conus cordis","Smooth outflow tracts of both ventricles"],["Proximal bulbus cordis","Trabeculated right ventricle"],["Primitive ventricle","Most of the trabeculated left ventricle"],["Primitive atrium","Trabeculated atria, including the auricles"],["Right horn of sinus venosus","Smooth right atrium (sinus venarum)"],["Left horn of sinus venosus","Coronary sinus and oblique vein of the left atrium"],["Primitive pulmonary vein","Smooth part of the left atrium"]],"caption":"Rough walls come from the original atrial and ventricular segments; smooth walls come from incorporated veins or the outflow tract."},{"type":"p","text":"Looping direction is set at the primitive node, where dynein-driven tilted monocilia beat in a rotary fashion and move fluid to the left. This induces NODAL and LEFTY2 in left lateral plate mesoderm, midline LEFTY1 blocks spread to the right, and NODAL induces PITX2, which specifies left-sided identity of the heart, lungs, and gut."},{"type":"flow","title":"Left-right signaling that directs looping","steps":["Dynein drives rotation of tilted nodal cilia","Leftward nodal flow","NODAL and LEFTY2 on the left; midline LEFTY1 barrier","PITX2 expressed on the left","Left-sided organ identity; heart tube loops right"],"caption":"If nodal flow fails, the later steps still occur but on a random side."},{"type":"p","text":"In primary ciliary dyskinesia, nonfunctional dynein stops nodal flow, so sidedness is random: about half of patients have situs inversus totalis with dextrocardia (Kartagener syndrome: situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis), most others have normal situs, and a minority have heterotaxy (situs ambiguus). Immotile airway cilia cause neonatal respiratory distress, wet cough, otitis media, and bronchiectasis; immotile sperm flagella cause male infertility, and poor tubal transport raises ectopic pregnancy risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk depends on whether heart and body agree about sidedness: mirror-image dextrocardia keeps connections concordant, so the heart is usually normal; dextrocardia with normal abdominal situs or heterotaxy (often asplenia or polysplenia) carries a high risk of complex lesions. Mirror-image dextrocardia inverts P, QRS, and T in lead I and gives diminishing precordial R waves."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C02","obj":"CV-1.2","title":"Atrial septation and atrial septal defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Before birth, oxygenated blood must cross from the right to the left atrium; after birth the atria must be sealed. Two incomplete, overlapping septa with offset openings solve this by acting as a one-way flap valve."},{"type":"flow","title":"Atrial septation","steps":["Septum primum grows toward the cushions; gap below is the ostium primum","Apoptosis in the upper septum primum opens the ostium secundum","Septum primum fuses with the cushions; ostium primum closes","Thick septum secundum grows down on the right side","Its free edge leaves the foramen ovale","Lower septum primum acts as the flap valve; flow is right to left","After birth the flap is pressed shut; fusion leaves the fossa ovalis"],"caption":"The ostium secundum is a hole in the septum primum; the foramen ovale is the gap left by the septum secundum."},{"type":"p","text":"Cushion tissue closes the ostium primum at the end of week 5. Because the thin septum primum lies on the left and the rigid septum secundum on the right with nonaligned openings, blood crosses only right to left: higher fetal right atrial pressure pushes the flap open, and after birth higher left atrial pressure presses it shut (section C06). The floor of the adult fossa ovalis is septum primum, and its rim (limbus) is the edge of the septum secundum."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomic fusion takes months and fails in about a quarter of adults, leaving a patent foramen ovale (PFO). No tissue is missing, so blood crosses only when right atrial pressure transiently exceeds left, as on release of a Valsalva strain, with coughing, or with acute pulmonary hypertension from pulmonary embolism. A venous thrombus can then reach the systemic arteries (paradoxical embolism) and cause a stroke."},{"type":"p","text":"An atrial septal defect (ASD) is a true tissue deficiency. Because the right ventricle is more compliant, blood shunts left to right, volume loading the right heart and lungs but not the left ventricle. The secundum ASD, the most common, lies at the fossa ovalis and results from excess resorption of the septum primum or a deficient septum secundum; TBX5 (Holt-Oram syndrome, with upper limb defects) and NKX2-5 (with AV conduction disease) mutations are known causes. Primum and sinus venosus defects are compared in the table in section C03."},{"type":"p","text":"The small interatrial pressure difference makes flow across the defect nearly silent. The murmur is a systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border from increased flow across a normal pulmonary valve, with a mid-diastolic tricuspid flow rumble when the shunt is large. The ECG shows right axis deviation and an rsR prime pattern in V1. Over decades, high pulmonary flow can remodel the arterioles, raise resistance, and reverse the shunt with cyanosis (Eisenmenger syndrome), a possible end stage of any large left-to-right shunt."},{"type":"example","title":"Why S2 is widely and fixedly split in ASD","text":"Normally inspiration increases right heart return, prolongs right ventricular ejection, delays pulmonic closure (P2), and widens the split. In an ASD the right ventricle receives systemic return plus the shunt every beat, so P2 is always late and the split is wide. In inspiration the extra systemic return raises right atrial pressure and shrinks the left-to-right shunt by about the same amount; in expiration the reverse occurs. The atria act as one chamber, so right ventricular filling and P2 timing barely change with respiration. A right bundle branch block delays P2 electrically, so its wide split still varies with breathing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetal foramen ovale closes prematurely in mid-gestation. Which side of the heart is underdeveloped at birth?","explain":"The left side. Most left atrial and left ventricular filling in the fetus arrives through the foramen ovale because pulmonary venous return is small. Without it the left heart receives little flow and grows poorly, while the right heart enlarges. Embryonic chambers grow in proportion to the flow through them.","check":"Compare with the one-way flap valve and the fetal circulation in section C05."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C03","obj":"CV-1.3","title":"Endocardial cushions, the atrioventricular canal, and AV septal defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A single atrioventricular (AV) canal opens mainly over the future left ventricle. It must shift right and divide into tricuspid and mitral orifices. The endocardial cushions do both and, sitting at the center of the heart, also build the lower atrial and upper ventricular septa, so one cushion defect damages several structures."},{"type":"p","text":"At the end of week 4, AV canal and outflow myocardium signals the endocardium (TGF-beta and BMP families). Endocardial cells undergo epithelial-to-mesenchymal transformation, losing their junctions and invading the cardiac jelly. Superior and inferior AV cushions form, with smaller lateral cushions; the outflow ridges form separately and receive neural crest cells (section C04). In week 5 the superior and inferior cushions fuse, dividing the canal into right and left orifices. The fused mass closes the ostium primum (lowest atrial septum), and inferior cushion tissue joins the conal ridges and muscular septum to close the interventricular foramen as the membranous septum by about the end of week 7. Cushion mesenchyme around each orifice is undermined on its ventricular side; the muscular strands left behind become chordae tendineae (muscle replaced by dense connective tissue) and papillary muscles. The left orifice gets two leaflets and the right three."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscular ventricular septum forms as the expanding ventricles press their medial walls together. The membranous septum closes last and needs three tissue sources to meet, so most ventricular septal defects (VSDs) are perimembranous; muscular VSDs, sometimes multiple, are less common, and small ones often close."},{"type":"p","text":"Failure of cushion fusion produces an atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD, AV canal defect). The complete form has a primum ASD above an inlet VSD with one common five-leaflet AV valve. The partial form has two orifices but a primum ASD and a cleft anterior mitral leaflet with mitral regurgitation. In the complete form the VSD transmits left ventricular pressure to the pulmonary arteries, so high flow and high pressure cause heart failure within months and earlier pulmonary vascular disease than an isolated ASD. The AV node and His bundle are displaced inferiorly and posteriorly and the left anterior fascicle is short, giving left (superior) axis deviation, often with first degree AV block."},{"type":"table","title":"Interatrial and AV septal communications","cols":["Lesion","Developmental failure","Location and anatomy","Key findings"],"rows":[["Patent foramen ovale","Septa do not fuse after birth","Between overlapping septa; no tissue loss","Flow only when right atrial pressure exceeds left; paradoxical embolism"],["Secundum ASD","Excess resorption of septum primum or deficient septum secundum","Fossa ovalis; AV valves normal","Most common ASD; right axis deviation; Holt-Oram (TBX5); often silent into adulthood"],["Sinus venosus ASD","Abnormal incorporation of the sinus venosus","High, near the superior vena cava","Often anomalous right pulmonary venous return"],["Partial AVSD (primum ASD)","Septum primum fails to meet the cushions","Low septum above AV valves; cleft mitral leaflet","Left axis deviation; mitral regurgitation"],["Complete AVSD","AV cushions fail to fuse","Primum ASD, inlet VSD, common AV valve","Infant heart failure; early pulmonary hypertension; trisomy 21"]],"caption":"Only the PFO lacks tissue loss; only cushion defects involve the AV valves and give left axis deviation."},{"type":"p","text":"About 40 to 50% of children with trisomy 21 have congenital heart disease, and AVSD, especially the complete form, is the characteristic lesion; a large share of complete AVSDs occur in Down syndrome. The extra chromosome is thought to disturb cushion cell adhesion, migration, and matrix, though the genes are not fully established. VSD, secundum ASD, PDA, and tetralogy are also more frequent."},{"type":"p","text":"In Ebstein anomaly the septal and posterior tricuspid leaflets are poorly undermined, so their attachment is displaced toward the apex, part of the right ventricle is atrialized, and the valve leaks. In tricuspid atresia the right AV orifice is obliterated, so right atrial blood must cross the atrial septum, the hypoplastic right ventricle fills through a VSD, and the lungs are supplied through that VSD or the ductus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C04","obj":"CV-1.4","title":"Neural crest, the aorticopulmonary septum, and conotruncal defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The single outflow tract must become a pulmonary trunk leaving the right ventricle anteriorly and to the left and an aorta leaving the left ventricle posteriorly and to the right. Cardiac neural crest cells from the hindbrain migrate through pharyngeal arches 3, 4, and 6; some support the arch arteries and help form the thymus and parathyroids, and others populate the truncal and conal ridges and the semilunar valves. Removing this crest in animal embryos produces persistent truncus arteriosus."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 5 the right superior and left inferior truncal ridges grow distally while swinging around the truncal wall, so when they fuse the aorticopulmonary septum twists about 180 degrees. The right dorsal and left ventral conal ridges fuse into the conal (outlet) septum, which separates the right ventricular infundibulum from the aortic vestibule and meets the muscular septum and inferior cushion to complete the membranous septum. Because outlet and ventricular septa must align, displacing one affects both."},{"type":"table","title":"Conotruncal defects and the step that failed","cols":["Defect","Developmental failure","Anatomy","Circulation and key findings"],"rows":[["Persistent truncus arteriosus","Truncal ridges fail to form or fuse","One trunk and valve over a VSD, giving coronary, pulmonary, and systemic arteries","Mixing with mild cyanosis; excess pulmonary flow and heart failure as resistance falls"],["D-transposition of the great arteries","Septum fails to spiral","Aorta from right ventricle, pulmonary trunk from left ventricle","Parallel circuits; survival requires mixing (foramen ovale, ductus, VSD); severe early cyanosis; narrow pedicle; maternal diabetes"],["Tetralogy of Fallot","Anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum","Pulmonary stenosis, malalignment VSD, overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy","Right-to-left shunt set by outflow obstruction; hypercyanotic spells; boot-shaped heart"]],"caption":"No septum, a straight septum, and a displaced septum produce the three classic conotruncal defects."},{"type":"p","text":"In transposition the circuits run in parallel, so cyanosis appears within hours to days and worsens as the ductus closes; the great vessels lie one in front of the other, narrowing the vascular pedicle. In tetralogy, unequal division of the conus explains all four features: the narrow infundibulum causes pulmonary stenosis, the misplaced outlet septum leaves a malalignment VSD, the enlarged aortic outflow overrides it, and right ventricular hypertrophy follows from the obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the VSD is large, both ventricles share systolic pressure, and shunt direction depends on right ventricular outflow resistance versus systemic resistance; mild obstruction gives a mostly left-to-right shunt and a pink child. The murmur comes from the narrowed outflow tract, so it softens during a spell. The radiograph shows a boot-shaped heart (raised apex, concave upper left border), and chronic hypoxemia causes secondary polycythemia and clubbing."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hypercyanotic (tet) spell and relief by squatting","steps":["Crying or exertion lowers systemic resistance","Right outflow resistance rises relative to systemic","More blood shunts right to left; pulmonary flow falls","Hypoxemia and acidosis lower systemic resistance further","Squatting kinks femoral arteries and raises systemic resistance, breaking the cycle"],"caption":"Anything that lowers systemic resistance worsens cyanosis in tetralogy, and anything that raises it improves cyanosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge or velocardiofacial syndrome) removes one copy of TBX1 and neighbors, disrupting the pharyngeal tissue through which crest cells migrate. Outflow defects (truncus arteriosus, tetralogy, interrupted aortic arch) arrive with thymic hypoplasia and T cell deficiency, parathyroid hypoplasia with hypocalcemia, and facial and palatal anomalies. Excess retinoic acid, as with maternal isotretinoin, disturbs crest migration and causes conotruncal and craniofacial defects."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn with D-transposition, a small foramen ovale, and no VSD is pink at birth but deeply cyanotic on day 2. What changed?","explain":"The ductus arteriosus closed. With parallel circuits, oxygenated blood reaches the body only through the interatrial opening, the ductus, or a VSD. When rising O2 and falling prostaglandin E2 constricted the ductus, mixing depended on a small foramen ovale alone and systemic oxygen delivery collapsed.","check":"Compare with transposition above and with ductal closure in section C06."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C05","obj":"CV-1.5","title":"Fetal circulation and delivery of the best-oxygenated blood to the brain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The fetus exchanges gases in the placenta, so its circulation runs in parallel: both ventricles pump into the systemic circuit, three shunts bypass the liver and lungs, and streaming sends the best-oxygenated blood to the heart and brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high because the lungs are fluid filled and their vessels compressed, low alveolar PO2 causes hypoxic vasoconstriction, and mediators favor constriction (high endothelin, low nitric oxide). The placenta is a large low-resistance circuit that keeps systemic resistance low. Pulmonary trunk pressure therefore exceeds descending aortic pressure, and most right ventricular output crosses the ductus arteriosus; only a small fraction of combined output passes through the lungs."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of blood through the fetal circulation","steps":["Umbilical vein brings the best blood (about 80% saturated)","Ductus venosus shunts part of it past the liver into the IVC","Crista dividens streams IVC blood through the foramen ovale","Left ventricle ejects; coronary, carotid, and subclavian arteries branch first","SVC blood goes right atrium to right ventricle to pulmonary trunk","Most pulmonary trunk flow crosses the ductus to the descending aorta","Umbilical arteries return blood (about 60% saturated) to the placenta"],"caption":"IVC blood goes to the left heart and the brain; SVC blood goes to the right heart, the ductus, and the lower body."},{"type":"p","text":"The umbilical vein is the most saturated fetal vessel (about 80%, yet with a PO2 of only about 30 to 35 mmHg). Part of its flow enters the hepatic sinusoids through the left portal vein, and the rest passes through the ductus venosus, whose inlet regulates the bypass and protects the heart from sudden volume loads such as during uterine contractions. In the right atrium the lower edge of the septum secundum (crista dividens) and the IVC valve direct the better-oxygenated stream across the foramen ovale. It is diluted only slightly by the small pulmonary venous return before the left ventricle ejects it."},{"type":"p","text":"Desaturated SVC blood, with coronary sinus blood and part of the IVC stream, enters the right ventricle. The right ventricle supplies slightly more than half of combined output, which is why both ventricles have similar wall thickness at birth. Its output enters the aorta through the ductus distal to the left subclavian artery, so the myocardium and brain receive left ventricular blood before it mixes with desaturated ductal blood, while the lower body receives the mixture. The umbilical arteries, branches of the internal iliac arteries, return blood to the placenta."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal tissues are well oxygenated despite low PO2. HbF binds 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate weakly because the gamma chain has serine where the beta chain has a 2,3-BPG-binding histidine (position 143), so its P50 is about 19 mmHg versus about 27 mmHg for adult hemoglobin. The fetus also has a higher hemoglobin concentration and a high cardiac output for its mass. In the placenta, the double Bohr effect helps: fetal blood loses CO2 and gains affinity, while maternal blood gains CO2 and loses affinity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C06","obj":"CV-1.6","title":"Circulatory changes at birth and remnants of fetal vessels","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"At birth the circulation changes from parallel to series. The first breaths expand the alveoli and raise alveolar PO2, relieving hypoxic vasoconstriction, and O2 and shear stress release nitric oxide and prostacyclin, so pulmonary resistance falls and flow rises several-fold. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta and raises systemic resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Increased pulmonary venous return raises left atrial pressure while loss of umbilical return lowers right atrial pressure, so the septum primum is pressed against the septum secundum and the foramen ovale closes functionally within minutes to hours (section C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Across the ductus the gradient reverses, so flow briefly runs aorta to pulmonary trunk. Rising arterial PO2 constricts ductal smooth muscle, partly because O2-sensitive K+ channels close, the membrane depolarizes, and L-type Ca2+ channels open. Prostaglandin E2 falls because the placenta is gone and the perfused lungs metabolize it; in the fetus PGE2 acting on EP receptors raised cAMP and kept the muscle relaxed. Functional closure occurs within 1 to 3 days in term infants; intimal thickening and fibrosis over weeks leave the ligamentum arteriosum. Cyclooxygenase inhibition promotes closure and a PGE analog keeps the ductus open, confirming the mechanism. The umbilical arteries constrict within minutes to mechanical, thermal, and O2 stimuli; the umbilical vein and ductus venosus close slightly later, and liver perfusion then comes from the portal vein and hepatic artery."},{"type":"flow","title":"Closure of the fetal shunts at birth","steps":["Lungs expand and alveolar PO2 rises","Pulmonary resistance falls; pulmonary flow rises","Cord clamped; systemic resistance rises, IVC return falls","Left atrial pressure exceeds right; foramen ovale closes","Rising PO2 and falling PGE2 constrict the ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus and umbilical vessels fibrose into ligaments"],"caption":"Pressure closes the foramen ovale; chemical signals (O2 and PGE2) close the ductus arteriosus."},{"type":"table","title":"Fetal shunts and vessels: why they work and what remains","cols":["Fetal structure","Route and reason for flow","Adult remnant and location"],"rows":[["Umbilical vein","Placenta to liver","Ligamentum teres hepatis, free edge of the falciform ligament"],["Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein to IVC past the sinusoids; regulated inlet","Ligamentum venosum, fissure between caudate and left lobes"],["Foramen ovale","Right to left atrium; streaming and right atrial pressure above left","Fossa ovalis, right side of the interatrial septum"],["Ductus arteriosus","Pulmonary trunk to descending aorta; high pulmonary resistance, low PO2, PGE2","Ligamentum arteriosum, left pulmonary artery to underside of the aortic arch"],["Umbilical arteries","Internal iliac arteries to placenta","Medial umbilical ligaments (distal); superior vesical arteries (proximal)"],["Allantois and urachus (not a vessel)","None","Median umbilical ligament, bladder apex to umbilicus"]],"caption":"Do not confuse ligamentum venosum with ligamentum arteriosum, or the paired medial umbilical ligaments with the single median umbilical ligament."},{"type":"p","text":"A patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is most common in prematurity, because immature ductal muscle responds less to O2 and more to PGE2; congenital rubella and high altitude also predispose. Aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure throughout the cycle, giving a continuous machinery murmur below the left clavicle, and diastolic runoff widens pulse pressure. The shunt volume loads the left heart. Ductal closure unmasks ductus-dependent lesions: transposition (mixing), pulmonary atresia (lung supply), and hypoplastic left heart or critical coarctation (systemic supply). In persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, resistance stays high and blood shunts right to left through the foramen ovale and ductus despite normal anatomy."},{"type":"example","title":"Differential cyanosis in a long-standing PDA","text":"A large PDA first shunts aorta to pulmonary artery. Years of high flow and pressure remodel the pulmonary arterioles until pulmonary pressure exceeds aortic pressure and the shunt reverses (Eisenmenger physiology). The ductus enters the aorta distal to the left subclavian artery, so the head and arms still receive unmixed left ventricular blood while the descending aorta carries desaturated blood to the lower body. The toes are cyanotic and clubbed while the fingers stay pink. A reversed VSD or ASD mixes blood before the aorta, so fingers and toes are equally cyanotic."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-01-01-C07","obj":"CV-1.7","title":"Pharyngeal arch arteries and the recurrent laryngeal nerves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each pharyngeal arch artery runs from the aortic sac to the paired dorsal aortae, which fuse caudally into one descending aorta. The arteries appear craniocaudally in weeks 4 and 5, are never all present together, and are remodeled by selective persistence and regression into the asymmetric adult pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Origins of the great arteries","cols":["Embryonic source","Adult derivative"],"rows":[["Truncus arteriosus","Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"],["Aortic sac","Right horn: brachiocephalic trunk; left horn: arch up to the left common carotid"],["First and second arch arteries","Part of the maxillary artery; hyoid and stapedial arteries (largely regress)"],["Third arch artery","Common carotid and first part of internal carotid (rest from cranial dorsal aorta); external carotid sprouts from it"],["Fourth arch artery, left","Arch between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries"],["Fourth arch artery, right","Proximal right subclavian artery (distal part from right dorsal aorta and seventh intersegmental artery)"],["Fifth arch artery","None"],["Sixth arch artery","Proximal right and left pulmonary arteries; distal left becomes the ductus arteriosus, distal right regresses"],["Left seventh intersegmental artery","Left subclavian artery"],["Left dorsal aorta","Descending aorta beyond the left subclavian"]],"caption":"The dorsal aorta between the third and fourth arches disappears on both sides, and the right dorsal aorta regresses below the right seventh intersegmental artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The vagus supplies the fourth arch through the superior laryngeal nerve (cricothyroid) and the sixth arch through the recurrent laryngeal nerve (all other intrinsic laryngeal muscles). Each recurrent nerve first hooks under its sixth arch artery, and as the heart descends the nerves are dragged caudally around the lowest persisting arch vessel. On the right the distal sixth arch artery and the fifth regress, so the nerve slides up to the right subclavian artery (fourth arch) at the root of the neck. On the left the distal sixth arch persists as the ductus, so the nerve stays under the aortic arch just behind the ligamentum arteriosum in the superior mediastinum. Both ascend in the tracheoesophageal groove."},{"type":"p","text":"The recurrent nerve supplies the posterior cricoarytenoid, the only vocal fold abductor, so injury leaves the fold near the midline with hoarseness. The left nerve's thoracic course near the aortic arch, left pulmonary artery, left main bronchus, and hilar nodes makes hoarseness a sign of aortic arch aneurysm, left hilar or mediastinal tumor or nodes, a dilated left atrium or pulmonary artery (cardiovocal or Ortner syndrome), or PDA ligation. The right nerve is vulnerable at the neck root to an apical lung tumor or subclavian aneurysm. Both are at risk in thyroid surgery."},{"type":"p","text":"If the right fourth arch artery and cranial right dorsal aorta regress while the caudal right dorsal aorta persists, the right subclavian becomes the last arch branch and crosses behind the esophagus (aberrant right subclavian artery), which can cause dysphagia; with no right fourth arch vessel to hold it, the right laryngeal nerve does not recur (nonrecurrent laryngeal nerve). Persistence of the right dorsal aorta gives a double aortic arch, a vascular ring that can compress the trachea and esophagus. Abnormal regression of the left fourth arch artery interrupts the arch between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, so the lower body depends on a PDA; because neural crest supports the fourth arch artery, this type is most strongly linked to the 22q11.2 deletion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 60-year-old with long-standing mitral stenosis develops hoarseness. Which nerve is involved, and what developmental fact explains why only one side is at risk?","explain":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve. A large left atrium and dilated pulmonary artery compress it against the aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum. Only the left nerve passes there, because the left distal sixth arch artery persisted as the ductus and held the nerve in the thorax, while on the right it regressed and the nerve rose to the subclavian artery.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the nerve paths and the arch derivatives table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The adult heart records its development. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the tube, endocardium becomes the cushions that build the valves and central septa, and hindbrain neural crest divides the outflow tract and supports the arch arteries, so the fossa ovalis is septum primum, the membranous septum and valve leaflets are cushion tissue, and the ligamentum arteriosum marks the left sixth arch artery. Physiology explains the shunts: each runs along a gradient created by high pulmonary resistance and a low-resistance placenta, HbF and the double Bohr effect favor placental O2 transfer, and O2-sensitive channels and PGE2 control the ductus. ECG findings follow the conduction anatomy of CV-7: right ventricular volume load in secundum ASD and a displaced AV node in AVSD."},{"type":"p","text":"Genetics and pathology tie the heart to other systems: trisomy 21 disrupts the cushions, 22q11.2 deletion links outflow defects to T cell deficiency and hypocalcemia, TBX5 and NKX2-5 link septal defects to limb and conduction abnormalities, and ciliary dynein defects link organ position to sinusitis and infertility. Maternal diabetes, rubella, and retinoic acid act on the same steps. Most defects have no recognized cause, and real lesions vary in severity."}],"summary":["Splanchnic mesoderm forms paired endocardial tubes that fuse into one heart tube by the end of week 3; it beats by about day 22 and loops right during days 23 to 28.","Dynein-driven leftward nodal flow activates NODAL and PITX2 on the left; ciliary dynein defects randomize sidedness, giving situs inversus in about half (Kartagener syndrome) with sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and infertility.","The ostium secundum is a hole in the septum primum, the foramen ovale is the gap left by the septum secundum, and the septum primum is the one-way flap valve.","A PFO is an unsealed flap that allows paradoxical embolism; a secundum ASD is a true deficiency with right heart volume loading, right axis deviation, and a fixed split S2.","Fused endocardial cushions divide the AV canal, close the ostium primum, help form the membranous septum, and form the AV valves; their failure gives AVSD with left axis deviation, typical of trisomy 21.","Most VSDs are perimembranous because the membranous septum closes last and needs three tissue sources to meet.","Cardiac neural crest builds the spiral aorticopulmonary septum: no septum gives truncus arteriosus, a straight septum gives transposition, and a displaced infundibular septum gives tetralogy of Fallot.","Fetal shunts bypass the liver and lungs, and streaming plus ductal entry distal to the head vessels deliver the best-oxygenated blood to the heart and brain.","At birth falling pulmonary resistance raises left atrial pressure and closes the foramen ovale, while rising O2 and falling PGE2 constrict the ductus arteriosus.","The right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops under the right subclavian (fourth arch); the left loops under the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum (sixth arch), giving it a long thoracic course."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular proteins, fetal hemoglobin and 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and Neonatal Physiology, readjustment of the circulation at birth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and neonatal physiology, the fetal circulation and oxygen transport"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, congenital heart disease"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax and neck, recurrent laryngeal nerves and the great vessels"}]},"CV-10":{"unit":"CV-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01","title":"Lymphatic Vessels, Lymphoid Tissues, and Lymphoid Organs","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows lymph from the lymphatic capillary, where filtered fluid and escaped plasma protein enter, through collecting vessels, nodes, the cisterna chyli, and the two terminal ducts into the great veins of the neck. It explains how lymph moves without a central pump, how lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, bone marrow, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue are built to bring antigen and lymphocytes together, and what happens when drainage fails, as in lymphedema and chylothorax. Board items usually link structure to consequence: which nodes drain a region, why lymphedema becomes nonpitting, which compartment empties in an immunodeficiency, where T cells are selected, and why a milky effusion follows esophageal surgery.","prerequisites":["Starling forces across the capillary wall: capillary and interstitial hydrostatic pressures and plasma and interstitial oncotic pressures (CV-11)","Histology of capillaries, veins, and the three tunics of the vessel wall (CV-2)","Gross anatomy of the posterior mediastinum, aortic hiatus, and root of the neck (CV-3)","Chylomicron assembly and lipoprotein metabolism (CV-13)","Basic immunology: B and T lymphocytes, antigen-presenting cells, MHC class I and II, and antibody classes"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Lymph flow equals the net capillary filtration that Starling forces produce, so any change in those forces changes lymph formation."},{"to":"Hemodynamic conditions (CV-18-H)","how":"Lymphedema is one of the four basic mechanisms of edema, alongside raised capillary pressure, low plasma oncotic pressure, and increased capillary permeability."},{"to":"Immune responses in cardiovascular disease (CV-16)","how":"Lymph nodes, spleen, and MALT are where adaptive immune responses to infection and to cardiac self antigens begin."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal physiology and biochemistry","how":"Lacteals carry long-chain dietary fat as chylomicrons through the thoracic duct, which is why thoracic duct injury produces a fat-rich effusion."},{"to":"Pathology of neoplasia","how":"Carcinomas spread along lymphatic drainage routes, so regional node anatomy predicts where metastases appear first."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lymphatic capillary","def":"A blind-ended, thin-walled vessel of overlapping endothelial cells with little or no basal lamina that is the entry point for lymph."},{"term":"Anchoring filaments","def":"Fine fibrils that tether the outer surface of lymphatic capillary endothelium to the surrounding connective tissue and pull the vessel open when the tissue swells."},{"term":"Lymphangion","def":"The segment of a collecting lymphatic between two valves, which acts as an individual contractile pump."},{"term":"Chyle","def":"Milky intestinal lymph containing chylomicrons from absorbed dietary fat."},{"term":"Cisterna chyli","def":"The dilated beginning of the thoracic duct anterior to the upper lumbar vertebrae, where the lumbar and intestinal lymphatic trunks join."},{"term":"Venous angle","def":"The junction of the internal jugular and subclavian veins on each side, where the thoracic duct (left) and right lymphatic duct (right) end."},{"term":"High endothelial venule (HEV)","def":"A postcapillary venule with plump cuboidal endothelium, found in the lymph node paracortex and MALT, through which naive lymphocytes leave the blood."},{"term":"Germinal center","def":"The pale center of a secondary lymphoid follicle where activated B cells proliferate, mutate their antibody genes, switch class, and are selected for high affinity."},{"term":"Positive selection","def":"Survival of thymocytes whose T-cell receptors bind self peptide and self MHC weakly, which makes the mature repertoire MHC restricted."},{"term":"Negative selection","def":"Deletion, or diversion to regulatory T cells, of thymocytes whose receptors bind self peptide and self MHC strongly, which establishes central tolerance."},{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized epithelial cell over mucosal lymphoid follicles that transports antigen from the lumen to underlying immune cells."},{"term":"Lymphedema","def":"Swelling caused by failure of lymphatic drainage, in which protein-rich fluid accumulates and the tissue progressively becomes fibrotic and fatty."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01-C01","obj":"CV-10.1","title":"Lymphatic capillaries and the formation of lymph","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In most capillary beds the outward Starling forces (capillary hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure) slightly exceed the inward forces (plasma oncotic pressure and interstitial hydrostatic pressure), so a small net volume of fluid is filtered into the interstitium. A little plasma protein, mainly albumin, escapes as well, and no mechanism can move it back into blood against its concentration gradient. The lymphatic system is the only route by which this fluid and protein return to the circulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Structure of the lymphatic capillary"},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic capillaries (initial lymphatics) begin as blind-ended tubes near blood capillary beds. Their thin endothelium has an incomplete or absent basal lamina and no pericytes or smooth muscle. Neighboring endothelial cells overlap like roof shingles and are held only by loose, discontinuous junctions, so the free inner edge of each overlap swings inward as a flap, the primary lymphatic valve. Anchoring filaments tie the outer surface of the endothelium to the collagen and elastic fibers of the surrounding tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"When interstitial fluid accumulates, the swelling tissue pulls the anchoring filaments taut, so the capillary is pulled open instead of being compressed. Fluid enters while interstitial pressure exceeds the pressure inside; once the vessel fills or the tissue is squeezed, internal pressure presses the flaps shut, so lymph can only move onward. The gaps admit proteins, lipoproteins, bacteria, and whole cells, the opposite of a continuous blood capillary, which is built to retain them."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation of lymph","steps":["Net capillary filtration adds fluid and protein to the interstitium","Tissue swells and anchoring filaments pull the lymphatic capillary open","Overlapping endothelial flaps part and interstitial fluid enters","Rising pressure inside the vessel closes the flaps","Lymph moves on into valved collecting lymphatics"],"caption":"Tissue swelling opens the lymphatic capillary through its anchoring filaments, and pressure inside the vessel closes it, so flow is one way."},{"type":"h","text":"What the lymphatics return"},{"type":"p","text":"With Guyton and Hall's whole-body values, a net filtration pressure of only about 0.3 mmHg acting on a large filtration coefficient filters about 2 mL per minute. Resting lymph flow is about 120 mL per hour, about 100 mL of it through the thoracic duct, which is 2 to 3 L per day and matches net filtration. However much fluid is reabsorbed at venous ends (glycocalyx work suggests little), the remainder and all escaped protein leave by lymph."},{"type":"p","text":"Returning protein is the essential job. Lymph from most tissues contains about 2 g/dL of protein, hepatic lymph about 6 g/dL because sinusoids leak albumin freely, and intestinal lymph about 3 to 4 g/dL. Retained protein would raise interstitial oncotic pressure toward plasma levels, erase the gradient that holds fluid in vessels, and cause edema."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph also carries what cannot enter blood capillaries. Enterocytes package long-chain fatty acids as triglyceride in chylomicrons, which are too large for blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteal of each villus and make intestinal lymph milky (chyle). Most dietary fat therefore reaches the blood through the thoracic duct and bypasses the liver on first pass, while medium-chain fatty acids enter portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic capillaries are absent from epidermis, cartilage, cornea, bone marrow, and brain and spinal cord parenchyma, which drain along perivascular routes to meningeal lymphatics."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01-C02","obj":"CV-10.2","title":"Lymphatic drainage routes to the venous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic capillaries drain into collecting lymphatics with smooth muscle and paired valves. These carry lymph through afferent vessels to regional nodes and through efferent vessels to further node groups, so nearly all lymph crosses at least one node before reaching blood. Because each region drains to a predictable first node group, that group is where infection causes tender lymphadenitis and where carcinoma usually metastasizes first."},{"type":"h","text":"Trunks and the cisterna chyli"},{"type":"p","text":"Below the diaphragm, the right and left lumbar trunks carry lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, abdominal wall, kidneys, adrenals, and gonads after it passes the iliac and lumbar (para-aortic) nodes. The intestinal trunk carries lymph, including chyle, from the stomach, intestines, liver, pancreas, and spleen after the preaortic (celiac and mesenteric) nodes. These trunks join at the cisterna chyli, a variable sac anterior to about L1 to L2 beside the aorta."},{"type":"h","text":"Course of the thoracic duct"},{"type":"p","text":"The thoracic duct ascends with the aorta through the aortic hiatus at about T12. In the posterior mediastinum it lies on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the aorta on its left and the azygos vein on its right. At about T4 to T6 it crosses to the left behind the esophagus and ascends along the esophagus's left edge. At about C7 it arches laterally behind the left carotid sheath and ends at the left venous angle, the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins, where a terminal valve keeps blood out. Near its end it receives the left jugular, left subclavian, and often the left bronchomediastinal trunks. Its course and termination vary."},{"type":"h","text":"The right lymphatic duct"},{"type":"p","text":"On the right, the jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks may join as a right lymphatic duct about 1 cm long that ends at the right venous angle, or they may open into the veins separately. They drain only the right head and neck, right upper limb, and right thorax, including the right breast and right lung. Much of the left lower lobe of the lung also drains to right tracheobronchial nodes. The thoracic duct drains everything else."},{"type":"table","title":"First node groups for commonly tested regions","cols":["Region drained","First node group"],"rows":[["Upper limb and lateral breast (most breast lymph)","Axillary; medial hand and forearm first to cubital (supratrochlear) nodes"],["Medial breast","Parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels"],["Stomach","Celiac nodes"],["Testis or ovary","Lumbar (para-aortic) nodes near the renal vessels"],["Scrotum, vulva, anal canal below the pectinate line, superficial lower limb","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Rectum and anal canal above the pectinate line","Internal iliac nodes; upper rectum to inferior mesenteric nodes"],["Lateral foot and posterolateral calf","Popliteal nodes, then deep inguinal nodes"]],"caption":"Only the right head, neck, upper limb, and thorax return lymph through the right lymphatic duct; everything else reaches the thoracic duct."},{"type":"p","text":"Development explains the classic findings. The testes descend from the posterior abdominal wall with their lymphatics, so testicular cancer spreads first to para-aortic nodes, while scrotal skin drains to superficial inguinal nodes. The anal canal above the pectinate line (hindgut) drains to internal iliac nodes and the part below it (ectoderm) to superficial inguinal nodes. Because abdominal lymph ascends the thoracic duct to the left venous angle, gastric carcinoma can appear first in a left supraclavicular node (Virchow node)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A retroperitoneal lymphoma obstructs the thoracic duct just above the cisterna chyli. Which regions lose their main lymphatic outflow, and which still drain?","explain":"Both lower limbs, the pelvis, and the abdominal viscera, including intestinal chyle, cannot drain past the block, so chylous ascites or lower body swelling can follow. The left upper limb, left head and neck, and left thorax still drain because their trunks join the duct near its end, and the right head, neck, upper limb, and thorax use the right lymphatic duct.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on the trunks, the thoracic duct, and the right lymphatic duct."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01-C03","obj":"CV-10.3","title":"Forces that move lymph forward","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymph has no central pump. It begins in an interstitium slightly below atmospheric pressure and ends in veins a few mmHg above it, in the legs against gravity. It moves forward because valves allow flow in only one direction, so any compression of a valved segment pushes lymph toward the veins. These mechanisms form the lymphatic pump."},{"type":"h","text":"Valves and the intrinsic pump"},{"type":"p","text":"The flap valves of the lymphatic capillaries are the first set. Collecting lymphatics carry bicuspid endothelial valves every few millimeters, and the segment between two valves is a lymphangion. Smooth muscle in its wall contracts rhythmically, and stretch from filling increases the rate and force of contraction, so the pump works harder when more lymph arrives, much as stretch increases cardiac stroke volume. Sympathetic stimulation increases this activity, endothelial nitric oxide relaxes the muscle between contractions, and inflammation depresses it. The pump is strong: an obstructed thoracic duct can reach about 50 to 100 mmHg. If vessels dilate, as in chronic obstruction, the valve leaflets no longer meet, lymph refluxes between contractions, and the pump fails."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The lymphangion pump cycle","steps":["Lymph enters through the open upstream valve","Filling stretches the smooth muscle of the wall","Stretch triggers contraction and pressure rises","Upstream valve closes, downstream valve opens","Lymph is ejected and stretches the next lymphangion","The emptied segment relaxes and refills from upstream"],"caption":"Each lymphangion is a small pump whose output rises with filling; sequential contraction moves lymph toward the veins."},{"type":"h","text":"Extrinsic pumps"},{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal muscle contraction, passive movement, pulsation of neighboring arteries (deep lymphatics share arterial sheaths), and external compression all squeeze lymphatics. Exercise can raise lymph flow about 10 to 30 times, and immobility lowers it. During inspiration the descending diaphragm raises abdominal pressure around the cisterna chyli while intrapleural pressure falls around the thoracic duct, so lymph is drawn upward; valves block backflow during expiration."},{"type":"h","text":"Interstitial pressure and outflow pressure"},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph flow depends on interstitial fluid pressure and on pump activity. Subcutaneous interstitial pressure is normally about -3 mmHg, largely because lymphatics keep pumping fluid out. As it rises from about -6 mmHg to slightly above 0 mmHg, lymph flow increases about 20-fold. Above about 1 to 2 mmHg flow plateaus, because the tissue pressure that fills the lymphatic capillaries also compresses the larger collecting vessels, and edema accumulates once filtration exceeds this maximum. Lymph finally empties against central venous pressure, so right heart failure or superior vena cava obstruction slows lymph outflow while also raising filtration, which contributes to effusions and ascites."},{"type":"example","title":"The safety factors against edema, and their loss","text":"Guyton and Hall describe three safety factors that oppose edema when capillary pressure rises. First, low compliance of the negative-pressure interstitium makes a small fluid gain raise interstitial pressure steeply, worth about 3 mmHg. Second, lymph flow can rise 10 to 50 times, worth about 7 mmHg. Third, that extra flow washes protein out faster than it leaks in, lowering interstitial oncotic pressure, worth about 7 mmHg. Together they let capillary pressure rise about 17 mmHg before significant edema appears. Now remove the regional nodes and vessels. The second and third factors are lost at once, and leaking protein accumulates, so interstitial oncotic pressure rises instead of falling. The tissue gains protein-rich fluid even with normal capillary pressure, which is the basis of lymphedema (section C05)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a spinal cord injury has a flaccid, paralyzed leg with normal veins and normal plasma proteins. Why does that leg swell more than the other when it hangs down?","explain":"Dependency raises capillary pressure in both legs, but only the healthy leg has active skeletal muscle compressing its lymphatics and veins. Without the muscle pump, lymph flow from the paralyzed leg cannot rise enough to match the increased filtration, and venous pressure also stays higher, so fluid accumulates.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on extrinsic pumps."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01-C04","obj":"CV-10.4","title":"Lymphoid organs: lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and MALT","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the primary lymphoid organs (bone marrow for B cells, thymus for T cells) lymphocytes generate antigen receptors and are tested against self. In the secondary organs (lymph nodes, spleen, and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, MALT) naive lymphocytes meet antigen. Nodes sample lymph, the spleen samples blood, and MALT samples mucosal surfaces, while recirculating lymphocytes pass through each."},{"type":"p","text":"In the marrow, pro-B cells rearrange heavy chain genes with RAG1 and RAG2, and a functional heavy chain paired with a surrogate light chain forms the pre-B cell receptor. After light chain rearrangement, an immature B cell bearing IgM that binds self antigen strongly undergoes receptor editing, then deletion if editing fails. Survivors mature in the spleen into naive B cells bearing IgM and IgD."},{"type":"p","text":"The thymus lies in the anterior superior mediastinum, develops from third pharyngeal pouch endoderm, and involutes into fat after puberty. Each lobule has a dark cortex packed with thymocytes behind a blood-thymus barrier and a pale medulla containing Hassall corpuscles (whorls of keratinized epithelial cells). It has no follicles and no afferent lymphatics. DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) leaves a hypoplastic thymus, and AIRE mutation causes autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis)."},{"type":"flow","title":"T-cell maturation and selection in the thymus","steps":["Marrow precursor enters near the corticomedullary junction","Double-negative (CD4- CD8-) cell rearranges TCR beta","Pre-TCR signaling makes it double positive","TCR alpha rearrangement completes the alpha beta receptor","Cortex: weak self MHC binding survives; no binding dies by neglect","Class II binding keeps CD4; class I binding keeps CD8","Medulla: strong binding to AIRE-driven self antigen causes deletion or regulatory fate","Naive, MHC-restricted, self-tolerant T cells leave"],"caption":"Cortical positive selection makes T cells useful; medullary negative selection makes them safe."},{"type":"p","text":"Afferent lymph enters a node's subcapsular sinus, percolates through macrophage-lined sinuses, and leaves at the hilum. B cells gather in cortical follicles (CXCL13 acting on CXCR5) around follicular dendritic cells that display intact antigen. In germinal centers, activation-induced cytidine deaminase drives somatic hypermutation and class switching, and higher-affinity B cells are selected by antigen and follicular helper T cells (affinity maturation). Naive lymphocytes enter the paracortex through high endothelial venules using L-selectin and CCR7. Follicular hyperplasia reflects a B-cell response; paracortical hyperplasia, a T-cell (often viral) one."},{"type":"p","text":"The spleen has no afferent lymphatics. In its white pulp, T cells sheath central arterioles beside B-cell follicles, and the marginal zone traps blood-borne antigen. In the red pulp, blood empties into splenic cords and must squeeze through narrow slits between sinusoidal endothelial cells, so stiff, aged, or antibody-coated red cells are removed and inclusions are pitted. Asplenia therefore causes Howell-Jolly bodies, thrombocytosis, and overwhelming sepsis from encapsulated bacteria (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis), which need splenic clearance and marginal zone IgM."},{"type":"p","text":"MALT (Peyer patches, largest in the ileum; appendix; bronchial tissue; and tonsils, which have a partial capsule) is unencapsulated. M cells transcytose luminal antigen to underlying lymphoid cells. B cells there switch to IgA under TGF-beta, home back to mucosa through alpha4 beta7 integrin binding MAdCAM-1, and secrete dimeric IgA that the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor carries across the epithelium."},{"type":"table","title":"Compartments of lymphoid organs","cols":["Compartment","Main cells and function","Depleted in"],"rows":[["Node cortical follicles and germinal centers","B cells, follicular dendritic cells; affinity maturation","X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia"],["Node paracortex","T cells, dendritic cells, high endothelial venules","DiGeorge syndrome"],["Node medullary cords","Plasma cells secreting antibody","Agammaglobulinemia"],["Spleen periarteriolar lymphoid sheath","T cells around the central arteriole","DiGeorge syndrome"],["Spleen follicles and marginal zone","B cells; T-independent IgM to polysaccharides","X-linked agammaglobulinemia"],["Spleen red pulp","Macrophages; culling and pitting; about one third of platelets","Asplenia"],["Thymic cortex and medulla","Thymocytes; positive then negative selection","DiGeorge syndrome (hypoplasia)"]],"caption":"An immunodeficiency that removes one cell type empties its compartment in every organ."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-10-01-C05","obj":"CV-10.5","title":"Lymphedema and chylothorax","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most edema comes from too much filtration: raised capillary pressure, low plasma oncotic pressure, or increased permeability. In lymphedema filtration is normal and the drain fails. Because protein leaves the interstitium only by lymph, the retained fluid is rich in protein, and that fact explains its course. Secondary causes far outnumber primary ones."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of lymphedema","cols":["Cause","How drainage fails","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Lymph node dissection","Removes nodes and the vessels through them","Arm after axillary dissection; leg after pelvic or inguinal dissection"],["Radiation","Fibrosis narrows and obliterates vessels, often years later","Axilla, pelvis, or groin, especially with surgery"],["Tumor","Tumor emboli block vessels and nodes","Metastatic carcinoma, lymphoma; peau d'orange over breast carcinoma"],["Filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia species)","Adult worms dilate lymphatics and make valves incompetent; inflammation and infection scar them","Legs, scrotum, and breast in endemic tropical regions"],["Primary (developmental)","Too few or malfunctioning vessels or valves","Milroy disease (VEGFR3 mutation); FOXC2 mutation; Turner syndrome"]],"caption":"Every cause removes, obstructs, or disables lymphatic pathways, and the downstream course is the same."},{"type":"p","text":"In filariasis, mosquitoes deposit Wuchereria bancrofti larvae that mature in lymphatics into adult worms living for years, while microfilariae circulate in blood, typically at night. Living worms dilate lymphatics; dying worms and their Wolbachia endosymbionts provoke inflammation, and repeated bacterial infection adds scarring. The result is leg lymphedema progressing to elephantiasis, hydrocele, and sometimes chyluria."},{"type":"h","text":"From pitting to firm nonpitting swelling"},{"type":"p","text":"Early lymphedema is soft and pitting and improves with elevation, although its high protein content holds water in the tissue so it returns when the limb hangs down. Over months to years the retained protein and lipid drive chronic inflammation with CD4 T cells and macrophages, TGF-beta stimulates fibroblasts to lay down collagen, and lymph stasis promotes fat growth. Fibrosis and fat do not move under pressure, so the limb becomes firm and nonpitting and no longer shrinks with elevation, and the skin thickens with hyperkeratosis and papillomatous change. Inability to pinch a skin fold at the base of the second toe is the Stemmer sign. Low-protein edema of heart failure or hypoalbuminemia stays pitting, and myxedema is nonpitting because it is glycosaminoglycan, not free fluid."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self-perpetuating cycle of lymphedema","steps":["Lymphatic outflow falls","Protein-rich fluid accumulates and holds water","Stagnant protein and lipid provoke chronic inflammation","Fibroblasts deposit collagen and fat expands","Fibrosis damages remaining lymphatics; cellulitis recurs","Each infection and increment of fibrosis reduces outflow further"],"caption":"Fibrosis and infection attack the remaining lymphatics, so lymphedema tends to worsen."},{"type":"p","text":"Lost immune cell traffic and protein-rich fluid favor recurrent streptococcal cellulitis, and lymphangiosarcoma is a rare late complication, classically in the arm years after mastectomy with axillary dissection."},{"type":"h","text":"Chylothorax"},{"type":"p","text":"A break or obstruction of the thoracic duct in the chest leaks chyle into the pleural space. Surgical trauma and malignancy, especially lymphoma, are the leading causes. Chyle stays turbid after centrifugation, unlike empyema fluid. Pleural triglyceride above about 110 mg/dL supports the diagnosis, below about 50 mg/dL makes it unlikely, and chylomicrons confirm it. Pseudochylothorax, a chronic cholesterol effusion inside a thickened pleura, has cholesterol above about 200 mg/dL and no chylomicrons."},{"type":"example","title":"A milky effusion after esophagectomy","text":"Five days after a right-chest esophagectomy, a patient who has resumed eating develops a large right pleural effusion. The fluid stays milky after centrifugation, with triglyceride 450 mg/dL, cholesterol 70 mg/dL, and mostly lymphocytes. Anatomy: below its crossing at about T4 to T6 the duct lies behind the esophagus to the right of midline, so a tear there drains to the right pleura; injury above the crossing gives a left effusion. Timing: fasting lymph is clear and scant, but eating fat sends chylomicrons through the duct. Laboratory: high triglyceride and persistent turbidity mean chylomicrons, and acute onset with low cholesterol argues against pseudochylothorax. A continuing leak depletes albumin, lymphocytes, and fat-soluble vitamins."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The drainage map has developmental roots. Lymphatics bud from the cardinal veins as PROX1-expressing endothelium responding to VEGF-C through VEGFR3, which is why VEGFR3 loss causes congenital lymphedema and failed jugular sac connection leaves the nuchal cystic hygroma of Turner syndrome. The thoracic duct forms from the lower right and upper left of paired channels, so it begins on the right and ends on the left. The thymus from the third pharyngeal pouch explains the T-cell defect of DiGeorge syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, immunology, and pathology then share one set of pathways. Lymph flow is the measurable result of Starling forces, and protein return keeps the oncotic gradient intact. The same vessels deliver antigen to nodes, so regional nodes are where adaptive responses begin and where carcinoma and escaping microbes arrive first. Infection can cause lymphatic failure, as in filariasis, and lymphatic failure invites infection, as in recurrent cellulitis. Drainage tables describe the usual pattern; the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct vary between individuals."}],"summary":["Lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended, with overlapping endothelial flaps that act as inward valves and anchoring filaments that pull them open when tissue swells.","Lymph returns the net capillary filtrate, about 2 to 3 L per day, and essentially all leaked plasma protein, which keeps interstitial oncotic pressure low.","Long-chain fat enters lacteals as chylomicrons and reaches the blood through the thoracic duct, bypassing the liver on first pass.","The thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli (about L1 to L2), passes the aortic hiatus, crosses to the left at about T4 to T6, and ends at the left venous angle; the right lymphatic duct drains only the right head, neck, upper limb, and thorax.","Regional drainage predicts spread: testis to para-aortic nodes, scrotum and lower anal canal to superficial inguinal nodes, abdominal cancer to a left supraclavicular node.","Valves make any compression one-way; lymphangions contract harder when stretched; muscle, arterial pulsation, and breathing add extrinsic pumping; flow plateaus once interstitial pressure exceeds about 1 to 2 mmHg.","B cells are selected in the marrow; T cells are positively selected in the thymic cortex and negatively selected in the AIRE-expressing medulla.","Nodes place B cells in follicles, T cells and high endothelial venules in the paracortex, and plasma cells in medullary cords; the spleen has no afferent lymphatics and clears blood-borne antigen, old red cells, and encapsulated bacteria.","MALT samples mucosal antigen through M cells and generates IgA plasma cells whose dimeric IgA crosses the epithelium on the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor.","Lymphedema is protein-rich edema that becomes firm and nonpitting as fibrosis and fat develop; thoracic duct injury causes chylothorax with triglyceride above about 110 mg/dL."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow; section on edema and safety factors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview of the lymphoid system; Thorax, posterior mediastinum and thoracic duct; lymphatic drainage of the breast, limbs, and perineum"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular system, development of the lymphatic system; Head and neck, pharyngeal pouches"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Cells and Tissues of the Immune System; Antigen Recognition in the Adaptive Immune System; Immunological Tolerance and Autoimmunity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, section on edema; Blood Vessels, section on lymphangitis and lymphedema; Infectious Diseases, section on lymphatic filariasis"}]},"CV-11":{"unit":"CV-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01","title":"Hemodynamics and Blood Flow Regulation","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the physics of blood flow and how the body adjusts it: pressure, flow, and resistance with Poiseuille's law; series and parallel resistance; velocity and turbulence; arterial compliance and pulse pressure; the Starling forces and edema; and the local, neural, and hormonal control of arteriolar tone in each major organ. Part I questions here are prediction questions. A stem changes one variable, such as radius, hematocrit, albumin, or arterial stiffness, and asks what happens to resistance, flow, pulse pressure, or tissue fluid. Treat each equation as a cause and effect statement and you can reason to findings such as a carotid bruit, ankle edema, or a wide pulse pressure in an older patient.","prerequisites":["Cardiac output, stroke volume, and mean arterial pressure calculations (CV-6)","Histology of elastic arteries, muscular arteries, arterioles, capillaries, and veins (CV-2)","Osmosis, oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure, and the composition of plasma proteins","Smooth muscle contraction: Ca2+, calmodulin, myosin light chain kinase, and myosin light chain phosphatase","G protein signaling (Gq, Gs, Gi) and the second messengers IP3, cAMP, and cGMP"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac cycle and its regulation (CV-6)","how":"The baroreceptor reflex and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system act on the arteriolar resistance and venous capacitance described here to hold mean arterial pressure steady."},{"to":"Coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"Metabolic control of coronary flow is the clearest example of local regulation, and coronary stenosis applies Poiseuille's law to ischemia."},{"to":"Lymphatic vessels (CV-10)","how":"Lymph returns the net capillary filtrate and leaked protein, so lymphatic failure is one of the four routes to edema."},{"to":"Exercise and the cardiovascular system (CV-12)","how":"Redistribution of cardiac output during exercise is the parallel circuit principle in action, driven by metabolic dilation in muscle and sympathetic constriction elsewhere."},{"to":"Vascular pathology (CV-18-I)","how":"Atherosclerosis, arterial stiffening, and venous insufficiency change radius, compliance, and capillary pressure, which links each disease to the physics in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Resistance (R)","def":"The opposition to flow within a vessel or bed, equal to the pressure difference across it divided by the flow through it."},{"term":"Total peripheral resistance (TPR)","def":"The combined resistance of the entire systemic circulation, also called systemic vascular resistance."},{"term":"Poiseuille's law","def":"The relation for steady laminar flow in a rigid tube stating that resistance equals 8 times viscosity times length divided by pi times radius to the fourth power."},{"term":"Viscosity","def":"The internal friction of a fluid that resists sliding of one layer over another; in blood it depends mainly on hematocrit."},{"term":"Reynolds number","def":"A dimensionless value, density times velocity times diameter divided by viscosity, that predicts when laminar flow will become turbulent."},{"term":"Compliance","def":"The change in volume of a vessel per unit change in pressure; its reciprocal is stiffness."},{"term":"Pulse pressure","def":"Systolic pressure minus diastolic pressure, set mainly by stroke volume and arterial compliance."},{"term":"Starling forces","def":"The hydrostatic and oncotic pressures in the capillary and the interstitium that together determine net fluid movement across the capillary wall."},{"term":"Oncotic pressure","def":"The osmotic pressure exerted by proteins, mainly albumin, that cannot freely cross the capillary wall."},{"term":"Autoregulation","def":"The intrinsic ability of an organ to keep its blood flow nearly constant over a range of perfusion pressures."},{"term":"Myogenic response","def":"Contraction of vascular smooth muscle in response to stretch, and relaxation when stretch decreases."},{"term":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","def":"Constriction of small pulmonary arteries in response to low alveolar PO2, which diverts blood toward better ventilated lung."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C01","obj":"CV-11.1","title":"Pressure, flow, resistance, and Poiseuille's law","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood flows because of a pressure difference between two points: Q = ΔP / R, the hydraulic form of Ohm's law. Resistance is never measured directly; it is calculated as ΔP / Q. For the systemic circulation the driving pressure is mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus right atrial pressure (near 0 mmHg), the flow is cardiac output (CO), and the resistance is total peripheral resistance (TPR), so MAP = CO x TPR. Pressure can rise only if CO or TPR rises, and a fall in one must be offset by the other to hold pressure steady. With a MAP of about 93 mmHg and a CO of about 5 L/min, TPR is about 18 to 19 mmHg per L/min."},{"type":"h","text":"Poiseuille's law"},{"type":"p","text":"For steady laminar flow of a uniform fluid in a rigid tube, R = 8ηL / (πr⁴), where η is viscosity, L is length, and r is radius. Resistance rises in direct proportion to viscosity and length and in inverse proportion to the fourth power of radius. The fourth power arises because laminar flow has a parabolic velocity profile: a wider vessel has both a larger area (r²) and a larger central core far from the slowing effect of the wall, so velocity in that core rises too. Halving the radius raises resistance 16-fold, while a radius increase of only about 19% doubles flow (1.19 to the fourth power is about 2). This is why arterioles control flow so powerfully: doubling flow to one muscle by pressure would need a doubled arterial gradient that would also double flow everywhere else, whereas a 19% local dilation does it alone. Length is fixed in the adult, but it explains why long diffuse disease resists more than a short lesion of the same caliber."},{"type":"h","text":"Viscosity"},{"type":"p","text":"Whole blood at a hematocrit of about 40 to 45% is roughly 3 to 4 times as viscous as water, and hematocrit is the main determinant. The relation is steep: a hematocrit of 60 to 70% can raise viscosity two to three times. In polycythemia, flow can fall more than O2 content rises, so O2 delivery drops and sluggish flow favors thrombosis. Large plasma proteins (IgM paraprotein in Waldenström macroglobulinemia, high fibrinogen), rigid sickled cells, and extreme leukocytosis also raise viscosity. Blood is non-Newtonian: at low flow red cells form rouleaux and viscosity rises, while in vessels below about 0.3 mm a cell-poor plasma layer at the wall lowers apparent viscosity (the Fåhræus-Lindqvist effect)."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the effect of a single change (pressure gradient held constant)","cols":["Change","Effect on resistance","Effect on flow","Physiologic or clinical example"],"rows":[["Radius halved","Increases 16-fold","Falls to 1/16","Arteriolar constriction by sympathetic alpha-1 stimulation; severe focal stenosis"],["Radius doubled","Falls to 1/16","Increases 16-fold","Metabolic dilation of muscle arterioles during exercise"],["Radius increased about 19%","Halves","Doubles","Small dilation of a coronary arteriole when myocardial O2 demand rises"],["Viscosity doubled","Doubles","Halves","Severe polycythemia or hyperviscosity (anemia does the reverse)"],["Length doubled","Doubles","Halves","Long diffuse arterial disease compared with a short focal lesion"],["Pressure gradient doubled","Unchanged (in a rigid tube)","Doubles","Only true for a rigid tube; living vessels distend or constrict, see below"]],"caption":"Radius dominates because it is raised to the fourth power. Viscosity, length, and pressure gradient change flow only in direct proportion."},{"type":"p","text":"Poiseuille's law assumes rigid tubes, laminar flow, and a Newtonian fluid. Passive vessels distend as pressure rises, so flow rises more than in proportion; autoregulating vessels constrict, so flow stays constant (C06); turbulence needs a larger gradient for the same flow (C03). Use the law for direction and relative size of change."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C02","obj":"CV-11.2","title":"Series and parallel resistance and the role of arterioles","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In series, the same flow passes through each segment, resistances add (R total = R1 + R2 + ..., so 10 and 20 units give 30), and the segment with the highest resistance takes the largest pressure drop because ΔP = Q x R. The systemic segments from aorta to veins are in series, and the pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series with each other, so the two ventricles must have equal average outputs. In parallel, every branch sees the same pressure difference, flows add, and conductances (1/R) add: 1 / R total = 1 / R1 + 1 / R2 + .... Total parallel resistance is lower than the smallest branch, and n identical branches give R / n. For example, branches of 10, 20, and 20 units give 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/20 = 0.2, a total of 5 units; removing the 10-unit branch (as with an amputation) raises it to 10. Adding a parallel path, such as an arteriovenous fistula or the placenta, lowers TPR."},{"type":"h","text":"Why arterioles set TPR"},{"type":"p","text":"Each capillary is narrower than an arteriole, but billions of capillaries in parallel give a moderate combined resistance, and they have no smooth muscle. Arterioles are fewer, narrow, and have thick smooth muscle relative to their lumen, so they hold the largest share of systemic resistance and are its main adjustable part. With the small arteries just upstream, they produce the steepest pressure drop, from about 85 to 90 mmHg to about 30 to 35 mmHg, which also damps the arterial pulse so capillary flow is nearly steady. Terminal arterioles and precapillary sphincters decide how many capillaries are open."},{"type":"table","title":"Pressure, area, velocity, and volume along the systemic circuit","cols":["Segment","Mean pressure","Share of resistance","Total cross-sectional area","Share of blood volume"],"rows":[["Aorta and large arteries","About 95 to 100 mmHg, pulsatile","Small","Aorta about 2.5 cm²","About 13% (all systemic arteries)"],["Small arteries and arterioles","Falls from about 85 to 90 to about 30 to 35 mmHg","Largest; arterioles dominate","Arterioles about 40 cm²","About 7% with capillaries"],["Capillaries","About 30 to 35 mmHg arterial end, 10 to 15 venous end","Moderate (many in parallel)","About 2,500 cm²","Included above"],["Venules and veins","About 10 to 15 mmHg, near 0 at the right atrium","Small","Venules about 250 cm²; venae cavae about 8 cm²","About 64% (systemic veins)"]],"caption":"Approximate resting supine values (Guyton and Hall); the heart holds about 7% and the lungs about 9% of blood volume. Velocity (C03) mirrors total area."},{"type":"p","text":"Because systemic organs branch from the aorta in parallel, each receives nearly the same arterial pressure, and its flow equals MAP divided by its own resistance. With MAP held steady by the baroreceptor reflex (CV-6), an organ raises its flow by dilating its own arterioles without starving the others. A few beds lie deliberately in series: gut and spleen to liver through the portal vein, glomerular to peritubular capillaries, and hypothalamus to anterior pituitary. In each, raised downstream resistance raises upstream capillary pressure, which is how portal hypertension develops."},{"type":"flow","title":"Opening a parallel bed: what happens to TPR, MAP, and other organs","steps":["One large bed dilates, such as exercising muscle","TPR falls","MAP would fall if CO were unchanged","Baroreceptors raise sympathetic output","CO rises; gut, kidney, and inactive muscle constrict","MAP is defended and flow shifts to the dilated bed"],"caption":"Each organ sets its own resistance, while the baroreceptor reflex holds the common driving pressure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A large forearm arteriovenous fistula is created. Predict the change in TPR, cardiac output, and diastolic pressure.","explain":"The fistula is a low-resistance parallel path, so TPR falls. Cardiac output rises to hold MAP, and fast diastolic runoff lowers diastolic pressure and widens pulse pressure. A very large fistula can cause high-output heart failure.","check":"Compare with the parallel rule and the flow figure on opening a parallel bed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C03","obj":"CV-11.3","title":"Velocity, cross-sectional area, and turbulence","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Flow (Q, volume per time) and velocity (v, distance per time) are linked by cross-sectional area: v = Q / A. Because the same cardiac output passes through every level of the series circuit, velocity at each level varies inversely with the total cross-sectional area at that level. Mean velocity is about 33 cm/s in the aorta and about 0.3 mm/s in the capillaries, whose combined area is about 1,000 times larger. Capillary velocity is lowest because there are so many capillaries in parallel, and the 1 to 3 seconds each red cell spends in a systemic capillary allow diffusion. Velocity rises again in the veins but stays below aortic values because the venae cavae together are larger than the aorta."},{"type":"p","text":"Locally, the continuity principle means a narrowed segment of an artery or valve must carry the same flow through a smaller area, so velocity rises. Doppler measures that velocity, and the simplified Bernoulli relation (gradient in mmHg is about 4 times peak velocity in m/s squared) estimates the pressure gradient across a stenotic valve."},{"type":"h","text":"Laminar and turbulent flow"},{"type":"p","text":"Laminar flow moves in silent concentric layers. Turbulent flow forms eddies, wastes energy, needs more pressure for the same flow (effectively raising resistance), and vibrates the wall, producing a murmur, a bruit, or a palpable thrill. The Reynolds number, Re = ρvD / η (density, velocity, diameter, viscosity), predicts turbulence, which appears above about 2,000 in a straight smooth tube and at lower values at branches, curves, and abrupt changes in caliber. Density barely varies, so velocity, diameter, and viscosity are the clinical variables. In a stenosis, halving the radius cuts area to one-quarter, so velocity rises 4-fold while diameter halves, and Re doubles; the jet then breaks into eddies as it enters the wider vessel beyond. When a stenosis becomes critical, flow, velocity, and Re fall, and the bruit can soften or vanish, so a quiet carotid does not exclude severe stenosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that favor turbulence and their clinical correlates","cols":["Factor","Effect on Reynolds number","Clinical example"],"rows":[["High velocity","Raises Re","Flow murmurs in pregnancy, fever, hyperthyroidism, exercise, anemia"],["Low viscosity","Raises Re","Anemia, which also raises cardiac output"],["Large diameter","Raises Re","Proximal aorta in rapid ejection; dilated aortic root"],["Stenosis","High-velocity jet and abrupt change in caliber","Carotid, renal, femoral bruits; aortic stenosis murmur"],["High to low pressure connection","Very high velocity","Continuous bruit over an arteriovenous fistula; ventricular septal defect"],["Cuff compression","Narrowed segment with a jet","Korotkoff sounds"],["High viscosity","Lowers Re","Polycythemia tends to abolish flow murmurs"]],"caption":"Anemia acts on velocity and viscosity at once, which makes it the classic cause of a flow murmur across normal valves."},{"type":"flow","title":"From anemia to a flow murmur","steps":["Hemoglobin and hematocrit fall","Viscosity falls and resistance falls","Low O2 content dilates arterioles, so TPR falls further","Cardiac output and stroke volume rise","Ejection velocity through normal valves rises","Higher velocity and lower viscosity raise Re","Soft systolic flow murmur across normal valves"],"caption":"The same reasoning explains flow murmurs in pregnancy, fever, and hyperthyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"Korotkoff sounds use turbulence deliberately. Just below systolic pressure, blood spurts through the partly compressed brachial artery at each peak and the first tapping sound marks systolic pressure; once cuff pressure falls below diastolic pressure, flow is laminar throughout and the disappearance of sound (phase V) marks diastolic pressure. Disturbed, oscillating flow at branch points and bifurcation walls gives low shear stress, which reduces endothelial nitric oxide and favors atherosclerosis, whereas steady laminar high shear keeps the endothelium quiescent and antithrombotic. Jets beyond a stenotic valve can cause post-stenotic dilation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C04","obj":"CV-11.4","title":"Arterial compliance, pulse pressure, and isolated systolic hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Compliance is C = ΔV / ΔP; stiffness (elastance) is its reciprocal. Systemic veins are about 24 times as compliant as the corresponding arteries (about 8 times as distensible with about 3 times the volume), which is why they hold most of the blood. The elastin-rich large arteries stretch during ejection to store part of the stroke volume and recoil during diastole to push it onward. This reservoir (Windkessel) function turns pulsatile output into near-continuous capillary flow, keeps diastolic pressure from falling to zero, and lowers the peak pressure the ventricle must generate."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulse pressure, systolic minus diastolic (normally about 40 mmHg), is roughly proportional to stroke volume divided by arterial compliance. Diastolic pressure depends on how fast stored volume runs off, governed by the time constant R x C: low resistance, low compliance, or a long diastole lets pressure fall lower. At resting heart rates MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one-third of pulse pressure, an estimate that loses accuracy at fast rates when systole takes a larger share of the cycle."},{"type":"table","title":"What widens or narrows pulse pressure","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Effect on pulse pressure"],"rows":[["Aortic stiffening with age","Low compliance: higher systolic, lower diastolic","Wide"],["Aortic regurgitation","Large stroke volume plus diastolic backflow into the ventricle","Very wide"],["Hyperthyroidism, anemia, fever, pregnancy","Larger stroke volume and lower TPR","Wide"],["Arteriovenous fistula, early vasodilatory sepsis","Low TPR, rapid diastolic runoff","Wide"],["Exercise","Larger, faster stroke volume","Wide (mainly higher systolic)"],["Hemorrhage, aortic stenosis, tamponade, severe heart failure","Small stroke volume (reflex constriction holds diastolic pressure in hemorrhage)","Narrow"]],"caption":"Larger stroke volume, stiffer arteries, or faster runoff widen pulse pressure; a small stroke volume narrows it."},{"type":"h","text":"Aging and isolated systolic hypertension"},{"type":"p","text":"With age, elastin fragments, collagen increases and is cross-linked by advanced glycation end products, and the media may calcify; hypertension, diabetes, and chronic kidney disease accelerate this. A stiff aorta stores little of each stroke volume, so systolic pressure rises and fast runoff lets diastolic pressure fall. Faster pulse wave velocity returns the reflected wave in late systole, augmenting the systolic peak instead of supporting diastole. Diastolic pressure levels off or falls after about age 50 to 60, producing isolated systolic hypertension, the most common form in older adults (systolic 130 mmHg or higher with diastolic below 80 mmHg in the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline; 140 and 90 mmHg in others). The wider pulsatile pressure reaches the low-resistance brain and kidney, contributing to lacunar infarcts, white matter disease, and declining renal function, and stiffness rises with stretch, so hypertension and stiffening reinforce each other."},{"type":"flow","title":"From aortic stiffening to isolated systolic hypertension","steps":["Elastin fragments; glycation cross-links collagen in the aortic media","Compliance falls and pulse wave velocity rises","Higher systolic peak, augmented by late-systolic reflected waves","Less stored volume, so diastolic pressure falls","Wide pulse pressure: isolated systolic hypertension","Higher afterload drives concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","Lower diastolic pressure reduces coronary perfusion of the subendocardium"],"caption":"Left ventricular coronary flow occurs mainly in diastole (CV-8), so a low diastolic pressure matters more than its size suggests."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the blood pressure pattern from stroke volume and compliance","text":"Two people share a stroke volume of 70 mL, heart rate, and TPR. Person A is 25; person B is 80, with aortic compliance halved. Pulse pressure is roughly stroke volume divided by compliance, so the same 70 mL produces about twice the rise in pressure: 40 mmHg in A becomes about 80 mmHg in B. The runoff time constant (R x C) is also halved in B, so pressure falls faster and further before the next beat. MAP is set by CO and TPR, which are unchanged, so MAP stays about 93 mmHg. A pressure of about 120/80 mmHg in A therefore becomes something like 150/65 mmHg in B. Stiffening alone raises systolic pressure, lowers diastolic pressure, and widens pulse pressure without changing mean pressure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C05","obj":"CV-11.5","title":"Starling forces, lymph, and the mechanisms of edema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Small solutes cross the capillary wall freely, so water movement is set by hydrostatic pressure and by the oncotic pressure of poorly permeant proteins, chiefly albumin. Net filtration Jv = Kf x [(Pc - Pi) - σ(πc - πi)]. Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) pushes fluid out: about 30 mmHg at the arterial end and 10 mmHg at the venous end in Guyton and Hall's teaching values. Interstitial pressure (Pi) is about -3 mmHg in loose tissue, so it adds to filtration. Plasma oncotic pressure (πc, about 28 mmHg, about 80% from albumin) pulls fluid in; interstitial oncotic pressure (πi, about 8 mmHg) pulls it out. The reflection coefficient σ is near 1 in continuous capillaries and low in hepatic sinusoids; Kf is high in glomeruli and low in the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"With these values the net pressure is (30 + 3) - (28 - 8) = +13 mmHg at the arterial end and (10 + 3) - 20 = -7 mmHg at the venous end. Filtration slightly exceeds reabsorption, and lymph returns the excess and leaked protein, about 2 to 3 L per day (CV-10). In the revised glycocalyx model most capillaries filter along their whole length; both models predict edema identically."},{"type":"h","text":"Mechanisms of edema"},{"type":"p","text":"Edema appears when filtration exceeds lymphatic removal, and each cause acts on one Starling term. In cirrhosis, portal hypertension raises splanchnic Pc while low albumin lowers πc, so ascites forms readily. In heart failure, raised venous pressure is the direct cause, and reduced effective arterial volume activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and ADH, whose Na+ and water retention raises venous pressure further. Left failure causes pulmonary edema; right failure causes dependent edema, ascites, and hepatic congestion."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of edema","cols":["Mechanism","Starling term changed","Examples","Character of fluid"],"rows":[["Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure","Pc up","Heart failure, deep vein thrombosis, portal hypertension, renal volume overload, prolonged standing, dihydropyridine arteriolar dilation","Transudate, pitting"],["Low plasma oncotic pressure","πc down","Nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, protein malnutrition (kwashiorkor), protein-losing enteropathy","Transudate, pitting, generalized and periorbital"],["Increased permeability","Kf up, σ down, πi up","Inflammation, burns, sepsis, ARDS, angioedema","Exudate, localized in inflammation"],["Lymphatic obstruction","Removal fails; πi rises","Node dissection or radiation, tumor, filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti)","Protein rich; nonpitting once fibrosed"]],"caption":"Transudates have specific gravity below about 1.012 and exudates above about 1.020, so the fluid points to the mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Three safety factors delay edema: negative interstitial pressure rises quickly and opposes filtration, lymph flow increases many-fold, and lymph washes out interstitial protein, lowering πi. Guyton and Hall estimate these allow Pc to rise about 17 mmHg before significant edema. Above a Pi of about 0 mmHg the interstitium becomes highly compliant and large volumes accumulate."},{"type":"example","title":"Working through hypoalbuminemia with the Starling equation","text":"In nephrotic syndrome, suppose plasma oncotic pressure falls from 28 to 16 mmHg. At the arterial end, net outward pressure rises from 33 - 20 = 13 mmHg to 33 - (16 - 8) = 25 mmHg. At the venous end, -7 mmHg (reabsorption) becomes 13 - 8 = +5 mmHg (filtration). Every capillary is affected, so edema is generalized, appearing first where tissue pressure is low (periorbital, scrotal) and in dependent areas. Washout of interstitial protein blunts the change at first, which is why oncotic pressure must fall substantially before edema appears. The wall is intact, so the fluid is a transudate. In a burn, by contrast, protein enters the interstitium, the oncotic difference collapses locally, and the result is a localized high-protein exudate."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Arm swelling follows axillary node dissection. Which Starling term changes, and why does the swelling stop pitting?","explain":"Capillary forces are normal at first, but filtrate and protein cannot leave, so πi rises and draws more fluid out. Chronic protein-rich fluid drives fibrosis and fat deposition, and fibrosed tissue no longer pits.","check":"Compare with the lymphatic obstruction row of the edema table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-11-01-C06","obj":"CV-11.6","title":"Control of arteriolar tone and regional blood flow","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arteriolar tone is sustained partial contraction of smooth muscle. Contraction follows a rise in cytosolic Ca2+, which binds calmodulin and activates myosin light chain kinase; relaxation follows a fall in Ca2+ or activation of myosin light chain phosphatase. Constrictors act through Gq receptors (IP3 releases Ca2+) or by depolarization that opens L-type Ca2+ channels; dilators raise cGMP or cAMP or open K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Metabolic control: adenosine, CO2, H+, K+, lactate, phosphate, and low PO2 accumulate when activity rises or flow falls short and dilate arterioles, partly by opening K+ channels. Flow washes them out, a negative feedback that produces active hyperemia and reactive hyperemia (a surge after occlusion proportional to its duration). Myogenic control (Bayliss response): stretch opens mechanosensitive cation channels, depolarizes the muscle, opens L-type Ca2+ channels, and constricts the arteriole. Together these give autoregulation over a MAP of roughly 60 to 70 up to 150 to 160 mmHg in brain and kidney; chronic hypertension shifts the range upward, so a drop a normotensive person tolerates can cause hypoperfusion. The kidney adds tubuloglomerular feedback."},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial control: shear stress and agonists (acetylcholine, bradykinin, histamine, substance P) activate eNOS, and the NO pathway below relaxes the muscle; dilation spreads to feeding arteries (flow-mediated dilation). Prostacyclin raises cAMP, hyperpolarizing factors matter in small arterioles, and endothelin-1 constricts through ETA receptors. With damaged endothelium, acetylcholine constricts through smooth muscle muscarinic receptors. Neural control: sympathetic norepinephrine on alpha-1 (Gq) receptors sets basal tone; its withdrawal is the main neural dilator, as systemic arterioles lack parasympathetic supply. Hormonal control: angiotensin II (AT1) and high-dose vasopressin (V1) constrict through Gq; epinephrine dilates muscle and liver arterioles through beta-2 (Gs) at physiologic levels and constricts through alpha-1 at high levels; natriuretic peptides raise cGMP; histamine and bradykinin dilate through NO and open venular gaps that cause inflammatory edema."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nitric oxide pathway from endothelium to smooth muscle","steps":["Shear stress or acetylcholine and bradykinin act on endothelium","Ca2+-calmodulin and phosphorylation activate eNOS","eNOS converts L-arginine to NO and citrulline","NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase in smooth muscle","cGMP rises and activates protein kinase G","Ca2+ falls; myosin light chain phosphatase activates","Myosin light chains dephosphorylate; arteriole dilates"],"caption":"Nitrates release NO inside smooth muscle, bypassing eNOS, so they work even with endothelial dysfunction."},{"type":"h","text":"Dominant control in each bed"},{"type":"p","text":"The heart extracts about 70 to 75% of arterial O2 at rest, so more demand needs more flow, and metabolic dilation overrides alpha-1 constriction. Cerebral flow follows PaCO2: CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier and lowers perivascular pH, so flow roughly doubles from 40 to 80 mmHg and hyperventilation constricts; plasma H+ crosses slowly. In exercising muscle, metabolites and NO override sympathetic tone (functional sympatholysis) and flow rises about 20-fold. Skin flow serves heat loss, so skin is pale and cool in hemorrhage. Pulmonary resistance falls passively as cardiac output rises, by recruitment and distension, and low alveolar PO2 inhibits O2-sensitive K+ channels, depolarizing and constricting smooth muscle to divert blood to ventilated alveoli; global hypoxia causes pulmonary hypertension (CV-15)."},{"type":"table","title":"Main control of flow by vascular bed","cols":["Bed","Dominant control","Hypoxia","Sympathetic effect"],"rows":[["Coronary","Metabolites (adenosine, NO, low O2)","Dilation","Alpha-1 effect overridden by metabolism"],["Cerebral","PaCO2 (through pH) and autoregulation","Dilation below a PaO2 of about 50 mmHg","Minor"],["Skeletal muscle","Sympathetic at rest; metabolites in exercise","Dilation","Strong at rest; overridden in active muscle"],["Skin","Sympathetic thermoregulation","Little role","Strong, especially arteriovenous anastomoses"],["Pulmonary","Hypoxic vasoconstriction; recruitment and distension","Constriction","Minor"],["Kidney","Myogenic and tubuloglomerular feedback","Minor","Constriction when strong"]],"caption":"Heart and brain rely on local control and escape sympathetic constriction; skin, gut, kidney, and resting muscle do not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a hyperventilating patient in a panic attack feel lightheaded?","explain":"Low PaCO2 raises perivascular pH and constricts cerebral arterioles, so cerebral flow falls despite normal arterial pressure.","check":"Compare with the cerebral sentences and bed table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Structure sets the coefficients. Elastic arteries supply the compliance behind pulse pressure, muscular arterioles supply the adjustable radius that Poiseuille's law makes dominant, and capillary type sets Kf and σ: tight brain capillaries, fenestrated glomeruli, and discontinuous hepatic sinusoids. Biochemistry supplies the signals (NO and cGMP, adenosine from ATP breakdown, Gq and Gs receptors converging on myosin light chain phosphorylation) and the aging of the wall through glycation, which links diabetes to stiffening."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology applies the same physics. Inflammatory mediators dilate arterioles and open venular gaps to produce a protein-rich exudate, and sepsis does this body-wide, lowering TPR and diastolic pressure. Plaques form where shear is low, raise resistance by the fourth power, and generate bruits. The equations predict direction and relative size; textbook values vary, so answer from the relationships."}],"summary":["Q = ΔP / R; for the whole body MAP is approximately CO x TPR.","Poiseuille's law makes radius dominant: halving radius raises resistance 16-fold, and a 19% wider radius doubles flow; hematocrit sets viscosity.","Series resistances add and parallel conductances add, so adding a parallel bed lowers TPR; arterioles hold the largest, adjustable share of resistance.","Velocity equals flow divided by total area, so it is lowest in capillaries; stenosis raises local velocity and the Reynolds number.","High velocity and low viscosity favor turbulence, so anemia causes flow murmurs; critical stenoses can lose their bruit.","Pulse pressure rises with stroke volume and falling compliance; fast runoff (low TPR, aortic regurgitation) lowers diastolic pressure.","Aortic stiffening with early wave reflection produces isolated systolic hypertension with unchanged MAP.","Jv = Kf x [(Pc - Pi) - σ(πc - πi)]; edema comes from high Pc, low πc, high permeability (exudate), or lymphatic obstruction.","Metabolic, myogenic, and endothelial controls match flow to need; sympathetic and hormonal controls defend arterial pressure.","Coronary flow follows metabolism, cerebral flow follows PaCO2, skin and resting muscle follow sympathetic tone, and pulmonary vessels constrict to hypoxia."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Overview of the Circulation; Biophysics of Pressure, Flow, and Resistance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Vascular Distensibility and Functions of the Arterial and Venous Systems"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow; Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiovascular system sections on arteries and veins, the microcirculation, and special circulations"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolism, and Shock (section on edema)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"}]},"CV-12":{"unit":"CV-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01","title":"Exercise and the Cardiovascular System","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the circulation meets the demands of exercise, first in a single bout and then after months of training. It covers the neural signals that start the response, uses the Fick principle to predict how heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, and O2 extraction change with work, and follows the redistribution of cardiac output among organs. It then contrasts the pressure response to dynamic and static exercise and separates the endurance athlete's heart from hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Board questions usually ask which variable rises, falls, or stays the same. Three relationships answer nearly all of them: cardiac output equals heart rate times stroke volume, pressure equals flow times resistance, and the Fick equation.","prerequisites":["Determinants of cardiac output: heart rate, preload, afterload, contractility, and the Frank-Starling relationship (CV-6)","Autonomic control of the SA node through M2 and beta-1 receptors (CV-7)","Pressure, flow, and resistance, including parallel resistances and local metabolic control of blood flow (CV-11)","The baroreceptor reflex and the medullary cardiovascular centers","Oxygen content of blood and the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac cycle and its regulation (CV-6)","how":"Exercise raises stroke volume through the same preload and contractility mechanisms that shape the pressure-volume loop."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Vagal withdrawal and sympathetic drive change heart rate through IKACh channels and the funny current, and the athlete's resting bradycardia reflects the same nodal mechanisms."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Metabolic vasodilation in parallel muscle beds explains why total peripheral resistance falls while mean arterial pressure rises."},{"to":"Blood distribution to body regions (CV-9) and coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"Each organ bed responds according to its balance of local and neural control, and the coronary bed must raise flow because its O2 extraction is already near maximal at rest."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E) and ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy must be distinguished from athlete's heart, and exercise unmasks coronary stenosis because it raises myocardial O2 demand."}],"terms":[{"term":"Central command","def":"Feedforward signals from higher brain centers that activate motor units and, in parallel, the medullary cardiovascular centers at the onset of exercise."},{"term":"Exercise pressor reflex","def":"The reflex rise in sympathetic activity and blood pressure driven by muscle afferents, made up of the mechanoreflex (mainly group III fibers) and the metaboreflex (mainly group IV fibers)."},{"term":"Vagal withdrawal","def":"A reduction in parasympathetic discharge to the heart that raises heart rate within a beat or two."},{"term":"Fick principle","def":"For the whole body, O2 uptake (VO2) equals cardiac output times the arteriovenous O2 difference."},{"term":"Arteriovenous O2 difference","def":"Arterial minus mixed venous O2 content, the O2 removed from each unit of blood, about 5 mL O2/dL at rest."},{"term":"VO2max","def":"Maximal oxygen uptake, identified by a plateau in VO2 despite further increases in work rate."},{"term":"Functional sympatholysis","def":"The blunting of sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction in contracting muscle by local metabolites and nitric oxide."},{"term":"Static (isometric) exercise","def":"Sustained muscle contraction without a change in muscle length, such as a handgrip or a heavy hold."},{"term":"Rate-pressure product","def":"Heart rate multiplied by systolic pressure, an index of myocardial O2 demand."},{"term":"Eccentric hypertrophy","def":"Ventricular growth by adding sarcomeres in series, enlarging the cavity with proportional wall thickening; the response to volume load."},{"term":"Concentric hypertrophy","def":"Ventricular growth by adding sarcomeres in parallel, thickening the wall relative to cavity size; the response to pressure load."},{"term":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","def":"An inherited, usually autosomal dominant sarcomere disease that causes unexplained, often asymmetric, left ventricular hypertrophy with myocyte disarray."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01-C01","obj":"CV-12.1","title":"Starting the exercise response: central command, muscle reflexes, and the autonomic sequence","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heart rate rises within the first second of exercise, too fast for circulating metabolites to explain. The nervous system starts it through two inputs to the medullary cardiovascular centers: a feedforward signal from the brain (central command) and feedback from the contracting muscles (the exercise pressor reflex)."},{"type":"h","text":"Central command"},{"type":"p","text":"As the motor cortex and subcortical locomotor regions recruit motor units, they signal the medullary centers in parallel. The output scales with the effort commanded rather than the force produced: when muscles are partly weakened by a neuromuscular blocking drug, the same force takes more effort, and heart rate and blood pressure rise more. Central command also explains the anticipatory rise in heart rate before a race. It resets the arterial baroreflex to a higher set point, so the reflex keeps buffering moment-to-moment changes without opposing the rise in pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"The two arms of the exercise pressor reflex","cols":["Feature","Mechanoreflex","Metaboreflex"],"rows":[["Main afferents","Group III (thinly myelinated)","Group IV (unmyelinated C fibers)"],["Stimulus","Stretch and tension of contracting muscle","H+, lactate, K+, inorganic phosphate, ATP, bradykinin, prostaglandins"],["Timing","First seconds of contraction","Builds over tens of seconds"],["Strongest when","Contraction begins","Flow is too low for metabolism (static or ischemic muscle)"],["Main effect","Early heart rate rise, largely by reducing vagal outflow","Sympathetic activation and a rise in arterial pressure"]],"caption":"Both arms converge on the same brainstem centers and interact."},{"type":"p","text":"The metaboreflex works as an error signal: when flow is inadequate for metabolism, metabolites accumulate and the reflex raises arterial pressure to push blood into the underperfused muscle. If a cuff traps metabolites in the arm at the end of a sustained handgrip, blood pressure stays high while heart rate returns toward baseline, which shows that the heart rate response depends mostly on central command and the mechanoreflex."},{"type":"h","text":"Why vagal withdrawal comes first"},{"type":"p","text":"Vagal acetylcholine acts on M2 receptors, and Gi beta-gamma subunits open IKACh channels directly (CV-7). When vagal firing stops, acetylcholinesterase clears acetylcholine within milliseconds, so heart rate rises within one or two beats. Norepinephrine acts through beta-1 receptors, Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A and is cleared mainly by reuptake, so its effects build and fade over seconds. Vagal withdrawal therefore produces most of the early rise, up to roughly 100 bpm in a young adult. Above that, sympathetic drive raises rate further, increases contractility, and constricts veins and arterioles in inactive tissues."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac output can rise only if venous return rises with it. Sympathetic venoconstriction of the splanchnic and cutaneous veins raises mean systemic filling pressure, the skeletal muscle pump drives blood past venous valves toward the heart, and deeper breathing lowers intrathoracic pressure (the respiratory pump). Greater filling raises stroke volume through the Frank-Starling mechanism."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cardiovascular response at the onset of exercise","steps":["Central command signals medullary centers as motor units are recruited","Vagal outflow falls; heart rate rises within one or two beats","Group III mechanoreceptors reinforce the early heart rate rise","Sympathetic outflow builds over seconds: rate, contractility, venous tone","Muscle and respiratory pumps raise venous return and preload","Stroke volume and cardiac output rise as active arterioles dilate","If flow lags metabolism, group IV afferents trigger the metaboreflex"],"caption":"Central command starts the response; muscle feedback matches it to the working muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"A transplanted heart is denervated, so its resting rate sits near the intrinsic SA node rate and barely changes at exercise onset. Cardiac output first rises through the Frank-Starling response, and rate climbs over minutes as circulating catecholamines rise, to a lower peak than normal. Beta-1 blockade blunts the sympathetic part of the rate response but spares vagal withdrawal. In heart failure, an exaggerated exercise pressor reflex adds excess sympathetic vasoconstriction during exercise."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01-C02","obj":"CV-12.2","title":"Graded exercise and the Fick principle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Oxygen uptake (VO2) rises in proportion to external work. Because O2 is not stored, the Fick principle states that VO2 equals cardiac output times the arteriovenous O2 difference (a-vO2 difference), which is arterial minus mixed venous O2 content. Exercise raises both terms."},{"type":"p","text":"Arterial O2 content is about 1.34 mL O2 per gram of hemoglobin times hemoglobin concentration times saturation, plus 0.003 mL/dL per mmHg of PO2 dissolved, which gives about 20 mL/dL at a hemoglobin of 15 g/dL and saturation of 98%. Healthy lungs keep this nearly constant during exercise, so the a-vO2 difference widens from the venous side, as mixed venous content falls from about 15 mL/dL (saturation about 75%) to about 4 to 5 mL/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Extraction rises for three reasons. Metabolites recruit closed capillaries, enlarging the exchange surface and shortening diffusion distance (section C03). Tissue PO2 falls onto the steep part of the dissociation curve, where a small drop in PO2 releases much O2. Higher PCO2, H+, and temperature in active muscle shift the curve to the right (the Bohr effect for CO2 and H+)."},{"type":"h","text":"Heart rate, stroke volume, and cardiac output"},{"type":"p","text":"Heart rate rises almost linearly with work rate. Maximal heart rate falls with age, and 220 minus age is only an estimate (standard deviation about 10 to 12 bpm). Stroke volume rises because end-diastolic volume increases and greater contractility lowers end-systolic volume, raising ejection fraction from about 60% toward 80%. In untrained people it plateaus at about 40% to 60% of VO2max, because shortened diastole limits filling, so further gains in cardiac output come from heart rate; trained endurance athletes may keep raising stroke volume. Cardiac output rises about 5 to 6 L/min for each 1 L/min rise in VO2."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical values at rest and at maximal exercise (young adult, upright)","cols":["Variable","Rest","Maximum, untrained","Maximum, elite endurance athlete"],"rows":[["Heart rate","About 70 bpm (athlete about 50)","About 195 bpm","About 185 to 195 bpm"],["Stroke volume","About 70 mL (athlete about 100)","About 110 mL","About 160 mL or more"],["Cardiac output","About 5 L/min","About 20 to 22 L/min","About 30 to 35 L/min"],["Mixed venous O2 content","About 15 mL/dL","About 5 mL/dL","About 3 to 4 mL/dL"],["a-vO2 difference","About 5 mL/dL","About 15 mL/dL","About 16 to 17 mL/dL"],["VO2","About 250 mL/min (3.5 mL/kg/min, 1 MET)","About 3 L/min (35 to 45 mL/kg/min)","About 5 to 6 L/min (70 to 85 mL/kg/min)"]],"caption":"Approximate values for a 70 kg adult; arterial O2 content stays about 20 mL/dL throughout."},{"type":"p","text":"VO2 rises linearly with work until it plateaus at VO2max. Above the lactate threshold, often about 50% to 60% of VO2max in untrained people, lactate production exceeds clearance, and bicarbonate buffering of the extra H+ releases CO2, so ventilation rises out of proportion to VO2. In healthy people at sea level, VO2max is limited mainly by maximal cardiac output rather than by the lungs or muscle: raising arterial O2 content raises VO2max, and lowering it reduces VO2max. Maximal stroke volume is therefore its main determinant (section C05)."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the Fick equation","text":"A 25-year-old untrained man of 75 kg has a resting cardiac output of 70 bpm times 70 mL, about 4.9 L/min, and an a-vO2 difference of 20 minus 15, or 5 mL/dL (50 mL/L), so VO2 is about 245 mL/min. At maximum, 195 bpm times 110 mL gives about 21.5 L/min, and venous content falls to 5 mL/dL, so the difference is 150 mL/L. VO2max is about 3,200 mL/min (43 mL/kg/min): a 4.4-fold rise in flow times a threefold rise in extraction. With anemia (hemoglobin 9 g/dL), arterial content is about 12 mL/dL, so even at a venous content of 4 mL/dL the maximal difference is 80 mL/L. Consuming 2,400 mL/min would then need 30 L/min of cardiac output, so VO2max falls and heart rate is higher at any submaximal load."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01-C03","obj":"CV-12.3","title":"Redistribution of cardiac output during exercise","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Extra cardiac output goes to active muscle, the heart, and, as body temperature rises, the skin. Each bed's flow depends on whether sympathetic vasoconstriction or local metabolic vasodilation wins in its arterioles."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate distribution of cardiac output at rest and at maximal exercise","cols":["Vascular bed","Rest (total about 5.8 L/min)","Maximal exercise (total about 25 L/min)","Dominant control"],"rows":[["Skeletal muscle","About 1.2 L/min (20%)","About 22 L/min (about 87%)","Metabolites and NO"],["Coronary","About 0.25 L/min (4%)","About 1 L/min (4%)","Metabolic, mainly adenosine"],["Brain","About 0.75 L/min (13%)","About 0.75 L/min (3%)","Autoregulation and PCO2"],["Skin","About 0.5 L/min (9%)","About 0.6 L/min (higher in heavy submaximal work)","Hypothalamic thermoregulation"],["Splanchnic","About 1.4 L/min (24%)","About 0.3 L/min","Sympathetic constriction"],["Kidneys","About 1.1 L/min (19%)","About 0.25 L/min","Sympathetic constriction and angiotensin II"]],"caption":"Illustrative classic values; figures vary among sources, but the pattern does not."},{"type":"h","text":"Active skeletal muscle"},{"type":"p","text":"Muscle flow rises from about 3 to 4 mL/100 g/min at rest, when arteriolar tone is high and few capillaries are open, to about 50 to 80 mL/100 g/min in strenuous exercise, up to about 15 to 25 times resting. Interstitial K+ from repeated action potentials hyperpolarizes vascular smooth muscle through inward rectifier K+ channels and Na+/K+-ATPase. Adenosine, inorganic phosphate, H+, CO2, and low PO2 add to the dilation, and low PO2 makes red cells release ATP, which stimulates endothelial nitric oxide (NO). Shear stress then releases NO upstream, spreading dilation to feed arteries. Blocking any one mediator only partly reduces it."},{"type":"p","text":"Sympathetic nerves keep firing, but the same metabolites blunt alpha-adrenergic constriction in active muscle, especially through alpha-2 receptors (functional sympatholysis), so inactive beds constrict while active muscle is protected. Some residual constriction is needed, because the dilating capacity of all muscle exceeds maximal cardiac output. Because beds lie in parallel, their conductances add, so total peripheral resistance (TPR) falls steeply in dynamic exercise even though other beds constrict."},{"type":"flow","title":"How flow concentrates in working muscle","steps":["Sympathetic outflow rises to all vascular beds","Gut, kidney, and inactive muscle arterioles constrict through alpha receptors","In active muscle, K+, adenosine, H+, CO2, low PO2, and NO dilate arterioles","The same metabolites blunt alpha constriction (functional sympatholysis)","Resistance falls in active muscle and rises in inactive beds","Parallel beds share one arterial pressure, so flow shifts to active muscle"],"caption":"Each parallel bed's share depends on its resistance relative to the others."},{"type":"h","text":"The other organs"},{"type":"p","text":"The myocardium already extracts about 70% to 75% of delivered O2 at rest, so higher demand must be met by flow, rising about three- to fourfold through mainly adenosine-mediated dilation. A stenosis that is silent at rest therefore causes angina on exertion. Cerebral flow stays nearly constant through autoregulation. Splanchnic and renal flow fall to about one quarter of resting at maximal effort; splanchnic venoconstriction moves reservoir blood centrally, and renal filtration fraction rises because renal blood flow falls more than GFR."},{"type":"p","text":"Core temperature rises toward 38 to 39 °C (100.4 to 102.2 °F) in sustained heavy exercise. The hypothalamus withdraws cutaneous vasoconstrictor tone and activates sympathetic cholinergic fibers that cause active vasodilation and sweating, carrying heat to the skin. Pooling in compliant skin veins and sweat loss then reduce filling, so in prolonged exercise, especially in heat, stroke volume falls and heart rate drifts upward (cardiovascular drift). At maximal effort skin flow falls again, favoring muscle but limiting heat loss; above 40 °C (104 °F) core temperature reaches the range of exertional heat stroke."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A person runs at the same speed on a cool day and a hot, humid day. On the hot day, what happens to stroke volume, heart rate, and leg muscle flow?","explain":"Skin pooling and sweat loss lower stroke volume, so heart rate rises to hold cardiac output, and muscle shares flow with skin, lowering the sustainable workload.","check":"Compare with the skin blood flow paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01-C04","obj":"CV-12.4","title":"Blood pressure and resistance in dynamic and static exercise","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mean arterial pressure (MAP) equals cardiac output times TPR, ignoring the small central venous pressure. Dynamic and static exercise differ because they change resistance in opposite directions."},{"type":"h","text":"Dynamic exercise"},{"type":"p","text":"In running, cycling, or swimming, cardiac output rises several-fold while TPR falls, so MAP rises only moderately. Systolic pressure rises progressively, commonly to about 160 to 200 mmHg at peak, because a larger stroke volume is ejected faster. Diastolic pressure stays about the same or falls slightly as blood runs off through dilated muscle beds, so pulse pressure widens. The heart does mainly volume work, and the pressure-volume loop widens more than it rises. Arm exercise at the same VO2 raises heart rate and pressure more than leg exercise, because less muscle dilates while the inactive leg beds stay constricted."},{"type":"h","text":"Static exercise"},{"type":"p","text":"A sustained isometric contraction raises intramuscular pressure and compresses the muscle's own vessels, reducing flow above a moderate fraction of maximal force and nearly stopping it at high force. Metabolites accumulate, and the metaboreflex together with central command drives a strong sympathetic pressor response, graded by relative force and duration, so even the forearm can raise pressure substantially. Cardiac output rises modestly through heart rate, stroke volume changes little, and TPR stays the same or rises, so systolic and diastolic pressure both rise sharply. A Valsalva maneuver during maximal lifting adds intrathoracic pressure to the aorta. This is pressure work: the loop becomes taller, and by the Laplace relationship (wall stress proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness) wall stress and O2 demand rise."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute responses to dynamic and static exercise","cols":["Variable","Dynamic (running, cycling)","Static (handgrip, heavy hold)"],"rows":[["Heart rate","Large rise with work rate","Moderate rise"],["Stroke volume","Rises","Little change or slight fall"],["Cardiac output","Large rise","Modest rise, mainly from heart rate"],["TPR","Falls","Unchanged or rises"],["Systolic pressure","Rises progressively","Rises sharply"],["Diastolic pressure","Unchanged or falls slightly","Rises sharply"],["Main pressor drive","Central command and mechanoreflex","Metaboreflex and central command"],["Load on left ventricle","Volume load","Pressure load"]],"caption":"Most real activities mix the two patterns; rowing, for example, adds a large static component to a volume load."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure work costs more O2 than volume work, and the rate-pressure product indexes myocardial O2 demand, so static effort can provoke ischemia at a lower heart rate than running. A fall in systolic pressure as dynamic workload rises is abnormal: cardiac output cannot offset the falling resistance, as in severe left ventricular dysfunction, extensive ischemia, or severe aortic stenosis. An exaggerated rise suggests resistance is not falling and may precede resting hypertension. Sustained handgrip raises afterload, so the murmurs of mitral regurgitation, aortic regurgitation, and ventricular septal defect get louder, while the murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy softens (section C05). After exercise, persisting vasodilation lowers pressure for an hour or more, and stopping abruptly removes the muscle pump and can cause lightheadedness."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out TPR from pressure and cardiac output","text":"Use MAP equals diastolic pressure plus one third of pulse pressure, and TPR equals MAP divided by cardiac output. At rest, 120/80 mmHg and 5 L/min give a MAP of about 93 mmHg and TPR of about 19 mmHg per L/min. Running, 180/76 mmHg and 20 L/min give a MAP of about 111 mmHg and TPR of about 5.5: cardiac output quadrupled while MAP rose only about 17 mmHg, because TPR fell to under one third. In sustained handgrip, 165/110 mmHg and 6.5 L/min give a MAP of about 128 mmHg and TPR of about 20: cardiac output rose 30%, yet MAP rose 35 mmHg because resistance did not fall. At high heart rates diastole shortens, so true running MAP lies nearer the systolic and diastolic midpoint, which still leaves TPR far below resting."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-12-01-C05","obj":"CV-12.5","title":"The trained endurance heart versus pathologic hypertrophy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adult myocytes rarely divide, so the heart adapts by enlarging them. A recurring volume load adds sarcomeres in series, enlarging the cavity with proportional wall thickening (eccentric hypertrophy). A sustained pressure load adds sarcomeres in parallel, thickening the wall relative to the cavity (concentric hypertrophy). Both bring wall stress back toward normal (Laplace relationship)."},{"type":"h","text":"The endurance-trained heart"},{"type":"p","text":"Endurance training remodels the left ventricle eccentrically, with mild symmetric wall thickening, a normal wall-to-cavity ratio, enlarged right ventricle and atria, and normal or enhanced diastolic filling. Plasma volume expands within days (renin-angiotensin-aldosterone, vasopressin, albumin) faster than red cell mass, so hemoglobin concentration may fall slightly by dilution, not anemia."},{"type":"p","text":"With resting cardiac output still about 5 L/min, a stroke volume of about 100 mL needs only about 50 bpm. The slowing comes mainly from vagal tone, plus a lower intrinsic SA node rate; vagal tone also explains benign sinus arrhythmia, first degree AV block, and Mobitz I block that disappear with exercise. At the same submaximal workload, the trained person reaches the same VO2 and cardiac output with a lower heart rate, a larger stroke volume, and less lactate; at maximum, cardiac output, extraction, and VO2max rise, but heart rate does not. Greater muscle capillary and mitochondrial density raise extraction and fat oxidation, and VO2max typically increases about 15% to 20% in previously sedentary people."},{"type":"flow","title":"From repeated volume load to higher VO2max","steps":["Repeated dynamic exercise imposes volume overload","Plasma volume and preload rise","Sarcomeres added in series enlarge the ventricle","Stroke volume rises at rest and at every workload","Same resting output needs fewer beats","Larger maximal stroke volume raises maximal output and VO2max"],"caption":"Bradycardia follows from the larger stroke volume."},{"type":"h","text":"Physiologic versus pathologic hypertrophy"},{"type":"p","text":"Physiologic hypertrophy is driven by insulin-like growth factor 1 through PI3K and Akt, with matching capillary growth and no fibrosis. Pathologic hypertrophy is driven by wall stress and neurohormones (angiotensin II, endothelin 1, and norepinephrine at alpha-1 receptors) through Gq-coupled receptors and calcineurin and NFAT signaling. It reactivates fetal genes (beta-myosin heavy chain, natriuretic peptides) and brings fibrosis, capillary insufficiency, and diastolic then systolic dysfunction."},{"type":"p","text":"HCM, the main mimic of athlete's heart, is usually autosomal dominant with variable penetrance, caused by sarcomere gene mutations, most often MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (cardiac myosin binding protein C). The stiff ventricle fills poorly, causing high filling pressures and exertional dyspnea; a large muscle mass with diseased intramural arterioles causes angina despite normal epicardial arteries; and disarray with fibrosis is a substrate for reentrant ventricular arrhythmias, a leading cause of sudden death in young athletes."},{"type":"p","text":"Many patients have dynamic outflow obstruction from the septum and systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, worsened by anything that makes the ventricle smaller or more forceful: lower preload (standing, Valsalva strain, dehydration), lower afterload, greater contractility, or tachycardia. The murmur therefore gets louder with standing or Valsalva and softer with squatting or handgrip."},{"type":"table","title":"Athlete's heart versus hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","cols":["Feature","Athlete's heart (endurance)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"rows":[["Wall thickness","Symmetric, usually 12 mm or less","Often asymmetric septal, 15 mm or more"],["Cavity","Enlarged (often above 55 mm)","Small or normal (often below 45 mm)"],["Diastolic function","Normal or enhanced","Impaired relaxation"],["Histology","Orderly myocytes, no fibrosis","Disarray, fibrosis"],["Outflow tract","No obstruction","Dynamic obstruction in many"],["ECG","Bradycardia, isolated high voltage","Deep T wave inversion, pathologic Q waves"],["Peak VO2","High","Normal or reduced"],["After about 3 months of detraining","Regresses","No regression"],["Family history","Usually negative","Often HCM or sudden death"]],"caption":"Thresholds are approximate; in the 13 to 15 mm gray zone the features are weighed together."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with obstructive HCM becomes dehydrated running in the heat. What happens to the outflow gradient?","explain":"Lower preload, lower afterload, and higher contractility and rate each make the ventricle smaller and more forceful, so obstruction worsens.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on dynamic outflow obstruction."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Structure sets up every exercise response. Venous valves and muscle-enclosed deep veins make the muscle pump work, compliant splanchnic and cutaneous veins supply the blood that venoconstriction moves centrally, and muscle capillary density sets the surface for O2 exchange. ATP hydrolysis in working muscle produces the adenosine, H+, and CO2 that dilate arterioles and shift the oxyhemoglobin curve, and glycolysis outpacing oxidation explains the lactate threshold. The kidney links to training through the plasma volume expansion driven by renin-angiotensin-aldosterone and vasopressin."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology appears wherever the response cannot be completed. A coronary stenosis or peripheral artery disease blocks the active hyperemia that working myocardium or calf muscle needs, causing angina or claudication. In heart failure, limited stroke volume reserve and exaggerated muscle reflexes lower peak VO2. HCM shows that the same muscle mass seen in an athlete is pathologic when it comes from a different stimulus and signaling pathway. The values in this chapter are typical for healthy young adults and vary with age, sex, body size, and heredity."}],"summary":["Central command starts the exercise response and resets the baroreflex upward; group III mechanoreceptors and group IV metaboreceptors adjust it through the exercise pressor reflex.","Heart rate rises first through vagal withdrawal (IKACh, within one or two beats); sympathetic effects through cAMP build over seconds and dominate at higher intensities.","VO2 equals cardiac output times the a-vO2 difference; from rest to maximum, cardiac output rises about four- to fivefold and extraction about threefold.","Stroke volume rises from greater filling and contractility and plateaus at about 40% to 60% of VO2max in untrained people; heart rate rises linearly.","Extraction rises through capillary recruitment, low tissue PO2, and a right shift of the oxyhemoglobin curve.","Metabolites and NO dilate active muscle and blunt sympathetic constriction, so TPR falls; gut and kidney flow fall, coronary flow rises, brain flow stays constant, and skin flow rises to shed heat.","Dynamic exercise lowers TPR, raises systolic pressure, leaves diastolic pressure about the same, and imposes a volume load.","Static exercise compresses muscle vessels, triggers the metaboreflex, raises systolic and diastolic pressure, and imposes a more O2-costly pressure load.","Endurance training produces eccentric remodeling and a larger blood volume, raising stroke volume and VO2max while resting cardiac output and maximal heart rate stay about the same.","HCM is an autosomal dominant sarcomere disease with asymmetric septal hypertrophy, a small stiff cavity, myocyte disarray, and dynamic outflow obstruction, and it does not regress with detraining."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Integrated control of the cardiovascular system; exercise physiology"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: cardiac hypertrophy and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-13":{"unit":"CV-13","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01","title":"Cardiovascular Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter links the biochemistry of lipids, fuels, vitamins, and minerals to the heart and blood vessels. It covers how lipoproteins move fat and cholesterol, how inherited defects produce recognizable lipid patterns, and how cells regulate their own cholesterol. It then treats the heart as a metabolic organ: which fuels it burns, how fatty acids reach its mitochondria, and how its energy supply fails in ischemia. The last sections explain how thiamine, B vitamins, vitamin C, and copper protect the heart and vessel wall, and how K+, Ca2+, and Mg2+ change the action potential and the ECG. Board questions usually ask what accumulates and what runs short when one step fails.","prerequisites":["Structure of triglycerides, phospholipids, cholesterol, and cholesteryl esters","Glycolysis, the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, the TCA cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation","Receptor-mediated endocytosis, endosomes, and lysosomes","Ventricular myocyte action potential and its ion currents (CV-5)","Normal ECG waves and intervals (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"ATP from cardiac fuel metabolism powers cross-bridge cycling and Ca2+ reuptake, so ischemic energy failure impairs both contraction and relaxation."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Electrolyte disorders act on the same K+, Na+, and Ca2+ currents that shape the T wave, QRS, and QT interval."},{"to":"Cardiovascular genetics (CV-14)","how":"Familial hypercholesterolemia and other inherited lipid disorders build on the LDL receptor pathway taught here."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"LDL retention and oxidation, homocysteine, and weak collagen or elastin explain atherosclerosis, thrombosis, and aneurysm."},{"to":"Congestive heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Wet beriberi is a classic cause of high-output heart failure with low systemic vascular resistance."}],"terms":[{"term":"Apolipoprotein","def":"A protein on a lipoprotein surface that provides structure, activates or inhibits enzymes, or binds a receptor."},{"term":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL)","def":"An enzyme on the luminal surface of capillary endothelium that hydrolyzes triglyceride in chylomicrons and VLDL to release fatty acids, and requires apo C-II as its activator."},{"term":"LCAT","def":"Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase, a plasma enzyme activated by apo A-I that esterifies free cholesterol on HDL so it moves into the particle core."},{"term":"HMG-CoA reductase","def":"The endoplasmic reticulum enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate, the rate-limiting and main regulated step of cholesterol synthesis."},{"term":"SREBP","def":"Sterol regulatory element-binding protein, a membrane-bound transcription factor released by proteolysis when cell sterol is low, which turns on genes for the LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase."},{"term":"Carnitine shuttle","def":"The system of carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, a translocase, and carnitine palmitoyltransferase II that carries long-chain fatty acyl groups into the mitochondrial matrix."},{"term":"Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)","def":"The active coenzyme form of vitamin B1, required for oxidative decarboxylation of alpha-keto acids and for transketolase."},{"term":"High-output heart failure","def":"Heart failure in which cardiac output is above normal but still inadequate, usually because systemic vascular resistance is low or metabolic demand is high."},{"term":"Homocysteine","def":"A sulfur amino acid formed from methionine after methyl donation, which is either remethylated to methionine or converted to cysteine through cystathionine."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"A copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that oxidizes lysine side chains in collagen and elastin so they can form covalent cross-links."},{"term":"U wave","def":"A small deflection after the T wave, in the same direction as the T wave, that becomes prominent when repolarization is slowed, classically in hypokalemia."},{"term":"Torsades de pointes","def":"A polymorphic ventricular tachycardia in the setting of a long QT interval, in which the QRS axis appears to twist around the baseline."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C01","obj":"CV-13.1","title":"Lipoprotein transport: exogenous, endogenous, and reverse pathways","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Triglyceride and cholesteryl ester are almost insoluble in water, so plasma carries them in lipoproteins: spheres with a core of triglyceride and cholesteryl ester and a surface of phospholipid, free cholesterol, and apolipoproteins. Apolipoproteins give each particle its identity; some are structural, some activate enzymes, and some are ligands for receptors. As triglyceride is removed, particles become smaller, denser, and richer in cholesterol, which is why density runs from chylomicrons (largest, lightest) through VLDL, IDL, and LDL to HDL (smallest, most protein)."},{"type":"table","title":"Lipoproteins and their apolipoproteins","cols":["Particle","Made by","Key apolipoproteins","Main role"],"rows":[["Chylomicron","Enterocytes","B-48 (needed for assembly, lacks the LDL receptor-binding region); C-II and E from HDL","Carries dietary triglyceride to muscle and adipose tissue; remnant returns to liver"],["VLDL","Liver","B-100; C-II and E from HDL","Carries liver-made triglyceride to tissues"],["IDL","VLDL in plasma","B-100 and E","Taken up by liver (about half) or converted to LDL"],["LDL","IDL in plasma","B-100 only (LDL receptor ligand)","Main plasma carrier of cholesterol to liver and peripheral cells"],["HDL","Liver and intestine (lipid-poor apo A-I)","A-I (activates LCAT, accepts cholesterol through ABCA1); lends C-II and E","Reverse cholesterol transport"]],"caption":"Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase; apo E mediates hepatic uptake of remnants and IDL. Apo B never leaves its particle, so each chylomicron carries one B-48 and each VLDL, IDL, and LDL one B-100."},{"type":"h","text":"Exogenous and endogenous pathways"},{"type":"p","text":"Enterocytes resynthesize absorbed fat into triglyceride, and microsomal triglyceride transfer protein loads it with cholesteryl ester and fat-soluble vitamins onto apo B-48. Apo B-48 comes from the same APOB gene as B-100: intestinal RNA editing changes one cytosine to uracil, creating a stop codon at about 48% of full length. Chylomicrons are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct, delivering dietary fat to peripheral tissues before the liver."},{"type":"flow","title":"Exogenous lipoprotein pathway","steps":["Enterocytes package dietary triglyceride with apo B-48 as chylomicrons","Chylomicrons travel through lacteals and the thoracic duct","HDL donates apo C-II and apo E","Apo C-II activates capillary lipoprotein lipase","Fatty acids enter adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and heart","Liver takes up the remnant through apo E"],"caption":"Loss of apo C-II or lipoprotein lipase stops the pathway at step 4, so chylomicrons accumulate. Normally chylomicrons are gone from plasma after a 12-hour fast."},{"type":"p","text":"Insulin raises lipoprotein lipase in adipose tissue after a meal, while the cardiac enzyme has a lower Km and keeps supplying the heart during fasting. The liver uses the same machinery with apo B-100 to secrete VLDL. Lipoprotein lipase converts VLDL to IDL; about half of IDL is taken up through apo E, and hepatic lipase converts the rest to LDL, which loses apo E and depends on apo B-100 binding the LDL receptor. LDL therefore stays in plasma for days, mostly cleared by the liver, with adrenal cortex and gonads taking it for steroid synthesis."},{"type":"h","text":"HDL and reverse cholesterol transport"},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral cells cannot degrade the sterol ring, so excess cholesterol must return to the liver for excretion in bile or as bile acids. Lipid-poor apo A-I collects free cholesterol and phospholipid from cells through ABCA1 (absent in Tangier disease, in which HDL is nearly absent). Apo A-I activates LCAT, which esterifies the cholesterol so it moves into the core, keeping the gradient that draws more out of cells. HDL delivers the ester to the liver through SR-B1, or CETP transfers it to apo B particles in exchange for triglyceride. When triglyceride-rich particles are abundant, CETP loads HDL with triglyceride that hepatic lipase then removes, producing small HDL that is cleared quickly; this is why high triglyceride usually comes with low HDL. LCAT works on HDL in plasma, whereas ACAT esterifies cholesterol inside cells for storage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C02","obj":"CV-13.2","title":"Familial dyslipidemias: defects and lipid patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A dyslipidemia is a blocked or overloaded step: a particle that cannot be broken down, cannot be taken up, or is overproduced accumulates. The Fredrickson types group primary disorders by the raised lipoprotein. High triglyceride points to chylomicrons or VLDL, and high cholesterol with normal triglyceride points to LDL. On standing overnight in the cold, chylomicrons float as a creamy top layer, VLDL leaves the plasma uniformly turbid, and LDL leaves it clear. Approximate desirable adult values are total cholesterol below 200 mg/dL, LDL below 100 mg/dL, HDL at least 40 mg/dL in men and 50 mg/dL in women, and fasting triglyceride below 150 mg/dL."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary dyslipidemias by Fredrickson type","cols":["Type","Defect","Raised particle and lipids","Consequences"],"rows":[["I (familial chylomicronemia), autosomal recessive","Lipoprotein lipase or apo C-II deficiency","Chylomicrons; triglyceride often above 1,000 mg/dL","Pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas, lipemia retinalis; little added atherosclerosis"],["IIa (familial hypercholesterolemia), autosomal dominant","Defective LDL receptor (also apo B-100 or gain-of-function PCSK9)","LDL; high cholesterol, normal triglyceride","Premature atherosclerosis, tendon xanthomas, xanthelasma, early corneal arcus"],["IIb (combined)","Hepatic overproduction of apo B particles, usually polygenic","LDL and VLDL; both lipids raised","Premature atherosclerosis"],["III (dysbetalipoproteinemia)","Apo E2 homozygosity plus a second metabolic factor","Remnants and IDL; both lipids raised to similar levels","Palmar and tuberoeruptive xanthomas; premature coronary and peripheral disease"],["IV (familial hypertriglyceridemia)","Hepatic VLDL overproduction","VLDL; triglyceride raised, cholesterol near normal","Pancreatitis if very high; linked to insulin resistance"],["V","VLDL overproduction with impaired clearance","VLDL and chylomicrons","Pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas"]],"caption":"Secondary causes such as diabetes, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, and alcohol must be excluded before a pattern is called familial."},{"type":"p","text":"In type I, pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes chylomicron triglyceride in pancreatic capillaries, releasing free fatty acids that injure acinar cells, which is the accepted explanation for pancreatitis. Chylomicrons are too large to enter the intima efficiently, so atherosclerosis is not markedly increased. In type IIa, hepatocytes with too few receptors behave as if cholesterol-poor and keep synthesizing cholesterol and secreting VLDL, adding still more LDL; tendon xanthomas on the Achilles and hand extensor tendons are characteristic. In type III, apo E2 binds hepatic receptors poorly; most homozygotes have near-normal lipids until obesity, diabetes, hypothyroidism, or alcohol adds a second load. In type IV, insulin resistance, alcohol, and estrogen increase hepatic triglyceride output, and the high triglyceride, low HDL, small dense LDL pattern is the dyslipidemia of the metabolic syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Classifying a dyslipidemia from the laboratory picture","text":"A 9-year-old with acute pancreatitis has fasting triglyceride of 2,800 mg/dL, cholesterol of 260 mg/dL, and a thick creamy layer over clear plasma after refrigeration. Step 1: triglyceride far exceeds cholesterol, so the excess particle is chylomicrons or VLDL. Step 2: the floating cream means chylomicrons, and the clear infranatant means VLDL is not greatly raised. Step 3: fasting chylomicrons mean failed clearance by lipoprotein lipase or apo C-II, so this is type I; the modest cholesterol rise is the cholesterol each chylomicron carries. Contrast an obese adult with triglyceride of 450 mg/dL and uniformly cloudy plasma (VLDL, type IV), and a patient with cholesterol of 360 mg/dL, triglyceride of 340 mg/dL, and yellow palmar creases (remnants, type III, apo E2)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C03","obj":"CV-13.3","title":"Cholesterol synthesis, SREBP, and LDL receptor uptake","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cells obtain cholesterol by synthesis from acetyl-CoA or by LDL uptake, and balance both against need, since excess free cholesterol is toxic to membranes. The same control system sets hepatic LDL receptor number and therefore plasma LDL."},{"type":"h","text":"Synthesis and control of HMG-CoA reductase"},{"type":"p","text":"In the cytosol, three acetyl-CoA form HMG-CoA through cytosolic HMG-CoA synthase (the mitochondrial isoform makes HMG-CoA for ketogenesis). HMG-CoA reductase, an ER membrane enzyme, reduces HMG-CoA to mevalonate with 2 NADPH; this is the rate-limiting, regulated step. Mevalonate becomes isoprene units, then farnesyl pyrophosphate, squalene, lanosterol, and finally cholesterol. Farnesyl pyrophosphate also supplies ubiquinone, dolichol, and protein lipid anchors, so blocking the reductase lowers these as well."},{"type":"p","text":"The reductase is controlled at four levels. SREBP-2 raises its transcription when sterol is low. Accumulating sterols make it bind Insig, which leads to ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. AMPK phosphorylates and inactivates it when energy is short. Insulin and thyroid hormone increase activity or expression, whereas glucagon and glucocorticoids decrease them."},{"type":"h","text":"SREBP: how the cell senses its own sterol"},{"type":"flow","title":"SREBP activation when cell sterol is low","steps":["ER membrane cholesterol falls","SCAP releases Insig and escorts SREBP-2 to the Golgi","Site-1 then site-2 protease cleave SREBP-2","The freed N-terminal transcription factor enters the nucleus","It binds sterol regulatory elements","LDL receptor and HMG-CoA reductase transcription rise","More LDL uptake and synthesis restore cell sterol"],"caption":"When sterol is high, cholesterol-bound SCAP binds Insig and the complex stays in the ER, so the genes are turned down. SREBP-1c mainly controls fatty acid synthesis genes in response to insulin."},{"type":"h","text":"LDL receptor-mediated endocytosis"},{"type":"p","text":"The LDL receptor binds apo B-100 and apo E. Its NPXY tail motif gathers it into clathrin-coated pits, which pinch off with the help of dynamin. In the acidified endosome LDL is released and the receptor recycles to the surface. LDL goes to the lysosome, where apo B-100 is degraded and acid lipase frees cholesterol, which leaves through NPC1 and NPC2 (trapped in Niemann-Pick type C). PCSK9, itself an SREBP target, diverts receptors to the lysosome and shortens their life."},{"type":"p","text":"The released cholesterol is the feedback signal: it suppresses SREBP processing, speeds reductase degradation, and activates ACAT to store the excess as ester. Macrophage scavenger receptors such as SR-A and CD36 take up oxidized LDL and are not downregulated, so macrophages fill with cholesteryl ester and become foam cells (CV-18-I). This system also explains why lowering liver cholesterol lowers plasma LDL: inhibiting hepatic HMG-CoA reductase releases SREBP-2, raises LDL receptor number, and increases clearance, which requires receptor genes that can respond. Hypothyroidism lowers receptor expression and is a common secondary cause of high LDL."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has two null LDL receptor alleles. Predict hepatic cholesterol synthesis, and whether an HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor would lower plasma LDL as much as in a person with normal receptors.","explain":"Synthesis stays high, because the liver receives little LDL cholesterol and SREBP-2 remains active. The inhibitor would lower LDL much less than usual, because its main plasma effect comes from SREBP-driven synthesis of new receptors, and this child has no functional receptor gene to turn on. Any effect would come from reduced VLDL output.","check":"Compare with the SREBP figure and the paragraph on how lowering liver cholesterol lowers plasma LDL."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C04","obj":"CV-13.4","title":"Fuels of the heart and energy failure in ischemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The heart stores only seconds of ATP and must remake it continuously, almost entirely by oxidative phosphorylation. Mitochondria fill about a third of a ventricular myocyte, and O2 extraction is already high at rest, so the heart meets greater demand mainly by raising coronary flow. At rest the adult heart obtains roughly 60 to 90% of its ATP from fatty acids, which arrive bound to albumin or are released by cardiac lipoprotein lipase, enter mainly through CD36, and are activated to fatty acyl-CoA in the cytosol."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardiac fuels","cols":["Fuel","Entry into oxidation","When its share rises"],"rows":[["Fatty acids","Carnitine shuttle, then beta-oxidation","Fasting, resting adult state, diabetes"],["Glucose","Glycolysis, then pyruvate dehydrogenase","High insulin after a carbohydrate meal; ischemia (anaerobic); fetal heart"],["Lactate","Lactate dehydrogenase to pyruvate","Exercise"],["Ketone bodies","Succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase to acetyl-CoA","Prolonged fasting, diabetic ketoacidosis"]],"caption":"The liver makes ketone bodies but cannot use them, because it lacks the transferase that heart, muscle, and brain use."},{"type":"h","text":"The carnitine shuttle and fuel choice"},{"type":"p","text":"Long-chain acyl-CoA cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane. CPT-I on the outer membrane forms acylcarnitine, a translocase moves it into the matrix in exchange for carnitine, and CPT-II re-forms acyl-CoA for beta-oxidation; short- and medium-chain fatty acids need no carnitine. Carnitine or CPT-II defects can cause cardiomyopathy. CPT-I is inhibited by malonyl-CoA, the product of acetyl-CoA carboxylase, so high glucose and insulin raise malonyl-CoA and reduce fatty acid entry, while AMPK inhibits the carboxylase and increases it. In the reverse direction, active fatty acid oxidation raises acetyl-CoA and NADH, which inactivate pyruvate dehydrogenase through its kinase, and raises citrate, which inhibits phosphofructokinase-1 (the glucose-fatty acid cycle). Creatine kinase carries high-energy phosphate from mitochondria to myofibrils as phosphocreatine, which also buffers ATP for the first seconds of a shortfall."},{"type":"example","title":"Why fatty acids cost more oxygen per ATP","text":"Use 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 per FADH2. Glucose consumes 6 O2 (12 oxygen atoms) and yields about 31 ATP, or about 2.6 ATP per oxygen atom. Palmitate gives 8 acetyl-CoA (80 ATP), 7 NADH (17.5), and 7 FADH2 (10.5), totaling 108, minus 2 for activation, so 106 ATP. It consumes 23 O2 (46 oxygen atoms), about 2.3 ATP per oxygen atom. Glucose therefore yields roughly 10% more ATP for the same O2, although palmitate yields over three times as much per molecule. Fatty acids suit a well-oxygenated heart, but when O2 is limited, shifting toward glucose oxidation gets more work from the same coronary flow."},{"type":"h","text":"Energy failure in ischemia"},{"type":"flow","title":"From coronary occlusion to energy failure","steps":["Flow stops; myocardial O2 is exhausted within seconds","Oxidative phosphorylation, TCA cycle, and beta-oxidation halt","Phosphocreatine, then ATP fall; AMP and phosphate rise","Glycogenolysis and anaerobic glycolysis make lactate","Lactate and H+ accumulate; acidosis inhibits phosphofructokinase-1","Phosphate and H+ weaken cross-bridges; contraction fails within about a minute","Pump failure causes K+ loss and Ca2+ overload","Injury becomes irreversible after about 20 to 40 minutes"],"caption":"Approximate times for severe ischemia follow Robbins. Early loss of contraction is reversible and spares the remaining ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Anaerobic glycolysis gives only 2 ATP per glucose (3 per glucose unit from glycogen), and lactate dehydrogenase regenerates the NAD+ it needs. The normal heart takes up lactate, so lactate release into coronary sinus blood is a marker of ischemia. As ATP falls, the Na+/K+-ATPase slows, K+ leaks out through ATP-sensitive channels, and Na+ gain reduces Ca2+ extrusion by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, while Na+/H+ exchange adds more Na+. Cytosolic Ca2+ rises, SERCA failure impairs relaxation, and irreversible membrane damage releases troponin. Raised local extracellular K+ depolarizes ischemic cells, linking energy failure to conduction slowing and arrhythmia (section C07)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C05","obj":"CV-13.5","title":"Thiamine pyrophosphate and wet beriberi","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine (B1) is absorbed mainly in the jejunum and converted by thiamine pyrophosphokinase to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP). Stores are small and turnover is fast, so deficiency can develop within weeks, first affecting tissues with high aerobic glucose use, the nervous system and the heart. The thiazolium carbanion of TPP attacks the carbonyl carbon of an alpha-keto acid, releasing CO2 and carrying the acyl fragment, which is why TPP is needed for oxidative decarboxylation and for transketolase."},{"type":"table","title":"Enzymes that require thiamine pyrophosphate","cols":["Enzyme","Reaction","Effect of deficiency"],"rows":[["Pyruvate dehydrogenase (E1)","Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","Pyruvate accumulates and becomes lactate"],["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (E1)","Alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA","TCA cycle slows, cutting NADH and ATP from every fuel"],["Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase","Catabolism of leucine, isoleucine, valine","Keto acids accumulate (minor in acquired deficiency)"],["Transketolase","Two-carbon transfer in the pentose phosphate pathway","Low red cell activity, used as a test"]],"caption":"The dehydrogenase complexes also need lipoic acid, CoA (B5), FAD (B2), and NAD+ (B3). A large rise in red cell transketolase activity when TPP is added indicates deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"The block produces lactic acidosis even with normal oxygenation: cells have O2 but cannot feed enough fuel into the TCA cycle to use it. Deficiency follows alcohol use disorder (poor intake, absorption, storage, and conversion to TPP), polished rice diets, persistent vomiting or bariatric surgery, and long diuretic use. A glucose load given to someone with marginal stores increases flux through TPP enzymes and can unmask it. Dry beriberi is a peripheral neuropathy, and Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome are the central forms."},{"type":"h","text":"Wet beriberi: high-output heart failure"},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of wet beriberi","steps":["Low TPP blocks pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Aerobic ATP falls; pyruvate and lactate accumulate","Metabolic vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance","Venous return and cardiac output rise","Sympathetic and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone activation retain Na+ and water","The ATP-starved myocardium dilates and fails"],"caption":"Low resistance and a failing myocardium come from the same metabolic block. The exact vasodilator mediators are not fully established."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding follows from the mechanism. Low resistance with a high stroke volume gives warm extremities, a bounding pulse, and a wide pulse pressure; sympathetic drive gives tachycardia; salt and water retention gives raised jugular venous pressure, edema, and sometimes pulmonary congestion; the heart is enlarged on radiograph; and lactate is raised. Shoshin beriberi is a fulminant form with severe lactic acidosis and circulatory collapse. Other high-output states have different clues: severe anemia (low hemoglobin), thyrotoxicosis (low TSH, high free T4 or T3), and arteriovenous fistula (continuous bruit, heart rate falls on compression), whereas ordinary low-output failure gives cool extremities and a narrow pulse pressure (CV-18-B)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A malnourished man with alcohol use disorder has warm hands, heart rate 115 bpm, wide pulse pressure, leg edema, and lactate 6 mmol/L (normal below about 2) with normal O2 saturation. Why is lactate high despite normal oxygenation?","explain":"Without TPP, pyruvate dehydrogenase cannot convert pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, so pyruvate accumulates and is reduced to lactate. The problem is fuel entry into the TCA cycle, not O2 delivery. The same block drives the metabolic vasodilation behind his warm skin and wide pulse pressure.","check":"Compare with the TPP enzyme table and the wet beriberi figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C06","obj":"CV-13.6","title":"Homocysteine, vitamin C, and copper in vascular health","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Micronutrients protect vessels in two ways. Folate, B12, and B6 keep plasma homocysteine low, and their lack injures the endothelium and favors thrombosis. Vitamin C and copper are cofactors for enzymes that build collagen and elastin, and their lack weakens the wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Homocysteine metabolism"},{"type":"cycle","title":"The methionine cycle and the exits for homocysteine","steps":["Methionine is activated by ATP to S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","SAM donates a methyl group, becoming S-adenosylhomocysteine","Hydrolysis yields homocysteine","Methionine synthase (B12, N5-methyltetrahydrofolate) remethylates it to methionine","Or cystathionine beta-synthase (B6) commits it to cysteine"],"caption":"Homocysteine leaves only through these enzymes, so loss of B12, folate, B6, or the enzymes makes it accumulate."},{"type":"p","text":"Methionine synthase uses B12 as methylcobalamin and regenerates tetrahydrofolate; its methyl donor is made by MTHFR, and the liver also remethylates through betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase. Transsulfuration through cystathionine beta-synthase and cystathionine gamma-lyase, both pyridoxal phosphate enzymes, removes homocysteine permanently. In B12 deficiency folate is trapped as N5-methyltetrahydrofolate (the methylfolate trap), so both deficiencies cause megaloblastic anemia, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is also the cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of raised homocysteine","cols":["Cause","Step affected","Methylmalonic acid","Other clues"],"rows":[["Folate deficiency","Remethylation","Normal","Megaloblastic anemia"],["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Remethylation","Raised","Megaloblastic anemia; neurologic signs"],["Vitamin B6 deficiency","Transsulfuration","Normal","Sideroblastic anemia, neuropathy; isoniazid"],["Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency (homocystinuria)","Transsulfuration","Normal","Autosomal recessive; homocysteine often above 100 µmol/L, raised methionine, downward lens dislocation, marfanoid habitus, early thrombosis"],["MTHFR C677T variant","Methyl donor formation","Normal","Mild rise, mainly with low folate"],["Chronic kidney disease","Reduced clearance","May be raised","Low glomerular filtration rate"]],"caption":"Normal fasting total homocysteine is about 5 to 15 µmol/L."},{"type":"p","text":"Homocysteine oxidizes readily, generating reactive oxygen species that injure endothelium and reduce nitric oxide. It promotes smooth muscle proliferation and favors thrombosis, for example by raising tissue factor and impairing the thrombomodulin and protein C pathway. Homocystinuria shows the extreme, with arterial and venous thrombosis in the young. Whether a mild elevation causes atherosclerosis or only accompanies it is unsettled, so a mild elevation is best read as a risk marker."},{"type":"h","text":"Vitamin C, copper, and the vessel wall"},{"type":"p","text":"In the rough ER, prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases hydroxylate collagen chains; their iron must be kept as Fe2+ by ascorbate. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix at 37 °C (98.6 °F), and hydroxylysine provides glycosylation and cross-link sites. Without vitamin C, collagen in capillary basement membranes and perivascular tissue is weak, giving the bleeding of scurvy: perifollicular hemorrhages, bleeding gums, hemarthrosis, subperiosteal bleeding, poor wound healing, and corkscrew hairs. Stores last about 1 to 3 months."},{"type":"p","text":"After secretion, copper-dependent lysyl oxidase oxidizes lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes that form covalent cross-links, including desmosine in elastin. Without copper the aorta and large arteries lose strength and recoil and become tortuous and aneurysmal. In Menkes disease (X-linked recessive, ATP7A), copper cannot leave enterocytes, so serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low; boys have fragile tortuous arteries, kinky hair, hypopigmentation, hypotonia, and neurodegeneration. Acquired deficiency follows bariatric surgery or excess zinc. Wilson disease (ATP7B) is the opposite, with copper retained in liver and brain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-13-01-C07","obj":"CV-13.7","title":"Electrolytes and the ECG: potassium, calcium, and magnesium","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Electrolytes change the ECG by changing action potential currents, so ask which phase is affected and where it appears on the tracing. Approximate adult ranges are K+ 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L (hemolysis falsely raises it), total calcium 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (ionized about 4.5 to 5.3 mg/dL; add 0.8 mg/dL for each 1 g/dL that albumin is below 4.0 g/dL), and Mg2+ about 1.7 to 2.2 mg/dL (plasma can be normal despite cellular depletion)."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ventricular-ap","caption":"Phase 4 rest near -90 mV (K+), phase 0 Na+ upstroke (QRS), phase 2 Ca2+ plateau (ST segment), phase 3 K+ repolarization (T wave).","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-13-6.png"},{"type":"h","text":"Potassium"},{"type":"p","text":"The resting potential lies near the K+ equilibrium potential. In hyperkalemia, the first change is in repolarization: higher extracellular K+ increases IKr and inward rectifier conductance, so phase 3 is faster and T waves become tall, narrow, and peaked. As K+ rises further, the resting membrane depolarizes and inactivates a growing fraction of fast Na+ channels, slowing phase 0 and conduction, so the P wave flattens, PR lengthens, the QRS widens, and finally a sine-wave pattern can lead to ventricular fibrillation or asystole. The K+ level for each change varies widely, and a normal ECG does not exclude danger."},{"type":"p","text":"In hypokalemia, low extracellular K+ reduces IK1 and IKr conductance, so phase 3 slows despite a more negative equilibrium potential. T waves flatten or invert, the ST segment may sag, and prominent U waves appear, often merging into a long QU interval, mainly below about 3 mEq/L. Long repolarization allows early afterdepolarizations and torsades, and reduced Na+/K+-ATPase activity raises cell Ca2+, favoring delayed afterdepolarizations. Low K+ also increases cardiac glycoside toxicity, because K+ competes with these drugs at the pump."},{"type":"h","text":"Calcium and magnesium"},{"type":"p","text":"Extracellular Ca2+ sets the length of phase 2. In hypocalcemia, slower Ca2+-dependent inactivation of L-type channels lengthens the plateau, so the ST segment and QT are long with a normal T wave; in hypercalcemia both are short. Ca2+ also raises the threshold of excitable membranes, so low ionized Ca2+ causes tingling and tetany (Chvostek and Trousseau signs), and alkalosis can cause tetany with normal total calcium by increasing albumin binding."},{"type":"p","text":"Mg2+ is needed for Na+/K+-ATPase function and normally blocks K+ exit through renal ROMK channels, so hypomagnesemia causes K+ loss and hypokalemia that persists until Mg2+ is restored. Severe deficiency also impairs parathyroid hormone release and action, causing hypocalcemia. Torsades de pointes, a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia twisting around the baseline, arises when a long QT lets early afterdepolarizations, driven by L-type Ca2+ channel reactivation, reach threshold, often after a short-long-short sequence. Low Mg2+ promotes it through low K+ and Ca2+ and by removing its limit on inward Ca2+ current. Causes include alcohol use disorder, diarrhea, loop and thiazide diuretics, and long-term proton pump inhibitors."},{"type":"table","title":"Electrolyte disorders and their ECG signatures","cols":["Disorder","Action potential change","ECG findings","Main rhythm risk"],"rows":[["Hyperkalemia","Faster phase 3; depolarized rest with Na+ channel inactivation","Peaked T, then flat P, long PR, wide QRS, sine wave","Ventricular fibrillation, asystole"],["Hypokalemia","Slower phase 3","Flat or inverted T, ST depression, U waves, long QU","Torsades, tachyarrhythmias; glycoside toxicity"],["Hypocalcemia","Longer phase 2","Long ST and QT, normal T","Torsades (uncommon); tetany"],["Hypercalcemia","Shorter phase 2","Short ST and QT","Bradyarrhythmia, AV block at high levels"],["Hypomagnesemia","Long repolarization, mostly through low K+ and Ca2+","Long QT, often with hypokalemic features","Torsades de pointes"]],"caption":"Kidney failure often combines hyperkalemia and hypocalcemia, giving a long ST segment followed by a peaked T wave."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Plasma K+ rises gradually from 4.5 to 8.5 mEq/L. Why do T waves peak before the QRS widens?","explain":"Repolarizing K+ currents respond first: higher extracellular K+ increases their conductance and speeds phase 3 at moderate levels. QRS widening needs enough resting depolarization to inactivate many fast Na+ channels, which happens only at higher K+, slowing phase 0 and conduction.","check":"Compare with the potassium paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One rule unifies the chapter: when a step fails, the substrate before it accumulates and the products after it run short. Missing lipoprotein lipase leaves chylomicrons, missing LDL receptors leave LDL and unchecked hepatic synthesis, missing TPP leaves pyruvate and lactate, missing B12, folate, or B6 leaves homocysteine, and missing O2 leaves NADH, lactate, and H+. The same logic reaches the membrane, where a change in extracellular K+ or Ca2+ alters one current and one part of the ECG. Anatomy places these events: chylomicrons bypass the liver through the thoracic duct, the intima retains LDL and remnants but largely excludes chylomicrons, and vessel strength depends on collagen and elastin."},{"type":"p","text":"Inherited defects such as familial hypercholesterolemia, apo E2 homozygosity, homocystinuria, and Menkes disease show each pathway in extreme form, while acquired states often combine several; alcohol use disorder can bring thiamine and folate deficiency, hypomagnesemia, hypokalemia, and hypertriglyceridemia together. Limits matter: thresholds for ECG changes vary between patients, a mildly raised homocysteine marks risk without proven causation, and Fredrickson types describe patterns rather than single diseases."}],"summary":["Chylomicrons (apo B-48) carry dietary triglyceride through the lymph; VLDL (apo B-100) carries liver-made triglyceride.","Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase, apo E mediates hepatic uptake of remnants and IDL, and hepatic lipase converts IDL to LDL, which is cleared through apo B-100 and the LDL receptor.","HDL (apo A-I) collects cell cholesterol through ABCA1, LCAT esterifies it, and SR-B1 or CETP returns it to the liver.","Type I (lipoprotein lipase or apo C-II) raises chylomicrons and causes pancreatitis; type IIa (LDL receptor) raises LDL with tendon xanthomas and early atherosclerosis; type III (apo E2) raises remnants with palmar xanthomas; type IV raises VLDL.","HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis; low ER sterol releases SREBP-2, which raises reductase and LDL receptor expression.","The LDL receptor recycles from the acidic endosome while LDL is degraded in the lysosome; unregulated scavenger receptors turn macrophages into foam cells.","The resting heart burns mainly fatty acids through the carnitine shuttle (CPT-I inhibited by malonyl-CoA), which yield about 10% less ATP per O2 than glucose.","In ischemia, anaerobic glycolysis makes lactate and H+, contraction fails within about a minute, and injury becomes irreversible after about 20 to 40 minutes.","TPP deficiency blocks pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, causing lactic acidosis and wet beriberi; folate, B12, or B6 deficiency raises homocysteine, and only B12 also raises methylmalonic acid; vitamin C and copper are needed for collagen and elastin.","Hyperkalemia peaks T waves then widens the QRS, hypokalemia flattens T waves and adds U waves, calcium sets ST and QT length, and low Mg2+ predisposes to torsades de pointes."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fatty Acid, Triacylglycerol, and Ketone Body Metabolism; Vitamins"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Lipid Metabolism; Fundamentals of Electrocardiography"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac electrophysiology and cardiac energy metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (atherosclerosis); The Heart (myocardial infarction); Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular disorders and disorders of electrolyte balance"}]},"CV-14":{"unit":"CV-14","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01","title":"Cardiovascular Genetics","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how single-gene variants and chromosomal changes produce cardiovascular disease, and why one variant can look different from one relative to the next. It covers familial hypercholesterolemia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, the heritable aortic disorders, the long QT and Brugada channelopathies, chromosomal and developmental syndromes with characteristic heart defects, neuromuscular and mitochondrial disorders of the myocardium, and the cell biology that makes adult heart muscle grow by hypertrophy and heal by scar. Board items usually describe a family, an ECG, or a set of physical findings and ask for the inheritance pattern, the protein, and the mechanism that links them. A few rules about protein dose, dominant negative effects, penetrance, and energy supply let you reason through unfamiliar variants.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance patterns, pedigree analysis, and X inactivation","Cholesterol synthesis, lipoprotein metabolism, and receptor-mediated endocytosis (CV-13)","Sarcomere structure and the cross-bridge cycle (CV-5)","Phases of the ventricular action potential and the ion currents behind them (CV-5, CV-7)","Development of the endocardial cushions and outflow tract (CV-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiovascular biochemistry and nutrition (CV-13)","how":"The LDL receptor pathway and SREBP feedback taught there are the normal processes that familial hypercholesterolemia disrupts."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"The early afterdepolarization mechanism of torsades de pointes explained in CV-7 is the final common pathway for every congenital long QT genotype."},{"to":"Cardiovascular embryology (CV-1) and congenital heart conditions (CV-18-G)","how":"Each chromosomal syndrome damages a specific embryonic structure, such as the endocardial cushions or the neural crest contribution to the outflow tract, which predicts its typical defect."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E)","how":"Hypertrophic, dilated, and mitochondrial cardiomyopathies have their genetic basis here and their pathology and hemodynamics there."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"Aortic aneurysm and dissection in Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndromes are the genetic counterparts of medial degeneration in acquired aortic disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Penetrance","def":"The proportion of people carrying a pathogenic genotype who show any sign of the disease."},{"term":"Expressivity","def":"The range of severity and features among people who do show the disease phenotype."},{"term":"Locus heterogeneity","def":"The same clinical disorder arising from variants in different genes, as familial hypercholesterolemia does from LDLR, APOB, or PCSK9."},{"term":"Allelic heterogeneity","def":"Different variants in one gene producing different disorders or severities, as SCN5A gain and loss of function do."},{"term":"Pleiotropy","def":"One gene affecting several apparently unrelated organs, as FBN1 affects the aorta, the lens, and the skeleton."},{"term":"Haploinsufficiency","def":"Disease that results because one normal allele cannot make enough protein to maintain normal function."},{"term":"Dominant negative effect","def":"Disease that results because a mutant protein combines with or interferes with the normal protein made from the other allele."},{"term":"Gene dosage effect","def":"A graded relation between the number of affected alleles and severity, so homozygotes are more severely affected than heterozygotes."},{"term":"Heteroplasmy","def":"The presence of both mutant and normal mitochondrial DNA within one cell or tissue, in proportions that can differ between tissues."},{"term":"Myocyte disarray","def":"Loss of the normal parallel alignment of cardiac myocytes and myofibrils, which instead lie in disorganized whorls; characteristic of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"term":"Fetal gene program","def":"Genes normally active in the fetal heart, such as ANP, BNP, and beta-myosin heavy chain, that are switched back on in pathologically stressed adult myocardium."},{"term":"Permanent tissue","def":"Tissue whose cells have largely left the cell cycle, so lost cells are replaced by scar rather than by new parenchymal cells."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C01","obj":"CV-14.1","title":"Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDL receptor genetics, gene dosage, and lost feedback","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each low-density lipoprotein (LDL) particle carries one apolipoprotein B-100 (apoB-100), the ligand for the LDL receptor. The liver clears about 70% of plasma LDL, so hepatic receptor number sets plasma LDL. The receptor also binds apolipoprotein E on intermediate-density lipoprotein (IDL), so when receptors are scarce, more IDL survives to become LDL and production rises as clearance falls. LDL is internalized in clathrin-coated pits, released in the acidified endosome while the receptor recycles, and hydrolyzed in the lysosome to free cholesterol."},{"type":"p","text":"Low cholesterol in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) lets the escort protein SCAP carry SREBP-2 to the Golgi, where proteases release its active fragment. This switches on the genes for HMG-CoA reductase (the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis), the LDL receptor, and PCSK9; high ER cholesterol keeps SREBP-2 anchored by INSIG. PCSK9, secreted by hepatocytes, binds the receptor and sends it to the lysosome instead of recycling it, so PCSK9 activity sets how many trips each receptor makes."},{"type":"p","text":"Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is autosomal dominant, with heterozygotes about 1 in 250. It shows locus heterogeneity: loss-of-function LDLR variants (chromosome 19p, most cases), APOB variants in the receptor-binding region (familial defective apoB-100, usually milder because apoE-mediated IDL clearance is preserved), and gain-of-function PCSK9 variants (rarest). LDLR variants fall into five classes by the step that fails: synthesis, ER to Golgi transport (most common), ligand binding, clustering in coated pits, and recycling."},{"type":"p","text":"FH shows a gene dosage effect. Heterozygotes have LDL cholesterol about twice normal from birth, usually above about 190 mg/dL (4.9 mmol/L) in untreated adults against a desirable value below about 100 mg/dL (2.6 mmol/L), with normal triglycerides (Fredrickson type IIa). Homozygotes and compound heterozygotes, about 1 in several hundred thousand, are far more severe."},{"type":"table","title":"Heterozygous versus homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","cols":["Feature","Heterozygous FH","Homozygous FH"],"rows":[["Functional LDL receptors","About half of normal","Little or none"],["Total cholesterol","About 2 to 3 times normal","About 5 to 6 times normal"],["Xanthomas","Tendon (Achilles, hand extensors) in adulthood; corneal arcus before about 45","Cutaneous and tendon, often in the first decade; aortic root deposits"],["Coronary disease","Premature, often by the fourth or fifth decade in men","Childhood or adolescence, often before age 20"]],"caption":"Severity tracks the number of working receptor alleles."},{"type":"p","text":"The hepatocyte is short of cholesterol while plasma is overloaded. Little LDL reaches the ER, so SREBP-2 stays active and synthesis continues, feeding VLDL secretion and future LDL. FH fibroblasts ignore LDL in the medium yet suppress synthesis normally when cholesterol enters by a route that bypasses the receptor, which proves that uptake, not feedback, is defective. For the same reason, lowering hepatic synthesis upregulates the normal allele in a heterozygote but does little in a receptor-negative homozygote. Macrophages take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors that are not downregulated, forming the foam cells of xanthomas and plaques (CV-18-C). Loss-of-function PCSK9 variants, the mirror image, give lifelong low LDL and markedly lower coronary risk."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of familial hypercholesterolemia","steps":["LDLR, APOB, or gain-of-function PCSK9 variant","Fewer working receptor trips at the hepatocyte surface","Less LDL and IDL cleared; more IDL becomes LDL","Low ER cholesterol keeps SREBP-2 active and synthesis high","Plasma LDL high from birth","Foam cells: xanthomas, arcus, premature atherosclerosis"],"caption":"Reduced clearance plus continued production keeps LDL high for life, so coronary disease appears decades early."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C02","obj":"CV-14.2","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: sarcomere genes, myocyte disarray, and variable penetrance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is left ventricular thickening not explained by abnormal loading such as hypertension or aortic stenosis. It affects about 1 in 500 adults and in most familial cases is an autosomal dominant disease of the sarcomere. MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) and MYBPC3 (cardiac myosin-binding protein C) account for most genotype-positive cases; troponin T and I, alpha-tropomyosin, the myosin light chains, and cardiac actin are less common. Testing finds a variant in only about a third to a half of patients, so a negative test does not exclude a genetic cause."},{"type":"p","text":"MYH7 missense variants make a mutant myosin head that is built into the thick filament beside normal myosin, a dominant negative effect. MYBPC3 variants are usually truncating, and nonsense-mediated decay lowers the amount of protein (haploinsufficiency), with later and milder disease on average. Many variants release myosin heads from their energy-saving folded resting state, and thin filament variants raise Ca2+ sensitivity, so the heart is hypercontractile, wastes ATP, and relaxes poorly."},{"type":"flow","title":"From sarcomere variant to the HCM phenotype","steps":["MYH7 dominant negative or MYBPC3 haploinsufficient variant","More heads cycling or higher Ca2+ sensitivity","Hypercontractility, high ATP use, impaired relaxation","Energy deficit activates hypertrophic and fibrotic signaling (C07)","Hypertrophy, myocyte disarray, fibrosis, small vessel disease","Stiff, nondilated ventricle with an arrhythmic substrate"],"caption":"Hypertrophy follows from a faulty motor, so it appears even though load is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The ventricle is thick but not dilated, often with asymmetric septal hypertrophy and a small cavity; adult wall thickness of about 15 mm or more is diagnostic, against about 11 mm or less normally. The hallmark is myocyte disarray, with short branched myocytes in whorls, plus interstitial fibrosis and thick-walled intramural arteries. Disarray and patchy fibrosis slow and scatter conduction, supporting reentry."},{"type":"p","text":"The stiff ventricle fills at high pressure, causing exertional dyspnea and a fourth heart sound, and it depends on atrial contraction, so atrial fibrillation is poorly tolerated. About a third of patients have dynamic outflow obstruction at rest: the septum narrows the outflow tract and flow drags the anterior mitral leaflet toward it (systolic anterior motion), adding mitral regurgitation. Smaller ventricular volume (standing, Valsalva strain) worsens obstruction and the murmur; larger volume or afterload (squatting, leg raising, handgrip) softens it. The aortic stenosis murmur responds oppositely to volume changes. Angina occurs with normal coronaries because demand is high and intramural vessels are narrow. Sudden death from ventricular arrhythmia can be the first event, especially in young athletes. The ECG shows high voltage and deep narrow Q waves from the thick septum."},{"type":"p","text":"Each child of a carrier has a 50% risk, but relatives with one variant range from sudden death to a normal echocardiogram (variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance). Penetrance is age dependent, with hypertrophy usually appearing in adolescence or early adulthood and, with MYBPC3, sometimes not until middle age, so genotype-positive relatives need repeated evaluation. A second sarcomere variant raises severity, and male sex, hypertension, and modifier genes raise penetrance. Phenocopies include athlete's heart (enlarged cavity, normal relaxation, regression with detraining), hypertensive hypertrophy, Fabry disease (X-linked GLA), Friedreich ataxia, and Noonan syndrome."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict what happens to the systolic murmur of obstructive HCM when a patient moves from squatting to standing, and explain why.","explain":"The murmur becomes louder. Standing pools blood in the legs, reducing venous return and left ventricular volume. A smaller cavity brings the septum and the anterior mitral leaflet closer during systole, so obstruction and turbulence increase.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on dynamic outflow obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C03","obj":"CV-14.3","title":"Heritable aortic disease: Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The aortic media is built from elastic lamellae with smooth muscle between them, held by collagen and microfibrils. All three disorders here are autosomal dominant, but each damages a different component, which predicts its presentation."},{"type":"p","text":"Marfan syndrome (about 1 in 5,000, about a quarter de novo) is caused by FBN1 variants on chromosome 15 (15q21). Fibrillin-1 forms microfibrils that scaffold elastin and anchor the lens zonule. Missense variants act as dominant negatives and truncating variants by haploinsufficiency. Microfibrils also sequester latent TGF-beta, so fibrillin deficiency increases TGF-beta signaling, matrix metalloproteinase activity, and matrix loss. The lesion is medial degeneration (elastic fiber fragmentation, smooth muscle loss, proteoglycan pools; the older term cystic medial necrosis is misleading), worst at the aortic root. By Laplace's law, wall tension rises with radius, so dilation feeds further dilation. The stretched annulus causes aortic regurgitation, and myxomatous mitral valves prolapse. Pleiotropy explains upward ectopia lentis, tall stature, arachnodactyly, pectus, scoliosis, and joint laxity. Homocystinuria mimics the habitus but displaces the lens downward and causes thrombosis and intellectual disability."},{"type":"flow","title":"Marfan syndrome from gene to aortic dissection","steps":["FBN1 variant: abnormal or reduced fibrillin-1","Weak microfibrils and excess TGF-beta signaling","Medial degeneration of the aortic wall","Root dilates at the sinuses of Valsalva","Larger radius raises wall tension (Laplace)","Aortic regurgitation, then dissection or rupture"],"caption":"Ascending aortic dissection is the classic cause of death in untreated Marfan syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Loeys-Dietz syndrome is usually caused by TGFBR1 or TGFBR2 variants (also SMAD3, TGFB2, TGFB3). The variants reduce receptor function in cells, yet aortic TGF-beta signaling is paradoxically increased. Aneurysms and dissections occur younger, at smaller diameters, and throughout tortuous arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is caused by COL3A1 variants affecting type III collagen, a homotrimer abundant in arteries, bowel, uterus, and skin. Most variants replace a glycine in the Gly-X-Y repeat, which only glycine fits, so the helix misfolds. Medium-sized arteries rupture or dissect without prior aneurysm, and the sigmoid colon and gravid uterus can rupture. Skin is thin and translucent but not markedly hyperextensible, and median survival is about 50 years."},{"type":"table","title":"Heritable disorders of the arterial wall","cols":["Feature","Marfan","Loeys-Dietz","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos"],"rows":[["Gene and protein","FBN1, fibrillin-1","TGFBR1 or TGFBR2","COL3A1, type III collagen"],["Arterial pattern","Aortic root dilation, ascending dissection","Widespread aneurysms, tortuosity, younger and smaller","Medium artery rupture without aneurysm"],["Extravascular clues","Upward ectopia lentis, tall, arachnodactyly","Hypertelorism, bifid uvula or cleft palate, craniosynostosis; no ectopia lentis","Thin skin, bruising, colon and uterine rupture"]],"caption":"All three are autosomal dominant; the failing wall component predicts the pattern."},{"type":"example","title":"Why one bad allele spoils most multimers","text":"If mutant and normal chains are made equally and combine at random, a type III collagen trimer is fully normal with probability (1/2)^3 = 1/8, so a glycine substitution leaves about 12.5% normal molecules. A null allele makes half as many chains, all normal, so about 50% of the usual collagen is normal. The same reasoning applies to the tetrameric KCNQ1 channel: (1/2)^4 = 1/16, about 6% working channels with a dominant negative subunit, against about 50% with a null allele. This is why dominant negative missense variants are often more severe than null alleles, and why null COL3A1 alleles cause later, milder disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C04","obj":"CV-14.4","title":"Inherited channelopathies: congenital long QT syndrome and Brugada syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Channelopathies cause sudden death in a structurally normal heart, so diagnosis rests on the ECG, family history, and genetic testing. Phase 3 repolarization depends on the rapid delayed rectifier current IKr (KCNH2, also called hERG) and the slow current IKs (KCNQ1 with KCNE1), finished by IK1. Shared work gives repolarization reserve: losing part of one current may barely lengthen the resting QT but leaves the cell vulnerable to hypokalemia, bradycardia, a sympathetic surge, or an IKr-blocking drug, which explains much incomplete penetrance."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ventricular-ap","caption":"Ventricular action potential, phases 0 to 4. Less outward K+ current or a late inward Na+ current prolongs phases 2 and 3 and the QT. In Brugada syndrome, reduced phase 0 Na+ current lets Ito deepen the phase 1 notch in right ventricular epicardium.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-14-4.png"},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital long QT syndrome (about 1 in 2,000) leads to early afterdepolarizations and torsades de pointes (CV-7, section C06). IKs is increased by sympathetic stimulation and shortens the action potential during exercise, so LQT1 events occur with exertion. IKr dominates resting repolarization and falls with low extracellular K+, so LQT2 events follow startle and hypokalemia is dangerous. A late Na+ current matters most at slow rates, so LQT3 events occur at rest or in sleep."},{"type":"table","title":"Main genotypes of congenital long QT syndrome","cols":["Type","Gene and change","Typical trigger","T wave"],"rows":[["LQT1 (most common)","KCNQ1, loss of IKs","Exercise, especially swimming","Broad-based"],["LQT2","KCNH2, loss of IKr","Startle, emotion; hypokalemia","Low, notched or bifid"],["LQT3","SCN5A, gain of function (late Na+ current)","Rest or sleep","Late, after a long isoelectric ST"]],"caption":"K+ channel genes lose function; the Na+ channel gene gains function."},{"type":"p","text":"Romano-Ward syndrome is the common autosomal dominant form, with normal hearing. Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is autosomal recessive, from two abnormal alleles of KCNQ1 or KCNE1, and combines congenital sensorineural deafness with marked QT prolongation (often above 500 ms) and childhood sudden death. The stria vascularis secretes K+ into endolymph through KCNQ1/KCNE1 channels to generate the endocochlear potential; with none, hearing fails. One working allele suffices for the ear, so heterozygous parents hear normally but may have a mildly long QT."},{"type":"p","text":"Brugada syndrome causes ventricular fibrillation in a normal heart, mostly in men and more often in Southeast Asia. SCN5A loss-of-function variants (autosomal dominant, incomplete penetrance) occur in only about 20% to 25%. The type 1 ECG shows coved ST elevation of at least 2 mm in V1 or V2 into a negative T wave, unmasked by fever (some variant channels inactivate faster when warm), vagal tone, and Na+ channel blockers. Right ventricular outflow epicardium has a large transient outward current (Ito). A weaker upstroke lets Ito pull the membrane below L-type Ca2+ activation, so the epicardial dome is lost while endocardium keeps its plateau; slowed outflow conduction probably contributes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Brugada syndrome from SCN5A loss to ventricular fibrillation","steps":["Reduced peak Na+ current","Ito deepens the epicardial phase 1 notch","Epicardial dome lost; endocardium keeps plateau","Transmural gradient: coved ST elevation in V1 and V2","Phase 2 reentry gives a closely coupled beat","Polymorphic VT degenerates to ventricular fibrillation"],"caption":"Events cluster at rest, in sleep, and with fever; larger male epicardial Ito helps explain male predominance."},{"type":"p","text":"SCN5A shows allelic heterogeneity: gain of function causes LQT3, while loss of function causes Brugada syndrome and conduction disease, and some variants produce both."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 35-year-old man with a Brugada type 1 pattern develops pneumonia with high fever. Does his risk of ventricular fibrillation rise or fall, and why?","explain":"It rises. Some variant channels inactivate faster when warm, so fever cuts Na+ current further, deepens the Ito notch, and favors loss of the dome and phase 2 reentry.","check":"Compare with the Brugada paragraph and flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C05","obj":"CV-14.5","title":"Chromosomal and developmental syndromes with characteristic heart defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each syndrome's defect reflects the embryonic process it disturbs. The endocardial cushions fuse to divide the atrioventricular canal, close the ostium primum, and form parts of the atrioventricular valves. Neural crest cells build the aorticopulmonary septum that divides the truncus arteriosus (CV-1; defects in CV-18-G)."},{"type":"p","text":"Down syndrome (trisomy 21) arises about 95% from nondisjunction, mostly maternal meiosis I with advanced maternal age, about 4% from Robertsonian translocation, and about 1% from mosaicism. About 40% to 50% have a heart defect, classically an atrioventricular septal defect (ostium primum defect, inlet ventricular septal defect, common valve) from failed cushion fusion. Large shunts plus a tendency to early pulmonary vascular disease bring pulmonary hypertension sooner."},{"type":"p","text":"Turner syndrome (45,X in about half, otherwise mosaic or structural X changes) favors left-sided lesions: bicuspid aortic valve in about a fifth to a third, coarctation in about a tenth, and later aortic dilation and dissection. One hypothesis is that fetal jugular lymphedema, which leaves the webbed neck, compresses the ascending aorta and reduces left heart flow. Coarctation causes arm hypertension, weak delayed femoral pulses, and rib notching from collaterals."},{"type":"p","text":"The 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge or velocardiofacial syndrome, about 1 in 4,000) removes about 3 million base pairs, usually de novo but transmitted as autosomal dominant. Loss of one TBX1 copy disturbs the pharyngeal arches and the neural crest, giving conotruncal defects (interrupted aortic arch type B, truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, right-sided arch). Third and fourth pouch failure causes thymic hypoplasia with T cell deficiency and parathyroid hypoplasia with hypocalcemia (CATCH-22)."},{"type":"p","text":"Williams syndrome is a 7q11.23 microdeletion including ELN. Elastin also tells smooth muscle to stop proliferating, so haploinsufficiency thickens the media, causing supravalvular aortic stenosis at the sinotubular junction and peripheral pulmonary and renal artery stenosis. Neighboring genes explain the rest, since an ELN variant alone causes only familial supravalvular aortic stenosis."},{"type":"p","text":"VACTERL is an association, a nonrandom clustering without a single cause, unlike a syndrome (one cause) or a sequence (a cascade from one defect, such as Potter sequence). It needs at least three features, is usually sporadic with low recurrence, and reflects early mesodermal disturbance. Holt-Oram syndrome (TBX5, septal defects with upper limb anomalies) and Fanconi anemia (radial defects with marrow failure) are single-gene look-alikes."},{"type":"table","title":"Genetic syndromes and their characteristic cardiovascular lesions","cols":["Syndrome","Genetic basis","Characteristic lesion","Other clues"],"rows":[["Down","Trisomy 21","Atrioventricular septal defect","Hypotonia, flat face, duodenal atresia"],["Turner","45,X or mosaic","Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation","Short stature, webbed neck, ovarian insufficiency"],["22q11.2 deletion","Microdeletion with TBX1","Interrupted arch type B, truncus, tetralogy","Thymic hypoplasia, hypocalcemia, cleft palate"],["Williams","7q11.23 deletion with ELN","Supravalvular aortic stenosis","Elfin facies, hypercalcemia, friendly personality"],["VACTERL","Sporadic association","Ventricular septal defect most often","Vertebral, anal, tracheoesophageal, renal, radial limb defects"],["Noonan","Autosomal dominant RAS pathway, often PTPN11","Dysplastic pulmonary valve stenosis, HCM","Turner-like features, normal karyotype, both sexes"]],"caption":"The disturbed developmental process predicts the heart defect."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C06","obj":"CV-14.6","title":"Muscular dystrophies, Friedreich ataxia, and mitochondrial DNA disease in the heart","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dystrophin links actin through beta-dystroglycan and alpha-dystroglycan to laminin in the basement membrane, protecting the sarcolemma during contraction. DMD (Xp21) is the largest human gene, so about a third of cases are new variants. Duchenne and Becker dystrophies are X-linked recessive; Duchenne affects about 1 in 3,500 to 5,000 males, and deletions make up about two thirds of variants. By the reading frame rule, in-frame deletions make a shortened, partly functional dystrophin (Becker), and frameshifts create a premature stop with no dystrophin (Duchenne), correct in about 90%."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying the reading frame rule","text":"Boy A has lost exons totaling 444 coding nucleotides; Boy B has lost 290. 444 divided by 3 is 148, so 148 codons are removed, the downstream frame is intact, and a shortened dystrophin with both binding ends is made: Becker. 290 divided by 3 leaves a remainder of 2, so the frame shifts, a premature stop appears, the transcript undergoes nonsense-mediated decay, and no dystrophin is made: Duchenne, despite the smaller deletion."},{"type":"p","text":"Without dystrophin, contraction tears the membrane, Ca2+ enters, and proteases and mitochondrial Ca2+ overload cause necrosis. Creatine kinase is very high, with Gowers sign and calf pseudohypertrophy. Cardiac fibrosis starts subepicardially in the inferolateral left ventricle, producing dilated cardiomyopathy by the late teens, tall R waves in V1, lateral Q waves, and sinus tachycardia. Becker cardiomyopathy can outpace limb weakness. Female carriers can develop cardiomyopathy when random or skewed X inactivation leaves many myocytes expressing only the mutant allele."},{"type":"p","text":"Friedreich ataxia is autosomal recessive, caused by a GAA repeat expansion in the first intron of FXN (normal up to about 33 repeats; disease about 66 to more than 1,000). The expansion silences transcription, so frataxin, needed for mitochondrial iron-sulfur cluster assembly (aconitase, complexes I to III), is low. Energy failure, mitochondrial iron accumulation, and oxidative damage hit dorsal root ganglia and spinocerebellar tracts, the heart, and beta cells. Cardiomyopathy is concentric hypertrophy without extensive disarray, later fibrosis and dilation, and is the usual cause of death."},{"type":"p","text":"Mitochondrial DNA (about 16,600 base pairs) encodes 13 respiratory chain subunits, 22 transfer RNAs, and 2 ribosomal RNAs and is maternally inherited. Heteroplasmy, random segregation, and the oocyte bottleneck make mutant fractions vary between tissues and siblings, and disease appears where a tissue's threshold is exceeded, lowest in brain, retina, muscle, and heart. Kearns-Sayre syndrome is a large, usually sporadic mtDNA deletion with onset before 20: progressive external ophthalmoplegia, pigmentary retinopathy, and conduction block, high CSF protein, or ataxia. His-Purkinje block can progress to complete heart block, and muscle shows ragged red fibers."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited neuromuscular and mitochondrial disorders that affect the heart","cols":["Disorder","Gene and inheritance","Cardiac involvement"],"rows":[["Duchenne","DMD, X-linked recessive; no dystrophin","Dilated cardiomyopathy from inferolateral fibrosis"],["Becker","DMD, X-linked recessive; in-frame, partial dystrophin","Dilated cardiomyopathy, can exceed limb weakness"],["Friedreich ataxia","FXN GAA expansion, autosomal recessive","Concentric hypertrophy, later fibrosis and dilation"],["Kearns-Sayre","Large mtDNA deletion, usually sporadic","Progressive His-Purkinje conduction block"]],"caption":"Membrane fragility causes dilation, energy failure causes hypertrophy, and mtDNA deletion favors the conduction system."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 30-year-old woman whose son has Duchenne dystrophy has no weakness but develops a dilated, poorly contracting left ventricle. How can a carrier of an X-linked recessive disease develop cardiomyopathy?","explain":"Random X inactivation leaves some myocytes expressing only the mutant DMD allele. If enough do, through chance or skewed inactivation, those cells are damaged with each contraction and replaced by fibrosis that the heart cannot repair.","check":"Compare with the carrier sentence and the dystrophin pathogenesis paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-14-01-C07","obj":"CV-14.7","title":"Cardiomyocyte cell cycle exit, hypertrophy, the fetal gene program, and scar","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fetal cardiomyocytes divide, but around birth they largely leave the cell cycle, and many become polyploid or binucleate. Exit is maintained by low cyclin and cyclin-dependent kinase activity, the inhibitors p21 and p27, and retinoblastoma protein repression of E2F. Carbon-14 dating shows renewal of only about 1% per year in young adults, and no confirmed adult cardiac stem cell rebuilds muscle, so the heart is a permanent tissue. Skeletal muscle regenerates from satellite cells and smooth muscle can divide, but cardiac muscle has no such reserve."},{"type":"p","text":"By Laplace's law, ventricular wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice wall thickness. Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) adds sarcomeres in parallel, thickening the wall (concentric hypertrophy), while volume overload (regurgitation) adds sarcomeres in series, enlarging the cavity (eccentric hypertrophy). Both tend to return wall stress to normal. Normal heart weight is about 250 to 300 g in women and 300 to 350 g in men."},{"type":"p","text":"Stretch is sensed by integrins, Z disc proteins, and stretch-activated channels. Angiotensin II, endothelin-1, and norepinephrine act through Gq, phospholipase C, Ca2+, and protein kinase C; sustained Ca2+ activates calcineurin, which dephosphorylates NFAT so it enters the nucleus and activates hypertrophic genes with GATA4 and MEF2. Exercise hypertrophy uses IGF-1 through PI3K and Akt instead. Pathologic hypertrophy reactivates the fetal gene program: ANP in ventricle, BNP in proportion to stretch, beta-myosin and skeletal alpha-actin up, alpha-myosin and SERCA2a down, and a shift toward glucose use. Slower myosin saves energy, less SERCA2a slows relaxation, and natriuretic peptides raise cGMP to oppose the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. BNP below about 100 pg/mL makes heart failure an unlikely cause of acute dyspnea."},{"type":"table","title":"Physiologic compared with pathologic hypertrophy","cols":["Feature","Physiologic (exercise, pregnancy)","Pathologic (pressure, volume, infarction)"],"rows":[["Signals","IGF-1, PI3K, Akt","Gq, calcineurin, NFAT"],["Fetal gene program","Not activated","ANP, BNP, beta-myosin"],["Capillaries and fibrosis","Capillaries keep pace; no fibrosis","Capillaries lag; interstitial fibrosis"],["Outcome","Regresses","May progress to failure"]],"caption":"Both enlarge existing myocytes; the signaling route decides the outcome."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Maladaptive cycle of pathologic hypertrophy","steps":["Sustained load raises wall stress","Myocytes hypertrophy; fetal genes switch on","Capillaries lag, SERCA2a falls, collagen accumulates","Ischemia and Ca2+ mishandling kill myocytes","Survivors are overloaded; the ventricle dilates","Larger radius raises wall stress again"],"caption":"Lost myocytes are not replaced, so each turn leaves fewer cells to carry the load."},{"type":"p","text":"After infarction (stages in CV-18-C), macrophages clear dead tissue and release TGF-beta, converting fibroblasts into contractile myofibroblasts that lay down type III collagen, later replaced by cross-linked type I collagen. The scar is strong but cannot contract or conduct, so survivors hypertrophy and border-zone strands support reentrant ventricular tachycardia (CV-7). Stunned and hibernating myocardium can recover; scar cannot. The same repair follows myocyte loss in dystrophy and Friedreich ataxia."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"A few genetic rules connect this chapter. When disease comes from too little protein (LDL receptor, elastin, myosin-binding protein C), heterozygotes are affected and homozygotes are worse. When a protein is built from repeated subunits (type III collagen, KCNQ1 channels, the thick filament), a dominant negative subunit spoils normal partners. Recessive disease such as Friedreich ataxia or Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome appears when half the protein is enough, and X inactivation and heteroplasmy make severity differ between cells, tissues, and relatives. Penetrance and expressivity vary with modifiers, sex, age, and environment, and a negative test does not exclude disease when only some patients carry a detectable variant, as in HCM and Brugada syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy, embryology, and biochemistry predict where each disease appears: cushions in Down syndrome, neural crest derivatives in the 22q11.2 deletion, matrix pleiotropy in Marfan and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, and energy-hungry tissues in Friedreich ataxia and Kearns-Sayre syndrome. Pathology links these to acquired disease, since FH accelerates ordinary atherosclerosis and the 22q11.2 deletion is also a T cell immunodeficiency. Because cardiomyocytes are permanent, every disease here that kills them ends in fibrosis, hypertrophy of survivors, and an arrhythmic substrate."}],"summary":["Familial hypercholesterolemia is autosomal dominant with a gene dosage effect: LDLR, APOB, or gain-of-function PCSK9 variants cut receptor-mediated LDL clearance, giving about 2 to 3 times normal cholesterol in heterozygotes and 5 to 6 times in homozygotes.","In FH, low cholesterol delivery to the hepatocyte keeps SREBP-2 active, so synthesis stays high while plasma LDL is already elevated, and fewer receptors let more IDL become LDL.","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is autosomal dominant, mostly MYH7 (dominant negative) or MYBPC3 (haploinsufficient), with disarray, diastolic dysfunction, age-dependent penetrance, and dynamic obstruction that worsens when ventricular volume falls.","Marfan (FBN1) causes medial degeneration with excess TGF-beta signaling, aortic root dilation and dissection, and upward ectopia lentis; Loeys-Dietz (TGFBR1 or TGFBR2) causes earlier widespread aneurysms; vascular Ehlers-Danlos (COL3A1) causes rupture of arteries, bowel, and uterus.","In multimeric proteins such as type III collagen and KCNQ1 channels, a dominant negative subunit spoils most assembled molecules, so missense variants are often worse than null alleles.","Long QT results from loss of IKs (KCNQ1, exercise) or IKr (KCNH2, startle) or a late Na+ current from SCN5A gain of function (rest); Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome adds deafness because the same channel makes endolymph.","Brugada syndrome, with SCN5A loss of function in a minority, gives coved ST elevation in V1 and V2 and ventricular fibrillation at rest or with fever through loss of the epicardial dome and phase 2 reentry.","Down syndrome causes atrioventricular septal defects, Turner syndrome bicuspid valve and coarctation, 22q11.2 deletion conotruncal defects with thymic and parathyroid hypoplasia, Williams syndrome supravalvular aortic stenosis, and VACTERL is a sporadic association.","Duchenne (out-of-frame) and Becker (in-frame) dystrophies cause dilated cardiomyopathy; Friedreich ataxia causes concentric hypertrophy through iron-sulfur cluster failure; Kearns-Sayre syndrome causes His-Purkinje block.","Adult cardiomyocytes have left the cell cycle, so load produces hypertrophy with the fetal gene program and lost myocytes are replaced by scar."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders (Mendelian disorders, cytogenetic disorders, and diseases caused by mitochondrial mutations)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (cardiac hypertrophy, cardiomyopathies, congenital heart disease, and arrhythmias)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (aneurysms and dissection)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-15":{"unit":"CV-15","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01","title":"Heart and Lung Interactions","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The heart and lungs work as one circuit inside the thorax. This chapter covers the low-pressure, low-resistance pulmonary bed and why its resistance falls as flow rises, the opposite responses of pulmonary and systemic vessels to hypoxia, the effect of breathing on venous return and ventricular filling, how left heart disease floods the lung and lung disease loads the right heart, how gravity distributes blood and air, and how shunts and the pulmonary endothelium fit in. Most board questions here are solved with four tools: Ohm's law applied to the pulmonary bed, Starling forces across the pulmonary capillary, intrathoracic pressure acting on the cardiac chambers, and the shape of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve.","prerequisites":["Pressure, flow, and resistance (Ohm's law and Poiseuille's law) and the series arrangement of the circulation (CV-11)","Starling forces across the capillary wall and the role of lymphatics (CV-10, CV-11)","Cardiac cycle, heart sounds, and the Frank-Starling relationship (CV-4, CV-6)","Pleural pressure, lung compliance, and functional residual capacity (FRC)","The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve and oxygen content of blood"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"The pulmonary bed obeys the same pressure, flow, and resistance relationships as the systemic bed, but its local response to hypoxia is reversed."},{"to":"Cardiovascular embryology (CV-1) and congenital heart conditions (CV-18-G)","how":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction keeps fetal pulmonary resistance high until birth, and a left-to-right shunt that remodels the pulmonary bed can later reverse."},{"to":"Congestive heart failure (CV-18-B) and valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Left ventricular failure and mitral stenosis raise pulmonary capillary pressure and produce pulmonary edema, the main cause of dyspnea in left heart disease."},{"to":"Pericardial disease (CV-18-F)","how":"Cardiac tamponade exaggerates the normal inspiratory interaction between the ventricles and produces pulsus paradoxus."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology and the renin-angiotensin system","how":"Ventilation-perfusion matching and shunt explain the blood gases in lung disease, and pulmonary endothelial ACE makes angiotensin II and degrades bradykinin."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR)","def":"Mean pulmonary artery pressure minus left atrial pressure, divided by cardiac output."},{"term":"Recruitment and distension","def":"Opening of closed pulmonary capillaries and widening of open ones, the two passive ways PVR falls as flow rises."},{"term":"Alveolar and extra-alveolar vessels","def":"Capillaries in the alveolar walls, compressed at high lung volume, and larger vessels in the parenchyma, pulled open at high lung volume."},{"term":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)","def":"Constriction of small pulmonary arteries and arterioles in response to low alveolar PO2, which diverts blood toward better ventilated lung."},{"term":"Pulmonary hypertension","def":"A sustained mean pulmonary artery pressure above about 20 mmHg at rest."},{"term":"Ventricular interdependence","def":"The influence of one ventricle's volume on the filling of the other through the shared septum and the limited space of the pericardium."},{"term":"Pulsus paradoxus","def":"An inspiratory fall in systolic arterial pressure greater than 10 mmHg, an exaggeration of the normal small inspiratory dip."},{"term":"Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)","def":"Pressure measured through a catheter wedged in a small pulmonary artery branch, which reflects left atrial pressure."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure caused by pulmonary hypertension from lung, pulmonary vascular, or ventilatory disease rather than left heart disease."},{"term":"Ventilation-perfusion ratio (V/Q)","def":"The ratio of alveolar ventilation to capillary blood flow in a lung unit, which sets that unit's alveolar PO2 and PCO2."},{"term":"Shunt","def":"Blood that reaches the systemic arteries without passing through ventilated alveoli (V/Q of zero)."},{"term":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)","def":"An endothelial peptidase, abundant in the pulmonary capillaries, that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II and inactivates bradykinin (also called kininase II)."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C01","obj":"CV-15.1","title":"Pressure and resistance in the pulmonary and systemic circuits","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pulmonary and systemic circulations are in series, so both ventricles eject about 5 L/min at rest, and any difference in pressure between the beds reflects a difference in resistance. Pulmonary artery walls are about one third as thick as the aorta's, and pulmonary arterioles have little smooth muscle, whereas systemic arterioles are the main resistance vessels of the body. The pulmonary circuit holds about 450 mL of blood and is highly compliant. Bronchial arteries from the aorta separately supply the airways at systemic pressure with about 1 to 2% of cardiac output."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical resting pressures and resistance in the two circuits","cols":["Measurement","Pulmonary circuit","Systemic circuit"],"rows":[["Arterial pressure (mean)","About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15)","About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93)"],["Receiving atrium","Left atrium about 5 mmHg","Right atrium about 2 mmHg"],["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","About 7 mmHg (some sources about 10)","About 17 mmHg"],["Resistance at 5 L/min","About 2 Wood units","About 18 Wood units"],["Main resistance site","Spread across arteries, capillaries, and veins","Arterioles"]],"caption":"With equal flow, a ninefold smaller pressure drop means a ninefold smaller resistance. A Wood unit is 1 mmHg per L/min."},{"type":"p","text":"Low pressure has two consequences. The thin right ventricle tolerates a sudden rise in afterload poorly (section C04), and a capillary pressure far below the plasma oncotic pressure of about 28 mmHg keeps the alveoli dry. Pulmonary hypertension is now defined as a mean pulmonary artery pressure above about 20 mmHg at rest, against a normal mean of about 15 mmHg."},{"type":"h","text":"Why pulmonary resistance falls as flow rises"},{"type":"p","text":"With so little muscle, pulmonary vessel caliber is set mainly by the pressures inside and around the vessels. A small rise in pulmonary artery pressure opens capillaries that were closed or unperfused at rest, mostly in the upper lung (recruitment), which adds parallel channels. It also widens capillaries that are already open (distension), and because resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius, modest widening lowers resistance greatly. Recruitment dominates at low pressure and distension at higher pressure. This spares the right ventricle, limits filtration, and enlarges the gas exchange surface; capillary transit falls from about 0.8 s to about 0.3 s, still enough for oxygen to equilibrate."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating PVR at rest and in exercise","text":"At rest, mean pulmonary artery pressure is about 15 mmHg and left atrial pressure about 5 mmHg, so PVR is (15 minus 5) divided by 5 L/min, or 2 Wood units. In heavy exercise, using illustrative values, cardiac output is 15 L/min, mean pulmonary artery pressure about 22 mmHg, and left atrial pressure about 10 mmHg. PVR is (22 minus 10) divided by 15, or 0.8 Wood units. Flow tripled because resistance fell by more than half; had PVR stayed at 2 Wood units, mean pulmonary artery pressure would have neared 40 mmHg."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung volume acts on two classes of vessel in opposite directions. Alveolar vessels, the capillaries in the alveolar walls, are stretched and compressed as alveoli expand, so their resistance rises at high lung volume, as in hyperinflation or high positive end-expiratory pressure. Extra-alveolar vessels in the parenchyma are pulled open by radial traction and more negative pleural pressure as the lung expands, and they narrow at low volume, as in atelectasis. Total PVR is therefore lowest near FRC and rises at both extremes."},{"type":"p","text":"Recruitment and distension are a reserve that disease uses up. When vascular bed is lost to emphysema, emboli, or arterial remodeling, the remaining vessels are already recruited at rest and further flow raises pressure steeply. Pulmonary vascular disease therefore often appears first as pulmonary hypertension and dyspnea during exercise, whereas removal of one healthy lung causes little rise in resting pressure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C02","obj":"CV-15.2","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and hypoxic pulmonary hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood sent to an unventilated alveolus is wasted, so pulmonary arterioles, unlike systemic ones, constrict when the alveoli they supply become hypoxic. This hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) is the main local control of pulmonary flow and of V/Q matching. The stimulus is mainly alveolar PO2, which diffuses directly to the small muscular arteries; the response becomes strong below about 70 mmHg and persists in an isolated, denervated lung."},{"type":"h","text":"Opposite ion channel events in the two beds"},{"type":"p","text":"In pulmonary artery smooth muscle, hypoxia inhibits the voltage-gated K+ channels (Kv1.5 and Kv2.1) that hold the resting potential negative. The membrane depolarizes, L-type Ca2+ channels open, and Ca2+ entry plus sarcoplasmic reticulum release activates calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase. Rho-kinase inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase and raises Ca2+ sensitivity, and less endothelial nitric oxide and more endothelin-1 add to the constriction. The upstream oxygen sensor is debated; the leading proposal is a change in mitochondrial reactive oxygen species and redox state. Hypoxic systemic arterioles do the reverse: adenosine acts on A2 receptors, falling ATP opens ATP-sensitive K+ (KATP) channels and hyperpolarizes the muscle, and lactate, H+, CO2, nitric oxide, and prostacyclin add to dilation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","steps":["Regional alveolar PO2 falls below about 70 mmHg","Kv channels in pulmonary artery smooth muscle are inhibited","Membrane depolarizes and L-type Ca2+ channels open","Ca2+ and Rho-kinase drive contraction","Small arteries and arterioles of that region constrict","Blood is diverted to ventilated lung, preserving V/Q matching"],"caption":"Low alveolar oxygen is linked to contraction through K+ channel inhibition and Ca2+ entry."},{"type":"table","title":"Pulmonary versus systemic arteriolar responses","cols":["Feature","Pulmonary arterioles","Systemic arterioles"],"rows":[["Response to local hypoxia","Constriction","Dilation"],["Key ion channel event","Kv channels close, membrane depolarizes","KATP channels open, membrane hyperpolarizes"],["Purpose","Send blood to ventilated alveoli","Send blood to tissue that needs oxygen"],["Response to acidosis and high CO2","Constriction (strengthens HPV)","Dilation"],["Result of global hypoxia","Pulmonary hypertension","Vasodilation, offset by reflex sympathetic tone"]],"caption":"The lung loads oxygen and systemic tissues consume it, so the two beds respond in opposite directions."},{"type":"h","text":"Regional hypoxia helps; global hypoxia harms"},{"type":"p","text":"When hypoxia is regional, as in lobar pneumonia or atelectasis, HPV diverts blood to healthy lung and limits the shunt, and mean pulmonary artery pressure barely changes because the rest of the bed recruits. In the fetus, HPV in the fluid-filled lung keeps PVR high and sends right ventricular output through the ductus arteriosus. At birth, lung expansion, rising alveolar PO2, and nitric oxide lower PVR sharply; failure of this fall is persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn."},{"type":"p","text":"When hypoxia is global (high altitude, COPD, chronic hypoventilation, advanced interstitial disease), every region constricts at once, PVR rises, and nothing is gained in matching. Hypercapnia and acidosis strengthen the constriction. Uneven HPV on rapid ascent overperfuses less constricted regions at high pressure, the accepted explanation for high-altitude pulmonary edema."},{"type":"p","text":"Sustained hypoxia also remodels the vessels. Stabilized hypoxia-inducible factors (HIF-1 alpha and HIF-2 alpha) drive medial hypertrophy, muscularization of arterioles that normally lack muscle, and intimal and adventitial fibrosis, adding a fixed component to the reversible constriction. HIF also raises erythropoietin, and the resulting polycythemia raises viscosity and resistance. In COPD, emphysematous loss of capillary bed adds to this, and the mild to moderate pulmonary hypertension leads over years to chronic cor pulmonale (section C04)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with COPD develops right lower lobe pneumonia. Another person climbs rapidly to 4,000 m. In which does HPV mainly protect arterial PO2, and in which does it mainly raise pulmonary artery pressure?","explain":"In pneumonia the hypoxia is regional, so HPV diverts flow to ventilated lung and limits the shunt. At altitude it is global, so every region constricts and PVR rises with no gain in matching.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on regional and global hypoxia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C03","obj":"CV-15.3","title":"Breathing, venous return, and ventricular filling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The heart and great veins lie within the pleural (intrathoracic) pressure. A chamber fills according to its transmural pressure, inside minus outside, so a change in pleural pressure changes the filling pressure of every chamber at once. In quiet breathing, pleural pressure is about -5 cmH2O at FRC and about -7.5 cmH2O at end inspiration."},{"type":"p","text":"Venous return is driven by mean systemic filling pressure (about 7 mmHg) minus right atrial pressure. Spontaneous inspiration lowers right atrial pressure relative to the extrathoracic veins, and the descending diaphragm raises abdominal pressure and pushes blood toward the thorax. Right ventricular filling and stroke volume rise within the same breath. Left ventricular stroke volume falls slightly for three reasons: the expanded pulmonary vessels hold more blood, the fuller right ventricle bows the septum to the left (ventricular interdependence), and the more negative pleural pressure slightly raises left ventricular afterload. The extra right output reaches the left heart a few beats later, in early expiration. The normal inspiratory fall in systolic pressure is less than 10 mmHg."},{"type":"flow","title":"Inspiration, ventricular output, and S2","steps":["Pleural pressure falls","Venous return and right ventricular stroke volume rise","Right ventricular ejection lasts longer, delaying P2","Pulmonary vessels hold more blood; septum bows left","Left stroke volume falls slightly, so A2 comes slightly earlier","S2 splits; systolic pressure dips less than 10 mmHg"],"caption":"One fall in pleural pressure explains physiologic splitting of S2 and the small inspiratory dip in arterial pressure. Expiration reverses each step."},{"type":"p","text":"P2 is delayed both by the longer right ventricular ejection and by the low impedance of the expanded pulmonary vessels, which lets blood keep moving forward after right ventricular pressure starts to fall. The split is best heard at the left upper sternal border, because P2 is soft. Abnormal splitting follows the same timing logic."},{"type":"table","title":"Splitting of S2","cols":["Pattern","What is heard","Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Physiologic","Split in inspiration only","Inspiration delays P2, advances A2","Normal"],["Wide","Split in both phases, wider in inspiration","Right ventricular emptying delayed","Right bundle branch block, pulmonic stenosis"],["Fixed","Wide split unchanged by breathing","Shunt flow falls as venous return rises, keeping right ventricular volume constant","Atrial septal defect"],["Paradoxical","Split in expiration, single in inspiration","A2 delayed until after P2","Left bundle branch block, severe aortic stenosis"]],"caption":"Each abnormal pattern is predicted from which ventricle is delayed or volume loaded."},{"type":"h","text":"Pulsus paradoxus"},{"type":"p","text":"Pulsus paradoxus, an inspiratory fall in systolic pressure greater than 10 mmHg, exaggerates the normal dip rather than reversing it. In cardiac tamponade, pericardial fluid fixes the total volume of the two ventricles, so inspiratory right ventricular filling pushes the septum into the left ventricle and left stroke volume falls far more than normal; the same compression equalizes diastolic pressures and explains hypotension and distended neck veins. In severe asthma or COPD the pericardium is normal, but very large pleural pressure swings raise right heart filling and left afterload. Massive pulmonary embolism and severe hypovolemia can also produce it. It is less common in constrictive pericarditis, where the rigid pericardium shields the heart and jugular venous pressure rises on inspiration instead of falling."},{"type":"h","text":"Positive pressure ventilation reverses the pattern"},{"type":"p","text":"A ventilator raises pleural pressure during inspiration, and positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) keeps it raised. Right atrial pressure rises, the gradient for venous return narrows, and cardiac output falls, most of all in hypovolemia, when mean systemic filling pressure is already low. High lung volumes also compress alveolar vessels and can create zone 1 lung (section C05). The higher pressure around the left ventricle lowers its afterload, so in a failing left ventricle with high filling pressures stroke volume may be maintained, while with low filling pressures the loss of preload dominates. The strain phase of a Valsalva maneuver acts the same way, lowering venous return and pulse pressure and triggering a baroreflex rise in heart rate."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C04","obj":"CV-15.4","title":"When the heart loads the lung and the lung loads the heart: pulmonary edema and cor pulmonale","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In series, a failing left heart floods the lung, and a diseased lung overloads the right ventricle."},{"type":"h","text":"Starling forces and the safety margin"},{"type":"p","text":"In the pulmonary capillary, the outward forces are capillary hydrostatic pressure (about 7 mmHg), a negative interstitial pressure (about -8 mmHg, acting as suction), and a relatively high interstitial oncotic pressure (about 14 mmHg), totaling about 29 mmHg. The inward force is plasma oncotic pressure, about 28 mmHg. Lymphatics carry off the net outward filtration of about 1 mmHg. As capillary pressure rises, increased lymph flow, loss of interstitial suction, and dilution of interstitial protein hold off edema until capillary pressure approaches plasma oncotic pressure, a margin of about 21 mmHg. Beyond that, interstitial edema forms and then the alveoli flood. Low albumin narrows the margin, chronic elevation widens it (lymphatics can enlarge about tenfold, and some patients with mitral stenosis tolerate 40 mmHg), and permeability injury, as in ARDS, causes protein-rich edema at a normal capillary pressure."},{"type":"h","text":"Left heart failure and mitral stenosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Left atrial pressure passes back through the valveless pulmonary veins. It rises in left ventricular failure, acute mitral regurgitation, and mitral stenosis, which worsens with anything that shortens diastole (exercise, fever, pregnancy, rapid atrial fibrillation). PCWP estimates left atrial pressure and is normally about 6 to 12 mmHg. In mitral stenosis it exceeds left ventricular diastolic pressure, and in ARDS it is normal despite severe edema. Stiff lungs and juxtacapillary (J) receptor stimulation cause dyspnea, lying flat shifts blood into the thorax (orthopnea), and flooded alveoli give basal crackles and act as shunt. The chest film shows Kerley B lines and upper lobe vessel prominence (cephalization), and chronic congestion leaves hemosiderin-laden macrophages."},{"type":"h","text":"Cor pulmonale"},{"type":"p","text":"Cor pulmonale is right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure from pulmonary hypertension of lung, pulmonary vascular, or ventilatory origin. Right heart failure from left heart disease, the most common cause of right heart failure overall, is excluded."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical groups of pulmonary hypertension","cols":["Group","Cause","Main mechanism","Cor pulmonale?"],"rows":[["1","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (idiopathic, BMPR2, connective tissue disease)","Endothelial dysfunction, plexiform lesions","Yes"],["2","Left heart disease","Backward transmission of left atrial pressure","No"],["3","Lung disease or hypoxia (COPD, interstitial disease, sleep apnea)","Hypoxic constriction, remodeling, loss of capillary bed","Yes"],["4","Chronic thromboembolism","Organized clots obstruct arteries","Yes"],["5","Multifactorial (for example sarcoidosis)","Mixed","Depends"]],"caption":"Groups 1, 3, and 4 load the right ventricle from the lung side."},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic cor pulmonale, most often from COPD, the right ventricle meets slowly rising afterload with concentric hypertrophy, since by the law of Laplace a thicker wall lowers wall stress. It later dilates, the tricuspid valve leaks, and systemic venous pressure rises, causing distended jugular veins, a nutmeg liver, and dependent edema. Pulmonary hypertension gives a loud P2 and a parasternal heave, and the ECG shows right axis deviation, a tall R wave in V1, and P pulmonale in lead II."},{"type":"p","text":"A massive pulmonary embolus acts in minutes. Clot obstructs the bed and platelet thromboxane A2 and serotonin constrict the rest. The thin, unhypertrophied right ventricle cannot generate a mean pressure much above about 40 mmHg, and obstruction of about 60% or more of the circulation typically causes acute failure or sudden death."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Right ventricular failure spiral in massive pulmonary embolism","steps":["Right ventricular afterload rises","Right ventricle dilates; its output falls","Septum shifts into the left ventricle","Left ventricular output and arterial pressure fall","Right coronary perfusion falls as wall stress stays high","Right ventricular ischemia weakens contraction further"],"caption":"Each turn lowers output further, so hypotension in pulmonary embolism signals high mortality."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with long-standing mitral stenosis develops distended neck veins, hepatomegaly, and ankle edema. Is this cor pulmonale?","explain":"No. The right ventricle failed from pulmonary hypertension caused by the left heart (group 2).","check":"Compare with the cor pulmonale definition and table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C05","obj":"CV-15.5","title":"Gravity, the lung zones, and ventilation-perfusion ratios","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because the pulmonary circulation runs at low pressure, the weight of the blood column matters. Pressure rises about 1 cmH2O per centimeter of depth (1 mmHg is about 1.36 cmH2O). The apex lies about 20 cm above the pulmonic valve and the base about 10 cm below it, so with a pulmonary artery pressure of 25/8 mmHg, apical pressure is about 10 mmHg in systole and below zero in diastole, while basal pressure is about 33/16 mmHg. Blood flow per unit volume therefore rises steeply from apex to base."},{"type":"h","text":"The three zones"},{"type":"p","text":"Capillaries in the alveolar walls are also exposed to alveolar pressure, which stays near atmospheric during quiet breathing. Comparing arterial (Pa), alveolar (PA), and venous (Pv) pressure divides the upright lung into three zones."},{"type":"table","title":"Zones of the upright lung","cols":["Zone","Pressure order","Blood flow","Driving pressure","Usual location"],"rows":[["1","PA greater than Pa greater than Pv","None (alveolar dead space)","None","Normally absent; apex with low Pa or high PA"],["2","Pa greater than PA greater than Pv","Intermittent, mainly in systole","Pa minus PA","Upper and middle lung"],["3","Pa greater than Pv greater than PA","Continuous","Pa minus Pv","Lower lung"]],"caption":"Moving down the lung, Pa and Pv rise with depth while PA stays constant."},{"type":"p","text":"In zone 2 the outflow end of the capillary collapses to alveolar pressure, so venous pressure does not affect flow, like a waterfall whose flow does not depend on the drop below it. Zone 1 is normally absent because apical systolic pressure still exceeds alveolar pressure. It appears when pulmonary artery pressure falls (hemorrhage, shock) or alveolar pressure rises (positive pressure ventilation with PEEP). A PEEP of 15 cmH2O, about 11 mmHg, exceeds an apical systolic pressure of 10 mmHg. Zone 1 alveoli are dead space, so arterial PCO2 rises unless ventilation increases. Exercise does the reverse, recruiting apical capillaries and converting zone 2 to zone 3."},{"type":"h","text":"Ventilation and V/Q from apex to base"},{"type":"p","text":"Ventilation also rises toward the base, but less steeply. The weight of the lung makes pleural pressure about -10 cmH2O at the apex and about -2.5 cmH2O at the base at FRC, so apical alveoli are more expanded and sit on the flat, less compliant part of the pressure-volume curve, while basal alveoli expand more with each breath. Average V/Q is about 0.8 (alveolar ventilation about 4 L/min over cardiac output about 5 L/min). Because flow rises more steeply than ventilation, V/Q is about 3 at the apex, with alveolar PO2 about 130 mmHg and PCO2 about 28 mmHg, and about 0.6 at the base, with PO2 about 90 mmHg and PCO2 about 42 mmHg."},{"type":"p","text":"The high apical PO2 is the usual explanation for the upper lobe location of reactivation tuberculosis, since Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe. The apex contributes little to arterial blood because it receives little flow. High V/Q units cannot make up for low V/Q units in oxygen, because their blood is already nearly saturated on the flat top of the dissociation curve, but they can unload extra CO2, whose curve is nearly linear. V/Q mismatch therefore causes hypoxemia with a normal or low PaCO2, and normal regional mismatch plus the small anatomical shunt produce the normal alveolar-arterial (A-a) PO2 gradient of about 5 to 15 mmHg in young adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Supine, the apex-to-base differences largely disappear and dependent posterior regions receive more flow. In pulmonary embolism the blocked region becomes dead space, and the diverted blood overperfuses the rest of the lung; these low V/Q regions, with atelectasis and sometimes a right-to-left shunt through a patent foramen ovale, cause the hypoxemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-15-01-C06","obj":"CV-15.6","title":"Shunt hypoxemia and the nonrespiratory functions of the pulmonary circulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A shunt is blood that reaches the systemic arteries without passing ventilated alveoli (V/Q of zero). Anatomical shunts include right-to-left cardiac connections, pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, and the normal 2% from bronchial and Thebesian veins. Intrapulmonary shunts are perfused alveoli that are collapsed or filled, as in atelectasis, lobar pneumonia, pulmonary edema, and ARDS."},{"type":"p","text":"Breathing 100% oxygen corrects other causes of hypoxemia but barely corrects shunt. Shunted blood never meets alveolar gas, and blood from ventilated alveoli is already about 97 to 98% saturated, so raising its PO2 adds only dissolved oxygen (0.003 mL/dL per mmHg). Mixing combines oxygen content, not partial pressure, and the steep part of the dissociation curve turns the diluted content into a low PO2. PaCO2 is usually normal or low, because chemoreceptor-driven hyperventilation unloads the extra CO2."},{"type":"example","title":"A 30% shunt on room air and on 100% oxygen","text":"Assume hemoglobin 15 g/dL (20.1 mL O2/dL saturated) and an arteriovenous difference of 5 mL/dL. On room air, ventilated blood carries about 19.9 mL/dL. Arterial content (Ca) is 0.7 times 19.9 plus 0.3 times (Ca minus 5), so Ca is about 17.8 mL/dL, a saturation near 88% and a PaO2 near 55 mmHg. On 100% oxygen, alveolar PO2 is about 660 mmHg, so ventilated blood carries 20.1 bound plus about 2.0 dissolved, or 22.1 mL/dL. Ca rises only to about 20.0 mL/dL, and PaO2 to about 100 mmHg, against more than 500 mmHg in a normal lung. That failure to rise identifies a shunt."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of arterial hypoxemia","cols":["Mechanism","A-a gradient","Response to 100% O2","Example"],"rows":[["Low inspired PO2","Normal","Corrected","High altitude"],["Hypoventilation","Normal (PaCO2 high)","Corrected","Opioid overdose"],["Diffusion limitation","Increased, worse with exercise","Corrected","Interstitial fibrosis"],["Low V/Q units","Increased","Corrected","COPD, asthma, pulmonary embolism"],["Shunt (V/Q zero)","Increased","Little improvement","Right-to-left cardiac shunt, lobar pneumonia, alveolar edema"]],"caption":"Only shunt resists 100% oxygen. Alveolar dead space (V/Q near infinity) wastes ventilation and raises PaCO2 instead."},{"type":"p","text":"A congenital left-to-right shunt (ventricular or atrial septal defect, patent ductus arteriosus) causes no cyanosis at first. The extra pulmonary flow, and in a ventricular defect or ductus the systemic-level pressure, drives the remodeling described in section C02. If PVR rises above systemic resistance, the shunt reverses (Eisenmenger syndrome), producing late cyanosis, clubbing, and polycythemia, by which time the vessels no longer relax."},{"type":"flow","title":"Eisenmenger reversal of a left-to-right shunt","steps":["Left-to-right shunt raises pulmonary flow and often pressure","Endothelial injury, medial hypertrophy, and intimal fibrosis follow","PVR and right-sided pressures rise progressively","Right-sided pressures exceed left-sided pressures","Shunt reverses to right-to-left","Late cyanosis, clubbing, and polycythemia"],"caption":"The heart lesion remodels the lung vessels, and the lung vessels then reverse the heart lesion."},{"type":"h","text":"Nonrespiratory functions"},{"type":"p","text":"The pulmonary endothelium meets the whole cardiac output over an enormous surface. Its angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), a zinc peptidase, removes a terminal dipeptide from angiotensin I to form angiotensin II, and as kininase II it inactivates bradykinin. Blocking ACE lets bradykinin accumulate, which explains the dry cough and angioedema of ACE inhibition. In one passage the lung also removes much of the serotonin, prostaglandins E2 and F2 alpha, and leukotrienes and about 30% of norepinephrine, while epinephrine, angiotensin II, and vasopressin pass largely unchanged."},{"type":"p","text":"The capillaries filter small venous clots, fat, and air. Small emboli rarely infarct, because bronchial arteries keep the tissue alive and endothelial fibrinolysis dissolves clots; infarction is more likely when bronchial flow is also poor, as in left heart failure. A clot crossing a patent foramen ovale, present in about a quarter of adults, is a paradoxical embolism."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Patients with lobar pneumonia and with pulmonary embolism are both hypoxemic. Predict how each responds to 100% oxygen.","explain":"Pneumonia is true shunt, so PaO2 rises little. Embolism hypoxemia comes mainly from low V/Q regions, so PaO2 rises substantially unless a patent foramen ovale shunts.","check":"Compare with the shunt example, the hypoxemia table, and section C05."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy sets the stage: thin pulmonary arteries with little muscle behave passively, bronchial arteries protect against infarction, and a pericardium of limited volume underlies ventricular interdependence and tamponade. Physiology supplies the four tools: Ohm's law with recruitment and distension, Starling forces with the lymphatic safety margin, transmural pressure in spontaneous and positive pressure breathing, and the oxyhemoglobin curve in V/Q mismatch and shunt. At the molecular level, hypoxia closes Kv channels in pulmonary smooth muscle, opens KATP channels in systemic arterioles, and through HIF links chronic hypoxia to remodeling and polycythemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows the series arrangement. Left heart failure moves upstream as hydrostatic edema and eventually loads the right ventricle, lung disease moves downstream as cor pulmonale, and congenital shunts can remodel the lung until they reverse. These models are simplifications: pressures vary between texts and with posture, the three-zone model ignores variation within horizontal levels, PCWP overestimates left ventricular filling in mitral stenosis, and real patients often combine oxygen-responsive low V/Q regions with shunt that does not respond."}],"summary":["The two circuits carry the same flow in series, but the pulmonary pressure drop is about 10 mmHg against about 90 mmHg, so PVR is about one ninth of systemic resistance.","Rising flow recruits closed capillaries and distends open ones, so PVR falls in exercise; PVR is lowest near FRC.","Low alveolar PO2 closes Kv channels and depolarizes pulmonary artery smooth muscle, which constricts; systemic arterioles dilate through adenosine and KATP channels.","Regional HPV preserves V/Q matching, but global hypoxia raises PVR and, through HIF-driven remodeling and polycythemia, causes fixed pulmonary hypertension.","Spontaneous inspiration raises right heart filling, delays P2, and slightly lowers left stroke volume and systolic pressure; pulsus paradoxus is a fall above 10 mmHg, as in tamponade or severe asthma.","Positive pressure ventilation lowers venous return and cardiac output, most in hypovolemia, while lowering left ventricular afterload.","Hydrostatic pulmonary edema appears when capillary pressure exceeds plasma oncotic pressure (about 28 mmHg); permeability edema forms at a normal PCWP.","Cor pulmonale is right ventricular hypertrophy or failure from lung or pulmonary vascular disease, not from left heart disease; massive embolism can start a fatal ischemic spiral.","Upright, V/Q falls from about 3 at the apex to about 0.6 at the base, and zone 1 appears only with low arterial or high alveolar pressure.","Shunt hypoxemia barely improves with 100% oxygen, and pulmonary ACE makes angiotensin II and inactivates bradykinin."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Principles of Gas Exchange; Diffusion of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Through the Respiratory Membrane (ventilation-perfusion ratio)"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Respiratory physiology: pulmonary circulation and ventilation-perfusion matching"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: lungs, pulmonary and bronchial vessels, pericardium"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (cor pulmonale) and The Lung (pulmonary edema, embolism, pulmonary hypertension)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease; Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-16":{"unit":"CV-16","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01","title":"Immune Responses in Cardiovascular Disease","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter treats the heart and blood vessels as targets of the immune system. It begins with atherosclerosis as chronic inflammation of the arterial intima, then covers rheumatic fever as molecular mimicry, the vasculitides grouped by immune effector, autoimmune disease of the pericardium, valves, and AV node, heart transplant rejection, myocarditis, and two tumors explained by immunity and cytokines (Kaposi sarcoma and atrial myxoma). Board questions give a patient, biopsy, serologic pattern, or time course and ask for the mechanism: preformed antibody, immune complexes, ANCA, T cells and macrophages, or failed immune surveillance. Placing each disease in its hypersensitivity type explains its timing, histology, and complement and antibody findings.","prerequisites":["Innate and adaptive immunity: neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, CD4 and CD8 T cells, B cells and antibody classes","The four hypersensitivity types (I to IV) and the classical, lectin, and alternative complement pathways","MHC class I and class II antigen presentation and HLA in self versus nonself recognition","Histology of the arterial wall and heart wall (CV-2) and lipoprotein metabolism with the LDL receptor pathway (CV-13)","Conduction system anatomy and AV block patterns on the ECG (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Inflammatory thinning of the fibrous cap is the event that converts a stable plaque into the ruptured plaque and thrombus of acute coronary syndromes."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"The vasculitides are classified there by vessel size; this chapter adds the immune mechanism that explains their serology and biopsy findings."},{"to":"Cardiovascular pathogens (CV-17)","how":"Group A streptococci, coxsackievirus, hepatitis B and C viruses, and HHV-8 are the infectious triggers of the immune diseases taught here."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Maternal anti-Ro antibodies injure the fetal AV node, producing complete heart block with a junctional escape rhythm."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E)","how":"Viral myocarditis is a major acquired cause of dilated cardiomyopathy, and eosinophilic myocarditis can progress to restrictive endomyocardial fibrosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host antigen."},{"term":"Foam cell","def":"A macrophage (or smooth muscle cell) whose cytoplasm is filled with droplets of cholesteryl ester derived from ingested modified LDL."},{"term":"Scavenger receptor","def":"A macrophage surface receptor, such as SR-A or CD36, that internalizes oxidized or otherwise modified LDL without feedback inhibition by cell cholesterol."},{"term":"Inflammasome","def":"A cytoplasmic protein complex (for example NLRP3 with ASC and caspase-1) that cleaves pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta in response to danger signals."},{"term":"Aschoff body","def":"The diagnostic lesion of acute rheumatic carditis: a focus of fibrinoid change surrounded by T lymphocytes, plasma cells, and activated macrophages (Anitschkow cells)."},{"term":"Immune complex","def":"A circulating or locally formed aggregate of antigen and antibody that can deposit in vessel walls and activate complement (type III hypersensitivity)."},{"term":"ANCA","def":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, autoantibodies against neutrophil granule enzymes, mainly proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO)."},{"term":"Pauci-immune","def":"Describes vasculitis or glomerulonephritis in which immunofluorescence shows few or no immunoglobulin and complement deposits, typical of ANCA disease."},{"term":"Allorecognition","def":"Recognition by recipient T cells of donor HLA molecules, either intact on donor cells (direct) or as processed peptides on recipient antigen-presenting cells (indirect)."},{"term":"C4d","def":"A stable fragment of complement C4 that binds covalently to tissue where the classical pathway was activated, used as a tissue marker of antibody-mediated injury."},{"term":"Cardiac allograft vasculopathy","def":"Chronic rejection of a transplanted heart, seen as diffuse concentric intimal thickening of the graft coronary arteries."},{"term":"Latency (viral)","def":"Persistence of a viral genome in a host cell with expression of only a few viral genes and no production of new virions."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C01","obj":"CV-16.1","title":"Atherosclerosis as chronic inflammation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Atherosclerosis is a chronic inflammatory and healing response of the intima of large and medium arteries to endothelial injury, sustained by LDL retained in the wall. Inflammation explains how plaques start, grow, and rupture."},{"type":"p","text":"Healthy endothelium, maintained by laminar shear stress, releases nitric oxide (NO) and prostacyclin and displays few adhesion molecules. High LDL, hypertension, smoking, hyperglycemia, and disturbed flow at branch points cause endothelial dysfunction: the still intact endothelium becomes more permeable to LDL, makes less NO, and activates NF-kappa B. This is why plaques favor the coronary and renal ostia and the carotid and aortic bifurcations."},{"type":"h","text":"Recruitment and foam cells"},{"type":"p","text":"NF-kappa B induces selectins, which make leukocytes roll, and vascular cell adhesion molecule 1 (VCAM-1), which binds VLA-4 on monocytes and T cells. Because VCAM-1 is selective for mononuclear cells, early lesions contain monocytes and lymphocytes rather than neutrophils. Monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 (MCP-1) draws them into the intima, where M-CSF turns monocytes into macrophages. The T cells are mainly Th1 cells, whose IFN-gamma activates macrophages."},{"type":"p","text":"LDL trapped on intimal proteoglycans is oxidized. Oxidized LDL no longer fits the LDL receptor, which the SREBP pathway shuts down when cell cholesterol rises. It enters macrophages instead through scavenger receptors (SR-A, CD36), which are not down-regulated by cell cholesterol, so uptake has no brake. ACAT esterifies the excess cholesterol into droplets, creating foam cells, and efflux to HDL through ABCA1 and ABCG1 is the main exit. Foam cells form the fatty streak, already present in many children."},{"type":"h","text":"IL-1 beta, CRP, and the fibrous cap"},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidized LDL acting on Toll-like receptors primes macrophages to make pro-IL-1 beta and NLRP3. Ingested cholesterol crystals then damage lysosomes and trigger the NLRP3 inflammasome, whose caspase-1 releases active IL-1 beta. IL-1 beta recruits more monocytes and induces IL-6, which makes the liver produce CRP, so high-sensitivity CRP reflects plaque inflammation (below 1 mg/L lower risk, 1 to 3 mg/L average, above 3 mg/L higher). The same cytokines accelerate atherosclerosis in rheumatoid arthritis and lupus."},{"type":"p","text":"PDGF from macrophages, endothelium, and platelets draws medial smooth muscle cells into the intima, and TGF-beta makes them lay down collagen, forming a cap over a necrotic core of lipid, cholesterol clefts, debris, and tissue factor. Inflammation undoes this repair: macrophage matrix metalloproteinases (MMP-1, MMP-8, MMP-13, MMP-9) and cathepsins degrade collagen, Th1 IFN-gamma blocks its synthesis, and smooth muscle cells undergo apoptosis. Rupture usually begins at the macrophage-rich shoulders and exposes tissue factor, and thrombus forms within minutes. A minority of thrombi form over plaque erosion, where endothelium is lost over an intact plaque."},{"type":"flow","title":"Atheroma formation as a chronic inflammatory response","steps":["Endothelial dysfunction from LDL, hypertension, smoking, hyperglycemia, disturbed flow","NF-kappa B induces selectins, VCAM-1, and MCP-1","Monocytes and Th1 cells enter the intima","Retained LDL is oxidized","Scavenger receptor uptake creates foam cells (fatty streak)","PDGF and TGF-beta build a smooth muscle and collagen cap","Metalloproteinases and IFN-gamma thin the cap","Rupture exposes tissue factor and thrombus forms"],"caption":"Mononuclear cells start the lesion and macrophage enzymes decide whether it ruptures."},{"type":"table","title":"Stable versus vulnerable plaque","cols":["Feature","Stable plaque","Vulnerable plaque"],"rows":[["Fibrous cap","Thick, collagen-rich","Thin (classically under about 65 micrometers)"],["Lipid core","Small","Large"],["Macrophages and T cells","Few","Many, especially at the shoulders"],["Smooth muscle cells","Abundant","Sparse, apoptotic"],["Usual stenosis","Often severe","Often only mild to moderate"],["Typical result","Stable angina","Rupture, thrombosis, acute coronary syndrome"]],"caption":"Composition and inflammation, more than size, decide whether a plaque ruptures."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One coronary plaque narrows the lumen 40% and has a large lipid core under a thin, macrophage-rich cap; another narrows it 85% under a thick collagenous cap. Which is more likely to cause an infarction?","explain":"The 40% lesion: metalloproteinases and IFN-gamma thin its cap, so it is likely to rupture and expose tissue factor. The 85% lesion more likely causes stable angina.","check":"Compare with the plaque table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C02","obj":"CV-16.2","title":"Acute rheumatic fever and molecular mimicry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is an immune-mediated multisystem disease that follows group A streptococcal (Streptococcus pyogenes) pharyngitis, usually in children of about 5 to 15 years, after a latent period of about 2 to 3 weeks. The organism is absent from the heart lesions. Risk is higher with certain HLA class II alleles and rheumatogenic strains. ARF follows throat infection, not impetigo, whereas poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows either, because it is caused by immune complexes rather than mimicry."},{"type":"p","text":"The M protein is an alpha-helical coiled coil like cardiac myosin and tropomyosin, and antibodies to the N-acetylglucosamine of the group A carbohydrate cross-react with valve laminin. Antibodies and T cells raised against these bacterial antigens therefore recognize heart proteins (molecular mimicry). The latent period is the time needed to build this adaptive response. Cross-reactive antibody activates valve endothelium and raises VCAM-1, letting CD4 T cells enter the valve and myocardium and sustain inflammation with IFN-gamma and IL-17. ARF is classed as type II hypersensitivity, but the chronic valve damage is driven largely by T cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rheumatic fever by molecular mimicry","steps":["Group A streptococcal pharyngitis in a susceptible child","Antibodies and T cells form against M protein and group A carbohydrate","About 2 to 3 weeks later they cross-react with myosin and valve proteins","Activated valve endothelium admits CD4 T cells","Pancarditis with Aschoff bodies and verrucae on closure lines","Repeated attacks heal by fibrosis: chronic mitral stenosis"],"caption":"Carditis begins after the organism is gone."},{"type":"h","text":"Pathology and laboratory evidence"},{"type":"p","text":"The Aschoff body is fibrinoid collagen surrounded by T cells, plasma cells, and Anitschkow cells, activated macrophages with a wavy ribbon of chromatin (caterpillar cells). Aschoff bodies occur in all three layers, so carditis is a pancarditis. Fibrinous pericarditis resolves; myocarditis can cause heart failure and is the main cause of acute death; valvulitis, mitral far more than aortic, leaves small (about 1 to 2 mm) verrucae along the lines of closure. Repeated attacks heal by fibrosis with leaflet thickening, commissural fusion, and short fused chordae, producing fish-mouth mitral stenosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Throat cultures are often negative by then, so recent infection is shown serologically. Antistreptolysin O (ASO) rises after about one week and peaks at about 3 to 6 weeks; anti-DNase B peaks later and persists longer, which helps when chorea appears late. A rising titer is stronger evidence than a single value."},{"type":"table","title":"Revised Jones criteria (AHA 2015), low-risk populations","cols":["Criterion","Type","Mechanism and features"],"rows":[["Carditis","Major","Pancarditis; new murmur (usually mitral regurgitation) or echocardiographic only"],["Polyarthritis","Major","Migratory large-joint arthritis that heals without deformity"],["Sydenham chorea","Major","Antibodies cross-react with basal ganglia neurons; appears months later"],["Erythema marginatum","Major","Evanescent pink rings with clear centers on trunk and proximal limbs"],["Subcutaneous nodules","Major","Painless nodules over bony prominences and tendons; linked to carditis"],["Polyarthralgia","Minor","Joint pain without objective arthritis"],["Fever","Minor","38.5 degrees C (101.3 degrees F) or higher"],["Acute phase reactants","Minor","ESR 60 mm/h or higher, or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or higher"],["Prolonged PR interval","Minor","Inflammation slows AV nodal conduction; not counted if carditis is a major criterion"]],"caption":"An initial attack needs evidence of streptococcal infection plus two major, or one major and two minor, criteria. Higher-risk populations use lower thresholds (fever 38.0 degrees C (100.4 degrees F), ESR 30 mm/h, monoarthritis or polyarthralgia as major)."},{"type":"p","text":"Chorea and indolent carditis may appear after titers fall and can support the diagnosis alone. Recurrences after new streptococcal infections are common, and each adds valve scarring."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does rheumatic fever appear weeks after the sore throat, and why do the valves scar permanently while the joints heal?","explain":"Cross-reactive antibodies and T cells take about 2 to 3 weeks to develop. Valve inflammation heals by fibrosis, whereas the arthritis is a transient exudative synovitis that does not scar.","check":"Compare with the mimicry and pathology paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C03","obj":"CV-16.3","title":"Vasculitis classified by immune mechanism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Grouping vasculitides by immune mechanism, rather than by vessel size (CV-18-I), explains their serology, complement levels, and immunofluorescence."},{"type":"h","text":"Immune complex vasculitis (type III)"},{"type":"p","text":"Small complexes formed in slight antigen excess escape clearance and deposit where flow is turbulent or filtered, as in glomeruli and dermal venules. They activate the classical pathway, and C5a recruits neutrophils whose enzymes and oxidants cause fibrinoid necrosis. Complement may fall, biopsy shows granular immunoglobulin and complement, and nuclear dust from dying neutrophils gives leukocytoclastic vasculitis. In dermal venules this produces palpable purpura: raised, nonblanching lesions on dependent skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) affects medium and small muscular arteries, in some cases through hepatitis B antigen-antibody complexes. Segmental lesions of different ages and microaneurysms involve renal arteries (infarcts, renin-dependent hypertension, no glomerulonephritis), mesenteric arteries, and vasa nervorum (mononeuritis multiplex), sparing the lungs. IgA vasculitis often follows a respiratory infection in children: complexes of galactose-deficient IgA1 deposit in skin, gut, joints, and mesangium, and because IgA uses the alternative and lectin pathways, complement stays normal. In mixed cryoglobulinemia, usually from hepatitis C driving clonal B cells, IgM rheumatoid factor binds IgG; these complexes precipitate below 37 degrees C (98.6 degrees F) and consume the classical pathway, so C4 is very low."},{"type":"p","text":"In anti-GBM disease antibody binds fixed type IV collagen, so immunofluorescence is linear."},{"type":"h","text":"ANCA vasculitis and granulomatous T-cell vasculitis"},{"type":"p","text":"ANCA are IgG against neutrophil PR3 (c-ANCA, cytoplasmic) or MPO (p-ANCA, perinuclear, because ethanol fixation lets cationic MPO move to the nucleus). Infection, TNF, or C5a primes neutrophils and moves these enzymes to the surface. ANCA bind them by Fab and engage Fc-gamma receptors on the same cell, which then releases oxidants, proteases, and extracellular traps onto endothelium and activates the alternative pathway, generating more C5a. The neutrophil delivers the injury, so lesions are pauci-immune and complement is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"In giant cell and Takayasu arteritis, adventitial dendritic cells activate CD4 T cells; IFN-gamma recruits macrophages and giant cells to the media around a fragmented internal elastic lamina, and intimal hyperplasia narrows the lumen. There are no deposits, and IL-6 drives a very high ESR and CRP. Giant cell arteritis involves cranial carotid branches and the vertebral arteries: jaw claudication, temporal headache, and blindness from posterior ciliary artery occlusion."},{"type":"table","title":"Vasculitis by immune mechanism","cols":["Disease","Mechanism","Marker","Complement and deposits","Key features"],"rows":[["Polyarteritis nodosa","Type III","Hepatitis B in some; ANCA negative","May be low","Renal and mesenteric infarcts, mononeuritis multiplex; lungs spared"],["IgA vasculitis","Type III","IgA1 complexes","Normal; granular IgA","Child after URI; purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain, nephritis"],["Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Type III","IgM rheumatoid factor; hepatitis C","Very low C4; granular","Purpura, arthralgia, membranoproliferative GN, neuropathy"],["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","ANCA","PR3-ANCA","Normal; pauci-immune","Granulomas of sinuses, nose, lungs; crescentic GN"],["Microscopic polyangiitis","ANCA","MPO-ANCA","Normal; pauci-immune","No granulomas; pulmonary hemorrhage; crescentic GN"],["Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","ANCA plus eosinophils (IL-5)","MPO-ANCA in about 40%","Pauci-immune","Asthma, eosinophilia, neuropathy, eosinophilic myocarditis"],["Giant cell arteritis","Type IV granulomatous","Very high ESR","No deposits","Over 50; headache, jaw claudication, vision loss, polymyalgia rheumatica"],["Takayasu arteritis","Type IV granulomatous","High ESR","No deposits","Under 40, often women; weak arm pulses, arm blood pressure difference"]],"caption":"Granular deposits mean immune complexes; few or none mean ANCA or T-cell disease."},{"type":"example","title":"Using complement to identify a vasculitis","text":"Three adults have palpable purpura and red cell casts. A has very low C4, rheumatoid factor, and hepatitis C. B has normal complement, MPO-ANCA, and hemoptysis. C, aged 19, had a recent cold, normal complement, and IgA deposits. Step 1: A and C have deposited complexes; B has pauci-immune injury. Step 2: low C4 in A means classical pathway consumption by IgM-IgG complexes (mixed cryoglobulinemia); IgA spares C4 (IgA vasculitis in C). Step 3: MPO-ANCA without sinus disease favors microscopic polyangiitis; PR3-ANCA with cavitating nodules would mean GPA."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C04","obj":"CV-16.4","title":"Immune-mediated pericardial, valvular, and conduction disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each condition here reaches the heart by a different immune route: antigens exposed by injury (Dressler syndrome), systemic autoimmunity (Libman-Sacks endocarditis), or maternal antibody crossing the placenta (congenital heart block)."},{"type":"h","text":"Dressler syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Early pericarditis within about 1 to 3 days of a transmural infarct is a local fibrinous reaction to necrosis. Dressler syndrome appears weeks to months later and is autoimmune: necrosis releases sequestered myocardial proteins, antibodies form against them, and immune complexes are thought to deposit on pericardium and pleura. The delay is the time to mount this response. Patients have fever, pleuritic pain relieved by sitting forward, a friction rub, effusions, and high ESR and CRP, with diffuse ST elevation and PR depression rather than regional changes. Post-pericardiotomy syndrome has the same mechanism."},{"type":"h","text":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis"},{"type":"p","text":"In lupus, immune complex and complement injury of valve endothelium, aided by antiphospholipid antibodies that promote thrombosis, produces sterile verrucous vegetations of fibrin, platelets, and mononuclear cells, sometimes with hematoxylin bodies. They can sit on either leaflet surface, mitral most often, and heal by fibrosis that causes regurgitation; fragments can embolize to the brain."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing endocardial vegetations","cols":["Feature","Rheumatic fever","Libman-Sacks (SLE)","Infective","Nonbacterial thrombotic"],"rows":[["Cause","Molecular mimicry","Immune complexes, antiphospholipid antibodies","Bacteria or fungi","Hypercoagulable state, often cancer"],["Size","Small (1 to 2 mm)","Small to medium (1 to 4 mm)","Large, friable","Small to medium"],["Location","Lines of closure","Either or both surfaces","Upstream surface of closure line","Lines of closure"],["Organisms","Absent","Absent","Present","Absent"],["Valve outcome","Fibrosis after repeated attacks","Fibrosis, regurgitation","Destruction, perforation, abscess","Little damage; bland emboli"]],"caption":"Only infective vegetations contain organisms; position on the leaflet separates the sterile types."},{"type":"h","text":"Congenital heart block"},{"type":"p","text":"In neonatal lupus, maternal IgG against Ro (SSA), often with La (SSB), crosses the placenta on the neonatal Fc receptor from the second trimester. The mother may have lupus or Sjogren syndrome or no symptoms. Antibody binds Ro and La exposed on apoptotic fetal myocytes, and macrophages release TNF and TGF-beta that drive fibrosis and calcification of the AV node at about 18 to 24 weeks. Anti-Ro also inhibits L-type and T-type Ca2+ channels, and since AV nodal cells depolarize by Ca2+ current, conduction slows even before scarring."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of congenital heart block","steps":["Mother has IgG anti-Ro, often with anti-La","FcRn carries IgG across the placenta from the second trimester","Antibody binds apoptotic fetal myocytes and inhibits Ca2+ channels","Macrophages release TNF and TGF-beta","Fibrosis and calcification replace the AV node","Permanent complete heart block with a slow escape rhythm"],"caption":"Maternal antibody starts the injury; fetal fibrosis makes it permanent."},{"type":"p","text":"The atria beat normally while the ventricles follow a slow escape rhythm, below the normal fetal rate of about 110 to 160 bpm. Risk is about 1 to 2% per anti-Ro positive pregnancy and about 15 to 20% after an affected child. The rash, cytopenias, and hepatitis of neonatal lupus resolve over about 6 to 8 months as maternal IgG is cleared, but the fibrosed AV node cannot regenerate, so the block is permanent."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has a ventricular rate of 55 bpm with dissociated P waves at 140 per minute and an annular facial rash; the mother is well. At 8 months the rash has gone but the bradycardia persists. Why?","explain":"Maternal anti-Ro IgG fibrosed the fetal AV node, causing complete heart block with an escape rhythm. The same antibody caused the rash, which cleared as the IgG was catabolized, but nodal scar cannot regenerate.","check":"Compare with the congenital heart block flow figure and the last paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C05","obj":"CV-16.5","title":"Heart transplant rejection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every nucleated donor cell displays donor HLA. Rejection type is defined by the effector, whose speed sets the timing."},{"type":"p","text":"In direct allorecognition, recipient T cells recognize intact donor HLA on donor dendritic cells (passenger leukocytes) carried in the graft. Alloreactive T cells are numerous, so this drives the strong early response. In indirect allorecognition, recipient antigen-presenting cells present processed donor HLA peptides on recipient class II molecules. It persists after passenger leukocytes are gone and gives B cells the CD4 help to make donor-specific antibody, so it matters in antibody-mediated and chronic rejection."},{"type":"h","text":"The four types"},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperacute rejection, within minutes, is caused by preformed anti-ABO IgM or anti-HLA IgG from prior pregnancy, transfusion, or transplant. Antibody binds endothelium, activates the classical pathway, and small vessels thrombose. ABO matching and crossmatch screening have made it rare."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute cellular rejection, the most common, occurs mainly in the first year because naive T cells need costimulation, IL-2, and clonal expansion. CD8 T cells kill myocytes with perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand, and Th1 IFN-gamma activates macrophages and raises graft HLA expression. Endomyocardial biopsy shows perivascular and interstitial lymphocytes with myocyte damage. It is often silent."},{"type":"p","text":"Antibody-mediated rejection is caused by donor-specific anti-HLA antibody binding capillary endothelium and activating complement. C4d, which binds covalently where C4 was cleaved, marks the capillaries, and biopsy shows swollen endothelium and intravascular macrophages with few interstitial lymphocytes. Widespread microvascular injury causes more hemodynamic compromise than cellular rejection of similar grade."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (CAV), the chronic form, is diffuse concentric intimal thickening along epicardial and intramyocardial arteries, with an intact internal elastic lamina and little lipid or calcium, unlike focal eccentric atherosclerosis. Immune injury (indirect allorecognition, IFN-gamma, donor-specific antibody) and nonimmune injury (ischemia-reperfusion, cytomegalovirus, hyperlipidemia, hypertension) make endothelium release PDGF and TGF-beta, driving smooth muscle into the intima. Diffuse narrowing leaves no normal reference segment, so angiography underestimates it."},{"type":"p","text":"The graft is denervated. Without vagal tone the resting rate is the intrinsic SA nodal rate, about 90 to 110 bpm, and exercise raises it slowly through circulating catecholamines. Without sensory afferents, ischemia causes no angina, so CAV presents as heart failure, arrhythmia, silent infarction, or sudden death."},{"type":"table","title":"Heart transplant rejection by mechanism and timing","cols":["Type","Timing","Effector","Hypersensitivity","Graft findings"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes to hours","Preformed anti-ABO or anti-HLA antibody","II","Small vessel thrombosis, necrosis"],["Acute cellular","Weeks to months","CD8 and Th1 T cells","IV","Lymphocytes with myocyte damage"],["Antibody-mediated","Days to years","Donor-specific anti-HLA antibody","II","Endothelial swelling, capillary C4d"],["Allograft vasculopathy","Months to years","T cells, antibody, nonimmune injury","Mixed","Diffuse concentric intimal thickening"]],"caption":"Preformed antibody acts at once, T cells need weeks to expand, and vessel remodeling takes years."},{"type":"example","title":"Matching a biopsy to the type of rejection","text":"Recipient 1, at 3 weeks, has lymphocytes around vessels and between damaged myocytes without C4d. Recipient 2, at 5 months, is hypotensive, with swollen capillary endothelium, intravascular macrophages, strong C4d, and new anti-HLA antibody. Recipient 3, at 6 years, has painless heart failure and smooth diffuse narrowing of all coronary branches. Step 1: recipient 1 has T-cell infiltration timed to clonal expansion, so acute cellular rejection. Step 2: recipient 2 has complement-marked microvascular injury with donor-specific antibody, so antibody-mediated rejection. Step 3: recipient 3 has allograft vasculopathy, painless because the graft is denervated. Graft thrombosis within minutes would instead mean hyperacute rejection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C06","obj":"CV-16.6","title":"Myocarditis: viral, hypersensitivity, and post-vaccine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myocarditis is myocardial inflammation with myocyte injury not explained by ischemia. Because biopsy (Dallas criteria: infiltrate with nonischemic myocyte damage) can miss patchy disease, MRI is used; late gadolinium enhancement is subepicardial or midwall, unlike the subendocardial start of infarction. Nonviral infectious causes are taught in CV-17."},{"type":"h","text":"Viral myocarditis in three phases"},{"type":"p","text":"Coxsackievirus B is the classic cause; adenovirus, parvovirus B19 (mainly endothelial), HHV-6, influenza, and SARS-CoV-2 are others. Coxsackievirus enters myocytes through the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor (CAR) with CD55. In phase 1 (days), viral protease 2A cleaves dystrophin and kills myocytes while type I interferons, NK cells, and macrophages limit spread. In phase 2 (from about 1 to 2 weeks), CD8 T cells kill infected myocytes and CD4 T cells release IFN-gamma and TNF; this clears virus but causes most of the muscle loss, and released myosin with molecular mimicry can trigger autoimmunity. Most patients recover; in phase 3 a minority develop dilated cardiomyopathy from persistent virus or autoimmunity."},{"type":"flow","title":"From viral infection to dilated cardiomyopathy","steps":["Coxsackievirus B enters myocytes through CAR and CD55","Protease 2A cleaves dystrophin; myocytes die (days)","Interferons, NK cells, and macrophages limit spread","CD8 T cells kill infected myocytes (from 1 to 2 weeks)","Released myosin and mimicry trigger autoimmunity in some","Persistent inflammation causes fibrosis and remodeling","Dilated cardiomyopathy in a minority (months to years)"],"caption":"Immune killing, more than the virus itself, causes most myocyte loss."},{"type":"p","text":"A viral prodrome often precedes cardiac symptoms. Necrosis raises troponin, myocardial and pericardial inflammation cause chest pain and ST changes outside a single coronary territory, muscle loss causes heart failure, and patchy scar supports reentry, so ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death occur, sometimes in young athletes. Fulminant myocarditis can recover substantially because many myocytes are dysfunctional rather than dead."},{"type":"h","text":"Other forms"},{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity myocarditis is a dose-independent type IV drug reaction in which Th2 cytokines such as IL-5 recruit eosinophils, often with rash, fever, and eosinophilia. In hypereosinophilic syndrome and EGPA, eosinophil major basic protein and cationic protein kill myocytes and injure endocardium, leading to endomyocardial fibrosis (Loffler endocarditis). Myocarditis after mRNA COVID-19 vaccination clusters in young males after the second dose, begins within days, and is usually mild; its mechanism is not established."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing forms of myocarditis","cols":["Form","Trigger","Effector and histology","Course"],"rows":[["Viral","Coxsackievirus B, others","CD8 T cells; lymphocytes with necrosis","Most recover; some dilated cardiomyopathy"],["Hypersensitivity","Sulfonamides, beta-lactams, methyldopa, clozapine","T cells and eosinophils; limited necrosis","Subsides after exposure ends"],["Hypereosinophilic","Hypereosinophilic syndrome, EGPA, parasites","Eosinophil granule proteins; necrosis, mural thrombus","Endomyocardial fibrosis, restrictive physiology"],["Giant cell","Autoimmunity, thymoma","T cells, giant cells, wide necrosis, no granulomas","Rapid heart failure, arrhythmias, poor prognosis"],["Post mRNA vaccine","Second dose, young males","Not established; subepicardial MRI enhancement","Onset in days, usually self-limited"]],"caption":"The effector cell on histology predicts the course."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-16-01-C07","obj":"CV-16.7","title":"Immune surveillance, Kaposi sarcoma, and the cytokines of atrial myxoma","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Kaposi sarcoma and HHV-8"},{"type":"p","text":"Kaposi sarcoma (KS) is a low-grade vascular tumor caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), a gamma-herpesvirus spread mainly by saliva and also sexually. HHV-8 latently infects spindle cells of lymphatic endothelial type. LANA keeps the viral episome and inhibits p53 and Rb, v-cyclin pairs with CDK6 to drive G1 to S, and v-FLIP activates NF-kappa B and blocks apoptosis. Lytic genes add paracrine growth signals: a viral G protein-coupled receptor induces VEGF, and viral IL-6 signals through gp130 without the IL-6 receptor alpha chain. Lesions are spindle cells with slit-like vascular spaces, extravasated red cells, and hemosiderin, progressing from patches to plaques to nodules in skin, oral mucosa, gut, nodes, and lungs; LANA staining confirms HHV-8. The virus also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease."},{"type":"p","text":"CD8 T cells and NK cells normally hold HHV-8 in check, and CD8 responses need CD4 help. In AIDS, CD4 loss lets infected cells proliferate, and HIV Tat further stimulates them, so AIDS-associated KS is the most aggressive and AIDS-defining. After transplantation, drugs such as calcineurin inhibitors block T-cell activation. Classic KS (older men of Mediterranean or Eastern European descent) is indolent on the lower legs, and endemic African KS can involve lymph nodes in children. Early lesions depend on viral and cytokine signals, so restoring CD4 counts or reducing immunosuppression often makes them regress, after a possible transient flare from immune reconstitution; advanced monoclonal lesions may not."},{"type":"h","text":"Atrial myxoma and IL-6"},{"type":"p","text":"Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults (rhabdomyoma in children; metastases outnumber both). About 90% arise in the atria, roughly 80% in the left atrium, as a pedunculated gelatinous mass on the septum near the fossa ovalis, with stellate cells in a glycosaminoglycan-rich matrix. Most are sporadic in middle-aged women; familial cases in Carney complex (PRKAR1A) are younger, multiple, and accompanied by spotty pigmentation and endocrine tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"The mass can prolapse into the mitral orifice in diastole, causing positional dyspnea or syncope and a tumor plop, and friable fragments embolize systemically. The systemic picture comes from IL-6 secreted by myxoma cells, and it resolves after removal."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a myxoma causes constitutional symptoms","steps":["Myxoma cells secrete IL-6","Hypothalamus: COX-2 and PGE2 raise the set point (fever)","Liver: CRP, fibrinogen (high ESR), and hepcidin (anemia of inflammation)","B cells: polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia","Catabolism and anorexia: weight loss and fatigue"],"caption":"One cytokine lets a benign tumor mimic infection, vasculitis, or malignancy."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Organize these diseases by immune effector. Antibody against fixed tissue antigen (type II) explains hyperacute and antibody-mediated rejection, anti-GBM disease, and much of rheumatic fever. Immune complexes (type III) explain PAN, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia, Libman-Sacks lesions, and probably Dressler syndrome, leaving granular deposits and, with classical pathway use, low complement. ANCA disease uses the neutrophil and leaves few deposits. T cells and macrophages (type IV) explain large-vessel arteritis, acute cellular rejection, the immune phase of viral myocarditis, and hypersensitivity myocarditis. Atherosclerosis and allograft vasculopathy share endothelial injury and cytokine-driven intimal smooth muscle growth. Many diseases use more than one effector, as rheumatic carditis and allograft vasculopathy show."},{"type":"p","text":"The interval after a trigger points to the mechanism: direct infection acts in days, a primary adaptive response in about 1 to 3 weeks, and chronic remodeling over months to years. Anatomy predicts manifestations: inflamed venules leak into purpura, glomeruli produce casts, renal arteries cause renin-dependent hypertension, and a Ca2+-dependent AV node is vulnerable to anti-Ro. IL-1 beta and IL-6 link plaque inflammation to hs-CRP, giant cell arteritis to a very high ESR, and myxoma to fever and anemia."}],"summary":["Endothelial dysfunction activates NF-kappa B, and VCAM-1 and MCP-1 recruit monocytes and Th1 cells into the intima.","Oxidized LDL enters macrophages through scavenger receptors that cholesterol does not down-regulate; cholesterol crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, and IL-1 beta and IL-6 raise CRP.","Macrophage metalloproteinases degrade cap collagen and IFN-gamma blocks its synthesis, so inflamed, often moderately stenotic plaques rupture.","Rheumatic fever follows streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 3 weeks through mimicry of M protein and group A carbohydrate; Aschoff bodies define the pancarditis and repeated valvulitis scars into mitral stenosis.","Immune complex vasculitis leaves granular deposits, with very low C4 in hepatitis C cryoglobulinemia and normal complement in IgA vasculitis; ANCA vasculitis is pauci-immune.","Giant cell and Takayasu arteritis are granulomatous T-cell diseases of large arteries with IL-6 driven high ESR, separated mainly by age and distribution.","Dressler syndrome is delayed autoimmune pericarditis; Libman-Sacks vegetations are sterile and sit on either leaflet surface; maternal anti-Ro causes permanent fetal AV node fibrosis while the rest of neonatal lupus resolves.","Transplant rejection timing follows the effector: preformed antibody in minutes, T cells in weeks to months, donor-specific antibody with capillary C4d, and painless diffuse allograft vasculopathy over years.","In viral myocarditis CD8 T cells cause most myocyte loss, and persistent virus or autoimmunity can lead to dilated cardiomyopathy; eosinophilic forms cause endomyocardial fibrosis.","Kaposi sarcoma grows when CD8 control of latent HHV-8 fails and often regresses when immunity returns; atrial myxoma IL-6 causes fever, weight loss, anemia, and a high ESR."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels (sections on atherosclerosis, vasculitis, and Kaposi sarcoma)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (sections on rheumatic heart disease, endocarditis, myocarditis, cardiac transplantation, and cardiac tumors)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: Disorders Caused by Immune Responses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Section on transplantation immunology and graft rejection"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on streptococci, enteroviruses, and herpesviruses"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"}]},"CV-17":{"unit":"CV-17","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01","title":"Cardiovascular Pathogens","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the organisms that infect the heart and great vessels: the bacteria of infective endocarditis, Trypanosoma cruzi, the tick-borne bacteria Borrelia burgdorferi and Rickettsia rickettsii, the hemorrhagic fever viruses, the myocarditis viruses, and three older infections that still appear on boards (diphtheria, tuberculosis, and syphilis). Board questions give a Gram stain, a bench test, a vector, or an exposure and ask for the organism, or name the organism and ask which structure or virulence factor explains the disease. If you can connect each organism's structure and life cycle to the tissue it damages, you can reason through most questions in this area.","prerequisites":["Gram stain, bacterial cell wall structure, and catalase and coagulase tests","Viral classification by genome type, strand sense, and envelope","Fc receptors, opsonization, complement, and type I interferon","Valve and conduction system anatomy (CV-4, CV-7)","Aortic wall histology and the vasa vasorum (CV-2)"],"connections":[{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"That chapter covers the vegetations, acute versus subacute course, and peripheral signs of endocarditis caused by the organisms described here."},{"to":"Immune responses in cardiovascular disease (CV-16)","how":"The T-cell phase of viral myocarditis and the immune basis of rheumatic fever continue the story of direct viral and bacterial injury told here."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Lyme disease, Chagas disease, and diphtheria all damage conduction tissue, so the AV and bundle branch blocks of CV-7 appear as their findings."},{"to":"Pericardial disease (CV-18-F)","how":"Tuberculous pericarditis is a leading cause of the constrictive physiology described in that chapter."},{"to":"Infectious vascular diseases (CV-18-K)","how":"Syphilitic aortitis and rickettsial vasculitis are vascular infections whose mechanisms are taught here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Vegetation","def":"A mass of platelets, fibrin, and microorganisms attached to a heart valve or the endocardium."},{"term":"Adhesin","def":"A microbial surface molecule that binds a host molecule and lets the organism attach to tissue."},{"term":"Coagulase","def":"A Staphylococcus aureus product that converts fibrinogen to fibrin, either by activating prothrombin (free coagulase) or by binding fibrinogen on the cell surface (bound coagulase, or clumping factor)."},{"term":"Protein A","def":"A S. aureus surface protein that binds the Fc region of IgG and so blocks opsonization and complement activation."},{"term":"Biofilm","def":"A community of microorganisms enclosed in a self-made polymer matrix and attached to a surface, which protects them from phagocytes, antibodies, and antibiotics."},{"term":"Fastidious organism","def":"An organism that grows only on enriched media or under special conditions, and usually slowly."},{"term":"Obligate intracellular organism","def":"An organism that can multiply only inside a host cell because it depends on host metabolites or energy."},{"term":"Trypomastigote","def":"The flagellated, extracellular form of Trypanosoma cruzi that circulates in blood and invades cells but does not divide in humans."},{"term":"Amastigote","def":"The rounded intracellular form of Trypanosoma cruzi, without an external flagellum, that multiplies by binary fission inside host cells."},{"term":"Antigenic variation","def":"Repeated change in an organism's surface antigens that keeps it ahead of the host's specific antibodies."},{"term":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","def":"Increased viral entry into Fc receptor-bearing cells when antibodies bind a virus without neutralizing it."},{"term":"Obliterative endarteritis","def":"Concentric thickening of the intima of small arteries that narrows or closes their lumen and makes the tissue they supply ischemic."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C01","obj":"CV-17.1","title":"The major endocarditis bacteria: morphology and virulence factors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infective endocarditis begins when organisms in the blood attach to a heart valve and multiply in a vegetation. Each organism's adhesins decide which valves it can colonize, and its toxins decide how fast the valve is destroyed. The four classic organisms are gram-positive cocci, separated by their arrangement on Gram stain, by catalase (made by staphylococci, not by streptococci or enterococci), and by coagulase."},{"type":"h","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococci divide in several planes and form grapelike clusters. S. aureus is catalase and coagulase positive. Bound coagulase (clumping factor) binds fibrinogen, and free coagulase activates prothrombin so that fibrin coats the organism and hinders phagocytosis. Clumping factor and fibronectin-binding proteins let S. aureus attach to fibrinogen and fibronectin on minimally injured or even normal endothelium and then enter endothelial cells. Protein A binds the Fc region of IgG, holding antibody in an orientation that cannot opsonize or fix complement. Pore-forming alpha toxin and tissue-degrading enzymes then destroy leaflets within days. These factors explain why S. aureus causes acute endocarditis on previously normal valves, why abscesses and perforation are common, and why it leads in people who inject drugs, in whom skin bacteria reach the tricuspid valve first."},{"type":"h","text":"Viridans streptococci"},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococci divide in one plane and form chains. Viridans streptococci (for example S. sanguinis, S. mutans, and S. mitis) are normal mouth flora with alpha hemolysis, a partial greenish hemolysis produced when their hydrogen peroxide oxidizes hemoglobin. They are optochin resistant and not bile soluble, which separates them from Streptococcus pneumoniae, the other alpha-hemolytic streptococcus. They invade poorly and need an abnormal valve. Turbulent flow across a scarred or congenitally abnormal valve injures the endothelium, and a sterile platelet-fibrin deposit forms. During the brief bacteremia of tooth brushing or dental work, strains that make dextran, a glucan polymer built from dietary sucrose, stick to this deposit and become buried in it, shielded from neutrophils. A slow-growing, weakly toxic organism gives subacute disease over weeks to months."},{"type":"h","text":"Enterococcus faecalis and Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"type":"p","text":"Enterococcus faecalis is a catalase-negative bowel commensal in pairs and short chains that carries the Lancefield group D antigen, grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile, and hydrolyzes esculin, all signs of a hardy organism. It reaches the blood from the urinary or gastrointestinal tract, often after instrumentation in older adults, and causes subacute endocarditis; surface adhesins and biofilm help it attach, and it is intrinsically resistant to many antibiotics. Staphylococcus epidermidis is a coagulase-negative, novobiocin-sensitive skin commensal of low virulence. On a prosthetic valve, pacemaker lead, or catheter it secretes a polysaccharide adhesin and builds biofilm that excludes neutrophils and antibodies. It is the classic cause of prosthetic valve endocarditis in the first year after surgery, but a single positive blood culture is often skin contamination."},{"type":"table","title":"Gram-positive cocci of infective endocarditis","cols":["Organism","Stain and bench tests","Key virulence factors","Typical setting and course"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Clusters; catalase positive, coagulase positive, beta-hemolytic","Clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins, protein A, alpha toxin","Normal valves, injection drug use (tricuspid); acute"],["Viridans streptococci","Chains; catalase negative, alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant, bile insoluble","Dextran binds platelet-fibrin deposits","Damaged valves after dental bacteremia; subacute"],["Enterococcus faecalis","Pairs and short chains; catalase negative; grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile","Adhesins, biofilm, intrinsic drug resistance","Urinary or bowel source, older adults; subacute"],["Staphylococcus epidermidis","Clusters; catalase positive, coagulase negative, novobiocin sensitive","Biofilm","Prosthetic valves and devices; indolent"]],"caption":"Arrangement and catalase split staphylococci from streptococci and enterococci; coagulase then separates S. aureus from S. epidermidis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can S. aureus infect a normal tricuspid valve when viridans streptococci rarely infect a normal valve?","explain":"S. aureus adhesins bind fibrinogen and fibronectin on minimally injured endothelium, and its toxins destroy tissue. Viridans streptococci depend on dextran binding to a platelet-fibrin deposit already present on a damaged valve.","check":"The S. aureus and viridans streptococci subsections."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C02","obj":"CV-17.2","title":"Culture-negative endocarditis and Streptococcus gallolyticus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Culture-negative endocarditis is endocarditis, by clinical and echocardiographic findings, with no growth in routine blood cultures. The most common reason is antibiotics given before cultures were drawn. The rest come from organisms that grow slowly, need special media, or live inside cells, and each has a recognizable exposure."},{"type":"h","text":"HACEK organisms"},{"type":"p","text":"HACEK names fastidious gram-negative rods and coccobacilli that are normal oropharyngeal flora: Haemophilus species (mainly H. parainfluenzae), Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus and some Haemophilus species), Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens, and Kingella kingae. They need enriched media and added CO2 and grow slowly, which is why they were classically culture-negative, although modern automated blood culture systems usually recover them within about 5 days. They enter the blood from the mouth, often with dental disease, and tend to form large, friable vegetations that embolize."},{"type":"h","text":"Coxiella burnetii and Bartonella"},{"type":"p","text":"Coxiella burnetii, the agent of Q fever, is a small gram-negative obligate intracellular bacterium that multiplies inside the acidic phagolysosome of macrophages. A spore-like small-cell form survives drying for long periods, so people are infected by inhaling dust or aerosols contaminated with the birth products (placenta, amniotic fluid) of cattle, sheep, and goats; no vector is needed. Acute infection is usually mild or causes pneumonia or hepatitis, but in people with damaged or prosthetic valves, vascular grafts, or weak immunity the organism persists and causes chronic endocarditis with small or absent vegetations. It does not grow in routine culture, so diagnosis rests on serology, with high antibody titers against phase I antigen marking chronic infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Bartonella species are fastidious, facultatively intracellular gram-negative rods. Bartonella quintana is carried by the human body louse and causes endocarditis in people experiencing homelessness or alcohol use disorder, and Bartonella henselae comes from cats through scratches or cat fleas. Diagnosis uses serology, PCR, or silver-stained valve tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of culture-negative endocarditis","cols":["Cause","Organism type","Source or exposure","Why routine cultures fail"],"rows":[["Prior antibiotics","Any organism","Antibiotics before blood cultures","Growth suppressed"],["HACEK group","Fastidious gram-negative rods","Mouth flora, dental disease","Slow growth; needs enriched media and CO2"],["Coxiella burnetii","Obligate intracellular gram-negative","Inhaled aerosols from livestock birth products","Does not grow in routine culture media"],["Bartonella quintana","Fastidious gram-negative rod","Body lice; homelessness","Very slow growth"],["Bartonella henselae","Fastidious gram-negative rod","Cats and cat fleas","Very slow growth"]],"caption":"Less common causes include Brucella, Tropheryma whipplei, Legionella, and fungi."},{"type":"h","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus and colon cancer"},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly Streptococcus bovis biotype I) is a group D streptococcus of the colon. Like Enterococcus it is bile-esculin positive, but unlike Enterococcus it does not grow in 6.5% NaCl. A colorectal adenoma or carcinoma disrupts the mucosal barrier and lets the organism into the blood, and it appears to colonize tumor tissue more readily than normal mucosa. A large share of patients with S. gallolyticus bacteremia or endocarditis have colorectal neoplasia, so isolating it calls for evaluation of the colon even without bowel symptoms. Whether the bacterium promotes tumor growth or only exploits the tumor is still debated."},{"type":"example","title":"Identifying a group D organism from the bench tests","text":"Blood cultures from an older adult with a new murmur grow gram-positive cocci in chains that are catalase negative and nonhemolytic. The isolate hydrolyzes esculin in the presence of bile but does not grow in 6.5% NaCl. Step 1: chains and a negative catalase test mean a streptococcus or an enterococcus. Step 2: growth on bile esculin means a group D organism, either Enterococcus or S. gallolyticus. Step 3: failure to grow in 6.5% NaCl excludes Enterococcus, so the organism is S. gallolyticus. Step 4: its source is the colon, and its presence suggests a colorectal tumor that should be looked for. Had it grown in salt, the answer would be Enterococcus, with a urinary or bowel source."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C03","obj":"CV-17.3","title":"Trypanosoma cruzi: life cycle, transmission, and Chagas disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Trypanosoma cruzi is a flagellated protozoan that causes Chagas disease (American trypanosomiasis). It is endemic in rural Mexico, Central America, and South America, where triatomine bugs (reduviid or kissing bugs) live in the cracks of poorly built houses. The bugs feed at night, often on the face, and defecate during or soon after the blood meal. The parasite is in the feces, not the saliva, so the bite itself does not transmit it."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Life cycle of Trypanosoma cruzi","steps":["Bug ingests blood trypomastigotes during a blood meal","In the bug midgut, epimastigotes multiply","In the hindgut, metacyclic trypomastigotes form and pass in feces","Feces rubbed into the bite wound, conjunctiva, or mucosa","Trypomastigotes invade host cells and become amastigotes","Amastigotes divide by binary fission in the cytoplasm","Amastigotes become trypomastigotes and the cell ruptures","Blood trypomastigotes infect new cells or a feeding bug"],"caption":"Multiplication occurs only as epimastigotes in the bug and as intracellular amastigotes in the human."},{"type":"p","text":"The two human forms divide the work. The trypomastigote is the flagellated extracellular form that circulates in blood and invades cells but does not divide, unlike the African trypanosomes, which multiply in blood. After entry the parasite escapes its vacuole into the cytoplasm and becomes an amastigote, a rounded form without an external flagellum that divides until it fills the cell. Amastigotes then change back into trypomastigotes and rupture the cell, so parasite numbers rise only inside host cells. Favored cells are macrophages, cardiac myocytes, smooth muscle, and neurons of the enteric plexuses. Entry through the conjunctiva gives painless swelling of one eyelid (Romaña sign), and entry through skin gives a local inflammatory nodule (chagoma)."},{"type":"p","text":"Because trypomastigotes circulate and amastigotes persist in tissue for life, the parasite can spread without the bug. Blood transfusion and organ transplant from an infected donor, passage across the placenta, food or juice contaminated with bugs or their feces, and laboratory accidents all transmit it, which is why chronic carriers far from endemic areas matter to blood banks and transplant programs. Oral outbreaks deliver a large inoculum and tend to cause severe acute myocarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"The acute phase lasts several weeks to about 2 months, with many trypomastigotes visible on a stained blood smear, and is usually mild. Most people then enter a lifelong indeterminate phase with few circulating parasites, so diagnosis depends on serology. Over decades about 20 to 30% develop chronic disease. Persisting amastigotes and the inflammation they provoke destroy myocytes and replace them with fibrosis, producing dilated cardiomyopathy, a thin apical aneurysm that can hold thrombus, and conduction disease, typically right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block. Loss of myenteric neurons leaves the esophagus and colon unable to relax and propel their contents, so they dilate into megaesophagus and megacolon."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C04","obj":"CV-17.4","title":"Tick-borne bacteria: Borrelia burgdorferi and Rickettsia rickettsii","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi"},{"type":"p","text":"Borrelia is a spirochete, a long, thin, helical bacterium whose flagella lie in the periplasm between its inner and outer membranes. Their rotation turns the whole cell like a corkscrew, letting it move through connective tissue. The outer membrane lacks classic lipopolysaccharide and is rich in lipoproteins, and the cell is too thin to see well on Gram stain, so it is shown with Giemsa, Wright, or silver stains. Its genome is a linear chromosome plus many linear and circular plasmids, which carry most surface protein genes. The vectors are Ixodes ticks (Ixodes scapularis in the eastern United States, Ixodes pacificus in the West). Larval ticks acquire Borrelia from the white-footed mouse, the main reservoir; nymphs transmit most human infections; deer feed adult ticks but are not a significant reservoir."},{"type":"flow","title":"Borrelia surface switching from tick to host","steps":["Unfed tick gut: Borrelia expresses OspA, anchoring it to the gut","Blood meal warms the tick and supplies nutrients","OspA falls, OspC rises, and spirochetes multiply","Spirochetes migrate to the tick salivary glands","OspC binds tick salivary protein Salp15, shielding the spirochete","Injection into skin, usually after 36 to 48 hours attached","In the host, VlsE variation keeps specific antibody behind"],"caption":"The switch and migration take time, so a tick removed within the first day rarely transmits infection."},{"type":"p","text":"In the host, recombination moves segments of silent vls cassettes into the expressed vlsE gene, so the surface lipoprotein VlsE keeps changing and antibody to the old version no longer binds. Spread through skin produces erythema migrans, and spread through blood carries it to the heart, joints, and nervous system. In Lyme carditis, spirochetes and a lymphocyte and plasma cell infiltrate involve the myocardium and conduction tissue, most often producing AV block, usually at the AV node, that can change degree within hours and usually resolves as inflammation subsides."},{"type":"h","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},{"type":"p","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii, the cause of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that stains poorly with Gram stain and is shown with Giemsa or Gimenez stain. It is obligate intracellular because it imports ATP and other metabolites from host cytoplasm and cannot make enough of its own coenzyme A and NAD+. Dermacentor ticks (the American dog tick and the Rocky Mountain wood tick) are both vector and reservoir, passing the organism to their offspring through the eggs. Despite the name, most US cases occur in southeastern and south-central states."},{"type":"p","text":"Outer membrane proteins (OmpA and OmpB) bind endothelial cells and trigger uptake. The bacterium lyses the phagosome, multiplies in the cytoplasm, and polymerizes host actin into a tail that pushes it into the next endothelial cell, so infection travels along small vessels of the skin, brain, lungs, and kidneys. Injured endothelium becomes leaky and procoagulant, causing a small-vessel vasculitis with edema, loss of intravascular volume, hypotension, and platelet consumption. The rash, which appears a few days after fever begins, starts as macules on the wrists and ankles, spreads to the trunk and onto the palms and soles, and becomes petechial as vessel damage progresses."},{"type":"table","title":"Borrelia burgdorferi versus Rickettsia rickettsii","cols":["Feature","Borrelia burgdorferi","Rickettsia rickettsii"],"rows":[["Structure","Spirochete with periplasmic flagella","Small gram-negative coccobacillus"],["Lifestyle","Extracellular, invades tissue","Obligate intracellular, endothelium"],["Stain","Giemsa, Wright, silver","Giemsa, Gimenez"],["Vector","Ixodes (black-legged) tick","Dermacentor tick"],["Reservoir","White-footed mouse","Ticks (passed through eggs)"],["Survival strategy","OspA to OspC switch, VlsE variation","Actin-based spread from cell to cell"],["Cardiovascular effect","Carditis with AV block","Vasculitis, capillary leak, hypotension"]],"caption":"Ixodes carries Borrelia; Dermacentor carries Rickettsia rickettsii."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does removing an Ixodes tick within the first 24 hours greatly lower the risk of Lyme disease?","explain":"Borrelia in an unfed tick sits in the gut expressing OspA. It must switch to OspC, multiply, and reach the salivary glands before injection, which usually takes 36 to 48 hours.","check":"The Borrelia flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C05","obj":"CV-17.5","title":"Hemorrhagic fever viruses: flaviviruses, filoviruses, and enhancement in dengue","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Flaviviruses and filoviruses are enveloped single-stranded RNA viruses that can cause viral hemorrhagic fever, a syndrome of fever, vascular leak, bleeding, and shock. Their genomes are read in opposite ways. A positive-sense genome is itself mRNA, so it is translated directly and naked genome RNA is infectious. A negative-sense genome is complementary to mRNA, so the virion must carry an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to copy it, and naked genome RNA is not infectious."},{"type":"table","title":"Flaviviruses versus filoviruses","cols":["Feature","Flaviviruses","Filoviruses"],"rows":[["Examples","Yellow fever virus, dengue virus","Ebola virus, Marburg virus"],["Shape","Small, spherical, enveloped","Long, filamentous, enveloped"],["Genome","Positive-sense ssRNA; one polyprotein cut by proteases","Negative-sense ssRNA; virion carries polymerase"],["Reservoir","Humans (dengue); monkeys in the forest cycle (yellow fever)","Fruit bats"],["Transmission","Aedes mosquito bite","Contact with blood and body fluids; no vector"],["Main target cells","Monocytes and dendritic cells; hepatocytes (yellow fever)","Macrophages, dendritic cells, then endothelium and hepatocytes"],["Key mechanism","Plasma leak (dengue); hepatocyte apoptosis (yellow fever)","Interferon blockade, cytokine storm, DIC"]],"caption":"Both end in endothelial leak and coagulopathy."},{"type":"h","text":"Yellow fever and dengue"},{"type":"p","text":"Both are spread mainly by Aedes aegypti, a day-biting urban mosquito; yellow fever also circulates in a forest cycle among monkeys and forest mosquitoes in Africa and South America. Yellow fever virus targets the liver. Hepatocytes in the midzone of the lobule (zone 2) die by apoptosis and appear as eosinophilic Councilman bodies, and loss of hepatocyte function causes jaundice and reduced clotting factor synthesis, which with endothelial damage produces bleeding, including black vomit, and renal failure. Dengue virus has four serotypes (DENV-1 to DENV-4) and infects dendritic cells, monocytes, and macrophages. Severe dengue is a plasma leak syndrome that peaks as fever falls, with hemoconcentration, low platelets, and shock; the secreted viral protein NS1 adds to leak by damaging the endothelial glycocalyx."},{"type":"h","text":"Antibody-dependent enhancement in dengue"},{"type":"p","text":"One serotype gives lasting immunity to itself but only brief cross-protection against the others. Years later, antibodies from the first infection still bind a different serotype but cannot neutralize it. The coated virus attaches to Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, which take it in more efficiently than the virus could enter alone, and this route of entry also dampens interferon responses. Viral load rises, and cross-reactive memory T cells add a burst of cytokines that worsens leak. The same mechanism explains severe dengue in infants who still carry waning maternal antibody at their first infection."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the labs of a second dengue infection","text":"A traveler had mild DENV-1 infection 4 years ago and now has fever after another trip. On day 5, as her fever breaks, she becomes lightheaded; hematocrit has risen from 40% to 48%, and platelets are 40,000/µL (normal about 150,000 to 400,000/µL). Step 1: worsening as fever falls marks the critical phase. Step 2: a rising hematocrit without fluid loss means plasma has leaked from the vessels. Step 3: a different serotype now meets nonneutralizing DENV-1 antibodies, which carry it into Fc receptor-bearing monocytes, raising viral load and cytokines. Step 4: low platelets add bleeding risk to shock."},{"type":"h","text":"Ebola and Marburg"},{"type":"p","text":"Fruit bats are the reservoir; Rousettus bats are proven for Marburg virus, and bats are strongly suspected for Ebola virus. Humans are infected through contact with bats or infected wild animals, then spread the virus by direct contact with blood and other body fluids, including those of the dead, so caregiving and burial practices amplify outbreaks. Virus can persist for months in semen. The glycoprotein GP mediates entry into macrophages, monocytes, and dendritic cells first. Ebola VP35 blocks type I interferon induction and VP24 blocks interferon signaling, so replication outruns innate defense, infected dendritic cells fail to activate T cells, and bystander lymphocytes die by apoptosis. Infected macrophages release TNF, IL-6, and tissue factor, which triggers disseminated intravascular coagulation, and later infection of endothelium and hepatocytes adds leak and clotting factor loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C06","obj":"CV-17.6","title":"Viral myocarditis: coxsackie B virus and other cardiotropic viruses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myocarditis is inflammation of the myocardium with myocyte injury, and in high-income countries viruses are its most commonly identified cause. Coxsackie B virus (serotypes B1 to B6) is the classic example. It is an enterovirus in the picornavirus family: a small, nonenveloped (naked), icosahedral virus with a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome. Without an envelope it resists stomach acid, bile, and drying, so it spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route, and also through respiratory secretions, mostly in summer and fall. It replicates first in the pharynx and intestinal lymphoid tissue and reaches the heart during viremia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Coxsackie B virus from gut to dilated heart","steps":["Fecal-oral or respiratory entry; replication in gut lymphoid tissue","Viremia carries virus to the heart","Virus binds DAF (CD55), then CAR at intercalated discs","Genome translated into one polyprotein","Protease 2A cleaves eIF4G and dystrophin","Sarcolemma tears; myocytes die and release troponin","T-cell inflammation and fibrosis; possible dilated cardiomyopathy"],"caption":"Direct viral injury in the first days is followed by immune-mediated injury over weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Entry depends on the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR), a transmembrane protein of the immunoglobulin superfamily that sits in tight junctions and, in the heart, at intercalated discs. Many strains first bind decay-accelerating factor (DAF, CD55) on the cell surface, which helps deliver them to CAR. CAR is abundant in the developing heart and is expressed again in injured myocardium, one reason neonatal coxsackie myocarditis is severe. Inside the cell the genome is translated from an internal ribosome entry site into one polyprotein, which the viral proteases 2A and 3C cut into functional proteins. Protease 2A also cleaves eIF4G, shutting off cap-dependent host translation, and cleaves dystrophin, which links sarcomeric actin to the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex in the sarcolemma. Without that link the membrane tears under contractile stress, the same structural failure that causes cardiomyopathy in Duchenne muscular dystrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Myocyte loss reduces contractility and releases troponin, and inflamed myocardium is electrically unstable, causing arrhythmias and ST and T wave changes. The T-cell response that follows clears virus but also kills infected myocytes (CV-16), and persisting viral RNA with disrupted dystrophin can leave a dilated ventricle. Coxsackie B often inflames the pericardium as well (myopericarditis, with pleuritic chest pain and a friction rub) and also causes pleurodynia and aseptic meningitis."},{"type":"h","text":"Other myocarditis viruses"},{"type":"table","title":"Other viruses found in myocarditis","cols":["Virus","Structure","Spread","Cardiac relevance"],"rows":[["Adenovirus","Nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA","Respiratory droplets, fecal-oral, fomites","Fiber protein binds CAR; important cause in children"],["Parvovirus B19","Nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA","Respiratory, blood, across the placenta","Binds P antigen (globoside) on erythroid precursors and endothelium; injures small vessels"],["Human herpesvirus 6","Enveloped, double-stranded DNA herpesvirus","Saliva","Latent in lymphocytes; can reactivate or be integrated into chromosomes"]],"caption":"Influenza virus, SARS-CoV-2, HIV, and hepatitis C virus are other recognized causes."},{"type":"p","text":"Parvovirus B19 and HHV-6 genomes are now among the most frequent viral findings in heart biopsies, but both are also found in hearts without myocarditis, so a positive result does not by itself prove that the virus caused the disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-17-01-C07","obj":"CV-17.7","title":"Diphtheria myocarditis, tuberculous pericarditis, and syphilitic aortitis","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Diphtheria toxin myocarditis"},{"type":"p","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae is a club-shaped gram-positive rod that lies in V and L patterns, contains metachromatic (polyphosphate) granules, and forms black colonies on tellurite agar. It colonizes the pharynx without invading deeply. Only strains carrying a lysogenic beta-prophage with the tox gene make toxin, and the bacterial repressor DtxR blocks tox transcription when iron is plentiful, so toxin is made when iron is scarce. The toxin is an A-B exotoxin: the B portion binds the heparin-binding EGF-like growth factor precursor, the toxin enters an endosome, and acidification moves the A fragment into the cytoplasm, where it ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 (EF-2). Protein synthesis stops and the cell dies."},{"type":"p","text":"Locally, dead epithelium, fibrin, and leukocytes form a gray, adherent pseudomembrane. Absorbed toxin reaches the myocardium and peripheral nerves. Myocarditis usually appears 1 to 2 weeks into the illness, often as the throat improves: myocyte degeneration and necrosis with relatively little inflammation cause AV and bundle branch block, arrhythmias, and heart failure, and it is a major cause of death in diphtheria. Demyelinating neuropathy follows later."},{"type":"h","text":"Tuberculous constrictive pericarditis"},{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an acid-fast rod whose mycolic acid-rich wall resists decolorization and survives inside macrophages. It usually reaches the pericardium by retrograde lymphatic spread from mediastinal (peritracheal and peribronchial) lymph nodes, or by blood. The cell-mediated response forms caseating granulomas and a fibrinous, often bloody effusion. As inflammation heals, the visceral and parietal layers fuse into a thick fibrous, sometimes calcified shell. Tuberculosis is the leading cause of constrictive pericarditis where it is common, especially with HIV, whereas idiopathic, postsurgical, and radiation causes predominate elsewhere."},{"type":"p","text":"The rigid shell fixes the total volume of the heart. Early diastolic filling is rapid until the ventricles meet the shell, then stops abruptly, producing a pericardial knock and a steep y descent in the jugular venous pulse. The inspiratory fall in intrathoracic pressure is not transmitted through the shell, so the extra venous return of inspiration cannot enter the right heart and jugular venous pressure rises (Kussmaul sign). Diastolic pressures equalize in all four chambers, and systemic venous congestion causes hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema."},{"type":"h","text":"Syphilitic aortitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum is a thin spirochete that is invisible on Gram stain and is seen by dark-field microscopy or silver stain. In tertiary syphilis, usually 10 to 30 years after untreated infection, treponemes persist around the vasa vasorum, the small adventitial arteries that nourish the outer media of the thoracic aorta."},{"type":"flow","title":"From vasa vasorum endarteritis to aortic aneurysm","steps":["Treponemes persist in the adventitia around vasa vasorum","Lymphocyte and plasma cell cuffs surround the vessels","Intimal thickening (obliterative endarteritis) narrows vasa vasorum","Outer media becomes ischemic","Elastic fibers and smooth muscle lost; patchy scarring","Weakened ascending aorta and arch dilate","Aneurysm, aortic regurgitation, coronary ostial narrowing"],"caption":"The aortic wall fails from ischemia of its media, not from direct destruction by the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"The ascending aorta and arch are affected because they depend most on vasa vasorum. The intima over the scarred media wrinkles into a tree-bark pattern and may calcify. Dilation of the aortic root stretches the valve ring and causes aortic regurgitation, narrowing of the coronary ostia causes angina, and a large aneurysm can compress the trachea, esophagus, or left recurrent laryngeal nerve."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does syphilis cause aneurysms of the ascending aorta, while atherosclerosis mainly causes aneurysms of the infrarenal abdominal aorta?","explain":"Syphilis injures the vasa vasorum, which are richest in the thoracic aorta. Atherosclerotic intimal thickening impairs diffusion from the lumen, which matters most in the infrarenal aorta because it has few vasa vasorum.","check":"The syphilitic aortitis flow and the paragraph after it."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The organisms in this chapter sort by the cardiovascular structure they reach and how they reach it. Endocarditis bacteria arrive through bacteremia from the skin, mouth, gut, or urinary tract and attach to valves by adhesins; Trypanosoma cruzi, Borrelia, and coxsackie B reach the myocardium during parasitemia, spirochetemia, or viremia; rickettsiae and the hemorrhagic fever viruses injure endothelium everywhere; diphtheria toxin travels without its bacterium. Anatomy then predicts the findings: conduction tissue explains the AV blocks of Lyme disease, Chagas disease, and diphtheria, the vasa vasorum explain why syphilis targets the thoracic aorta, and the fixed pericardial shell explains constrictive physiology after tuberculosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Many lesions are produced partly by the host. T-cell attack drives the chronic phase of Chagas disease and the later phase of viral myocarditis, antibody plus Fc receptors drives severe dengue, and cytokines and tissue factor from infected macrophages drive DIC in Ebola. Organism-level facts such as reservoirs, vector ranges, and the proportion of infections that progress vary between sources and change with surveillance, so they are stated here as approximate, and the virulence factors named are the best established among many."}],"summary":["S. aureus (clusters, catalase and coagulase positive) uses clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins, protein A, and toxins to destroy normal valves acutely, especially the tricuspid valve in injection drug use.","Viridans streptococci (chains, alpha-hemolytic, optochin resistant) use dextran to stick to platelet-fibrin deposits on damaged valves after dental bacteremia, giving subacute disease.","Enterococcus grows in 6.5% NaCl and bile and follows urinary or bowel procedures; S. epidermidis forms biofilm on prosthetic valves and devices.","Culture-negative endocarditis usually reflects prior antibiotics; otherwise think HACEK (oral fastidious gram-negative rods), Coxiella (livestock aerosols, serology), or Bartonella (body lice, cats).","S. gallolyticus is bile-esculin positive but does not grow in 6.5% NaCl, and its isolation calls for a search for colorectal neoplasia.","Trypanosoma cruzi is transmitted in triatomine bug feces and also by transfusion, transplant, the placenta, and food; nondividing blood trypomastigotes seed cells where amastigotes multiply, leading decades later to dilated cardiomyopathy, apical aneurysm, conduction block, megaesophagus, and megacolon.","Borrelia burgdorferi switches from OspA to OspC before transmission from Ixodes ticks (36 to 48 hours), varies VlsE in the host, and causes AV block; Rickettsia rickettsii from Dermacentor ticks infects endothelium and spreads by actin tails, causing vasculitis and a rash that moves from wrists and ankles inward.","Flaviviruses (positive-sense, Aedes-borne) cause yellow fever hepatitis with Councilman bodies and dengue plasma leak worsened by antibody-dependent enhancement; filoviruses (negative-sense, bat reservoir, body fluid spread) block interferon and trigger DIC.","Coxsackie B, a naked positive-sense picornavirus spread fecal-orally, enters through CAR and cleaves dystrophin and eIF4G with protease 2A; adenovirus, parvovirus B19, and HHV-6 also appear in myocarditis.","Diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates EF-2 and causes myocarditis with conduction block; tuberculosis causes constrictive pericarditis; tertiary syphilis causes obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum and ascending aortic aneurysm."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci; The Streptococci, Enterococci, and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spirochetes and Other Spiral Microorganisms; Rickettsia and Related Genera"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Picornaviruses (Enterovirus and Rhinovirus Groups); Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Viral Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology, section on Trypanosoma cruzi; section on Corynebacterium diphtheriae; Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, sections on infective endocarditis, myocarditis, and pericardial disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on syphilitic aneurysm and aortitis"}]},"CV-18-A":{"unit":"CV-18-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01","title":"Hypertensive Heart Diseases: Systemic and Pulmonary Hypertension","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the damage that chronically raised pressure does in both circulations. In the systemic circuit, sustained hypertension thickens the left ventricle, narrows arterioles in the kidney, brain, and retina, and sets the stage for stroke, heart failure, kidney disease, and aortic dissection; a rapid, severe rise causes acute organ injury. In the pulmonary circuit, raised pressure remodels small pulmonary arteries and ends in right ventricular failure. Board questions reward mechanism: why low renin points to the adrenal, why a thick ventricle produces an S4, why onion-skin arterioles signal an emergency, and why a loud P2 with right axis deviation points to the lungs.","prerequisites":["Determinants of arterial pressure: cardiac output, systemic vascular resistance, and pressure natriuresis","The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and adrenal cortex and medulla hormones","Ventricular wall stress (law of Laplace) and the atrial contribution to ventricular filling","Normal pulmonary circulation pressures and hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Endothelial mediators: nitric oxide, prostacyclin, and endothelin-1"],"connections":[{"to":"Congestive heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Concentric hypertrophy is a leading cause of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, and left heart failure is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Hypertension accelerates coronary atherosclerosis and makes a hypertrophied ventricle vulnerable to subendocardial ischemia."},{"to":"Arterial disease and atherosclerosis (CV-18-I)","how":"Hypertension is a major risk factor for atherosclerosis and the most important risk factor for aortic dissection."},{"to":"Renal and endocrine systems","how":"The kidney sets long-term pressure and is injured by it, and adrenal disorders produce secondary hypertension with predictable laboratory patterns."},{"to":"Respiratory system","how":"Chronic lung disease, sleep apnea, and pulmonary embolism raise pulmonary vascular resistance and lead to cor pulmonale."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primary (essential) hypertension","def":"Sustained high arterial pressure with no single identifiable cause, arising from many genetic and environmental influences."},{"term":"Secondary hypertension","def":"Hypertension produced by a specific, identifiable disorder such as renal artery stenosis or primary aldosteronism."},{"term":"Pressure natriuresis","def":"The rise in renal sodium and water excretion that follows a rise in arterial pressure, which lets the kidney set long-term pressure."},{"term":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","def":"Homogeneous pink, glassy thickening of arteriolar walls with a narrowed lumen, from leaked plasma proteins and excess matrix."},{"term":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","def":"Concentric, laminated (onion-skin) thickening of arteriolar walls by smooth muscle cells and reduplicated basement membrane, typical of severe hypertension."},{"term":"Fibrinoid necrosis","def":"Necrosis of a vessel wall in which fibrin and other plasma proteins seep in, producing bright pink, smudgy material on routine stains."},{"term":"Concentric hypertrophy","def":"Thickening of a ventricular wall with sarcomeres added in parallel, typical of pressure overload, with little chamber enlargement."},{"term":"Diastolic dysfunction","def":"Impaired ventricular relaxation and filling, so a normal end-diastolic volume requires a higher filling pressure."},{"term":"Hypertensive emergency","def":"Severe blood pressure elevation, often 180/120 mmHg or higher, with new or worsening acute damage to target organs."},{"term":"Pulmonary hypertension","def":"Mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mmHg at rest, measured by right heart catheterization."},{"term":"Pulmonary vascular resistance","def":"Mean pulmonary artery pressure minus wedge pressure, divided by cardiac output, expressed in Wood units."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure caused by pulmonary hypertension from disease of the lungs or pulmonary vessels, not from left heart disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C01","obj":"CV-18-A.1","title":"Defining systemic hypertension and the origins of primary hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"How blood pressure is classified"},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiovascular risk rises continuously from a systolic pressure of about 115 mmHg, so any definition of hypertension is a chosen threshold. Under the 2017 American College of Cardiology and American Heart Association (ACC/AHA) guideline, which most United States board material follows, normal is below 120/80 mmHg, elevated is 120 to 129 systolic with diastolic below 80, stage 1 is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic, and stage 2 is 140 or higher systolic or 90 or higher diastolic. When the two values fall in different categories the higher one applies, so 128/86 mmHg is stage 1."},{"type":"p","text":"The diagnosis rests on the average of repeated readings on two or more occasions, ideally confirmed outside the office. White coat hypertension is high only in the office, from an alerting sympathetic response, and masked hypertension is high only outside it. Pressure normally falls about 10 to 20% during sleep, and loss of this dipping suggests sleep apnea, kidney disease, or autonomic dysfunction. Hypertension is usually silent until an organ is damaged, so it is found by measurement."},{"type":"h","text":"Why pressure stays high: the kidney"},{"type":"p","text":"Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output multiplied by systemic vascular resistance. In established primary hypertension, output is usually normal and resistance is raised. Baroreceptors defend pressure over minutes but reset within a day or two, so they cannot set long-term pressure. The kidney does: a rise in pressure increases sodium and water excretion (pressure natriuresis) until volume and pressure return to the level at which sodium output equals intake. Because this feedback has a very high gain, pressure can stay high only if the kidney's pressure to sodium excretion relationship shifts, so that a higher pressure is needed to excrete the daily salt load. Every chronic hypertension, primary or secondary, involves such a shift."},{"type":"h","text":"Primary hypertension is polygenic"},{"type":"p","text":"Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for about 90 to 95% of cases. Inheritance explains roughly 30 to 50% of blood pressure variation, spread across hundreds of variants of small effect in renal sodium handling, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), vascular tone, and sympathetic control. Rare monogenic forms confirm the central role of renal salt handling. In Liddle syndrome, a gain-of-function mutation keeps the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) of the collecting duct in the membrane, so sodium is reabsorbed regardless of aldosterone. In apparent mineralocorticoid excess, deficiency of 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 lets cortisol occupy the mineralocorticoid receptor, and glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice inhibits the same enzyme. Both inherited disorders produce early-onset hypertension with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and low renin and aldosterone."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for primary hypertension","cols":["Risk factor","Main mechanism"],"rows":[["Older age","Aortic stiffening raises systolic and pulse pressure and lowers diastolic pressure (isolated systolic hypertension)"],["Genetic background","Many small-effect variants; prevalence and severity are higher in Black adults in the United States"],["Obesity, especially visceral","Hyperinsulinemia increases tubular sodium reabsorption, leptin raises sympathetic outflow, adipose tissue releases angiotensinogen; also promotes sleep apnea"],["High sodium, low potassium intake","Volume expansion in salt-sensitive people; low potassium favors distal sodium retention"],["Heavy alcohol intake","Sympathetic and RAAS activation; pressure falls when intake stops"],["Physical inactivity","Higher sympathetic tone; contributes to obesity and insulin resistance"],["Diabetes, chronic kidney disease","Nephron loss and arteriolar disease shift pressure natriuresis upward"]],"caption":"Each risk factor raises output, resistance, or renal sodium retention; most act through more than one."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C02","obj":"CV-18-A.2","title":"Secondary hypertension: identifiable causes and their clues","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary hypertension, about 5 to 10% of cases, has a specific cause. Suspect it when hypertension begins before about age 30 or abruptly, is severe or resistant, or comes with an unexpected finding such as hypokalemia. Chronic kidney disease of any cause is common, because fewer nephrons must excrete the same salt load."},{"type":"h","text":"Renal artery stenosis"},{"type":"p","text":"A narrowed renal artery lowers afferent arteriolar pressure, so juxtaglomerular cells release renin as though the whole body were underperfused. Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone, giving a high-renin, high-aldosterone state. Atherosclerosis causes most cases, in older adults with vascular disease, and narrows the ostium or proximal artery. Fibromuscular dysplasia, a noninflammatory and usually medial thickening, affects women about 15 to 50 years old, involves the mid to distal artery with a string of beads appearance, and can also involve the carotid and vertebral arteries. Clues include an abdominal bruit, hypokalemia, and a small kidney on the affected side. Filtration in the stenotic kidney depends on angiotensin II constricting the efferent arteriole, so blocking angiotensin II can sharply lower the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Bilateral disease can cause recurrent flash pulmonary edema."},{"type":"h","text":"Adrenal causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Primary aldosteronism, from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia or an aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome), is the most common endocrine cause. Aldosterone opens more ENaC channels in principal cells, and the more negative lumen drives potassium secretion, while alpha-intercalated cells secrete more H+, producing metabolic alkalosis. Volume expansion suppresses renin, the key contrast with renal artery stenosis. Edema does not develop because rising pressure and atrial natriuretic peptide restore sodium balance within days (aldosterone escape), but potassium wasting continues, although many patients are normokalemic."},{"type":"p","text":"Pheochromocytoma, a catecholamine-secreting chromaffin cell tumor of the adrenal medulla (paraganglioma if extra-adrenal), raises resistance through alpha-1 receptors and output through beta-1 receptors. Episodic headache, sweating, and palpitations occur on sustained or fluctuating hypertension. Tumor catechol-O-methyltransferase forms metanephrines continuously, so plasma or urine metanephrines stay high between episodes. Epinephrine-secreting tumors are adrenal because cortisol from the cortex induces phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase. Hereditary forms occur in MEN 2 (RET), von Hippel-Lindau disease, and neurofibromatosis type 1. In Cushing syndrome, cortisol overwhelms renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 and activates mineralocorticoid receptors, raises angiotensinogen, and increases vascular sensitivity to pressors."},{"type":"h","text":"Coarctation and sleep apnea"},{"type":"p","text":"Adult coarctation narrows the aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery near the ligamentum arteriosum. The arms are hypertensive, femoral pulses are weak and delayed, and underperfused kidneys add renin. Intercostal collaterals notch the posterior ribs but spare ribs 1 and 2, whose posterior intercostal arteries arise from the costocervical trunk rather than the aorta. Coarctation is associated with a bicuspid aortic valve and Turner syndrome. In obstructive sleep apnea, each apnea causes hypoxemia and each arousal a sympathetic surge; repeated nightly, these raise daytime sympathetic tone and activate the RAAS, a common cause of resistant hypertension."},{"type":"table","title":"Secondary causes of hypertension","cols":["Cause","Key clue","Renin / aldosterone"],"rows":[["Renal artery stenosis","Bruit, small kidney, GFR falls when angiotensin II is blocked","High / high"],["Primary aldosteronism","Hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, no edema","Low / high"],["Cushing syndrome, licorice, Liddle syndrome","Cushingoid habitus, licorice intake, or early onset with hypokalemia","Low / low or normal"],["Pheochromocytoma","Episodic headache, sweating, palpitations; high metanephrines","Not the primary abnormality"],["Coarctation of the aorta","Arm hypertension, weak delayed femoral pulses, rib notching","Renin may be raised"],["Obstructive sleep apnea","Snoring, daytime sleepiness, loss of nocturnal dipping","Variable"]],"caption":"Both high points to renal hypoperfusion, low renin with high aldosterone points to the adrenal cortex, and both low points to a mineralocorticoid effect from something other than aldosterone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C03","obj":"CV-18-A.3","title":"Chronic hypertensive damage to arterioles, heart, kidney, retina, and brain","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Arterioles are the resistance vessels, so they bear the strain. Chronic stress lets plasma proteins leak through injured endothelium into the wall, and smooth muscle cells add basement membrane and matrix. The wall becomes homogeneous, pink, and glassy, and the narrowed lumen causes slow, patchy ischemia of the tissue supplied. The lesion is also typical of diabetes and appears mildly with aging."},{"type":"h","text":"The heart: concentric left ventricular hypertrophy"},{"type":"p","text":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. A higher afterload raises systolic wall stress, and myocytes respond by adding sarcomeres in parallel, becoming thicker with enlarged boxcar nuclei. The wall thickens with little chamber enlargement (concentric hypertrophy), which returns stress toward normal. Volume overload instead adds sarcomeres in series and dilates the chamber (eccentric hypertrophy). The normal left ventricular wall is about 1.0 to 1.5 cm and can approach or exceed 2 cm in hypertensive heart disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pressure overload to diastolic failure and atrial fibrillation","steps":["Sustained hypertension raises left ventricular afterload","Systolic wall stress rises (law of Laplace)","Sarcomeres added in parallel: concentric hypertrophy","Thick, fibrotic wall relaxes slowly and is stiff","Higher left atrial pressure is needed for filling","Left atrium dilates; pulmonary venous pressure rises","Atrial fibrillation and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction"],"caption":"Concentric hypertrophy normalizes systolic wall stress at the price of a stiff ventricle; late in the course the ventricle can dilate and lose systolic function."},{"type":"p","text":"A stiff ventricle fills poorly in early diastole and depends on atrial contraction, so losing the atrial kick in atrial fibrillation can cause abrupt congestion. Greater muscle mass, lagging capillary density, and high diastolic pressure compressing subendocardial vessels make the hypertrophied wall prone to ischemia, worsened by accelerated coronary atherosclerosis. Atrial contraction into the stiff ventricle produces an S4. On the ECG, tall R waves in V5 and V6 with deep S waves in V1 reflect the larger mass, and lateral ST depression with T wave inversion forms the strain pattern."},{"type":"h","text":"Kidney, retina, and brain"},{"type":"p","text":"In benign nephrosclerosis, hyaline afferent arterioles and thickened interlobular arteries cause glomerulosclerosis, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis, leaving symmetrically small kidneys with a finely granular surface, mild proteinuria, and slowly falling GFR. Hypertension is the second leading cause of end-stage kidney disease after diabetes, with higher risk in carriers of high-risk APOL1 variants. Lost nephrons impair sodium excretion and worsen the hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"The retina shows arteriolar disease directly: narrowing, copper or silver wiring from a thickened wall, arteriovenous nicking where a thick arteriole compresses a vein in their shared sheath, flame hemorrhages, hard exudates, and cotton wool spots (nerve fiber layer microinfarcts)."},{"type":"p","text":"Penetrating arteries such as the lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery receive nearly full parent pressure. Lipohyalinosis occludes them, causing lacunar infarcts under about 15 mm in the basal ganglia, internal capsule, thalamus, and pons. Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms weaken them, and rupture causes deep intracerebral hemorrhage, most often in the putamen, then thalamus, pons, and cerebellum, unlike the lobar hemorrhages of amyloid angiopathy. Hypertension is also the most important risk factor for aortic dissection."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing hypertension develops atrial fibrillation and, within hours, becomes short of breath at rest although his ejection fraction is 60%. Why did losing atrial contraction affect him so severely?","explain":"Concentric hypertrophy made his ventricle stiff and slow to relax, so it depends on atrial contraction for filling. Fibrillation removes that contribution and the fast rate shortens diastole, so filling falls and left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise despite preserved systolic function.","check":"Compare with the pressure overload flow figure and the paragraph after it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C04","obj":"CV-18-A.4","title":"Hypertensive emergency and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A hypertensive emergency is severe elevation, often 180/120 mmHg or higher, with new or worsening acute damage to a target organ. Equally high pressure without acute damage is sometimes called hypertensive urgency. The older term malignant hypertension (severe hypertension with retinal hemorrhages, exudates, or papilledema, often with acute kidney injury) is now considered a form of emergency. The rate of rise and the failure of protective mechanisms matter more than the level reached."},{"type":"h","text":"Autoregulation and breakthrough"},{"type":"p","text":"The brain and kidney keep flow nearly constant across a range of pressures by arteriolar constriction and dilation, in the brain roughly 60 to 150 mmHg mean arterial pressure. Chronic hypertension shifts this curve to the right. Above the upper limit, constriction is forced open (breakthrough), capillaries meet full arterial pressure, and the blood-brain barrier leaks, causing vasogenic edema and hypertensive encephalopathy with headache, confusion, visual disturbance, and seizures. The edema often favors the posterior circulation, which has less sympathetic innervation (posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome). A previously normotensive person has an unshifted curve and breaks through at lower pressure, as in eclampsia or acute glomerulonephritis."},{"type":"h","text":"The acute arteriolar lesion"},{"type":"p","text":"A rapid, severe rise injures arteriolar endothelium, plasma proteins and fibrin enter the wall, and smooth muscle cells die, producing fibrinoid necrosis (bright pink, smudgy wall, often with luminal thrombus). Surviving arterioles respond with concentric layers of smooth muscle and reduplicated basement membrane, the onion-skin lesion of hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, most conspicuous in renal interlobular arteries and afferent arterioles. The same lesion occurs in scleroderma renal crisis and other thrombotic microangiopathies."},{"type":"table","title":"Chronic hypertension compared with hypertensive emergency","cols":["Feature","Chronic hypertension","Hypertensive emergency"],"rows":[["Time course","Years of gradual, silent elevation","Abrupt rise over hours to weeks"],["Arteriolar lesion","Hyaline: glassy wall from protein leak and matrix","Hyperplastic onion-skin layering with fibrinoid necrosis"],["Heart","Concentric hypertrophy, diastolic dysfunction","Acute pulmonary edema, acute coronary syndrome"],["Brain and retina","Lacunes, deep hemorrhage; arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking","Encephalopathy, hemorrhage; papilledema"],["Kidney","Benign nephrosclerosis: small, finely granular kidneys","Acute kidney injury, hematuria; petechial flea-bitten surface"],["Renin and blood","Renin variable; blood count normal","Renin often very high; schistocytes and thrombocytopenia if microangiopathy develops"]],"caption":"Glassy thickening marks chronic moderate pressure; onion-skin layering with fibrinoid necrosis marks severe, accelerated pressure with acute organ injury."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The renal vicious cycle of hypertensive emergency","steps":["Severe rise in arterial pressure","Fibrinoid necrosis and onion-skin narrowing of renal arterioles","Glomerular ischemia downstream","Juxtaglomerular cells release large amounts of renin","Angiotensin II constricts arterioles; pressure natriuresis shrinks volume and adds renin","Pressure rises further and injures more arterioles"],"caption":"Arteriolar injury causes renal ischemia, ischemia drives renin release, and renin raises pressure, which injures more arterioles."},{"type":"p","text":"Other acute injuries follow the same logic. Sudden afterload can cause left ventricular failure or ischemia, shear stress can tear the aortic intima (dissection), and fibrin strands in damaged small vessels shear red cells into schistocytes, causing microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with thrombocytopenia and raised lactate dehydrogenase. Triggers include untreated primary hypertension, renovascular disease, glomerulonephritis, scleroderma renal crisis, preeclampsia, pheochromocytoma, cocaine or amphetamines, and abrupt clonidine withdrawal, which causes a rebound sympathetic surge."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 72-year-old woman with 30 years of hypertension has a pressure of 186/112 mmHg with no symptoms and a normal fundus. A previously normotensive 24-year-old woman at 36 weeks of pregnancy has 168/108 mmHg with headache and confusion. Which has a hypertensive emergency, and why did the lower pressure cause more harm?","explain":"The pregnant woman. Her autoregulation curve has not shifted right, so 168/108 mmHg exceeds her upper limit, causing breakthrough, vasogenic edema, and encephalopathy. The older woman has severe hypertension without acute organ damage.","check":"Compare with the subsection on autoregulation and breakthrough."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C05","obj":"CV-18-A.5","title":"Pulmonary hypertension: definition and classification","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pulmonary circulation carries the whole cardiac output at about 25/8 mmHg (mean about 14 mmHg), with a resistance about one tenth of systemic resistance or less. During exercise, recruitment and distension of capillaries lower resistance as flow rises. The thin-walled right ventricle handles volume well but tolerates a rise in pressure poorly."},{"type":"h","text":"Definition and hemodynamic types"},{"type":"p","text":"Pulmonary hypertension is a mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mmHg at rest on right heart catheterization (the 2018 World Symposium threshold; many textbooks still quote 25 mmHg). Echocardiography estimates systolic pressure from the tricuspid regurgitant jet and raises suspicion. Mean pressure equals cardiac output times pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) plus left atrial pressure, estimated by the wedge pressure. Precapillary disease has a wedge pressure of 15 mmHg or less and a PVR above 2 Wood units. Isolated postcapillary disease has a wedge pressure above 15 mmHg and a PVR of 2 or less. Combined disease has both a high wedge pressure and a high PVR. One Wood unit equals 1 mmHg per L/min."},{"type":"example","title":"Separating precapillary from postcapillary pulmonary hypertension","text":"Two patients each have a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 36 mmHg and a cardiac output of 5 L/min. Patient A has a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg; patient B, 28 mmHg. PVR is (mean pressure minus wedge pressure) divided by cardiac output. For A, (36 minus 10) / 5 = 5.2 Wood units with a normal wedge: the disease is in the pulmonary arteries or lung, as in pulmonary arterial hypertension, lung disease, or chronic thromboembolism. For B, (36 minus 28) / 5 = 1.6 Wood units, which is normal: high left atrial pressure is being transmitted backward, as in left ventricular failure or mitral stenosis. The same mean pressure reflects different diseases."},{"type":"h","text":"Classification by cause"},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical classification of pulmonary hypertension","cols":["Group","Main mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["1. Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Disease of small pulmonary arteries: endothelial dysfunction, constriction, proliferative remodeling","Idiopathic; heritable (BMPR2); anorexigens, methamphetamine; systemic sclerosis; HIV; portal hypertension; congenital shunts; schistosomiasis"],["2. Left heart disease (most common)","High left atrial pressure transmitted backward","Heart failure with reduced or preserved ejection fraction, mitral and aortic valve disease"],["3. Lung disease or hypoxia","Hypoxic vasoconstriction, capillary loss, remodeling","COPD, interstitial lung disease, sleep apnea, hypoventilation, high altitude"],["4. Pulmonary artery obstruction","Organized thrombi obstruct arteries; open vessels remodel","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],["5. Unclear or multifactorial","Several mechanisms","Sarcoidosis, myeloproliferative disorders"]],"caption":"Group 2 is the most common cause, followed by group 3; group 1 is uncommon but the most tested for molecular pathogenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"In group 2, high left atrial pressure first raises pulmonary pressure passively, but persistent venous hypertension, classically from long-standing mitral stenosis, can remodel the arteries and add a precapillary component. In group 3, low alveolar oxygen closes oxygen-sensitive K+ channels in pulmonary arterial smooth muscle, the cells depolarize, L-type Ca2+ channels open, and the muscle contracts. This diverts blood from poorly ventilated alveoli locally, but global hypoxia constricts the whole lung and drives medial hypertrophy, while hypoxemia-driven polycythemia raises viscosity. In group 4, a small minority of pulmonary emboli organize into fibrous webs instead of lysing; ventilation-perfusion scanning shows mismatched perfusion defects."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-01-C06","obj":"CV-18-A.6","title":"Pathogenesis of pulmonary arterial hypertension and its effects on the right heart","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Genetic and acquired triggers"},{"type":"p","text":"Heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in BMPR2, a receptor of the transforming growth factor beta superfamily whose SMAD signaling restrains smooth muscle proliferation and promotes apoptosis. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, so a second hit such as inflammation or hypoxia is thought to be needed. Idiopathic PAH is more common in women. Acquired triggers include serotonergic anorexigens such as fenfluramine (serotonin is a smooth muscle mitogen), systemic sclerosis, HIV, and portal hypertension. A large left-to-right shunt remodels the arteries until resistance exceeds systemic resistance and the shunt reverses (Eisenmenger syndrome). In hepatosplenic Schistosoma mansoni infection, eggs reach the lungs through portosystemic collaterals and provoke granulomatous arteriolar injury."},{"type":"table","title":"Endothelial mediators in pulmonary arterial hypertension","cols":["Mediator","Signaling","Change in PAH"],"rows":[["Nitric oxide","Soluble guanylyl cyclase, cGMP: dilation, antiproliferative","Decreased"],["Prostacyclin (PGI2)","IP receptor, Gs, cAMP: dilation, inhibits platelets","Decreased, while thromboxane A2 rises"],["Endothelin-1","ETA receptor, Gq, Ca2+: constriction, mitogen","Increased"]],"caption":"Too little of two dilator and antiproliferative signals and too much of one constrictor and mitogen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of pulmonary arterial hypertension","steps":["BMPR2 loss or another trigger injures endothelium","Nitric oxide and prostacyclin fall; endothelin-1 and thromboxane rise","Small arteries constrict; in situ thrombi form","Apoptosis-resistant smooth muscle proliferates","Medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, plexiform lesions","Pulmonary vascular resistance becomes fixed","Right ventricular hypertrophy, then failure (cor pulmonale)"],"caption":"Endothelial imbalance starts the process, proliferative remodeling makes it permanent, and the right ventricle bears the load."},{"type":"p","text":"Medial hypertrophy and muscularization of normally nonmuscular arterioles come first, then concentric intimal fibrosis. In severe idiopathic, heritable, and shunt-related disease, plexiform lesions form: tufts of slit-like endothelial channels at small artery branch points that resemble a glomerulus. Large pulmonary arteries develop atherosclerotic plaques, which normally do not form at pulmonary pressures."},{"type":"h","text":"The right ventricle and cor pulmonale"},{"type":"p","text":"The right ventricle first hypertrophies concentrically, then dilates. Dilation stretches the tricuspid annulus (regurgitation) and pushes the septum left into a D shape, limiting left ventricular filling. When right ventricular pressure nears systemic, its normal systolic coronary perfusion is lost while demand rises, so ischemia accelerates failure. Cor pulmonale is right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure from disease of the lungs or pulmonary vessels, excluding left heart and congenital heart disease; it is chronic and hypertrophic in COPD or PAH, and acute and dilated after massive pulmonary embolism. Exertional dyspnea and fatigue come first; exertional syncope means output cannot rise across a fixed resistance. A dilated pulmonary artery can compress the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, causing hoarseness."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in pulmonary hypertension and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Loud P2","High pulmonary artery diastolic pressure closes the pulmonic valve forcefully"],["Left parasternal heave, right-sided S4","Hypertrophied, stiff right ventricle"],["Holosystolic murmur louder on inspiration; large v waves","Tricuspid regurgitation from annular dilation"],["Raised jugular pressure, hepatomegaly, edema","Raised systemic venous pressure"],["Right axis deviation, tall R in V1, P pulmonale","Right ventricular mass and right atrial enlargement"]],"caption":"Each sign reflects the pressure-loaded right heart or the high-pressure pulmonary artery."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a patient with severe pulmonary arterial hypertension have low systemic pressure even though the left heart is structurally normal?","explain":"The right ventricle cannot push enough blood across the high resistance, so left atrial return falls, and the dilated right ventricle shifts the septum left and restricts left ventricular filling. Low preload lowers stroke volume and systemic pressure.","check":"Compare with the subsection on the right ventricle and cor pulmonale."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and biochemistry explain where and why. Coarctation forms near the ligamentum arteriosum, so the arms are hypertensive and rib notching spares ribs 1 and 2, which are not supplied from the aorta; lenticulostriate arteries leave the middle cerebral artery at right angles, so hypertensive bleeds and lacunes cluster deep. The adrenal is a lesson in enzymes: 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 shields the mineralocorticoid receptor from cortisol, cortisol induces the enzyme that makes epinephrine, and catechol-O-methyltransferase produces the metanephrines that reveal pheochromocytoma. Systemic and pulmonary arterioles respond oppositely to hypoxia, which is why lung disease raises only pulmonary resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties the chapter together through three responses: hyaline thickening under chronic moderate pressure, onion-skin proliferation with fibrinoid necrosis under acute severe pressure, and concentric hypertrophy of whichever ventricle faces the load. These patterns have limits. Hyaline change also occurs with diabetes and aging, onion-skin arterioles also occur in scleroderma renal crisis, and one patient often has several mechanisms at once, such as obesity, sleep apnea, and primary aldosteronism together."}],"summary":["Under the 2017 ACC/AHA scheme, stage 1 hypertension is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic mmHg and stage 2 is 140/90 mmHg or higher, based on repeated readings.","Primary hypertension (about 90 to 95%) is polygenic, and because the kidney sets long-term pressure, every sustained hypertension involves a shift in pressure natriuresis.","Renal artery stenosis raises renin and aldosterone; primary aldosteronism raises aldosterone with low renin, hypokalemia, and alkalosis but no edema; low renin with low aldosterone suggests Cushing syndrome, licorice, or Liddle syndrome.","Pheochromocytoma causes episodic headache, sweating, and palpitations with high metanephrines; coarctation causes arm hypertension, weak delayed femoral pulses, and rib notching.","Chronic hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis, benign nephrosclerosis, retinal arteriolar changes, lacunes, and deep hemorrhage from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms, most often in the putamen.","Pressure overload causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy that normalizes wall stress but produces diastolic dysfunction, an S4, left atrial enlargement, and atrial fibrillation.","Hypertensive emergency is severe elevation with acute organ damage; its lesion is onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis, and renal ischemia drives a high-renin vicious cycle.","Pulmonary hypertension is a mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mmHg; precapillary disease has a normal wedge pressure and a PVR above 2 Wood units, and left heart disease (group 2) is the most common cause.","Pulmonary arterial hypertension combines BMPR2-related loss of a growth brake with low nitric oxide and prostacyclin and high endothelin-1, producing remodeling and plexiform lesions.","Right ventricular pressure overload explains a loud P2, a parasternal heave, right axis deviation, and, with failure, tricuspid regurgitation, raised venous pressure, and edema (cor pulmonale)."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the Kidneys in Long-Term Control of Arterial Pressure and in Hypertension: The Integrated System for Arterial Pressure Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on hypertensive vascular disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, sections on systemic and pulmonary hypertensive heart disease; The Lung, section on pulmonary hypertension"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Integrated control of arterial pressure and the pulmonary circulation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease; disorders of the adrenal cortex and adrenal medulla"}]},"CV-18-B":{"unit":"CV-18-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02","title":"Congestive Heart Failure","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Heart failure is a syndrome in which the heart cannot pump enough blood for tissue needs, or can do so only at abnormally high filling pressures. Nearly every finding comes from congestion behind the failing ventricle (backward failure), inadequate flow ahead of it (forward failure), or the neurohormonal response to both. This chapter moves from left-sided and right-sided findings to reduced versus preserved ejection fraction, the compensations that drive progression, the complications, high-output failure, and acute mitral regurgitation. Board questions usually describe a patient and ask you to place the lesion (left or right, systolic or diastolic, low or high output) or predict the next consequence, so the emphasis is on mechanism.","prerequisites":["Determinants of stroke volume and the Frank-Starling relationship (CV-6)","Pressure-volume loops and the phases of the cardiac cycle (CV-4, CV-6)","Starling forces across capillaries and the role of lymphatics (CV-10, CV-11)","Baroreceptor reflex and renal handling of sodium and water, renin, aldosterone, and ADH (CV-11, renal physiology)","Hepatic acinus zones and their blood supply (GI histology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Loss of contracting muscle after infarction is the most common cause of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, and papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation."},{"to":"Hypertensive heart diseases (CV-18-A)","how":"Long-standing hypertension produces concentric hypertrophy and is the leading cause of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, and pulmonary hypertension produces cor pulmonale."},{"to":"Valvular heart diseases and primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-D, CV-18-E)","how":"Pressure and volume overload from valve lesions and intrinsic muscle disease from cardiomyopathies are the other main roads to a failing ventricle."},{"to":"Cardiovascular biochemistry and nutrition (CV-13)","how":"Thiamine deficiency causes wet beriberi, a high-output failure rooted in failed oxidative metabolism."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The kidney reads low cardiac output as low blood volume, so its sodium and water retention is the engine of edema and of cardiorenal syndrome."}],"terms":[{"term":"Heart failure","def":"A syndrome in which the heart cannot deliver enough blood for tissue needs, or does so only at elevated filling pressures."},{"term":"Backward failure","def":"The congestion and edema that result when pressure rises in the chamber and veins behind a failing ventricle."},{"term":"Forward failure","def":"The consequences of inadequate cardiac output, such as fatigue, renal hypoperfusion, and confusion."},{"term":"HFrEF","def":"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (40% or lower), caused mainly by impaired contraction (systolic dysfunction)."},{"term":"HFpEF","def":"Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (50% or higher), caused mainly by impaired relaxation and filling (diastolic dysfunction)."},{"term":"Orthopnea","def":"Dyspnea that appears when lying flat and is relieved by sitting up."},{"term":"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","def":"Sudden breathlessness that wakes a patient from sleep, typically one to a few hours after lying down."},{"term":"Heart failure cells","def":"Alveolar macrophages that have engulfed red cells and stored their iron as hemosiderin, a sign of chronic pulmonary congestion."},{"term":"Cor pulmonale","def":"Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure caused by pulmonary hypertension from disease of the lungs or pulmonary vessels, not from left heart disease or congenital heart disease."},{"term":"Nutmeg liver","def":"The gross appearance of chronic passive hepatic congestion: dark red congested centrilobular areas alternating with paler periportal tissue."},{"term":"Effective arterial blood volume","def":"The portion of blood volume that the arterial baroreceptors and kidney sense as filling the arterial tree; it falls in heart failure even when total volume is expanded."},{"term":"High-output heart failure","def":"Congestive failure in which cardiac output is above normal but still inadequate, usually because systemic vascular resistance is low or metabolic demand is high."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C01","obj":"CV-18-B.1","title":"Left-sided heart failure: causes and the consequences of pulmonary venous congestion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"When the left ventricle cannot empty in systole or fill at a normal pressure in diastole, its end-diastolic pressure rises. The open mitral valve passes that pressure to the left atrium, and the valveless pulmonary veins pass it on to the pulmonary capillaries, so left-sided failure shows itself first in the lungs."},{"type":"h","text":"Causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause, through loss of muscle after infarction and through chronic ischemia. Systemic hypertension and aortic stenosis add a pressure load that first produces concentric hypertrophy and impaired filling, aortic and mitral regurgitation add a volume load that dilates the ventricle, and cardiomyopathies, myocarditis, sustained tachyarrhythmias, alcohol, and anthracyclines damage or stiffen the muscle directly. Mitral stenosis and left atrial myxoma produce every pulmonary sign below while the left ventricle stays normal or underfilled."},{"type":"h","text":"From raised left atrial pressure to pulmonary edema"},{"type":"p","text":"Normal pulmonary capillary pressure is about 7 mmHg against a plasma oncotic pressure of about 28 mmHg, so lymphatics easily clear the small net filtration. Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) approximates left atrial pressure and is normally about 6 to 12 mmHg. As it rises, fluid collects first in the interstitium around vessels and small airways, and once capillary pressure exceeds roughly the plasma oncotic pressure and filtration outruns lymph flow, fluid floods the alveoli. A low plasma albumin lowers this threshold. The rate of rise matters as much as the level: in chronic failure or mitral stenosis the lymphatics enlarge and carry many times their normal flow, so a wedge pressure that causes frank edema after an acute infarct may be tolerated."},{"type":"h","text":"Dyspnea, orthopnea, and PND"},{"type":"p","text":"Interstitial fluid stiffens the lungs and stimulates juxtacapillary (J) receptors that drive rapid, shallow breathing, and alveolar fluid adds ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxemia; bronchial wall edema can cause wheeze (cardiac asthma). Lying flat shifts several hundred milliliters of blood from the legs and splanchnic veins into the thorax, and a failing ventricle on the flat part of its Frank-Starling curve turns that extra preload into higher capillary pressure. PND appears one to a few hours after lying down, because edema fluid from the legs is reabsorbed while sympathetic support and respiratory center sensitivity fall during sleep."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a failing left ventricle to orthopnea and PND","steps":["Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure rises","Left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise","Capillary filtration exceeds lymphatic clearance: interstitial edema","Stiff lungs and J receptor stimulation: dyspnea on exertion","Lying flat shifts blood into the chest: orthopnea","Leg edema reabsorbed during sleep: paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","Alveolar flooding: crackles, hypoxemia, pulmonary edema"],"caption":"Each step adds central volume or pressure that a failing left ventricle cannot absorb, so congestion worsens in a predictable order."},{"type":"h","text":"Crackles, heart failure cells, and forward findings"},{"type":"p","text":"Fine late inspiratory crackles come from fluid-filled small airways snapping open and begin at the bases, where capillary hydrostatic pressure is highest. Pleural effusions are transudates and often bilateral. In chronic congestion, red cells leak into the alveoli and alveolar macrophages store their iron as hemosiderin, which stains with Prussian blue; these heart failure cells lie in the lung and sputum, and with septal fibrosis they give brown induration. Forward failure adds fatigue, cool pale extremities, confusion in older patients, and prerenal azotemia, with a BUN to creatinine ratio above about 20 because enhanced proximal and ADH-driven urea reabsorption accompany the fall in filtration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C02","obj":"CV-18-B.2","title":"Right-sided heart failure: causes and the consequences of systemic venous congestion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thin-walled right ventricle handles volume loads well but fails quickly against a high afterload. Its failure raises right atrial pressure, which passes into the venae cavae and every systemic venous bed."},{"type":"h","text":"Causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Left-sided failure is the most common cause. Chronically raised pulmonary venous pressure raises pulmonary artery pressure, first passively and then through arteriolar remodeling, and the right ventricle hypertrophies, dilates, and fails (biventricular failure). Cor pulmonale is right ventricular failure from pulmonary hypertension arising in the lungs or pulmonary vessels. Acute cor pulmonale follows massive pulmonary embolism, and chronic cor pulmonale follows chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, interstitial lung disease, hypoventilation syndromes, pulmonary arterial hypertension, and chronic thromboembolic disease, largely through hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and later medial hypertrophy. Right ventricular infarction, which accompanies an inferior infarct from proximal right coronary occlusion, causes hypotension with a raised JVP and clear lungs, because less blood reaches the left ventricle and the dilated right ventricle shifts the septum leftward."},{"type":"h","text":"Neck veins and pitting edema"},{"type":"p","text":"The right internal jugular vein acts as a manometer for right atrial pressure, which normally averages about 0 to 6 mmHg. With the patient at about 45 degrees, a pulsation more than about 3 to 4 cm above the sternal angle indicates raised pressure. In hepatojugular reflux, abdominal pressure for about 10 to 15 seconds pushes splanchnic blood toward the right heart; a failing right ventricle cannot accept it, so the JVP stays up while pressure is held."},{"type":"p","text":"Dependent edema needs both raised venous pressure, which increases capillary filtration, and renal sodium and water retention, which supplies the volume. Several liters accumulate before swelling is visible, so weight gain comes first. The edema settles at the ankles in ambulatory patients and the sacrum in bedridden ones, and it pits because it is a low-protein transudate, unlike lymphedema or the myxedema of hypothyroidism."},{"type":"h","text":"Nutmeg liver"},{"type":"p","text":"Raised hepatic venous pressure distends the central veins and centrilobular sinusoids. Zone 3 hepatocytes, farthest from portal inflow, become hypoxic and atrophy, while zone 1 may show fatty change, which gives the dark red and tan nutmeg pattern. Low output added to congestion causes centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, and years of congestion cause centrilobular fibrosis (cardiac sclerosis). The liver is enlarged and tender, and congestion also produces ascites, congestive splenomegaly, and bowel wall edema with anorexia."},{"type":"table","title":"Left-sided versus right-sided heart failure","cols":["Feature","Left-sided failure","Right-sided failure"],"rows":[["Where pressure backs up","Left atrium, pulmonary veins and capillaries","Right atrium, venae cavae, systemic veins"],["Symptoms","Dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, cough","Swelling of legs and abdomen, right upper quadrant discomfort, anorexia"],["Signs","Basal crackles, S3, displaced apex beat, pleural effusions","Raised JVP, hepatojugular reflux, tender hepatomegaly, ascites, dependent pitting edema"],["Organ pathology","Pulmonary edema, heart failure cells, brown induration","Nutmeg liver, centrilobular necrosis or fibrosis, congestive splenomegaly"],["Lungs","Congested","Clear in isolated right-sided failure"]],"caption":"Isolated right-sided failure leaves the lungs clear, which helps separate cor pulmonale or right ventricular infarction from biventricular failure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with an inferior myocardial infarction is hypotensive, has a JVP 9 cm above the sternal angle, and has clear lung fields. Predict what has happened to left ventricular filling.","explain":"Left ventricular filling is reduced. A right ventricular infarction lowers right ventricular output, so less blood crosses the lungs to the left heart, and the dilated right ventricle shifts the septum leftward. Systemic venous pressure rises while pulmonary venous pressure does not, which is why the neck veins are full and the lungs are clear.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on right ventricular infarction and the left versus right table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C03","obj":"CV-18-B.3","title":"Reduced versus preserved ejection fraction, and the S3, BNP, and chest radiograph","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ejection fraction (EF) is stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, normally about 55% to 70%. HFrEF (EF 40% or lower) is mainly a failure of contraction, HFpEF (EF 50% or higher) is mainly a failure of relaxation and filling, and an EF of 41% to 49% is called mildly reduced. Both raise filling pressure, so both cause the same congestion."},{"type":"h","text":"HFrEF: a weak, dilated ventricle"},{"type":"p","text":"After infarction or in dilated cardiomyopathy, the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship flattens, so the ventricle ejects less and keeps a larger end-systolic volume. It compensates by adding sarcomeres in series (eccentric hypertrophy) and using the Frank-Starling mechanism at a larger end-diastolic volume. By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so the dilated ventricle has high wall stress, high oxygen demand, and a stretched mitral annulus that may leak (functional mitral regurgitation)."},{"type":"h","text":"HFpEF: a stiff ventricle that empties normally"},{"type":"p","text":"Active relaxation requires ATP to return Ca2+ to the sarcoplasmic reticulum through SERCA2a and to release cross-bridges, so ischemia, hypertrophy, and aging slow it. Passive stiffness rises with concentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in parallel), fibrosis, titin changes, and infiltration such as amyloid. The end-diastolic pressure-volume relationship steepens, so even a normal volume requires a high diastolic pressure. HFpEF is most common in older adults, women, and patients with hypertension, obesity, diabetes, and atrial fibrillation. The stiff ventricle depends on atrial contraction, so atrial fibrillation or a fast rate that shortens diastole can tip it into pulmonary edema."},{"type":"table","title":"HFrEF compared with HFpEF","cols":["Feature","HFrEF (systolic)","HFpEF (diastolic)"],"rows":[["Ejection fraction","40% or lower","50% or higher"],["Core defect","Reduced contractility","Impaired relaxation and increased stiffness"],["Pressure-volume loop","Flatter end-systolic relationship; loop shifts to the right","Steeper end-diastolic relationship; loop narrow at high diastolic pressure"],["Ventricular geometry","Dilated, eccentric hypertrophy, normal or thin wall","Normal size, concentric hypertrophy, thick wall"],["Typical causes","Myocardial infarction, dilated cardiomyopathy, myocarditis, chronic volume overload","Hypertension, aging, obesity, diabetes, hypertrophic and restrictive cardiomyopathy, amyloidosis"],["Characteristic extra sound","S3","S4 (if the atria are contracting)"],["Dependence on atrial contraction","Moderate","High; atrial fibrillation is poorly tolerated"]],"caption":"Both forms raise filling pressure and cause congestion; they differ in whether the ventricle fails to empty or fails to fill."},{"type":"h","text":"S3, BNP, and the chest radiograph"},{"type":"p","text":"The S3 is a low-pitched early diastolic sound at the apex, produced when rapid filling from a high-pressure atrium is suddenly halted by a dilated ventricle near its limit of distension. It marks volume overload and high filling pressure, classically in HFrEF, and is normal in children, young adults, athletes, and pregnancy. The S4 is a late diastolic sound from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle and disappears in atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"p","text":"BNP is released from stretched ventricular myocytes as proBNP, which is cleaved into active BNP and inactive NT-proBNP. BNP raises cGMP through the NPR-A receptor and is cleared by neprilysin and NPR-C, while NT-proBNP is cleared mainly by the kidney. A BNP below about 100 pg/mL or an NT-proBNP below about 300 pg/mL makes acute heart failure unlikely. Levels rise with age, renal failure, atrial fibrillation, and right ventricular strain, fall with obesity, and are lower in HFpEF because a thick wall lowers wall stress for a given pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Radiographic signs follow pulmonary venous pressure in a set order, and cardiomegaly (cardiothoracic ratio above 0.5 on a posteroanterior film) supports a dilated heart but is often absent in HFpEF."},{"type":"flow","title":"Chest radiograph signs as wedge pressure rises","steps":["Normal, about 6 to 12 mmHg: lower-lobe vessels larger than upper","About 13 to 18 mmHg: upper-lobe diversion (cephalization)","About 18 to 25 mmHg: Kerley B lines, peribronchial cuffing, hazy hila","Above about 25 mmHg: perihilar bat-wing alveolar opacities"],"caption":"Signs lag behind rapid pressure changes and appear at higher pressures in chronic failure because lymphatic drainage has increased."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C04","obj":"CV-18-B.4","title":"Neurohormonal compensation: support that becomes the disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The baroreceptors and kidney cannot tell a failing pump from hemorrhage, so a fall in cardiac output triggers the same defense: sympathetic activation, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), and antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Total extracellular volume is expanded, yet effective arterial blood volume is low, so these systems stay switched on."},{"type":"h","text":"Sympathetic activation, RAAS, and ADH"},{"type":"p","text":"Reduced baroreceptor firing raises sympathetic outflow: beta-1 stimulation raises heart rate and contractility, alpha-1 arteriolar constriction holds arterial pressure, and venoconstriction raises preload. Over months, catecholamines raise oxygen demand and afterload, cause Ca2+ overload, arrhythmias, and myocyte death, and downregulate beta-1 receptors through receptor kinases and beta-arrestin."},{"type":"p","text":"Renin is released in response to low afferent arteriolar pressure, low macula densa NaCl delivery, and renal beta-1 stimulation. Angiotensin II acts on AT1 receptors to constrict arterioles, constrict the efferent arteriole more than the afferent, increase proximal Na+ reabsorption, stimulate thirst and ADH, and release aldosterone, which drives Na+ reabsorption in collecting duct principal cells at the cost of K+ and H+. On a flat Frank-Starling curve the retained volume adds little stroke volume and mostly raises venous pressure. Baroreceptor-driven (nonosmotic) ADH release adds aquaporin-2 water retention through V2 receptors, so hyponatremia marks advanced disease."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The neurohormonal cycle of heart failure","steps":["Myocardial injury lowers cardiac output and arterial filling","Baroreceptors and the kidney sense low effective arterial volume","Sympathetic activation, renin release, and nonosmotic ADH release","Short-term support: higher heart rate, contractility, vascular tone, and blood volume","Vasoconstriction raises afterload; salt and water retention raise preload beyond benefit, causing congestion","Catecholamines, angiotensin II, and aldosterone cause myocyte loss, hypertrophy, and fibrosis","Remodeling lowers pump function further, which lowers output again"],"caption":"A response designed for hemorrhage, sustained for months, worsens the pump it is trying to support."},{"type":"h","text":"Remodeling and natriuretic peptides"},{"type":"p","text":"Remodeling is the progressive change in ventricular size, shape, and composition after injury: myocytes enlarge and re-express fetal genes, some die by apoptosis, and angiotensin II and aldosterone drive fibrosis. In HFrEF the ventricle becomes larger and more spherical, which raises wall stress and pulls the papillary muscles apart, so the process becomes self-sustaining. Blocking beta-1 receptors, angiotensin II, or aldosterone slows remodeling but raising contractility alone does not, so the hormones themselves drive progression. ANP from atria and BNP from ventricles oppose these systems through NPR-A and cGMP, but receptor downregulation, neprilysin, and low renal perfusion blunt their effect."},{"type":"table","title":"Compensations in heart failure","cols":["Mechanism","Short-term benefit","Long-term harm"],"rows":[["Frank-Starling (increased preload)","More stroke volume from more filling","Little gain once the curve flattens; congestion and edema"],["Sympathetic activation","Higher heart rate and contractility; maintained arterial pressure","Higher oxygen demand and afterload, arrhythmias, myocyte death, beta-1 receptor downregulation"],["Angiotensin II","Maintained arterial pressure and glomerular filtration","Higher afterload, sodium retention, hypertrophy, fibrosis"],["Aldosterone","Restored extracellular volume","Edema, hypokalemia, myocardial and vascular fibrosis"],["ADH","Restored blood volume, vasoconstriction","Free water retention, dilutional hyponatremia"],["Ventricular hypertrophy","Spreads wall stress over more muscle","Stiffness, relative ischemia, eventual dilation"],["Natriuretic peptides","Natriuresis, vasodilation, suppression of renin and aldosterone","Effect too weak to offset the vasoconstrictor systems"]],"caption":"Every compensation in the upper rows is helpful in a short crisis and harmful when sustained; natriuretic peptides are the only one that pushes the other way."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the serum sodium and the urine sodium of a patient with advanced heart failure and marked edema.","explain":"Serum sodium tends to be low because nonosmotic ADH release retains water in excess of sodium. Urine sodium is low because angiotensin II and aldosterone avidly reabsorb filtered sodium. The body is overloaded with both sodium and water, yet the kidney behaves as if the patient were volume depleted, because it senses low effective arterial volume.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on ADH and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C05","obj":"CV-18-B.5","title":"Complications of heart failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complications follow from high filling pressures, low forward output, or enlarged, scarred, neurohormonally stimulated chambers."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock"},{"type":"p","text":"Acute pulmonary edema occurs when left atrial pressure rises faster than lymphatics can adapt, triggered by ischemia, a hypertensive surge, a fast arrhythmia, acute valve regurgitation, or a sudden salt load. It produces severe dyspnea, diffuse crackles, frothy pink sputum, and hypoxemia, and the sympathetic surge it provokes raises afterload further. The same picture at a normal wedge pressure suggests noncardiogenic edema, as in acute respiratory distress syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiogenic shock is hypoperfusion from pump failure: systolic pressure below about 90 mmHg for at least 30 minutes, cardiac index below about 2.2 L/min/m2, and wedge pressure above about 15 to 18 mmHg. It classically follows infarction of 40% or more of the left ventricle. The patient is cold and wet, with low mixed venous oxygen saturation and lactic acidosis, and hypotension lowers coronary perfusion, which worsens ischemia. Its profile is low output, high wedge pressure, and high systemic vascular resistance (SVR); hypovolemic shock shares low output and high SVR but has a low wedge pressure, and early septic shock has high output with low SVR."},{"type":"h","text":"Arrhythmias"},{"type":"p","text":"Atrial stretch and angiotensin II and aldosterone driven fibrosis promote atrial fibrillation, which removes the atrial kick and shortens diastole, and persistently fast rates can cause tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy. Ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation cause many sudden deaths: scar supports reentry, fewer repolarizing K+ channels prolong the action potential and favor early afterdepolarizations, catecholamines and Ca2+ overload favor delayed afterdepolarizations, and hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia destabilize repolarization."},{"type":"h","text":"Mural thrombi, cardiorenal syndrome, and cachexia"},{"type":"p","text":"Virchow's triad explains mural thrombi: stasis in a dilated ventricle, akinetic segment, or fibrillating left atrial appendage, endocardial injury over an infarct, and a procoagulant state. Thrombi show lines of Zahn and embolize to the brain, gut, kidneys, spleen, and limbs."},{"type":"p","text":"In cardiorenal syndrome, low output and renal vasoconstriction reduce renal flow, while raised central venous pressure raises renal venous and interstitial pressure inside the capsule and lowers the gradient for filtration. Creatinine and BUN rise, and the added sodium retention worsens congestion. Cardiac cachexia comes from TNF alpha and interleukin-6 driven proteolysis, gut congestion with anorexia and malabsorption, and higher energy expenditure, and edema can hide the weight loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C06","obj":"CV-18-B.6","title":"High-output heart failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In high-output heart failure, cardiac output is above normal, yet the patient has congestion and edema. The defining feature is low systemic vascular resistance (SVR) from arteriolar vasodilation, arteriovenous shunting, or high metabolic demand."},{"type":"h","text":"Why low resistance produces congestion"},{"type":"p","text":"Mean arterial pressure is approximately cardiac output times SVR, so a fall in SVR lowers arterial pressure unless output rises. The baroreceptors and kidney sense arterial underfilling, exactly as in low-output failure, and sympathetic activation, RAAS, and ADH retain sodium and water. Blood volume, venous return, and output rise. A normal heart sustains this for a time, but the persistent volume and rate load eventually raises filling pressures and dilates the ventricles, and a diseased heart reaches that point sooner."},{"type":"example","title":"Arteriovenous fistula and the cardiac output equation","text":"With a mean arterial pressure of about 93 mmHg and a cardiac output of 5 L/min, SVR is about 18.6 mmHg per L/min (Wood units). A large dialysis fistula adds a low-resistance channel in parallel and lowers total SVR to about 10 Wood units. To keep mean arterial pressure near 90 mmHg, cardiac output must rise to about 9 L/min. Much of this extra flow passes through the fistula without reaching capillaries, so tissues gain little, venous blood returns well oxygenated, and the heart carries the extra work. Compressing the fistula raises SVR and arterial pressure, and the baroreflex slows the heart (the Nicoladoni-Branham sign)."},{"type":"h","text":"Causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Severe anemia causes hypoxic vasodilation and lowers blood viscosity. Thyrotoxicosis raises metabolic demand and heat dissipation, and T3 relaxes vascular smooth muscle while increasing SERCA2a, alpha myosin heavy chain, and beta-1 receptors and decreasing phospholamban. Wet beriberi is thiamine deficiency: thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase, so ATP production falls, arterioles dilate, and the energy-starved myocardium dilates; Shoshin beriberi is the fulminant form with lactic acidosis. Extensive Paget disease creates hypervascular bone that behaves like many shunts. Pregnancy, sepsis, cirrhosis, severe obesity, and some myeloproliferative disorders also produce high-output states."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of high-output failure and their mechanisms","cols":["Cause","Main mechanism lowering SVR or raising demand","Clues"],"rows":[["Severe anemia","Hypoxic vasodilation and low blood viscosity","Pallor, low hemoglobin, flow murmur"],["Thyrotoxicosis","High metabolic rate, heat dissipation, direct T3 vasodilation and inotropy","Weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, atrial fibrillation"],["Arteriovenous fistula","Low-resistance shunt bypassing capillaries","Continuous bruit or thrill, bradycardia on compression"],["Wet beriberi","Thiamine deficiency impairs oxidative metabolism; arteriolar dilation","Alcohol use or poor diet, edema, neuropathy, lactic acidosis"],["Paget disease of bone","Hypervascular bone acting as multiple shunts","Bone pain, enlarging skull, warm skin, raised alkaline phosphatase"]],"caption":"Each cause lowers resistance or raises demand, so the heart must deliver more flow than normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Low-output compared with high-output failure","cols":["Feature","Low-output failure","High-output failure"],"rows":[["Cardiac output","Low","High"],["Systemic vascular resistance","High (vasoconstriction)","Low"],["Extremities","Cool, pale","Warm, flushed"],["Pulse and pulse pressure","Weak, narrow pulse pressure","Bounding, wide pulse pressure"],["Arteriovenous O2 difference","Wide (tissues extract more)","Narrow (shunting or excess flow)"],["Mixed venous O2 saturation","Low","Normal or high"],["Shared features","Congestion, edema, neurohormonal activation, S3","Congestion, edema, neurohormonal activation, S3"]],"caption":"The opposite resistances explain the opposite skin, pulse, and oxygen findings."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on hemodialysis develops orthopnea and edema. Cardiac output is 9 L/min and mixed venous oxygen saturation is high. Predict the SVR and explain the oxygen finding.","explain":"SVR is low because the dialysis fistula provides a low-resistance path from artery to vein. A large fraction of the output passes through the fistula without unloading oxygen in capillaries, so venous blood returning to the heart is well saturated.","check":"Compare with the worked example and the low-output versus high-output table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-02-C07","obj":"CV-18-B.7","title":"Acute mitral regurgitation from chordal or papillary muscle rupture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chordae tendineae anchored to the anterolateral and posteromedial papillary muscles keep the mitral leaflets closed in systole. If a chord or papillary muscle ruptures, part of a leaflet flails into the left atrium and a large regurgitant jet appears within seconds."},{"type":"h","text":"Causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Chordal rupture occurs with myxomatous degeneration (mitral valve prolapse), infective endocarditis, blunt chest trauma, and less often rheumatic disease. Papillary muscle rupture complicates myocardial infarction about 3 to 7 days after the infarct, when necrotic muscle has been removed before collagen forms. The posteromedial muscle, usually supplied only by the posterior descending artery, ruptures with inferior infarcts (CV-18-C). Rupture of a whole trunk is usually rapidly fatal, while rupture of one head can be survivable."},{"type":"h","text":"Hemodynamics and findings"},{"type":"p","text":"The ventricle now ejects into both the aorta and the low-pressure left atrium, from the start of systole. The normal-sized, noncompliant atrium cannot absorb the volume, so its pressure rises steeply (giant v wave) and causes sudden pulmonary edema. The undilated ventricle cannot raise total stroke volume enough, so forward output falls into cardiogenic shock, and sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts still more flow backward. Because atrial pressure approaches ventricular pressure in late systole, the murmur may be early systolic, decrescendo, soft, or absent, and heart size on radiograph is normal. Ventricular septal rupture presents similarly but gives a loud holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border and an oxygen saturation step-up from right atrium to right ventricle."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rupture of a chord or papillary muscle to shock","steps":["Chord or papillary muscle ruptures; a leaflet flails into the left atrium","Large regurgitant volume enters a small, noncompliant left atrium each systole","Left atrial pressure rises steeply (giant v wave)","Pulmonary capillary pressure rises abruptly: acute pulmonary edema","Forward stroke volume falls because the undilated ventricle cannot raise total output","Hypotension and cardiogenic shock; sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts more flow backward"],"caption":"The suddenness of the lesion, not its size alone, explains the severity of the presentation."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the ejection fraction looks normal in shock","text":"Before rupture: end-diastolic volume 120 mL, end-systolic volume 50 mL, stroke volume 70 mL, EF 58%, heart rate 72 per minute, cardiac output about 5.0 L/min. After rupture, the ventricle also empties into the low-pressure atrium, so end-systolic volume falls to 30 mL. Total stroke volume is 90 mL and EF is 75%. But if 50 mL of each beat goes into the left atrium (a regurgitant fraction, the backward share of total stroke volume, of about 56%), forward stroke volume is only 40 mL. Even at a heart rate of 100 per minute, forward output is 4.0 L/min, and 50 mL per beat entering a small atrium drives the wedge pressure sharply upward. EF overstates forward performance in mitral regurgitation."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute compared with chronic mitral regurgitation","cols":["Feature","Acute (chordal or papillary muscle rupture)","Chronic (degenerative, rheumatic, functional)"],"rows":[["Left atrial size and compliance","Normal size, low compliance","Dilated, high compliance"],["Left atrial pressure","Very high, with giant v waves","Modestly raised"],["Left ventricle","Normal size; hyperdynamic","Dilated with eccentric hypertrophy"],["Forward output","Abruptly reduced; hypotension or shock","Maintained for years by a larger total stroke volume"],["Pulmonary findings","Sudden pulmonary edema","Gradual exertional dyspnea"],["Murmur","Early systolic, decrescendo, may be soft or absent","Holosystolic, radiating to the axilla"],["Heart size on radiograph","Normal","Enlarged left atrium and left ventricle"],["Rhythm","Usually sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation common from atrial dilation"]],"caption":"Chronic compensation, atrial dilation and eccentric ventricular hypertrophy, is exactly what the acute lesion lacks."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The two hearts are pumps in series, so failure of either side backs pressure into the veins that drain into it and reduces flow beyond it. Starling forces and lymphatic capacity decide when that pressure becomes edema, the law of Laplace explains high wall stress in a dilated ventricle and lower BNP in a thick one, and the hepatic acinus explains why congestion injures zone 3. The same hormones that normally regulate sodium and water now respond to a falsely low effective arterial volume, and their receptors (AT1, mineralocorticoid, V2, NPR-A) are where the cycle can be interrupted."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and microbiology supply causes and tissue findings: infarct repair sets the timing of papillary muscle rupture, Virchow's triad explains mural thrombi, and coxsackie B myocarditis, Chagas disease, rheumatic heart disease, and endocarditis are infectious routes to failure. Every marker has mechanistic limits: EF overstates forward output in mitral regurgitation, BNP rises in renal failure and falls in obesity, radiographic signs lag behind pressure, and an S3 is normal in the young."}],"summary":["Heart failure is inadequate output, or adequate output only at high filling pressure; findings reflect backward congestion, forward hypoperfusion, and neurohormonal activation.","Left-sided failure raises pulmonary capillary pressure, causing edema, dyspnea, orthopnea, PND, basal crackles, and heart failure cells; mitral stenosis does this with a normal left ventricle.","Left-sided failure is the most common cause of right-sided failure; cor pulmonale arises from lung or pulmonary vascular disease, and right ventricular infarction gives hypotension, raised JVP, and clear lungs.","Right-sided failure raises systemic venous pressure: raised JVP, sustained hepatojugular reflux, dependent pitting edema, ascites, and nutmeg liver from zone 3 hypoxia.","HFrEF (EF 40% or lower) is a weak, dilated ventricle with an S3; HFpEF (EF 50% or higher) is a stiff, thick ventricle with an S4; both raise filling pressure.","BNP reflects ventricular wall stress; a low level makes acute heart failure unlikely, and radiographs progress from cephalization to Kerley B lines to bat-wing alveolar edema.","Sympathetic activation, RAAS, and nonosmotic ADH defend arterial pressure but raise afterload, retain sodium and water, cause hyponatremia, and drive remodeling; natriuretic peptides cannot overcome them.","Complications follow from high pressure, low flow, or damaged chambers: pulmonary edema, cardiogenic shock, arrhythmias, mural thrombi, cardiorenal syndrome, and cachexia.","High-output failure (anemia, thyrotoxicosis, arteriovenous fistula, wet beriberi, Paget disease) has low SVR, warm extremities, wide pulse pressure, and normal or high mixed venous saturation.","Acute mitral regurgitation loads a small, stiff left atrium, causing giant v waves, sudden pulmonary edema, low forward output despite a normal EF, and a murmur that may be soft."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Circulation, Pulmonary Edema, Pleural Fluid"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on heart failure; Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac function, the pressure-volume loop, and integrated control of the circulation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease, section on heart failure"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins, section on thiamine"}]},"CV-18-C":{"unit":"CV-18-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03","title":"Ischemic Heart Disease","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"Ischemic heart disease is the group of conditions in which coronary blood flow cannot meet the oxygen needs of the myocardium. Coronary atherosclerosis causes more than 90% of cases, and the presentation depends on how fast and how completely flow falls: a fixed narrowing causes exertional angina, a disrupted plaque with a partial thrombus causes unstable angina, an occlusive thrombus causes infarction, and repeated or extensive damage causes chronic ischemic heart disease. This chapter explains each stage by its mechanism so that ECG changes, biomarker patterns, tissue changes, and complications can be predicted rather than memorized. Treatment is a Part II topic; drugs appear only where they reveal a mechanism.","prerequisites":["Coronary artery anatomy and dominance (left anterior descending, left circumflex, right coronary, posterior descending arteries)","Determinants of myocardial oxygen demand and the regulation of coronary blood flow","Ventricular action potential and the normal 12-lead ECG","Reversible and irreversible cell injury, coagulative necrosis, and acute inflammation","Lipoprotein metabolism (LDL, HDL, LDL receptor) and the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis"],"connections":[{"to":"Atherosclerosis (CV-18-I)","how":"The plaque that causes most ischemic heart disease is the same lesion studied in arterial disease, and its stability decides whether a patient has stable angina or an acute coronary syndrome."},{"to":"Heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Loss of contracting muscle after infarction and chronic remodeling are the most common causes of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."},{"to":"Conduction system and ECG (CV-7)","how":"Injury currents, Q waves, ventricular arrhythmias, and AV block after infarction are applications of normal cardiac electrophysiology."},{"to":"Cell injury and inflammation (general pathology)","how":"The infarct timeline is a textbook model of coagulative necrosis followed by acute inflammation, macrophage clearance, granulation tissue, and scar."},{"to":"Hemostasis (HE)","how":"Platelet adhesion through von Willebrand factor and tissue factor driven coagulation convert a ruptured plaque into an occlusive coronary thrombus."}],"terms":[{"term":"Ischemia","def":"Inadequate blood flow to a tissue, causing both oxygen deprivation and accumulation of metabolic waste, as opposed to hypoxia alone."},{"term":"Coronary flow reserve","def":"The ability of coronary flow to rise several fold above its resting value by arteriolar dilation when demand increases."},{"term":"Angina pectoris","def":"Episodic chest pain or pressure caused by transient myocardial ischemia that is not severe or long enough to kill myocytes."},{"term":"Acute coronary syndrome","def":"The spectrum of unstable angina, non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI), all usually caused by an acutely disrupted plaque with thrombus."},{"term":"Vulnerable plaque","def":"An atheroma with a thin fibrous cap, a large lipid-rich necrotic core, and abundant inflammatory cells, which is prone to rupture."},{"term":"Myocardial infarction","def":"Death of cardiac myocytes caused by prolonged ischemia, demonstrated by a rise of cardiac troponin with evidence of ischemia."},{"term":"Transmural infarct","def":"An infarct that extends through all or nearly all of the ventricular wall in the territory of one occluded artery."},{"term":"Subendocardial infarct","def":"An infarct limited to the inner third to half of the ventricular wall, the zone most vulnerable to reduced perfusion."},{"term":"Injury current","def":"Current that flows between ischemic and normal myocardium because of their different membrane potentials, displacing the ST segment on the ECG."},{"term":"Cardiac troponin","def":"Regulatory protein of the cardiac thin filament (troponin I or T) whose release into blood is the most specific marker of myocyte necrosis."},{"term":"Stunned myocardium","def":"Myocardium that stays dysfunctional for hours to days after a brief ischemic episode even though flow has been restored and the cells are alive."},{"term":"Hibernating myocardium","def":"Living myocardium that reduces its contraction during chronic hypoperfusion and can recover function if flow is restored."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C01","obj":"CV-18-C.1","title":"Causes of ischemic heart disease: supply, demand, and coronary atherosclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The myocardium is almost entirely aerobic and extracts about 70% or more of the oxygen in coronary blood at rest, so it can meet higher demand only by raising flow. Resting coronary flow is about 225 mL/min (about 4 to 5% of cardiac output) and can rise roughly three to four fold in heavy exercise. This reserve comes from dilation of small resistance arterioles in response to local metabolic signals, chiefly adenosine from ATP breakdown, along with low oxygen tension, carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, and potassium."},{"type":"p","text":"Demand is set by heart rate, contractility, and ventricular wall stress. By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so a high afterload or a large chamber raises oxygen use. Ischemia is therefore a mismatch between supply and demand rather than a disease of one artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The left ventricle is perfused mainly in diastole. Systolic compression of intramural vessels is greatest in the subendocardium, and penetrating branches reach it last, so the subendocardium becomes ischemic first whenever perfusion pressure falls, diastole shortens, or ventricular diastolic pressure rises. The effective driving pressure is approximately aortic diastolic pressure minus left ventricular (LV) end-diastolic pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"Myocardial oxygen balance and the conditions that disturb it","cols":["Determinant","Effect when disturbed","Conditions"],"rows":[["Perfusion pressure (aortic diastolic minus LV end-diastolic)","Smaller gradient for subendocardial flow","Shock, hypotension, aortic regurgitation, stiff hypertrophied ventricle"],["Diastolic time","Less time for LV perfusion","Tachycardia from any cause, fever, hyperthyroidism, sepsis"],["Coronary resistance","Flow limited once arteriolar reserve is spent","Fixed stenosis; vasospasm (Prinzmetal, cocaine, amphetamines, ergots); embolism (atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, endocarditis vegetations, prosthetic valves); vasculitis (Kawasaki aneurysms, polyarteritis nodosa, Takayasu at the ostia)"],["Oxygen content","Less oxygen per mL of flow","Severe anemia, hypoxemia, carbon monoxide poisoning"],["Heart rate and contractility","More oxygen used per minute; tachycardia also shortens diastole","Sympathetic surge, tachyarrhythmia, positive inotropes"],["Wall stress","More oxygen used per beat","Hypertension, aortic stenosis, dilated chamber, hypertrophy with fewer capillaries per myocyte"]],"caption":"Most triggers act on several rows at once. Aortic stenosis causes angina with clean coronary arteries because high systolic pressure and hypertrophy raise demand while a long ejection time and high LV diastolic pressure cut subendocardial supply."},{"type":"h","text":"Coronary atherosclerosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Atherosclerosis causes more than 90% of ischemic heart disease (its formation is taught with arterial disease, CV-18-I). Endothelial dysfunction lets LDL enter the intima, where it is oxidized. Monocytes enter, become macrophages, and take up oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, which, unlike the LDL receptor, are not downregulated by cellular cholesterol, so foam cells form. Cytokines and platelet-derived growth factor recruit smooth muscle cells that lay down a collagen cap over a lipid-rich necrotic core."},{"type":"p","text":"A plaque harms the heart in two ways. A slowly enlarging fixed plaque limits flow reserve and causes stable angina. A vulnerable plaque, with a thin cap, a large lipid core, and many macrophages and T cells, can rupture or erode and seed a thrombus, causing an acute coronary syndrome. Macrophage matrix metalloproteinases degrade cap collagen while interferon gamma from T cells suppresses collagen synthesis by smooth muscle cells, so inflammation weakens the cap from both sides."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk factors injure the endothelium, raise atherogenic lipoprotein, or amplify inflammation. Nonmodifiable factors are increasing age, male sex (the gap narrows after menopause), and a family history of premature disease; familial hypercholesterolemia (defective LDL receptors) shows how strongly LDL alone drives the disease. The major modifiable factors are high LDL, low HDL (which removes tissue cholesterol by reverse cholesterol transport), hypertension, smoking (oxidant injury and a prothrombotic state), and diabetes (glycation end products and dyslipidemia). Obesity, inactivity, and the metabolic syndrome act largely through these, and lipoprotein(a), chronic kidney disease, hyperhomocysteinemia, and raised C-reactive protein add risk."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C02","obj":"CV-18-C.2","title":"Angina pectoris: stable, unstable, and Prinzmetal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Angina pectoris is episodic chest pressure or heaviness from transient ischemia that does not kill myocytes, often radiating to the left arm, neck, or jaw. Ischemic myocytes release adenosine, bradykinin, hydrogen ions, and potassium, which stimulate visceral afferents that travel with the cardiac sympathetic nerves into roughly T1 to T5. The same dorsal horn neurons receive somatic input from the chest wall and inner arm, so the pain is referred there. Within an episode, events follow the ischemic cascade with pain last, so ischemia can be silent, especially when diabetic autonomic neuropathy damages the afferent fibers."},{"type":"flow","title":"The ischemic cascade","steps":["Demand exceeds supply, or supply suddenly falls","Anaerobic glycolysis; lactate, hydrogen ions, and adenosine accumulate","Relaxation slows because calcium reuptake needs ATP","Regional contraction weakens","ST segment changes from injury currents","Chest pain from visceral afferent stimulation"],"caption":"Rest restores balance without necrosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Stable angina"},{"type":"p","text":"Stable angina results from a fixed stenosis of about 70% or more. Resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius (Poiseuille), so halving the radius raises segment resistance 16 fold. At rest, downstream arterioles dilate enough to keep flow normal, but this spends the reserve, so flow cannot rise with exertion and the subendocardium becomes ischemic. Pain is predictable and brief and eases with rest. Relief by nitroglycerin is a mechanistic clue: nitric oxide raises cGMP and dilates veins more than arteries, lowering ventricular volume, wall stress, and demand. Narrowing of about 90% or more can limit flow even at rest."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the ST segment moves"},{"type":"p","text":"During the ST segment, healthy ventricular cells sit at the same plateau voltage, so the segment is isoelectric. Ischemic cells lose potassium, depolarize partially, and have shorter, lower plateaus, creating an injury current. Subendocardial ischemia points the ST vector toward the cavity, away from surface electrodes, giving ST depression that does not localize the artery. Transmural ischemia points it toward the epicardium, giving ST elevation in the facing leads, which does localize the artery."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ecg-waveform","caption":"The ST segment, from the end of QRS to the start of the T wave, is normally isoelectric.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-18-C-2.png"},{"type":"h","text":"Unstable, Prinzmetal, and microvascular angina"},{"type":"p","text":"Unstable angina is the mildest acute coronary syndrome. A fissured plaque with a nonocclusive platelet thrombus, sometimes with spasm from thromboxane A2 and serotonin, causes rest pain, new severe pain, or crescendo pain. Troponin stays below the upper reference limit, but the thrombus can grow and cause infarction. Prinzmetal (variant) angina is intense spasm of an epicardial artery, with or without plaque, linked to smooth muscle hyperreactivity and reduced endothelial nitric oxide. Attacks come at rest, often at night, in younger patients whose main risk factor is smoking, and can be provoked by cocaine, amphetamines, ergot alkaloids, triptans, and hyperventilation. Microvascular angina causes exertional ischemia with normal epicardial arteries."},{"type":"table","title":"Stable, unstable, and Prinzmetal angina compared","cols":["Feature","Stable","Unstable","Prinzmetal"],"rows":[["Lesion","Fixed stenosis about 70% or more","Disrupted plaque, nonocclusive thrombus","Epicardial vasospasm"],["Pattern","Exertional, predictable, relieved by rest","Rest, new, or crescendo pain","Spontaneous rest pain, often at night"],["Layer ischemic","Subendocardial","Usually subendocardial","Transmural"],["ECG during pain","ST depression","ST depression, T inversion, or normal","Transient ST elevation"],["Troponin","Normal","Normal (separates it from NSTEMI)","Normal unless infarction occurs"],["Main risk","Gradual progression","Infarction","Arrhythmia or infarction"]],"caption":"The ischemic layer decides whether the ST segment falls or rises."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has 30 minutes of chest pain at rest with ST depression. Troponin at presentation and 3 hours later stays below the upper reference limit. Predict the diagnosis and what would make it an infarction.","explain":"Unstable angina: rest pain with ST depression means subendocardial ischemia from an active plaque, and normal serial troponin means no myocytes died. A troponin rise above the 99th percentile limit would make it an NSTEMI.","check":"The unstable angina paragraph and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C03","obj":"CV-18-C.3","title":"Pathogenesis of myocardial infarction: from plaque rupture to necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most infarctions begin with rupture of a thin, inflamed fibrous cap; fewer arise from superficial erosion over an intact plaque. Rupture clusters in the early morning and after intense exertion or emotional stress, when an adrenergic surge raises blood pressure, heart rate, and shear stress. Many culprit plaques were only mildly to moderately stenotic, so infarction is often the first sign of coronary disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"From plaque rupture to transmural infarction","steps":["Cap ruptures, exposing collagen and a lipid core rich in tissue factor","Von Willebrand factor binds platelet glycoprotein Ib; platelets adhere","ADP and thromboxane A2 recruit platelets; IIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen","Tissue factor drives thrombin and fibrin; thrombus occludes the artery","Contraction stops within about 1 to 2 minutes","Irreversible injury after about 20 to 40 minutes, subendocardium first","Necrosis spreads toward the epicardium over about 3 to 6 hours"],"caption":"Collateral flow and reperfusion can stop the wavefront before it becomes transmural."},{"type":"h","text":"Inside the ischemic myocyte"},{"type":"p","text":"Without oxygen, oxidative phosphorylation stops and anaerobic glycolysis yields little ATP while generating lactate and hydrogen ions. Contraction fails long before the cell dies, causing the immediate wall motion abnormality. Failure of the sodium-potassium ATPase lets sodium and water enter, so the cell swells, while potassium leaks out, and calcium accumulates as ATP-dependent pumps fail. If flow returns within roughly 20 minutes the injury is reversible, although the cells may stay stunned for hours to days."},{"type":"p","text":"After about 20 to 40 minutes, sarcolemmal disruption and mitochondrial amorphous densities mark irreversible injury. Calcium floods in, phospholipases and proteases are activated, and troponin, the MB isoenzyme of creatine kinase (CK-MB), and myoglobin leak into blood. The subendocardium dies first and the outer layers, supported by collaterals, survive longest, so salvage is greatest in the first hours."},{"type":"h","text":"Transmural and subendocardial infarction"},{"type":"p","text":"A transmural infarct spans the full or nearly full wall in one artery's territory, usually from persistent complete occlusion. It produces ST elevation and later Q waves, making it the typical lesion of ST-elevation MI (STEMI), and because necrosis reaches the epicardium it can cause pericarditis and free wall rupture. A subendocardial infarct involves the inner third to half of the wall, from partial or transient occlusion or global hypoperfusion. It produces ST depression or T inversion, the typical lesion of non-ST-elevation MI (NSTEMI), and rarely ruptures. Both raise troponin, and the electrical and pathologic labels are strongly associated but not identical. In shock or profound hypoxemia, a circumferential subendocardial infarct ignores arterial territories."},{"type":"h","text":"Reperfusion"},{"type":"p","text":"Early reperfusion limits infarct size but changes the infarct. Reperfused infarcts are often hemorrhagic, and irreversibly injured myocytes take up a surge of calcium and hypercontract, producing contraction band necrosis with dense eosinophilic cross bands. Reactive oxygen species and calcium opening the mitochondrial permeability transition pore kill some cells that might have survived, which is reperfusion injury."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Pain lasts more than about 20 to 30 minutes and is not relieved by rest because ischemia is sustained. Diaphoresis, pallor, and tachycardia reflect a sympathetic surge. Nausea and bradycardia, especially in inferior infarction, reflect vagal reflexes from the inferior and posterior wall. Dyspnea and crackles reflect raised left atrial pressure from a stiff, weak ventricle. Low-grade fever and leukocytosis reflect the response to necrosis. Silent infarction is more common in older adults and in diabetes with autonomic neuropathy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C04","obj":"CV-18-C.4","title":"Localizing the infarct by ECG and interpreting cardiac biomarkers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The left anterior descending artery (LAD) supplies the anterior LV wall, the apex, and the anterior two thirds of the septum with much of the bundle branches; the left circumflex supplies the lateral wall. The right coronary artery (RCA) supplies the right ventricle, the inferior LV wall, and usually the SA and AV nodes. The posterior descending artery arises from the RCA in about 80% (right dominance), otherwise from the circumflex. The LAD is occluded most often (about 40 to 50%), then the RCA and circumflex."},{"type":"table","title":"Infarct location, artery, and ECG leads","cols":["Region","Usual artery","Leads with ST elevation","Associated findings"],"rows":[["Anterior","LAD (proximal if extensive)","V1 to V4 (also V5, V6, I, aVL if extensive)","Largest infarcts; shock, bundle branch block, apical thrombus, aneurysm"],["Lateral","Left circumflex (or LAD diagonal)","I, aVL, V5, V6","Reciprocal inferior depression"],["Inferior","RCA in most (circumflex if left dominant)","II, III, aVF","Bradycardia, AV block, papillary muscle rupture"],["Right ventricular","Proximal RCA","V4R, with inferior changes","Hypotension, clear lungs, raised neck veins"],["Posterior","RCA or circumflex","None; mirror image is ST depression and tall R in V1 to V3","With inferior or lateral infarction"]],"caption":"ST elevation lead groups localize the artery."},{"type":"p","text":"Reciprocal ST depression supports infarction over pericarditis, which elevates the ST segment diffusely without reciprocal change. Pathologic Q waves appear because dead myocardium is electrically silent, so an electrode over it records the opposite wall depolarizing away. A pathologic Q wave is commonly at least 40 ms wide or over about one quarter of the R wave height."},{"type":"flow","title":"ECG evolution in a transmural infarction","steps":["Minutes: hyperacute peaked T waves from potassium efflux","Minutes to hours: ST elevation with reciprocal depression","Hours to days: pathologic Q waves","Days: T inversion as the ST segment returns toward baseline","Weeks onward: Q waves persist; lasting ST elevation suggests aneurysm"],"caption":"Timing varies, but the order is consistent."},{"type":"p","text":"Infarction is a rise or fall of cardiac troponin with at least one value above the assay-specific 99th percentile, plus evidence of ischemia. Cardiac isoforms of troponin I and T make them the most specific markers. A small cytosolic pool leaks early and a large myofilament-bound pool is released slowly, explaining long persistence, whereas CK-MB is cytosolic and clears quickly. Myocarditis, pulmonary embolism, sepsis, heart failure, tachyarrhythmia, contusion, and chronic kidney disease also raise troponin, so infarction requires a rise and fall with ischemia."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardiac biomarkers after myocardial infarction","cols":["Marker","First rise","Peak","Normal again","Use"],"rows":[["Troponin I or T","About 3 to 4 h (earlier with high-sensitivity assays)","About 24 to 48 h","About 7 to 10 days (troponin T up to about 14)","Most specific; too persistent to show early reinfarction"],["CK-MB","About 3 to 6 h","About 24 h (up to 48 h in some sources)","About 48 to 72 h","Less specific; a new rise suggests reinfarction"],["Myoglobin","About 1 to 3 h","About 6 to 12 h","About 24 h","Earliest but nonspecific"]],"caption":"Approximate values; the order of rise and clearance is what boards test."},{"type":"example","title":"Recurrent chest pain on day 4","text":"On day 4 after an anterior STEMI, chest pain recurs. Troponin is still high from the first infarct, so one value cannot show new necrosis. CK-MB normalized by about 48 to 72 hours, so a new CK-MB rise points to reinfarction. If CK-MB is normal and the pain is pleuritic and positional, fibrinous pericarditis is more likely."},{"type":"stop","predict":"ST elevation in II, III, and aVF with reciprocal depression, a heart rate of 42 beats/min, and hypotension with clear lungs. Which artery is occluded, and why the slow rate and clear lungs?","explain":"The RCA. It usually supplies the SA and AV nodes, and inferior infarction triggers vagal reflexes. A proximal occlusion also infarcts the right ventricle, which cannot fill the left heart, so pressure falls while the lungs stay clear.","check":"The territory table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C05","obj":"CV-18-C.5","title":"The morphologic timeline of infarction and healing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An infarct heals by the sequence that follows acute injury in a tissue that cannot regenerate: coagulative necrosis, neutrophils, macrophage clearance, granulation tissue, and a collagen scar. Adult myocytes have very little capacity to divide, so dead muscle is replaced by scar. Ischemia and acidosis denature structural proteins and lytic enzymes alike, so dead myocytes keep their outlines for several days while nuclei fade and cytoplasm becomes more eosinophilic. Healing advances from the viable margins inward, so a large infarct heals more slowly and its center lags behind its edges."},{"type":"p","text":"Several early details are tested often. Wavy fibers appear at the border within hours because contracting living fibers stretch the noncontracting dead ones. Triphenyltetrazolium chloride stains viable muscle brick red through dehydrogenase activity, so an infarct appears as a pale unstained zone after about 2 to 3 hours, before light microscopy shows necrosis. Contraction bands appear at the margins in the first day and throughout reperfused infarcts. Neutrophils arrive near the end of day 1, drawn by chemokines and complement fragments."},{"type":"table","title":"Morphologic timeline of a myocardial infarct","cols":["Time","Gross","Light microscopy","Linked complication"],"rows":[["0 to 30 min","None","None (reversible changes by electron microscopy)","Arrhythmia"],["30 min to 4 h","None; pale on tetrazolium stain after about 2 to 3 h","Usually none; wavy fibers at the border","Ventricular fibrillation, sudden death"],["4 to 24 h","Occasional dark mottling","Early coagulative necrosis, edema, hemorrhage, contraction bands, early neutrophils","Arrhythmia, LV failure, cardiogenic shock"],["1 to 3 days","Mottling, yellow-tan center","Complete coagulative necrosis, loss of nuclei, brisk neutrophils","Fibrinous pericarditis"],["3 to 7 days","Hyperemic border, central softening","Disintegrating myocytes, macrophages at the border","Free wall, septal, or papillary muscle rupture"],["7 to 10 days","Softest and most yellow, red-tan margins","Phagocytosis, early granulation tissue at margins","Rupture risk persists"],["10 to 14 days","Red-gray depressed borders","Established granulation tissue, new vessels, collagen","Rupture less likely"],["2 to 8 weeks","Gray-white scar from border inward","Increasing collagen, decreasing cellularity","Aneurysm, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome"],["Over 2 months","Firm white scar","Dense collagen","Heart failure, scar-related ventricular tachycardia"]],"caption":"Ranges overlap and are approximate; the sequence of necrosis, neutrophils, macrophages, granulation tissue, and scar is constant."},{"type":"p","text":"Because each stage depends on the one before, the appearance of an infarct dates it, and the strength of the wall at each stage predicts its complications. The wall is weakest after macrophages have removed dead muscle and before collagen has formed, about days 3 to 10, which is the window for rupture. By about 2 months a typical infarct is dense collagen that is strong but cannot contract."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C06","obj":"CV-18-C.6","title":"Complications of myocardial infarction predicted by timing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complications follow from where the infarct is, how much muscle is lost, and how far healing has progressed (see the timeline in the previous section)."},{"type":"h","text":"First hours to days: arrhythmia and pump failure"},{"type":"p","text":"Ventricular fibrillation is the main cause of death in the first hours. Ischemic myocytes have a less negative resting potential, slow conduction, and altered refractoriness, so a wavefront can block in one direction and re-enter tissue it has passed, the substrate for reentrant tachycardia and fibrillation; automaticity, triggered activity, local hyperkalemia, and catecholamines add to the instability. Inferior infarcts cause sinus bradycardia and AV block through RCA supply of the nodes and vagal reflexes. Bundle branch block from an anterior septal infarct is more ominous because it reflects extensive necrosis below the AV node."},{"type":"p","text":"Lost muscle lowers stroke volume and a stiff ventricle raises filling pressure, causing pulmonary congestion; loss of about 40% or more of the LV causes cardiogenic shock. Right ventricular infarction from proximal RCA occlusion causes hypotension, distended neck veins, and clear lungs because the right ventricle cannot deliver blood to the left heart."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Vicious cycle of cardiogenic shock after large infarction","steps":["Loss of about 40% or more of LV muscle","Stroke volume and cardiac output fall","Aortic pressure falls; LV diastolic pressure rises","Coronary perfusion pressure falls","Ischemia extends into surviving muscle","Contractility falls further"],"caption":"Each turn removes more working muscle."},{"type":"h","text":"Pericarditis, rupture, and thrombus"},{"type":"p","text":"Over a transmural infarct, a fibrinous or fibrinohemorrhagic pericarditis forms within about 1 to 3 days, with pleuritic positional pain and a friction rub. Rupture occurs mostly at about 3 to 7 days, from day 1 to about 2 weeks or later, when macrophages have removed muscle and collagen has not yet formed. The posteromedial papillary muscle ruptures more often because it usually depends on the posterior descending artery alone, whereas the anterolateral muscle receives both LAD and circumflex branches. Rupture of a whole trunk is often rapidly fatal, and ischemia without rupture causes milder regurgitation. Over a large anterior apical infarct, infarct expansion, stasis, and endocardial injury (Virchow triad) favor a mural thrombus that can embolize to the brain, kidneys, spleen, or limbs."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanical complications of infarction","cols":["Complication","Typical infarct and timing","Mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Free wall rupture","Transmural, often anterolateral, classically a first infarct without hypertrophy or collaterals","Blood enters the pericardial sac","Tamponade (hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds), pulseless electrical activity, sudden death"],["Septal rupture","Anterior (apical septum) or inferior (basal septum)","Left-to-right shunt","New holosystolic murmur at left lower sternal border; oxygen step-up in the right ventricle"],["Papillary muscle rupture","Inferior, posteromedial muscle","Acute severe mitral regurgitation","Sudden pulmonary edema and hypotension; apical murmur that may be soft as pressures equalize"],["Pseudoaneurysm","Contained free wall rupture","Wall is pericardium and thrombus","Narrow neck; high risk of later rupture"],["True aneurysm","Large anterior apical transmural scar; weeks to months","Thin scar bulges in systole (dyskinesis)","Wide neck, rarely ruptures; heart failure, thrombus, ventricular tachycardia, persistent ST elevation"]],"caption":"The site that tears decides between tamponade, a shunt, and acute mitral regurgitation."},{"type":"h","text":"Weeks later: Dressler syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Dressler syndrome (post-myocardial infarction syndrome) is pericarditis several weeks after infarction, often with fever, pleuritis, and effusion. Necrosis exposes sequestered myocardial antigens, and antibodies and immune complexes inflame the pericardium. The delay fits an adaptive immune response, which separates it from the direct fibrinous pericarditis of days 1 to 3."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Five days after an inferior infarct, a patient abruptly develops severe dyspnea, bilateral crackles, and hypotension. Predict the structural cause and why it appears on day 5.","explain":"Posteromedial papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation. The muscle depends on the posterior descending artery, usually from the RCA, so it infarcts with the inferior wall, and on day 5 dead muscle has been removed before collagen forms.","check":"The mechanical complications table and the papillary muscle blood supply."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-03-C07","obj":"CV-18-C.7","title":"Chronic ischemic heart disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic ischemic heart disease, also called ischemic cardiomyopathy, is progressive heart failure from ischemic myocardial damage. Usually it is the late result of one or more healed infarcts, sometimes silent, plus ongoing ischemia from diffuse atherosclerosis; less often it develops without a recognized infarct. It is one of the most common causes of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart is enlarged and heavy, with a dilated, hypertrophied left ventricle, gray-white scars in coronary territories, moderate to severe coronary atherosclerosis, and sometimes patchy endocardial fibrosis and mural thrombi. Microscopy shows hypertrophied surviving myocytes, subendocardial myocyte vacuolization (a sign of chronic sublethal ischemia), and interstitial and replacement fibrosis. Regional scars matching coronary territories separate it from other dilated cardiomyopathies, where fibrosis is diffuse and coronary arteries are usually normal."},{"type":"h","text":"Remodeling"},{"type":"p","text":"Scar cannot contract, so surviving muscle carries the whole load. Wall stress and neurohormonal activation (sympathetic and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone, RAAS, with angiotensin II and aldosterone promoting fibrosis) drive remodeling: myocytes add sarcomeres in series (eccentric hypertrophy), the chamber enlarges and becomes more spherical, and myocytes continue to die by apoptosis and ischemia. A larger radius raises wall stress at any pressure, so dilation increases demand in a heart with limited supply."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Remodeling cycle in chronic ischemic heart disease","steps":["Infarction and ongoing ischemia reduce contracting muscle","Stroke volume falls; sympathetic and RAAS activation","Volume retention and eccentric hypertrophy dilate the LV","Larger radius raises wall stress and oxygen demand","Subendocardial ischemia, fibrosis, and myocyte loss worsen","Contractile function declines further"],"caption":"Dilation begins as compensation but perpetuates itself by raising the oxygen cost of each beat."},{"type":"example","title":"The cost of dilation by the law of Laplace","text":"Wall stress in a roughly spherical ventricle is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. Compare a ventricle generating 120 mmHg with a 2.5 cm radius and a 1.0 cm wall to a remodeled one generating 120 mmHg with a 3.5 cm radius and an average wall thinned to 0.8 cm by dilation and scarring. The ratio is (120 x 3.5 / 0.8) divided by (120 x 2.5 / 1.0), or 525 divided by 300, about 1.75. The dilated wall bears about 75% more stress for the same pressure, so it uses more oxygen. Hypertrophy partly offsets this, but adds muscle the narrowed arteries cannot supply."},{"type":"h","text":"Recoverable myocardium, valves, and rhythm"},{"type":"p","text":"Not all poorly contracting muscle is dead. Hibernating myocardium reduces contraction to match chronically reduced flow and can recover if flow is restored; repeated stunning may also contribute. Scar is permanent. Dilation pulls the papillary muscles apart and enlarges the mitral annulus, causing functional (secondary) mitral regurgitation, which adds volume load. Fibrosis and hypertrophy stiffen the ventricle and raise filling pressures, and left atrial enlargement predisposes to atrial fibrillation and atrial thrombus."},{"type":"p","text":"At scar borders, surviving myocyte strands interwoven with collagen conduct slowly, creating fixed reentry circuits for monomorphic ventricular tachycardia that can degenerate into fibrillation. Ischemic heart disease is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in adults, from a new ischemic event or from an old scar. Patients present with exertional dyspnea, fatigue, orthopnea, and edema; findings include a displaced apical impulse, a third heart sound from rapid filling of a dilated ventricle, an apical murmur of functional mitral regurgitation, and Q waves from old infarcts."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic heart disease shows how Part I disciplines combine in one patient. Anatomy explains why an inferior infarct involves the AV node, why the posteromedial papillary muscle ruptures, and why LAD infarcts are largest. Physiology explains supply and demand: high resting oxygen extraction, diastolic perfusion, subendocardial compression, and the law of Laplace. Biochemistry explains LDL uptake by scavenger receptors, the path from ATP depletion to calcium overload and necrosis, and troponin's two intracellular pools. Immunology and inflammation run throughout: macrophages and T cells weaken the plaque cap, healing follows neutrophils, macrophages, and fibroblasts, Dressler syndrome is a delayed autoimmune reaction, and Kawasaki disease, a childhood vasculitis of unknown cause, can leave coronary aneurysms. Microbiology contributes embolic infarction from endocarditis vegetations."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties these together by timing, because necrosis, inflammation, clearance, and repair proceed in a fixed order. The numbers in this chapter (the 70% stenosis threshold, the 20 to 40 minute window, healing day ranges, and biomarker kinetics) are consensus approximations that vary between sources, assays, and patients, and the STEMI with transmural and NSTEMI with subendocardial pairings are associations rather than identities. Board questions reward knowing the order of events and the mechanism behind each."}],"summary":["Ischemic heart disease is a mismatch between myocardial oxygen supply and demand; coronary atherosclerosis causes more than 90% of cases.","The heart extracts most coronary oxygen at rest, so it meets higher demand only by arteriolar dilation; the subendocardium, compressed in systole and perfused last, is most vulnerable.","Stable angina comes from a fixed stenosis of about 70% or more that exhausts flow reserve with exertion, causing subendocardial ischemia and ST depression.","Unstable angina is rest or crescendo pain from a disrupted plaque with a nonocclusive thrombus and normal troponin; Prinzmetal angina is transmural vasospasm with transient ST elevation.","Infarction follows plaque rupture and an occlusive platelet and fibrin thrombus; contraction stops within about 1 to 2 minutes and irreversible injury begins after about 20 to 40 minutes.","Necrosis spreads from subendocardium to epicardium over about 3 to 6 hours; transmural infarcts usually present as STEMI and subendocardial infarcts as NSTEMI.","ST elevation localizes the artery: LAD in V1 to V4, left circumflex in I, aVL, V5, V6, and RCA in II, III, aVF.","Troponin is the most specific marker and stays high about 7 to 10 days; CK-MB normalizes in about 2 to 3 days, so a new CK-MB rise suggests reinfarction.","Healing runs from coagulative necrosis to neutrophils (1 to 3 days), macrophages (3 to 7 days), granulation tissue, and scar by about 2 months, and complications follow: arrhythmia early, pericarditis at 1 to 3 days, rupture at 3 to 7 days, aneurysm and Dressler syndrome weeks later.","Chronic ischemic heart disease combines healed scars, hypertrophy, and LV dilation, leading to progressive heart failure, scar-related ventricular arrhythmia, and sudden cardiac death."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: ischemic heart disease (angina pectoris, myocardial infarction, chronic ischemic heart disease, sudden cardiac death)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels: atherosclerosis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease (coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction)"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Coronary circulation and myocardial oxygen consumption (section on special circulations)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, lipoprotein, and steroid metabolism"}]},"CV-18-D":{"unit":"CV-18-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04","title":"Valvular Heart Diseases","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how diseases of the heart valves change the load on the chambers around them and how those loads produce the murmurs, pulses, and symptoms that identify each lesion. It covers the four left-sided lesions, infective and sterile endocarditis, mitral valve prolapse, chronic rheumatic heart disease, carcinoid heart disease, and prosthetic valve complications. One principle runs through all of it: a stenotic valve imposes a pressure load on the chamber behind it, a regurgitant valve imposes a volume load, and the speed at which a lesion develops decides whether the heart can adapt. Board questions describe a murmur, pulse, maneuver, vegetation, or laboratory pattern and ask you to reason back to the lesion and its cause.","prerequisites":["Pressures, volumes, and valve events of the cardiac cycle, and the origin of S1 to S4 (CV-4)","Preload, afterload, the Frank-Starling mechanism, and the law of Laplace","Acute rheumatic fever and molecular mimicry (CV-16.2)","Endocarditis organisms and their virulence factors (CV-17)","Virchow's triad and the basic steps of platelet adhesion and coagulation"],"connections":[{"to":"Heart valves and the cardiac cycle (CV-4)","how":"Every murmur in this chapter is a predictable result of the pressure differences across valves during the cardiac cycle, and the maneuver rules apply them."},{"to":"Heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Pressure overload from stenosis and volume overload from regurgitation are major causes of both diastolic and systolic heart failure."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Papillary muscle ischemia or rupture after infarction and ischemic LV dilation are leading causes of mitral regurgitation."},{"to":"Congenital heart conditions (CV-18-G)","how":"The bicuspid aortic valve is the most common congenital cause of early aortic stenosis, regurgitation, and endocarditis."},{"to":"Infectious vascular diseases (CV-18-K) and immunology (CV-16)","how":"Endocarditis produces septic emboli, mycotic aneurysms, and immune complex disease, linking the valves to the vessels, kidneys, and skin."}],"terms":[{"term":"Stenosis","def":"Failure of a valve to open fully, which obstructs forward flow and imposes a pressure load on the chamber behind it."},{"term":"Regurgitation (insufficiency)","def":"Failure of a valve to close completely, so that blood flows backward and the chambers on both sides carry an extra volume."},{"term":"Concentric hypertrophy","def":"Thickening of the ventricular wall with a normal or small cavity, produced by adding sarcomeres in parallel in response to a pressure load."},{"term":"Eccentric hypertrophy","def":"Enlargement of the ventricular cavity with proportional wall growth, produced by adding sarcomeres in series in response to a volume load."},{"term":"Pulse pressure","def":"The difference between systolic and diastolic arterial pressure, which widens when stroke volume is large and diastolic runoff is rapid."},{"term":"Pulsus parvus et tardus","def":"A weak, slowly rising, late-peaking arterial pulse characteristic of severe aortic stenosis."},{"term":"Opening snap","def":"A high-pitched early diastolic sound produced when fused, stiff mitral leaflets dome open in mitral stenosis."},{"term":"Commissural fusion","def":"Joining of adjacent valve leaflets at their edges by scar tissue, the hallmark of rheumatic valve disease."},{"term":"Myxomatous degeneration","def":"Accumulation of proteoglycan in the spongiosa with weakening of the collagenous fibrosa, producing floppy, redundant leaflets."},{"term":"Vegetation","def":"A mass of platelets and fibrin, with or without organisms and inflammatory cells, attached to the endocardium or a valve."},{"term":"Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis","def":"Small sterile fibrin-platelet vegetations on the lines of closure of normal valves, arising in hypercoagulable states such as advanced cancer."},{"term":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis","def":"Sterile vegetations on either or both surfaces of the valve leaflets in systemic lupus erythematosus."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C01","obj":"CV-18-D.1","title":"Aortic stenosis: causes, pressure overload, and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The normal aortic valve has three cusps and an area of about 3 to 4 cm2. Aortic stenosis (AS) is fixed narrowing at the valve, and symptoms usually appear only when the area falls to about 1 cm2 or less, so the disease progresses silently for years."},{"type":"p","text":"Age-related calcific AS is the most common cause in developed countries and usually presents after about age 70 on a once-normal valve. It is an active process rather than simple wear: mechanical stress injures the cusp endothelium, low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a) enter the cusp, inflammation follows, and valve interstitial cells become osteoblast-like and deposit calcium. Heaped calcified masses bulge into the sinuses of Valsalva while the commissures stay unfused. The calcification is dystrophic (normal serum calcium), and the risk factors match those of atherosclerosis. A congenitally bicuspid valve (about 1 to 2% of people) concentrates stress, so the same process presents one to two decades earlier (CV-18-G.6). Rheumatic AS shows fused commissures and almost always accompanies rheumatic mitral disease (section C08)."},{"type":"p","text":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness. To push a normal stroke volume through a narrow orifice the left ventricle (LV) must generate a much higher pressure, so myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel and the wall thickens around a normal cavity (concentric hypertrophy). Ejection fraction is preserved for years, but the stiff wall needs atrial contraction to fill (S4), and demand outgrows the capillaries until fibrosis and failure follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Angina occurs even with normal coronary arteries: demand is high, a raised LV diastolic pressure compresses subendocardial vessels, and prolonged systole shortens diastolic perfusion time. Syncope is exertional, because exercise lowers peripheral resistance while output across a fixed orifice cannot rise; a ventricular mechanoreceptor reflex and arrhythmias add to it. Dyspnea comes from high diastolic pressure transmitted to the pulmonary veins. Once symptoms appear, classic series give average survival of about 5 years after angina, 3 after syncope, and 2 after heart failure."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in aortic stenosis and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur, right second intercostal space, radiating to the carotids","Flow rises and falls with ejection, starting after isovolumetric contraction; the jet aims up the aorta"],["Later peak as AS worsens; soft A2, single or paradoxically split S2","A tighter orifice delays peak flow; calcified cusps barely move, and prolonged ejection delays A2"],["Ejection click (bicuspid valve)","Mobile domed cusps stop abruptly; lost once calcified"],["Pulsus parvus et tardus; sustained apical impulse","The orifice limits how fast aortic pressure rises; a thick wall contracts long and hard"],["Softer with standing, Valsalva, or handgrip","Less preload means less flow; handgrip raises afterload and lowers the gradient"]],"caption":"Murmur loudness does not track severity, because a failing ventricle makes less flow and a softer murmur."},{"type":"p","text":"Doppler converts jet velocity (v, in m/s) to a gradient with the simplified Bernoulli equation, about 4 times v squared in mmHg. Severe AS is an area of about 1 cm2 or less, a peak velocity of about 4 m/s or more, and a mean gradient of about 40 mmHg or more. High shear unfolds von Willebrand factor multimers so that ADAMTS13 cleaves them, and the loss of large multimers favors bleeding from intestinal angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome)."},{"type":"example","title":"From Doppler velocity to wall thickness","text":"An aortic jet of 4.5 m/s gives a peak gradient of 4 times 4.5 squared, about 81 mmHg, so with an arterial systolic pressure of 130 mmHg the LV must generate about 210 mmHg. By Laplace, with an unchanged radius, wall stress normalizes only if thickness rises in proportion to pressure: 210 divided by 130 is about 1.6, so a 1.0 cm wall must become about 1.6 cm thick. That wall is stiff (S4, dyspnea), hungry for oxygen while compressing its subendocardial vessels (angina), and unable to raise output during exercise (syncope)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C02","obj":"CV-18-D.2","title":"Aortic regurgitation: causes, volume overload, and the wide pulse pressure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aortic regurgitation (AR) is diastolic backflow from the aorta into the LV, from damaged cusps (leaflet disease) or from normal cusps pulled apart by a dilated root (root disease, with its own risk of aneurysm and dissection)."},{"type":"p","text":"Root causes include hypertension and aging; Marfan syndrome, in which mutant fibrillin-1 (FBN1) releases excess TGF-beta activity and causes cystic medial degeneration; tertiary syphilis, in which endarteritis of the vasa vasorum scars the aortic media and gives the intima a tree-bark look; ankylosing spondylitis; and ascending aortic dissection. Leaflet causes include a bicuspid valve, endocarditis that perforates a cusp, and rheumatic cusp retraction, usually with mitral disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Chronic AR: volume and pressure load together"},{"type":"p","text":"The LV receives atrial inflow plus the regurgitant volume, so end-diastolic volume and, by the Frank-Starling mechanism, total stroke volume rise. Ejecting that large volume into the high-pressure aorta adds a pressure load to the volume load. Sarcomeres are added in series, the chamber dilates with proportional wall growth (eccentric hypertrophy), and chronic AR produces some of the largest ventricles seen. The dilated chamber is compliant, so diastolic pressure stays near normal and patients may be asymptomatic for years, until dilation outruns hypertrophy, contractility falls, and failure follows."},{"type":"h","text":"Wide pulse pressure"},{"type":"p","text":"Systolic pressure rises with the large, rapidly ejected stroke volume, and diastolic pressure falls because the aorta empties both back into the LV and forward into vasodilated tissues (for example 170/45 mmHg). The rapid rise and collapse give bounding (water-hammer or Corrigan) pulses and named signs of the same wide pulse pressure: head bobbing (de Musset), nail bed capillary pulsation (Quincke), a pistol-shot femoral sound (Traube), and a to-and-fro femoral bruit on compression (Duroziez). The apical impulse is hyperdynamic and displaced down and laterally. Low aortic diastolic pressure also lowers coronary perfusion pressure and can cause angina."},{"type":"h","text":"Murmur and related sounds"},{"type":"p","text":"The murmur is high-pitched, blowing, early diastolic, and decrescendo, starting at A2 when the gradient from aorta to LV is greatest. It is heard best at the left sternal border with the patient sitting forward in held expiration, and handgrip makes it louder by raising aortic diastolic pressure. Because regurgitation occurs only in diastole, tachycardia reduces the regurgitant volume per beat and bradycardia increases it. An Austin Flint murmur is an apical mid-to-late diastolic rumble from the regurgitant jet partly closing the anterior mitral leaflet; unlike mitral stenosis it has no opening snap or loud S1."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute AR (endocarditis, dissection, trauma) strikes a normal-sized, stiff LV: diastolic pressure rises steeply and may close the mitral valve early, and pulmonary edema and shock follow. Aortic and LV pressures equalize quickly, so the murmur is short and the pulse pressure is not wide (table in section C04)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C03","obj":"CV-18-D.3","title":"Mitral stenosis: the atrium under pressure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The normal mitral valve area is about 4 to 6 cm2, so left atrial (LA) and LV pressures are nearly equal in diastole. Mitral stenosis (MS) is almost always rheumatic (section C08); heavy mitral annular calcification in older adults and congenital malformations are uncommon causes."},{"type":"h","text":"Hemodynamics: the upstream chambers pay"},{"type":"p","text":"Flow across an orifice varies with its area times the square root of the pressure difference. Below about 2.5 cm2 a gradient appears, and at about 1.5 cm2 or less (severe MS) LA pressure must rise substantially to fill the LV. The LA hypertrophies and dilates, while the LV downstream is underfilled, normal or small in size, with normal or low diastolic pressure. The hallmark is a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure higher than LV diastolic pressure. Hemoptysis comes from rupture of bronchial veins that connect with the congested pulmonary veins."},{"type":"flow","title":"Backward transmission of pressure in mitral stenosis","steps":["Fused, narrowed mitral orifice","LA pressure rises to drive flow into the LV","LA hypertrophies and dilates; the LV stays small","Pulmonary venous pressure rises: dyspnea, edema, hemoptysis","Reactive arteriolar constriction and remodeling: pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, tricuspid regurgitation","Right heart failure: neck veins, hepatomegaly, edema"],"caption":"Every chamber upstream of the narrowed valve is loaded, while the LV downstream is protected."},{"type":"p","text":"The LV fills only in diastole, and tachycardia shortens diastole more than systole, so the same volume must cross the valve at a faster rate. Because the gradient rises with the square of the flow rate, a modest rise in heart rate raises LA pressure sharply; a rise from 70 to 120 per minute can roughly double the gradient. Exercise, fever, anemia, atrial fibrillation, and pregnancy (more blood volume, output, and rate) therefore precipitate the first pulmonary edema in previously silent MS."},{"type":"p","text":"The stretched LA fibroses and fibrillates. Atrial fibrillation removes the atrial contribution to filling and shortens diastole, and stasis in the LA appendage forms mural thrombi that embolize to the brain, kidneys, spleen, and limbs. The enlarged LA, the most posterior chamber, can indent the esophagus (dysphagia), compress the left recurrent laryngeal nerve between the aortic arch and the LA or a dilated pulmonary artery (hoarseness, Ortner syndrome), and elevate the left main bronchus, widening the carinal angle."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in mitral stenosis and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Loud S1","High LA pressure holds the leaflets wide open until systole begins; S1 softens once they calcify"],["Opening snap after S2","Fused leaflets dome open abruptly when LV pressure falls below LA pressure"],["Shorter S2 to opening snap interval","Higher LA pressure opens the valve sooner after A2, so the interval shortens as MS worsens"],["Low-pitched apical mid-diastolic rumble","Turbulent flow through the orifice, heard with the bell in left lateral decubitus; its length, not loudness, tracks severity"],["Presystolic accentuation","Atrial contraction boosts flow; lost in atrial fibrillation"],["Broad notched P in lead II and deep terminal negative P in V1; right axis deviation","LA enlargement (P mitrale); pulmonary hypertension with right ventricular hypertrophy"]],"caption":"The opening snap is diastolic and belongs to MS; the mid-systolic click belongs to mitral valve prolapse (section C07)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a narrowed mitral valve and pulmonary edema. Predict the LV end-diastolic pressure and LV size.","explain":"Both are normal or low. The obstruction lies upstream of the LV, so the ventricle is underfilled rather than overloaded. The edema comes from high LA and pulmonary venous pressure behind the valve, which is why wedge pressure exceeds LV diastolic pressure in MS, unlike pulmonary edema from LV failure.","check":"Compare with the hemodynamics paragraph and the backward transmission figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C04","obj":"CV-18-D.4","title":"Mitral regurgitation: causes, volume overload, and the holosystolic murmur","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mitral competence depends on the whole apparatus: annulus, leaflets, chordae, papillary muscles, and the LV wall that holds them. Mitral regurgitation (MR) is systolic backflow into the LA from failure of any part. In primary MR the leaflets or chordae are abnormal: mitral valve prolapse (the most common primary cause in developed countries, section C07), rheumatic disease (C08), endocarditis (C05), and papillary muscle or chordal rupture. After infarction the posteromedial papillary muscle, which usually has a single arterial supply, ruptures most often (CV-18-C.6). In secondary (functional) MR a dilated LV stretches the annulus and displaces the papillary muscles, tethering normal leaflets, and the added volume dilates the LV further, so functional MR feeds itself. Ischemic papillary muscle dysfunction causes transient MR."},{"type":"p","text":"Because LA pressure is low, backflow starts at the onset of systole, before the aortic valve opens, so there is no true isovolumetric contraction. The regurgitant volume returns to the LV in early diastole, so both chambers are volume loaded. The LA dilates and becomes compliant, holding a large volume at modest pressure, and often fibrillates. The LV develops eccentric hypertrophy, but because it unloads into the low-pressure LA, ejection fraction overstates contractility, and a low-normal ejection fraction already signals impairment."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a normal ejection fraction misleads in MR","text":"An LV in chronic MR has an end-diastolic volume of 200 mL and an end-systolic volume of 70 mL. Total stroke volume is 130 mL and ejection fraction is 65%, which looks normal. If 65 mL flows back into the LA, the regurgitant fraction (backflow as a share of total stroke volume) is 50%, and forward stroke volume is only 65 mL. If contractility declines so end-systolic volume rises to 100 mL with the same filling and regurgitant fraction, ejection fraction falls to 50% and forward stroke volume to 50 mL. A value a clinician might call low-normal reflects a ventricle that is already failing, because the low-pressure outlet flatters the ejection fraction."},{"type":"p","text":"The murmur is holosystolic, high-pitched, and blowing, from S1 through A2, loudest at the apex and radiating to the left axilla. Handgrip and squatting make it louder and Valsalva or standing softens it (MVP behaves differently, section C07); a ventricular septal defect murmur lies at the left lower sternal border instead. Rapid early diastolic inflow from the loaded LA produces an S3 that reflects volume rather than proving failure. S1 is soft, and early A2 widens the split of S2."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute MR, from papillary muscle or chordal rupture or endocarditis, fills a small, stiff LA: tall v waves on the wedge tracing, sudden pulmonary edema, and hypotension. LA pressure approaches LV pressure late in systole, so the murmur may be short, soft, or nearly absent."},{"type":"table","title":"Chronic versus acute regurgitation (AR and MR)","cols":["Feature","Chronic","Acute"],"rows":[["Receiving chamber","Dilated and compliant (LV in AR; LA and LV in MR)","Normal size and stiff"],["Filling pressure","Near normal or moderately raised for years","Rises steeply (LV diastolic in AR; LA with tall v waves in MR)"],["Murmur","Long: early diastolic decrescendo (AR), holosystolic (MR)","Short and soft, sometimes nearly absent"],["Pulse pressure in AR","Wide, with bounding pulses","Not wide"],["Presentation","Silent for years, then exertional dyspnea and failure","Sudden pulmonary edema and shock"],["Typical causes","AR: root dilation, bicuspid, rheumatic. MR: MVP, rheumatic, LV dilation","Endocarditis, aortic dissection, trauma; papillary muscle or chordal rupture"]],"caption":"Compliance of the receiving chamber, not regurgitant volume alone, decides whether the patient compensates."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C05","obj":"CV-18-D.5","title":"Infective endocarditis: acute versus subacute disease and its peripheral signs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infective endocarditis is microbial infection of the endocardium, usually a valve, forming vegetations of platelets, fibrin, organisms, and inflammatory cells. Organisms grow inside an avascular fibrin mesh that neutrophils reach poorly, so the vegetation sheds them continuously."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal endothelium resists attachment. Turbulence, a scarred or congenitally abnormal valve, a prosthesis, or a catheter exposes collagen and tissue factor, and a sterile platelet-fibrin deposit forms (section C06). A transient bacteremia from chewing, dental work, procedures, injection drug use, or skin infection then seeds it. Viridans streptococci attach through adhesins and dextran, and Staphylococcus aureus binds fibrinogen and fibronectin through surface proteins such as clumping factor (CV-17.1)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of infective endocarditis","steps":["Turbulence or prior damage injures valve endothelium","Platelets and fibrin form a sterile nidus","Transient bacteremia delivers organisms","Adhesins attach organisms to the deposit","Organisms multiply under fibrin, shielded from phagocytes","Vegetation grows, destroys tissue, sheds emboli and organisms"],"caption":"Virulent S. aureus can invade intact endothelium and skip the first two steps, which is why it infects normal valves."},{"type":"p","text":"Virulence sets the tempo. S. aureus destroys tissue and infects normal valves, causing acute endocarditis over days with high fever, rigors, large destructive vegetations, ring abscesses, and metastatic infection; skin, injection drug use, and catheters are its portals. Viridans streptococci, oral flora of low virulence, need a damaged valve (rheumatic, congenital, bicuspid, or MVP with MR) and cause subacute disease over weeks to months with low-grade fever, fatigue, weight loss, anemia, splenomegaly, and, when long-standing, clubbing. Prolonged bacteremia allows immune complexes to form, so immunologic signs dominate subacute disease. Enterococci after genitourinary or gastrointestinal procedures and Streptococcus gallolyticus with colon cancer usually follow a subacute course (CV-17.2)."},{"type":"p","text":"Left-sided valves face higher pressures and turbulent jets, so the mitral valve is most often involved, closely followed by the aortic. In people who inject drugs the tricuspid valve is the most common site, because skin organisms and injected particles reach it first; S. aureus dominates, with Pseudomonas and Candida less often. Right-sided vegetations embolize to the lungs as multiple, often cavitating septic emboli. Left-sided fragments cause infarcts and abscesses in the brain, spleen, kidneys, and limbs and seed arterial walls as mycotic aneurysms (CV-18-K.2)."},{"type":"table","title":"Peripheral signs of infective endocarditis","cols":["Sign","Appearance","Pain","Usual mechanism"],"rows":[["Janeway lesions","Flat red or hemorrhagic macules on palms and soles","Painless","Septic microemboli with small dermal abscesses; more typical of acute disease"],["Osler nodes","Raised red-purple nodules on finger and toe pads","Painful","Immune complex small vessel vasculitis; more typical of subacute disease"],["Roth spots","Retinal hemorrhages with a pale center","Painless","Fibrin-platelet plug from immune vasculitis or microemboli"],["Splinter hemorrhages, conjunctival petechiae","Linear streaks under the nails; palpebral petechiae","Painless","Microemboli or capillary damage; nonspecific"],["Glomerulonephritis","Hematuria, red cell casts, low complement","Not applicable","Immune complex deposition in glomeruli"]],"caption":"Embolic lesions are painless, while immune complex lesions such as Osler nodes hurt because the vessel wall is inflamed."},{"type":"p","text":"Continuous shedding makes bacteremia persistent, so cultures drawn at different times grow the same organism. The Duke criteria combine positive cultures and echocardiographic evidence (vegetation, abscess, new regurgitation) as major criteria with minor criteria such as a predisposing condition, fever of 38.0°C (100.4°F) or higher, and embolic or immunologic phenomena. Inflammation raises the erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein and causes anemia of chronic inflammation through hepcidin, and rheumatoid factor may be positive in subacute disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C06","obj":"CV-18-D.6","title":"Sterile vegetations: nonbacterial thrombotic and Libman-Sacks endocarditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Two noninfective forms produce sterile vegetations whose main danger is embolism, not infection or valve destruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis (NBTE) consists of small (about 1 to 5 mm), bland, loosely attached fibrin-platelet thrombi along the lines of closure of normal mitral and aortic leaflets, with no organisms and little inflammation. It arises when hypercoagulability meets the normal wear at the closure line. The key association is advanced malignancy, especially mucin-producing adenocarcinoma of the pancreas or lung, whose tissue factor and mucins (which engage selectins on platelets and leukocytes) also cause migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome). DIC, sepsis, antiphospholipid syndrome, and indwelling catheters are other settings. There is usually no murmur, but fragments cause infarcts in the brain, kidneys, and spleen; multiple strokes in different arterial territories in a patient with cancer are classic. An NBTE lesion can also become the nidus for infective endocarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis occurs in systemic lupus erythematosus, often with antiphospholipid antibodies. Its small to medium (about 1 to 4 mm) warty vegetations can sit on either or both leaflet surfaces, including the ventricular undersurface of the mitral valve, and on the chordae and mural endocardium. They show fibrinoid necrosis with inflammation from immune complex injury (CV-16.4), and healing fibrosis deforms the leaflets, most often causing MR. Emboli and the thrombotic tendency of antiphospholipid syndrome add risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of valve vegetations","cols":["Feature","Infective","NBTE","Libman-Sacks","Acute rheumatic fever"],"rows":[["Size","Large, bulky, friable","About 1 to 5 mm","About 1 to 4 mm","About 1 to 2 mm warty nodules"],["Position","Closure lines; can spread to chordae and wall","Lines of closure","Either or both surfaces, chordae, mural endocardium","Lines of closure"],["Organisms","Present","Absent","Absent","Absent"],["Inflammation and damage","Marked; perforation, abscess","Minimal; none","Fibrinoid necrosis; scarring with MR","Part of pancarditis; scarring after recurrences"],["Emboli","Septic","Bland, often multiple infarcts","Bland","Uncommon"],["Setting","Bacteremia","Cancer, DIC, hypercoagulable states","Lupus, antiphospholipid antibodies","2 to 4 weeks after group A streptococcal pharyngitis"]],"caption":"Size and destruction separate infected from sterile vegetations; both leaflet surfaces point to Libman-Sacks; the clinical setting separates NBTE from rheumatic lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Negative blood cultures do not prove a vegetation is sterile, since fastidious organisms and prior antibiotics cause culture-negative infective endocarditis. Persistent fever, valve destruction, new regurgitation, and immunologic signs favor infection, whereas cancer, lupus, or a coagulopathy with bland infarcts favors a sterile lesion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C07","obj":"CV-18-D.7","title":"Mitral valve prolapse: myxomatous degeneration and the moving click","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is systolic billowing of one or both mitral leaflets above the annulus into the LA. It affects about 2 to 3% of adults and is usually asymptomatic."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion is myxomatous degeneration. The spongiosa expands with proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans, and the collagen of the fibrosa becomes thin and disorganized as valve interstitial cells overproduce matrix-degrading enzymes. Leaflets become thick, redundant, and hooded between chordal attachments, the chordae elongate, thin, and may rupture, the posterior leaflet is most often involved, and the annulus may dilate. Most MVP is sporadic or familial, but heritable connective tissue disorders show the mechanism. In Marfan syndrome, deficient fibrillin-1 microfibrils fail to hold TGF-beta inactive, and excess signaling disrupts the valve matrix; in Ehlers-Danlos syndromes defective collagen weakens the fibrosa; and autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, with abnormal polycystins, is associated with MVP as well as berry aneurysms (CV-14.3)."},{"type":"h","text":"The mid-systolic click and why maneuvers move it"},{"type":"p","text":"As the ventricle empties in systole, the papillary muscles and annulus move closer together and the redundant leaflets lose support. At a critical LV size they billow into the LA until the slack chordae suddenly tighten, producing a sharp mid-systolic click at the apex. If coaptation then fails, a late systolic MR murmur follows the click to S2."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that makes the LV smaller (standing, the strain phase of Valsalva) reaches the critical size sooner, so the click moves toward S1 and the murmur lengthens. Anything that enlarges it (squatting, passive leg raise, sustained handgrip) delays the click toward S2 and shortens the murmur. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy also worsens with a smaller ventricle, whereas the AS murmur softens. The click is systolic and moves with LV size, while the opening snap of MS is diastolic and moves with LA pressure (general maneuver physiology in CV-4.7)."},{"type":"p","text":"Most people never develop complications; those that occur follow from the weak tissue: progressive MR as leaflets stretch, acute severe MR when a thinned chorda ruptures and leaves a flail segment, infective endocarditis on prolapsing leaflets (especially with MR), platelet-fibrin thrombi on the atrial surface that embolize and cause transient ischemic attacks or stroke, and atrial and ventricular arrhythmias linked to papillary muscle traction and adjacent fibrosis. Sudden cardiac death is rare."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with MVP squats from standing. Predict the timing of the click and the length of the murmur.","explain":"The click moves later toward S2 and the murmur shortens. Squatting raises venous return and afterload, so the LV stays larger through systole and takes longer to shrink to the size at which the leaflets prolapse.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on why maneuvers move the click."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C08","obj":"CV-18-D.8","title":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease: from repeated carditis to scarred valves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies and T cells against streptococcal antigens, particularly M protein, cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve tissue (molecular mimicry), causing a pancarditis with Aschoff bodies and tiny sterile vegetations along the lines of closure (acute disease, Jones criteria, and antistreptolysin O titers in CV-16.2). Chronic rheumatic heart disease is the scar that follows."},{"type":"p","text":"A single attack may heal with little damage, but each new streptococcal throat infection can reinflame already scarred valves. Healing replaces inflamed tissue with fibrous tissue and brings new vessels into normally avascular leaflets, so damage accumulates and becomes evident years to decades after the first attack. Rheumatic disease remains the leading cause of MS worldwide, especially where streptococcal pharyngitis goes untreated."},{"type":"flow","title":"From streptococcal pharyngitis to chronic rheumatic valve disease","steps":["Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","Molecular mimicry causes acute rheumatic carditis","Valvulitis with small vegetations on closure lines","Healing by fibrosis and neovascularization","Recurrent infections reinflame scarred valves","Commissural fusion; thick, short, fused chordae","Fish-mouth MS, often with MR and aortic disease"],"caption":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease is the scar of repeated immune injury, not an ongoing valve infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Four changes define the chronic lesion: thickened, fibrotic, later calcified leaflets; fused commissures; thickened, shortened, fused chordae; and leaflet neovascularization. Seen from the atrium, the stenotic mitral valve has a rigid fish-mouth or buttonhole orifice, and Aschoff bodies are rarely found because scar has replaced them. Commissural fusion causes stenosis, the signature rheumatic lesion, while retracted leaflets and short chordae prevent closure, so combined stenosis and regurgitation is common. Degenerative disease, by contrast, usually causes isolated AS or isolated MR."},{"type":"p","text":"The mitral valve is involved in nearly all cases, alone in about two-thirds and together with the aortic valve in about one-quarter. Tricuspid involvement almost always accompanies left-sided disease, and the pulmonic valve is rarely affected; the usual explanation is that left-sided valves close against systemic pressure and scar more after each episode. Rheumatic aortic disease almost never occurs without mitral disease, so isolated aortic disease in an older adult suggests a calcific or bicuspid valve (section C01). Consequences are those of MS (section C03), plus subacute endocarditis by viridans streptococci (C05) and classic decompensation in pregnancy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C09","obj":"CV-18-D.9","title":"Carcinoid heart disease: serotonin and the right-sided valves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carcinoid tumors are well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors of enterochromaffin cells, most often in the ileum. Many synthesize serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) from tryptophan through tryptophan hydroxylase and aromatic amino acid decarboxylase, and they also release histamine, kallikrein (which generates bradykinin), tachykinins, and prostaglandins. Monoamine oxidase (MAO) normally converts serotonin to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), which is excreted in the urine."},{"type":"p","text":"Gut venous blood passes through the liver, where MAO inactivates these products, so a tumor confined to the bowel usually causes no systemic effects. Liver metastases release products into the hepatic veins and the right atrium, exposing the tricuspid and pulmonic valves first. The lungs protect the left heart, because pulmonary endothelium takes up serotonin through its transporter and degrades it with MAO. Left-sided lesions appear with a patent foramen ovale or other right-to-left shunt, very high levels, or a bronchial carcinoid draining into the pulmonary veins. Ovarian carcinoids, which drain outside the portal system, can cause the syndrome without liver metastases."},{"type":"flow","title":"Carcinoid heart disease","steps":["Serotonin-secreting tumor, often in the ileum","Metastasis to the liver","Serotonin enters hepatic veins, bypassing hepatic MAO","High serotonin reaches the right heart","5-HT2B signaling activates valve interstitial cells","Fibrous plaques on tricuspid and pulmonic valves","Lung MAO lowers serotonin before the left heart"],"caption":"The portal circulation and the lungs act as two filters, which explains why liver metastases are required and why the left heart is usually spared."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesions are pearly white plaques of myofibroblasts in glycosaminoglycan and collagen, laid on right-sided endocardium without destroying the valve beneath. Serotonin acting on 5-HT2B receptors of valve interstitial cells, partly through TGF-beta, drives proliferation and matrix production; 5-HT2B agonists such as fenfluramine and certain ergot-derived dopamine agonists produce similar plaques. Tricuspid leaflets are fixed open, causing tricuspid regurgitation, the most common lesion, and tricuspid stenosis can also occur. Pulmonic cusps thicken and retract, so pulmonic stenosis often coexists with regurgitation, and the right ventricle fails under combined volume and pressure loads."},{"type":"p","text":"Kinins, histamine, and tachykinins cause episodic flushing and bronchospasm with wheezing, and serotonin causes watery diarrhea and cramps. Tricuspid regurgitation gives a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration (more venous return to the right heart), with large jugular v waves, a pulsatile liver, edema, and ascites; pulmonic stenosis adds a systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border. Urine 5-HIAA over 24 hours is high, and diversion of tryptophan to serotonin can leave too little for niacin synthesis, causing pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a bronchial carcinoid without liver metastases and develops a new valve lesion. Predict which side of the heart is affected.","explain":"The left-sided valves are at risk. A bronchial tumor drains into the pulmonary veins, which carry its products to the LA without passing through the liver or the pulmonary capillary bed where MAO would inactivate them, while the right heart receives systemic venous blood with little serotonin.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the two filters and the carcinoid figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-04-C10","obj":"CV-18-D.10","title":"Complications of prosthetic heart valves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mechanical valves (usually bileaflet pyrolytic carbon) last decades and click crisply, but their foreign surface and abnormal flow make them highly thrombogenic. Bioprosthetic valves are porcine valves or bovine pericardium fixed in glutaraldehyde, which crosslinks collagen, kills the cells, and lowers antigenicity. The tissue is far less thrombogenic but nonviable and unable to repair itself, so structural deterioration commonly occurs within about 10 to 20 years, faster in younger patients. Transcatheter valves are bioprosthetic tissue on a metal frame."},{"type":"p","text":"Virchow's triad explains thrombosis: the foreign surface adsorbs plasma proteins and activates platelets and the contact pathway, flow around the hinges creates zones of stasis and high shear, and hypercoagulability or interrupted anticoagulation completes the triad. Mitral prostheses are at greater risk than aortic ones because mitral flow is slower and the LA is often large and fibrillating. Thrombus can immobilize a leaflet (new murmur, muffled clicks, heart failure, shock) or embolize. Pannus is a slower fibrous ingrowth from the sewing ring that narrows the orifice over months to years."},{"type":"p","text":"High-velocity turbulent flow fragments red cells, especially through a paravalvular leak (a gap between the sewing ring and the tissue). The intravascular hemolysis gives schistocytes (fragmented red cells, including helmet cells), high lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and unconjugated bilirubin, low or absent haptoglobin, reticulocytosis, and hemoglobinuria. Tubular cells store filtered hemoglobin iron as hemosiderin and shed it, so chronic urinary iron loss can cause iron deficiency. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative because the destruction is mechanical, and a normal platelet count separates it from DIC, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, and hemolytic uremic syndrome, which also produce schistocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanical hemolysis from a prosthetic valve","steps":["High-velocity jet, worst with a paravalvular leak","Shear stress fragments red cells","Schistocytes form; hemoglobin enters plasma","Haptoglobin is depleted; LDH and bilirubin rise","Filtered hemoglobin: hemoglobinuria, hemosiderinuria","Chronic urinary iron loss: iron deficiency"],"caption":"Each laboratory abnormality marks a step in the destruction of red cells within the circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Bioprosthetic tissue undergoes dystrophic calcification as calcium binds phospholipids of the devitalized cells and altered collagen, faster in children and young adults, and collagen fatigue from repeated flexing adds to it. Cusps stiffen (stenosis) or tear (regurgitation, sometimes sudden)."},{"type":"p","text":"Prosthetic valve endocarditis begins at the sewing ring and often spreads into the annulus, causing ring abscess, dehiscence, paravalvular leak, and heart block. Early disease (within about the first year, most often the first 2 months) reflects perioperative contamination: coagulase-negative staphylococci, especially Staphylococcus epidermidis, along with S. aureus, gram-negative bacilli, and Candida. This low-virulence skin organism makes a polysaccharide biofilm that binds synthetic material and shields it from neutrophils, complement, and antibiotics; it is a catalase-positive, coagulase-negative, novobiocin-sensitive gram-positive coccus in clusters. Late disease resembles native valve endocarditis."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of prosthetic heart valves","cols":["Complication","Valve most affected","Key findings"],"rows":[["Thrombosis and embolism","Mechanical, especially mitral","New murmur, muffled clicks, heart failure; stroke or limb infarction"],["Mechanical hemolysis","Mechanical, or any valve with a paravalvular leak","Schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin, hemosiderinuria, negative Coombs test"],["Structural deterioration","Bioprosthetic","Gradual stenosis, or sudden regurgitation from a cusp tear"],["Endocarditis","Both","Fever, paravalvular regurgitation, ring abscess, heart block; early often S. epidermidis"],["Pannus","Both","Slowly progressive obstruction"]],"caption":"Each complication follows from the material, the abnormal flow, or the foreign-body interface."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology show where valve disease begins. Each leaflet is a layered structure of fibrosa, spongiosa, and ventricularis maintained by valve interstitial cells: calcific AS is a disease of those cells turning bone-forming, MVP a disease of excess proteoglycan and weak collagen, rheumatic disease a scar that fuses commissures and shortens chordae, and carcinoid disease a plaque laid on the surface. Chamber relationships explain remote signs, such as an enlarged LA pressing on the esophagus and left recurrent laryngeal nerve, and portal drainage explains why carcinoid tumors need liver metastases."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology supplies the unifying rule: pressure loads produce concentric hypertrophy, volume loads eccentric hypertrophy, and receiving-chamber compliance separates chronic from acute regurgitation. Microbiology and immunology explain the causes (virulence, adhesins, biofilm, molecular mimicry, immune complexes), and coagulation links the sterile lesions and prosthetic thrombosis. Bedside findings have limits: murmur loudness misleads in a failing ventricle, acute regurgitation can be nearly silent, several peripheral signs of endocarditis are nonspecific, and severity thresholds are guideline consensus applied with the whole picture."}],"summary":["Stenosis imposes a pressure load and produces concentric hypertrophy; regurgitation imposes a volume load and produces eccentric hypertrophy.","Calcific AS is an active bone-forming process (earlier on a bicuspid valve) causing a carotid-radiating crescendo-decrescendo murmur, pulsus parvus et tardus, and angina, exertional syncope, and dyspnea.","Chronic AR widens the pulse pressure through a large stroke volume and two-way diastolic runoff; its murmur is early diastolic and decrescendo.","Acute AR and acute MR strike a stiff chamber, so filling pressures rise steeply, edema and shock appear, and the murmur may be short and soft.","Mitral stenosis is almost always rheumatic, loads the LA, lungs, and right heart while sparing the LV, worsens with tachycardia, and a shorter S2 to opening snap interval means higher LA pressure.","Mitral regurgitation gives a holosystolic apical murmur radiating to the axilla and an S3, and the low-pressure LA outlet makes ejection fraction overstate LV function.","Acute endocarditis is usually S. aureus on normal valves, subacute is usually viridans streptococci on damaged valves, and injection drug use favors the tricuspid valve; Janeway lesions are painless and embolic, Osler nodes painful and immune.","NBTE (cancer, hypercoagulability) and Libman-Sacks endocarditis (lupus, either leaflet surface) are sterile and nondestructive, and their main danger is embolism.","MVP is myxomatous degeneration; a smaller LV moves the click earlier and lengthens the murmur. Chronic rheumatic disease fuses commissures and chordae, mainly of the mitral valve.","Carcinoid heart disease needs serotonin to bypass the liver, causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis, and spares the left heart through lung MAO; prosthetic valves fail by thrombosis, shear hemolysis, tissue degeneration, and endocarditis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: valvular heart disease (calcific aortic stenosis, myxomatous mitral valve, rheumatic heart disease, infective and noninfective vegetations, carcinoid heart disease, complications of artificial valves)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump: cardiac cycle, heart sounds, and ventricular pressure-volume relationships"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease (valvular heart disease and infective endocarditis)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Staphylococci; streptococci and enterococci"}]},"CV-18-E":{"unit":"CV-18-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05","title":"Primary Myocardial Diseases","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers diseases in which the heart muscle itself is the problem: dilated, hypertrophic, and restrictive cardiomyopathy, myocarditis, stress (takotsubo) cardiomyopathy, and tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy. Board questions describe a patient, an echocardiogram, a murmur that changes with a maneuver, an ECG, or a biopsy and ask you to name the disease, its cause, or its mechanism. The key skill is deciding whether the ventricle fails to squeeze (systolic failure) or fails to fill (diastolic failure), and then explaining each finding from that defect. If you can connect wall stress, compliance, and preload to chamber shape, heart sounds, and murmurs, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Sarcomere structure and excitation-contraction coupling (CV-5)","Cardiac cycle, heart sounds, and pressure-volume loops (CV-4)","Preload, afterload, contractility, and the Frank-Starling mechanism","Law of Laplace and ventricular wall stress","Systolic and diastolic heart failure (CV-18-B)"],"connections":[{"to":"Heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Dilated cardiomyopathy is a model of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, and hypertrophic and restrictive disease are models of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction."},{"to":"Conduction system and ECG (CV-7)","how":"Fibrosis, disarray, infiltration, and inflammation of the myocardium create the reentry substrate and conduction block behind sudden death in these diseases."},{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Functional mitral regurgitation in dilated disease and the dynamic outflow murmur of hypertrophic disease use the same maneuver logic as valve murmurs."},{"to":"Pericardial disease (CV-18-F)","how":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis both limit filling and raise venous pressure, so they are classic look-alikes."},{"to":"Cardiovascular pathogens and immunology (CV-16, CV-17)","how":"Coxsackie B virus, Trypanosoma cruzi, Borrelia burgdorferi, diphtheria toxin, and T-cell autoimmunity are causes of myocarditis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cardiomyopathy","def":"A disease of the heart muscle that causes mechanical or electrical dysfunction and is not explained by coronary disease, hypertension, valve disease, or congenital defects alone."},{"term":"Ejection fraction (EF)","def":"Stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, normally about 55% to 70%."},{"term":"Systolic dysfunction","def":"Reduced ability of the ventricle to contract, so the ejection fraction falls and end-systolic volume rises."},{"term":"Diastolic dysfunction","def":"Impaired relaxation or reduced compliance, so the ventricle needs abnormally high pressure to fill to a normal volume."},{"term":"Compliance","def":"The change in ventricular volume per change in filling pressure; a stiff ventricle has low compliance."},{"term":"Eccentric hypertrophy","def":"Myocyte growth by adding sarcomeres in series, which lengthens cells and enlarges the chamber, typical of volume overload and dilated disease."},{"term":"Concentric hypertrophy","def":"Myocyte growth by adding sarcomeres in parallel, which thickens the wall and narrows the cavity, typical of pressure overload."},{"term":"Myocyte disarray","def":"Haphazard, branching arrangement of hypertrophied myocytes and myofibrils seen in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"term":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM)","def":"Movement of the anterior mitral leaflet toward the septum during systole, which obstructs the outflow tract and causes mitral regurgitation."},{"term":"Amyloid","def":"Extracellular protein deposits folded into beta-pleated sheets that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence under polarized light."},{"term":"Myocarditis","def":"Inflammation of the myocardium with injury or necrosis of myocytes that is not caused by ischemia."},{"term":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","def":"Transient left ventricular dysfunction, usually apical ballooning, triggered by a catecholamine surge without obstructive coronary disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C01","obj":"CV-18-E.1","title":"Dilated cardiomyopathy: causes, remodeling, and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is the most common cardiomyopathy. Its central defect is loss of contractile force. Whatever the cause, myocytes generate less tension, so stroke volume falls and end-systolic volume rises. The ventricle compensates through the Frank-Starling mechanism and neurohormonal activation, retaining salt and water so that a larger end-diastolic volume restores some stroke volume. Myocytes add sarcomeres in series (eccentric hypertrophy), so the cavity enlarges and the wall stays normal or thin even though total heart weight increases."},{"type":"p","text":"Dilation becomes self-perpetuating because of the law of Laplace: wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness. A wider, thinner ventricle must develop more wall tension for the same pressure, which raises oxygen demand, injures more myocytes, and drives further dilation. The ejection fraction falls, usually below about 40%."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The remodeling spiral of dilated cardiomyopathy","steps":["Myocyte injury or defective sarcomere protein","Contractility and stroke volume fall","Volume retention and series sarcomeres enlarge the cavity","Larger radius and thin wall raise wall stress (Laplace)","Higher oxygen demand injures more myocytes"],"caption":"Each turn of the loop enlarges the ventricle and lowers the ejection fraction further."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy and how they injure myocytes","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Familial (about 30% to 50%)","Usually autosomal dominant; truncating variants of TTN (titin) are the most common; also lamin A/C, dystrophin (X-linked)"],["Alcohol","Ethanol and acetaldehyde damage mitochondria and contractile proteins; may improve with abstinence"],["Prior myocarditis","Myocyte loss and fibrosis after viral or immune injury"],["Doxorubicin","Dose-dependent; iron-dependent free radical injury and topoisomerase II beta damage in myocytes"],["Peripartum","Late pregnancy to about 5 months postpartum; oxidative stress activates cathepsin D, which cleaves prolactin to an antiangiogenic 16 kDa fragment"],["Chagas disease","Chronic Trypanosoma cruzi infection with inflammation and fibrosis, often an apical aneurysm"],["Thiamine deficiency (wet beriberi)","Loss of thiamine pyrophosphate, the cofactor for pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, lowers ATP; vasodilation adds high-output failure"],["Hemochromatosis","Iron catalyzes free radical (Fenton) injury; dilated pattern, sometimes restrictive"]],"caption":"Many causes converge on one phenotype: a weak, dilated ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings follow from a large, weak ventricle. Stretching of the mitral and tricuspid annuli pulls the leaflets apart, so they fail to coapt (functional regurgitation), giving holosystolic murmurs even though the valves are structurally normal. An S3 occurs when blood rushing into an overfilled ventricle in early diastole is abruptly decelerated. Low output and high filling pressures produce fatigue, dyspnea, orthopnea, raised jugular venous pressure, and edema. Chest radiography shows a globular, enlarged heart. Stasis in dilated, poorly contracting chambers favors mural thrombi and emboli, and stretched, fibrotic myocardium supports atrial fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia. Histology is nonspecific: hypertrophied myocytes with large nuclei, attenuated fibers, and interstitial fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Distinguish idiopathic or familial DCM from ischemic cardiomyopathy, in which prior infarcts and hibernating myocardium produce the same dilated, low-EF ventricle with regional rather than diffuse wall motion loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C02","obj":"CV-18-E.2","title":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: disarray, outflow obstruction, and sudden death","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common inherited heart disease, affecting about 1 in 500 people. It is autosomal dominant with variable penetrance, caused by mutations in sarcomere protein genes, most often beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) and myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3). The mutant sarcomeres are hypercontractile and use energy inefficiently, and the myocardium hypertrophies without any pressure overload. Hypertrophy is usually asymmetric, thickest in the interventricular septum, and the cavity is small. Histology shows myocyte disarray, interstitial and replacement fibrosis, and thickened intramural coronary arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"The main hemodynamic defect is diastolic. A thick, fibrotic wall is stiff and relaxes slowly, so filling depends on high pressure and on atrial contraction, which produces an S4 and makes atrial fibrillation poorly tolerated. Systolic function is preserved and the EF is normal or high. Thick muscle with narrowed intramural arteries outgrows its blood supply, causing exertional angina even with normal epicardial coronaries."},{"type":"flow","title":"Dynamic outflow obstruction in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","steps":["Septal hypertrophy narrows the left ventricular outflow tract","Systolic flow accelerates through the narrow tract","Drag pulls the anterior mitral leaflet toward the septum","Leaflet contact obstructs outflow in mid systole","Malcoapting leaflet leaks: posteriorly directed mitral regurgitation"],"caption":"Obstruction is dynamic: it grows as the cavity shrinks or contractility rises."},{"type":"p","text":"Because systolic anterior motion depends on how close the leaflet sits to the septum, the obstruction varies with ventricular size. Anything that reduces left ventricular volume (less preload, less afterload) or increases contractility brings the leaflet and septum closer and worsens obstruction. Anything that enlarges the ventricle separates them. The murmur is a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that does not radiate well to the carotids; the carotid pulse rises briskly, then falls as obstruction develops, and may be bifid."},{"type":"table","title":"Maneuvers and the HCM murmur","cols":["Maneuver","Hemodynamic change","LV size","Murmur"],"rows":[["Valsalva strain phase","Venous return falls","Smaller","Louder"],["Standing from squatting","Venous return falls","Smaller","Louder"],["Squatting","Venous return and afterload rise","Larger","Softer"],["Sustained handgrip","Afterload rises","Larger","Softer"],["Passive leg raise","Venous return rises","Larger","Softer"]],"caption":"Aortic stenosis responds the opposite way to Valsalva and standing, which separates the two murmurs."},{"type":"p","text":"HCM is the most commonly identified cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes in the United States. Disarray and patchy fibrosis create regions of slow, nonuniform conduction that support reentrant ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation, and exertion adds catecholamines, a small cavity, and ischemia. Exertional syncope can come from arrhythmia or from obstruction that worsens as heart rate and contractility rise. The ECG often shows left ventricular hypertrophy voltage and deep, narrow septal Q waves. Distinguish the athlete's heart, whose physiologic hypertrophy is symmetric, has a normal or large cavity, normal diastolic filling, and regresses with rest from training."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the murmur in a dehydrated patient","text":"A 19-year-old with known HCM collapses at a summer football practice after heavy sweating. Volume loss lowers venous return and preload, so the left ventricle is smaller at the start of systole. A smaller cavity places the anterior mitral leaflet closer to the hypertrophied septum, so systolic anterior motion begins earlier and obstruction is greater. Tachycardia shortens diastole, further reducing filling of a stiff ventricle, and sympathetic drive raises contractility, which narrows the outflow tract more. Stroke volume falls, cerebral perfusion drops, and the murmur becomes louder. The same reasoning predicts that lying him flat and raising his legs would soften the murmur."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In HCM, would a drug that increases contractility make the outflow murmur louder or softer?","explain":"Louder. Stronger contraction empties the ventricle further and accelerates outflow, which brings the mitral leaflet to the septum earlier and worsens the dynamic obstruction.","check":"The obstruction flow and the maneuver table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C03","obj":"CV-18-E.3","title":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy: infiltration, fibrosis, and failed filling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM) is the least common of the three. The ventricular wall becomes stiff because something is deposited in it or because the myocardium and endocardium are replaced by fibrous tissue. Compliance falls, so a small increase in volume produces a steep rise in diastolic pressure. Early diastolic filling is rapid, then stops abruptly when the rigid wall reaches its limit, which gives the dip-and-plateau (square root) pattern on a ventricular pressure tracing. Contraction is relatively preserved, so ventricular cavity size and EF are near normal until late disease."},{"type":"p","text":"High filling pressures are transmitted backward. Both atria dilate markedly, which predisposes to atrial fibrillation and thrombus. Right-sided congestion often dominates the picture: raised jugular venous pressure that may rise on inspiration (Kussmaul sign), hepatomegaly, ascites, and peripheral edema, along with dyspnea from pulmonary congestion. Because stroke volume cannot rise by filling more, cardiac output is fixed and exercise tolerance is poor. Constrictive pericarditis produces a similar picture, but the restriction there comes from the pericardium, not the myocardium (CV-18-F)."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of restrictive cardiomyopathy","cols":["Cause","What stiffens the wall","Clues"],"rows":[["Amyloidosis (most common)","Extracellular AL light chain or transthyretin (ATTR) amyloid","Low ECG voltage with thick walls; Congo red apple-green birefringence; myeloma in AL; older men in wild-type ATTR"],["Sarcoidosis","Noncaseating granulomas and scar","Heart block, ventricular arrhythmia; lung and node disease"],["Hemochromatosis","Intracellular iron and fibrosis","Diabetes, skin pigmentation, liver disease; may be dilated instead"],["Radiation","Interstitial and endocardial fibrosis","Prior chest irradiation; may coexist with constriction"],["Endomyocardial fibrosis","Fibrosis of apex and inflow tracts","Children and young adults in tropical Africa and Asia"],["Loeffler endocarditis","Eosinophil-driven endocardial injury, thrombus, then fibrosis","Hypereosinophilic syndrome with marked blood eosinophilia"]],"caption":"Infiltrative causes thicken the wall; endomyocardial causes obliterate the cavity from the inside."},{"type":"p","text":"In Loeffler endocarditis, eosinophil granule proteins such as major basic protein damage the endocardium. Mural thrombus forms on the injured surface and then organizes into fibrosis that obliterates the apex, the same end stage seen in tropical endomyocardial fibrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why amyloid gives low voltage with a thick wall","steps":["Amyloid fibrils deposit between myocytes","Echocardiogram shows a thick, often speckled wall","Deposits add mass but no electrically active cells","Myocytes atrophy and are separated by protein","Less depolarizing muscle gives low QRS voltage"],"caption":"In true hypertrophy (HCM, hypertension), added muscle raises voltage; amyloid does the opposite."},{"type":"p","text":"This mismatch, low QRS voltage in the limb leads despite increased wall thickness, is the classic clue to cardiac amyloidosis. Q waves without infarction (pseudoinfarct pattern) and conduction disease also occur. Endomyocardial biopsy shows pink extracellular material that stains with Congo red."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have a left ventricular wall of 17 mm on echocardiography. One has tall QRS complexes and the other low voltage. Which has amyloidosis?","explain":"The patient with low voltage. Amyloid thickens the wall with extracellular protein, not myocytes, so electrical mass falls while measured thickness rises. Hypertrophied muscle adds electrical mass and raises voltage.","check":"The amyloid flow and causes table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C04","obj":"CV-18-E.4","title":"Comparing dilated, hypertrophic, and restrictive cardiomyopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The three cardiomyopathies are separated by asking which half of the cardiac cycle fails. In DCM the ventricle cannot empty: contractility is lost, end-systolic volume rises, and EF falls (systolic dysfunction, heart failure with reduced EF). In HCM and RCM the ventricle cannot fill: contraction is preserved but the wall is stiff, so a normal end-diastolic volume requires high filling pressure (diastolic dysfunction, heart failure with preserved EF). HCM is stiff because of thick, disorganized muscle; RCM is stiff because of infiltration or fibrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"The three cardiomyopathies side by side","cols":["Feature","Dilated","Hypertrophic","Restrictive"],"rows":[["Ventricular cavity","Enlarged, all four chambers","Small LV cavity","Normal or small; atria greatly enlarged"],["Wall thickness","Normal or thin","Thick, often asymmetric septum","Normal or thick (thick in amyloid)"],["Hypertrophy pattern","Eccentric (series sarcomeres)","Disarrayed, independent of load","None; infiltration or fibrosis"],["Ejection fraction","Reduced (usually under about 40%)","Normal or high","Normal or near normal"],["Primary dysfunction","Systolic","Diastolic, plus outflow obstruction in many","Diastolic"],["Pressure-volume loop","Shifted right, narrow; ESPVR flatter","Shifted left; steep EDPVR","Steep EDPVR; small loop"],["Typical extra sound","S3","S4","S3 from abrupt filling halt; S4 if atria still contract"],["Characteristic ECG","Nonspecific; arrhythmias, bundle branch block","High LV voltage, septal Q waves","Low voltage in amyloid"]],"caption":"EDPVR, end-diastolic pressure-volume relationship (compliance curve); ESPVR, end-systolic pressure-volume relationship (contractility)."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure-volume reasoning ties the table together. Lower contractility in DCM flattens the end-systolic line, so the loop moves to larger volumes and narrows. Lower compliance in HCM and RCM steepens the end-diastolic curve, so every filling volume costs more pressure, and that pressure is what the lungs and systemic veins feel. All three share one consequence: scarred, stretched, or disorganized myocardium supports reentry, so ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death occur in each."},{"type":"example","title":"Classifying three echocardiograms","text":"Patient A: LV end-diastolic diameter enlarged, wall 8 mm, EF 25%. Large cavity, thin wall, and low EF mean the ventricle cannot empty: systolic failure, DCM. Patient B: septum 22 mm and posterior wall 11 mm, small cavity, EF 75%, mitral leaflet touching the septum in systole. Asymmetric thickening with preserved EF and systolic anterior motion: HCM with obstruction. Patient C: normal LV cavity, wall 15 mm, EF 55%, both atria very large, low-voltage ECG. Preserved emptying with failed filling, a thick wall without electrical mass, and atrial enlargement point to restrictive disease from amyloid. In each case, first decide whether emptying or filling fails, then use wall thickness and the ECG to name the cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C05","obj":"CV-18-E.5","title":"Myocarditis: causes, histology, and consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myocarditis is inflammation of the myocardium with myocyte injury that is not ischemic. In developed countries most cases are viral. Coxsackie B virus, an enterovirus, is the classic cause; adenovirus, parvovirus B19, human herpesvirus 6, influenza, and SARS-CoV-2 are others. Coxsackie B and adenovirus enter myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor, and enteroviral protease 2A cleaves dystrophin, weakening the myocyte cytoskeleton. Myocytes are injured by the virus and then by the cytotoxic T-cell response that clears it."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of myocarditis and their distinguishing features","cols":["Cause","Mechanism and features"],"rows":[["Coxsackie B and other viruses","Direct lysis plus T-cell injury; lymphocytic infiltrate; often follows a viral illness"],["Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi)","Triatomine bug vector in Latin America; amastigotes in myocytes acutely; chronic DCM with conduction block and apical aneurysm years later, plus megaesophagus and megacolon"],["Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi)","Weeks to months after the tick bite; AV block of varying degree that usually resolves"],["Diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae)","Exotoxin ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2, halting protein synthesis; myocyte injury and conduction block"],["Drug hypersensitivity","Interstitial infiltrate rich in eosinophils, often with rash and fever"],["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Immune complex and autoantibody injury; may coexist with pericarditis and Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],["Giant cell myocarditis","Multinucleated giant cells, widespread necrosis; young to middle-aged adults, linked to autoimmunity and thymoma; often fulminant"]],"caption":"Eosinophils suggest hypersensitivity; giant cells without granulomas suggest giant cell myocarditis; parasites in myocytes suggest Chagas."},{"type":"p","text":"The defining histology is an inflammatory infiltrate with myocyte necrosis (the Dallas criteria). In viral myocarditis the infiltrate is mainly lymphocytes, patchy and interstitial, surrounding damaged myocytes. Hypersensitivity myocarditis is instead perivascular and interstitial with many eosinophils, and giant cell myocarditis shows multinucleated giant cells scattered through extensive necrosis without the well-formed granulomas of sarcoidosis. In diphtheria, toxin injures myocytes with relatively little inflammation. Because inflammation is often focal, a biopsy can miss it."},{"type":"p","text":"Each manifestation follows from the lesion. Myocyte necrosis releases troponin, so troponin rises without coronary occlusion. Edema and patchy inflammation slow and block conduction and create foci of abnormal automaticity and reentry, giving palpitations, AV block, ventricular arrhythmias, and occasionally sudden death in a young person. Widespread loss of contractile myocytes lowers EF and causes heart failure, sometimes fulminant cardiogenic shock. Chest pain may be ischemic-like or pleuritic when the pericardium is involved (myopericarditis), and the ECG may show diffuse ST elevation. Many patients recover fully, but the sequence below explains progression to dilated cardiomyopathy."},{"type":"flow","title":"From viral infection to dilated cardiomyopathy","steps":["Virus enters myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor","Viral replication injures myocytes directly","Cytotoxic T cells kill infected myocytes","Exposed cardiac proteins can trigger persistent autoimmunity","Lost myocytes are replaced by fibrosis","Remodeling produces dilated cardiomyopathy"],"caption":"Viral injury and the immune response both remove myocytes; the scar that replaces them cannot contract."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-05-C06","obj":"CV-18-E.6","title":"Stress (takotsubo) and tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Stress cardiomyopathy, also called takotsubo cardiomyopathy or broken heart syndrome, is acute, transient left ventricular dysfunction triggered by a surge of catecholamines. About 90% of patients are women, most of them postmenopausal, and the trigger is usually sudden emotional or physical stress such as bereavement, an argument, surgery, or severe illness. The typical pattern is apical ballooning: the apex and mid ventricle are akinetic while the base contracts vigorously, so in systole the ventricle resembles a narrow-necked Japanese octopus pot (takotsubo)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Proposed mechanism of takotsubo cardiomyopathy","steps":["Acute stress releases epinephrine and norepinephrine","Very high epinephrine acts on dense apical beta receptors","Beta-2 signaling switches from Gs to Gi; apical contraction falls","cAMP-driven Ca2+ overload and microvascular spasm stun myocytes","Apex balloons while the base hypercontracts","Function recovers over days to weeks"],"caption":"The mechanism is not fully settled; catecholamine excess is the shared feature of all proposed explanations."},{"type":"p","text":"Takotsubo mimics acute myocardial infarction. Patients present with chest pain and dyspnea, the ECG shows ST elevation or deep T wave inversion often with a long QT, and troponin rises, but the rise is small relative to the large area of dysfunction. Coronary angiography shows no obstructive disease that explains the wall motion defect, and the akinetic region extends beyond the territory of any single coronary artery. Biopsy, when done, shows contraction band necrosis, the pattern of catecholamine excess. Complications follow from the acute weakness and its geometry: heart failure and cardiogenic shock, dynamic outflow obstruction from the hypercontracting base, apical thrombus, and ventricular arrhythmias. Because the myocytes are stunned rather than dead, wall motion usually normalizes within weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy is ventricular dysfunction caused by a persistently fast heart rate, most often atrial fibrillation or flutter with rapid ventricular response, incessant supraventricular tachycardia, or very frequent premature ventricular contractions. A sustained high rate shortens diastole, the period of coronary perfusion and Ca2+ reuptake, while increasing energy demand. The result is myocardial energy depletion, abnormal Ca2+ handling, beta receptor downregulation, and subendocardial ischemia, which lead to dilation and a reduced EF resembling DCM. The defining feature is reversibility: once the rate normalizes, function usually recovers over weeks to months. A low EF that improves after an arrhythmia is controlled therefore points to rate as the cause rather than primary muscle disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old woman has chest pain after her husband's funeral, ST elevation in the anterior leads, a mild troponin rise, apical akinesis, and clean coronary arteries. What should her echocardiogram show in six weeks?","explain":"Near-normal wall motion. Catecholamine-stunned myocytes recover because they were not killed; an infarct of the same size would leave a permanent scar.","check":"The takotsubo flow and the paragraph on its MI mimicry."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These diseases connect every Part I discipline. Genetics explains familial DCM (titin) and HCM (sarcomere genes) with autosomal dominant inheritance and variable penetrance, and biochemistry explains thiamine, iron, and doxorubicin injury through energy failure and free radicals. Microbiology and immunology explain myocarditis: coxsackie B uses a receptor shared with adenovirus, Trypanosoma cruzi and Borrelia burgdorferi have distinct vectors and conduction effects, diphtheria toxin halts protein synthesis, and T cells or autoantibodies cause much of the damage. Pathology adds the tissue clues: disarray, amyloid with Congo red, granulomas, eosinophils, giant cells, and contraction bands."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology unifies them. Contractility and compliance define systolic versus diastolic failure, Laplace explains why dilation worsens, and preload dependence explains the HCM murmur. The categories have limits: hemochromatosis and sarcoidosis can look dilated or restrictive, late HCM can thin and dilate (burned-out phase), and ischemic, hypertensive, and valvular disease produce similar ventricles, so the history, ECG voltage, and tissue findings decide the diagnosis."}],"summary":["DCM is systolic failure: four-chamber dilation, thin walls, low EF, S3, and functional mitral and tricuspid regurgitation from stretched annuli.","Laplace explains DCM progression: a larger radius and thinner wall raise wall stress, oxygen demand, and further dilation.","DCM causes include TTN variants, alcohol, prior myocarditis, doxorubicin, peripartum injury, Chagas, thiamine deficiency, and hemochromatosis.","HCM is autosomal dominant (MYH7, MYBPC3) with asymmetric septal hypertrophy, myocyte disarray, diastolic dysfunction, and normal or high EF.","The HCM outflow murmur worsens when the LV is smaller (Valsalva, standing) and softens when it is larger (squatting, handgrip, leg raise).","Disarray and fibrosis make HCM a leading cause of sudden death in young athletes through ventricular arrhythmia.","RCM is diastolic failure from infiltration or fibrosis: near-normal cavity and EF, huge atria, and right-sided congestion.","Amyloid thickens the wall without adding myocytes, so the ECG shows low voltage despite a thick wall.","Myocarditis is inflammation with myocyte necrosis, classically lymphocytic after coxsackie B; it raises troponin, causes arrhythmia, and can progress to DCM.","Takotsubo is reversible catecholamine-induced apical ballooning in postmenopausal women with clean coronaries; tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy reverses when the rate normalizes."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: cardiomyopathies and myocarditis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Failure; Heart Valves and Heart Sounds"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump: ventricular pressure-volume relationships"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease (cardiomyopathies and myocarditis)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Picornaviruses (enterovirus group); Corynebacteria; Spirochetes; Medical parasitology (Trypanosoma cruzi)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins: thiamine"}]},"CV-18-F":{"unit":"CV-18-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06","title":"Pericardial Disease","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the diseases of the sac around the heart: acute and secondary pericarditis, constrictive pericarditis, pericardial effusion, cardiac tamponade, and metastatic tumor in the pericardium. Each one is easiest to understand as a problem of diastolic filling inside a stiff bag, or as inflammation spreading to the epicardium beneath it. Board questions give a chest pain history, an ECG, a neck vein or blood pressure finding, or a clinical setting such as a recent infarct, renal failure, or known cancer, and ask you to name the process and explain the finding. If you understand the pericardial pressure-volume curve and ventricular interdependence, most of these questions answer themselves.","prerequisites":["Layers of the pericardium and the mediastinum (CV-2, CV-3)","Cardiac cycle, atrial and jugular venous pressure waves, and diastolic filling (CV-4)","ECG leads, the ST segment, and the current of injury (CV-7)","Acute inflammation, exudates, and repair by fibrosis (general pathology)","Myocardial infarction timeline and complications (CV-18-C)"],"connections":[{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Transmural infarction causes early fibrinous pericarditis, Dressler syndrome weeks later, and hemopericardium when the free wall ruptures."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E)","how":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy produces the same filling limit as constrictive pericarditis from a stiff myocardium instead of a stiff sac, and viral myocarditis often accompanies viral pericarditis."},{"to":"Renal pathophysiology","how":"Uremia causes fibrinous pericarditis, and nephrotic syndrome causes transudative effusion through low oncotic pressure."},{"to":"Immunology and microbiology","how":"Coxsackie B, tuberculosis, pyogenic bacteria, lupus, and rheumatoid arthritis each injure the pericardium through a different mechanism and leave a different exudate."},{"to":"Cardiac tumors (CV-18-J)","how":"Metastases to the heart greatly outnumber primary tumors such as myxoma and usually present through the pericardium."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pericardial reserve volume","def":"The small extra volume the pericardial sac can accept before its pressure rises steeply."},{"term":"Friction rub","def":"A scratchy, high-pitched sound made by roughened pericardial layers moving against each other during the cardiac cycle."},{"term":"Current of injury","def":"Current flowing between injured and healthy myocardium that shifts the ST segment on the ECG."},{"term":"Exudate","def":"Protein-rich inflammatory fluid formed by increased vascular permeability, in contrast to a low-protein transudate formed by pressure or oncotic imbalance."},{"term":"Dressler syndrome","def":"Autoimmune pericarditis appearing weeks after myocardial infarction, thought to follow sensitization to released cardiac antigens."},{"term":"Constrictive pericarditis","def":"Chronic fibrous, often calcified, thickening of the pericardium that encases the heart and limits diastolic filling."},{"term":"Kussmaul sign","def":"A rise, or failure to fall, of jugular venous pressure during inspiration."},{"term":"Pericardial knock","def":"An early diastolic sound produced when rapid ventricular filling stops abruptly against a rigid pericardium."},{"term":"Cardiac tamponade","def":"Compression of the heart by pericardial contents at a pressure high enough to impair filling and reduce cardiac output."},{"term":"Pulsus paradoxus","def":"An inspiratory fall in systolic arterial pressure of more than 10 mmHg."},{"term":"Electrical alternans","def":"Beat-to-beat alternation of QRS amplitude or axis caused by the heart swinging within a large effusion."},{"term":"Ventricular interdependence","def":"The filling of one ventricle reducing the filling of the other because both share the septum and a fixed pericardial space."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C01","obj":"CV-18-F.1","title":"Acute (primary) pericarditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pericardium has an outer fibrous sac of dense collagen and an inner serous sac. The parietal serous layer lines the fibrous sac, and the visceral layer (the epicardium) covers the heart. Between them lies about 15 to 50 mL of serous fluid, a plasma ultrafiltrate that lets the heart move with little friction. The fibrous and parietal layers receive sensory fibers from the phrenic nerves (C3 to C5); the visceral layer is insensitive to pain."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute pericarditis is inflammation of these layers. In developed countries most cases are labeled idiopathic, and most idiopathic cases are presumed viral, because they follow a respiratory or gastrointestinal illness and behave like proven viral cases. Coxsackie B virus is the classic cause; echovirus, adenovirus, influenza, and HIV are others. Young and middle-aged adults, more often men, are typical. The virus and the host response raise vascular permeability, so fibrinogen escapes and polymerizes on both serous surfaces, giving a fibrinous or serofibrinous exudate that usually resolves without scarring."},{"type":"p","text":"Each classic finding follows from this inflammation. The pain is sharp and pleuritic because the inflamed parietal layer is stretched and rubbed with each heartbeat and breath; it worsens lying flat and with deep inspiration and eases on sitting up and leaning forward, which is thought to reduce pressure on the inflamed parietal surface. Phrenic innervation refers it to the trapezius ridge, a feature that ischemic pain lacks. Fibrin-coated layers grating together make the friction rub, a scratchy sound with up to three components (atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early diastolic filling), best heard at the lower left sternal border with the patient leaning forward. It comes and goes and can persist even when some fluid separates the layers. Low-grade fever, leukocytosis, and raised C-reactive protein reflect systemic inflammation; a troponin rise means the adjacent myocardium is inflamed (myopericarditis)."},{"type":"p","text":"The pericardium itself generates no electrical signal. ECG changes come from inflammation of the superficial myocardium beneath the visceral layer. Injured epicardial cells create a current of injury that elevates the ST segment in leads facing the epicardium, and because the whole surface is involved, elevation appears in most limb and precordial leads rather than one coronary territory. Atrial epicardial injury displaces the PR segment, giving PR depression in most leads. Lead aVR looks into the cavities from above, so it shows the mirror image: ST depression and PR elevation. The changes evolve over days to weeks: diffuse ST elevation, then return of ST to baseline with T flattening, then T inversion, then normalization."},{"type":"table","title":"ECG of acute pericarditis versus ST elevation myocardial infarction","cols":["Feature","Acute pericarditis","ST elevation MI"],"rows":[["Distribution of ST elevation","Diffuse, most leads","One coronary territory"],["ST shape","Concave upward","Often convex or straight"],["Reciprocal ST depression","Only aVR, sometimes V1","In the opposite leads"],["PR segment","Depressed; elevated in aVR","Usually unchanged"],["Q waves","Absent","Develop with necrosis"],["T wave inversion","After ST returns to baseline","Often while ST is still elevated"]],"caption":"Epicardial injury that covers the whole heart has no opposite wall to show reciprocal change, apart from leads that look into the cavity."},{"type":"p","text":"Most cases settle within weeks. The main complications are recurrence, effusion with tamponade (uncommon in idiopathic disease), and, rarely, later constriction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 25-year-old with pleuritic chest pain has ST elevation in II, III, aVF, I, and V2 to V6, PR depression, and ST depression only in aVR. Why does this pattern argue against an inferior infarct?","explain":"An inferior infarct elevates ST in II, III, and aVF and produces reciprocal depression in aVL and often I. Elevation in inferior, lateral, and anterior leads together with PR depression and no reciprocal change outside aVR indicates diffuse epicardial inflammation.","check":"The ECG comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C02","obj":"CV-18-F.2","title":"Secondary pericarditis and the type of exudate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary pericarditis arises from a disease outside the pericardium, and the exudate it produces reflects how strongly and in what way that disease injures the serosa. Mild injury with a modest rise in permeability yields serous fluid. Greater permeability lets fibrinogen escape, so fibrin coats the layers; the shaggy surface of severe fibrinous pericarditis is described as bread and butter. Organisms that recruit neutrophils produce pus. Damaged vessels, fragile tumor vessels, or a bleeding tendency add blood. Granulomatous infection produces caseous necrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of secondary pericarditis and their usual exudate","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Usual exudate"],"rows":[["Early post-infarction (about 1 to 3 days)","Necrosis of a transmural infarct inflames the overlying pericardium","Fibrinous or fibrinohemorrhagic, over the infarct"],["Dressler syndrome (weeks after MI)","Autoimmune reaction to released cardiac antigens","Fibrinous, often with effusion"],["Cardiac surgery","Direct trauma; later a Dressler-like post-pericardiotomy syndrome","Fibrinous or hemorrhagic"],["Uremia","Retained metabolic toxins injure the serosa","Fibrinous; hemorrhagic with uremic platelet dysfunction"],["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Immune complex deposition","Serous or fibrinous"],["Rheumatoid arthritis","Immune complexes and rheumatoid inflammation","Fibrinous; may later constrict"],["Mediastinal radiation","Endothelial and serosal injury; later fibrosis","Fibrinous acutely; fibrosis later"],["Tuberculosis","Granulomatous response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Caseous, often hemorrhagic"],["Pyogenic bacteria","Spread from pneumonia or empyema, blood, or surgery","Purulent"],["Cancer","Tumor implants with fragile vessels and blocked lymphatics","Hemorrhagic"]],"caption":"Fibrinous and serofibrinous exudates are the most common; purulent and caseous exudates are the most likely to organize into constriction."},{"type":"p","text":"The two forms after infarction differ in timing and mechanism. Early pericarditis appears within the first few days over a transmural infarct, because the necrosis reaches the epicardium; it is localized, fibrinous, and self-limited. It is not expected with subendocardial infarcts, which spare the epicardium. Dressler syndrome appears weeks later and is systemic: fever, pleuritis, and pericarditis with effusion. Its timing fits an adaptive immune response rather than direct injury, and the same picture after cardiac surgery is called post-pericardiotomy syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"Dressler syndrome as an immune reaction to injury","steps":["Infarction or surgery injures myocardium and pericardium","Hidden myocardial antigens are released","Antibodies and T cells are primed over weeks","Immune attack on pericardium and pleura","Fever, pleuritic pain, rub, and effusion weeks later"],"caption":"The delay of weeks is what separates Dressler syndrome from early post-infarction pericarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"Uremic pericarditis develops in severe renal failure from retained toxins rather than infection. Because the inflammation tends not to involve the epicardial myocardium, the typical diffuse ST elevation is often absent, and uremic platelet dysfunction makes the fluid bloody and more likely to cause tamponade. Bacterial pericarditis, usually Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pneumoniae, reaches the pericardium by spread from a pneumonia or empyema, through the blood, or after chest surgery, and it is acute and toxic. Tuberculous pericarditis spreads from mediastinal lymph nodes and is chronic, caseous, and often hemorrhagic. These two, together with radiation, are the forms most likely to heal by dense fibrosis and constriction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C03","obj":"CV-18-F.3","title":"Constrictive pericarditis and restrictive cardiomyopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Constrictive pericarditis is the chronic end of pericardial inflammation. Granulation tissue organizes the exudate, the layers fuse and obliterate the space, and dense fibrous tissue, sometimes calcified, forms a rigid shell around the heart. Worldwide, tuberculosis is a major cause. In developed countries most cases follow idiopathic or viral pericarditis, cardiac surgery, or mediastinal radiation, for example for Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pericarditis to constriction","steps":["Fibrinous, purulent, or caseous exudate","Granulation tissue organizes the exudate","Visceral and parietal layers fuse","Dense fibrosis, sometimes with calcification","Rigid shell fixes the total cardiac volume"],"caption":"Organization of exudate replaces a compliant sac with a fixed shell."},{"type":"p","text":"The shell sets a fixed limit on total cardiac volume, but the myocardium inside relaxes normally. In early diastole the ventricles fill very rapidly, driven by high atrial pressure, until they reach the limit of the shell, where filling stops abruptly. The sudden halt makes the pericardial knock, an early diastolic sound that comes earlier and is higher pitched than an S3. On ventricular pressure tracings the same event appears as a dip-and-plateau (square root) pattern, and the jugular venous pulse shows a steep y descent. Diastolic pressures in all four chambers rise and become nearly equal. Systolic function is preserved, but the limited filling lowers stroke volume, and the high venous pressure produces hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema out of proportion to lung congestion. Plain films or CT may show pericardial calcification."},{"type":"p","text":"Kussmaul sign is the jugular venous pressure rising, or failing to fall, during inspiration. Normally inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, the fall is transmitted to the right atrium, and venous return is drawn in, so the neck veins fall. In constriction the rigid shell blocks transmission of intrathoracic pressure to the heart, while descent of the diaphragm still pushes more abdominal venous blood toward a right heart that cannot expand, so venous pressure rises. The same isolation causes reciprocal filling: the pulmonary veins lie outside the shell, so inspiration lowers their pressure but not left heart pressure, and left filling falls while the extra right ventricular volume pushes the septum left. Kussmaul sign is not specific; it also occurs in restrictive cardiomyopathy and right ventricular infarction."},{"type":"p","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy produces a very similar filling limit, but the stiffness lies in a myocardium infiltrated or scarred by amyloid, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, or endomyocardial fibrosis, and the pericardium is normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Constrictive pericarditis versus restrictive cardiomyopathy","cols":["Feature","Constrictive pericarditis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"rows":[["Site of stiffness","Pericardium","Myocardium"],["Pericardium on imaging","Thickened, may be calcified","Normal"],["Myocardial relaxation","Normal","Impaired"],["Respiratory variation in ventricular filling","Exaggerated; ventricles fill reciprocally","Little"],["Left versus right diastolic pressure","Nearly equal","Left usually higher"],["Pulmonary hypertension","Usually mild","Often marked"],["Natriuretic peptide","Normal or mildly raised (shell limits myocardial stretch)","Markedly raised"],["Extra sound","Pericardial knock","S3 may be present"],["ECG","Low voltage, nonspecific T changes","Low voltage with conduction disease suggests amyloid"]],"caption":"Both limit filling with preserved ejection fraction and show dip-and-plateau tracings; the location of the stiffness explains every difference."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do the two ventricles fill reciprocally with breathing in constriction but not in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","explain":"In constriction total cardiac volume is fixed by the shell, so more right ventricular filling on inspiration pushes the septum left and reduces left ventricular filling. The pulmonary veins lie outside the shell and their pressure falls with inspiration, which further lowers left heart filling. In restrictive cardiomyopathy the pericardium is compliant and each stiff ventricle limits only its own filling.","check":"The Kussmaul sign paragraph and the respiratory variation row of the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C04","obj":"CV-18-F.4","title":"Pericardial effusion: causes, findings, and the rate of accumulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pericardial effusion is fluid in excess of the normal 15 to 50 mL. Its composition points to the cause. Serous effusions are transudates that form when Starling forces shift: raised venous pressure in heart failure, or low plasma oncotic pressure in nephrotic syndrome and cirrhosis. Hypothyroidism produces a large, slowly forming, protein-rich effusion through increased capillary leak and reduced lymphatic drainage. Exudates come from the pericarditis causes in C02. Hemorrhagic fluid points to trauma, free wall rupture, aortic dissection, malignancy, tuberculosis, uremia, or a bleeding tendency."},{"type":"p","text":"The fibrous pericardium has a J-shaped pressure-volume curve. Over the small reserve volume, added fluid barely raises pressure; beyond it, collagen fibers straighten and pressure climbs steeply. Fluid added over minutes reaches the steep limb at once, so as little as about 150 to 200 mL can compress the heart. Fluid that collects over weeks to months lets the sac stretch and remodel, shifting the curve to the right, so a liter or more can accumulate with little rise in pressure. For this reason the speed of accumulation predicts compression better than the volume."},{"type":"example","title":"Why rate matters more than volume","text":"Patient A has hypothyroidism and 1,200 mL of pericardial fluid that built up over four months; her blood pressure is normal. Patient B has a stab wound and 200 mL of blood that collected in 20 minutes; he is hypotensive with distended neck veins. In A, slow stretch enlarged the sac, so pericardial pressure stayed below right atrial pressure (about 2 to 6 mmHg) and filling continued. In B, the sac had no time to stretch, so 200 mL pushed pericardial pressure up the steep limb past right atrial and right ventricular diastolic pressure. The chamber with the lowest pressure stops filling first, and output falls. Volume alone would have predicted the opposite outcome."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings of a large effusion and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Muffled, distant heart sounds","Fluid separates the heart from the chest wall and dampens sound"],["Rub may fade","Fluid separates the inflamed layers, though a rub can persist"],["Low-voltage QRS (all limb leads under 5 mm)","Fluid between heart and electrodes attenuates the signal"],["Electrical alternans","The heart swings within a large effusion, changing its axis on alternate beats"],["Enlarged, globular (water bottle) cardiac silhouette","Fluid fills the sac; usually needs more than about 250 mL to show"],["Echo-free space on echocardiography","Fluid separates the visceral and parietal layers"]],"caption":"Low voltage is not specific (obesity, emphysema, hypothyroidism, amyloid); electrical alternans in this setting suggests a large effusion and possible tamponade."},{"type":"p","text":"A large chronic effusion can be well tolerated while still producing these signs, and lung fields are usually clear because left heart pressures are not raised. The signs describe the amount of fluid; whether filling is impaired depends on pericardial pressure, which is the subject of tamponade."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C05","obj":"CV-18-F.5","title":"Cardiac tamponade","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tamponade is a hemodynamic state, not a fluid volume: it exists once pericardial pressure rises enough to impair filling. Any effusion can cause it, but the classic acute cause is hemopericardium. Penetrating trauma often enters the right ventricle, the most anterior chamber. Free wall rupture after transmural infarction, most often about 3 to 7 days after the event when macrophages have softened the necrotic wall, causes sudden tamponade and pulseless electrical activity. Type A aortic dissection can rupture backward into the sac, because the proximal ascending aorta lies inside the pericardial reflection. Nontraumatic causes also include malignancy, uremia, and pericarditis."},{"type":"flow","title":"How rising pericardial pressure lowers cardiac output","steps":["Fluid or blood collects faster than the sac can stretch","Pericardial pressure exceeds right heart diastolic pressure","Right atrium and ventricle collapse in diastole","Diastolic pressures in all chambers equalize with pericardial pressure","Preload and stroke volume fall; tachycardia compensates","Hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds"],"caption":"The thin-walled, low-pressure right heart is compressed first."},{"type":"p","text":"All four chambers sit inside one tense sac, so their diastolic pressures (right atrial, right ventricular diastolic, pulmonary artery diastolic, and pulmonary capillary wedge) become nearly equal to pericardial pressure. Filling now occurs mainly during ventricular systole, when ejection briefly lowers intrapericardial volume and pressure; the jugular x descent is preserved and the y descent is blunted or absent. Beck triad follows: hypotension from low stroke volume, distended neck veins from high venous pressure, and distant heart sounds from the fluid. Sinus tachycardia is the compensating response, and electrical alternans may appear."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pulsus paradoxus in tamponade","steps":["Inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure","Venous return to the right ventricle increases","Right ventricle cannot expand outward against the tense sac","Septum bulges into the left ventricle","Pulmonary veins also hold more blood on inspiration","Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall","Systolic pressure drops more than 10 mmHg"],"caption":"Total cardiac volume is fixed, so right heart gain on inspiration becomes left heart loss."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulsus paradoxus is an exaggeration of the normal small inspiratory fall in systolic pressure, made large by ventricular interdependence. It is not specific to tamponade: severe asthma, COPD, and massive pulmonary embolism produce it through large swings in intrathoracic pressure or right ventricular strain. Kussmaul sign is usually absent in tamponade, because the fluid still transmits the inspiratory fall in intrathoracic pressure to the right atrium."},{"type":"example","title":"Measuring pulsus paradoxus","text":"The cuff is inflated above systolic pressure and deflated slowly while the patient breathes quietly. At 122 mmHg the first Korotkoff sounds appear only during expiration. Deflation continues until, at 98 mmHg, sounds are heard with every beat in both phases of breathing. The pulsus is 122 minus 98, or 24 mmHg, more than 10 mmHg and therefore abnormal. The beats lost on inspiration are those with the smallest left ventricular stroke volume, which is the interdependence described above. With jugular venous distension and muffled heart sounds, this points to tamponade."},{"type":"table","title":"Tamponade versus constrictive pericarditis","cols":["Feature","Tamponade","Constriction"],"rows":[["Onset","Acute or subacute","Chronic"],["Pulsus paradoxus","Usual","Less common"],["Kussmaul sign","Usually absent","Typical"],["Jugular venous y descent","Blunted or absent","Steep"],["Diastolic filling impaired","Throughout diastole","Mid and late diastole only"],["Equal diastolic pressures","Yes","Yes"],["Extra heart sound","None","Pericardial knock"]],"caption":"Fluid compresses the heart throughout diastole; a shell stops filling only once its limit is reached."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient 5 days after a large anterior infarct suddenly becomes hypotensive with distended neck veins and an organized rhythm on the monitor but no pulse. What happened and why is there no pulse?","explain":"Free wall rupture has filled the pericardium with blood. Pericardial pressure exceeds diastolic pressure in every chamber, so the ventricles cannot fill and stroke volume approaches zero even though electrical activity continues (pulseless electrical activity).","check":"The causes paragraph and the first tamponade flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-06-C06","obj":"CV-18-F.6","title":"Metastatic disease of the heart and pericardium","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tumors that reach the heart from elsewhere are far more common than primary cardiac tumors such as myxoma; autopsy series find cardiac or pericardial metastases in roughly 5 to 10% of patients who die of cancer. The pericardium is the most frequent site, followed by the epicardium and myocardium. Because lung and breast carcinoma are common and lie next to the heart, they account for the largest numbers of cases. Melanoma, by contrast, is less common but involves the heart in a high proportion of patients with widespread disease, and leukemia and lymphoma often infiltrate it."},{"type":"table","title":"Routes of spread to the heart","cols":["Route","Typical tumors"],"rows":[["Direct extension","Lung carcinoma, breast carcinoma, esophageal carcinoma, mediastinal tumors"],["Retrograde lymphatic spread through mediastinal nodes","Lung and breast carcinoma, lymphoma"],["Hematogenous seeding","Melanoma, leukemia, lymphoma, sarcomas"],["Growth along the inferior vena cava into the right atrium","Renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma"]],"caption":"Proximity explains lung and breast; wide bloodstream spread explains melanoma and the hematologic cancers."},{"type":"p","text":"Pericardial deposits produce effusion through two mechanisms. Tumor implants on the serosa have fragile, leaky new vessels that bleed, and tumor blocking the mediastinal lymphatics prevents the normal drainage of pericardial fluid. The result is a hemorrhagic exudate that keeps accumulating, often to tamponade; malignancy is one of the most common causes of nontraumatic tamponade. Finding malignant cells on cytology of the pericardial fluid confirms the cause."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows from the fluid. Patients usually present with progressive dyspnea, chest discomfort, and the effusion signs in C04, and a rapidly growing effusion or one that keeps reaccumulating after drainage raises suspicion of tumor."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pericardial metastasis to tamponade","steps":["Tumor reaches the pericardium by extension, lymphatics, or blood","Serosal implants with fragile new vessels bleed","Lymphatic obstruction blocks fluid drainage","Bloody exudate with malignant cells accumulates","Pericardial pressure rises until filling fails"],"caption":"Effusion forms faster than it drains, so it tends to recur and progress."},{"type":"p","text":"Two related problems complete the picture. Tumor that encases the heart can behave like constrictive pericarditis, and mediastinal radiation given for the same cancer can later cause radiation pericarditis and constriction, so a pericardial problem in a patient with cancer is not always due to tumor. Myocardial deposits are less often symptomatic but can disturb conduction and cause arrhythmias, and intracavitary tumor can obstruct flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across pericardial disease, anatomy and physiology explain the findings and pathology explains the causes. The fibrous pericardium is a stiff collagen bag with a J-shaped pressure-volume curve, so whether fluid compresses the heart depends on how fast it arrives, and a shared, fixed space makes the two ventricles compete for volume, which produces pulsus paradoxus and reciprocal respiratory filling. The pathology of the exudate links the pericardium to microbiology and immunology: viruses and immune complexes give serous or fibrinous fluid, pyogenic bacteria give pus, tuberculosis gives caseation, and tumors and bleeding give blood."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits of each sign matter. Diffuse ST elevation can be absent in uremic pericarditis, low voltage has many causes, Kussmaul sign occurs in restrictive cardiomyopathy and right ventricular infarction as well as constriction, and pulsus paradoxus occurs in severe asthma and pulmonary embolism. Constriction and restriction look alike at the bedside and are separated by where the stiffness lies, sac or muscle."}],"summary":["Acute pericarditis is usually idiopathic or viral (coxsackie B) and gives pleuritic pain eased by leaning forward, a friction rub, diffuse concave ST elevation, and PR depression, with the mirror image in aVR.","The pericardium is electrically silent; ECG changes come from inflammation of the underlying epicardium, which is why they span coronary territories without reciprocal depression.","The exudate reflects the cause: fibrinous after infarction, in Dressler syndrome, and in uremia; serous or fibrinous in lupus; purulent with bacteria; caseous with tuberculosis; hemorrhagic with cancer.","Early post-infarction pericarditis appears within days over a transmural infarct; Dressler syndrome appears weeks later as an autoimmune reaction to released cardiac antigens.","Constrictive pericarditis is a rigid, often calcified shell that stops filling abruptly in early diastole, producing a pericardial knock, steep y descent, Kussmaul sign, and equal diastolic pressures.","Restrictive cardiomyopathy limits filling from a stiff myocardium with a normal pericardium, markedly raised natriuretic peptide, and little reciprocal respiratory variation.","Pericardial pressure depends on the rate of accumulation: about 150 to 200 mL added acutely can cause tamponade, while a liter or more collected slowly may not.","Tamponade lowers output by preventing filling; Beck triad, pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg, a blunted y descent, and equal diastolic pressures follow from compression inside a fixed sac.","Metastases to the heart greatly outnumber primary tumors; lung, breast, melanoma, lymphoma, and leukemia reach the pericardium and produce a bloody effusion with malignant cells that can cause tamponade."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: pericardial disease (pericarditis, pericardial effusion and hemopericardium) and cardiac tumors"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac output, venous return, and their regulation; the normal electrocardiogram and the current of injury"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump: the cardiac cycle and ventricular filling"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease (pericarditis, pericardial effusion, and tamponade)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Picornaviruses (enteroviruses, including coxsackieviruses); mycobacteria"}]},"CV-18-G":{"unit":"CV-18-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07","title":"Congenital Heart Conditions","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the congenital heart conditions most often tested: ventricular and atrial septal defects, patent foramen ovale, patent ductus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta, and transposition of the great vessels. Each is taught from its embryologic origin to the pressures and resistances that set shunt direction, then to the murmur, the pulse findings, cyanosis, and late complications such as Eisenmenger syndrome. Board questions usually give a murmur, a pulse or saturation pattern, a chest film, or a syndrome and ask you to name the lesion or predict its course. If you can say which side has the higher pressure and which chambers carry the extra volume, most of these questions answer themselves.","prerequisites":["Cardiac septation, endocardial cushions, and the spiral aorticopulmonary septum (CV-1)","Fetal circulation and the changes at birth (CV-1)","Pressures in each cardiac chamber and the cardiac cycle with heart sounds","Flow, pressure, and resistance (Poiseuille relationship) and the causes of turbulence (CV-11)","Oxygen content, hemoglobin saturation, and the Fick principle"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac embryology (CV-1)","how":"Each defect here is a specific failure of septation, cushion fusion, conotruncal spiraling, or ductal closure described in the embryology chapter."},{"to":"Pulmonary hypertension (CV-18-A)","how":"Eisenmenger syndrome is pulmonary arterial hypertension caused by chronic shunt flow, with the same endothelial imbalance and plexiform lesions."},{"to":"Valvular heart disease and endocarditis (CV-18-D)","how":"Bicuspid aortic valve is a leading cause of aortic stenosis and regurgitation in younger adults, and turbulent jets from shunts and abnormal valves seed endocarditis."},{"to":"Genetics and microbiology","how":"Trisomy 21, 22q11.2 deletion, Turner syndrome, maternal diabetes, and congenital rubella each predict a particular lesion."},{"to":"Hematology and neurology","how":"Chronic hypoxemia drives erythropoietin and secondary polycythemia, and right-to-left shunts let venous clots and bacteria reach the brain."}],"terms":[{"term":"Shunt","def":"Abnormal flow of blood between the systemic and pulmonary circulations through a defect in a septum or a persistent vessel."},{"term":"Qp:Qs","def":"The ratio of pulmonary blood flow to systemic blood flow; above 1 means a net left-to-right shunt, below 1 a net right-to-left shunt."},{"term":"Cyanosis","def":"Bluish skin and mucosa that appears when capillary blood contains about 5 g/dL or more of deoxygenated hemoglobin."},{"term":"Eisenmenger syndrome","def":"Reversal of a long-standing left-to-right shunt to right-to-left after pulmonary vascular disease raises right-sided pressure above left-sided pressure."},{"term":"Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR)","def":"Resistance of the pulmonary arterioles to flow; high in the fetus, it falls sharply after birth and rises again with shunt-induced vascular disease."},{"term":"Paradoxical embolism","def":"Passage of a venous thrombus from the right heart to the systemic arteries through an intracardiac defect, causing stroke or limb ischemia."},{"term":"Endocardial cushions","def":"Swellings of the atrioventricular canal that fuse to divide it into right and left channels and contribute to the membranous septum, the lower atrial septum, and the AV valves."},{"term":"Infundibulum","def":"The smooth-walled outflow tract of the right ventricle leading to the pulmonary valve, also called the conus arteriosus."},{"term":"Fixed split S2","def":"A widely split second heart sound whose aortic and pulmonic components stay apart in both inspiration and expiration, typical of atrial septal defect."},{"term":"Differential cyanosis","def":"Cyanosis of the lower body with pink upper extremities, caused by deoxygenated blood entering the aorta distal to the left subclavian artery."},{"term":"Ductal-dependent lesion","def":"A defect in which survival depends on flow through the ductus arteriosus, so the infant deteriorates when the ductus closes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C01","obj":"CV-18-G.1","title":"Left-to-right shunts and Eisenmenger syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood crossing a defect moves toward the side with lower pressure or, for defects between filling chambers, toward the side that fills more easily. After birth the left side runs at systemic pressures (left ventricle about 120/10 mmHg) and the right side at pulmonary pressures (right ventricle about 25/5 mmHg), so a ventricular septal defect (VSD), an atrial septal defect (ASD), or a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) sends oxygenated blood back to the lungs. Systemic arterial blood is still fully saturated, so the child is not cyanotic. The cost is volume: the lungs and the chambers carrying the recirculated blood receive more flow than the body uses, expressed as a Qp:Qs ratio above 1."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is high and falls over the first several weeks after birth. A large VSD or PDA therefore causes few symptoms at birth, then shunts more and causes heart failure within the first one to two months. The location of the shunt also decides what the pulmonary arteries face. A defect before the tricuspid valve (ASD) adds flow at low pressure, while a defect after it (VSD, PDA) transmits systemic pressure as well as extra flow, so pulmonary vascular disease develops earlier and more often with VSD and PDA."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic high flow and pressure injure the pulmonary endothelium. Nitric oxide and prostacyclin fall, endothelin-1 rises, and the arterioles undergo medial hypertrophy, intimal proliferation and fibrosis, and finally plexiform lesions, the same changes seen in pulmonary arterial hypertension (CV-18-A). As PVR rises, the right ventricle hypertrophies, the shunt shrinks, then becomes bidirectional, and finally reverses when right-sided pressure exceeds left-sided pressure. Advanced remodeling is irreversible."},{"type":"flow","title":"From left-to-right shunt to Eisenmenger syndrome","steps":["Left-to-right shunt overloads the pulmonary circulation","High flow and pressure injure pulmonary endothelium","Medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis, plexiform lesions raise PVR","Right ventricle hypertrophies as its pressure rises","Right pressure exceeds left; shunt reverses","Late cyanosis, clubbing, polycythemia"],"caption":"Eisenmenger syndrome is the end stage of an uncorrected left-to-right shunt; the reversal follows years of pulmonary vascular remodeling."},{"type":"p","text":"Each Eisenmenger finding follows from the reversal. Deoxygenated blood enters the systemic arteries, so cyanosis appears late and does not correct with supplemental oxygen, because the shunted blood never passes the alveoli. Renal hypoxia stimulates erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia, which raises viscosity and makes cyanosis more visible. Clubbing accompanies chronic hypoxemia; a leading explanation is that platelet clumps bypass the pulmonary capillaries and release growth factors in the fingertips. The right ventricle hypertrophies, P2 becomes loud, and the original murmur softens as the pressure difference across the defect disappears."},{"type":"table","title":"Shunt categories","cols":["Feature","Left-to-right (VSD, ASD, PDA)","Right-to-left (tetralogy of Fallot, transposition)","Eisenmenger (reversed)"],"rows":[["Cyanosis","None at first","Early, often in infancy","Late, after years of shunting"],["Pulmonary blood flow","Increased","Decreased (tetralogy) or separated circuits (transposition)","Falls as PVR rises"],["Main burden","Volume overload, heart failure","Hypoxemia","Hypoxemia plus right ventricular pressure overload"],["Response to oxygen","Saturation normal on room air","Little rise in arterial PO2","Little rise in arterial PO2"]],"caption":"Shunt direction, not the defect itself, determines whether the patient is cyanotic."},{"type":"example","title":"Estimating Qp:Qs from oxygen saturations","text":"By the Fick principle, Qp:Qs = (systemic arterial minus mixed venous saturation) divided by (pulmonary venous minus pulmonary arterial saturation). A child with a VSD has systemic arterial saturation 98%, superior vena cava saturation 70%, pulmonary venous saturation 98%, and pulmonary arterial saturation 85%. Qp:Qs = (98 minus 70) divided by (98 minus 85) = 28 divided by 13, about 2.2, a large shunt. The step up in saturation from the vena cava to the pulmonary artery is the shunted oxygenated blood; with no step up, Qp:Qs would be 1."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C02","obj":"CV-18-G.2","title":"Ventricular septal defect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ventricular septal defect is the most common congenital heart defect at birth when bicuspid aortic valve is not counted. The interventricular septum has a thick muscular part and a small membranous part just below the aortic valve. The membranous septum forms last, from fusion of the endocardial cushions, the conotruncal ridges, and the muscular septum, so it is the part most often left incomplete; most VSDs are membranous or perimembranous. Muscular defects lie within the trabeculated septum and may be multiple. VSD is associated with trisomy 21, fetal alcohol exposure, and other chromosomal syndromes, and it is one of the four components of tetralogy of Fallot."},{"type":"p","text":"Left ventricular pressure exceeds right ventricular pressure from the start of isovolumetric contraction until the end of ejection, so blood crosses the defect throughout systole. That produces a harsh holosystolic murmur, starting with S1 and often obscuring it, loudest at the left lower sternal border (third and fourth intercostal spaces), frequently with a thrill. Raising systemic resistance, as with sustained handgrip, increases the left-to-right gradient and makes the murmur louder."},{"type":"p","text":"The shunted blood leaves the left ventricle during systole and passes almost directly into the pulmonary artery, so the volume load falls on the pulmonary circulation, the left atrium, and the left ventricle. That is why a large VSD enlarges the left atrium and left ventricle, while the right ventricle enlarges mainly when pulmonary pressure rises."},{"type":"table","title":"Small versus large VSD","cols":["Feature","Small (restrictive)","Large (nonrestrictive)"],"rows":[["Pressure gradient across defect","Large; RV pressure stays low","Small; RV pressure approaches LV pressure"],["Murmur","Loud, harsh holosystolic","Softer or shorter; apical mid-diastolic rumble from high mitral flow; loud P2"],["Shunt size","Limited by the defect","Set by PVR versus systemic resistance"],["Course","Often closes spontaneously; endocarditis risk if it persists","Heart failure in the first months; Eisenmenger if uncorrected"]],"caption":"Murmur loudness reflects the pressure gradient, not defect size, so the loudest VSDs are often the smallest."},{"type":"p","text":"Many small defects, especially muscular ones, close spontaneously during early childhood as surrounding muscle grows or as tricuspid septal leaflet tissue covers a perimembranous opening. A large defect allows ventricular pressures to equalize, so the size of the shunt is set by the ratio of pulmonary to systemic resistance. As PVR falls over the first weeks, pulmonary flow rises and left heart volume overload produces heart failure: tachypnea, sweating and fatigue with feeds, poor weight gain, and hepatomegaly. Without correction, pulmonary vascular disease can become irreversible within the first few years of life, and Eisenmenger reversal follows. A defect just below the aortic valve can let an aortic cusp prolapse into it, adding aortic regurgitation, and the high-velocity jet damages the right ventricular endocardium where it strikes, creating a site for endocarditis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C03","obj":"CV-18-G.3","title":"Atrial septal defects and patent foramen ovale","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The atrial septum forms from two overlapping partitions (CV-1). The septum primum grows toward the endocardial cushions, closing the ostium primum, while perforations in its upper part create the ostium secundum. The thicker septum secundum then grows on its right side, leaving an opening, the foramen ovale, whose flap valve is the septum primum. In the fetus, high right atrial pressure pushes the flap open; after birth, left atrial pressure rises above right and presses the flap shut, and the two septa usually fuse over the following months."},{"type":"table","title":"Defects of the atrial septum","cols":["Lesion","Embryologic basis","Associations and distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Ostium secundum ASD","Excess resorption of septum primum or a septum secundum too small to cover the ostium secundum; true tissue deficit at the fossa ovalis","Most common ASD; fetal alcohol exposure, Holt-Oram syndrome (upper limb defects); ECG right axis deviation and incomplete RBBB"],["Ostium primum ASD","Septum primum fails to meet the endocardial cushions; a partial atrioventricular septal defect","Trisomy 21; usually a cleft anterior mitral leaflet with mitral regurgitation; ECG left (superior) axis deviation"],["Patent foramen ovale","Septum primum and septum secundum fail to fuse; no tissue is missing, the flap is simply unsealed","Present in about one in four adults; no shunt while left atrial pressure exceeds right"]],"caption":"An ASD is missing septal tissue; a patent foramen ovale is complete tissue that never sealed."},{"type":"p","text":"Left atrial pressure is only a few mmHg above right, so an ASD shunts left to right mainly because the thin-walled right ventricle is more compliant and fills more easily than the left. Flow across the defect itself is low velocity and silent. The extra volume passes through the right atrium, right ventricle, and pulmonary arteries, enlarging them, while the left atrium decompresses through the defect. The resulting increased flow across a normal pulmonic valve produces a systolic ejection (flow) murmur at the left upper sternal border, and a large shunt adds a mid-diastolic rumble from increased tricuspid flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Normally inspiration increases venous return to the right heart and delays pulmonic valve closure, so S2 splits in inspiration and narrows in expiration. In an ASD the right ventricle already carries a large stroke volume in both phases. In inspiration the extra systemic venous return is offset by less left-to-right shunting; in expiration the shunt grows again. Right ventricular volume, and therefore the delay of P2, stays nearly constant, giving a wide, fixed split S2."},{"type":"p","text":"Many ASDs cause few symptoms until adulthood, when right atrial stretch produces atrial fibrillation or flutter, exercise intolerance, right heart failure, or, in a minority, Eisenmenger syndrome. Any atrial communication can briefly reverse when right atrial pressure exceeds left, as during release of a Valsalva maneuver, coughing, straining, or acute pulmonary embolism. A deep vein thrombus can then cross to the left atrium and cause a stroke or limb ischemia: a paradoxical embolism. This is why an unexplained stroke in a young adult raises suspicion of a PFO or ASD."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patent foramen ovale cause no murmur and no volume overload, yet still allow a paradoxical embolism?","explain":"While left atrial pressure exceeds right, the septum primum flap is held against the septum secundum and no blood crosses. It opens only when right atrial pressure transiently exceeds left, which is exactly when a venous clot can pass right to left.","check":"The table of atrial septal lesions and the paragraph on transient reversal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C04","obj":"CV-18-G.4","title":"Patent ductus arteriosus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ductus arteriosus develops from the distal part of the left sixth aortic arch. It joins the pulmonary trunk near its bifurcation, at the root of the left pulmonary artery, to the aortic arch just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery. In the fetus, pulmonary vascular resistance is high, so most right ventricular output bypasses the lungs through the ductus into the descending aorta. Its smooth muscle is held relaxed by low fetal oxygen tension and by prostaglandin E2 (PGE2), produced by the placenta and the ductus and poorly cleared by the unventilated fetal lungs."},{"type":"flow","title":"Closure of the ductus arteriosus after birth","steps":["First breaths expand the lungs; PVR falls","Arterial PO2 rises and constricts ductal smooth muscle","Placenta removed; perfused lungs clear PGE2","Ductus closes functionally within the first few days","Intimal proliferation and fibrosis form the ligamentum arteriosum"],"caption":"Rising oxygen and falling PGE2 act together; a ductus that misses either signal can stay open."},{"type":"p","text":"Prematurity is the major risk factor because immature ductal muscle constricts weakly to oxygen and remains sensitive to PGE2. Congenital rubella, especially from first trimester infection, causes PDA together with cataracts and sensorineural deafness, and birth at high altitude, where arterial PO2 is low, also predisposes. Prostaglandin dependence is confirmed pharmacologically: cyclooxygenase inhibition lowers PGE2 and promotes closure, whereas exogenous prostaglandin E1 holds the ductus open."},{"type":"p","text":"After birth, aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure in both systole and diastole, so blood flows from aorta to pulmonary artery throughout the cycle. The result is a continuous machine-like murmur, loudest below the left clavicle (left infraclavicular area) and peaking around S2. Because the aorta drains into the low-resistance pulmonary circuit during diastole, diastolic pressure falls, and the extra left ventricular stroke volume raises systolic pressure, giving a wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses. As with a VSD, the recirculated blood returns to the left atrium and left ventricle, so they dilate and a large PDA causes heart failure. In a premature infant whose PVR is still high, the diastolic gradient may be small and the murmur only systolic."},{"type":"p","text":"An uncorrected large PDA leads to pulmonary vascular disease and Eisenmenger reversal. Deoxygenated pulmonary arterial blood then enters the aorta beyond the left subclavian artery, so the lower body receives desaturated blood while the head and arms are supplied from vessels proximal to the ductus. The result is differential cyanosis: cyanotic, clubbed toes with pink fingers. Other complications are infective endarteritis at the ductal jet and, rarely, ductal aneurysm. Anatomically, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the ligamentum arteriosum, which is why surgery in this region can cause hoarseness."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a PDA with Eisenmenger syndrome, which limbs have the lower oxygen saturation, and why are the arms spared?","explain":"The legs. The ductus joins the aorta distal to the left subclavian artery, so reversed flow mixes only into the descending aorta; the brachiocephalic trunk and left subclavian artery receive left ventricular blood that has not been diluted.","check":"The anatomy of the ductus in the first paragraph and the Eisenmenger paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C05","obj":"CV-18-G.5","title":"Tetralogy of Fallot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic congenital heart defect. Its four features arise from one developmental error: anterosuperior (and leftward) displacement of the infundibular (outlet) septum. The displaced septum crowds the right ventricular outflow tract, producing infundibular pulmonary stenosis, often with a small pulmonary valve and arteries. Because it no longer lines up with the muscular septum, it leaves a large malalignment VSD, and the aortic root, shifted rightward, sits above that defect (overriding aorta). Right ventricular hypertrophy is the consequence, since the right ventricle pumps against the obstruction at systemic pressure through the unrestrictive VSD."},{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells migrate into the conotruncal ridges and help build the outflow septum. That explains the link with 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome), which also causes thymic and parathyroid hypoplasia and is associated with truncus arteriosus and interrupted aortic arch. Trisomy 21 and fetal alcohol exposure are other associations."},{"type":"p","text":"With a large VSD the two ventricles share one pressure, so where right ventricular blood goes depends on the ratio of outflow tract resistance to systemic vascular resistance (SVR). Severe stenosis diverts deoxygenated blood across the VSD into the aorta, causing early cyanosis; mild stenosis gives a balanced or even left-to-right shunt and little cyanosis. The degree of outflow obstruction therefore sets how blue the child is. The VSD is silent because there is no pressure gradient across it. The murmur is a systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border from flow through the narrowed outflow tract, and S2 is single because P2 is soft. Pulmonary blood flow is reduced, so Eisenmenger physiology does not develop."},{"type":"p","text":"On the chest film the hypertrophied right ventricle lifts the apex, and the small main pulmonary artery leaves a concave upper left heart border, giving a boot-shaped heart with reduced pulmonary vascular markings; a right-sided aortic arch is common. Chronic hypoxemia causes clubbing and secondary polycythemia, and the right-to-left shunt allows paradoxical emboli and brain abscess."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hypercyanotic (tet) spell","steps":["Crying, feeding, or dehydration; outflow narrows or SVR falls","More right-to-left flow across the VSD","Arterial PO2 falls; metabolic acidosis develops","Hyperpnea increases systemic venous return to the RV","Acidosis lowers SVR further; shunt increases again"],"caption":"Each turn of the loop sends more right ventricular output to the aorta and less to the lungs; raising SVR breaks the cycle."},{"type":"p","text":"During a spell the child becomes deeply cyanotic and hyperpneic, and the outflow murmur softens or disappears because less blood crosses the obstructed outflow tract. Squatting kinks the femoral arteries and raises SVR, so more right ventricular output is pushed through the pulmonary outflow; it also briefly reduces return of desaturated blood from the legs. The knee-chest position in an infant works the same way."},{"type":"example","title":"Why squatting helps and exertion hurts","text":"The right ventricle has two exits: the stenotic pulmonary outflow (fixed high resistance) and the VSD into the aorta (resistance equal to SVR). Flow divides in inverse proportion to resistance. During exercise, muscle arteriolar dilation lowers SVR, so a larger share of right ventricular output takes the aortic exit and saturation falls. Squatting raises SVR, so the share sent to the lungs rises and saturation improves. The same logic predicts that fever or a hot bath, which lower SVR, worsen cyanosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C06","obj":"CV-18-G.6","title":"Bicuspid aortic valve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bicuspid aortic valve is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly, present in about 1 to 2% of people and more often in males. The valve forms from cushion tissue in the outflow tract; when two of the three cusps fail to separate, usually the right and left coronary cusps, the valve has two unequal leaflets, often with a raphe (ridge) marking the fused commissure. Most cases are sporadic, but it clusters in families, and some familial cases involve NOTCH1 mutations. It is common in Turner syndrome (45,X) and in coarctation of the aorta, and the three conditions form a classic triad on examinations."},{"type":"p","text":"Two cusps must fold and crease more than three to open fully, and they open to an eccentric, often domed orifice with turbulent flow. Repeated mechanical stress injures the leaflet endothelium and starts the same process that causes age-related calcific aortic stenosis: lipid infiltration, chronic inflammation, transformation of valve interstitial cells toward an osteoblast-like phenotype, and dystrophic calcification. In a bicuspid valve this proceeds faster, so significant stenosis typically appears one to two decades earlier, often between the 40s and 60s instead of the 70s or 80s for a normal three-cusp valve. The findings of aortic stenosis are covered in CV-18-D. A specific clue is an early systolic ejection click from the domed valve snapping open, heard while the leaflets are still mobile and lost as they calcify."},{"type":"p","text":"The same valve can also leak. A larger fused cusp may prolapse, or a dilated root may pull the commissures apart, so aortic regurgitation is more typical in younger patients and stenosis in older ones. Damaged leaflet surfaces and turbulent jets favor platelet and fibrin deposits that circulating bacteria can colonize, so infective endocarditis is more frequent than with a normal valve."},{"type":"p","text":"The ascending aorta is often abnormal too. The media shows smooth muscle loss and elastic fiber fragmentation, an intrinsic wall weakness that accompanies the valve defect and can be present even when the valve works normally, and the eccentric jet adds focal wall stress. The ascending aorta may dilate into an aneurysm, and the risk of aortic dissection is higher than in the general population, especially when Turner syndrome or coarctation coexists."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of bicuspid aortic valve and their mechanisms","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Calcific aortic stenosis","Abnormal leaflet stress accelerates inflammatory calcification"],["Aortic regurgitation","Cusp prolapse or root dilation prevents coaptation"],["Infective endocarditis","Endothelial injury from turbulent flow allows bacterial seeding"],["Ascending aortic aneurysm or dissection","Intrinsic medial weakness plus eccentric jet stress"]],"caption":"One structural defect explains four distinct complications, so a single patient may develop several over a lifetime."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C07","obj":"CV-18-G.7","title":"Coarctation of the aorta","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Coarctation of the aorta is a localized narrowing, almost always at the junction with the ductus arteriosus or ligamentum arteriosum (juxtaductal), just distal to the left subclavian artery. A leading explanation is that ductal tissue extends into the aortic wall and constricts along with the ductus after birth. It is more common in males, occurs in a substantial minority of girls with Turner syndrome, and is accompanied by a bicuspid aortic valve in most patients."},{"type":"p","text":"Two presentations follow from where the narrowing sits and how severe it is. A severe narrowing, often with arch hypoplasia (historically called infantile or preductal), leaves the lower body dependent on right ventricular blood flowing through the PDA, which causes lower body cyanosis. When the ductus closes in the first weeks, perfusion of the lower body and kidneys collapses, producing shock, acidosis, and absent femoral pulses. A milder narrowing (historically adult or postductal) allows time for collateral vessels to develop, and the patient may be detected only in childhood or adulthood because of hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"The narrowing is a high resistance in series with everything downstream. Pressure upstream rises, so the arms, supplied by vessels arising proximal to the coarctation, are hypertensive. Pressure and flow beyond it are reduced, so femoral pulses are weak and delayed relative to the radial pulse (radiofemoral delay), leg blood pressure is lower than arm pressure (normally it is slightly higher), and the legs may be cool or claudicate with exercise. Reduced renal perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which adds to the hypertension. The left ventricle hypertrophies against the high afterload, and long-standing hypertension proximal to the narrowing raises the risk of heart failure, aortic dissection, and rupture of cerebral berry aneurysms, which are associated with coarctation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collateral flow around a coarctation","steps":["Subclavian artery above the coarctation","Internal thoracic artery","Anterior intercostal arteries","Posterior intercostal arteries, flow reversed","Descending aorta below the coarctation"],"caption":"Chronically high collateral flow enlarges the posterior intercostal arteries, which erode the lower rib margins."},{"type":"p","text":"The enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries run in the costal grooves and erode the inferior margins of the ribs, producing rib notching on the chest film, usually from about the third rib downward. The first two ribs are spared because their intercostal arteries arise from the costocervical trunk rather than the aorta. Notching takes years to appear and is rarely seen in young children. The coarctation itself and its indented contour, with dilation above and below, may form a figure 3 sign, and flow through the narrowing and collaterals produces a systolic or continuous murmur heard over the back between the scapulae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-07-C08","obj":"CV-18-G.8","title":"Transposition of the great vessels","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normally neural crest cells form a pair of conotruncal ridges that grow in a spiral, so the aorticopulmonary septum twists about 180 degrees and connects the aorta to the left ventricle and the pulmonary trunk to the right ventricle. In transposition of the great vessels, the septum grows straight instead of spiraling. The aorta arises from the right ventricle, usually anterior and to the right, and the pulmonary artery arises from the left ventricle. Maternal diabetes before conception is a recognized risk factor, and the lesion is more common in males."},{"type":"p","text":"The result is two parallel circulations instead of two in series. Systemic venous blood returns to the right atrium and right ventricle and is pumped back out the aorta to the body without being oxygenated. Pulmonary venous blood returns to the left atrium and left ventricle and is pumped back to the lungs. The left-sided circuit is highly saturated but useless to the tissues, while the aortic blood is deeply desaturated."},{"type":"flow","title":"Parallel circulations in transposition","steps":["Body to right atrium and right ventricle","Right ventricle to aorta, back to body","Lungs to left atrium and left ventricle","Left ventricle to pulmonary artery, back to lungs","Survival requires mixing through PFO or ASD, PDA, or VSD"],"caption":"Without a connection between the two loops, oxygenated blood never reaches the systemic arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"Life is possible only if blood crosses between the two circuits, and the crossing must go both ways so that volume entering one circuit is returned. In the fetus this causes little difficulty, because oxygen comes from the placenta and the foramen ovale and ductus are open. After birth, an infant with an intact ventricular septum depends on a foramen ovale or ASD and a PDA. Severe cyanosis appears within hours to days and worsens as the ductus closes, and anything that keeps the ductus open or enlarges the atrial opening improves mixing. A large VSD allows more mixing, so cyanosis is milder, but pulmonary overcirculation brings early heart failure. Untreated, most infants die within the first months of life."},{"type":"p","text":"Supplemental oxygen raises the saturation only slightly, because it oxygenates blood that simply recirculates through the lungs. Transposition is the most common cause of cyanosis that appears on the first day of life, whereas tetralogy of Fallot is the more common cyanotic lesion overall. Because the great arteries lie front to back, the shadow of the upper mediastinum is narrow, and with a rounded heart this gives the egg-on-a-string appearance on the chest film."},{"type":"table","title":"The two classic cyanotic lesions compared","cols":["Feature","Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of the great vessels"],"rows":[["Embryologic error","Anterosuperior displacement of infundibular septum","Aorticopulmonary septum fails to spiral"],["Associations","22q11.2 deletion, trisomy 21","Maternal diabetes, male sex"],["Circulation","In series; right-to-left across VSD","Parallel; mixing required"],["Onset of cyanosis","Variable; set by outflow obstruction","Within hours to days of birth"],["Pulmonary blood flow","Reduced","Normal or increased"],["Chest film","Boot-shaped heart, dark lung fields","Egg on a string, narrow mediastinum"]],"caption":"Both are conotruncal defects of outflow tract septation, but they cause cyanosis by different mechanisms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant with transposition and an intact ventricular septum becomes much bluer on day 3. What changed, and why does more inspired oxygen barely help?","explain":"The ductus arteriosus is closing, removing one of the only mixing sites. Extra oxygen only raises saturation in the pulmonary loop, which recirculates to the lungs; it reaches the aorta only through whatever mixing remains.","check":"The paragraphs on parallel circulation and on mixing sites."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology predicts the lesion and genetics predicts the embryology. Endocardial cushion failure explains ostium primum ASD and atrioventricular septal defects in trisomy 21; neural crest failure in the conotruncal ridges explains tetralogy of Fallot, truncus arteriosus, and interrupted arch in 22q11.2 deletion, and failed spiraling of the same septum gives transposition; disordered outflow cushion and aortic wall development links bicuspid valve, coarctation, and aortic dilation in Turner syndrome. Teratogens act during cardiogenesis in weeks 3 to 8, so first trimester rubella, maternal diabetes, and alcohol carry the greatest risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology then predict the course. Shunt direction follows pressure and compliance, murmurs follow gradients, chamber enlargement follows where the extra volume travels, and cyanosis follows any route that lets venous blood bypass the lungs. Pathology adds the late picture: pulmonary vascular remodeling, endocarditis on turbulent jets, dystrophic calcification, and medial degeneration of the aorta. The limits of this reasoning matter too. Many patients have more than one defect, the same lesion can be silent or severe depending on its size, and findings change with age as PVR falls in infancy and rises again with vascular disease."}],"summary":["Shunt direction depends on pressure or compliance; after birth the left side is higher, so VSD, ASD, and PDA shunt left to right without early cyanosis.","Eisenmenger syndrome: chronic shunt flow remodels pulmonary arterioles, PVR rises, the shunt reverses, and late cyanosis, clubbing, polycythemia, and RVH follow.","VSD, usually membranous, gives a harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; small defects are loud and often close, large ones cause heart failure as PVR falls.","Secundum ASD is most common; primum ASD is an endocardial cushion defect of trisomy 21; PFO is unfused but complete tissue. ASD gives a fixed split S2 and a pulmonic flow murmur.","Any atrial communication can allow paradoxical embolism when right atrial pressure transiently exceeds left.","PDA, from the sixth arch, persists with prematurity and congenital rubella; it gives a continuous machine-like murmur, bounding pulses, and with Eisenmenger, blue toes and pink fingers.","Tetralogy of Fallot stems from anterosuperior infundibular septum displacement; outflow obstruction sets cyanosis, and squatting raises SVR to push blood to the lungs.","Bicuspid aortic valve calcifies early and also causes regurgitation, endocarditis, and ascending aortic aneurysm or dissection; it clusters with Turner syndrome and coarctation.","Coarctation raises arm pressure, weakens and delays femoral pulses, and drives intercostal collaterals that notch the ribs.","Transposition creates parallel circulations; survival depends on two-way mixing through a PFO or ASD, PDA, or VSD."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on congenital heart disease"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, heart and great vessels"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Fetal and neonatal physiology"}]},"CV-18-H":{"unit":"CV-18-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08","title":"Hemodynamic Disorders: Thrombosis, Embolism, Infarction, Hemorrhage, Edema, and Shock","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the general pathology of blood flow: how thrombi form, where emboli travel, why dead tissue looks pale or red, how bleeding and edema develop, and how circulatory failure becomes shock and then multiorgan failure. These processes link into chains. A deep vein thrombus becomes a pulmonary embolus, a mural thrombus becomes a stroke or renal infarct, and septic shock produces acute tubular necrosis, ARDS, and DIC. A few principles explain nearly everything here: the balance of procoagulant and anticoagulant forces, Starling forces at the capillary, and the relationship mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output times systemic vascular resistance.","prerequisites":["Platelet adhesion and aggregation, the coagulation cascade, the natural anticoagulants (antithrombin, protein C, protein S), and fibrinolysis","Starling forces and lymphatic return at the capillary (CV-11.5, CV-10.3)","Flow, pressure, and resistance, and the baroreceptor reflex (CV-11)","Reversible and irreversible cell injury and the patterns of necrosis","Innate immune recognition of microbes by Toll-like receptors and the actions of TNF and IL-1"],"connections":[{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Coronary thrombosis on a disrupted plaque is the arterial thrombus of this chapter, and the myocardial infarct is its white, coagulative infarct."},{"to":"Congestive heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"Raised venous pressure and renal sodium retention in heart failure explain transudative edema, and pump failure is the core of cardiogenic shock."},{"to":"Pericardial disease (CV-18-F) and heart and lung interactions (CV-15)","how":"Tamponade and massive pulmonary embolism are the main causes of obstructive shock, and pulmonary embolism disturbs ventilation and perfusion matching."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Inherited and acquired hypercoagulable states, platelet and factor disorders behind petechiae and hematomas, and DIC all rest on the hemostatic balance taught here."},{"to":"Immunology and microbiology (CV-16, CV-17)","how":"Endotoxin signaling through TLR4 and the cytokine storm of TNF and IL-1 connect bacterial infection to septic shock."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thrombus","def":"A solid mass of platelets, fibrin, and trapped blood cells formed inside a vessel or heart chamber in living, flowing blood."},{"term":"Virchow triad","def":"The three influences that promote thrombosis: endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence), and hypercoagulability."},{"term":"Lines of Zahn","def":"Alternating pale layers of platelets and fibrin and dark layers of red cells, laid down in flowing blood, that mark a thrombus formed before death."},{"term":"Embolus","def":"A detached solid, liquid, or gaseous mass carried in the blood to a site distant from its origin, where it lodges and blocks the vessel."},{"term":"Infarct","def":"An area of ischemic necrosis caused by blocked arterial supply or, less often, blocked venous drainage."},{"term":"Hypercoagulability","def":"Any change in the blood, inherited or acquired, that tips the balance toward thrombus formation; also called thrombophilia."},{"term":"Transudate","def":"Protein-poor, cell-poor fluid that leaves vessels with intact walls because of high hydrostatic or low oncotic pressure."},{"term":"Exudate","def":"Protein-rich, often cell-rich fluid that leaves vessels whose permeability has been increased by inflammation."},{"term":"Shock","def":"Widespread tissue hypoperfusion in which oxygen delivery falls below metabolic demand, from low cardiac output or low effective circulating volume."},{"term":"Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","def":"Pressure measured with a balloon catheter wedged in a pulmonary artery branch; it approximates left atrial pressure and so left ventricular filling pressure."},{"term":"Multiorgan dysfunction syndrome","def":"Progressive failure of two or more organ systems after shock, sepsis, or severe injury, driven by hypoperfusion and systemic inflammation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C01","obj":"CV-18-H.1","title":"Thrombosis: Virchow triad, arterial and venous thrombi, and their fates","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal endothelium keeps blood fluid. It expresses thrombomodulin and heparan sulfate, releases nitric oxide and prostacyclin, and makes tissue plasminogen activator. A thrombus forms when this balance tips toward clotting, and the Virchow triad names the three ways it tips. Each limb can act alone, but most thrombi need two or more."},{"type":"table","title":"Virchow triad","cols":["Limb","Mechanism","Typical settings"],"rows":[["Endothelial injury or dysfunction","Exposes collagen and von Willebrand factor for platelet adhesion and tissue factor to start coagulation; activated endothelium also loses its anticoagulant surface","Ruptured atherosclerotic plaque, myocardial infarction (endocardium), vasculitis, valve injury, hypertension, smoking"],["Abnormal flow (stasis or turbulence)","Disrupts laminar flow so platelets contact the wall; stops activated factors from being washed away and diluted; delays inflow of inhibitors; turbulence also injures endothelium","Immobility and venous stasis, atrial fibrillation, ventricular aneurysm, dilated chambers, aortic aneurysm, hyperviscosity, sickle cell disease"],["Hypercoagulability","More procoagulant activity or less anticoagulant or fibrinolytic activity in the blood itself","Inherited and acquired thrombophilias (section C02)"]],"caption":"Endothelial injury dominates in the heart and arteries; stasis dominates in veins."},{"type":"p","text":"Flow decides the composition of the thrombus. In fast arterial flow, platelets adhering to an injured surface are the first step, and fibrin binds them together; the result is a pale gray, platelet-rich thrombus firmly attached to the wall. Arterial thrombi usually start on a ruptured plaque in coronary, cerebral, or femoral arteries and grow back toward the heart from their point of attachment. In slow venous flow, coagulation proceeds in nearly stagnant blood and traps large numbers of red cells, giving a soft red thrombus (red or stasis thrombus). Venous thrombi grow in the direction of flow, toward the heart, so a long loosely attached tail can break off. Most form in the deep veins of the leg, and those above the knee (popliteal, femoral, and iliac) are the main source of pulmonary emboli. Mural thrombi form on the wall of a heart chamber or the aorta, and vegetations are thrombi on valves."},{"type":"p","text":"Lines of Zahn are alternating pale bands of platelets and fibrin and dark bands of red cells. They form only when layers are laid down in moving blood, so they identify a thrombus formed during life. They are most obvious in thrombi of the heart and aorta and fainter in veins. A postmortem clot lacks lines of Zahn and is not attached to the wall; it is gelatinous, dark red where red cells settled, and yellow above (\"chicken fat\"), because cells sedimented in still blood."},{"type":"table","title":"Fates of a thrombus","cols":["Fate","What happens","Consequence"],"rows":[["Propagation","More platelets and fibrin accumulate","Occlusion of the vessel or a longer, less stable thrombus"],["Embolization","Part or all of the thrombus breaks off","Blocks a distant vessel (section C03)"],["Dissolution","Plasmin degrades fibrin in a recent thrombus","Thrombus disappears; older, cross-linked thrombi resist lysis"],["Organization","Endothelial cells, smooth muscle cells, and fibroblasts grow in; the thrombus becomes granulation tissue then scar","Thrombus is incorporated into the wall as a fibrous thickening"],["Recanalization","New capillary channels form through the organized thrombus","Partial restoration of flow"]],"caption":"Thrombi can also become infected, producing a septic thrombus or a mycotic aneurysm."},{"type":"stop","predict":"At autopsy a soft red clot fills the femoral vein. It lifts out easily, has no layering, and has a yellow upper portion. Did it cause the pulmonary embolus found in the lung?","explain":"Probably not. A clot with no attachment, no lines of Zahn, and a chicken-fat layer formed after death in still blood; the thrombus that embolized formed in life and would show layering.","check":"The paragraph on lines of Zahn and postmortem clots."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C02","obj":"CV-18-H.2","title":"Hypercoagulable states: inherited and acquired","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypercoagulability is the blood limb of the Virchow triad. Thrombin is the central enzyme: it converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates platelets and factors V, VIII, and XI. Three brakes restrain it. Antithrombin inactivates thrombin and factor Xa (and other serine protease factors), and heparan sulfate on the endothelium greatly speeds this reaction. Protein C is activated when thrombin binds thrombomodulin on the endothelium, and activated protein C, with protein S as its cofactor, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. Plasmin dissolves fibrin. Inherited thrombophilias either add procoagulant activity or remove one of these brakes."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited hypercoagulable states","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Mechanism of thrombosis","Notes"],"rows":[["Factor V Leiden","Point mutation in factor V (arginine 506 to glutamine), autosomal dominant","Factor Va resists cleavage by activated protein C (activated protein C resistance), so thrombin generation continues","Most common inherited cause; heterozygous in about 2 to 15% of people of European descent; risk about 5-fold in heterozygotes and about 50-fold in homozygotes"],["Prothrombin G20210A","Mutation in the 3' untranslated region of the prothrombin gene","Higher plasma prothrombin levels","Second most common; about 1 to 2% of the population"],["Antithrombin deficiency","Reduced or defective antithrombin","Thrombin and factor Xa are not inactivated","Heparin works by enhancing antithrombin, so its effect is blunted"],["Protein C or S deficiency","Reduced or defective protein C or its cofactor protein S","Factors Va and VIIIa are not inactivated","Homozygous protein C deficiency causes neonatal purpura fulminans"]],"caption":"All four mainly cause venous thrombosis. They are suspected when thrombosis occurs before about age 50, recurs, appears in unusual sites (cerebral sinuses, mesenteric or hepatic veins), or runs in the family."},{"type":"p","text":"Protein C has a short half-life, which explains warfarin-induced skin necrosis. Warfarin blocks vitamin K-dependent synthesis of factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Protein C and factor VII fall first, before factors II, IX, and X, so in the first days of treatment a person with low protein C becomes transiently more hypercoagulable, and dermal microvessels thrombose. Mild hyperhomocysteinemia is another inherited or acquired risk; homocysteine injures endothelium and promotes both arterial and venous thrombosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Acquired states are far more common, and most act through more than one limb of the triad. Antiphospholipid syndrome is an autoimmune disorder in which antibodies bind phospholipid-binding proteins, mainly beta2-glycoprotein I, and activate platelets, endothelium, and complement. It causes both arterial and venous thrombosis and recurrent pregnancy loss, and it may be primary or secondary to systemic lupus erythematosus. A paradox is typical: the lupus anticoagulant prolongs the partial thromboplastin time in the test tube, because the antibodies interfere with the phospholipid in the assay, while the patient clots in vivo. Anticardiolipin antibodies can give a false-positive nontreponemal syphilis test."},{"type":"table","title":"Acquired hypercoagulable states","cols":["Setting","Main mechanism"],"rows":[["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Antibodies to beta2-glycoprotein I activate platelets, endothelium, and complement"],["Cancer","Tumor tissue factor and mucin (adenocarcinomas of pancreas, lung, and gut) activate coagulation; migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome); also immobility and surgery"],["Pregnancy and the postpartum period","Higher hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen and other factors, lower protein S, compression of pelvic veins by the uterus"],["Estrogen (oral contraceptives, hormone therapy)","Higher hepatic synthesis of coagulation factors and lower antithrombin; risk multiplied by smoking or factor V Leiden"],["Immobility and prolonged travel","Venous stasis without the calf muscle pump"],["Surgery and trauma","Tissue factor release and endothelial injury plus postoperative immobility; orthopedic surgery of the hip and knee carries the highest risk"],["Others","Nephrotic syndrome (urinary loss of antithrombin), heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (antibodies to heparin and platelet factor 4 activate platelets), myeloproliferative neoplasms, obesity, smoking, older age"]],"caption":"Acquired states often coexist with an inherited trait, which is why a first thrombosis may appear only during pregnancy, estrogen use, or after surgery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C03","obj":"CV-18-H.3","title":"Embolism: thromboemboli, fat, amniotic fluid, and gas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An embolus travels until it reaches a vessel too narrow to pass, so its source predicts its destination. Material entering systemic veins passes through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries. Material from the left heart, aorta, or large arteries lodges in systemic arteries. A venous embolus reaches the systemic circulation only through a right-to-left opening such as a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect (paradoxical embolism). The great majority of emboli are dislodged thrombi (thromboemboli)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From deep vein thrombosis to pulmonary embolism","steps":["Stasis, injury, or hypercoagulability in deep leg veins","Red thrombus propagates toward the heart","Thrombus fragment detaches","Passes through the right atrium and ventricle","Lodges in a pulmonary artery branch","Perfusion lost; ventilation of that lung continues"],"caption":"More than 95% of pulmonary emboli arise from deep vein thrombi of the leg, most from veins above the knee."},{"type":"p","text":"The effect of a pulmonary embolus depends on its size and on the state of the circulation. Most are small and clinically silent; they organize and are incorporated into the vessel wall. Obstruction of more than about 60% of the pulmonary circulation, as by a saddle embolus at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery, causes acute right heart failure (acute cor pulmonale), circulatory collapse, and sudden death. Emboli in medium-sized arteries usually cause hemorrhage but not infarction, because bronchial arteries keep supplying lung tissue; infarction follows when left heart failure has already impaired bronchial flow. Repeated small emboli can cause pulmonary hypertension. Embolized lung is ventilated but not perfused, so dead space rises, and blood diverted to the remaining lung causes ventilation and perfusion mismatch, hypoxemia, and hyperventilation with low PaCO2."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic thromboemboli come from intracardiac mural thrombi in about 80% of cases: about two-thirds of these form over a left ventricular infarct and about a quarter in a dilated, fibrillating left atrium. The rest come from aortic aneurysms, ulcerated plaques, valve vegetations, and paradoxical emboli. About 75% lodge in the lower limbs and about 10% in the brain; the intestines, kidneys, spleen, and upper limbs make up the rest. Because these organs are supplied by end arteries, the usual result is infarction."},{"type":"table","title":"Nonthrombotic emboli","cols":["Type","Setting","Mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Fat and marrow","Long-bone or pelvic fracture; less often soft tissue trauma or burns","Fat globules block microvessels; free fatty acids released from them injure endothelium over the next days; platelets adhere to fat and are consumed","Fat embolism syndrome 1 to 3 days after injury in a small minority of those with fat in the lungs: hypoxemia, tachypnea, neurologic change, petechial rash, thrombocytopenia"],["Amniotic fluid","Labor and the immediate postpartum period","Fluid enters maternal veins through tears in membranes or uterine veins, triggering an anaphylactoid reaction and activating coagulation","Sudden dyspnea, cyanosis, shock, seizures, then DIC; fetal squames, lanugo hair, vernix fat, and mucin in maternal pulmonary vessels"],["Gas (air)","Chest wall injury, obstetric procedures, opened venous lines","Bubbles coalesce and obstruct flow; usually more than about 100 mL is needed for symptoms","Sudden collapse, ischemia of the target organ"],["Gas (nitrogen): decompression sickness","Rapid ascent by divers or rapid decompression in flight","Nitrogen dissolved under high pressure comes out of solution as bubbles","Joint and muscle pain (the bends), respiratory distress (the chokes); chronic caisson disease causes ischemic necrosis of the femoral head, tibia, and humerus"],["Atheroembolism","Manipulation of an atherosclerotic aorta (catheterization, surgery)","Cholesterol crystals and plaque debris lodge in small arteries","Renal failure, livedo reticularis, digital ischemia; cholesterol clefts in arterioles"]],"caption":"Septic emboli (from infected vegetations) and tumor emboli also occur and carry their infection or tumor to the site where they lodge."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C04","obj":"CV-18-H.4","title":"Infarction: white and red infarcts, necrosis pattern, and infarct size","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An infarct is ischemic necrosis caused by loss of blood flow. Nearly all infarcts follow arterial occlusion by thrombosis or embolism; less common causes are vasospasm, external compression, torsion, rupture, and venous outflow obstruction. Infarcts in solid organs are usually wedge-shaped, with the occluded vessel at the apex and the organ surface at the base, where a fibrinous exudate often covers the overlying serosa. Two separate questions describe every infarct: how much blood it contains (white or red) and how the dead tissue breaks down (coagulative or liquefactive)."},{"type":"table","title":"White versus red infarcts","cols":["Feature","White (pale, anemic) infarct","Red (hemorrhagic) infarct"],"rows":[["Cause","Arterial occlusion","Venous occlusion, or arterial occlusion where blood can still enter"],["Tissue","Solid, dense organs with end-arterial supply","Loose tissue, dual-supply organs, previously congested tissue, reperfused tissue"],["Why","No alternative inflow, and dense tissue limits seepage of blood into the necrotic area","Blood from a second supply, from backed-up venous drainage, or from restored flow enters damaged vessels and leaks into the necrotic area"],["Examples","Heart, kidney, spleen","Lung (pulmonary and bronchial arteries), small intestine (rich anastomoses), testis or ovary after torsion, brain after embolus lysis, heart after reperfusion"]],"caption":"Infarcts caused by infected emboli (septic infarcts) become abscesses."},{"type":"p","text":"The necrosis pattern depends on the tissue, not on the color. In almost every organ, ischemia denatures structural proteins and lytic enzymes alike, so cell outlines and tissue architecture persist for days while nuclei disappear: this is coagulative necrosis. Neutrophils then arrive at the border, macrophages remove debris, and granulation tissue is replaced by a scar. The brain is the exception. It has little collagen framework, and its lipid-rich tissue is digested by released lysosomal enzymes and cleared by microglia, so an infarct liquefies (liquefactive necrosis) and leaves a fluid-filled cavity rather than a scar. A brain infarct can therefore be red and liquefactive, and a reperfused myocardial infarct red and coagulative."},{"type":"p","text":"Coagulative necrosis follows a predictable time course that dates an infarct. Cell death is not visible by light microscopy for the first several hours; nuclear loss and eosinophilic cytoplasm appear over about 4 to 12 hours, neutrophils infiltrate the border within the first 1 to 2 days, and macrophages and granulation tissue follow over the next week or two. The margin of a recent infarct is often hyperemic. The infarcted area is replaced by scar over weeks, whereas a brain infarct leaves a cyst lined by reactive astrocytes."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that decide whether and how large an infarct forms","cols":["Factor","Effect"],"rows":[["Anatomy of the blood supply","Dual supply (lung, liver, hand and forearm) or rich anastomoses protect; end arteries (kidney, spleen) do not"],["Rate of occlusion","Slow narrowing allows collateral vessels to enlarge, as in chronic coronary disease; sudden occlusion does not"],["Vulnerability to hypoxia","Neurons die after about 3 to 4 minutes, myocytes after about 20 to 30 minutes, fibroblasts survive many hours"],["Oxygen content of blood","Anemia, hypoxemia, or heart failure enlarge an infarct and can cause one after a partial occlusion"]],"caption":"These factors explain why the same occlusion may cause no damage in one person and a large infarct in another."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with chronic left heart failure develops a medium-sized pulmonary embolus. A second patient with a normal heart has an embolus of the same size. Which one infarcts, and what color is the infarct?","explain":"The patient in heart failure. Bronchial arterial flow normally keeps lung tissue alive, but congestion and reduced output impair it; when infarction occurs it is red, because blood still enters from the bronchial circulation and congested veins.","check":"The white versus red table and the factors table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C05","obj":"CV-18-H.5","title":"Hemorrhage: patterns in tissue and effects by volume and site","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemorrhage is escape of blood from a vessel. Its pattern in the skin and mucosa points to the defect behind it. Pinpoint bleeds come from capillaries and venules that are normally sealed by platelets, so they suggest a platelet or small-vessel problem. Large, deep bleeds into muscles, joints, and soft tissue follow minor injury when the fibrin clot that should stabilize the platelet plug cannot form, so they suggest a coagulation factor deficiency."},{"type":"table","title":"Hemorrhage into tissues","cols":["Term","Size and form","Typical causes"],"rows":[["Petechiae","1 to 2 mm pinpoint hemorrhages in skin, mucosa, or serosa; do not blanch","Thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction, raised intravascular pressure, loss of vessel wall support (scurvy)"],["Purpura","3 mm or larger, up to about 1 cm","The causes of petechiae, plus vasculitis, trauma, and vascular fragility; palpable purpura suggests small-vessel vasculitis"],["Ecchymosis","Larger, flat subcutaneous hemorrhage (a bruise), usually over 1 to 2 cm","Trauma, worsened by any bleeding disorder"],["Hematoma","A collected mass of blood within tissue, raised or space-occupying","Trauma, factor deficiencies such as hemophilia, ruptured vessel or aneurysm"]],"caption":"Bleeding into body cavities is named for the site: hemothorax, hemopericardium, hemoperitoneum, hemarthrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"A bruise changes color as macrophages break down hemoglobin. It begins red-blue from hemoglobin, turns blue-green as heme is converted to biliverdin and then bilirubin, and ends golden-brown from hemosiderin. A large hematoma can raise serum unconjugated bilirubin as it is resorbed."},{"type":"h","text":"Volume and rate"},{"type":"p","text":"Blood volume is about 70 mL/kg in adults, about 5 L in a 70 kg person. Rapid loss of up to about 10 to 15% is tolerated with little change. Beyond that, the baroreceptor reflex raises heart rate and constricts arterioles and veins, and the pulse pressure narrows. When acute loss exceeds about 20% of blood volume, these compensations begin to fail and hypovolemic (hemorrhagic) shock develops; losses of about 40% are immediately life-threatening. Slow or recurrent external loss, as from a bleeding peptic ulcer or heavy menstruation, depletes iron and causes iron deficiency anemia, whereas blood shed into tissues or body cavities is broken down and its iron reused."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the hematocrit is normal right after a large hemorrhage","text":"A man loses 1.5 L of blood from a stab wound and arrives within 30 minutes, pale and tachycardic. His hematocrit is 44%. He has lost whole blood, cells and plasma in the same proportion, so the concentration of red cells in what remains is unchanged even though total red cell mass has fallen by about 30%. Over the next hours, lower capillary hydrostatic pressure draws interstitial fluid into the vessels (transcapillary refill), and the kidney retains sodium and water through renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone, and ADH. This cell-free volume dilutes the remaining red cells, and the hematocrit falls over the following day. An early normal hematocrit therefore does not exclude severe acute blood loss."},{"type":"p","text":"Site can matter more than volume. A small hemorrhage inside the rigid skull raises intracranial pressure and can cause herniation. A modest but rapid collection of blood in the pericardium causes tamponade, because the stiff pericardial sac cannot stretch quickly. Retroperitoneal bleeding from a ruptured aortic aneurysm can hide liters of blood without external signs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C06","obj":"CV-18-H.6","title":"Edema: transudate, exudate, and lymphedema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Edema is excess fluid in the interstitium. Normally capillaries filter slightly more fluid than they reabsorb, and lymphatics return the excess (CV-11.5). Edema appears when filtration exceeds lymphatic capacity, which happens through four mechanisms: raised capillary hydrostatic pressure, lowered plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic obstruction. Renal sodium and water retention adds to the first two by expanding plasma volume. Which mechanism operates decides what the fluid contains."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of edema","cols":["Mechanism","Examples","Fluid"],"rows":[["Raised hydrostatic pressure","Heart failure, deep vein thrombosis or venous obstruction, portal hypertension (ascites), volume overload in renal failure","Transudate"],["Lowered plasma oncotic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome (albumin loss), cirrhosis (low synthesis), protein malnutrition (kwashiorkor), protein-losing enteropathy","Transudate"],["Sodium and water retention","Renal failure, activation of the renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone system in heart failure","Transudate"],["Increased permeability","Acute inflammation, burns, ARDS, anaphylaxis","Exudate"],["Lymphatic obstruction","Filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti), tumor, lymph node dissection or radiation, congenital lymphatic hypoplasia","Protein-rich lymph"]],"caption":"Transudates arise when vessel walls are intact; exudates when inflammation has opened them."},{"type":"p","text":"A transudate forms when the vessel wall is intact but the forces are out of balance, so water and small solutes cross while protein stays behind. It is clear, with low protein and a specific gravity below about 1.012. An exudate forms when inflammatory mediators such as histamine, bradykinin, and leukotrienes contract endothelial cells and open gaps between them, or when the endothelium is directly injured. Protein, fibrinogen, and white cells then leave the vessel, giving cloudy fluid with high protein and a specific gravity above about 1.020. For pleural fluid, Light criteria apply the same idea: a fluid-to-serum protein ratio above 0.5, a fluid-to-serum LDH ratio above 0.6, or fluid LDH above two-thirds of the upper limit of normal for serum marks an exudate."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Edema in heart failure","steps":["Cardiac output falls","Renal perfusion falls; renin rises","Angiotensin II and aldosterone retain sodium and water","Plasma volume and venous pressure rise","Capillary hydrostatic pressure rises; edema forms","Failing heart is further overloaded"],"caption":"Renal retention, meant to restore perfusion, raises venous pressure and worsens edema in a failing heart."},{"type":"p","text":"Ordinary edema pits because the excess fluid is free in the interstitium: firm pressure pushes it aside, and the dent fills slowly. It collects where hydrostatic pressure is highest (legs when standing, sacrum when lying), and in nephrotic syndrome it appears first in loose tissue such as the eyelids. Lymphedema begins soft but becomes nonpitting. The retained lymph is rich in protein, which stimulates fibroblasts and adipose deposition, so the skin and subcutaneous tissue thicken and harden; over years this produces elephantiasis, and in breast cancer blocked dermal lymphatics give the dimpled peau d'orange skin. Myxedema of hypothyroidism is also nonpitting, for a different reason: glycosaminoglycans bind water in the dermis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C07","obj":"CV-18-H.7","title":"Classification of shock and its hemodynamic profiles","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Shock is widespread tissue hypoperfusion. Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output times systemic vascular resistance (SVR), and cardiac output depends on venous return, so perfusion fails when the volume, the pump, the path through the heart, or vascular tone fails. The classification names the failing part. Three measurements identify it: cardiac output, SVR, and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP), which approximates left ventricular filling pressure. The key is to decide which change is the primary problem and which is the reflex response."},{"type":"table","title":"Hemodynamic profiles of shock","cols":["Type","Primary problem","Cardiac output","SVR","PCWP","Skin"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic","Loss of blood or fluid (hemorrhage, burns, vomiting, diarrhea)","Decreased","Increased (reflex)","Decreased","Cold, clammy"],["Cardiogenic","Pump failure (myocardial infarction, myocarditis, arrhythmia, acute valve rupture)","Decreased","Increased (reflex)","Increased","Cold, clammy"],["Obstructive","Block to filling or outflow (tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism, tension pneumothorax)","Decreased","Increased (reflex)","Increased in tamponade (equal diastolic pressures); normal or low in pulmonary embolism","Cold, clammy"],["Septic (distributive)","Vasodilation and capillary leak from the host response to infection","Increased or normal early; may fall late","Decreased","Decreased or normal","Warm, flushed early"],["Anaphylactic (distributive)","Mast cell and basophil release of histamine and other mediators, usually IgE-mediated","Usually decreased by vasodilation and leak, may be normal","Decreased","Decreased","Warm, flushed, urticaria, angioedema"],["Neurogenic (distributive)","Loss of sympathetic tone after spinal cord injury (usually above about T6)","Decreased","Decreased","Decreased","Warm, dry; bradycardia"]],"caption":"Low-output shock raises SVR as a reflex; distributive shock lowers SVR as the primary defect. Central venous pressure follows PCWP except in right-sided obstruction (pulmonary embolism), where it rises while PCWP stays normal or low."},{"type":"p","text":"In hypovolemic shock, reduced venous return lowers filling pressures and stroke volume; the baroreceptor reflex responds with tachycardia and intense arteriolar constriction, so SVR is high and the skin is cold and pale. Cardiogenic shock shares low output and high SVR, but the failing ventricle cannot empty, so blood backs up and PCWP rises, causing pulmonary edema. Obstructive shock also has low output with reflex vasoconstriction. In tamponade, fluid around the heart limits filling of all chambers, and diastolic pressures in the atria, ventricles, and PCWP rise and equalize. In massive pulmonary embolism, the right ventricle cannot push blood through the lungs, so central venous pressure rises while the left ventricle is underfilled and PCWP is normal or low."},{"type":"p","text":"Distributive shock begins in the vessels. Arterioles dilate and SVR falls, venous capacitance rises, and in sepsis and anaphylaxis capillaries leak. In septic shock with adequate volume, the low resistance and reflex tachycardia raise cardiac output (hyperdynamic or warm shock), yet tissues remain hypoxic because flow is maldistributed and mitochondrial oxygen use is impaired; mixed venous oxygen saturation is therefore normal or high, unlike the low value of low-output shock. Neurogenic shock differs in one sign: cut sympathetic pathways cannot raise heart rate, and unopposed vagal tone causes bradycardia with warm, dry skin."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a hemodynamic profile","text":"Typical normal values are cardiac output about 4 to 8 L/min, SVR about 800 to 1,200 dyn·s/cm5, and PCWP about 6 to 12 mmHg. Patient A: cardiac output 2.5 L/min, SVR 1,800, PCWP 26 mmHg. Output is low, so the high SVR is a reflex; the high wedge pressure means the left ventricle is full but not emptying. This is cardiogenic shock. Patient B: cardiac output 2.8 L/min, SVR 1,700, PCWP 4 mmHg. Output is low with reflex vasoconstriction, but the ventricle is empty, which points to hypovolemic shock. Patient C: cardiac output 8.5 L/min, SVR 450, PCWP 8 mmHg. Resistance is the primary defect and output has risen to compensate, which fits early septic shock."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-08-C08","obj":"CV-18-H.8","title":"Stages of shock, septic shock mediators, and multiorgan dysfunction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Shock passes through three stages. In the nonprogressive (compensated) stage, baroreceptor reflexes, catecholamines, the renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone system, and ADH raise heart rate, constrict arterioles in skin, gut, and kidney, and retain salt and water. Autoregulation keeps coronary and cerebral flow nearly normal, so the patient has tachycardia, cool pale skin (except in early sepsis), and reduced urine output, but blood pressure may be near normal."},{"type":"p","text":"In the progressive stage, perfusion is inadequate despite these reflexes. Cells shift to anaerobic glycolysis, and lactate accumulates, causing a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with serum lactate above the normal of about 2 mmol/L. Acidosis blunts arteriolar responses to sympathetic stimulation while venules stay constricted, so blood pools and leaks in capillary beds, and it depresses contractility. Patients become confused, oliguric, and hypotensive."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Positive feedback in progressive shock","steps":["Cardiac output and arterial pressure fall","Tissue hypoxia; lactic acidosis","Arterioles dilate; blood pools in capillaries","Capillary leak; venous return falls","Acidosis depresses the myocardium","Cardiac output falls further"],"caption":"Once each fall in flow causes a further fall, shock worsens even if the original insult stops."},{"type":"p","text":"In the irreversible stage, restoring hemodynamics no longer saves the patient. ATP is depleted, lysosomal enzymes leak from injured cells, the myocardium fails, and ischemic bowel lets bacteria and endotoxin enter the blood, adding sepsis to the original insult."},{"type":"h","text":"Septic shock mediators"},{"type":"p","text":"Septic shock is caused by the host response, not by bacteria directly. Lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) of gram-negative bacteria, whose lipid A is the toxic portion, binds LPS-binding protein, CD14, and TLR4 on macrophages, neutrophils, and endothelium; gram-positive wall components signal through TLR2, and superantigens activate T cells nonspecifically. NF-kappa B then drives release of TNF and IL-1, which induce IL-6 and chemokines, while microbial products activate complement to generate C3a and C5a. Inducible nitric oxide synthase in endothelium and smooth muscle produces large amounts of nitric oxide, which relaxes vascular smooth muscle through cGMP and lowers SVR. TNF and IL-1 also loosen endothelial junctions and depress myocardial contraction."},{"type":"flow","title":"From endotoxin to septic shock","steps":["Endotoxin binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages and endothelium","NF-kappa B activation; TNF and IL-1 released","Inducible NO synthase; nitric oxide relaxes vascular smooth muscle","Endothelial leak; tissue factor up, protein C activation down","Low SVR, hypovolemia, microthrombi, myocardial depression","Hypoperfusion and multiorgan dysfunction"],"caption":"Vasodilation, leak, and clotting arise from the same cytokine-activated endothelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytokines also push coagulation toward clotting: they induce tissue factor, reduce thrombomodulin so less protein C is activated, and raise plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, suppressing fibrinolysis. Microthrombi and consumption of platelets and factors lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)."},{"type":"table","title":"Multiorgan dysfunction after shock","cols":["Organ","Lesion","Mechanism and findings"],"rows":[["Kidney","Acute tubular necrosis","Proximal straight tubule and thick ascending limb in the outer medulla have high energy demand and little oxygen; oliguria, rising creatinine, muddy brown granular casts"],["Lung","ARDS (diffuse alveolar damage)","Neutrophil and cytokine injury to endothelium and type I pneumocytes; protein-rich edema, hyaline membranes, hypoxemia with normal left atrial pressure"],["Blood","DIC","Microthrombi consume platelets and factors: low platelets and fibrinogen, long PT and PTT, high D-dimer, schistocytes"],["Liver","Ischemic hepatitis (shock liver)","Centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis; very high aminotransferases"],["Gut, brain, heart","Mucosal necrosis; encephalopathy; subendocardial necrosis","Areas perfused last fail first; gut injury allows bacterial translocation"],["Adrenal","Hemorrhagic necrosis","Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage in meningococcal sepsis (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome)"]],"caption":"Each organ fails where its blood supply is weakest relative to its metabolic demand."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient in septic shock has a cardiac output of 9 L/min, yet her lactate keeps rising. How can tissues be hypoxic when flow is high?","explain":"Dilated, maldistributed beds and microthrombi divert flow from nutrient capillaries, leak widens diffusion distances, and cytokines and nitric oxide impair mitochondrial oxygen use, so oxygen reaches and is used by cells poorly.","check":"The distributive shock paragraph in section C07 and the mediators in this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These disorders form chains that cross organ systems. Anatomy decides consequences: end arteries make kidney and spleen infarcts pale and wedge-shaped, the dual supply of the lung makes pulmonary infarction uncommon, and a patent foramen ovale lets a leg vein thrombus cause a stroke. Biochemistry and genetics explain who clots: a single amino acid change in factor V removes a brake on thrombin, and vitamin K-dependent proteins with different half-lives explain warfarin skin necrosis. Physiology explains edema and shock through Starling forces and the relationship of pressure, output, and resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and microbiology link infection to circulatory failure through TLR4, TNF, IL-1, and nitric oxide, and the same activated endothelium causes vasodilation, leak, and DIC. Pathology then shows the end result in each organ. The categories overlap in practice: septic shock often has a hypovolemic component from leak and a cardiogenic component from myocardial depression, and hemodynamic profiles shift over time, so a single measurement must be read against the clinical course."}],"summary":["Virchow triad: endothelial injury, abnormal flow, and hypercoagulability; injury dominates in arteries, stasis in veins.","Arterial thrombi are pale and platelet-rich and grow back toward the heart; venous thrombi are red and grow toward the heart with flow; lines of Zahn mark a thrombus formed in life.","Factor V Leiden causes activated protein C resistance; antithrombin and protein C or S deficiencies remove brakes on thrombin; antiphospholipid antibodies prolong the PTT in vitro but cause arterial and venous thrombosis.","Venous emboli go to the lungs; mural thrombi from a left ventricular infarct or fibrillating left atrium embolize mostly to the legs and brain; venous emboli reach the systemic side only through a right-to-left shunt.","Fat embolism syndrome follows long-bone fracture by 1 to 3 days with hypoxemia, neurologic change, and petechiae; amniotic fluid embolism causes shock and DIC with fetal squames in maternal lung vessels.","White infarcts occur in solid end-arterial organs; red infarcts need blood entering dead tissue (dual supply, loose tissue, venous occlusion, reperfusion); necrosis is coagulative everywhere except the brain.","Petechiae and purpura point to platelet or small-vessel defects; hematomas and hemarthroses point to factor defects; acute loss above about 20% of blood volume causes hypovolemic shock.","Transudates come from hydrostatic or oncotic imbalance across an intact wall; exudates come from inflammatory leak; protein-rich lymph causes fibrosis and nonpitting lymphedema.","Low-output shock (hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive) has reflex high SVR; distributive shock has primary low SVR; PCWP is high in cardiogenic shock and tamponade and low in hypovolemic and distributive shock.","Shock progresses from compensated to progressive with lactic acidosis to irreversible; endotoxin, TNF, IL-1, and nitric oxide drive septic vasodilation, leak, and DIC, leading to ATN, ARDS, and multiorgan failure."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: capillary fluid exchange, interstitial fluid, and lymph flow; Circulatory Shock; Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The microcirculation; integrated control of the cardiovascular system (response to hemorrhage)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood disorders (hemostasis and thrombosis); cardiovascular disorders (shock)"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity: Toll-like receptors, inflammatory cytokines, and septic shock"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Pathogenesis of bacterial infection: endotoxins of gram-negative bacteria"}]},"CV-18-I":{"unit":"CV-18-I","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09","title":"Vascular Conditions","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers disease of the blood vessels themselves: hardening and atherosclerosis of arteries, the inherited lipid disorder that accelerates it, aneurysm and dissection of the aorta, chronic limb ischemia and cholesterol embolism, venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, venous insufficiency, vasospasm, and the vasculitides. Board questions usually give a patient's age, sex, exposures, and the pattern of vessels involved, then ask for the lesion, the mechanism, or the complication. Most of these questions can be solved from three ideas: which layer of the wall fails, what size of vessel is affected, and how flow, pressure, and wall tension change as a result.","prerequisites":["Layers of the arterial and venous wall (intima, media, adventitia) and vasa vasorum","Hemodynamics: pressure, flow, resistance, and the law of Laplace (CV-11)","Lipoprotein classes, the LDL receptor, and cholesterol homeostasis (CV-13)","Hemostasis, coagulation cascade, and fibrinolysis","Hypersensitivity types II, III, and IV and the role of neutrophils and T cells"],"connections":[{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Coronary atherosclerosis and plaque rupture are the substrate for angina and myocardial infarction."},{"to":"Hypertensive disease (CV-18-A)","how":"Raised pressure drives arteriolosclerosis, accelerates atherosclerosis, and is the main risk factor for aortic dissection."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"Pulmonary embolism produces hypoxemia through ventilation-perfusion mismatch, which the alveolar-arterial gradient detects."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"Arteriolosclerosis, atheroemboli, polyarteritis nodosa, ANCA vasculitis, and IgA vasculitis each injure the kidney in a distinct pattern."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disease","how":"Polymyalgia rheumatica, systemic sclerosis, lupus, Marfan syndrome, and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome all present through the vessels."}],"terms":[{"term":"Arteriosclerosis","def":"Thickening and loss of elasticity of arterial walls; the umbrella term for atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and medial calcific sclerosis."},{"term":"Atheroma","def":"An intimal plaque with a lipid-rich necrotic core covered by a fibrous cap of smooth muscle cells and collagen."},{"term":"Foam cell","def":"A macrophage or smooth muscle cell filled with lipid droplets after taking up modified LDL through scavenger receptors."},{"term":"Aneurysm","def":"A localized permanent dilation of a vessel or the heart involving all layers of the wall (true) or contained by adventitia or clot (false)."},{"term":"Dissection","def":"Entry of blood through an intimal tear into the media, splitting the wall and creating a false lumen."},{"term":"Ankle-brachial index (ABI)","def":"Ankle systolic pressure divided by brachial systolic pressure; normal about 1.00 to 1.40."},{"term":"Virchow triad","def":"The three conditions that favor thrombosis: stasis or turbulent flow, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability."},{"term":"Alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient","def":"The difference between calculated alveolar PO2 and measured arterial PO2; it widens when gas exchange is impaired."},{"term":"Vasculitis","def":"Inflammation of the vessel wall, classified by the size of vessel mainly affected and by immune mechanism."},{"term":"ANCA","def":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, mainly against proteinase 3 (PR3) or myeloperoxidase (MPO), that activate neutrophils against small vessels."},{"term":"Pauci-immune","def":"Describing vasculitis or glomerulonephritis with little or no immunoglobulin or complement deposited in the lesion, typical of ANCA disease."},{"term":"Palpable purpura","def":"Raised, non-blanching skin hemorrhages caused by inflammation of small dermal vessels rather than by low platelets."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C01","obj":"CV-18-I.1","title":"Three patterns of arteriosclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arteriosclerosis means hardening of the arteries: the wall thickens and loses elasticity. It takes three patterns that differ in the size of vessel, the layer affected, and whether the lumen narrows. Keeping them apart matters because only two of the three reduce flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Atherosclerosis is an intimal disease of elastic and large to medium muscular arteries, in which lipid, inflammatory cells, and fibrous tissue form plaques that bulge into the lumen (C02). Arteriolosclerosis affects small arteries and arterioles. In the hyaline form, plasma proteins leak across injured endothelium and smooth muscle cells lay down excess matrix, giving a homogeneous pink, glassy wall. It accompanies aging, long-standing benign hypertension, and diabetes; in the kidney it causes nephrosclerosis with a finely granular cortical surface, and in diabetes it affects both afferent and efferent arterioles. In the hyperplastic form, seen with severe (malignant) hypertension, smooth muscle cells and reduplicated basement membrane form concentric onion-skin layers, often with fibrinoid necrosis of the wall, and the narrowed lumen produces renal ischemia with pinpoint cortical hemorrhages."},{"type":"p","text":"Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis is calcification of the media of medium muscular arteries, typically the femoral, tibial, radial, and ulnar arteries, in people over about 50. The calcium is deposited in the media and internal elastic lamina, so the intima and lumen are spared and flow is not reduced. The calcified vessel appears as a pipestem on radiographs and is palpably hard. It matters diagnostically: a stiff artery resists cuff compression, so ankle pressure reads falsely high and the ankle-brachial index can exceed 1.40 (C06), which is common in diabetes and chronic kidney disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of arteriosclerosis","cols":["Pattern","Vessels","Lesion","Lumen","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Atherosclerosis","Aorta, large and medium muscular arteries","Intimal plaque: lipid core and fibrous cap","Narrowed; may thrombose","Smoking, high LDL, hypertension, diabetes, age"],["Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Arterioles, small arteries","Protein leakage and matrix: glassy pink wall","Narrowed","Aging, benign hypertension, diabetes"],["Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","Arterioles, small arteries","Onion-skin smooth muscle and basement membrane; fibrinoid necrosis","Markedly narrowed","Severe (malignant) hypertension"],["Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis","Medium muscular arteries of limbs","Calcification of media and internal elastic lamina","Not narrowed","Age over about 50; diabetes, chronic kidney disease"]],"caption":"Only atherosclerosis and arteriolosclerosis reduce the lumen; Monckeberg sclerosis stiffens the vessel without obstructing flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C02","obj":"CV-18-I.2","title":"Pathogenesis, distribution, and complications of atherosclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Atherosclerosis is best understood as a chronic inflammatory response of the arterial wall to endothelial injury. Endothelial dysfunction develops at branch points and curvatures, where flow is disturbed rather than laminar, and is promoted by hypertension, smoking, hyperlipidemia, hyperglycemia, and inflammation. The dysfunctional endothelium becomes more permeable and expresses adhesion molecules such as VCAM-1, so LDL and monocytes enter the intima."},{"type":"flow","title":"From endothelial injury to complicated plaque","steps":["Endothelial dysfunction at branch points and curvatures","LDL enters the intima and is oxidized","Monocytes adhere, migrate in, become macrophages","Scavenger receptor uptake of oxidized LDL: foam cells","Fatty streak: flat intimal collection of foam cells","PDGF and other growth factors recruit smooth muscle from the media","Fibrous cap of collagen over a necrotic lipid core","Rupture, erosion, hemorrhage, or calcification: complicated plaque"],"caption":"Scavenger receptors are not down-regulated by cell cholesterol, so macrophages keep taking up oxidized LDL until they become foam cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Fatty streaks are present in the aortas of virtually all children over about 10 and are not themselves obstructive. A mature atheroma has a fibrous cap of smooth muscle cells and collagen over a core of lipid, necrotic debris, foam cells, and cholesterol crystals. Plaques are patchy and favor, in descending order, the abdominal aorta (more than the thoracic), the coronary arteries, the popliteal arteries, the internal carotid arteries, and the circle of Willis. Major modifiable risks are smoking, hypertension, raised LDL, and diabetes; fixed risks are age, male sex, and family history."},{"type":"p","text":"The danger of a plaque depends more on its stability than its size. A vulnerable plaque has a thin cap, a large lipid core, and many inflammatory cells: macrophage metalloproteinases digest collagen and T cell interferon gamma suppresses smooth muscle collagen synthesis. When the cap ruptures, exposed tissue factor and collagen trigger thrombosis, which is why many myocardial infarctions arise from plaques that were not severely stenotic. A stable plaque narrows the lumen gradually; once a stenosis reaches about 70%, flow can no longer rise to meet demand and ischemia appears with exertion."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of atherosclerosis","cols":["Complication","Mechanism","Example"],"rows":[["Stenosis","Slow plaque growth limits flow reserve","Stable angina, claudication, renal artery stenosis"],["Rupture or erosion with thrombosis","Cap disruption exposes tissue factor and collagen","Myocardial infarction, ischemic stroke"],["Embolism","Plaque debris or thrombus breaks off downstream","Atheroemboli to kidney and toes; carotid emboli to brain"],["Aneurysm","Intimal plaque starves and weakens the underlying media","Infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm"],["Hemorrhage into plaque","Rupture of plaque microvessels expands it abruptly","Sudden worsening of stenosis"]],"caption":"Thrombosis on a disrupted plaque causes most acute events; stenosis causes chronic demand ischemia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 58-year-old with a 40% coronary stenosis on a recent angiogram suffers an acute myocardial infarction in that artery. How can a modest stenosis cause an infarct?","explain":"The event depends on plaque stability, not size: a lipid-rich plaque with a thin, inflamed cap can rupture and expose thrombogenic contents, and the overlying thrombus occludes the vessel.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on vulnerable plaque and the complications table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C03","obj":"CV-18-I.3","title":"Familial hypercholesterolemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatic LDL receptors bind apolipoprotein B-100 on LDL and remove it from plasma by clathrin-mediated endocytosis. The cholesterol released in the lysosome keeps SREBP-2 inactive in the endoplasmic reticulum, which lowers the synthesis of HMG-CoA reductase and of new LDL receptors. Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is an autosomal dominant failure of this clearance, most often from loss-of-function mutations in the LDL receptor gene (LDLR), less often in APOB (receptor binding site) or from gain-of-function mutations in PCSK9, a protein that steers the receptor to lysosomal degradation."},{"type":"p","text":"Because clearance falls while the liver, sensing less cholesterol, keeps synthesizing it, plasma LDL rises in proportion to the receptor defect. Heterozygotes, about 1 in 250 people, have roughly half the normal receptor activity and LDL often above 190 mg/dL from childhood; coronary disease commonly appears in the fourth to fifth decade in men and somewhat later in women. Homozygotes have little or no receptor activity, LDL often above 500 mg/dL, and coronary disease or myocardial infarction in childhood or the teens. Triglycerides are usually normal, which separates FH from combined hyperlipidemias."},{"type":"p","text":"The physical findings are deposits of LDL-derived cholesterol taken up by macrophages. Tendon xanthomas are firm nodules in the Achilles tendon and the extensor tendons of the hands and are highly suggestive of FH. Xanthelasma (yellow plaques on the eyelids) and corneal arcus (a gray-white ring at the corneal margin) are less specific because both occur in older adults with normal lipids; arcus before about age 45 suggests FH. Homozygotes may also have planar cutaneous xanthomas and lipid deposits in the aortic root and valve. The same receptor-macrophage process in the arterial intima drives premature atherosclerosis (C02)."},{"type":"table","title":"Heterozygous versus homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","cols":["Feature","Heterozygous","Homozygous"],"rows":[["Frequency","About 1 in 250","Rare, about 1 in several hundred thousand"],["LDL receptor activity","About half of normal","Little or none"],["LDL cholesterol","Often above 190 mg/dL","Often above 500 mg/dL"],["Coronary disease","Premature, often fourth to fifth decade","Childhood or teens"],["Skin and eye","Tendon xanthomas, xanthelasma, early arcus","Same plus planar cutaneous xanthomas in childhood"]],"caption":"Gene dosage sets the LDL level and the age at which atherosclerosis becomes clinical."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C04","obj":"CV-18-I.4","title":"Aneurysms: abdominal, thoracic, berry, and mycotic","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An aneurysm forms when the media can no longer bear wall stress, because its elastin, collagen, or smooth muscle has been lost or weakened. A true aneurysm involves all three layers; a false aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm) is a leak contained by adventitia or clot, often at an arterial puncture or graft anastomosis. Once a segment dilates, the law of Laplace makes it worse: wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so a wider, thinner segment carries more stress and enlarges faster."},{"type":"p","text":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA), a dilation to 3 cm or more, is the most common aortic aneurysm. It is associated with atherosclerosis and is typical of men over 60 who smoke, with hypertension and family history as added risks. It usually lies below the renal arteries, where the media has few vasa vasorum and a thick plaque impairs diffusion of nutrients to it, while inflammation and matrix metalloproteinases degrade the matrix. Most are silent and found as a pulsatile abdominal mass or on imaging. Rupture risk rises steeply with diameter, from very low below 4 cm to high above about 5.5 cm, and presents with abdominal or back pain, hypotension, and a pulsatile mass."},{"type":"p","text":"Thoracic aortic aneurysms usually reflect cystic medial degeneration (loss of elastic fibers and smooth muscle with pools of ground substance) from hypertension or connective tissue disorders such as Marfan syndrome. Tertiary syphilis causes obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta, giving medial ischemia, a wrinkled tree-bark intima, and dilation of the aortic root with aortic regurgitation. Both can compress mediastinal structures, causing cough, dysphagia, or hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve stretch."},{"type":"table","title":"Aneurysm types","cols":["Type","Site","Mechanism or association","Key consequence"],"rows":[["Abdominal aortic","Infrarenal aorta","Atherosclerosis; older male smokers","Rupture; risk rises with diameter"],["Thoracic aortic","Ascending aorta and arch","Cystic medial degeneration (hypertension, Marfan); tertiary syphilis","Aortic root dilation and regurgitation; compression; dissection"],["Berry (saccular)","Circle of Willis, mostly anterior circulation branch points","Thin or absent media at branch points; ADPKD, Ehlers-Danlos, hypertension, smoking","Subarachnoid hemorrhage"],["Mycotic (infected)","Any artery, often aorta or cerebral branches","Septic emboli from infective endocarditis; bacteremic seeding of plaque (for example Salmonella)","Rapid growth and rupture"]],"caption":"Each aneurysm type points to a different process that weakens the media."},{"type":"example","title":"Why large aneurysms rupture faster","text":"Wall stress is about pressure times radius divided by wall thickness. Compare a normal infrarenal aorta of 2 cm diameter (radius 1 cm) with a 6 cm aneurysm (radius 3 cm) at the same blood pressure. Radius has tripled, so stress has tripled even before the wall thins. As the aneurysm stretches, its wall becomes thinner, which raises stress further. Higher stress speeds degradation of the remaining matrix, which allows more dilation. This positive feedback explains why expansion accelerates as diameter grows and why rupture risk climbs steeply above about 5.5 cm. Hypertension raises the pressure term and adds to every step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C05","obj":"CV-18-I.5","title":"Aortic dissection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In aortic dissection, blood enters the media through an intimal tear and splits the wall along its length, creating a false lumen. The underlying problem is a weakened media. Hypertension, present in most patients, causes medial degeneration and raises the shear and wall stress that drive the split. Younger patients usually have a connective tissue defect: cystic medial degeneration in Marfan syndrome (defective fibrillin-1 and excess TGF-beta signaling), Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, bicuspid aortic valve with its associated aortopathy, or Turner syndrome (often with bicuspid valve and coarctation). Abrupt surges in pressure, as with cocaine or the hemodynamic load of pregnancy, can also trigger it. Heavy atherosclerosis or medial scarring tends to stop a dissection from spreading, so dissection is not a typical complication of atherosclerosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Consequences of a proximal dissection","steps":["Weakened media plus high wall stress","Intimal tear, usually in the ascending aorta","Blood splits the media: false lumen","Proximal spread: aortic root, valve support lost, regurgitation","Rupture into pericardium: hemopericardium and tamponade","Branch compression: coronary, carotid, subclavian, renal, mesenteric ischemia","External rupture: hemothorax or exsanguination"],"caption":"Outward rupture, into the pericardium, pleural cavity, or peritoneum, is the usual cause of death."},{"type":"p","text":"The pain is sudden, severe, and tearing or ripping, felt in the anterior chest and radiating to the back between the scapulae as the dissection advances. If the false lumen narrows a subclavian artery, the pulse is weak and the blood pressure is lower in that arm, giving unequal arm pressures. The chest radiograph may show a widened mediastinum. A new diastolic murmur indicates aortic regurgitation from root dilation; hypotension with distended neck veins suggests tamponade; neurologic deficits, paraplegia, oliguria, or abdominal pain indicate branch occlusion."},{"type":"table","title":"Stanford classification","cols":["Type","Extent","Typical patient","Risk"],"rows":[["Stanford A (proximal)","Involves the ascending aorta, with or without the descending aorta","Hypertension; younger patients with Marfan or bicuspid valve","Highest: tamponade, aortic regurgitation, coronary occlusion"],["Stanford B (distal)","Does not involve the ascending aorta; usually begins beyond the left subclavian artery","Older patients with hypertension","Lower; complications mainly branch ischemia and rupture"]],"caption":"The ascending aorta decides the type because its involvement threatens the valve, pericardium, and coronary arteries."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 32-year-old tall man with long fingers develops tearing chest pain, then hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds. What has happened?","explain":"A Stanford A dissection in Marfan syndrome has ruptured back into the pericardium, causing tamponade that limits ventricular filling.","check":"Compare with the dissection flow and the Stanford table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C06","obj":"CV-18-I.6","title":"Peripheral arterial disease and atheroembolic disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) is atherosclerotic narrowing of the arteries to the legs, most strongly linked to smoking and diabetes. Its symptoms follow from flow reserve. At rest, a stenosed artery can still deliver enough blood, but during walking the calf arterioles dilate and demand rises while the fixed stenosis prevents flow from increasing. The result is intermittent claudication: cramping pain in the muscle group below the stenosis, reproduced by a set distance of walking and relieved within minutes by rest. Superficial femoral disease causes calf pain; aortoiliac disease causes buttock and thigh pain."},{"type":"p","text":"As stenosis worsens, resting flow becomes inadequate. Rest pain in the forefoot, typically at night and eased by hanging the foot down (gravity raises perfusion pressure), signals critical ischemia, followed by nonhealing ulcers on the toes and pressure points and gangrene. Chronic ischemia also produces weak or absent pulses, cool skin, hair loss, thin shiny skin, thickened nails, and dependent rubor with pallor on elevation. Leriche syndrome is occlusion at the aortic bifurcation and causes buttock and thigh claudication, absent or diminished femoral pulses, and erectile dysfunction from reduced internal iliac flow."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting the ankle-brachial index","cols":["ABI","Meaning"],"rows":[["Above 1.40","Noncompressible calcified arteries (Monckeberg, diabetes, chronic kidney disease); result unreliable"],["1.00 to 1.40","Normal"],["0.91 to 0.99","Borderline"],["0.90 or lower","PAD: pressure is lost across stenoses"],["Below about 0.40","Severe disease, often with rest pain or tissue loss"]],"caption":"Normally ankle pressure equals or slightly exceeds arm pressure because of pulse wave amplification; a lower ankle pressure means flow has crossed a resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"Atheroembolic (cholesterol embolization) disease occurs when an ulcerated plaque in the aorta or a large artery showers cholesterol crystals into small distal arteries. It is classically triggered by arterial catheterization, angiography, or vascular surgery that scrapes a plaque, and occasionally by anticoagulation or thrombolysis. Crystals lodge in arterioles of about 100 to 200 micrometers and incite inflammation, so the patient has blue or purple toes and livedo reticularis with intact pedal pulses, because the large arteries remain open. Kidney involvement causes progressive renal failure, and blood tests may show eosinophilia and low complement. Biopsy shows biconvex, needle-shaped clefts in arterioles where cholesterol crystals were dissolved during tissue processing."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C07","obj":"CV-18-I.7","title":"Pulmonary embolism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"More than 95% of pulmonary emboli arise from thrombi in the deep veins of the leg, especially the proximal veins (popliteal, femoral, and iliac), which are large enough to release sizable clots. A detached thrombus passes through the right heart into the pulmonary arteries. Its effects depend on size and on the patient's cardiopulmonary reserve: a large saddle embolus at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery can cause sudden death, while small emboli may be silent."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pulmonary arterial obstruction to signs","steps":["Embolus obstructs pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary vascular resistance rises abruptly","Right ventricle dilates and fails: acute cor pulmonale","Blood diverted to open lung units: low V/Q areas","Hypoxemia with widened A-a gradient","Hypoxemia and receptor stimulation drive hyperventilation","Low PaCO2: respiratory alkalosis"],"caption":"Obstruction of roughly 60% or more of the pulmonary circulation can cause shock or sudden death."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic presentation is sudden dyspnea with tachypnea and tachycardia; pleuritic chest pain and hemoptysis suggest a small peripheral embolus that has irritated the pleura or caused infarction. Hypoxemia arises mainly from ventilation-perfusion mismatch: obstructed regions are ventilated but not perfused (dead space), while their blood is redirected to open regions that become overperfused relative to ventilation; local loss of surfactant and atelectasis add shunt. Because alveolar gas is normal or even high in oxygen while arterial PO2 falls, the A-a gradient widens, which separates PE from hypoventilation. Acute right ventricular strain shows as a dilated right ventricle, raised jugular venous pressure, sinus tachycardia (the most common ECG finding), sometimes a new right bundle branch block or an S1Q3T3 pattern, and elevated troponin or BNP. D-dimer, a fibrin degradation product, is sensitive but not specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Only about 10% of emboli cause pulmonary infarction. The lung has a dual supply: bronchial arteries from the systemic circulation continue to perfuse the tissue distal to the block, and alveolar oxygen diffuses into the tissue directly. Infarction occurs mainly when bronchial flow is already compromised, as in left heart failure. When it occurs, the infarct is hemorrhagic, wedge-shaped, and based on the pleura. Repeated small emboli over time can produce chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the A-a gradient","text":"A 34-year-old woman on estrogen breathes room air at sea level. PaO2 is 65 mm Hg and PaCO2 is 30 mm Hg. Alveolar PO2 equals 0.21 times (760 minus 47) minus PaCO2 divided by 0.8. That is about 150 minus 37.5, or about 112 mm Hg. The A-a gradient is 112 minus 65, about 47 mm Hg, far above the normal of roughly 5 to 15 mm Hg in a young adult. The low PaCO2 shows she is hyperventilating, so pure hypoventilation cannot explain her hypoxemia. A widened gradient points to a gas exchange defect, here ventilation-perfusion mismatch from pulmonary embolism."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a moderate embolus to a lobar artery usually leave the lung tissue alive in a healthy young patient but cause a wedge-shaped infarct in a patient with left heart failure?","explain":"In the healthy patient bronchial arterial flow and alveolar oxygen sustain the tissue; in left heart failure bronchial flow is reduced and pulmonary venous pressure is high, so the dual supply fails.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on why most emboli do not cause infarction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C08","obj":"CV-18-I.8","title":"Deep vein thrombosis and superficial thrombophlebitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Venous thrombi form in low-flow, low-pressure vessels and are rich in red cells and fibrin (red thrombi), unlike platelet-rich arterial thrombi. They usually begin in the valve pockets of deep calf veins, where flow is slowest, and can propagate proximally. Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is explained by Virchow triad, and most patients have more than one element."},{"type":"table","title":"Virchow triad and deep vein thrombosis risk","cols":["Element","Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Stasis","Slow flow lets activated factors accumulate and keeps platelets near the endothelium","Immobility, long travel, paralysis, heart failure, compression by the pregnant uterus"],["Endothelial injury","Loss of anticoagulant surface and exposure of tissue factor","Surgery and trauma, especially hip and knee; central venous catheters; prior DVT"],["Hypercoagulability","Excess procoagulant or loss of natural anticoagulant","Factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, antiphospholipid antibodies, malignancy, estrogen and pregnancy, nephrotic syndrome (urinary antithrombin loss)"]],"caption":"Major surgery and cancer are high-risk because they combine all three elements."},{"type":"p","text":"A DVT obstructs venous outflow, raising capillary hydrostatic pressure, while inflammation of the vein wall adds pain and warmth. The typical finding is one swollen, warm, tender leg, sometimes with dilated superficial veins and a calf circumference larger than the other side. Many DVTs are silent because collateral veins drain the limb, which is why pulmonary embolism may be the first sign. Calf pain on dorsiflexion (Homans sign) is unreliable. Proximal thrombi carry the greatest embolic risk (C07). On ultrasound the thrombosed vein cannot be compressed because clot fills its lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"Superficial thrombophlebitis is thrombosis with inflammation in a superficial vein, often a varicose vein or a vein used for an intravenous catheter. It produces a tender, red, palpable cord along the vein, with little limb swelling because deep drainage is intact, and it rarely embolizes unless it extends into the deep system. Migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau sign of malignancy) is recurrent superficial and deep venous thrombosis appearing and resolving at different sites. It occurs with adenocarcinomas, classically pancreatic and also lung, gastric, and colon, which release tissue factor and mucin that activate coagulation. This Trousseau sign must not be confused with the Trousseau sign of hypocalcemia (carpal spasm under a blood pressure cuff)."},{"type":"table","title":"Deep vein thrombosis versus superficial thrombophlebitis","cols":["Feature","Deep vein thrombosis","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"rows":[["Site","Deep calf, popliteal, femoral, iliac veins","Superficial veins, often varicose or cannulated"],["Findings","Diffuse unilateral swelling, warmth, tenderness; may be silent","Red, tender, palpable cord; little swelling"],["Embolic risk","Significant, highest with proximal clot","Low unless clot extends into deep veins"]],"caption":"The deep veins drain most of the leg, so their occlusion causes diffuse edema and embolic risk."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C09","obj":"CV-18-I.9","title":"Varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Leg veins return blood against gravity using the calf muscle pump and one-way valves. During walking, calf contraction squeezes the deep veins, and competent valves let blood move only upward and from superficial to deep through perforating veins. This keeps standing venous pressure at the ankle low during movement. When valves fail, the column of blood is no longer interrupted, and pressure in the superficial veins stays high even with walking (ambulatory venous hypertension)."},{"type":"p","text":"Varicose veins are dilated, tortuous superficial veins, most often in the great saphenous system, produced by raised intraluminal pressure and weakness of the vein wall that make the valves incompetent. Risk rises with age, female sex, pregnancy (compression of the inferior vena cava by the uterus plus hormonal relaxation of the vein wall), obesity, prolonged standing, and family history. Prior DVT that damages deep valves causes secondary venous insufficiency. Superficial varicose veins rarely cause embolism. Varicosities can form elsewhere when pressure rises, such as esophageal varices and hemorrhoids in portal hypertension."},{"type":"flow","title":"From incompetent valves to stasis ulcer","steps":["Valve incompetence in superficial, perforating, or deep veins","Sustained venous hypertension in the lower leg","Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure: edema","Red cells leak; hemosiderin stains skin brown","Leukocyte trapping and pericapillary fibrin impair tissue oxygenation","Stasis dermatitis, fibrosis (lipodermatosclerosis)","Minor trauma fails to heal: venous ulcer"],"caption":"Chronic venous hypertension injures skin through edema, inflammation, and poor oxygen delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic venous insufficiency therefore produces edema that worsens through the day and improves with elevation, aching heaviness, brown pigmentation, itchy eczematous stasis dermatitis, and indurated skin around the lower leg. Ulcers form most often just above the medial malleolus, where pressure from incompetent perforators and the great saphenous system is highest. They are shallow, irregular, and moist, unlike arterial ulcers."},{"type":"table","title":"Venous versus arterial ulcers","cols":["Feature","Venous ulcer","Arterial ulcer"],"rows":[["Location","Above the medial malleolus (gaiter area)","Toes, heel, pressure points, distal foot"],["Appearance","Shallow, irregular, moist, granulating base","Punched out, dry, pale base"],["Pain","Mild to moderate, eased by elevation","Severe, worse with elevation"],["Surrounding skin","Edema, brown pigment, stasis dermatitis","Cool, shiny, hairless; weak pulses"]],"caption":"Elevation helps venous ulcers by lowering venous pressure but worsens arterial ischemia by lowering perfusion pressure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C10","obj":"CV-18-I.10","title":"Raynaud phenomenon, primary and secondary","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Raynaud phenomenon is episodic, reversible vasospasm of the small arteries and arterioles of the fingers and toes, and sometimes the nose and ears, triggered by cold or emotional stress. Digital vessels normally constrict to cold through sympathetic alpha-adrenergic signaling, mainly alpha-2 receptors, to conserve core heat. In Raynaud phenomenon this response is exaggerated and the lumen closes completely."},{"type":"flow","title":"Color sequence of a Raynaud attack","steps":["Cold or stress: intense digital artery spasm","White: pallor from absent arterial inflow","Blue: cyanosis as stagnant blood is deoxygenated","Spasm relaxes on rewarming","Red: reactive hyperemia with throbbing and tingling"],"caption":"The colors trace inflow stopping, oxygen being extracted from trapped blood, and inflow returning above baseline."},{"type":"p","text":"The red phase is reactive hyperemia: during ischemia, local metabolites such as adenosine, CO2, H+, and K+ accumulate and dilate the arterioles, so when the spasm lifts, flow rebounds well above normal. Not every attack shows all three colors, and pallor is the most specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon (Raynaud disease) is a functional disorder of vascular reactivity with no underlying disease, most common in young women, usually symmetric, and without tissue damage. Secondary Raynaud phenomenon results from structural or inflammatory disease of the vessels, so the lumen is already narrowed and spasm can cause ischemic injury. It is nearly universal in systemic sclerosis, where intimal proliferation narrows digital arteries, and occurs with lupus, thromboangiitis obliterans (C11), atherosclerosis, cryoglobulinemia, and repetitive use of vibrating tools. Signs that suggest secondary disease are onset after about 30 to 40, asymmetric attacks, digital pits or ulcers, abnormal dilated or dropped-out nailfold capillaries, and positive autoantibodies."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary versus secondary Raynaud phenomenon","cols":["Feature","Primary","Secondary"],"rows":[["Underlying disease","None; exaggerated vasomotor response","Systemic sclerosis, lupus, Buerger disease, others"],["Typical patient","Young woman, onset in teens to twenties","Older onset, features of the underlying disease"],["Distribution","Symmetric","Often asymmetric"],["Tissue damage","None","Digital ulcers, pits, gangrene possible"],["Nailfold capillaries and serology","Normal; ANA usually negative","Abnormal capillaries; autoantibodies often positive"]],"caption":"Tissue damage implies a structurally narrowed vessel, which points to secondary disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C11","obj":"CV-18-I.11","title":"Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thromboangiitis obliterans is a segmental, thrombosing, acute and chronic inflammation of medium and small arteries, mainly the tibial and radial arteries and their digital branches, that spreads secondarily to adjacent veins and nerves. It occurs almost exclusively in heavy smokers and classically begins before about age 35 to 40, more often in men. Tobacco exposure is central to the disease: the mechanism is thought to involve hypersensitivity or direct endothelial toxicity from tobacco products, and the disease rarely progresses once all tobacco use stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopically, the involved segment shows a luminal thrombus containing microabscesses, small collections of neutrophils surrounded by granulomatous inflammation, with the internal elastic lamina relatively preserved. Over time the thrombus organizes and recanalizes, and the artery, vein, and nerve become bound together in fibrous tissue. Lesions are sharply segmental, with normal vessel between them, which separates the disease from the diffuse plaques of atherosclerosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical features follow from distal small-vessel occlusion with nerve involvement. Early signs include cold sensitivity and Raynaud phenomenon, and nodular superficial phlebitis that migrates along veins. Claudication tends to involve the instep of the foot or the forearm and hand, because the disease is distal. Inflamed nerves add intense rest pain out of proportion to the visible lesion. Progressive occlusion leads to painful digital ulcers and gangrene, often needing amputation if smoking continues. Peripheral pulses at the wrist and ankle may be absent while proximal pulses are normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Buerger disease versus atherosclerotic PAD","cols":["Feature","Thromboangiitis obliterans","Atherosclerotic PAD"],"rows":[["Age","Usually under about 40","Usually over 50"],["Vessels","Small and medium distal arteries of arms and legs; adjacent veins and nerves","Large and medium arteries of legs"],["Lesion","Segmental inflammatory thrombosis with microabscesses","Lipid plaque with fibrous cap"],["Distinct features","Migratory superficial phlebitis, Raynaud, upper limb involvement, severe neural pain","Calf or buttock claudication; risk factors beyond smoking"]],"caption":"Distal arm and leg involvement in a young smoker with phlebitis suggests Buerger disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C12","obj":"CV-18-I.12","title":"Giant cell arteritis and polymyalgia rheumatica","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vasculitis is inflammation of the vessel wall. The Chapel Hill classification groups the vasculitides by the size of vessel mainly affected, which predicts the clinical picture: large-vessel disease narrows the aorta and its branches and causes limb or organ ischemia; medium-vessel disease causes infarcts and aneurysms in organs; small-vessel disease involves capillaries and venules, producing palpable purpura, glomerulonephritis, and alveolar hemorrhage."},{"type":"table","title":"Vasculitis by vessel size","cols":["Vessel size","Disease","Main mechanism","Key association"],"rows":[["Large","Giant cell arteritis","T cell granulomatous","Age over 50; polymyalgia rheumatica"],["Large","Takayasu arteritis","T cell granulomatous","Women under 40 to 50; Asian"],["Medium","Polyarteritis nodosa","Immune complex in some cases","Hepatitis B; ANCA negative"],["Medium","Kawasaki disease","T cell and antibody response to unknown trigger","Children under 5; coronary aneurysm"],["Small, ANCA","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","PR3-ANCA, granulomas","Sinus, lung, kidney"],["Small, ANCA","Microscopic polyangiitis","MPO-ANCA, no granulomas","Lung and kidney"],["Small, ANCA","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Eosinophils; MPO-ANCA in a minority","Asthma, eosinophilia"],["Small, immune complex","IgA vasculitis","IgA1 immune complexes","Children after upper respiratory infection"],["Variable","Behcet syndrome","Neutrophilic inflammation","Oral and genital ulcers, uveitis; HLA-B51"]],"caption":"Vessel size and immune mechanism together predict the organs involved and the laboratory findings."},{"type":"p","text":"Giant cell arteritis (GCA), also called temporal arteritis, is the most common vasculitis in older adults. It almost never begins before age 50, affects women more often, and is most frequent in people of Northern European ancestry. It is a T cell mediated granulomatous inflammation of large and medium arteries, mainly the branches of the external carotid artery (including the superficial temporal artery), the ophthalmic and posterior ciliary arteries, the vertebral arteries, and the aorta. Activated T cells and macrophages release cytokines including IL-6, which drives the acute-phase response."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation narrows the lumen, so symptoms are ischemic: new headache with a tender, thickened temporal artery; jaw claudication (pain on chewing from masseter ischemia); and visual loss from occlusion of the ophthalmic or posterior ciliary arteries, which causes anterior ischemic optic neuropathy and can produce sudden, permanent blindness. The erythrocyte sedimentation rate is markedly raised, often above 50 mm/h, with high CRP. Biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation of the inner media with multinucleated giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina, but lesions are segmental (skip lesions), so a short or unlucky biopsy can be negative. Aortic involvement can later cause thoracic aneurysm."},{"type":"p","text":"Polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR) is an inflammatory disorder of the same age group causing pain and morning stiffness of the shoulder and hip girdles and neck, with a high ESR but normal creatine kinase and normal strength, because the target is periarticular synovium and bursae rather than muscle. The two conditions overlap: about half of patients with GCA have PMR symptoms, and a smaller fraction of patients with PMR develop GCA."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C13","obj":"CV-18-I.13","title":"Takayasu arteritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Takayasu arteritis is a granulomatous vasculitis of large arteries, principally the aortic arch and its branches, and sometimes the descending and abdominal aorta, the renal arteries, and the pulmonary arteries. It most often affects women under about 40 to 50 and is more common in Asia. The histology resembles giant cell arteritis: mononuclear infiltrates, granulomas with giant cells, and destruction of the media. What separates the two is mainly age, so a granulomatous large-vessel arteritis in a young woman is Takayasu, and in a patient over 50 it is GCA."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic inflammation is followed by fibrosis of the media and adventitia and thickening of the intima, giving an irregularly thickened aortic wall and stenosis or occlusion of the origins of the great vessels. The disease often begins with nonspecific constitutional symptoms (fever, fatigue, weight loss, arthralgia, raised ESR) before ischemic signs appear. Because the subclavian arteries are narrowed at their origins, pulses in one or both arms become weak or absent, giving the older name pulseless disease. Blood pressure measured in the affected arm is lower, so the two arms differ, and the patient may have arm claudication with use. Bruits are heard over the subclavian and carotid arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"Other findings follow the vessels involved. Carotid and vertebral narrowing causes dizziness, syncope, visual disturbances, and stroke. Renal artery stenosis activates the renin-angiotensin system and causes hypertension, which can be masked when it is measured in a narrowed arm. Involvement of the aortic root can dilate it and cause aortic regurgitation, and pulmonary artery involvement can cause pulmonary hypertension. Weak arm pulses with unequal pressures in a young woman should raise Takayasu arteritis, while the same finding with sudden tearing pain suggests aortic dissection (C05)."},{"type":"table","title":"Giant cell arteritis versus Takayasu arteritis","cols":["Feature","Giant cell arteritis","Takayasu arteritis"],"rows":[["Age","Over 50","Usually under 40 to 50"],["Typical patient","Older woman, Northern European ancestry","Young woman, often Asian"],["Main vessels","Carotid branches (temporal, ophthalmic), aorta","Aortic arch and its branches"],["Key manifestations","Headache, jaw claudication, vision loss, PMR","Weak arm pulses, unequal arm pressures, arm claudication, bruits"],["Histology","Granulomatous with giant cells","Granulomatous with giant cells, then fibrosis"]],"caption":"Both are granulomatous large-vessel vasculitides; age and the vessels involved separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C14","obj":"CV-18-I.14","title":"Polyarteritis nodosa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is a systemic necrotizing vasculitis of small and medium muscular arteries, typically renal and visceral arteries, that spares the pulmonary arteries and does not involve arterioles, capillaries, or venules. Because the smallest vessels are spared, PAN does not cause glomerulonephritis. It most often affects young to middle-aged adults. A substantial minority of patients, historically about a third, have chronic hepatitis B infection, and in them immune complexes containing hepatitis B surface antigen deposit in the vessel wall (a type III mechanism). ANCA is characteristically negative."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion is segmental transmural inflammation with fibrinoid necrosis of the wall, initially rich in neutrophils, eosinophils, and mononuclear cells. Necrosis weakens the wall, producing small aneurysms that give involved arteries a beaded or nodular appearance (the source of the name), and it promotes thrombosis and infarction. A hallmark is that acute lesions and healed, fibrotic lesions coexist in the same patient and even the same artery, showing that injury occurs in waves. Angiography of the renal or mesenteric arteries shows multiple microaneurysms and stenoses."},{"type":"p","text":"Manifestations reflect ischemia and infarction of the organs supplied. Renal artery involvement causes renal infarcts and renin-mediated hypertension, a leading cause of death. Mesenteric arteritis produces abdominal pain worse after meals, bleeding, and bowel infarction or perforation. Inflammation of the vasa nervorum produces mononeuritis multiplex, such as foot drop. Skin findings include painful nodules, livedo reticularis, and ulcers, and testicular pain can occur. Constitutional symptoms are common: fever, malaise, and weight loss."},{"type":"table","title":"Polyarteritis nodosa features explained","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypertension, renal infarcts","Arteritis of renal and intrarenal arteries reduces glomerular perfusion and activates renin"],["Postprandial abdominal pain, melena","Mesenteric arteritis with ischemia that worsens with digestive demand"],["Mononeuritis multiplex","Infarction of nerves through vasa nervorum involvement"],["No lung involvement, no glomerulonephritis","Pulmonary arteries and small vessels spared"],["Microaneurysms on angiography","Fibrinoid necrosis weakens the wall at multiple points"]],"caption":"PAN is a medium-vessel disease, so it produces infarcts and aneurysms rather than capillary inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C15","obj":"CV-18-I.15","title":"Kawasaki disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Kawasaki disease (mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome) is an acute, self-limited vasculitis of medium arteries that mainly affects children under 5 and is most common in children of East Asian, especially Japanese, ancestry. It is the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. The trigger is unknown; the leading view is that an infectious agent provokes an exaggerated T cell and antibody response in genetically susceptible children, with activated macrophages and cytokines damaging the vessel wall. The arteritis is transmural with mixed inflammatory cells, and fibrinoid necrosis is usually less prominent than in PAN."},{"type":"p","text":"The diagnosis is clinical. A child has fever lasting at least 5 days plus most of the five principal features listed below. The mucocutaneous findings reflect widespread inflammation of small and medium vessels and are temporary; peeling of the fingers and toes typically follows in the second to third week. Irritability is marked, and laboratory tests show raised ESR and CRP, leukocytosis, and, after the first week, thrombocytosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Principal features of Kawasaki disease","cols":["Feature","Description"],"rows":[["Fever","High fever for 5 days or more"],["Eyes","Bilateral nonexudative conjunctival injection, sparing the limbus"],["Lips and mouth","Red, cracked lips; strawberry tongue; diffuse oral redness"],["Rash","Polymorphous rash, often on trunk and perineum"],["Extremities","Red, swollen hands and feet; later periungual desquamation"],["Lymph nodes","Cervical lymphadenopathy, usually unilateral, at least 1.5 cm"]],"caption":"Fever plus at least four of the five other features supports classic Kawasaki disease."},{"type":"p","text":"The key complication is coronary artery aneurysm, which develops in roughly a quarter of untreated children. Inflammation destroys the internal elastic lamina and media of the coronary arteries, which then dilate. Inside an aneurysm, flow is slow and turbulent, and the high platelet count adds to the tendency to thrombose, so coronary thrombosis can cause myocardial infarction or sudden death in a young child; aneurysms can also rupture, and healing can leave stenosis. Myocarditis, pericarditis, and valvular regurgitation can occur in the acute phase. Scarlet fever also gives a strawberry tongue and rash, but it is caused by group A streptococcal infection, usually with exudative pharyngitis, and does not cause conjunctivitis or coronary aneurysms."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C16","obj":"CV-18-I.16","title":"ANCA-associated small-vessel vasculitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA) target enzymes stored in neutrophil granules. PR3-ANCA (proteinase 3) gives a cytoplasmic pattern on immunofluorescence (c-ANCA), and MPO-ANCA (myeloperoxidase) gives a perinuclear pattern (p-ANCA). When infection or cytokines prime neutrophils, PR3 and MPO move to the cell surface, where ANCA bind them. Bound neutrophils degranulate and release reactive oxygen species while adhering to endothelium, injuring small vessels (arterioles, capillaries, venules). Because injury depends on activated cells rather than deposits, these diseases are pauci-immune: biopsies show little immunoglobulin or complement, unlike immune complex vasculitis."},{"type":"p","text":"The organs most affected are those rich in capillaries: the kidney, where necrotizing crescentic glomerulonephritis causes hematuria, red cell casts, and rapidly progressive renal failure; and the lung, where capillaritis causes alveolar hemorrhage and hemoptysis. Combined lung hemorrhage and glomerulonephritis is a pulmonary-renal syndrome, which also occurs with anti-GBM disease, where immunofluorescence instead shows linear IgG along the basement membrane."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the ANCA-associated vasculitides","cols":["Feature","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"rows":[["ANCA","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) in most","MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) in most","MPO-ANCA in a minority (about 40%)"],["Granulomas","Necrotizing granulomas","None","Eosinophil-rich granulomas"],["Upper airway","Chronic sinusitis, nasal ulcers, septal perforation, saddle nose, otitis","Usually absent","Allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps"],["Lung","Nodules that cavitate; hemorrhage","Capillaritis and hemorrhage","Asthma, transient infiltrates"],["Kidney","Pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis","Pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis","Less common and milder"],["Distinctive","Classic triad: upper airway, lung, kidney","Like GPA without granulomas or upper airway disease","Blood eosinophilia, mononeuritis multiplex, cardiac involvement"]],"caption":"Granulomas, eosinophils, and the ANCA type separate the three diseases."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) combines necrotizing granulomas of the upper and lower respiratory tract with small-vessel vasculitis and glomerulonephritis, so a patient with chronic bloody sinusitis, lung nodules or cavities, and hematuria fits the pattern. Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA) has the same small-vessel injury in lung, kidney, and skin (palpable purpura) but no granulomas. Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA, formerly Churg-Strauss syndrome) progresses from asthma and allergic rhinitis to blood and tissue eosinophilia and then vasculitis; eosinophil granule proteins contribute to injury, and cardiac involvement is the main cause of death. ANCA is positive in fewer than half of EGPA patients."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-09-C17","obj":"CV-18-I.17","title":"IgA vasculitis and Behcet syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"IgA vasculitis (formerly Henoch-Schonlein purpura) is the most common systemic vasculitis of childhood, typically between about 3 and 15 years, and often follows an upper respiratory infection. Patients have an excess of galactose-deficient IgA1, which is recognized by autoantibodies to form immune complexes that deposit in small vessels of the skin, gut, joints, and glomerular mesangium, where they activate complement through the alternative and lectin pathways. This is an immune complex (type III) mechanism, and immunofluorescence shows IgA in vessel walls and the mesangium."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings follow the sites of deposition. Dermal leukocytoclastic vasculitis produces palpable purpura over the buttocks and the extensor surfaces of the legs, where gravity and hydrostatic pressure favor deposition. Because the purpura comes from inflamed vessels, the platelet count and coagulation tests are normal, which separates it from thrombocytopenic purpura. Joint involvement causes arthralgia or arthritis, usually of the knees and ankles. Gut vasculitis causes colicky abdominal pain and bleeding and predisposes to intussusception, often ileoileal. Renal involvement is a mesangial glomerulonephritis identical to IgA nephropathy, presenting with hematuria and sometimes proteinuria; it is more often severe in adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Behcet syndrome is a variable-vessel vasculitis that can affect arteries and veins of any size. It is most common along the old Silk Road from the Mediterranean and Middle East to Japan and is associated with HLA-B51. Neutrophil hyperactivity drives recurrent painful oral aphthous ulcers (nearly all patients), genital ulcers, and uveitis that can cause blindness, sometimes with a hypopyon. Skin lesions include erythema nodosum and pathergy, an exaggerated pustular response to a needle prick. Venous thrombosis, arterial aneurysms, and neurologic involvement add to its morbidity."},{"type":"table","title":"IgA vasculitis versus Behcet syndrome","cols":["Feature","IgA vasculitis","Behcet syndrome"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Child after upper respiratory infection","Young adult, Mediterranean to East Asian ancestry"],["Vessels","Small vessels","Variable: any size, arteries and veins"],["Mechanism","IgA1 immune complex deposition","Neutrophil-driven inflammation; HLA-B51"],["Skin","Palpable purpura on buttocks and legs","Erythema nodosum, pathergy"],["Hallmarks","Arthralgia, abdominal pain, IgA nephropathy","Recurrent oral and genital ulcers, uveitis, thrombosis"]],"caption":"Purpura with normal platelets in a child suggests IgA vasculitis; recurrent mucosal ulcers with uveitis suggest Behcet syndrome."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Most vascular disease can be organized by which layer of the wall fails. Intimal disease (atherosclerosis) narrows the lumen and seeds thrombi and emboli. Medial disease weakens the wall: atherosclerotic starvation of the media, cystic medial degeneration in Marfan syndrome, syphilitic endarteritis of the vasa vasorum, and fibrinoid necrosis in PAN all lead to aneurysm or dissection, and the law of Laplace explains why dilation, once started, tends to progress. Biochemistry supplies the lipid side of the story through the LDL receptor and PCSK9, microbiology supplies syphilis, Salmonella, and hepatitis B, and hemostasis explains venous thrombosis through Virchow triad."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology explains the vasculitides better than memorized lists. T cell granulomatous inflammation (GCA, Takayasu) targets large arteries and causes stenosis; immune complexes (hepatitis B associated PAN, IgA vasculitis) and ANCA-activated neutrophils (GPA, MPA, EGPA) injure medium and small vessels, with immunofluorescence separating deposit-rich from pauci-immune disease. Vessel size then predicts the clinical picture: pulse loss and claudication for large vessels, infarcts and microaneurysms for medium vessels, and purpura, glomerulonephritis, and alveolar hemorrhage for small vessels. The main limit of this scheme is overlap, since Kawasaki disease and PAN can involve smaller vessels and Behcet syndrome affects vessels of every size."}],"summary":["Atherosclerosis is intimal and narrows the lumen; arteriolosclerosis narrows arterioles (hyaline with diabetes and hypertension, hyperplastic onion-skin with severe hypertension); Monckeberg calcifies the media without narrowing the lumen.","Atherogenesis runs from endothelial dysfunction through oxidized LDL, foam cells, and fatty streaks to a fibrous cap over a necrotic core; thin-capped plaques rupture and thrombose.","Familial hypercholesterolemia reduces LDL receptor clearance, so LDL rises in proportion to gene dosage and deposits in arteries, tendons, eyelids, and cornea.","Abdominal aortic aneurysms are infrarenal and atherosclerotic; thoracic aneurysms reflect cystic medial degeneration or syphilis; wall stress rises with radius, so large aneurysms expand and rupture faster.","Aortic dissection splits a weakened media under high pressure; Stanford A involves the ascending aorta and threatens tamponade, aortic regurgitation, and coronary occlusion.","Claudication reflects a fixed stenosis that cannot raise flow with exercise; an ABI of 0.90 or lower confirms PAD, while cholesterol emboli cause blue toes with intact pulses.","Pulmonary emboli come mainly from proximal leg DVT and cause hypoxemia with a widened A-a gradient through V/Q mismatch; the bronchial supply prevents infarction in most cases.","Virchow triad explains DVT risk; superficial thrombophlebitis is a tender cord with little embolic risk, and migratory thrombophlebitis signals visceral adenocarcinoma.","Incompetent venous valves produce ambulatory venous hypertension, edema, hemosiderin staining, stasis dermatitis, and ulcers above the medial malleolus.","Vessel size organizes vasculitis: GCA and Takayasu (large, granulomatous), PAN and Kawasaki (medium, aneurysms), ANCA and IgA vasculitis (small, purpura and glomerulonephritis)."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels: arteriosclerosis, atherosclerosis, aneurysms and dissection, vasculitis, Raynaud phenomenon, veins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock: thrombosis, embolism, and infarction"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Vascular Distensibility and Functions of the Arterial and Venous Systems; Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Vascular Disease"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity: immune complex and T cell mediated diseases"}]},"CV-18-J":{"unit":"CV-18-J","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10","title":"Vascular Neoplasms","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers tumors and tumor-like lesions built from endothelial cells, from the common benign hemangiomas of infancy to Kaposi sarcoma and angiosarcoma, and adds the primary tumors of the heart. Board questions usually give a patient (an infant, a pregnant woman, a man with AIDS, a transplant recipient, a PVC plant worker) and a lesion, then ask for the cause, the histology, or the likely course. The questions become easy once you can explain why an infantile hemangioma regresses and a cavernous one does not, how HHV-8 genes override p53 and Rb, and why immune status decides how Kaposi sarcoma behaves.","prerequisites":["Structure of capillaries, venules, and the endothelial lining (CV-12)","Cell cycle control by cyclin D, CDK4 and CDK6, Rb, E2F, and p53","Properties of benign versus malignant neoplasms and the meaning of clonality","T-cell and NK cell control of virus-infected cells; CD4 depletion in HIV infection","Left atrial and mitral valve anatomy and the diastolic filling sequence"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiovascular pathogens (CV-16)","how":"HHV-8 and Bartonella produce vascular proliferations, so the organism, not the vessel, is the root cause of two lesions in this chapter."},{"to":"Immunology and HIV infection","how":"Loss of T-cell surveillance explains why Kaposi sarcoma spreads in AIDS and after transplantation and shrinks when immunity returns."},{"to":"Valvular heart disease (CV-18-D)","how":"A left atrial myxoma obstructs the mitral orifice and can mimic mitral stenosis on examination."},{"to":"Cardiac tumors and metastases (CV-18-F)","how":"Metastases to the heart are far more common than the primary tumors described here."},{"to":"Hepatobiliary pathology and toxicology","how":"Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, while vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast cause hepatic angiosarcoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hemangioma","def":"A benign neoplasm of endothelial cells that forms blood-filled vascular channels."},{"term":"Vascular malformation","def":"An error in vessel development, present at birth, that grows in proportion to the child and never regresses."},{"term":"GLUT1","def":"A glucose transporter expressed by infantile hemangioma endothelium (and placental capillaries) but not by vascular malformations or congenital hemangiomas."},{"term":"Involution","def":"The spontaneous regression of an infantile hemangioma as its endothelial cells die by apoptosis and fibrofatty tissue replaces them."},{"term":"Lobular capillary hemangioma","def":"The histologic name for pyogenic granuloma: lobules of capillaries in edematous, inflamed stroma."},{"term":"Bacillary angiomatosis","def":"A Bartonella infection of immunocompromised hosts that causes a capillary proliferation with neutrophils and visible bacteria."},{"term":"HHV-8 (KSHV)","def":"Human herpesvirus 8, a double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus that causes Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease."},{"term":"Spindle cell","def":"The HHV-8 infected tumor cell of Kaposi sarcoma, of endothelial (largely lymphatic endothelial) lineage, that lines slit-like vascular spaces."},{"term":"LANA","def":"Latency-associated nuclear antigen of HHV-8, which keeps the viral episome in dividing cells and binds and inhibits p53 and Rb."},{"term":"Myxoma","def":"A benign tumor of primitive mesenchymal cells in an abundant mucopolysaccharide matrix, the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults."},{"term":"Rhabdomyoma","def":"A hamartoma of cardiac muscle cells, the most common primary cardiac tumor in children and strongly linked to tuberous sclerosis."},{"term":"Angiosarcoma","def":"A malignant endothelial neoplasm forming irregular, anastomosing channels lined by atypical cells, with an aggressive course."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C01","obj":"CV-18-J.1","title":"Infantile, capillary, and cavernous hemangiomas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Benign vascular tumors build well-formed channels lined by a single layer of bland endothelium, whereas malignant ones form irregular channels lined by crowded, atypical cells. A hemangioma is a benign proliferation of endothelial cells, which sets it apart from a vascular malformation, a developmental error in vessel formation whose cells are not proliferating abnormally."},{"type":"p","text":"A capillary hemangioma is a well-circumscribed but unencapsulated lobule of closely packed, thin-walled capillaries in skin, subcutaneous tissue, or oral mucosa."},{"type":"p","text":"The infantile (juvenile, strawberry) hemangioma is the most common tumor of infancy, affecting about 4 to 5% of infants and occurring more often in girls and in preterm and low birth weight infants. It is absent or only a faint precursor patch at birth, appears in the first weeks of life, and proliferates rapidly for several months. Growth then plateaus, and over years the endothelial cells die by apoptosis and are replaced by fibrofatty tissue, sometimes leaving lax skin or telangiectasias. Superficial lesions are bright red and raised; deep ones are bluish. Lesional endothelium expresses GLUT1 in every phase, a marker otherwise typical of placental capillaries, whereas vascular malformations and congenital hemangiomas (fully formed at birth) are GLUT1 negative."},{"type":"flow","title":"Life cycle of an infantile hemangioma","steps":["Absent or faint precursor patch at birth","Appears in the first weeks of life","GLUT1-positive endothelium proliferates rapidly for months","Apoptosis of endothelial cells; fibrofatty replacement","Most have regressed by early childhood"],"caption":"Regression follows a programmed sequence; location and growth rate decide which lesions cause harm."},{"type":"p","text":"Harm comes from where the lesion grows and how fast. Rapid growth can outstrip the blood supply of the overlying skin, so ulceration is the most common complication. A periorbital lesion that blocks the visual axis or compresses the globe causes amblyopia, and a lesion in the beard distribution may involve the subglottic airway. Large segmental facial lesions can be part of PHACE syndrome (posterior fossa malformations, hemangioma, arterial anomalies, cardiac defects such as coarctation, eye anomalies). Large hepatic infantile hemangiomas express type 3 iodothyronine deiodinase, which inactivates thyroid hormone and can cause consumptive hypothyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"A cavernous hemangioma is made of large, dilated, thin-walled channels filled with blood and separated by scant connective tissue. It is less well circumscribed, often extends into deeper tissue, and, because its vessels are mature rather than proliferating, it does not regress. In the liver it is the most common benign tumor, usually a small solitary lesion found incidentally on imaging in adults. Slow flow promotes thrombosis and dystrophic calcification, and needle biopsy risks hemorrhage. One distinction is often tested: the platelet-trapping consumptive coagulopathy of Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon arises in kaposiform hemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma, which are GLUT1 negative, not in the ordinary infantile hemangioma."},{"type":"table","title":"Infantile hemangioma, cavernous hemangioma, and port-wine stain","cols":["Feature","Infantile (strawberry) hemangioma","Cavernous hemangioma","Port-wine stain (capillary malformation)"],"rows":[["At birth","Absent or faint precursor","Variable; often found later","Present"],["Growth","Rapid for months, then plateaus","Slow or none","In proportion to the child; darkens and thickens with age"],["Regression","Yes, over years","No","No"],["GLUT1","Positive","Negative","Negative"],["Histology","Lobules of small packed capillaries","Large dilated blood-filled spaces","Ectatic dermal capillaries and venules"],["Typical sites","Head and neck skin","Skin, liver (most common benign liver tumor), brain","Face, often in a trigeminal distribution"],["Classic association","PHACE with large segmental facial lesions","Thrombosis and calcification","Sturge-Weber syndrome (somatic GNAQ mutation)"]],"caption":"Present at birth and never regresses means malformation; appears after birth, grows, then fades means infantile hemangioma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A red facial lesion was fully formed at birth and has grown only in proportion to the child. Would you expect GLUT1 staining and spontaneous regression?","explain":"No to both. A lesion present at birth that grows with the child is a capillary malformation (port-wine stain), not a proliferating tumor, so it is GLUT1 negative and persists.","check":"The comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C02","obj":"CV-18-J.2","title":"Pyogenic granuloma, cherry hemangioma, and bacillary angiomatosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Several red vascular papules mimic hemangiomas and Kaposi sarcoma. Telling them apart depends on the patient, the speed of growth, and, above all, the cells that accompany the vessels on histology."},{"type":"p","text":"Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma) is misnamed: it is neither infectious nor granulomatous. It is a rapidly growing, exophytic red polyp, enlarging over weeks, on the skin of the fingers or face or on the gingiva and oral mucosa, often after minor trauma. Histology shows lobules of capillaries divided by fibrous septa within edematous stroma containing inflammatory cells, so it resembles exuberant granulation tissue, and a collar of epidermis often wraps its base. Many small vessels lie under a thin, frequently ulcerated surface, which is why it bleeds with slight contact. The pregnancy tumor (granuloma gravidarum) is a gingival pyogenic granuloma in pregnant women; high estrogen and progesterone amplify the vascular response of the gingiva to local irritants such as dental plaque, and the lesion often regresses after delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"Cherry hemangiomas (senile angiomas) are small, bright red, dome-shaped papules on the trunk and proximal limbs. They appear from early adulthood, multiply with age, and are nearly universal in older adults. They are collections of dilated capillaries and venules in the papillary dermis that do not regress and have no malignant potential."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacillary angiomatosis is an infection, not a neoplasm. Bartonella henselae (from cats and their fleas) and Bartonella quintana (from the human body louse) are small gram-negative bacilli that, in immunocompromised hosts, especially people with AIDS and very low CD4 counts, induce host cells to produce hypoxia-inducible factor 1 alpha (HIF-1 alpha). This raises VEGF and drives capillary proliferation. The result is red to purple papules and nodules of skin, sometimes with involvement of bone, lymph nodes, or liver (peliosis hepatis, blood-filled hepatic cysts, with B. henselae). Histology shows a capillary proliferation with plump, epithelioid endothelial cells, a neutrophilic infiltrate with nuclear dust, and clumps of granular purple material that a Warthin-Starry silver stain resolves into bacilli. Because living bacteria sustain the proliferation, the lesions disappear when the organism is eradicated."},{"type":"table","title":"Red and purple vascular lesions compared","cols":["Lesion","Typical patient","Cause","Key histology","Course"],"rows":[["Infantile hemangioma","Infant, first weeks of life","Endothelial neoplasm","Packed capillaries, GLUT1 positive","Grows, then regresses"],["Pyogenic granuloma","Any age; after trauma; gingiva in pregnancy","Reactive capillary proliferation","Lobular capillaries in edematous inflamed stroma, ulcerated surface","Bleeds easily; pregnancy form often regresses after delivery"],["Cherry hemangioma","Adults, increasing with age","Unknown; age related","Dilated capillaries and venules in papillary dermis","Persists; benign"],["Bacillary angiomatosis","Immunocompromised, especially advanced AIDS","Bartonella henselae or quintana","Epithelioid endothelium, neutrophils, silver-stain-positive bacilli","Resolves when the infection is eradicated"],["Kaposi sarcoma","Older Mediterranean men, AIDS, transplant, African endemic","HHV-8 with immune dysfunction","Spindle cells, slit-like spaces, lymphocytes, plasma cells, hemosiderin","Low-grade malignancy; course depends on immune status"]],"caption":"Neutrophils and bacteria point to bacillary angiomatosis; spindle cells and slit-like spaces point to Kaposi sarcoma."},{"type":"example","title":"A purple papule in a man with AIDS","text":"A man with untreated HIV and a CD4 count of 40 cells per microliter has several violaceous skin papules, a cat at home, and fever. Both bacillary angiomatosis and Kaposi sarcoma fit the skin finding and the immune state, so the color does not decide. Fever and systemic illness favor an infection. On biopsy, the decision rests on the inflammatory cells and the shape of the vessels: rounded capillaries with plump endothelium, neutrophils, and silver-stain-positive bacilli mean bacillary angiomatosis, whereas fascicles of spindle cells around slit-like spaces with extravasated red cells, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and nuclear LANA staining mean Kaposi sarcoma. The first resolves when the bacteria are eradicated; the second depends on immune recovery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C03","obj":"CV-18-J.3","title":"Kaposi sarcoma pathogenesis and lesion progression","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular tumor of low to intermediate malignancy caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus), a double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus. HHV-8 spreads mainly through saliva and also through sexual contact, transplanted organs, and blood. Infection is necessary but not sufficient: most infected people never develop the tumor, and the usual cofactor is impaired T-cell immunity."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor cell is the spindle cell, an infected cell of endothelial lineage that expresses CD31 and CD34 along with lymphatic endothelial markers such as podoplanin and PROX1. In most spindle cells the virus is latent, kept as a circular episome that expresses only a few genes, and each of them pushes the cell toward division or survival. The viral cyclin, a homolog of cyclin D, binds CDK6, resists the CDK inhibitors p16, p21, and p27, and phosphorylates Rb, freeing E2F to drive the G1 to S transition. LANA tethers the viral episome to host chromosomes so daughter cells inherit it, and it binds and inhibits both p53 and Rb, removing the checkpoint that would arrest or kill a cell with unscheduled proliferation. Viral FLIP activates NF-kappaB and blocks apoptosis."},{"type":"p","text":"A small fraction of cells enters lytic replication and secretes viral IL-6 and expresses a constitutively active viral G protein-coupled receptor that increases VEGF. These paracrine signals promote angiogenesis and inflammation, which recruit and stimulate neighboring cells. Early lesions are polyclonal and advanced nodules may become clonal, which is why Kaposi sarcoma has been described as lying between reactive hyperplasia and true neoplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytotoxic T cells and NK cells normally hold latently infected cells in check. When HIV depletes CD4 T cells or transplant drugs suppress T-cell activation, infected cells escape and the inflammatory milieu feeds their growth. The fact that lesions often regress when immunity is restored shows that growth still depends partly on an immune-permissive host."},{"type":"flow","title":"From HHV-8 infection to a Kaposi sarcoma nodule","steps":["HHV-8 infects endothelial-lineage cells and becomes latent","Viral cyclin phosphorylates Rb; LANA inhibits p53 and Rb","Viral FLIP activates NF-kappaB and blocks apoptosis","Lytic cells release viral IL-6 and raise VEGF","Weak T-cell and NK control lets infected cells expand","Spindle cells form leaky slit-like spaces","Patch progresses to plaque, then nodule"],"caption":"Latent genes provide proliferation and survival, lytic genes provide paracrine growth signals, and immune suppression removes the brake."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of a Kaposi sarcoma lesion","cols":["Stage","Gross appearance","Histology"],"rows":[["Patch","Flat pink, red, or purple macule","Irregular dilated vascular spaces with lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages; resembles granulation tissue"],["Plaque","Raised violaceous plaque","Dermal spindle cells around jagged channels, extravasated red cells, hemosiderin-laden macrophages"],["Nodule","Purple to brown nodule, may ulcerate","Sheets of plump spindle cells with slit-like spaces holding red cells; eosinophilic hyaline globules"]],"caption":"Nuclear staining for LANA identifies HHV-8 in the spindle cells at every stage."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical findings follow from this structure. Slit-like channels leak red cells into tissue, giving the purple color, and hemoglobin breakdown to hemosiderin turns older lesions brown. Involvement of lymphatics and lymph nodes causes lymphedema, and mucosal or visceral lesions bleed. HHV-8 also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease, both more common with HIV infection."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a transplant recipient's T-cell immunosuppression were reduced, what would you expect to happen to Kaposi sarcoma lesions, and why?","explain":"They often shrink. Latent HHV-8 genes drive proliferation, but restored cytotoxic T-cell and NK cell activity can again kill infected spindle cells and reduce the inflammatory cytokines that feed them.","check":"The immune control paragraph and the pathogenesis flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C04","obj":"CV-18-J.4","title":"The four clinical forms of Kaposi sarcoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every form of Kaposi sarcoma contains HHV-8 and shows the same histology. What differs is the host, and two variables predict how the tumor behaves: how common HHV-8 infection is in the population, and how deeply T-cell immunity is suppressed. Mild immune decline gives slow disease confined to the skin of the legs, while profound suppression gives multifocal disease of skin, mucosa, lymph nodes, and viscera."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of Kaposi sarcoma","cols":["Form","Population","Typical sites","Course"],"rows":[["Classic","Older men (usually over 60) of Mediterranean, Eastern European, or Ashkenazi Jewish descent; HIV negative","Skin of the distal lower limbs, often multiple plaques and nodules","Indolent over years to decades; visceral spread uncommon"],["Endemic African","HIV-negative children and adults in sub-Saharan Africa","Adults: skin of limbs, may infiltrate bone; children: lymph nodes","Adults locally aggressive; the childhood lymphadenopathic form is rapidly progressive"],["Transplant-associated (iatrogenic)","Solid organ recipients on T-cell immunosuppression, months to years after transplant","Skin, mucosa, lymph nodes, viscera","Can be aggressive; often regresses when immunosuppression is reduced"],["AIDS-associated (epidemic)","People with HIV infection, mainly men who have sex with men","Skin anywhere (face, trunk), oral mucosa and hard palate, GI tract, lungs, lymph nodes","Most aggressive and widespread; an AIDS-defining illness; improves with immune reconstitution"]],"caption":"Sites and pace follow immune status: legs and slow in the classic form, everywhere and fast in AIDS."},{"type":"p","text":"Classic Kaposi sarcoma occurs where HHV-8 infection is relatively common in the population and where age-related decline in T-cell function is the only cofactor, so lesions grow slowly on the feet and lower legs and patients often die of unrelated causes. Endemic African disease occurs where most people acquire HHV-8 in childhood through saliva. Adults develop nodular or infiltrative limb disease, while children can develop a lymphadenopathic form that progresses quickly even without skin lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Transplant-associated disease arises from reactivation of the recipient's latent virus or from virus carried in the donor organ, in a host whose T cells are pharmacologically suppressed. Because the cofactor is a drug, lesions often regress when immunosuppression is lowered and T-cell control returns."},{"type":"p","text":"AIDS-associated Kaposi sarcoma is the most common form in the United States. It is far more frequent in men who have sex with men than in other groups with HIV, which reflects sexual and salivary transmission of HHV-8 within those networks rather than a property of HIV itself. Lesions appear on the face, trunk, and oral mucosa, especially the hard palate. Gastrointestinal lesions bleed or obstruct, and pulmonary lesions cause dyspnea, hemoptysis, and pleural effusions. Incidence falls when CD4 counts recover, but lesions may flare transiently during early immune reconstitution, when recovering immune cells increase local inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C05","obj":"CV-18-J.5","title":"Primary cardiac tumors: atrial myxoma and rhabdomyoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary cardiac tumors are rare, and metastases to the heart are far more common (CV-18-F). Myxoma dominates in adults and rhabdomyoma in children."},{"type":"p","text":"Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults, usually appearing in middle age and more often in women. About 90% arise in the atria, with the left atrium affected about four times as often as the right, typically from the interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis. The tumor ranges from a firm sessile mass to a soft, gelatinous, villous lesion on a stalk. Histologically, stellate or globular myxoma cells, thought to derive from primitive multipotent mesenchymal cells, lie in an abundant acid mucopolysaccharide ground substance, often with hemorrhage and chronic inflammation."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a pedunculated left atrial myxoma causes symptoms","steps":["Stalked mass attached to the atrial septum","Prolapses into the mitral orifice in diastole","Ball-valve obstruction to left ventricular filling","Left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure rise","Dyspnea, pulmonary edema, and positional syncope","Friable fragments break off as systemic emboli"],"caption":"Obstruction varies with tumor position, so symptoms are episodic and postural."},{"type":"p","text":"Three groups of findings follow. Obstruction mimics mitral stenosis, with a diastolic murmur and, as the tumor drops into the ventricle, a low-pitched early diastolic tumor plop; repeated trauma can also damage the leaflets and cause mitral regurgitation. Embolization of friable tumor or overlying thrombus causes stroke or limb ischemia, and a right atrial myxoma embolizes to the lungs. Constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss, fatigue, arthralgia) arise because many myxomas secrete IL-6, which drives the hepatic acute phase response, raising ESR and CRP and producing anemia of inflammation. Echocardiography shows a mobile mass attached to the septum. About 10% of myxomas are familial, as part of Carney complex, an autosomal dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in PRKAR1A (a regulatory subunit of protein kinase A); these tumors are often multiple, recur, arise in younger patients and unusual sites, and accompany spotty skin pigmentation and endocrine overactivity."},{"type":"p","text":"Rhabdomyoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor of infants and children. It is a hamartoma, often multiple, within the ventricular myocardium, and it is strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis, in which TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin) mutation removes inhibition of mTOR and allows excess cell growth. Its large, glycogen-filled myocytes have cytoplasm stretched in strands from a central nucleus to the cell membrane, the so-called spider cells. Rhabdomyomas can obstruct a valve orifice or outflow tract or cause arrhythmias, but many regress spontaneously during early childhood."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary cardiac tumors and the main mimic","cols":["Tumor","Patient","Usual site","Key features"],"rows":[["Myxoma","Adults, middle age, more often women","Left atrium, interatrial septum near fossa ovalis","Gelatinous mucopolysaccharide matrix; ball-valve obstruction, emboli, IL-6 symptoms; Carney complex if familial"],["Rhabdomyoma","Infants and children","Ventricular myocardium, often multiple","Hamartoma with spider cells; tuberous sclerosis; often regresses"],["Papillary fibroelastoma","Older adults","Valve surfaces, often aortic or mitral","Small frond-like avascular papillae; source of emboli"],["Angiosarcoma","Adults","Right atrium","Most common primary malignant cardiac tumor in adults (C06)"],["Metastasis","Adults with known cancer","Pericardium most often","Far more common than primary tumors; lung, breast, melanoma, lymphoma"]],"caption":"Adult plus left atrium suggests myxoma; child plus multiple ventricular masses suggests rhabdomyoma and tuberous sclerosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Syncope that depends on posture","text":"A 45-year-old woman has breathlessness, two fainting spells on standing, low-grade fever, weight loss, and a high ESR. A diastolic sound follows S2, and the murmur changes with position. Fixed mitral stenosis does not change with posture and would not explain fever and weight loss. A mobile mass does both: in some positions a stalked left atrial myxoma falls into the mitral orifice and blocks filling, cardiac output falls, and she faints, while tumor IL-6 drives the acute phase response. The early diastolic sound is a tumor plop, and echocardiography would show the mass on the atrial septum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-10-C06","obj":"CV-18-J.6","title":"Angiosarcoma: risk factors and course","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Angiosarcoma is a malignant neoplasm of endothelial cells. It occurs mainly in older adults and can arise anywhere, most often in the skin (especially of the scalp and face in the elderly), soft tissue, breast, and liver, and it is the most common primary malignant tumor of the heart in adults, usually in the right atrium. Well-differentiated tumors form irregular, anastomosing channels that dissect between collagen bundles and are lined by plump, atypical, multilayered endothelial cells. Poorly differentiated tumors grow as solid sheets of anaplastic spindle or epithelioid cells with mitoses and necrosis, so endothelial markers such as CD31 and ERG are needed to show their lineage."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic risk factors fall into two groups. Hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to chemical and radiation carcinogens that the liver concentrates or metabolizes. Vinyl chloride, the monomer used to make polyvinyl chloride (PVC) plastic, is oxidized by hepatic cytochrome P450 2E1 to chloroethylene oxide, a reactive epoxide that forms DNA adducts in sinusoidal cells. Arsenic, from pesticides, industrial exposure, or contaminated well water, also causes hepatic angiosarcoma along with skin and lung cancers. Thorotrast, a thorium dioxide contrast agent used from about 1930 to 1950, was taken up by hepatic and splenic macrophages and retained for life, irradiating nearby cells with alpha particles. All three act over long latent periods, often decades."},{"type":"p","text":"The second group involves damaged lymphatic drainage and radiation. Chronic lymphedema, classically in the arm after radical mastectomy with axillary node dissection, can give rise to angiosarcoma about a decade later (Stewart-Treves syndrome); impaired local immune surveillance and the growth factor-rich lymphedematous tissue are thought to contribute. Radiation to the breast or chest wall can cause secondary angiosarcoma years later, and these tumors often carry MYC amplification."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vinyl chloride to hepatic angiosarcoma","steps":["Inhaled vinyl chloride monomer in PVC manufacturing","Hepatic CYP2E1 oxidizes it to chloroethylene oxide","Epoxide forms DNA adducts in sinusoidal cells","Mutations accumulate over decades of latency","Malignant endothelial proliferation in the liver"],"caption":"A metabolic activation step explains why the liver is the target organ."},{"type":"table","title":"Angiosarcoma risk factors","cols":["Exposure","Typical setting","Main site"],"rows":[["Vinyl chloride","PVC plastic production workers","Liver"],["Arsenic","Pesticides, smelting, contaminated well water","Liver; also skin and lung cancers"],["Thorotrast","Radiocontrast used about 1930 to 1950","Liver (also cholangiocarcinoma and hepatocellular carcinoma)"],["Chronic lymphedema","Arm after radical mastectomy with axillary dissection (Stewart-Treves)","Lymphedematous limb"],["Prior radiation","Breast or chest wall irradiation","Irradiated field"],["None identified","Older adults","Scalp and face skin"]],"caption":"Hepatic carcinogens act through the liver; lymphedema and radiation act on the damaged field."},{"type":"p","text":"Angiosarcoma behaves aggressively. It infiltrates widely beyond its visible margin, is often multifocal in the skin, recurs locally, and metastasizes, frequently to the lungs. Five-year survival is about 30%, and hepatic tumors do worse. Bruise-like purple patches on an elderly scalp, a mass in a chronically swollen arm, or a liver mass in a former PVC worker should all raise this diagnosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why would a retired PVC worker and a woman with 12 years of arm lymphedema after mastectomy develop the same tumor in different organs?","explain":"Both develop malignant endothelial tumors, but the injury differs. Vinyl chloride is activated in the liver to a DNA-damaging epoxide, so the liver is the target, whereas lymphedema damages the drainage and immune surveillance of one limb, so the tumor arises in that field.","check":"The two groups of risk factors and the table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each lesion in this chapter is explained by what drives the endothelial cell. Infantile hemangioma has a built-in program of proliferation and apoptosis, pyogenic granuloma is a reactive response to trauma or hormones, bacillary angiomatosis is sustained by bacteria that induce HIF-1 alpha and VEGF, Kaposi sarcoma is driven by viral genes that disable Rb and p53, and angiosarcoma follows DNA damage from chemicals, radiation, or chronic lymphedema. Histology reflects the driver: GLUT1 for the infantile program, neutrophils and bacilli for infection, spindle cells with LANA for HHV-8, and cytologic atypia for true malignancy. Immunology decides the course of the two lesions most tied to AIDS, because both bacillary angiomatosis and Kaposi sarcoma need a host that cannot clear the agent."},{"type":"p","text":"The cardiac tumors connect to hemodynamics rather than to vessel biology: a myxoma is benign but dangerous because a mobile mass in the left atrium interrupts filling and sheds emboli, and the systemic symptoms come from a secreted cytokine, not from spread. Genetics links both cardiac tumors to signaling pathways (protein kinase A in Carney complex, mTOR in tuberous sclerosis). Some limits apply: the line between Kaposi sarcoma as hyperplasia and as neoplasm is unsettled, and names such as cavernous hemangioma are reclassified as venous malformations in newer vascular anomaly schemes."}],"summary":["A hemangioma is a benign endothelial neoplasm; a vascular malformation is a developmental error that is present at birth, grows with the child, and never regresses.","Infantile hemangioma appears in the first weeks of life, proliferates for months, then involutes by apoptosis; it is GLUT1 positive, and its harm depends on site (eye, airway, ulceration, PHACE).","Cavernous hemangioma has large mature channels, does not regress, and is the most common benign liver tumor.","Pyogenic granuloma is a bleeding lobular capillary proliferation after trauma or in pregnancy; cherry hemangiomas multiply with age; bacillary angiomatosis is Bartonella infection with neutrophils and silver-stain-positive bacilli.","HHV-8 causes Kaposi sarcoma through latent viral cyclin (Rb phosphorylation), LANA (p53 and Rb inhibition), and viral FLIP (NF-kappaB survival), with lytic viral IL-6 and VEGF as paracrine growth signals.","Kaposi sarcoma progresses from patch to plaque to nodule, with spindle cells around slit-like spaces holding extravasated red cells.","Classic Kaposi sarcoma is slow on the lower legs of older Mediterranean or Eastern European men; endemic disease is aggressive in African children; transplant and AIDS forms are widespread and regress when immunity returns.","Myxoma is the most common adult primary cardiac tumor, usually left atrial, causing ball-valve obstruction, emboli, and IL-6 driven constitutional symptoms.","Rhabdomyoma is the most common pediatric primary cardiac tumor, a hamartoma with spider cells strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis.","Angiosarcoma is an aggressive malignant endothelial tumor linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast in the liver and to chronic lymphedema (Stewart-Treves) and radiation elsewhere."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels: vascular tumors (benign tumors and tumor-like conditions, intermediate-grade tumors, malignant tumors)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: cardiac tumors"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses: human herpesvirus 8; Bartonella species"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to tumors; congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System: capillaries and endothelium"}]},"CV-18-K":{"unit":"CV-18-K","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11","title":"Infectious Vascular Diseases","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers infections whose main damage falls on the heart or blood vessels: bacterial endocarditis, Chagas disease, Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, the viral hemorrhagic fevers, and viral myocarditis. Each one is traced from the organism and its route of entry to the cell or tissue it injures, and from there to the findings a board question gives you. Three patterns recur: a damaged surface on which organisms settle (endocarditis), an organism that lives in heart muscle or conduction tissue (Chagas, Lyme, myocarditis), and an organism or immune response that injures endothelium and clotting (spotted fever and hemorrhagic fevers). If you can name the vector, the target cell, and the mechanism of injury, you can predict the presentation.","prerequisites":["Valve anatomy, pressure gradients, and normal flow through the heart (CV-3, CV-4)","Endothelial control of permeability and coagulation, including tissue factor and platelet adhesion","Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity and complement","Structure and classification of bacteria, protozoa, and RNA viruses (CV-17)","Conduction system anatomy and AV block on the ECG (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Rheumatic, degenerative, and congenital valve lesions create the turbulent jets and damaged endothelium on which subacute endocarditis develops."},{"to":"Primary myocardial diseases (CV-18-E)","how":"Chagas disease and viral myocarditis are infectious causes of dilated cardiomyopathy."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Lyme carditis, Chagas disease, and a perivalvular abscess from endocarditis all present with AV block because they inflame or destroy conduction tissue."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"Circulating immune complexes in endocarditis deposit in glomeruli and cause a hypocomplementemic proliferative glomerulonephritis."},{"to":"Hematology and hemostasis","how":"Endothelial injury in spotted fever and hemorrhagic fevers consumes platelets and clotting factors, the same process that underlies disseminated intravascular coagulation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Vegetation","def":"A mass of platelets, fibrin, inflammatory cells, and organisms attached to a valve or endocardial surface."},{"term":"Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis","def":"Sterile platelet and fibrin deposits on a valve that can later be colonized by circulating bacteria."},{"term":"Transient bacteremia","def":"Brief entry of bacteria into the blood, for example from the gums or the gut, that is normally cleared within minutes."},{"term":"Perivalvular (ring) abscess","def":"Extension of valve infection into the annulus and surrounding myocardium."},{"term":"Mycotic aneurysm","def":"An aneurysm caused by infection of an arterial wall, usually from septic emboli lodging in the vasa vasorum or lumen; the name does not mean a fungal cause."},{"term":"Amastigote","def":"The intracellular, nonflagellated replicating form of Trypanosoma cruzi found in muscle and other tissue cells."},{"term":"Erythema migrans","def":"The expanding red skin lesion at the site of an Ixodes tick bite that marks early localized Lyme disease."},{"term":"Vasculitis","def":"Inflammation and injury of the vessel wall; in rickettsial disease it results from infection of endothelial cells."},{"term":"Councilman body","def":"An eosinophilic, shrunken apoptotic hepatocyte, classically seen in yellow fever."},{"term":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","def":"Increased viral entry into Fc receptor bearing cells when non-neutralizing antibodies from a previous infection coat the virus."},{"term":"Plasma leakage","def":"Loss of fluid and protein from capillaries through damaged or activated endothelium, causing hemoconcentration, effusions, and shock."},{"term":"Coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR)","def":"A junctional protein on cardiac myocytes that coxsackie B viruses and adenoviruses use to enter heart muscle."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C01","obj":"CV-18-K.1","title":"How vegetations form and which organism to expect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infective endocarditis is infection of the endocardium, almost always on a valve. Normal endothelium resists bacterial attachment, so most cases need two conditions at once: a surface that bacteria can grip and bacteria in the blood. The surface is created by turbulent, high-velocity flow. A regurgitant or stenotic jet, a ventricular septal defect, a prosthetic ring, or repeated injection of particulate material injures the endothelium and exposes subendothelial collagen and tissue factor. Platelets and fibrin deposit on the injured area, forming a small sterile mass called nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria entering the blood during a transient bacteremia bind this deposit. Viridans streptococci produce extracellular dextran (a glucan) and other adhesins that stick to fibrin and platelets, so they need a damaged valve. Staphylococcus aureus carries surface proteins that bind fibrinogen and fibronectin and can attach to and invade intact endothelium, so it infects normal valves and produces acute disease. Neutrophils penetrate this avascular mass poorly, so bacteria grow to very high densities and shed continuously into the blood, which is why bacteremia is persistent rather than intermittent."},{"type":"p","text":"Vegetations usually sit along the line of valve closure, on the atrial surface of the mitral and tricuspid valves and the ventricular surface of the aortic and pulmonary valves, the low-pressure sides that receive a regurgitant jet. Left-sided valves (mitral, then aortic) are involved most often because they work at higher pressures. Injection drug use is the main exception: injected bacteria and particulate debris reach the right heart first, and the tricuspid valve is the most common site."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation of a vegetation","steps":["Turbulent jet or foreign material injures valve endothelium","Collagen and tissue factor exposed; platelets and fibrin deposit","Sterile deposit forms (nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis)","Transient bacteremia: organisms adhere through dextran or binding proteins","More fibrin covers bacteria and shields them from neutrophils","Vegetation grows and sheds organisms into the blood continuously"],"caption":"Staphylococcus aureus can skip the first three steps and infect a normal valve."},{"type":"p","text":"The organism follows from the source of the bacteremia and the surface available. Acute endocarditis from a virulent organism destroys a valve over days; subacute disease from a low-virulence organism smolders for weeks with fever, weight loss, and fatigue."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the organism by setting","cols":["Setting","Most likely organism","Reasoning"],"rows":[["Native valve, acute course, often a normal valve","Staphylococcus aureus","Most common cause overall; adhesins allow invasion of intact endothelium"],["Native damaged valve after dental work","Viridans streptococci (for example S. sanguinis, S. mutans)","Mouth flora enter blood from gums; dextran binds fibrin and platelets"],["Injection drug use","Staphylococcus aureus (also Pseudomonas, Candida)","Skin flora injected; tricuspid valve most often affected"],["Prosthetic valve, early after surgery","Staphylococcus epidermidis and other coagulase-negative staphylococci","Biofilm on foreign material; skin flora introduced at operation"],["GI or GU procedures, older adults","Enterococci (mainly Enterococcus faecalis)","Gut and urinary flora enter blood during manipulation"],["Bacteremia with colon pathology","Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis)","Associated with colorectal cancer, so the colon is examined"],["Blood cultures negative","HACEK group, Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella","Fastidious or intracellular organisms that grow poorly in routine culture"]],"caption":"Late prosthetic valve infection behaves more like native valve disease, with streptococci and S. aureus becoming common."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from setting to organism","text":"A 64-year-old with known mitral regurgitation has had low-grade fever and weight loss for 6 weeks after a tooth extraction. The slow course means a low-virulence organism, the regurgitant valve supplies the deposit, and the extraction released mouth flora: a viridans streptococcus. Now a 28-year-old who injects drugs has 4 days of high fever, a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border, and nodular lung lesions. The short course means a virulent organism, the murmur is tricuspid regurgitation, and the lung lesions are septic emboli from the right heart: Staphylococcus aureus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C02","obj":"CV-18-K.2","title":"Complications of bacterial endocarditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every complication of endocarditis comes from one of four processes: local destruction of the valve and surrounding tissue, fragments breaking off the vegetation (embolism), immune complexes formed against the persistent bacteremia, and infection of arterial walls. Sorting findings by these processes is easier than memorizing a list."},{"type":"h","text":"Local destruction"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial enzymes and neutrophil products perforate leaflets, tear chordae, and erode the valve, causing new or worsening regurgitation. Sudden severe regurgitation gives the ventricle no time to dilate, so acute heart failure with pulmonary edema follows; heart failure is the leading cause of death in endocarditis. Infection can also spread into the annulus as a perivalvular (ring) abscess. This is most common with the aortic valve and with prosthetic valves. Because the aortic annulus lies next to the AV node and His bundle, a new and lengthening PR interval or higher degree AV block in a patient with endocarditis suggests an abscess. Abscesses can also form fistulas between chambers."},{"type":"h","text":"Embolism and the side of the heart"},{"type":"p","text":"Fragments travel downstream from the infected valve. From the left heart they enter the systemic circulation and cause stroke or brain abscess, infarcts or abscesses of the spleen and kidney, and ischemic limbs or digits. Janeway lesions, painless hemorrhagic macules on the palms and soles, are usually attributed to septic microemboli. From the right heart, fragments lodge in the lungs as septic pulmonary emboli, seen as multiple peripheral nodules that may cavitate, with cough, pleuritic pain, and hemoptysis. Large, mobile vegetations embolize most often."},{"type":"h","text":"Immune complexes and arterial infection"},{"type":"p","text":"Continuous antigen release drives antibody production, and antigen and antibody combine into circulating immune complexes (type III hypersensitivity). Deposited in glomeruli, they activate complement and cause a proliferative glomerulonephritis with hematuria, red cell casts, and low complement levels. Osler nodes (painful nodules on finger and toe pads) and Roth spots (retinal hemorrhages with pale centers) are classically attributed to immune complex vasculitis, and rheumatoid factor may be positive. A mycotic aneurysm forms when septic emboli lodge in the vasa vasorum or lumen of an artery and weaken its wall; cerebral arteries, especially branches of the middle cerebral artery, are typical, and rupture causes intracranial hemorrhage."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications sorted by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Findings","Clue in a question"],"rows":[["Valve destruction","Acute regurgitation, heart failure, chordal rupture","New murmur, pulmonary edema"],["Perivalvular abscess","AV block, fistula","Aortic or prosthetic valve, new PR prolongation"],["Left-sided emboli","Stroke, splenic and renal infarcts, Janeway lesions, splinter hemorrhages","Neurologic deficit, flank or left upper quadrant pain"],["Right-sided emboli","Septic pulmonary emboli, lung abscess","Injection drug use, cavitating nodules"],["Immune complexes","Glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots","Hematuria with red cell casts, low complement"],["Arterial wall infection","Mycotic aneurysm","Sudden headache or hemorrhage weeks into illness"]],"caption":"Splinter hemorrhages under the nails are linear and nonspecific; they also follow trauma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have endocarditis with emboli. One has a splenic infarct and a stroke; the other has multiple cavitating lung nodules. Which valve is infected in each?","explain":"Emboli lodge in the first capillary bed downstream. Systemic infarcts mean a left-sided valve (mitral or aortic). Lung lesions mean a right-sided valve, usually the tricuspid in injection drug use.","check":"The embolism paragraph and the mechanism table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C03","obj":"CV-18-K.3","title":"Chagas disease: acute and chronic","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chagas disease is infection with Trypanosoma cruzi, a flagellated protozoan found in Mexico, Central America, and South America. Its vectors are triatomine (reduviid, or kissing) bugs, which live in cracks in mud and thatch houses and feed at night, often on the face. The bug defecates while or after feeding, and infective trypomastigotes in the feces are rubbed into the bite wound or onto the conjunctiva or other mucosa. The parasite is also spread by blood transfusion, organ transplantation, from mother to fetus, and by food or drink contaminated with bug feces. In nonendemic countries, most cases are in immigrants from endemic regions."},{"type":"p","text":"At the entry site trypomastigotes invade local cells, transform into intracellular amastigotes, multiply, and burst out as trypomastigotes that spread through the blood. The acute phase lasts weeks to about two months, and most infections are mild or unnoticed. Local inflammation produces a chagoma (an indurated skin lesion) at a skin entry site or Romana sign, painless unilateral swelling of the eyelids, when entry is through the conjunctiva. Trypomastigotes are numerous enough in the acute phase to be seen on a blood smear. A small minority, mainly young children and immunocompromised people, develop acute myocarditis or meningoencephalitis."},{"type":"p","text":"The immune response controls but does not clear the parasite, and most people enter a lifelong indeterminate phase without symptoms. Years to decades later, about 20 to 30 percent develop chronic disease. Parasites persist in cardiac muscle and enteric neurons, and the continuing inflammation, with an autoimmune contribution, causes patchy myocardial fibrosis, destruction of conduction tissue, and loss of myenteric (Auerbach) plexus neurons. Tissue sections show lymphocytic infiltrates and fibrosis with few parasites. Parasitemia is now too low for a smear, so chronic disease is identified by antibodies against T. cruzi."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute versus chronic Chagas disease","cols":["Feature","Acute","Chronic"],"rows":[["Timing","Weeks after infection","Years to decades later, in about 20 to 30 percent"],["Parasites","Abundant trypomastigotes in blood","Few; persist in heart and gut tissue"],["Main findings","Fever, chagoma, Romana sign, lymphadenopathy; often no symptoms","Dilated cardiomyopathy, megaesophagus, megacolon"],["Heart","Rare acute myocarditis","Fibrosis, conduction block, apical aneurysm, thrombi, arrhythmia"],["Detection","Blood smear","Serology (antibodies)"]],"caption":"Most infected people never progress beyond the indeterminate phase."},{"type":"flow","title":"From bug bite to chronic organ damage","steps":["Triatomine bug feeds and defecates near the bite or eye","Trypomastigotes enter wound or conjunctiva (chagoma, Romana sign)","Amastigotes multiply in cells; parasites spread through blood","Immune control without clearance: indeterminate phase","Years of inflammation: myocardial fibrosis and myenteric neuron loss","Cardiomyopathy with block; megaesophagus and megacolon"],"caption":"Chronic Chagas disease reflects decades of low-grade parasite persistence and inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy is a dilated cardiomyopathy with three distinctive features. Fibrosis of the conduction system produces right bundle branch block, often with left anterior fascicular block, and AV block. Thinning of the left ventricular apex produces an apical aneurysm in which mural thrombus forms and embolizes. Scarred myocardium supports reentrant ventricular tachycardia, so sudden death may be the first sign. In the gut, loss of myenteric neurons abolishes coordinated peristalsis and sphincter relaxation, giving an achalasia-like megaesophagus with dysphagia and megacolon with severe constipation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C04","obj":"CV-18-K.4","title":"Lyme disease: stages and exposure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lyme disease is caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete transmitted by Ixodes ticks (Ixodes scapularis, the black-legged or deer tick, in the eastern United States). Risk is concentrated in the Northeast, the mid-Atlantic states, and the upper Midwest (Wisconsin and Minnesota), where these ticks are common, with a smaller focus on the Pacific coast carried by Ixodes pacificus. White-footed mice are the main reservoir, and deer sustain the adult tick population. Most infections are acquired in late spring and summer from nymphs, which are tiny and easily missed. The spirochete sits in the tick midgut and must migrate to the salivary glands after feeding begins, so transmission usually requires attachment for more than about 36 hours."},{"type":"p","text":"The stages follow the spirochete as it spreads. It first moves outward through the skin from the bite, and the inflammatory response at its advancing edge produces erythema migrans, an expanding red patch at least about 5 cm across that may clear centrally into a target shape. It then disseminates through the blood to other skin sites, nerves, meninges, heart, and joints. Late disease reflects persistent organisms or antigen in tissue and the immune response against them. The same tick can transmit Babesia and Anaplasma, so coinfection is possible."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of Lyme disease","cols":["Stage and timing","Findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Early localized: days to about 1 month","Erythema migrans; fever, fatigue, headache, myalgia","Spirochete spreading through skin around the bite"],["Early disseminated: weeks to months","Multiple erythema migrans lesions; facial nerve palsy (unilateral or bilateral); lymphocytic meningitis; radiculoneuritis; carditis with AV block","Bloodstream spread to skin, nerves, meninges, and heart"],["Late: months to years","Recurrent arthritis of large joints, especially the knee; chronic neuropathy or encephalopathy","Persistent antigen and immune inflammation in synovium and nerve"]],"caption":"Not every patient passes through every stage, and many do not recall the tick bite."},{"type":"p","text":"Lyme carditis occurs in a small percentage of untreated patients during early dissemination. Spirochetes and a lymphocytic infiltrate inflame the myocardium and the conduction system, most often at the AV node. The result is AV block of any degree, from a prolonged PR interval to complete heart block, and the degree can change within hours. Because the node is inflamed rather than scarred, the block usually resolves. Myopericarditis can occur but heart failure is uncommon. A young person from an endemic area with a summer rash, facial palsy, or new heart block should raise the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on the biology of the antibody response. Erythema migrans appears before antibodies are reliably detectable, so early localized disease is recognized clinically in an exposed patient. Disseminated and late disease are confirmed by serology, usually a screening immunoassay followed by a confirmatory test such as an immunoblot. Bilateral facial palsy is unusual and helps point to Lyme disease rather than idiopathic (Bell) palsy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C05","obj":"CV-18-K.5","title":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever as rickettsial vasculitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever is caused by Rickettsia rickettsii, a small obligate intracellular gram-negative bacterium that stains poorly with Gram stain. It is carried by Dermacentor ticks (the American dog tick in the east and the Rocky Mountain wood tick in the west), and in parts of Arizona and Mexico by the brown dog tick. Despite the name, most United States cases occur in the southeastern and south-central states, such as North Carolina, Tennessee, Arkansas, Missouri, and Oklahoma, in spring and summer. Symptoms begin about 2 to 14 days after the bite."},{"type":"p","text":"The target cell explains the disease. Rickettsiae attach to vascular endothelial cells through outer membrane proteins, are taken up, and escape the phagosome into the cytoplasm. There they multiply and polymerize host actin into tails that push them into neighboring endothelial cells, so infection spreads cell to cell along the vessel. Infected endothelial cells and the inflammatory response to them produce a disseminated small vessel vasculitis in skin, brain, lungs, kidneys, heart, and gut."},{"type":"flow","title":"From tick bite to multiorgan failure","steps":["Dermacentor tick transmits Rickettsia rickettsii","Rickettsiae infect endothelium and spread cell to cell by actin","Small vessel vasculitis throughout the body","Increased permeability: edema, hypovolemia, low albumin and sodium","Red cells escape: petechial rash; platelets consumed","Severe cases: shock, pulmonary edema, encephalitis, renal failure"],"caption":"Each finding is a consequence of injured, leaky endothelium."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic triad is fever, headache, and rash with a history of a tick bite or exposure, but all three are present at first in only a minority, and some patients never develop a rash. The rash usually appears on about day 2 to 5 as small blanching pink macules on the wrists and ankles. It spreads centripetally to the arms, legs, and trunk and becomes petechial as red cells leak from damaged capillaries. Involvement of the palms and soles is characteristic but often late. Myalgia, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain are common and can mislead toward a gastrointestinal illness."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory findings follow the vascular injury. Platelets adhere to damaged endothelium and are consumed, causing thrombocytopenia. Fluid and albumin leak into tissues, causing edema and hypoalbuminemia, and the fall in effective circulating volume stimulates ADH release, causing hyponatremia. Liver enzymes may rise. In severe disease, leakage in the lungs causes noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, brain involvement causes confusion, seizures, or coma, and hypovolemia with myocardial involvement causes shock and acute kidney injury. Disseminated intravascular coagulation and gangrene of digits can occur but are less common. Because the course can be rapidly fatal, the diagnosis is made on clinical grounds; antibodies are often absent in the first week."},{"type":"table","title":"Rashes that involve the palms and soles","cols":["Condition","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Starts at wrists and ankles, spreads to trunk; fever, headache, tick exposure"],["Secondary syphilis","Copper-colored macules or papules; generalized lymphadenopathy; condylomata lata"],["Hand, foot, and mouth disease (coxsackie A)","Vesicles on hands and feet with oral ulcers, usually in young children"],["Infective endocarditis","Janeway lesions on palms and soles with fever and a murmur"]],"caption":"Palm and sole involvement narrows the differential but does not identify the cause on its own."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with Rocky Mountain spotted fever develop both edema and a low platelet count, when neither the liver nor the marrow is the primary target?","explain":"Both come from endothelial injury. Damaged, leaky capillaries let plasma and albumin escape into tissues, and exposed subendothelium consumes platelets as they adhere. The marrow is producing platelets; they are being used up at the vessel wall.","check":"The mechanism and laboratory paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C06","obj":"CV-18-K.6","title":"Yellow fever compared with dengue","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Yellow fever and dengue are both flaviviruses (enveloped, positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses) carried mainly by Aedes aegypti, a daytime-biting mosquito that breeds in standing water near houses. They differ in the organ they damage. Yellow fever is above all a hepatitis with bleeding, while dengue is a febrile illness whose dangerous form is a capillary leak."},{"type":"h","text":"Yellow fever"},{"type":"p","text":"Yellow fever occurs in tropical Africa and South America. A jungle (sylvatic) cycle between monkeys and forest mosquitoes maintains the virus, and Aedes aegypti carries it between people in towns. After 3 to 6 days the illness begins with fever, headache, myalgia, and back pain, and most patients recover. About 15 percent improve briefly and then enter a toxic phase. The virus damages hepatocytes, especially in the middle of the lobule (zone 2, midzonal necrosis), which is unusual because most toxic and ischemic injury is centrilobular. Dying hepatocytes undergo apoptosis and appear as Councilman bodies, shrunken eosinophilic cells, with microvesicular fat and relatively little inflammation. Liver failure causes jaundice, which gives the disease its name, and the loss of clotting factor synthesis combines with platelet consumption to cause bleeding. Blood swallowed or vomited after gastric bleeding is darkened by acid, producing black vomit. Kidney failure and shock follow, and a large share of patients in the toxic phase die."},{"type":"h","text":"Dengue"},{"type":"p","text":"Dengue is widespread throughout the tropics and subtropics and has four serotypes (DENV-1 to DENV-4). Classic dengue causes sudden high fever, often 39 to 40 °C (102.2 to 104 °F), headache with pain behind the eyes, rash, and severe muscle, joint, and bone pain (breakbone fever), with leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. Severe dengue usually appears around defervescence, about days 3 to 7, when endothelial activation by viral proteins and cytokines makes capillaries leak plasma. Hematocrit rises as plasma is lost, pleural effusions and ascites form, and hypovolemic shock can follow, while platelets fall and bleeding may occur. Severe dengue is more likely in a second infection with a different serotype because of antibody-dependent enhancement, and in infants who carry maternal antibody."},{"type":"table","title":"Yellow fever versus dengue","cols":["Feature","Yellow fever","Dengue"],"rows":[["Where","Tropical Africa and South America","Tropics and subtropics worldwide"],["Main target","Hepatocytes","Monocytes, macrophages, dendritic cells; endothelium affected"],["Hallmark pathology","Midzonal necrosis, Councilman bodies","Capillary plasma leak"],["Key findings","Jaundice, black vomit, bleeding, renal failure","Severe myalgia and arthralgia, retro-orbital pain, rash"],["Laboratory clue","Very high aminotransferases, bilirubin, long prothrombin time","Low platelets, low white cells, rising hematocrit"],["Severe form linked to","Toxic phase after brief remission","Second infection with a different serotype"]],"caption":"Both are Aedes-borne flaviviruses with thrombocytopenia and bleeding; the liver separates them."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the second dengue infection is worse","text":"A traveler had dengue serotype 1 three years ago and now acquires serotype 2. Her antibodies to serotype 1 bind serotype 2 but do not neutralize it. The Fc portion of the bound antibody attaches to Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, so more virus enters these cells than would enter without antibody. Viral load and cytokine release rise, and on day 5, as her fever falls, her hematocrit climbs from 40% to 48% while platelets drop to 30,000/microliter. The rising hematocrit is the sign of plasma leaving the vessels, and it warns of shock before blood pressure falls."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C07","obj":"CV-18-K.7","title":"Shared mechanism of viral hemorrhagic fevers; Ebola and Marburg","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Viral hemorrhagic fever is a syndrome rather than one disease. It is caused by enveloped RNA viruses from several families, including flaviviruses (yellow fever, dengue), filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg), arenaviruses (Lassa), and bunyaviruses (hantaviruses, Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever). Despite the name, visible bleeding is often modest; the deaths come mainly from loss of fluid out of the vessels, shock, and multiorgan failure. The viruses share three effects that produce this picture."},{"type":"p","text":"First, they infect monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells early and spread with them to lymph nodes, liver, and spleen. Many of these viruses block type I interferon signaling, so replication outpaces the innate response, and infected dendritic cells fail to start an effective adaptive response while many lymphocytes die by bystander apoptosis. Second, infected macrophages release large amounts of TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, and other mediators (a cytokine storm), and these, together with direct infection of endothelium in some viruses, loosen endothelial junctions and increase permeability. Third, activated macrophages express tissue factor, which starts coagulation throughout the circulation. Platelets and clotting factors are consumed faster than they are made, which is consumptive coagulopathy (disseminated intravascular coagulation), and hepatocyte injury lowers clotting factor production further. The patient therefore leaks plasma and bleeds at the same time."},{"type":"flow","title":"Common pathway of hemorrhagic fever viruses","steps":["Virus infects macrophages, dendritic cells, and in some cases endothelium","Interferon blocked; replication spreads to liver, spleen, lymph nodes","Cytokine storm: TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 released","Endothelial junctions loosen: plasma leak, hypovolemia","Macrophage tissue factor triggers consumptive coagulopathy","Low platelets and factors, liver injury: bleeding","Shock and multiorgan failure"],"caption":"Plasma leak and coagulopathy develop together, so shock and bleeding coexist."},{"type":"h","text":"Filoviruses: Ebola and Marburg"},{"type":"p","text":"Ebola and Marburg viruses are filoviruses, enveloped, nonsegmented, negative-sense single-stranded RNA viruses with long filamentous virions. Fruit bats are the likely reservoir, and outbreaks begin when a person handles an infected animal or its tissues, or enters bat-inhabited caves or mines in the case of Marburg. They occur in sub-Saharan Africa. Spread between people is by direct contact with the blood, vomit, stool, or other body fluids of a symptomatic patient or a body after death, or with contaminated objects. Health care workers and people preparing bodies for burial are therefore at high risk, while ordinary airborne spread does not occur. Patients become infectious when symptoms start, viral load rises as illness worsens, and virus can persist in semen and other protected sites for months after recovery."},{"type":"p","text":"After an incubation of about 2 to 21 days, illness starts with sudden fever, weakness, myalgia, and headache, followed by profuse vomiting and diarrhea that cause severe fluid and electrolyte loss. A maculopapular rash, conjunctival injection, and bleeding from gums, venipuncture sites, or the gut may appear in the second week. Laboratory findings include thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, high aminotransferases, and evidence of coagulopathy and kidney injury. Case fatality is high: about 50 percent on average for Ebola, ranging widely between outbreaks, and similarly high for Marburg."},{"type":"table","title":"Viruses that cause hemorrhagic fever","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Transmission"],"rows":[["Yellow fever, dengue","Flavivirus; positive-sense ssRNA","Aedes mosquito"],["Ebola, Marburg","Filovirus; negative-sense ssRNA, filamentous","Body fluids; fruit bat reservoir"],["Lassa","Arenavirus; ambisense segmented ssRNA","Rodent urine and droppings"],["Hantavirus, Crimean-Congo","Bunyavirus order; negative-sense segmented ssRNA","Rodent excreta (hantavirus); ticks (Crimean-Congo)"]],"caption":"All are enveloped RNA viruses; only the first two rows are named in this competency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-18-11-C08","obj":"CV-18-K.8","title":"Viral myocarditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myocarditis is inflammation of the myocardium with injury to myocytes that is not caused by ischemia. Viruses are the most common cause in developed countries. Coxsackie B virus, an enterovirus (a nonenveloped positive-sense RNA picornavirus spread by the fecal-oral and respiratory routes), is the classic cause, and adenovirus, parvovirus B19, human herpesvirus 6, influenza, and SARS-CoV-2 are also important. The typical patient is a young adult, more often male, who develops cardiac symptoms one to a few weeks after a respiratory or gastrointestinal illness, because the virus first replicates in the pharynx or gut and then reaches the heart through the blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Coxsackie B and adenovirus enter myocytes through the coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR), a junctional protein, and replicate inside them. Injury occurs in overlapping phases. In the first days, viral replication directly damages myocytes. Over the following weeks, the immune response dominates: CD8 cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells destroy infected myocytes, and cytokines depress contractility. In some patients, inflammation persists or autoimmunity develops against cardiac proteins such as myosin, and progressive myocyte loss with fibrosis leaves a dilated cardiomyopathy. Parvovirus B19 differs in infecting coronary microvascular endothelium rather than myocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of viral myocarditis","steps":["Viral infection of pharynx or gut","Viremia; coxsackie B enters myocytes through CAR","Direct viral injury to myocytes","T-cell attack: lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis","Most recover; some develop persistent inflammation","Fibrosis and dilation: dilated cardiomyopathy"],"caption":"Most patients recover fully; the minority with persistent inflammation are at risk of chronic dilated cardiomyopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding traces to myocyte necrosis or inflammation. Necrotic myocytes release troponin. Inflamed tissue disturbs conduction and repolarization, so the ECG shows ST and T changes, AV or bundle branch block, or ventricular arrhythmias, and myocarditis is an important cause of sudden death in young athletes. When the pericardium is involved (myopericarditis), chest pain is pleuritic and the ECG may show diffuse ST elevation. Loss of contracting myocytes lowers ejection fraction and causes heart failure with dyspnea, fatigue, and edema. Because chest pain, ST elevation, and raised troponin can mimic infarction, the clue is a young patient with a recent viral illness and normal coronary arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"Endomyocardial biopsy shows an inflammatory infiltrate, mainly lymphocytes, with necrosis of adjacent myocytes in a pattern that is not ischemic. Because inflammation is patchy, a single biopsy can miss it. Cardiac MRI often shows edema and late gadolinium enhancement in the subepicardial or midwall layers, unlike the subendocardial or transmural pattern of infarction. Other histologic patterns point to different causes: many eosinophils suggest hypersensitivity or eosinophilic myocarditis, and multinucleated giant cells suggest the rapidly progressive giant cell myocarditis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 23-year-old has chest pain, ST elevation, and a troponin rise one week after a febrile diarrheal illness. Coronary angiography is normal. What would a biopsy most likely show, and why is the troponin raised?","explain":"A lymphocytic infiltrate with adjacent myocyte necrosis, from coxsackie B entering myocytes and cytotoxic T cells destroying them. Troponin rises because myocytes are dying, from inflammation rather than ischemia, which is why the coronary arteries are normal.","check":"The mechanism and biopsy paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology explain where each infection strikes. The vector and route decide who is exposed: triatomine bugs in Latin America, Ixodes ticks in the Northeast and upper Midwest, Dermacentor ticks in the southeastern and south-central states, Aedes mosquitoes in the tropics, and body fluids for filoviruses. The target cell decides the lesion: valve deposits for endocarditis, myocytes and enteric neurons for Trypanosoma cruzi, conduction tissue for Borrelia, endothelium for Rickettsia, hepatocytes for yellow fever, macrophages and endothelium for the hemorrhagic fever viruses, and CAR-bearing myocytes for coxsackie B. In several of these diseases the host response causes as much damage as the organism, through immune complexes in endocarditis, T cells in myocarditis and Chagas disease, antibody-dependent enhancement in dengue, and cytokines in hemorrhagic fevers."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology connect these infections to the rest of cardiology. Inflamed or scarred conduction tissue causes the AV block of Lyme carditis, Chagas disease, and perivalvular abscess, and myocyte loss leads to the dilated cardiomyopathy of Chagas disease and chronic myocarditis. Endothelial injury links rickettsial vasculitis and hemorrhagic fevers to the general mechanisms of edema and disseminated intravascular coagulation. The limits matter as well: classic signs such as the triad of spotted fever, Romana sign, or Osler nodes are often absent, so a question usually turns on exposure plus the mechanism, not on a single sign."}],"summary":["Endocarditis needs a vegetation site plus bacteremia: viridans streptococci adhere to sterile platelet-fibrin deposits on damaged valves, while Staphylococcus aureus can invade normal valves.","Expected organisms: S. aureus in acute and injection drug use (tricuspid) disease, viridans streptococci after dental work, S. epidermidis on early prosthetic valves, enterococci after GI or GU procedures, S. gallolyticus with colon cancer.","Endocarditis complications follow four mechanisms: valve destruction and ring abscess (AV block), emboli to systemic organs (left) or lungs (right), immune complex glomerulonephritis, and mycotic aneurysm.","Acute Chagas disease is usually mild with Romana sign or chagoma; years later about 20 to 30 percent develop dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction block and apical aneurysm, megaesophagus, and megacolon.","Lyme disease spreads in stages: erythema migrans; then facial palsy, meningitis, and carditis with fluctuating AV block; then large joint arthritis and late neurologic disease, mainly in the Northeast and upper Midwest.","Rocky Mountain spotted fever is endothelial infection and vasculitis: fever, headache, a rash moving from wrists and ankles to trunk with palms and soles, thrombocytopenia, edema, hyponatremia, and possible shock.","Yellow fever is a hepatitis with midzonal necrosis, Councilman bodies, jaundice, and black vomit; dengue causes severe myalgia and arthralgia, thrombocytopenia, and in severe cases plasma leak with rising hematocrit.","Hemorrhagic fever viruses share macrophage and endothelial infection, interferon evasion, cytokine storm, and consumptive coagulopathy; Ebola and Marburg spread by body fluids and kill about half of those infected.","Viral myocarditis follows a viral illness in a young adult; coxsackie B enters myocytes through CAR, and biopsy shows a lymphocytic infiltrate with myocyte necrosis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: infective endocarditis and myocarditis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases: rickettsial infections, Chagas disease, Lyme disease, and viral hemorrhagic fevers"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Rickettsia, Borrelia, flaviviruses, filoviruses, and medical parasitology"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders and immune complex disease"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious diseases: infective endocarditis"}]},"CV-2":{"unit":"CV-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01","title":"Histology of the Heart and Blood Vessels","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic structure of the heart wall, the cardiac muscle cell, the conducting and endocrine cells of the heart, the walls of each class of blood vessel, and the products of the endothelium. Part I questions rarely stop at identification; they ask what a layer or cell does, what happens when it fails, and which disease begins there. The organizing idea is that structure follows demand: the left ventricle is thick to generate systemic pressure, the fibrous skeleton is collagen to anchor valves and block current, the aorta is elastic to smooth pulsatile flow, arterioles are muscular to set resistance, and capillaries are thin to exchange.","prerequisites":["The four basic tissue types and the general organization of epithelium and connective tissue","Sarcomere structure and the sliding filament mechanism in striated muscle","Cell junctions: adherens junctions, desmosomes, tight junctions, and gap junctions","Gross anatomy of the heart chambers, valves, and great vessels (CV-3)","Second messenger signaling through cAMP, cGMP, and IP3 with Ca2+"],"connections":[{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"The insulating fibrous skeleton and fast Purkinje fibers explain AV nodal delay, a narrow QRS, and preexcitation through accessory pathways."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Elastic recoil of large arteries, arteriolar resistance, and venous compliance become the pressure, flow, and capacitance relationships of the circulation."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"The subendocardium is the layer most vulnerable to ischemia, and endothelial dysfunction starts the plaque that narrows epicardial coronary arteries."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"Atherosclerosis begins in the intima, cystic medial degeneration and dissection involve the media, and syphilitic aortitis begins in the vasa vasorum."},{"to":"Hematology and hemostasis","how":"Endothelial von Willebrand factor, thrombomodulin, and prostacyclin start the platelet and coagulation pathways, and ADAMTS13 deficiency causes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura."}],"terms":[{"term":"Endocardium","def":"The inner layer of the heart wall: endothelium on a thin connective tissue layer, with a subendocardial layer that carries vessels, nerves, and Purkinje fibers."},{"term":"Fibrous cardiac skeleton","def":"Dense irregular connective tissue forming the fibrous rings around the four valve orifices, the fibrous trigones, and the membranous septum."},{"term":"Central fibrous body","def":"The right fibrous trigone, the thickest part of the cardiac skeleton, which the bundle of His pierces to reach the ventricles."},{"term":"Intercalated disc","def":"The specialized end to end junction between cardiac muscle cells, containing fascia adherens, desmosomes, and gap junctions."},{"term":"Gap junction","def":"A cluster of connexon channels (connexin 43 in working ventricular muscle) that lets ions and small molecules pass directly between neighboring cells."},{"term":"Functional syncytium","def":"A tissue of separate cells that behaves electrically like a single cell because gap junctions spread depolarization from cell to cell."},{"term":"Diad","def":"The pairing of one T tubule with one terminal cistern of sarcoplasmic reticulum, located at the Z line in ventricular cardiac muscle."},{"term":"Purkinje fiber","def":"A large, pale, glycogen rich conducting cell with few myofibrils that lies in the subendocardium and rapidly distributes the impulse through the ventricles."},{"term":"Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","def":"A peptide hormone stored in granules of atrial muscle cells and released by atrial stretch, which promotes Na+ and water excretion and relaxes vascular smooth muscle."},{"term":"Internal elastic lamina","def":"A fenestrated sheet of elastin that forms the outer boundary of the tunica intima in arteries and is most distinct in muscular arteries."},{"term":"Vasa vasorum","def":"Small vessels in the adventitia and outer media of large vessels that supply the part of the wall too far from the lumen to be fed by diffusion."},{"term":"Weibel-Palade body","def":"A rod shaped endothelial storage granule containing von Willebrand factor and P-selectin that is released within minutes of endothelial activation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01-C01","obj":"CV-2.1","title":"Layers of the heart wall and the fibrous cardiac skeleton","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The heart wall layers match those of a vessel: endocardium (intima), myocardium (media), and epicardium (adventitia). The fibrous cardiac skeleton has no vascular counterpart."},{"type":"h","text":"Endocardium and valves"},{"type":"p","text":"The endocardium is a simple squamous endothelium, continuous with that of the great vessels, on thin connective tissue. Its deepest part, the subendocardial layer, carries small vessels, nerves, and the Purkinje fibers. Valve leaflets are folds of endocardium around an essentially avascular core: a collagenous fibrosa, continuous with the fibrous ring, bears the load, a proteoglycan rich spongiosa cushions flexion, and an elastic layer lies on the inflow side. Proteoglycan expansion of the spongiosa is the lesion of myxomatous mitral valve (prolapse), and vessels growing into scarred leaflets mark chronic rheumatic disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Myocardium"},{"type":"p","text":"Myocardial thickness follows chamber pressure: atria a few millimeters, right ventricle about 0.3 to 0.5 cm, left ventricle about 1.3 to 1.5 cm. By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness, so a thicker wall keeps the stress on each myocyte tolerable. Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) adds sarcomeres in parallel and causes concentric hypertrophy; volume overload (regurgitation) adds them in series and causes eccentric hypertrophy with a larger cavity. Hypertrophied myocytes have large, dark, rectangular boxcar nuclei."},{"type":"example","title":"Wall stress and the purpose of hypertrophy","text":"A left ventricle with a radius of 2.5 cm and a wall of 1.0 cm at 120 mmHg has stress proportional to (120 x 2.5) / (2 x 1.0) = 150 units. If aortic stenosis raises systolic pressure to 200 mmHg, stress rises to 250 units. Thickening the wall to about 1.7 cm restores it to about 147 units, but the thicker wall is stiffer and outgrows its capillary supply, predisposing to subendocardial ischemia. A dilated ventricle with a radius of 3.5 cm and a wall of 0.8 cm at 120 mmHg has a stress of about 263 units, so dilation without matching hypertrophy sharply raises the energy cost of each beat."},{"type":"p","text":"The inner third of the left ventricular wall, the subendocardium, is the layer most vulnerable to ischemia, so a subendocardial infarct can occur without total occlusion of an artery."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the subendocardium is injured first","steps":["Coronary arteries run in epicardial fat and penetrate inward","Systole raises intramural pressure most near the cavity","Inner wall vessels are compressed, so inner flow is mainly diastolic","Wall stress and O2 demand are highest in the inner layers","Hypotension, tachycardia, or hypertrophy drops supply below demand","Necrosis appears first in the inner third of the wall"],"caption":"The subendocardium is the watershed of the ventricular wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Epicardium"},{"type":"p","text":"The epicardium is the visceral serous pericardium: mesothelium over fat that carries the coronary vessels and nerves, hence epicardial coronary disease. Roughened mesothelium in pericarditis produces a friction rub."},{"type":"h","text":"Fibrous cardiac skeleton"},{"type":"p","text":"The skeleton is dense irregular connective tissue, mainly type I collagen: four fibrous rings around the valve orifices, the right and left fibrous trigones, and the membranous interventricular septum. The right trigone, the thickest part, is the central fibrous body. It anchors the valves and myocardial sheets and completely separates atrial from ventricular muscle. Collagen has no gap junctions, so atrial depolarization stops at the rings. The only normal crossing is the bundle of His, which leaves the AV node, pierces the central fibrous body, and runs along the membranous septum, so every impulse passes the slow AV node and its delay (CV-7)."},{"type":"p","text":"A strand of myocardium crossing a ring forms an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) that bypasses AV delay, giving a short PR interval, a delta wave, and reentrant tachycardia (Wolff-Parkinson-White pattern). Because the His bundle lies beside the aortic ring and membranous septum, an aortic valve ring abscess in infective endocarditis, ring calcification, or surgery there can cause new AV block."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01-C02","obj":"CV-2.2","title":"Cardiac muscle cells compared with skeletal muscle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cardiomyocytes contain the same sarcomeres as skeletal muscle and are striated, but they differ in shape, junctions, and Ca2+ handling because the heart must contract as one unit, beat for a lifetime, and grade its force without recruiting more cells."},{"type":"p","text":"A ventricular myocyte is a short branched cylinder, about 10 to 20 micrometers wide and 80 to 100 micrometers long, with one or two central nuclei. A clear perinuclear zone holds the Golgi apparatus, glycogen, and lipofuscin, a wear and tear pigment that accumulates with age. Mitochondria fill about a third or more of the cell, with close to one capillary per myocyte, because the heart makes nearly all its ATP by oxidative phosphorylation, mainly of fatty acids. Contraction stops within about a minute of complete ischemia, and irreversible injury begins after roughly 20 to 40 minutes."},{"type":"h","text":"Intercalated discs"},{"type":"p","text":"Myocytes join end to end at intercalated discs, dark stepped lines across the fiber. The transverse portion, at the level of a Z line, holds the mechanical junctions; the lateral portion holds most of the gap junctions."},{"type":"table","title":"Junctions of the intercalated disc","cols":["Junction","Structure","Function"],"rows":[["Fascia adherens","Cadherins linked to actin of the last sarcomere","Transmits contractile pull to the next cell; replaces the Z line at the cell end"],["Desmosome","Desmosomal cadherins linked to desmin through plakoglobin, plakophilin, and desmoplakin","Keeps cells from pulling apart under repeated stress"],["Gap junction","Connexons of connexin 43 in ventricular muscle","Passes ions between cells, making the myocardium a functional syncytium"]],"caption":"Two junctions couple cells mechanically and one couples them electrically."},{"type":"p","text":"Gap junctions let depolarizing current flow from cell to cell, so excitation crosses a chamber in a fraction of a second and travels faster along the fiber axis than across it. At the border of an old infarct or in a failing ventricle, gap junctions are reduced or shifted to the lateral cell borders, which slows conduction and creates a substrate for reentry. Mutations in desmosomal proteins such as plakophilin 2 or desmoplakin cause arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy: myocytes detach under stretch and are replaced by fibrofatty tissue, mostly in the right ventricle, causing ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death in young people. Naxos disease, a recessive plakoglobin defect, adds woolly hair and thickened palm and sole skin because the same desmosomal protein holds the epidermis together."},{"type":"h","text":"Ca2+ handling and repair"},{"type":"p","text":"In cardiac muscle the L-type Ca2+ channel of the T tubule admits extracellular Ca2+ during the plateau, and that Ca2+ opens ryanodine receptor 2 in the adjacent cistern (calcium induced calcium release). Because force depends on Ca2+ entry and SR loading, it can be graded, and the long refractory period prevents tetanus (CV-5). Atrial myocytes have few T tubules. Adult cardiomyocytes are essentially postmitotic and have no satellite cell reserve, so the heart adapts by hypertrophy and heals infarcts with collagen scar."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardiac muscle compared with skeletal muscle","cols":["Feature","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"rows":[["Cells and nuclei","Short, branched; one or two central nuclei","Long, unbranched; many peripheral nuclei"],["Coupling","Intercalated discs; functional syncytium","No junctions between fibers; each has its own motor ending"],["T tubules","Wide, at the Z line; diad with one cistern","Narrow, at the A and I band junction; triad with two cisternae"],["Trigger for SR release","Ca2+ entry through L-type channels opens ryanodine receptor 2","Voltage sensor mechanically linked to ryanodine receptor 1; no extracellular Ca2+ needed"],["Tetanus","Not possible","Possible"],["Regeneration","Minimal; heals by scar","Satellite cells allow repair"]],"caption":"Every cardiac feature serves continuous, coordinated, gradable contraction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has greatly reduced ventricular connexin 43 but normal desmosomes and fascia adherens. Would the cells pull apart, and how would excitation spread?","explain":"The cells stay attached because adhesion depends on the desmosomes and fascia adherens. Excitation spreads slowly and irregularly because current passes between myocytes mainly through gap junctions, a substrate for reentrant arrhythmias.","check":"The table of intercalated disc junctions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01-C03","obj":"CV-2.3","title":"Purkinje fibers and atrial natriuretic peptide granules","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The conducting cells and the atrial endocrine cells are modified cardiomyocytes that trade contractile machinery for speed or hormone secretion, and their histology shows the trade."},{"type":"h","text":"Purkinje fibers"},{"type":"p","text":"Purkinje fibers form a network in the subendocardium of both ventricles, continuous with the bundle branches. They are larger than working myocytes, with pale glycogen rich cytoplasm (PAS positive), a few myofibrils pushed to the periphery, and one or two central nuclei often ringed by a clear halo. Their large diameter lowers internal resistance to longitudinal current, abundant gap junctions lower resistance between cells, and a large fast Na+ current drives depolarization. Conduction is therefore the fastest in the heart, about 1.5 to 4 m/s, so the ventricles depolarize almost together and the normal QRS is narrow."},{"type":"p","text":"Glycogen supports anaerobic glycolysis, which is thought to help Purkinje fibers tolerate ischemia; together with diffusion from cavity blood this lets surviving fibers at the inner edge of an infarct become a source of ectopic beats. Purkinje fibers also pace at about 20 to 40 beats per minute, the wide complex ventricular escape rhythm (CV-7). The cerebellar Purkinje neuron is an unrelated cell and a common matching trap."},{"type":"table","title":"Purkinje fibers, working myocytes, and nodal cells","cols":["Feature","Purkinje fiber","Working ventricular myocyte","SA or AV nodal cell"],"rows":[["Size and staining","Largest; pale, PAS positive","Intermediate; eosinophilic, densely striated","Smallest; pale, in dense connective tissue"],["Myofibrils","Few, peripheral","Abundant","Few"],["Gap junctions","Abundant","Abundant","Sparse"],["Conduction velocity","About 1.5 to 4 m/s","About 0.3 to 0.5 m/s","About 0.05 m/s in the AV node"],["Main role","Rapid distribution of the impulse","Force","Pacemaking and AV delay"]],"caption":"Purkinje fibers are built for speed and nodal cells for pacing and delay. Velocities are approximate."},{"type":"h","text":"Atrial granules and natriuretic peptides"},{"type":"p","text":"Electron micrographs of atrial myocytes, most numerous in the right atrium, show membrane bound dense granules near the nucleus and Golgi apparatus. They store atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), released when increased volume or pressure stretches the atrium, which makes the heart an endocrine organ. Ventricular myocytes make B type natriuretic peptide (BNP) mainly on demand in response to wall stretch rather than storing it in prominent granules, which is why plasma BNP or NT-proBNP rises in heart failure."},{"type":"p","text":"ANP binds natriuretic peptide receptor A, which has its own guanylyl cyclase domain, so its second messenger is cGMP. It dilates the afferent and constricts the efferent arteriole to raise GFR, inhibits collecting duct Na+ reabsorption, suppresses renin and aldosterone, and relaxes vascular smooth muscle."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Atrial stretch and natriuretic peptide feedback","steps":["Blood volume or atrial pressure rises","Atrial myocytes stretch and release ANP from granules","ANP binds natriuretic peptide receptor A and raises cGMP","GFR rises; Na+ reabsorption, renin, and aldosterone fall","Na+ and water loss lowers volume and atrial stretch"],"caption":"The effect of ANP removes the stimulus that released it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A ventricular section shows large pale PAS positive cells with peripheral myofibrils just beneath the endothelium. A right atrial section shows striated cells with small dense granules beside the nucleus. Name each cell and its job.","explain":"The first are Purkinje fibers, built for fast conduction. The second are atrial myocytes storing ANP, released with atrial stretch.","check":"The Purkinje comparison table and the paragraph on atrial granules."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01-C04","obj":"CV-2.4","title":"Wall structure of arteries, capillaries, and veins","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arteries and veins have an intima (endothelium and thin subendothelial tissue, bounded in arteries by the internal elastic lamina), a media of smooth muscle and the matrix it secretes, and a mainly collagenous adventitia carrying nerves and, in large vessels, the vasa vasorum, which feed the outer wall beyond the reach of luminal diffusion and are more plentiful in veins."},{"type":"table","title":"Wall structure and function of each vessel class","cols":["Vessel","Distinguishing wall features","Main function"],"rows":[["Elastic artery (aorta, pulmonary trunk, major branches)","Thick intima; media of about 40 to 70 fenestrated elastic lamellae","Stores energy in systole, recoils in diastole"],["Muscular artery (coronary, radial, renal)","Sharp wavy internal elastic lamina; thick smooth muscle media, up to about 40 layers","Distributes flow among organs"],["Arteriole","One or two smooth muscle layers; elastic lamina only in larger ones","Main resistance; controls capillary inflow"],["Capillary","Endothelium and basal lamina with pericytes only","Exchange"],["Postcapillary venule","Endothelium, pericytes, loose junctions","Leukocyte emigration and inflammatory leak"],["Medium vein","Thin media; intimal folds form valves","Low pressure return"],["Large vein","Thin media; adventitia thickest, with longitudinal muscle","Capacitance reservoir"]],"caption":"Arteries are built around the media; large veins are built around the adventitia."},{"type":"h","text":"Arteries and arterioles"},{"type":"p","text":"In systole the aorta stretches and stores ejection energy; in diastole it recoils, maintaining diastolic pressure and nearly continuous distal flow (the windkessel effect)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Elastic recoil of the aorta","steps":["Left ventricle ejects a stroke volume into the aorta","Elastic lamellae stretch and store ejection energy","Systolic pressure rises less than in a rigid tube","Aortic valve closes; the stretched wall recoils","Diastolic pressure and distal flow are maintained"],"caption":"Loss of elastin raises systolic pressure, lowers diastolic pressure, and widens pulse pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"With age, elastin fragments and is replaced by collagen, causing isolated systolic hypertension. In Marfan syndrome, defective fibrillin 1 weakens elastin, producing cystic medial degeneration, aortic root dilation, and dissection, which splits the media. In tertiary syphilis, obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum makes the outer media ischemic, and the ascending aorta becomes aneurysmal with a tree bark intima. Atherosclerosis affects the intima of elastic and muscular arteries, whereas Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis calcifies the internal elastic lamina and media of muscular arteries, giving pipestem calcification without narrowing the lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"Arterioles (generally under about 0.1 mm) are the main resistance vessels because resistance varies with the inverse fourth power of radius: a 20% narrowing raises resistance about 2.4 times. Pressure falls more across them than anywhere else, from about 85 to about 30 to 35 mmHg. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis (hypertension, diabetes) deposits leaked plasma protein in the wall, and hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis of severe hypertension adds concentric onion skin smooth muscle; both narrow the lumen and reduce renal perfusion."},{"type":"h","text":"Capillaries, venules, and veins"},{"type":"p","text":"Capillaries are about 5 to 10 micrometers wide, and their huge total cross section slows flow for exchange."},{"type":"table","title":"The three types of capillary","cols":["Type","Wall","Locations","What passes"],"rows":[["Continuous","Unbroken endothelium and basal lamina; transport vesicles","Muscle, lung, connective tissue, nervous system","Water, gases, small solutes; tight junctions form the blood brain barrier"],["Fenestrated","Pores about 60 to 80 nm, usually with a diaphragm; continuous basal lamina","Endocrine glands, intestinal villi, choroid plexus, kidney","Fluid, hormones, nutrients; glomerular pores lack diaphragms"],["Sinusoidal","Wide lumen, large gaps, discontinuous basal lamina","Liver, spleen, bone marrow","Large proteins and whole cells"]],"caption":"Permeability rises from continuous to fenestrated to sinusoidal."},{"type":"p","text":"Postcapillary venules are where leukocytes emigrate and where histamine and bradykinin open endothelial gaps, so inflammatory edema begins there; high endothelial venules admit lymphocytes to lymph nodes. Veins are thin walled and compliant and hold about 60% to 70% of blood volume. Limb vein valves, with the skeletal muscle pump, keep flow toward the heart; valves are absent from the venae cavae, portal system, and dural sinuses. Incompetent valves raise leg venous pressure and cause varicose veins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-02-01-C05","obj":"CV-2.5","title":"Endothelial products and endothelial dysfunction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial products control tone, platelets, coagulation, and leukocyte traffic. In health they favor relaxation and fluidity."},{"type":"h","text":"Nitric oxide"},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) makes NO and L-citrulline from L-arginine and O2, using NADPH and tetrahydrobiopterin. Besides relaxing smooth muscle, NO inhibits platelet adhesion and aggregation, leukocyte adhesion, and smooth muscle proliferation. Superoxide and hemoglobin destroy it within seconds, so its action is local. Increased flow raises shear stress and releases NO, so the upstream artery widens (flow mediated dilation)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nitric oxide and vascular relaxation","steps":["Shear stress, acetylcholine, or bradykinin act on endothelium","Ca2+ with calmodulin, and phosphorylation, activate eNOS","NO diffuses into the underlying smooth muscle","Soluble guanylyl cyclase raises cGMP; protein kinase G activates","Ca2+ falls and myosin light chain phosphatase activates","Smooth muscle relaxes and the vessel dilates"],"caption":"The same NO and cGMP signal inhibits platelets."},{"type":"example","title":"The acetylcholine paradox","text":"Acetylcholine relaxes an artery with intact endothelium but contracts one whose endothelium has been rubbed away. In the intact vessel it binds endothelial M3 receptors, raises endothelial Ca2+, activates eNOS, and NO relaxes the muscle, outweighing its direct effect. Without endothelium it reaches only smooth muscle M3 receptors, which act through Gq, IP3, and Ca2+ to cause contraction. Infused into a human coronary artery, acetylcholine dilates a healthy vessel but constricts one with atherosclerosis or risk factor driven dysfunction, revealing lost bioavailable NO."},{"type":"h","text":"Prostacyclin, endothelin-1, and Weibel-Palade bodies"},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial prostacyclin opposes platelet thromboxane A2. Aspirin irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase; anucleate platelets cannot replace it, but endothelial cells can, so low doses shift the balance toward prostacyclin. Endothelin-1, one of the most potent endogenous vasoconstrictors, is cleaved from big endothelin by endothelin converting enzyme and mainly secreted as it is made. Hypoxia, angiotensin II, and thrombin increase it; NO, prostacyclin, and laminar shear suppress it, and endothelial ETB receptors release NO and clear it. It is increased in pulmonary arterial hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"Weibel-Palade bodies are rod shaped endothelial granules of von Willebrand factor (vWF) with P-selectin in their membrane, released within minutes by thrombin, histamine, epinephrine, or vasopressin acting on V2 receptors. vWF bridges exposed collagen to platelet glycoprotein Ib, essential at high shear, and carries factor VIII, so severe vWF deficiency lowers factor VIII. ADAMTS13 trims ultralarge vWF multimers; its deficiency lets them aggregate platelets in small vessels, causing thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura. Preformed P-selectin starts leukocyte rolling within minutes, whereas E-selectin is synthesized over hours after interleukin 1 or tumor necrosis factor."},{"type":"table","title":"Main endothelial products","cols":["Product","Mechanism","Effect"],"rows":[["Nitric oxide","Soluble guanylyl cyclase, cGMP","Relaxation; inhibits platelets and leukocyte adhesion"],["Prostacyclin (PGI2)","IP receptor, Gs, cAMP","Relaxation; inhibits platelet aggregation"],["Endothelin-1","ETA receptor, Gq, IP3 and Ca2+","Strong sustained constriction; smooth muscle growth"],["von Willebrand factor","Bridges collagen to platelet GPIb; carries factor VIII","Platelet adhesion"],["P-selectin","Binds sialyl Lewis X on leukocytes","Leukocyte rolling"],["Thrombomodulin","Binds thrombin, which then activates protein C","Anticoagulant"],["Heparan sulfate","Enhances antithrombin","Anticoagulant"],["Tissue plasminogen activator","Converts plasminogen to plasmin","Fibrinolytic"]],"caption":"Opposing products balance tone and clotting."},{"type":"h","text":"Endothelial dysfunction"},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial dysfunction is a shift in this balance, with reduced bioavailable NO as its hallmark. Hypertension, diabetes, smoking, oxidized LDL, hyperhomocysteinemia, and aging raise oxidative stress; superoxide destroys NO, and loss of tetrahydrobiopterin uncouples eNOS so it makes superoxide. ET-1, adhesion molecules (VCAM-1, ICAM-1, selectins), tissue factor, and PAI-1 rise and thrombomodulin falls. The vessel dilates poorly or constricts paradoxically, the surface becomes prothrombotic (the endothelial injury of Virchow's triad), and monocytes and LDL enter the intima. Low, disturbed shear at branch points and curves explains where plaques form."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self reinforcing cycle of endothelial dysfunction","steps":["Risk factors raise endothelial oxidative stress","Superoxide destroys NO and eNOS uncouples","Adhesion molecules, ET-1, and LDL entry increase","Monocytes and oxidized LDL build an intimal lesion","The lesion generates more reactive oxygen species"],"caption":"Each stage feeds the next, driving progression toward atherosclerosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy explain placement. The endocardium is continuous with vascular endothelium because both line the primitive heart tube, and the AV rings form as connective tissue of the atrioventricular groove separates atrial from ventricular muscle; incomplete separation leaves an accessory pathway. Coronary arteries run in epicardial fat and penetrate inward, linking gross anatomy to subendocardial vulnerability. Wall structure maps onto hemodynamics (compliance, resistance, exchange, capacitance), and endothelial signals use familiar second messengers: cGMP for NO and natriuretic peptides, cAMP for prostacyclin, and IP3 with Ca2+ for endothelin-1."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology reads as damage to a layer: atherosclerosis in the intima, cystic medial degeneration and Monckeberg calcification in the media, syphilitic aortitis through the adventitial vasa vasorum, and desmosomal or gap junction defects in the myocardium. Postcapillary venule selectins link the vessel wall to every acute inflammatory response. A key limit is that light microscopy shows structure, not function: a normal looking endothelium can be profoundly dysfunctional, which is detected by its response to flow or acetylcholine."}],"summary":["The heart wall has an endocardium with a subendocardial layer carrying Purkinje fibers, a myocardium whose thickness follows chamber pressure, and an epicardium carrying the coronary vessels in fat.","Wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness, so pressure overload causes concentric hypertrophy and dilation without thickening sharply raises myocyte stress.","The subendocardium is injured first in ischemia because its supply arrives last, its vessels are compressed in systole, and its wall stress is highest.","The collagenous fibrous skeleton anchors the valves and insulates atria from ventricles, so the bundle of His through the central fibrous body is the only normal electrical link.","Cardiac myocytes are branched, with central nuclei, abundant mitochondria, diads at the Z line, and intercalated discs whose fascia adherens and desmosomes couple force and whose connexin 43 gap junctions make a functional syncytium.","Adult cardiomyocytes are essentially postmitotic, so the heart adapts by hypertrophy and heals infarcts by scar.","Purkinje fibers are large, pale, glycogen rich subendocardial cells with sparse myofibrils built for the fastest conduction; atrial myocytes store ANP, which acts through cGMP to promote natriuresis and vasodilation.","Elastic arteries recoil in diastole, muscular arteries distribute flow, and arterioles set resistance through the inverse fourth power of radius.","Capillary permeability rises from continuous to fenestrated to sinusoidal; veins are compliant capacitance vessels, and postcapillary venules are the site of leukocyte emigration.","Endothelial NO and prostacyclin relax vessels and inhibit platelets, endothelin-1 constricts, and Weibel-Palade bodies release vWF and P-selectin; endothelial dysfunction tips the balance toward constriction, thrombosis, and atherosclerosis."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System; Muscle Tissue"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Overview of the Circulation; Vascular Distensibility and Functions of the Arterial and Venous Systems; The Microcirculation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Arteries and veins; the microcirculation; regulation of vascular tone by the endothelium"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels; The Heart"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, heart wall and fibrous skeleton of the heart"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nitric oxide synthesis and eicosanoid metabolism"}]},"CV-3":{"unit":"CV-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01","title":"Heart, Great Vessels, and Pericardium","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the heart in the chest and works inward: its position and the chambers that form each surface, border, and radiographic contour; the front to back order of the chambers; the pericardium, its sinuses, and the nerve that carries its pain; the great vessels and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve; the internal features of each chamber; and the anatomy of blunt and penetrating chest trauma. Board questions rarely ask for a bare name. They describe a finding, such as hoarseness in mitral stenosis, shoulder pain in pericarditis, or hypotension with distended neck veins after a stab wound, and expect you to name the structure and explain the mechanism from position, embryology, and pressure in a closed space.","prerequisites":["Surface landmarks of the thoracic wall and the divisions of the mediastinum at the sternal angle (T4 to T5 disc)","Heart tube looping, atrial septation, and the fate of the pharyngeal arch arteries (CV-1)","Histology of serous membranes and of the heart and vessel wall (CV-2)","Normal systemic and pulmonary pressures and the relation between pressure, volume, and compliance","The cervical plexus, the phrenic nerve, and the dermatomes of the neck and shoulder"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiovascular embryology (CV-1)","how":"Looping of the heart tube explains why the right ventricle faces forward, and the sixth arch artery explains why the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"The SA node lies at the top of the crista terminalis and the right bundle branch runs in the moderator band, so internal chamber anatomy locates the conduction system."},{"to":"Coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"The coronary arteries are the only branches of the ascending aorta and run in the grooves that mark the chamber boundaries described here."},{"to":"Pericardial disease (CV-18-F)","how":"Pericarditis, effusion, tamponade, and constriction are all explained by the layers, innervation, and stiffness of the pericardium."},{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Mitral stenosis enlarges the left atrium, which then compresses the posterior structures described in this chapter."}],"terms":[{"term":"Middle mediastinum","def":"The part of the inferior mediastinum that contains the pericardium, the heart, and the roots of the great vessels."},{"term":"Base of the heart","def":"The posterior surface of the heart, formed mainly by the left atrium, opposite the apex."},{"term":"Cardiothoracic ratio","def":"The widest transverse diameter of the heart divided by the widest internal diameter of the thorax on a posteroanterior chest radiograph; normally less than 0.5."},{"term":"Fibrous pericardium","def":"The tough, inelastic outer sac of the pericardium, fused to the central tendon of the diaphragm and to the adventitia of the great vessels."},{"term":"Serous pericardium","def":"A closed mesothelial sac with a parietal layer lining the fibrous pericardium and a visceral layer (the epicardium) covering the heart."},{"term":"Transverse pericardial sinus","def":"The passage within the pericardial cavity behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the superior vena cava."},{"term":"Oblique pericardial sinus","def":"A blind recess of the pericardial cavity behind the left atrium, bounded by the reflections around the pulmonary veins and inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Ligamentum arteriosum","def":"The fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus, joining the origin of the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch."},{"term":"Aortic isthmus","def":"The short segment of the aorta between the origin of the left subclavian artery and the attachment of the ligamentum arteriosum."},{"term":"Crista terminalis","def":"A muscular ridge in the right atrium that separates the smooth sinus venarum from the rough wall bearing pectinate muscles."},{"term":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","def":"A muscular bundle crossing the right ventricle from the interventricular septum to the anterior papillary muscle, carrying part of the right bundle branch."},{"term":"Pulsus paradoxus","def":"An inspiratory fall in systolic arterial pressure of more than 10 mmHg, an exaggeration of the normal small inspiratory fall."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C01","obj":"CV-3.1","title":"Position of the heart and the chambers that form its surfaces and borders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The transverse thoracic plane, through the sternal angle and the T4 to T5 disc, divides the mediastinum into superior and inferior parts, and the pericardium divides the inferior part into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The middle mediastinum holds the pericardium, the heart, and the roots of the great vessels. Behind it, the posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus and descending aorta, the neighbors that matter most for the posterior chamber."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart is rotated. Its base faces backward and to the right, its apex points forward, downward, and to the left, and about two thirds of it lies left of the median plane. Its right side faces forward, so the right ventricle, not the left, forms most of the front, the adult result of looping of the heart tube (CV-1)."},{"type":"p","text":"On the chest wall, the outline runs from the left second costal cartilage to the right third cartilage (superior border), down to the right sixth cartilage just beside the sternum (right border), across to the left fifth intercostal space (inferior border), and back up to the left second cartilage (left border). The apex lies behind the left fifth intercostal space at or just medial to the midclavicular line, about 9 cm from the midline. Left ventricular contraction pushes it against the chest wall in early systole, producing the apex beat, normally no larger than one intercostal space."},{"type":"table","title":"Chambers that form each surface, border, and radiographic contour","cols":["Surface, border, or contour","Formed by"],"rows":[["Anterior (sternocostal) surface","Mainly right ventricle; right atrium on the right, left ventricle along the left edge"],["Inferior (diaphragmatic) surface","Mainly left ventricle; some right ventricle"],["Base (posterior surface)","Mainly left atrium; some right atrium"],["Apex","Left ventricle"],["Right border","Right atrium, between the two venae cavae"],["Left border","Left ventricle, with the left auricle near the top"],["Inferior border","Right ventricle, with the left ventricle near the apex"],["PA radiograph, right contour (top to bottom)","Superior vena cava, right atrium"],["PA radiograph, left contour (top to bottom)","Aortic knob, pulmonary trunk, left auricle, left ventricle"],["Lateral radiograph","Right ventricle anteriorly behind the lower sternum; left atrium above and left ventricle below posteriorly"]],"caption":"The base is the posterior surface, not the upper border. The right ventricle and left atrium form no PA contour because they are the most anterior and most posterior chambers, so they are judged on the lateral view."},{"type":"p","text":"A posteroanterior (PA) radiograph shows the heart edge only where it touches air-filled lung. The cardiothoracic ratio, the widest cardiac diameter divided by the widest internal thoracic diameter, is normally below 0.5 on a PA film in full inspiration. An anteroposterior (AP) portable film magnifies the heart because it lies farther from the detector, and a shallow breath raises the diaphragm and widens the shadow, so both can mimic enlargement."},{"type":"p","text":"Enlargement of one chamber changes the contour it forms. A dilated left ventricle, as in chronic mitral regurgitation, pushes the left border down and out and moves the apex beat lateral to the midclavicular line, often into the sixth intercostal space, raising the ratio above 0.5. Right atrial enlargement bulges the lower right border. Enlargement of the right ventricle and left atrium shows only indirectly (section C02)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C02","obj":"CV-3.2","title":"Front to back order of the chambers and its clinical consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"From front to back the chambers lie in this order: right ventricle, then right atrium and left ventricle, then left atrium. The right ventricle sits directly behind the sternum and the left fourth and fifth costal cartilages. The left atrium is the most posterior chamber, separated from the esophagus only by the pericardium and the oblique pericardial sinus (section C03), with the descending aorta behind and to its left and the tracheal bifurcation and left main bronchus just above it."},{"type":"h","text":"The anterior right ventricle"},{"type":"p","text":"A blade entering just left of the sternum meets the right ventricle first, so it is the chamber most often injured by penetrating trauma (section C06). When the right ventricle hypertrophies or dilates, as in pulmonary hypertension or pulmonary stenosis, its contraction lifts the left parasternal chest wall (a parasternal lift or heave), and on a lateral film it fills the clear space behind the lower sternum."},{"type":"h","text":"The posterior left atrium"},{"type":"p","text":"The left atrium enlarges under a sustained pressure or volume load, classically mitral stenosis and chronic mitral regurgitation. It cannot expand forward past the ventricles, so it pushes backward and upward. It compresses the esophagus from the front, which causes dysphagia for solids and an indentation on the front of the barium-filled esophagus. It lifts the left main bronchus and widens the carinal angle. Together with the dilated pulmonary artery of secondary pulmonary hypertension, it can stretch the left recurrent laryngeal nerve under the aortic arch and cause hoarseness (section C04). On the PA film the enlarged left auricle straightens the left border below the pulmonary trunk, and the atrium itself projects to the right as a second curved edge, a double density."},{"type":"flow","title":"How mitral stenosis produces symptoms outside the heart","steps":["Narrowed mitral valve raises left atrial pressure","Left atrium dilates over months to years","Atrium can expand only backward and upward","Compressed esophagus causes dysphagia","Left main bronchus is lifted and the carina widens","Stretched left recurrent laryngeal nerve causes hoarseness"],"caption":"The symptoms come from the neighbors of the left atrium. The same enlargement slows flow and favors atrial fibrillation and thrombus in the left auricle."},{"type":"p","text":"The same position is the basis of transesophageal echocardiography. A probe in the esophagus lies millimeters from the left atrium with no lung or chest wall between, so it images the left atrium and left auricle far better than a chest wall probe and is the preferred way to look for auricular thrombus."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from symptoms to the left atrium","text":"A 42-year-old woman with past rheumatic fever has exertional breathlessness, dysphagia for solids, and hoarseness. Her film shows a straight left heart border, a double density on the right, and an elevated left main bronchus. Step 1: the esophagus, left recurrent laryngeal nerve, and carina all lie behind or just above the left atrium, so the atrium is enlarged. Step 2: the straight border is the enlarged left auricle, and the double density is the atrium projecting right behind the right atrium. Step 3: rheumatic fever most often scars the mitral valve, and mitral stenosis raises left atrial pressure. Step 4: that pressure passes back through the pulmonary veins to the pulmonary capillaries and increases filtration, causing breathlessness. One lesion explains every finding through the position of the left atrium."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C03","obj":"CV-3.3","title":"Pericardium, pericardial sinuses, and referred pericardial pain","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Fibrous and serous layers"},{"type":"p","text":"The fibrous pericardium is a tough sac of dense connective tissue fused below to the central tendon of the diaphragm, blended above with the adventitia of the great vessels, and tied to the sternum by sternopericardial ligaments. It anchors the heart, moves it with the diaphragm, and is nearly inelastic over short periods, so it prevents sudden overdistension but also lets rapidly entering fluid compress the heart (section C06)."},{"type":"p","text":"The serous pericardium is a closed sac of mesothelium (CV-2). Its parietal layer lines the fibrous sac and reflects around the great vessels to become the visceral layer, the epicardium. Between them lies the pericardial cavity, a potential space holding about 15 to 50 mL of serous fluid that lets the surfaces glide. Like a fist pushed into an underinflated balloon, the heart lies outside the cavity, covered by one wall of it."},{"type":"h","text":"Reflections and sinuses"},{"type":"p","text":"The serous layer reflects in two separate sleeves: one around the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, the other around the venae cavae and pulmonary veins. Two recesses form between and behind them. The transverse sinus arises when the dorsal mesocardium breaks down during looping (CV-1); a finger passed through it isolates the great arteries from the venous side. The oblique sinus is a blind pouch behind the left atrium, closed above and at the sides by the venous reflection, and its back wall lies against the esophagus."},{"type":"table","title":"Transverse versus oblique pericardial sinus","cols":["Feature","Transverse sinus","Oblique sinus"],"rows":[["Location","Behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, in front of the superior vena cava","Behind the left atrium"],["Boundaries","Arterial reflection in front, venous reflection behind","Reflections around the pulmonary veins and inferior vena cava"],["Shape","A passage open at both ends","A blind pouch open only inferiorly"],["Origin","Breakdown of the dorsal mesocardium","Folding of the venous reflection as the pulmonary veins are incorporated into the left atrium"],["Practical importance","Allows the great arteries to be isolated from the veins during surgery","Lies between the left atrium and the esophagus"]],"caption":"The transverse sinus is behind the arteries, and the oblique sinus is behind the left atrium."},{"type":"h","text":"Blood supply, nerves, and referred pain"},{"type":"p","text":"The pericardium is supplied mainly by the pericardiacophrenic arteries from the internal thoracic arteries, and the epicardium by the coronary arteries. The phrenic nerves (C3 to C5) descend between the fibrous pericardium and mediastinal pleura, in front of the lung roots, and carry pain from the fibrous and parietal layers; the visceral layer is largely insensitive. Vagal and sympathetic fibers also reach the pericardium but do not explain its characteristic pain. Because the supraclavicular nerves (C3 and C4) bring skin input from the shoulder to the same segments, pericarditis and diaphragmatic irritation refer pain to the trapezius ridge."},{"type":"flow","title":"Referral of pericardial pain to the shoulder","steps":["Inflammation irritates the parietal pericardium","Phrenic nerve afferents carry the signal","Fibers enter the cord at C3 to C5","Same dorsal horn neurons receive shoulder skin input (supraclavicular nerves)","Brain attributes the signal to the skin","Pain is felt at the trapezius ridge and shoulder"],"caption":"Referral depends on shared spinal segments, not on any direct connection between the pericardium and the shoulder."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic myocardial pain differs because it travels with sympathetic afferents to about T1 to T5 and is felt as central chest pressure spreading to the medial left arm or jaw, with little change on breathing or posture. Pericardial pain is sharp, worse on deep inspiration and lying flat, and often eased by sitting forward. The inspiratory worsening reflects movement against the neighboring pleura; the postural effect is less certain. Inflamed serous surfaces rubbing together produce a scratchy friction rub with up to three components (atrial systole, ventricular systole, rapid filling)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C04","obj":"CV-3.4","title":"The great vessels and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ascending aorta lies inside the pericardium, so a tear there causes tamponade (section C06), and the pulmonary trunk spirals around it, reflecting the spiral aorticopulmonary septum (CV-1). When a mediastinal tumor or nodes obstruct the superior vena cava (SVC), the face, neck, and arms swell and chest wall veins distend as collaterals reach the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"table","title":"The great vessels","cols":["Vessel","Begins","Ends","Key relation"],"rows":[["Superior vena cava","Brachiocephalic veins, behind right first costal cartilage","Right atrium at right third costal cartilage","Right of ascending aorta; receives azygos at about T4"],["Inferior vena cava","Common iliac veins","Lower right atrium","Pierces central tendon at about T8"],["Pulmonary veins (usually four)","Each lung","Posterior left atrium","Number varies; bound the oblique sinus"],["Pulmonary trunk","Conus arteriosus","Under the arch at sternal angle level","Right artery passes behind ascending aorta and SVC"],["Ascending aorta","Aortic valve","Arch at sternal angle","Only branches are the coronary arteries"],["Aortic arch","Behind right second sternocostal joint","Left side of T4 to T5 disc","Branches right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian"]],"caption":"The left brachiocephalic vein crosses in front of the arch branches."},{"type":"p","text":"The commonest arch variation is a shared origin of the brachiocephalic trunk and left common carotid. A less common but tested variation is an aberrant right subclavian artery that arises last from the arch and passes behind the esophagus; it can cause dysphagia, and on that side the laryngeal nerve does not recur."},{"type":"h","text":"The ligamentum arteriosum and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"type":"p","text":"The fetal ductus arteriosus, from the distal left sixth arch artery, shunts right ventricular output from the left pulmonary artery to the aorta. It closes within a day or two of birth and fibroses into the ligamentum arteriosum, running from the origin of the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the arch just beyond the left subclavian origin. The aortic segment between these two points is the aortic isthmus, the site of most coarctations and most traumatic tears (section C06)."},{"type":"p","text":"The left vagus crosses the arch behind the left phrenic nerve and gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks under the arch behind the ligamentum and ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove. The right nerve hooks under the right subclavian artery. Each supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles on its side except the cricothyroid, so injury paralyzes that vocal fold and causes a hoarse, breathy voice."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the two recurrent laryngeal nerves loop at different levels","steps":["Both nerves hook under the sixth arch arteries in the embryo","Heart and arteries descend, pulling the nerves down","Right distal sixth and fifth arch arteries regress","Right nerve rises to hook under the right subclavian (fourth arch)","Left distal sixth arch artery persists as the ductus arteriosus","Left nerve stays hooked under the arch at the ligamentum arteriosum"],"caption":"The long intrathoracic course of the left nerve exposes it to disease of the aortic arch, left atrium, pulmonary artery, and left lung."},{"type":"p","text":"The left nerve passes through the aortopulmonary window between the arch and the left pulmonary artery. An arch aneurysm, lung cancer or nodes in the window, an enlarged left atrium with a dilated pulmonary artery (section C02), or ductus surgery can injure it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old smoker develops hoarseness, and imaging shows a mass in the space below the aortic arch and above the left pulmonary artery. Why would a similar mass on the right side at the same level be much less likely to cause hoarseness?","explain":"The left nerve hooks under the arch at the ligamentum arteriosum and so passes through the aortopulmonary window, where the mass lies. The right nerve hooks under the right subclavian artery in the root of the neck, well above the arch, because the right sixth arch artery regressed.","check":"Compare with the flow figure on why the two nerves loop at different levels."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C05","obj":"CV-3.5","title":"Internal features of the chambers and ventricular wall thickness","blocks":[{"type":"table","title":"Internal features of the four chambers","cols":["Chamber","Smooth part","Rough part","Distinctive features"],"rows":[["Right atrium","Sinus venarum (sinus venosus)","Pectinate muscles, right auricle","Crista terminalis, fossa ovalis, caval and coronary sinus openings"],["Right ventricle","Conus arteriosus","Coarse trabeculae carneae","Moderator band, three papillary muscles, supraventricular crest"],["Left atrium","Most of wall (pulmonary veins)","Left auricle only","Four pulmonary vein openings"],["Left ventricle","Aortic vestibule","Fine trabeculae carneae","Two papillary muscles; mitral and aortic valves in fibrous continuity"]],"caption":"Smooth atrial walls come from absorbed venous structures and rough walls from the primitive atrium; smooth ventricular walls are outflow tracts."},{"type":"h","text":"Atria"},{"type":"p","text":"The crista terminalis (sulcus terminalis outside) separates the smooth sinus venarum from the pectinate wall. The SA node lies at its upper end near the SVC, and the AV node lies in the septum just above the coronary sinus opening, which sits between the IVC opening and the tricuspid orifice (CV-7). The fossa ovalis floor is septum primum; its limbus is septum secundum."},{"type":"p","text":"In about one quarter of adults the septa never fuse, leaving a probe-patent foramen ovale, held shut by higher left atrial pressure until coughing, straining, or pulmonary hypertension reverses the gradient."},{"type":"p","text":"The left atrium is smooth because it incorporates the primitive pulmonary vein, which is why four veins open into it. Pectinate muscles are confined to the narrow, hooked left auricle, whose stagnant flow makes it the usual site of thrombus in atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"h","text":"Ventricles"},{"type":"p","text":"The right ventricle has coarse trabeculae and three papillary muscles whose chordae reach adjacent edges of the tricuspid cusps. The valve moves passively; the papillary muscles tighten the chordae in systole so the cusps do not evert. The moderator band carries part of the right bundle branch to the distant anterior papillary muscle so it contracts on time."},{"type":"diagram","name":"conduction-pathway","caption":"Conduction pathway: the right bundle branch continues through the moderator band to the anterior papillary muscle.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-3-4.png"},{"type":"p","text":"The left ventricle has finer trabeculae and two large papillary muscles, each sending chordae to both mitral cusps. The posteromedial muscle usually has a single arterial supply and ruptures more often after inferior infarction (CV-8). The anterior mitral cusp is in fibrous continuity with the aortic valve, and the tricuspid valve attaches slightly closer to the apex than the mitral."},{"type":"h","text":"Wall thickness and the law of Laplace"},{"type":"p","text":"The left ventricular wall, about 8 to 12 mm, is two to three times thicker than the right, about 3 to 5 mm, because peak systolic pressure is about 120 versus 25 mmHg. By the law of Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by twice the wall thickness, and stress is a main determinant of myocardial oxygen demand. A pressure load adds sarcomeres in parallel and thickens the wall (concentric hypertrophy), returning stress toward normal. A volume load adds sarcomeres in series and enlarges the cavity (eccentric hypertrophy), and the larger radius raises stress."},{"type":"p","text":"Both ventricles are equally thick at birth because the ductus exposes them to systemic pressure. After pulmonary resistance falls, the thin right wall handles volume easily but tolerates an acute pressure rise poorly, so a large pulmonary embolism can cause right ventricular failure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a deep vein thrombosis in the leg and a probe-patent foramen ovale has a pulmonary embolism, and an hour later develops weakness of one side of the body. Explain how a venous clot reached the brain.","explain":"The embolism raises right heart pressures. When right atrial pressure exceeds left, the flap opens right to left, and another thrombus fragment can pass to the left heart and aorta, bypassing the lung filter, and lodge in a cerebral artery (paradoxical embolism).","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the fossa ovalis and the probe-patent foramen ovale."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-03-01-C06","obj":"CV-3.6","title":"Blunt and penetrating chest trauma to the heart and aorta","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blunt trauma injures by deceleration and compression, so it damages tethered or trapped structures. Penetrating trauma injures whatever lies on the track, so anterior structures suffer most."},{"type":"h","text":"Aortic tear at the isthmus"},{"type":"p","text":"The heart and arch hang mobile from the great vessels, while intercostal arteries and pleura fix the descending aorta to the spine, so deceleration shear concentrates at the isthmus. Most full-thickness tears are fatal at the scene; survivors usually have a pseudoaneurysm contained by collagen-rich adventitia (CV-2), which can rupture without warning. The film shows a widened mediastinum, a blurred aortic knob, the trachea and nasogastric tube pushed right, a depressed left main bronchus, and sometimes a left apical cap; first and second rib fractures signal a large force. Because much mediastinal blood is venous, these signs only suggest the diagnosis, which rests on CT angiography."},{"type":"flow","title":"From sudden deceleration to a mediastinal hematoma","steps":["Collision or fall stops the body abruptly","Mobile heart and arch continue forward","Descending aorta is held to the spine","Shear concentrates at the isthmus near the ligamentum arteriosum","Intima and media tear; adventitia may contain a pseudoaneurysm","Mediastinal blood widens the mediastinum on the film"],"caption":"The tear site is predictable: the junction of a mobile and a fixed aortic segment."},{"type":"h","text":"Myocardial contusion and commotio cordis"},{"type":"p","text":"A sternal blow bruises the right ventricle most often, releasing troponin and causing arrhythmias; sinus tachycardia is commonest, and right bundle branch block fits the anterior course of the right bundle and moderator band. A precordial blow on the upstroke of the T wave, the vulnerable period (CV-7), can trigger ventricular fibrillation without structural injury (commotio cordis)."},{"type":"h","text":"Penetrating injury and tamponade"},{"type":"p","text":"Stab wounds injure the right ventricle most often, then the left ventricle, right atrium, and left atrium. If the pericardial wound seals, blood collects in the sac (hemopericardium); if it stays open, blood drains into the pleura as hemothorax. Over minutes the fibrous sac is stiff and its pressure-volume curve turns steeply upward, so about 150 to 200 mL can cause tamponade, whereas a slow effusion can reach a liter with little pressure rise."},{"type":"p","text":"Rising pericardial pressure first compresses the low-pressure right chambers in diastole. Filling, stroke volume, and arterial pressure fall, venous pressure rises, diastolic pressures in all four chambers equalize, and fluid muffles the heart sounds (Beck triad), with reflex tachycardia. On inspiration, extra right ventricular filling can only push the septum left in the fixed sac, so left stroke volume and systolic pressure fall by more than 10 mmHg (pulsus paradoxus). The jugular y descent is blunted. Electrical alternans and low voltage belong to large effusions, and hypovolemia can leave the neck veins flat."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The downward spiral of acute tamponade","steps":["Blood fills the stiff pericardial sac rapidly","Pressure compresses right atrium and ventricle in diastole","Ventricular filling and stroke volume fall","Arterial and aortic diastolic pressure fall","Coronary perfusion pressure falls as ventricular diastolic pressure rises","Ischemia weakens contraction and output falls further"],"caption":"Unless the blood is removed, each turn lowers output further, ending in pulseless electrical activity."},{"type":"table","title":"Tamponade versus tension pneumothorax after chest trauma","cols":["Finding","Cardiac tamponade","Tension pneumothorax"],"rows":[["Cause of low output","Pericardial pressure blocks ventricular filling","Raised intrathoracic pressure and mediastinal shift reduce venous return"],["Jugular veins","Distended unless hypovolemic","Distended unless hypovolemic"],["Breath sounds and percussion","Normal on both sides","Absent and hyperresonant on the affected side"],["Trachea","Midline","Shifted away (late sign)"],["Heart sounds","Muffled","Usually audible"]],"caption":"Both conditions cause shock with distended neck veins; the lung findings separate them."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains most of this anatomy. Looping rotates the right ventricle forward and the left atrium backward, which sets the radiograph borders, the chamber most often stabbed, and the chamber that compresses the esophagus. Breakdown of the dorsal mesocardium forms the transverse sinus, absorbed venous tissue smooths the atrial walls, unfused septa leave a patent foramen ovale, and persistence of the left sixth arch artery as the ductus explains both the course of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve and the isthmus as the site of coarctation and traumatic tears. Histology and physiology add the rest: collagen makes the fibrous pericardium stiff and the aortic adventitia briefly protective, and the law of Laplace and ventricular interdependence explain wall thickness and pulsus paradoxus."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology supplies the causes: rheumatic mitral disease after group A streptococcal infection enlarges the left atrium; pericarditis follows coxsackievirus B, tuberculosis, uremia, autoimmune disease, or infarction; and lung cancer and nodes cause hoarseness and SVC obstruction. Anatomic reasoning has limits. Radiographs are two-dimensional and distorted by AP projection or a shallow breath, vessel branching varies, and classic signs such as the Beck triad are often incomplete, so a missing sign should not exclude a diagnosis the mechanism predicts."}],"summary":["The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, two thirds left of midline, with its apex behind the left fifth intercostal space at or just medial to the midclavicular line.","The right ventricle forms most of the anterior surface, the left ventricle the apex and left border, the right atrium the right border, and the left atrium most of the base; on a PA film the left contour is aortic knob, pulmonary trunk, left auricle, and left ventricle.","The right ventricle is most anterior, so it is most often stabbed or bruised and lifts the parasternal wall when hypertrophied; the left atrium is most posterior, so its enlargement causes dysphagia, a widened carina, and hoarseness.","The inelastic fibrous pericardium and the serous parietal and visceral layers enclose a fluid-filled potential space; the transverse sinus lies behind the great arteries, and the oblique sinus is a blind pouch behind the left atrium.","Pericardial pain travels in the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) and is referred to the trapezius ridge because the supraclavicular nerves share those segments.","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum, a sixth arch remnant, so arch, aortopulmonary window, and left atrial disease cause hoarseness.","The crista terminalis separates the smooth sinus venarum from pectinate muscle; the moderator band carries the right bundle branch; the left auricle is the usual site of thrombus.","The left ventricular wall is two to three times thicker than the right because it generates about five times the systolic pressure; by the law of Laplace, the thicker wall keeps wall stress near normal.","Deceleration tears the aorta at the isthmus, the junction of the mobile arch and fixed descending aorta, widening the mediastinum.","Rapid hemopericardium causes tamponade with small volumes because the pericardial pressure-volume curve is steep, producing the Beck triad, equalized diastolic pressures, and pulsus paradoxus."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: pericardium, heart, and great vessels of the superior mediastinum"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular system: formation of the heart tube, septation, and the aortic arch arteries"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump: ventricular wall stress and the law of Laplace"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart: pericardial disease and rheumatic valvular disease"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease (pericardial disease)"}]},"CV-4":{"unit":"CV-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01","title":"Heart Valves and the Cardiac Cycle","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the four heart valves through one heartbeat. It covers valve structure and auscultation sites, the Wiggers diagram, the left ventricular pressure-volume loop, the jugular venous pulse, the heart sounds, and the timing of murmurs and their response to bedside maneuvers. One principle explains all of it: a valve is passive, opening when the pressure behind it exceeds the pressure in front of it and closing when that gradient reverses. Board questions give a tracing, a loop, a neck vein finding, or an auscultation description and ask for the phase, the valve, or the lesion. Knowing which two pressures are compared at each moment, and how a lesion or maneuver changes them, answers most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Chambers, septa, fibrous skeleton, and surface anatomy of the heart (CV-3)","Preload, afterload, contractility, and the Frank-Starling relationship (CV-6)","Normal ECG waves and intervals and the bundle branches (CV-7)","Pressure, flow, resistance, and compliance (CV-11)","Effect of breathing on intrathoracic pressure and venous return"],"connections":[{"to":"Gross anatomy of the heart (CV-3)","how":"The positions of the valves and great vessels explain where each valve is heard and where its murmurs radiate."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Bundle branch blocks displace A2 or P2, and AV dissociation produces cannon a waves."},{"to":"Coronary circulation and ischemic heart disease (CV-8, CV-18-C)","how":"The single blood supply of the posteromedial papillary muscle explains acute mitral regurgitation after inferior infarction."},{"to":"Valvular and congenital heart disease (CV-18-D, CV-18-G)","how":"Stenosis, regurgitation, and shunts are recognized by the pressures, loops, sounds, and murmurs taught here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Atrioventricular (AV) valve","def":"The tricuspid or mitral valve, which separates an atrium from its ventricle and is anchored by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles."},{"term":"Semilunar valve","def":"The pulmonary or aortic valve, made of three pocket-shaped cusps without chordae, which separates a ventricle from its great artery."},{"term":"Chordae tendineae","def":"Fibrous cords that connect the free edges and ventricular surfaces of AV valve cusps to papillary muscles."},{"term":"Isovolumetric contraction","def":"The first part of systole, when the ventricle contracts with all valves closed, so pressure rises while volume does not change."},{"term":"Isovolumetric relaxation","def":"The first part of diastole, when the ventricle relaxes with all valves closed, so pressure falls while volume does not change."},{"term":"Dicrotic notch (incisura)","def":"The small downward deflection and rebound on the aortic pressure curve at the moment the aortic valve closes."},{"term":"Pressure-volume loop","def":"A plot of ventricular pressure against ventricular volume through one beat, whose width is the stroke volume and whose area is the stroke work."},{"term":"Jugular venous pulse (JVP)","def":"The pulsation of the internal jugular vein, which reflects right atrial pressure and its waves."},{"term":"Cannon a wave","def":"A very large jugular a wave produced when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve."},{"term":"A2 and P2","def":"The aortic and pulmonic components of the second heart sound, produced by closure of each semilunar valve."},{"term":"S3","def":"A low-pitched early diastolic sound produced when rapid inflow into a ventricle is abruptly decelerated."},{"term":"S4","def":"A low-pitched late diastolic sound produced when atrial contraction forces blood into a stiff ventricle."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C01","obj":"CV-4.1","title":"Valve structure, chordae and papillary muscles, and auscultation areas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The two atrioventricular (AV) valves separate atria from ventricles, and the two semilunar valves separate ventricles from the great arteries. All four attach to the fibrous skeleton, dense connective tissue rings (anuli fibrosi) that hold the openings in shape and electrically insulate the atria from the ventricles. Each cusp is a fold of endocardium with a connective tissue core and no muscle, so valve motion is entirely passive and follows the pressure gradient across it."},{"type":"h","text":"Atrioventricular valves"},{"type":"p","text":"The tricuspid valve has anterior, posterior, and septal cusps; the mitral valve has anterior and posterior cusps. The larger anterior mitral cusp lies between left ventricular inflow and outflow, in fibrous continuity with the aortic valve. In systole, high ventricular pressure would evert the large, thin AV cusps into the atria. Chordae tendineae tether the cusp edges to papillary muscles, which contract with the ventricle and keep the chordae taut as it shortens. Each papillary muscle sends chordae to two adjacent cusps, so contraction pulls their edges together. Papillary muscles therefore hold the AV valves shut in systole; they do not pull them open in diastole."},{"type":"p","text":"The right ventricle has anterior, posterior, and septal papillary muscles; the anterior one receives the moderator band, which carries the right bundle branch. The left ventricle has anterolateral and posteromedial muscles. The anterolateral muscle usually has a dual supply (left anterior descending and circumflex branches), whereas the posteromedial muscle usually depends on the posterior descending artery alone, which arises from the right coronary artery in most hearts. Inferior infarction can therefore rupture the posteromedial muscle."},{"type":"flow","title":"Papillary muscle rupture after inferior infarction","steps":["Occlusion of the right coronary or posterior descending artery","Necrosis of the posteromedial papillary muscle (single blood supply)","Rupture of the softened muscle, usually within the first week","Mitral cusps flail into the left atrium in systole","Acute severe mitral regurgitation into a small, noncompliant atrium","Large v waves, pulmonary edema, and low forward stroke volume"],"caption":"Ruptured chordae from myxomatous disease or endocarditis cause the same loss of support. Acute regurgitation is poorly tolerated because the atrium has not dilated."},{"type":"h","text":"Semilunar valves and auscultation areas"},{"type":"p","text":"The pulmonary and aortic valves have three pocket-shaped cusps without chordae. Backflow at the end of systole fills the pockets and presses the cusps together, and a central nodule with thin lateral lunules on each cusp seals the valve. The coronary arteries arise from the right and left aortic sinuses behind the cusps, giving the names right coronary, left coronary, and noncoronary cusps. A bicuspid aortic valve, present in about 1% to 2% of people, calcifies and becomes stenotic early."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of the four heart valves","cols":["Valve","Location","Cusps","Support during closure","Area where best heard"],"rows":[["Tricuspid","RA to RV","Anterior, posterior, septal","Chordae to three papillary muscles","Left lower sternal border, 4th to 5th intercostal space"],["Mitral","LA to LV","Anterior, posterior","Chordae to anterolateral and posteromedial papillary muscles","Apex, left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line"],["Pulmonary","RV to pulmonary trunk","Anterior, right, left","Cusp shape; no chordae","Left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border"],["Aortic","LV to aorta","Right, left, posterior (right coronary, left coronary, noncoronary)","Cusp shape; no chordae","Right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border"]],"caption":"AV valves need chordae because their large cusps face high systolic pressure; semilunar valves seal by the shape of their cusps."},{"type":"p","text":"The valves lie close together behind the sternum, so each is heard where its sound is carried, downstream or over the nearest chamber. Aortic sounds follow the ascending aorta to the right second intercostal space, so aortic stenosis radiates to the carotids. Pulmonary sounds follow the pulmonary trunk to the left second space. Tricuspid sounds are heard over the right ventricle, which forms most of the anterior heart surface. Mitral sounds are heard at the apex, and mitral regurgitation radiates to the left axilla along the backward jet."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C02","obj":"CV-4.2","title":"The Wiggers diagram: pressure gradients, valve events, and S1 and S2","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Wiggers diagram plots left atrial, left ventricular, and aortic pressures, LV volume, the ECG, and heart sounds against time. Every valve event is a crossing of two pressure curves, so reading it means asking which pressure across each valve is higher. At about 75 beats per minute a cycle lasts about 0.8 s (systole about 0.3 s, diastole about 0.5 s). Faster rates shorten diastole far more than systole, reducing filling time and left ventricular coronary perfusion, which occurs mainly in diastole."},{"type":"h","text":"Systole"},{"type":"p","text":"Shortly after the QRS complex the ventricle contracts. When LV pressure exceeds LA pressure the mitral valve closes, then the tricuspid, producing S1 (best at the apex) from vibration of the tensed cusps, chordae, walls, and blood. During isovolumetric contraction (about 0.05 s) all valves are closed and pressure rises at constant volume until it exceeds aortic diastolic pressure (about 80 mmHg) and the aortic valve opens. Opening is silent unless a stiff, domed valve produces an ejection click."},{"type":"p","text":"About two thirds of the stroke volume leaves during rapid ejection, as ventricular and aortic pressures peak at about 120 mmHg. In reduced ejection the T wave begins and LV pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure while momentum carries blood forward. When flow briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes, and the backflow and aortic rebound produce the dicrotic notch. Volume falls from an end-diastolic volume (EDV) of about 120 mL to an end-systolic volume (ESV) of about 50 mL: stroke volume about 70 mL, ejection fraction about 55% to 70%. Pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg."},{"type":"h","text":"Diastole"},{"type":"p","text":"Aortic then pulmonic closure produces S2, best heard at the base. During isovolumetric relaxation all valves are closed and pressure falls at constant volume while the atria fill. When LV pressure drops below LA pressure the mitral valve opens and rapid filling, aided by active relaxation, delivers most of the filling. Reduced filling (diastasis) follows and is the part lost first as heart rate rises. Atrial contraction after the P wave supplies about the last 20% of EDV at rest, a share that grows at fast rates and in stiff ventricles."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Valve events in one left-sided cardiac cycle","steps":["Atrial systole completes filling to EDV","LV pressure exceeds LA pressure: mitral valve closes (S1)","Isovolumetric contraction: all valves closed, pressure rises","LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg): aortic valve opens","Rapid then reduced ejection: volume falls to ESV","Flow reverses: aortic valve closes (S2, dicrotic notch)","Isovolumetric relaxation: all valves closed, pressure falls","LV pressure falls below LA pressure: mitral valve opens","Rapid filling (S3 if present), then diastasis"],"caption":"Each valve event is a pressure crossover; only the isovolumetric phases have all valves closed."},{"type":"h","text":"Right heart timing and S1 intensity"},{"type":"p","text":"The right heart follows the same sequence at lower pressures. The pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve, since the right ventricle needs only about 8 mmHg to exceed pulmonary diastolic pressure, and closes after it, since the low-impedance pulmonary circulation prolongs forward flow; A2 therefore precedes P2. S1 is loud with a short PR interval or mitral stenosis (cusps still wide open when contraction starts) and soft with a long PR interval."},{"type":"example","title":"Identifying the phase from pressures","text":"A tracing shows left atrial pressure 8 mmHg, left ventricular pressure 50 mmHg, aortic pressure 82 mmHg, and LV volume 120 mL. Step 1: LV pressure exceeds LA pressure, so the mitral valve is closed. Step 2: aortic pressure exceeds LV pressure, so the aortic valve is closed. Step 3: with both valves closed volume cannot change, and 120 mL is the end-diastolic volume. This is isovolumetric contraction, and S1 has just occurred. If the same pressure relationships appear with a volume of 50 mL, the ventricle is at end-systolic volume, so the phase is isovolumetric relaxation just after S2. Volume, not pressure, separates the two isovolumetric phases."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C03","obj":"CV-4.3","title":"The left ventricular pressure-volume loop and its changes in valve disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pressure-volume loop plots LV pressure against LV volume for one beat, traced counterclockwise. At the bottom right corner (EDV about 120 mL, about 8 mmHg) the mitral valve closes. The right side is vertical: isovolumetric contraction. At the top right corner (about 80 mmHg) the aortic valve opens, and the top of the loop is ejection, peaking near 120 mmHg. At the top left corner (ESV about 50 mL, about 100 mmHg) the aortic valve closes. The left side is vertical isovolumetric relaxation, the mitral valve opens at the bottom left corner, and the bottom edge is filling with little rise in pressure in a compliant ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"The width (EDV minus ESV) is stroke volume and the enclosed area is stroke work. The bottom edge follows the end-diastolic pressure-volume relationship, which is steeper in a stiff ventricle, and the top left corner lies on the end-systolic pressure-volume line, whose slope rises with contractility (CV-6)."},{"type":"h","text":"Valve lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"In aortic stenosis the ventricle must generate far more pressure than the aorta to eject, so the loop is tall, with peak LV pressures of about 180 to 250 mmHg while aortic pressure stays normal or low. Incomplete ejection raises ESV and narrows the loop. Concentric hypertrophy normalizes wall stress but stiffens the ventricle, steepening the diastolic edge and raising end-diastolic pressure. The extra loop area means greater stroke work and oxygen demand."},{"type":"p","text":"In aortic regurgitation blood leaks back from the aorta as soon as ventricular pressure falls, so volume rises during what should be isovolumetric relaxation. Filling from both atrium and aorta makes EDV very large, and the Frank-Starling mechanism produces a large total stroke volume. Systolic pressure rises, diastolic aortic pressure falls, and pulse pressure widens. Chronic eccentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in series) makes the ventricle more compliant, shifting the loop right."},{"type":"p","text":"In mitral regurgitation blood escapes into the left atrium as soon as LV pressure exceeds LA pressure, long before the aortic valve opens, so volume falls during what should be isovolumetric contraction. The low-pressure atrium is a second outlet, so ESV tends to fall early in the disease, and the regurgitant volume returns next diastole, raising EDV. Total stroke volume rises but forward output falls. Mitral stenosis, by contrast, underfills the ventricle, so the loop becomes small and shifted left."},{"type":"table","title":"Pressure-volume loop changes in three valve lesions","cols":["Feature","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation"],"rows":[["Type of load","Pressure (afterload)","Volume","Volume"],["Peak LV pressure","Greatly increased; LV to aortic gradient","Increased","Normal or decreased"],["EDV","Normal or mildly increased","Greatly increased","Increased"],["ESV","Increased","Increased","Normal or decreased early"],["Total stroke volume","Normal or decreased","Greatly increased","Increased; forward volume reduced"],["Phase not truly isovolumetric","None","Relaxation (volume rises)","Contraction (volume falls)"],["Chronic hypertrophy","Concentric","Eccentric","Eccentric, with LA dilation"],["Loop shape","Tall, narrow, steep diastolic edge","Wide, shifted right, left side slopes","Wide, shifted right, right side slopes"]],"caption":"A regurgitant lesion abolishes the isovolumetric phase in which the leaking valve should be closed."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a lesion from the loop","text":"A loop shows EDV 200 mL, ESV 70 mL, and peak pressure 110 mmHg, and volume starts falling at the bottom right corner while pressure is only about 15 mmHg. Step 1: stroke volume is 130 mL, far above 70 mL, so there is a volume load. Step 2: the ventricle loses volume before its pressure could open the aortic valve, so blood is leaving backward through an incompetent mitral valve. Step 3: peak pressure is not raised, which argues against aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation. The answer is mitral regurgitation. A loop whose left side slopes to the right, gaining volume while pressure falls after ejection, is filling from the aorta: aortic regurgitation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C04","obj":"CV-4.4","title":"The jugular venous pulse: a, c, and v waves and x and y descents","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The right internal jugular vein runs nearly straight through the brachiocephalic vein and superior vena cava into the right atrium, so right atrial pressure changes appear as neck pulsations. Pulsation height estimates right atrial pressure: the sternal angle lies about 5 cm above the right atrium, so with the patient at about 45 degrees, a pulsation more than about 3 cm above the sternal angle suggests raised pressure. Unlike the carotid pulse, the jugular pulse is not palpable, usually shows two peaks per cycle, falls with inspiration, and changes with position. The left atrial (wedge) tracing has the same waves."},{"type":"h","text":"Waves and descents"},{"type":"p","text":"The a wave is right atrial contraction, after the P wave and just before S1. The c wave, just after S1, occurs as the closed tricuspid valve bulges into the atrium during right ventricular isovolumetric contraction, with some transmitted carotid pulsation. The x descent is the systolic fall in atrial pressure from atrial relaxation and the downward pull of the tricuspid ring as the ventricle ejects. The v wave is atrial filling from the venae cavae against the closed tricuspid valve, peaking near S2. The y descent is atrial emptying when the tricuspid valve opens, during rapid filling."},{"type":"h","text":"Abnormal waves"},{"type":"p","text":"Cannon a waves occur when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, forcing its contraction backward into the neck. In complete heart block, ventricular tachycardia with AV dissociation, and ventricular pacing, atria and ventricles beat independently, so cannon waves appear irregularly on beats when timing coincides. When atria and ventricles activate together on every beat, as in some junctional rhythms and AV nodal reentrant tachycardia, they appear regularly. Large but not cannon a waves reflect resistance to atrial emptying through an open valve, as in tricuspid stenosis or a stiff right ventricle. The a wave disappears in atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"p","text":"In tricuspid regurgitation the atrium also receives blood ejected backward from the right ventricle, so pressure rises throughout systole. The x descent is lost, c and v merge into a large systolic c-v wave, the y descent is steep, and systolic hepatic pulsation may be felt. Acute mitral regurgitation produces the same large v wave on the wedge tracing."},{"type":"table","title":"Jugular venous waves: normal origin and important abnormalities","cols":["Component","Mechanical cause","Timing","Abnormal pattern and cause"],"rows":[["a wave","Right atrial contraction","After the P wave, just before S1","Cannon a: atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve (AV dissociation). Large a: tricuspid stenosis, stiff RV. Absent a: atrial fibrillation"],["c wave","Tricuspid valve bulges into the atrium during RV isovolumetric contraction","Just after S1","Merged into a large systolic wave in tricuspid regurgitation"],["x descent","Atrial relaxation and downward pull of the tricuspid ring during ejection","Ventricular systole","Lost in tricuspid regurgitation; prominent in cardiac tamponade"],["v wave","Atrial filling from the venae cavae against the closed tricuspid valve","Late systole, peaking near S2","Large v (c-v) wave in tricuspid regurgitation"],["y descent","Tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties","Early diastole (rapid filling)","Steep in tricuspid regurgitation and constrictive pericarditis; slow in tricuspid stenosis; blunted in tamponade"]],"caption":"Tamponade and constriction patterns are taught with pericardial disease (CV-18-F)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 74-year-old with syncope has a regular pulse of 38 beats per minute. Looking at the neck, you see occasional very large venous pulsations that do not occur with every beat. What is the mechanism, and why are they intermittent?","explain":"This is complete heart block with independent atrial and ventricular rhythms. When a P wave falls during ventricular systole, the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve and produces a cannon a wave. Because the rhythms are unrelated, the coincidence occurs only on some beats.","check":"Compare with the cannon a wave paragraph and AV dissociation in CV-7."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C05","obj":"CV-4.5","title":"Physiologic, wide, fixed, and paradoxical splitting of S2","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"S2 has an aortic component (A2), loud and heard widely, and a pulmonic component (P2), soft and best heard at the left second intercostal space, where splitting is assessed. A2 normally comes first. A split is audible when the components are more than about 0.03 s apart."},{"type":"h","text":"Physiologic splitting"},{"type":"p","text":"Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart and lowers pulmonary impedance, so right ventricular ejection lasts longer and P2 is delayed. The expanded pulmonary vessels briefly hold more blood, so left ventricular filling dips and A2 comes slightly earlier. The split widens to about 0.04 s on inspiration and closes in expiration. It is most easily heard in children and young adults."},{"type":"flow","title":"Physiologic splitting of S2 during inspiration","steps":["Inspiration: intrathoracic pressure becomes more negative","Systemic venous return to the right heart rises","RV ejection lasts longer","Pulmonary vascular impedance falls, prolonging forward flow","P2 is delayed","Blood pools in the lungs, LV filling dips, A2 comes slightly earlier","The split widens"],"caption":"Breathing changes right heart filling more than left, so the split widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration."},{"type":"h","text":"Abnormal splitting"},{"type":"p","text":"Wide splitting is present in expiration and widens further on inspiration, because P2 is late throughout while the respiratory mechanism still works. In right bundle branch block the right ventricle is activated late; in pulmonic stenosis its ejection is prolonged. Severe mitral regurgitation gives the left ventricle a second outlet, shortening ejection and moving A2 earlier."},{"type":"p","text":"Fixed splitting, classically from an atrial septal defect, is wide and does not change with breathing. The left-to-right shunt volume-loads the right ventricle and distends the pulmonary bed, delaying P2. Because the atria act as one chamber, the extra systemic return of inspiration is offset by a smaller shunt, and the reverse occurs in expiration, so right ventricular volume and P2 timing stay nearly constant."},{"type":"p","text":"Paradoxical splitting occurs when A2 is delayed past P2. The split is heard in expiration and narrows or disappears on inspiration, as the normal delay of P2 moves it toward the late A2. A2 is delayed electrically in left bundle branch block and right ventricular pacing, and mechanically in severe aortic stenosis and obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of S2 splitting","cols":["Pattern","Order of components","Expiration","Inspiration","Mechanism","Classic causes"],"rows":[["Physiologic","A2 then P2","Single or very narrow","Split (about 0.04 s)","Inspiration delays P2 and slightly advances A2","Normal, especially in the young"],["Wide","A2 then P2","Split","Wider split","P2 delayed throughout (or A2 early)","Right bundle branch block, pulmonic stenosis; severe mitral regurgitation (early A2)"],["Fixed","A2 then P2","Wide split","Same wide split","Constant RV volume load; shunt changes offset changes in venous return","Atrial septal defect"],["Paradoxical","P2 then A2","Split","Single or narrower","A2 delayed past P2","Left bundle branch block, severe aortic stenosis, right ventricular pacing"]],"caption":"Ask two questions: is a split heard in expiration, and does inspiration widen it, leave it unchanged, or narrow it?"}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C06","obj":"CV-4.6","title":"S3 and S4, and functional flow murmurs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"S3 and S4 are low-pitched diastolic sounds made by the ventricle and its blood, not by valves. Left-sided sounds are heard best with the bell at the apex in the left lateral position; right-sided sounds at the left lower sternal border, louder on inspiration."},{"type":"h","text":"S3: a volume sound"},{"type":"p","text":"S3 occurs in early diastole, about 0.12 to 0.16 s after S2, during rapid filling. A large, fast inflow is suddenly decelerated as the ventricle reaches its limit of expansion, setting the walls and blood vibrating. It is often normal in children, young adults up to about age 40, trained athletes, and pregnancy, where a compliant ventricle fills briskly. In older adults it signals volume overload: a dilated failing ventricle with high atrial pressure, regurgitant volume returning in mitral or aortic regurgitation, a left-to-right shunt, or a high-output state. Significant mitral stenosis prevents rapid filling and therefore an S3. S3 can persist in atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"h","text":"S4: a stiffness sound"},{"type":"p","text":"S4 occurs in late diastole just before S1, with atrial contraction. Atrial ejection into a stiff ventricle causes a sharp rise in pressure and a low-frequency vibration. It indicates reduced compliance: hypertrophy from hypertension, aortic stenosis, or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and acute ischemia, which impairs relaxation. It may occur with age-related stiffening but is abnormal in the young. Because it requires atrial contraction, S4 is absent in atrial fibrillation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old with long-standing hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy has a late diastolic sound just before S1. A month later the ECG shows atrial fibrillation and the sound is gone, although the hypertrophy is unchanged. Why did the sound disappear?","explain":"The sound was an S4, produced by atrial contraction into the stiff hypertrophied ventricle. In atrial fibrillation there is no organized atrial contraction, so the sound disappears even though the ventricle is no more compliant.","check":"Compare with the S4 paragraph."},{"type":"h","text":"Functional flow murmurs"},{"type":"p","text":"A murmur is turbulent flow. Turbulence appears when the Reynolds number, which rises with velocity and vessel diameter and falls with viscosity, exceeds a critical value. A normal valve can therefore produce a murmur when flow is high or blood is thin. In pregnancy, blood volume rises about 40% to 50% and cardiac output about 30% to 50%. In anemia, hypoxic vasodilation raises cardiac output and low red cell mass reduces viscosity. Fever, thyrotoxicosis, and exercise raise output. Removing the cause removes the murmur."},{"type":"p","text":"Children commonly have innocent murmurs because of a thin chest wall and high output relative to size: a vibratory, musical early systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border or apex (Still murmur), a soft pulmonary flow murmur, and the venous hum, a continuous murmur above the clavicle that disappears when the child lies down or the jugular vein is compressed. Functional murmurs are soft (grade 1 or 2, no thrill), early to mid systolic ejection murmurs at the base or left sternal border, with normal S2 splitting and no extra sounds. A diastolic or holosystolic murmur, a thrill, or an abnormal S2 points to structural disease; the venous hum is the exception."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-04-01-C07","obj":"CV-4.7","title":"Classifying murmurs by timing and location, and their response to maneuvers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A murmur's timing and shape follow the time course of the pressure gradient that drives it. Intensity is graded 1 (faint) to 6 (heard with the stethoscope off the chest); a palpable thrill means grade 4 or higher."},{"type":"h","text":"Systolic murmurs"},{"type":"p","text":"Ejection murmurs arise across the aortic or pulmonic outflow. Flow begins only after isovolumetric contraction, so the murmur starts after S1, peaks with ejection velocity, and ends before S2. In aortic stenosis, as severity increases, the peak moves later and A2 softens."},{"type":"p","text":"Holosystolic murmurs (mitral and tricuspid regurgitation, ventricular septal defect) arise when blood escapes into a lower-pressure chamber from the onset of contraction, so they run evenly from S1 to S2; a small septal defect may be loud because its jet is fast. In mitral valve prolapse the cusps hold until the ventricle becomes small enough in mid systole, so a midsystolic click precedes a late systolic murmur."},{"type":"h","text":"Diastolic and continuous murmurs"},{"type":"p","text":"In aortic regurgitation the backward gradient is greatest right after the valve should close, so a high-pitched decrescendo murmur starts at A2, best heard sitting forward in held expiration; pulmonic regurgitation, usually from pulmonary hypertension, is similar. In mitral stenosis flow begins only when high atrial pressure snaps the valve open (opening snap), followed by a low rumble. Worse stenosis means higher atrial pressure and earlier opening, so a shorter S2 to opening snap interval means more severe disease. In patent ductus arteriosus aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure throughout the cycle, giving a continuous murmur."},{"type":"table","title":"Classification of murmurs by timing","cols":["Timing and shape","Lesion","Best heard","Key feature"],"rows":[["Ejection, crescendo-decrescendo","Aortic stenosis","Right 2nd intercostal space","Carotid radiation; soft A2"],["Ejection, crescendo-decrescendo","Pulmonic stenosis","Left 2nd intercostal space","Wide split S2"],["Ejection, crescendo-decrescendo","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Left lower sternal border","No carotid radiation"],["Holosystolic","Mitral regurgitation","Apex","Radiates to left axilla"],["Holosystolic","Tricuspid regurgitation","Left lower sternal border","Louder on inspiration"],["Holosystolic","Ventricular septal defect","Left lower sternal border","Harsh"],["Click, then late systolic","Mitral valve prolapse","Apex","Click earlier when LV is smaller"],["Early diastolic decrescendo","Aortic regurgitation","Left sternal border, 3rd to 4th space","Wide pulse pressure"],["Opening snap, then mid-diastolic rumble","Mitral stenosis","Apex, left lateral, bell","Presystolic accentuation"],["Mid-diastolic rumble","Tricuspid stenosis","Left lower sternal border","Louder on inspiration"],["Continuous, peaking at S2","Patent ductus arteriosus","Left infraclavicular area","Machinery-like"]],"caption":"Stenosis is heard when the valve should be open, regurgitation when it should be closed."},{"type":"h","text":"Dynamic maneuvers"},{"type":"p","text":"Maneuvers change preload, afterload, or both. Most murmurs grow louder with more preload, and regurgitant murmurs with more afterload. Handgrip raises afterload, increasing backflow in mitral regurgitation, aortic regurgitation, and ventricular septal defect while reducing the aortic stenosis gradient. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and mitral valve prolapse move opposite to most murmurs with preload. Valsalva strain or standing lowers venous return and shrinks the ventricle, bringing the hypertrophied septum and anterior mitral cusp closer and worsening outflow obstruction, and letting redundant mitral cusps prolapse earlier. In aortic stenosis the obstruction is fixed, so lower preload only reduces flow and softens the murmur."},{"type":"table","title":"Effects of maneuvers on murmurs","cols":["Maneuver","Main change","Most murmurs","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Mitral valve prolapse"],"rows":[["Inspiration","More right heart return","Right-sided louder","Little change","Little change"],["Squatting","More preload and afterload","Louder","Softer","Click later"],["Standing from squat","Less preload","Softer","Louder","Click earlier"],["Handgrip","More afterload","MR, AR, VSD louder; AS softer","Softer","Click later"],["Valsalva strain","Less preload","Softer","Louder","Click earlier"]],"caption":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and mitral valve prolapse worsen as the left ventricular cavity shrinks."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a midsystolic click and late systolic apical murmur stands up quickly from a squat. Predict the change in the click and murmur.","explain":"The click moves earlier and the murmur lengthens. Standing reduces venous return, so the ventricle starts systole smaller and the redundant cusps prolapse earlier in contraction.","check":"Compare with the maneuver table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The valves form from endocardial cushions, so cushion defects produce AV septal defects and abnormal valves together (CV-1), and the fibrous skeleton that anchors them also separates atrial from ventricular conduction (CV-7). Physiology ties every finding to pressure gradients: the Wiggers diagram, the pressure-volume loop, the jugular pulse, and the heart sounds are four views of valves opening when upstream pressure is higher. Preload, afterload, and contractility (CV-6) change those gradients, which is why breathing and maneuvers alter splitting and murmurs. Pathology supplies the causes (CV-18-D): calcific and bicuspid aortic stenosis, rheumatic mitral disease after group A streptococcal infection through cross-reactive immunity, endocarditis, and myxomatous degeneration in mitral valve prolapse and in Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1) or Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (collagen). Whether a lesion imposes a pressure or a volume load predicts concentric or eccentric hypertrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Auscultation has limits. Loudness depends on flow as well as severity, so a severely stenotic valve may soften as cardiac output falls and a small ventricular septal defect may be louder than a large one. Obesity and emphysema muffle sounds. Bedside findings identify the likely lesion and its mechanism, while imaging and pressure measurement define severity."}],"summary":["Valves move passively: each opens when the pressure behind it exceeds the pressure in front of it and closes when the gradient reverses.","Papillary muscles hold AV cusps shut in systole; the posteromedial muscle usually has a single supply from the posterior descending artery, so inferior infarction can rupture it and cause acute mitral regurgitation.","Aortic sounds are heard at the right 2nd intercostal space, pulmonic at the left 2nd, tricuspid at the left lower sternal border, and mitral at the apex.","S1 is AV valve closure at the start of isovolumetric contraction and S2 is semilunar closure at the start of isovolumetric relaxation; only these phases have all valves closed.","Normal LV values are about 120 mL EDV, 50 mL ESV, 70 mL stroke volume, and ejection fraction 55% to 70%; atrial contraction supplies about 20% of filling at rest.","The counterclockwise pressure-volume loop has stroke volume as its width; aortic stenosis makes it tall, aortic regurgitation removes isovolumetric relaxation, and mitral regurgitation removes isovolumetric contraction.","Jugular a is atrial contraction, c is tricuspid bulging, x is atrial relaxation and ring descent, v is atrial filling against a closed valve, and y is tricuspid opening; cannon a waves mean AV dissociation.","Inspiration delays P2; RBBB and pulmonic stenosis widen the split, ASD fixes it, and LBBB or aortic stenosis reverse it.","S3 is rapid filling of a volume-loaded ventricle and can be normal in the young; S4 is atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle and is absent in atrial fibrillation.","Stenosis is heard when a valve should be open and regurgitation when it should be closed; lower preload softens most murmurs but intensifies hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and makes the mitral valve prolapse click earlier."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Heart Valves and Heart Sounds; Valvular and Congenital Heart Defects"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump: cardiac cycle, pressure-volume loops, and heart sounds"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, heart valves, papillary muscles, and surface anatomy of the heart"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on valvular heart disease"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease, valvular disorders"}]},"CV-5":{"unit":"CV-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01","title":"Cardiac Muscle Contraction","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows one heartbeat in a ventricular myocyte: the ion currents of the action potential and its long refractory period, the conversion of that action potential into a rise in cytosolic Ca2+ by calcium-induced calcium release, the removal of Ca2+ that lets the ventricle relax, and the two ways the heart grades its force, sympathetic signaling and stretch. Board questions usually describe a change, such as a blocked channel, altered plasma K+, ischemia, or a larger end-diastolic volume, and ask you to predict its effect. Each prediction rests on knowing which current dominates each phase, where activator Ca2+ comes from and where it goes, which steps consume ATP, and why the heart cannot recruit cells or summate twitches.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential, equilibrium potentials, and the Nernst equation","Voltage-gated channel states (closed, open, inactivated) and the Na+/K+-ATPase","Skeletal muscle sarcomere structure and the sliding filament mechanism","G protein-coupled receptors, Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, and protein kinase A","Histology of cardiac muscle: intercalated discs, T tubules, and sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"connections":[{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"The ventricular action potential forms the QRS complex, ST segment, and T wave, and its K+ currents set the QT interval."},{"to":"Cardiac cycle and its regulation (CV-6)","how":"Sarcomere length-tension behavior is the cellular basis of the Frank-Starling mechanism, ventricular function curves, and pressure-volume loops."},{"to":"Coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"Contraction and relaxation both consume ATP, so the balance of coronary O2 supply and myocardial demand decides whether the myocyte can keep beating."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C) and heart failure (CV-18-B)","how":"ATP loss, acidosis, Ca2+ overload, reduced SERCA2a, and desensitized beta-1 signaling explain stiff, weak, and slowly relaxing myocardium."},{"to":"Skeletal and smooth muscle physiology","how":"Contrasting RyR1 mechanical coupling, motor unit recruitment, and calmodulin-based regulation with cardiac mechanisms is a common source of board questions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Functional syncytium","def":"A tissue whose cells are electrically joined by gap junctions so that excitation of one cell spreads to all of them and they contract as a unit."},{"term":"Inward rectifier K+ current (IK1)","def":"A K+ current that conducts well near the resting potential but very little at depolarized potentials, holding the resting myocyte near the K+ equilibrium potential."},{"term":"Effective refractory period","def":"The interval after an upstroke during which no stimulus, however strong, can produce a propagated action potential, because too few fast Na+ channels have recovered from inactivation."},{"term":"Dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR)","def":"The L-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channel of the T tubule; Cav1.2 in heart and Cav1.1 in skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Ryanodine receptor (RyR2)","def":"The Ca2+ release channel of the cardiac sarcoplasmic reticulum, opened by a rise in Ca2+ on its cytosolic side."},{"term":"Calcium-induced calcium release (CICR)","def":"The process by which a small trigger influx of Ca2+ through L-type channels opens RyR2 and releases a much larger amount of Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"term":"Dyad","def":"The cardiac junction where one T tubule lies against one terminal cisterna of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, placing L-type channels close to clusters of RyR2."},{"term":"SERCA2a","def":"The sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase of cardiac muscle, which uses ATP to pump cytosolic Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"term":"Phospholamban","def":"A small sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane protein that inhibits SERCA2a when unphosphorylated and releases that inhibition when phosphorylated."},{"term":"Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX)","def":"A sarcolemmal transporter that moves 3 Na+ into the cell for each Ca2+ moved out, using the Na+ gradient as its energy source."},{"term":"Inotropy and lusitropy","def":"Inotropy is the force and speed of contraction at a given sarcomere length; lusitropy is the rate of relaxation."},{"term":"Length-dependent activation","def":"The increase in myofilament Ca2+ sensitivity when a cardiac sarcomere is stretched, which makes force rise more steeply with length than filament overlap alone predicts."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01-C01","obj":"CV-5.1","title":"The ventricular action potential and the refractory period","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gap junctions in the intercalated discs pass current between myocytes, so the ventricle behaves as a functional syncytium and each cell is brought to threshold by a depolarized neighbor."},{"type":"p","text":"At rest (phase 4) the membrane sits at about -85 to -90 mV, near EK (about -95 mV with K+ of 140 mmol/L inside and 4 mmol/L outside), because the inward rectifier IK1 dominates resting conductance. Inward rectification is the key property: on depolarization, intracellular Mg2+ and polyamines plug the IK1 pore, so outward K+ current nearly stops. This gives working myocytes a stable resting potential (nodal cells have little IK1, CV-7) and limits K+ loss during the plateau."},{"type":"p","text":"At a threshold of about -70 mV, fast Na+ channels (Nav1.5, gene SCN5A) open in a positive feedback loop that drives the membrane to about +20 mV within 1 to 2 ms; this steep upstroke sets conduction velocity. The channels inactivate within milliseconds and stay inactivated until repolarization. Phase 1 is a brief notch from Na+ channel inactivation and transient outward K+ current (Ito). In phase 2, L-type Ca2+ channels (Cav1.2) carry a sustained inward current balanced by small outward K+ currents, holding the membrane near 0 mV for about 0.2 s; this Ca2+ is the trigger for contraction (C02). In phase 3, L-type channels inactivate, the delayed rectifiers IKr (KCNH2, hERG) and IKs (KCNQ1) grow, and below about -40 mV IK1 unblocks and completes repolarization."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ventricular-ap","caption":"Ventricular myocyte action potential: stable phase 4 near -90 mV held by IK1, rapid Na+ upstroke in phase 0, a brief notch from Ito in phase 1, the L-type Ca2+ plateau in phase 2, and K+ repolarization in phase 3.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-5-1.png"},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of the ventricular action potential","cols":["Phase","Dominant current and channel","ECG correlate"],"rows":[["4: rest, about -85 to -90 mV","IK1 (inward rectifier K+)","Interval from T wave to next QRS"],["0: upstroke to about +20 mV","Inward Na+ (Nav1.5)","QRS complex"],["1: brief notch","Na+ inactivation plus Ito","J point"],["2: plateau near 0 mV, about 0.2 s","Inward Ca2+ (Cav1.2) balanced by outward K+","ST segment"],["3: repolarization","IKr and IKs, then IK1","T wave"]],"caption":"The whole action potential, about 0.25 to 0.3 s, corresponds approximately to the QT interval (CV-7)."},{"type":"h","text":"Refractory periods and why the heart cannot be tetanized"},{"type":"p","text":"Because inactivated Na+ channels cannot reset until the membrane repolarizes, no stimulus can produce a propagated action potential from the upstroke through the plateau into phase 3. This effective refractory period lasts about 0.25 to 0.3 s and ends near -50 to -60 mV. In the following relative refractory period (about 0.05 s), only a stronger than normal stimulus fires the cell, and the upstroke is slower and smaller. Unlike skeletal muscle, whose brief refractory period allows twitches to summate into tetanus, cardiac refractoriness lasts nearly as long as contraction, so the ventricle must relax and refill before the next beat. The long refractory period also resists reentry; uneven shortening by ischemia or prolonged repolarization in long QT syndromes undermines this protection. Low plasma K+ reduces IKr and IK1, lengthening phase 3 and favoring afterdepolarizations."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the effect of hyperkalemia","text":"Plasma K+ rises from 4 to 8 mmol/L. Step 1: EK = 61.5 x log(8/140), about -76 mV instead of -95 mV. Step 2: because IK1 dominates resting conductance, the resting potential moves up to about -70 mV. Step 3: at this potential many fast Na+ channels stay inactivated between beats. Step 4: phase 0 is slower and smaller, conduction slows, and the QRS widens. Step 5: high extracellular K+ increases IKr conductance, so phase 3 speeds up and T waves become peaked, usually before the QRS widens. Step 6: if most Na+ channels are inactivated, conduction fails and ventricular fibrillation or asystole can follow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01-C02","obj":"CV-5.2","title":"Excitation-contraction coupling and calcium-induced calcium release","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Excitation-contraction coupling converts the sarcolemmal action potential into sarcomere force, and in the heart the link is Ca2+. T tubules carry the action potential into the cell at the Z lines. Where a T tubule meets one SR terminal cisterna it forms a dyad, and across a cleft of about 12 to 15 nm, L-type Ca2+ channels face clusters of RyR2. Atrial myocytes have few T tubules, so their Ca2+ release spreads inward from the surface. Diastolic cytosolic free Ca2+ is about 0.1 µmol/L, roughly ten thousand times lower than extracellular ionized Ca2+ (about 1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L), and the SR holds a large store buffered by calsequestrin."},{"type":"p","text":"During the plateau, trigger Ca2+ entering the tiny cleft binds RyR2 and opens it. One cluster releasing Ca2+ produces a Ca2+ spark, and thousands of synchronized sparks form the Ca2+ transient, which raises cytosolic Ca2+ to about 1 µmol/L. In human ventricle the SR supplies most activator Ca2+, about 70 percent. Release is graded: each L-type channel controls only its facing cluster, so a larger trigger or a fuller SR releases more Ca2+. These two levers are what sympathetic stimulation, beat frequency, and changes in Na+ handling adjust (C03 to C05). Release ends within tens of milliseconds, mainly because falling local SR Ca2+ lowers RyR2 open probability."},{"type":"p","text":"Ca2+ binds the single regulatory site of cardiac troponin C (fast skeletal TnC has two), which then grips troponin I, which releases actin, and tropomyosin rolls deeper into the groove, uncovering myosin-binding sites. Myosin heads carrying ADP and Pi bind actin, release Pi in the power stroke, and release ADP into the rigor state; a new ATP must bind before the head detaches, and its hydrolysis recocks the head. Adult human ventricle expresses mainly the slower, economical beta myosin heavy chain."},{"type":"flow","title":"Excitation-contraction coupling in a ventricular myocyte","steps":["Action potential spreads through gap junctions and down T tubules","L-type channels open; trigger Ca2+ enters the dyadic cleft","Cleft Ca2+ opens RyR2 (calcium-induced calcium release)","Cytosolic Ca2+ rises from about 0.1 to 1 µmol/L","Ca2+ binds troponin C; tropomyosin uncovers myosin-binding sites","Cross-bridges cycle using ATP and the sarcomere shortens"],"caption":"Ca2+ entry from outside the cell triggers release from the SR. Relaxation reverses these steps (C03)."},{"type":"table","title":"Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal and cardiac muscle","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac (ventricular) muscle"],"rows":[["Excitation","Acetylcholine at its own neuromuscular junction","Current from neighbors through gap junctions"],["T-tubule position and junction","A-I junction; triad","Z line; dyad"],["Coupling","Cav1.1 mechanically opens RyR1","Cav1.2 Ca2+ entry opens RyR2 (CICR)"],["Need for extracellular Ca2+","Not needed for a twitch","Required"],["Action potential duration","About 1 to 5 ms","About 250 to 300 ms"]],"caption":"Both striated muscles use troponin C as the Ca2+ sensor; smooth muscle uses calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase."},{"type":"p","text":"Because cardiac coupling has no mechanical link, removing extracellular Ca2+ or blocking L-type channels weakens the heart promptly while sparing skeletal muscle. RyR2 must also stay closed in diastole. Leaky RyR2 or loss of calsequestrin causes catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: under adrenergic stress, SR Ca2+ escapes in diastole, NCX extrudes it while bringing in 3 Na+ per Ca2+, and the net inward current produces delayed afterdepolarizations. Glycoside toxicity and failing myocardium share this mechanism."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An isolated heart and an isolated skeletal muscle are both moved into a Ca2+-free solution. What happens to contraction in each over the next minute, and why?","explain":"Cardiac contractions weaken rapidly and stop, because no trigger Ca2+ enters to open RyR2 and the SR store runs down through NCX. The skeletal muscle keeps twitching for a time, because its voltage sensor opens RyR1 mechanically.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on trigger Ca2+ and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01-C03","obj":"CV-5.3","title":"Relaxation and calcium removal; why ischemia impairs both phases","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Relaxation is active. Cytosolic Ca2+ must fall from about 1 to 0.1 µmol/L so Ca2+ leaves troponin C, and each attached myosin head needs a fresh ATP to release actin. SERCA2a pumps 2 Ca2+ into the SR per ATP and removes about 70 percent of activator Ca2+; its speed largely sets early diastolic relaxation. Unphosphorylated phospholamban lowers SERCA2a Ca2+ affinity; phosphorylation by PKA or CaMKII releases this brake, so phosphorylation activates the pump. NCX removes most of the rest, exchanging 3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out. It is electrogenic and depends on the Na+ gradient kept by the Na+/K+-ATPase, so it slows or reverses when cytosolic Na+ rises or the membrane stays depolarized."},{"type":"table","title":"Pathways that lower cytosolic Ca2+ in ventricular myocytes","cols":["Pathway","Location and energy source","Approximate share","Main regulator"],"rows":[["SERCA2a","SR membrane; ATP (2 Ca2+ per ATP)","About 70 percent","Phospholamban"],["NCX","Sarcolemma; Na+ gradient (3 Na+ in per Ca2+ out)","About 25 to 30 percent","Intracellular Na+ and membrane potential"],["PMCA and mitochondrial uptake","Sarcolemma (ATP) and inner mitochondrial membrane","About 1 to 2 percent together","Calmodulin (PMCA)"]],"caption":"Shares are approximate and come mainly from mammalian ventricular myocytes."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Ca2+ cycling in one heartbeat","steps":["L-type channels admit a small trigger Ca2+ influx","RyR2 releases most activator Ca2+ from the SR","Ca2+ binds troponin C and cross-bridges generate force","SERCA2a returns most Ca2+ to the SR","NCX exports an amount equal to what entered","Cytosolic Ca2+ returns to about 0.1 µmol/L and the muscle relaxes"],"caption":"In a steady state, reuptake balances release and extrusion balances entry. Shifting either balance changes SR load and the strength of later beats."},{"type":"h","text":"Why ischemia impairs contraction and relaxation"},{"type":"p","text":"The myocyte stores little energy and depends on aerobic metabolism. When coronary flow stops, creatine phosphate falls and anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate and H+. Force falls within about 60 s, while ATP is still substantial, because H+ lowers troponin C Ca2+ sensitivity and Pi reduces force per cross-bridge. Relaxation fails because SERCA2a and PMCA slow as ADP and Pi accumulate and ATP falls and NCX loses its driving force as Na+ rises (Na+/H+ exchange adds more Na+), so Ca2+ lingers and cross-bridges stay attached; severe depletion produces rigor and ischemic contracture. Impaired relaxation therefore appears first in the ischemic cascade, before reduced wall motion, ECG changes, and angina."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ATP loss to stiff, weak myocardium","steps":["Coronary occlusion halts oxidative phosphorylation","Glycolysis produces lactate and H+; Pi accumulates","Ca2+ sensitivity and force fall within about 60 s","Na+/K+-ATPase slows; Na+ rises and NCX fails","SERCA2a slows; diastolic Ca2+ stays high","Stiff ventricle raises end-diastolic pressure","Ca2+ overload causes irreversible injury after about 20 to 40 min"],"caption":"The same energy failure weakens systole and impairs diastole. With reperfusion, Ca2+ overload causes hypercontraction and contraction band necrosis (CV-18-C)."},{"type":"example","title":"Inhibiting the Na+/K+-ATPase","text":"Digoxin partially inhibits the Na+/K+-ATPase. Step 1: cytosolic Na+ rises a few mmol/L. Step 2: the smaller Na+ gradient slows NCX, so less Ca2+ leaves the cell each diastole. Step 3: SERCA2a pumps the retained Ca2+ into the SR. Step 4: over several beats SR content rises until extrusion again matches entry. Step 5: each beat releases more Ca2+, so contraction strengthens without any receptor signaling. Step 6: with excessive inhibition the SR overloads, releases Ca2+ spontaneously in diastole, and NCX turns that release into delayed afterdepolarizations. Ischemia, which also raises Na+, drives Ca2+ overload by the same route."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01-C04","obj":"CV-5.4","title":"Beta-1 adrenergic regulation of contraction and relaxation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic nerves and epinephrine from the adrenal medulla act on beta-1 receptors, the predominant beta receptors of ventricular myocytes. Within seconds, contraction becomes stronger, faster, and shorter, and relaxation speeds up. This matters because sympathetic activation also raises heart rate (CV-7), which shortens diastole more than systole, so the ventricle fills only if it relaxes faster."},{"type":"p","text":"The beta-1 receptor couples to Gs, which stimulates adenylyl cyclase (mainly types 5 and 6) to make cAMP. cAMP frees the catalytic subunits of protein kinase A, and A-kinase anchoring proteins hold PKA near its targets. Phosphodiesterases (mainly PDE3 and PDE4), phosphatases, and receptor desensitization by G protein-coupled receptor kinase 2 and beta-arrestin end the signal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Beta-1 adrenergic signaling in the ventricular myocyte","steps":["Catecholamine binds the beta-1 receptor","Gs alpha stimulates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP rises","cAMP activates protein kinase A","PKA phosphorylates L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I","Larger Ca2+ transient: positive inotropy","Faster Ca2+ removal: positive lusitropy"],"caption":"Phosphodiesterases and phosphatases terminate the signal when receptor stimulation stops."},{"type":"table","title":"Protein kinase A targets in the ventricular myocyte","cols":["Target","Effect of phosphorylation","Functional result"],"rows":[["L-type Ca2+ channel","Greater open probability and longer openings","Larger trigger and SR release: stronger contraction"],["Phospholamban","SERCA2a inhibition removed","Faster reuptake (relaxation) and higher SR load (stronger beats)"],["Troponin I","Lower Ca2+ affinity of troponin C","Faster Ca2+ dissociation: faster relaxation"],["Myosin-binding protein C","Faster cross-bridge kinetics","Faster rise and fall of force"],["IKs channel (KCNQ1)","Larger repolarizing K+ current","Action potential shortens at high rates"]],"caption":"The first three targets are the core of the response: a shorter, stronger twitch that fits the shorter cycle of a fast heart rate."},{"type":"p","text":"Phospholamban phosphorylation serves both effects: within a beat Ca2+ falls faster, and over successive beats the SR captures more Ca2+ before NCX removes it, so release grows. Troponin I phosphorylation alone would slightly lower force at a given Ca2+, but the larger transient more than compensates. Enhanced IKs keeps repolarization in step with the larger plateau current, which is why exercise provokes arrhythmias in long QT type 1 (loss of IKs). RyR2 phosphorylation is also reported, but its importance is debated."},{"type":"p","text":"Beta-1 stimulation raises force at any sarcomere length, shifting the ventricular function curve upward (CV-6) rather than moving along it. Vagal effects on ventricular contractility are small because ventricular parasympathetic innervation is sparse. In chronic heart failure, sustained sympathetic drive downregulates beta-1 receptors, increases G protein-coupled receptor kinase 2, reduces SERCA2a, and leaves phospholamban underphosphorylated, giving weaker contraction, slower relaxation, a blunted exercise response, and more arrhythmias."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A mouse expresses a mutant phospholamban that cannot be phosphorylated but still binds SERCA2a. How will its heart respond to a beta-1 agonist compared with a normal heart?","explain":"Heart rate and L-type Ca2+ current still rise, so contractility increases somewhat. SERCA2a stays inhibited, so reuptake does not speed up, the SR store grows less, and relaxation accelerates far less (mainly the troponin I effect remains). Filling suffers at high rates.","check":"Compare with the PKA targets table and the paragraph on phospholamban."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-05-01-C05","obj":"CV-5.5","title":"The cardiac length-tension relationship and how force is graded","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Active force depends on how many myosin heads can reach actin. Thick filaments are about 1.6 µm and thin filaments about 1 µm long, so overlap is optimal at about 2.0 to 2.2 µm. On the descending limb, stretch reduces overlap until force reaches zero near 3.6 µm in classic skeletal muscle studies. On the ascending limb, thin filaments from opposite sides interfere with each other, and at very short lengths thick filaments butt against the Z lines."},{"type":"p","text":"Ventricular sarcomeres work on the ascending limb, from about 1.8 to 1.9 µm at low filling to about 2.2 µm at normal to high filling. Over this range force rises several times more than overlap alone predicts because of length-dependent activation: stretch makes the myofilaments more sensitive to Ca2+, shifting the force versus Ca2+ curve to the left, so the same Ca2+ transient produces more force. Proposed mechanisms include titin strain that positions more myosin heads to bind actin, narrower filament spacing as the cell thins, and cooperative Ca2+ binding to troponin C. A slower rise in the Ca2+ transient develops over minutes after sustained stretch, but the beat-to-beat Frank-Starling response comes from overlap and Ca2+ sensitivity (CV-6)."},{"type":"p","text":"Stiff cardiac titin isoforms and collagen make passive tension rise steeply above about 2.2 µm, so intact hearts rarely reach the descending limb. A dilated failing heart weakens from reduced contractility, higher wall stress, and mitral regurgitation, not overstretch, because chronic volume overload adds sarcomeres in series. A stiff ventricle (hypertrophy, fibrosis, ischemia) needs higher filling pressure to reach the same volume."},{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal muscle grades force by recruiting motor units (size principle) and by summating twitches at higher firing rates. The heart can do neither, because every myocyte contracts each beat and the refractory period prevents summation (C01). It grades force within each cell through Ca2+ delivery (raised by beta-1 stimulation and by higher rate, which loads the SR in the positive force-frequency relationship, blunted in failing hearts), myofilament Ca2+ sensitivity (raised by stretch, lowered by acidosis and troponin I phosphorylation), and sarcomere length. These levers work because a normal Ca2+ transient does not saturate troponin C. A preload effect moves the heart along its curve with little change in the Ca2+ transient; an inotropic effect enlarges the transient and shifts the curve upward."},{"type":"table","title":"Length-tension behavior and force grading in skeletal and cardiac muscle","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"rows":[["Working sarcomere length","Near the optimum, set by bone attachments","Ascending limb, about 1.8 to 2.2 µm"],["Basis of active length-tension curve","Filament overlap","Overlap plus length-dependent Ca2+ sensitivity (steep)"],["Passive tension at long length","Low until well beyond optimum","High above about 2.2 µm"],["Recruitment and summation","Yes: motor units and tetanus","No: all cells contract; long refractory period"],["Ca2+ delivery and sensitivity","Minor role","Major levers of force"],["Role of starting length","Not used physiologically","Major: Frank-Starling mechanism"]],"caption":"Skeletal muscle varies how many fibers are active and how often they fire; cardiac muscle varies how strongly each myocyte contracts."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A cardiac muscle strip is stretched from 1.9 to 2.2 µm and its active force roughly doubles, yet filament overlap has changed only modestly. What accounts for the rest, and would a skeletal fiber show as large a change?","explain":"Most of the increase reflects length-dependent activation: the myofilaments become more sensitive to Ca2+, so the same transient recruits more cross-bridges. A skeletal fiber near its optimum relies on overlap alone and changes much less.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on length-dependent activation."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Structure sets up every mechanism here. Intercalated discs combine force-transmitting junctions with gap junctions, so the ventricle conducts and contracts as one unit and cannot recruit cells. Dyads at the Z lines place L-type channels against RyR2, and the myocyte's dense mitochondria reflect its dependence on coronary O2. Biochemistry ties it together: myosin ATPase, SERCA2a, PMCA, and the Na+/K+-ATPase all need ATP, and Gs, cAMP, and PKA link the autonomic nervous system to the Ca2+ transient."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows the same logic. Sarcomere gene mutations (MYH7, MYBPC3) cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; KCNQ1, KCNH2, and SCN5A mutations cause long QT syndromes; RYR2 or calsequestrin mutations cause catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia; skeletal RYR1 mutations cause malignant hyperthermia, a primary disorder of skeletal muscle. For any question, name the cellular lever first (trigger Ca2+, SR load, Ca2+ removal, myofilament sensitivity, or sarcomere length), then reason outward to the organ."}],"summary":["Ventricular myocytes rest at about -90 mV because IK1 dominates resting conductance and holds the membrane near EK.","Phase 0 is fast Na+ influx, phase 1 is Na+ inactivation with Ito, phase 2 is L-type Ca2+ influx balanced by K+ efflux, and phase 3 is delayed rectifier K+ efflux followed by IK1.","The effective refractory period (about 0.25 to 0.3 s) lasts nearly as long as contraction, preventing summation and tetany.","Raised extracellular K+ depolarizes the resting membrane, inactivates Na+ channels, slows conduction, and speeds repolarization.","Trigger Ca2+ through T-tubule L-type channels opens RyR2 in the dyad, so cardiac contraction requires extracellular Ca2+; skeletal Cav1.1 opens RyR1 mechanically.","SERCA2a removes about 70 percent of activator Ca2+ into the SR and NCX (3 Na+ in per Ca2+ out) most of the rest; phospholamban phosphorylation relieves SERCA2a inhibition.","Ischemia impairs relaxation first (SERCA2a, PMCA, and NCX failure) and contraction soon after (acidosis, Pi, and reduced SR loading).","Beta-1 signaling through Gs, cAMP, and PKA phosphorylates L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I, producing a stronger, faster, shorter twitch.","Cardiac sarcomeres work on the ascending limb (about 1.8 to 2.2 µm), where stretch raises both overlap and myofilament Ca2+ sensitivity; stiff titin and collagen keep them off the descending limb.","Skeletal muscle grades force by recruitment and summation; cardiac muscle grades it by Ca2+ delivery, Ca2+ sensitivity, and sarcomere length."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle; Excitation of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Electrophysiology and the Electrocardiogram; Cellular Physiology of Skeletal, Cardiac, and Smooth Muscle"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue, section on cardiac muscle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, sections on myocardial infarction and cardiomyopathies"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-6":{"unit":"CV-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01","title":"Cardiac Output and Its Regulation","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how cardiac output and arterial pressure are kept matched to the body's needs. It covers the equations linking cardiac output, stroke volume, ejection fraction, arterial pressure, and O2 consumption; the three controls of stroke volume; how the heart and the venous system jointly set output; the law of Laplace and myocardial O2 demand; the baroreceptor reflex; hormonal control of blood volume; and orthostatic hypotension. Most board questions here are predictions or calculations: a change is given, such as a hemorrhage, a stiffer aorta, or a patient who stands up, and you must say which way each variable moves. Name what the change acts on first, then follow the chain of consequences.","prerequisites":["The cardiac cycle and the left ventricular pressure-volume loop (CV-4)","The cardiac length-tension relationship and beta-1 adrenergic signaling in myocytes (CV-5)","Autonomic control of the SA and AV nodes (CV-7)","Flow equals pressure gradient divided by resistance, and vascular compliance (CV-11)","Oxygen content of blood: hemoglobin binding capacity, saturation, and dissolved O2"],"connections":[{"to":"Heart valves and the cardiac cycle (CV-4)","how":"Every change in preload, afterload, or contractility in this chapter appears as a predictable change in the shape and position of the pressure-volume loop."},{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"The Frank-Starling mechanism and inotropy are the organ-level expression of sarcomere length-dependent activation and myocyte Ca2+ handling."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output times total peripheral resistance, which is the circulation-wide form of the flow equation."},{"to":"Hypertensive heart disease and heart failure (CV-18-A, CV-18-B)","how":"Laplace's law explains concentric and eccentric remodeling, and chronic activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and ADH drives fluid retention in heart failure."},{"to":"Renal and endocrine physiology","how":"Renin, aldosterone, ADH, and the natriuretic peptides act on the nephron to set blood volume, which is the long-term determinant of arterial pressure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cardiac output (CO)","def":"The volume of blood pumped by each ventricle per minute, equal to stroke volume times heart rate."},{"term":"Stroke volume (SV)","def":"The volume ejected by a ventricle in one beat, equal to end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume."},{"term":"Ejection fraction (EF)","def":"The fraction of end-diastolic volume ejected in one beat, stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume."},{"term":"Fick principle","def":"The statement that O2 uptake by the body equals blood flow through the lungs times the arteriovenous O2 content difference."},{"term":"Preload","def":"The load that stretches ventricular muscle before contraction, best represented by end-diastolic volume."},{"term":"Afterload","def":"The load the ventricle must overcome to eject blood, best represented by systolic wall stress and approximated by aortic pressure."},{"term":"Contractility (inotropy)","def":"The intrinsic strength of contraction at a given preload and afterload, set mainly by myocyte Ca2+ handling."},{"term":"Frank-Starling mechanism","def":"The intrinsic property of the heart whereby greater end-diastolic filling produces a stronger contraction and a larger stroke volume."},{"term":"Mean systemic filling pressure","def":"The pressure that would exist throughout the systemic circulation if the heart stopped and pressures equalized, about 7 mmHg normally."},{"term":"Wall stress","def":"The force per unit cross-sectional area of the ventricular wall, which by the law of Laplace rises with pressure and radius and falls with wall thickness."},{"term":"Baroreceptor","def":"A stretch-sensitive nerve ending in the wall of the carotid sinus or aortic arch whose firing rate rises with arterial pressure."},{"term":"Orthostatic hypotension","def":"A sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C01","obj":"CV-6.1","title":"Calculating cardiac output, ejection fraction, mean arterial pressure, and Fick output","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood each ventricle pumps per minute. Because the two ventricles are in series, their outputs are equal over any period longer than a few beats. Resting CO is about 5 L/min; normalized to body surface area as the cardiac index, it is about 2.5 to 4 L/min per square meter."},{"type":"p","text":"Stroke volume (SV) is end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume. A resting left ventricle fills to an EDV of about 120 mL and empties to an ESV of about 50 mL, ejecting about 70 mL, so at 72 bpm CO is about 5 L/min. Ejection fraction (EF) is SV divided by EDV and is normally about 55 to 70%. EF partly corrects for chamber size: a dilated, weak ventricle can keep a near-normal SV by filling to a very large EDV, but its EF is low. EF is not a pure index of contractility, because it also falls when afterload rises (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Applying the flow equation to the systemic circulation gives CO = (MAP - RAP) / TPR, where MAP is mean arterial pressure, RAP is right atrial pressure, and TPR is total peripheral resistance. With RAP near 0 mmHg this becomes MAP = CO x TPR, which is why a fall in output must be offset by a rise in resistance if pressure is to hold, the job of the baroreceptor reflex (C05). At resting heart rates diastole fills about two thirds of the cycle, so MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one third of the pulse pressure: 120/80 mmHg gives about 93 mmHg. At fast rates diastole shortens more than systole, and this rule underestimates MAP. Normal TPR is about 18 mmHg per L/min, and MAP is normally about 70 to 100 mmHg."},{"type":"h","text":"The Fick principle"},{"type":"p","text":"The Fick principle is conservation of mass applied to O2. All the O2 the body consumes (VO2) must be loaded into blood flowing through the lungs, and pulmonary blood flow equals CO, so CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2). VO2 comes from expired gas analysis and is about 250 mL/min at rest. Arterial O2 content (CaO2) can be sampled from any systemic artery, but mixed venous content (CvO2) must come from the pulmonary artery, because venous blood from different organs differs widely (coronary sinus blood is far more desaturated than renal venous blood). O2 content is (1.34 x hemoglobin in g/dL x fractional saturation) + (0.003 x PO2 in mmHg), in mL O2 per dL; dissolved O2 adds only about 0.3 mL/dL. Multiply by 10 to express content per liter."},{"type":"p","text":"Read in reverse, the equation explains a key compensation. If CO falls while VO2 is unchanged, tissues extract more O2 from each liter of blood, so the arteriovenous O2 difference widens and mixed venous saturation falls. A low mixed venous saturation therefore signals that delivery is inadequate for demand. The same principle applied to one organ's O2 uptake and arteriovenous difference gives that organ's blood flow."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating output by two routes","text":"A resting patient has HR 75 bpm, EDV 140 mL, and ESV 60 mL, so SV is 80 mL, CO is 6.0 L/min, and EF is about 57%. To check by Fick: VO2 is 300 mL/min, hemoglobin 15 g/dL, arterial saturation 98% (PO2 95 mmHg), and pulmonary artery saturation 73% (PO2 40 mmHg). CaO2 = (1.34 x 15 x 0.98) + (0.003 x 95) = 20.0 mL/dL. CvO2 = (1.34 x 15 x 0.73) + (0.003 x 40) = 14.8 mL/dL. The difference, 5.2 mL/dL, equals 52 mL O2 per L of blood, so CO = 300 / 52 = 5.8 L/min, close to the volumetric value. Skipping the dL to L conversion gives an answer ten times off."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C02","obj":"CV-6.2","title":"Preload, afterload, contractility, and the Frank-Starling curve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Stroke volume has three determinants. Preload sets how far the muscle is stretched before it contracts, afterload sets how hard it must push to eject, and contractility sets how strongly it contracts at a given preload and afterload. For a prediction question, decide which one a change acts on first, then follow EDV and ESV, since SV is their difference."},{"type":"h","text":"Preload and the Frank-Starling mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Preload is strictly end-diastolic wall stress; EDV is its practical index. End-diastolic pressure (estimated for the left ventricle by pulmonary capillary wedge pressure) tracks volume only when compliance is normal, so a stiff ventricle can show high filling pressure at a normal EDV."},{"type":"p","text":"Within physiologic limits, the more the heart fills, the more it ejects (the Frank-Starling mechanism); the curve of SV against EDV rises steeply and then flattens. Its basis is length-dependent activation (CV-5): stretch toward an optimal sarcomere length of about 2.2 micrometers improves filament overlap and, more importantly, raises the Ca2+ sensitivity of troponin C, so the same Ca2+ transient produces more force. More filling raises SV with ESV about unchanged. The normal heart works on the ascending limb, where stiff myocardium and the pericardium resist overstretch. The mechanism also keeps right and left outputs matched beat to beat."},{"type":"h","text":"Afterload"},{"type":"p","text":"Afterload is systolic wall stress (C04), approximated for the left ventricle by aortic pressure, because the ventricle must exceed aortic diastolic pressure to open the aortic valve. It rises with hypertension, aortic stenosis, a stiff aorta, and a dilated chamber; right ventricular afterload is pulmonary arterial pressure. Higher afterload means more of each contraction is spent building pressure, so ESV rises and SV and EF fall. Over the next beats the residual blood adds to normal filling, EDV rises, and the Frank-Starling mechanism restores much of the lost SV at a higher filling pressure."},{"type":"h","text":"Contractility"},{"type":"p","text":"Contractility (inotropy) is the strength of contraction at fixed preload and afterload, set by myofilament Ca2+ delivery and responsiveness. On the pressure-volume loop it is the slope of the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship (ESPVR). An inotropic change shifts the whole Frank-Starling curve, giving a different SV at the same EDV. Contractility rises with beta-1 stimulation (protein kinase A phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels and phospholamban), with faster heart rate (the force-frequency or Bowditch effect), and with Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition by cardiac glycosides, which slows Ca2+ removal by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger and loads the SR. It falls with ischemia and hypoxia (less ATP), acidosis (H+ competes with Ca2+ for troponin C), loss of muscle, and beta-1 or L-type Ca2+ channel blockade."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the effect of each primary change on the left ventricle","cols":["Primary change","EDV","ESV","SV and EF","Frank-Starling curve","Pressure-volume loop"],"rows":[["Increased preload","Up","About the same","SV up; EF slightly up","Moves up the same curve","Loop widens to the right"],["Increased afterload","Same, then up","Up","Both down","Shifts down and right","Taller and narrower; ESV point moves right on the same ESPVR"],["Increased contractility","Same, then slightly down","Down","Both up","Shifts up and left","Steeper ESPVR; loop widens to the left"]],"caption":"Preload acts through EDV; afterload and contractility act through ESV. Loop width is SV. Decreases mirror these rows."},{"type":"p","text":"Faster rates raise CO, but extra beats come mostly out of diastole, so at very high rates EDV, SV, and then CO fall. Sympathetic lusitropy (faster relaxation) and inotropy raise this limit in exercise, while a stiff ventricle that depends on atrial contraction loses output at lower rates."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Aortic pressure rises suddenly with venous return and contractility unchanged. What happens to EDV over several beats, and why does SV partly recover?","explain":"ESV rises, normal filling adds to the larger residual volume, and EDV increases; the Frank-Starling mechanism then restores much of the lost SV at a higher filling pressure.","check":"Compare with the afterload paragraph and the increased afterload row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C03","obj":"CV-6.3","title":"Cardiac function and venous return curves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The heart can pump only what returns to it. Guyton's method plots the cardiac function curve and the venous return curve on the same axes, with right atrial pressure (RAP) on the horizontal axis and flow on the vertical axis. In the steady state CO equals venous return, so the operating point is where the two curves cross."},{"type":"p","text":"The cardiac function curve is the Frank-Starling curve with RAP as the measure of filling: CO rises steeply with RAP and then plateaus. Positive inotropy shifts it up and to the left. Negative inotropy, as after myocardial infarction, or increased afterload shifts it down and to the right."},{"type":"h","text":"The venous return curve"},{"type":"p","text":"Venous return = (MSFP - RAP) / RVR. Mean systemic filling pressure (MSFP), about 7 mmHg, is the pressure that would exist throughout the systemic circulation if the heart stopped and pressures equalized; RVR is the resistance to venous return, most of which lies in the veins. The curve meets the horizontal axis where RAP equals MSFP and flow is zero, rises with a slope of 1/RVR as RAP falls, and plateaus once RAP falls below atmospheric pressure, because the large veins entering the thorax collapse."},{"type":"p","text":"MSFP depends on blood volume relative to vascular capacity, chiefly the veins, which hold about two thirds of the blood. Only stressed volume, the blood above what fills the vessels without stretching them, generates pressure. Transfusion raises MSFP, and so does venoconstriction, which converts unstressed volume into stressed volume; hemorrhage and venodilation lower it. A change in MSFP shifts the curve parallel to itself, while a change in resistance rotates it about the same x-intercept. In the standard analysis, arteriolar constriction raises TPR, rotates the venous return curve downward, and adds afterload that shifts the cardiac function curve down; arteriolar dilation does the reverse."},{"type":"table","title":"New operating point after common perturbations","cols":["Change","Curve affected and how","Cardiac output","Right atrial pressure"],"rows":[["Hemorrhage","Venous return curve shifts left (MSFP falls)","Down","Down"],["Volume infusion or venoconstriction","Venous return curve shifts right (MSFP rises)","Up","Up"],["Increased contractility","Cardiac function curve shifts up and left","Up","Down"],["Decreased contractility (heart failure)","Cardiac function curve shifts down and right","Down","Up"],["Increased TPR (arteriolar constriction)","Venous return curve rotates down; cardiac function curve shifts down","Down","About unchanged"],["Decreased TPR (exercising muscle, AV fistula)","Venous return curve rotates up; cardiac function curve shifts up","Up","About unchanged"]],"caption":"Volume and venous tone act on MSFP and move CO and RAP in the same direction. Contractility moves them in opposite directions. TPR moves both curves together, so CO changes while RAP stays about the same."},{"type":"p","text":"The direction of RAP is the most useful clue. When CO and RAP change in the same direction, the primary problem is vascular (volume or venous tone); when they change in opposite directions, it is in the heart. A low CO with a high RAP points to pump failure, and a low CO with a low RAP points to inadequate filling."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hemorrhage on the cardiac function and venous return diagram","steps":["Blood loss reduces stressed volume","MSFP falls; venous return curve shifts left","New intersection at lower RAP and lower CO","Lower MAP reduces baroreceptor firing","Sympathetic venoconstriction raises MSFP toward normal","Sympathetic inotropy shifts the cardiac function curve up","CO partly recovers while RAP stays low"],"caption":"A vascular shift, partly corrected by reflex venoconstriction and inotropy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with an acute anterior infarction has a low cardiac output. Over the next days the kidneys retain salt and water. Show on the curves what the retention does to CO and RAP.","explain":"The infarct shifts the cardiac function curve down (low CO, high RAP). Retention raises MSFP and shifts the venous return curve right, so CO improves somewhat but RAP rises further, supporting output at the cost of venous congestion and edema.","check":"Compare with the operating point table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C04","obj":"CV-6.4","title":"Law of Laplace, ventricular remodeling, and myocardial O2 demand","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The law of Laplace, in the form used for a thick-walled chamber, gives wall stress = (P x r) / (2 x h), where P is intraventricular pressure, r is chamber radius, and h is wall thickness. Wall stress is the force each unit of wall must develop. In systole it is the afterload (C02) and a major determinant of myocardial O2 demand. Higher pressure or a larger radius raises stress, so a dilated ventricle must develop more force per unit of wall to produce the same pressure; a thicker wall lowers stress by sharing the load among more muscle."},{"type":"h","text":"Remodeling under chronic overload"},{"type":"p","text":"In pressure overload, as in hypertension or aortic stenosis, systolic wall stress rises. The sustained load, aided by angiotensin II and catecholamines, makes myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel, so the wall thickens with a normal or small cavity (concentric hypertrophy). The larger h returns systolic stress toward normal; for example, raising systolic pressure from 120 to 200 mmHg increases stress by about two thirds, and thickening the wall from 1.0 to about 1.7 cm restores it. The cost is a stiff chamber that needs a high filling pressure (diastolic dysfunction, S4), and capillary growth that lags myocyte growth, so the subendocardium becomes vulnerable to ischemia even with normal coronary arteries."},{"type":"p","text":"In volume overload, as in aortic or mitral regurgitation, raised diastolic wall stress makes myocytes add sarcomeres in series, so the chamber enlarges (eccentric hypertrophy). The wall thickens enough to keep the thickness to radius ratio about normal, holding stress near normal while the chamber delivers a large SV. When a failing heart dilates further, as in dilated cardiomyopathy or after a large infarction, radius grows without matching thickness, so wall stress and the O2 cost of every beat rise in muscle that is already weakened."},{"type":"table","title":"Concentric versus eccentric remodeling","cols":["Feature","Concentric hypertrophy","Eccentric hypertrophy and dilation"],"rows":[["Typical cause","Pressure overload: hypertension, aortic stenosis","Volume overload: aortic or mitral regurgitation; failing dilated ventricle"],["Signal","Raised systolic wall stress","Raised diastolic wall stress"],["Sarcomere addition","In parallel (wider cells)","In series (longer cells)"],["Thickness to radius ratio","Increased","About normal when compensated; decreased in decompensated dilation"],["Wall stress","Returns toward normal","Near normal when compensated; rises as the chamber dilates"],["Typical consequence","Stiff chamber, diastolic dysfunction, S4","Large SV when compensated; systolic dysfunction, S3 when decompensated"]],"caption":"Both patterns attempt to restore normal wall stress; they differ in whether the overload acts in systole or diastole."},{"type":"h","text":"Determinants of myocardial O2 demand"},{"type":"p","text":"Myocardial O2 demand is set mainly by heart rate (the number of tension-generating cycles per minute), contractility (ATP used in Ca2+ cycling and cross-bridge activity), and systolic wall stress. Developing pressure costs far more O2 than ejecting volume, so pressure work is much more expensive than volume work. The rate-pressure (double) product, heart rate times systolic pressure, is a simple index of demand."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart already extracts about 70 to 75% of the O2 delivered to it at rest, so increased demand must be met almost entirely by increased coronary flow (CV-8), and most left ventricular flow occurs in diastole. Tachycardia is therefore doubly harmful with a fixed coronary stenosis: it raises demand and shortens the diastolic time available for supply."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C05","obj":"CV-6.5","title":"The baroreceptor reflex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The arterial baroreceptor reflex is a negative feedback loop that buffers arterial pressure within seconds."},{"type":"h","text":"Sensors and afferent pathways"},{"type":"p","text":"Baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive endings in the adventitia of the carotid sinus, a dilation at the origin of the internal carotid artery, and of the aortic arch. Carotid sinus afferents run in the carotid sinus nerve (nerve of Hering) to the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX); aortic arch afferents run in the vagus (CN X). Both end in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) of the dorsal medulla. Carotid receptors are nearly silent below about 50 to 60 mmHg and fire maximally near 180 mmHg, with the steepest response around normal MAP; aortic receptors operate about 30 mmHg higher. Firing also rises with the rate of pressure change. Because the receptors reset within 1 to 2 days, the reflex corrects short-term changes but does not set long-term pressure, which depends on renal control of volume (C06)."},{"type":"h","text":"Central integration and effectors"},{"type":"p","text":"Afferents release glutamate onto NTS neurons. The NTS excites vagal preganglionic neurons in the nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and, through the caudal ventrolateral medulla, inhibits the sympathetic premotor neurons of the rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM) that drive the intermediolateral cell column. A rise in pressure therefore raises vagal and lowers sympathetic output, and a fall does the opposite. The effectors are the SA node, the AV node, the ventricular myocardium (sympathetic only; the vagus has little direct effect on ventricular force), arterioles (alpha-1), veins (venous tone and MSFP), the adrenal medulla, and juxtaglomerular cells (renin through beta-1). Nodal ion channel mechanisms are in CV-7."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Baroreceptor reflex response to a fall in arterial pressure","steps":["Arterial pressure falls","Carotid sinus and aortic arch stretch decreases","Afferent firing in CN IX and CN X to the NTS falls","Vagal output falls; RVLM sympathetic output rises","Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance, and venous tone rise","CO and TPR rise; MAP returns toward its set point"],"caption":"The response opposes the original change, and rising pressure restores baroreceptor firing. A rise in pressure runs the loop in reverse."},{"type":"h","text":"Predicting the response"},{"type":"p","text":"On standing, roughly half a liter of blood pools in the legs and pelvis. Reduced firing raises heart rate by about 10 to 20 bpm and constricts arterioles and veins, restoring MAP within about a minute with systolic pressure slightly lower and diastolic pressure slightly higher (C07). After hemorrhage the same reflex causes tachycardia, venoconstriction, and arteriolar constriction concentrated in skin, gut, and kidney, which explains cool pale skin, low urine output, and a narrow pulse pressure; a loss of about 10% of blood volume barely changes arterial pressure. Below a MAP of about 80 mmHg peripheral chemoreceptors add sympathetic drive, and below about 60 mmHg the brainstem ischemic response produces an intense sympathetic discharge."},{"type":"p","text":"Carotid sinus massage stretches the receptors as if pressure had risen, so vagal output rises and sympathetic output falls: the SA node slows, AV nodal conduction lengthens, and arterioles dilate. Slowed AV nodal conduction explains why carotid pressure can interrupt a tachycardia whose circuit runs through the AV node, and carotid sinus hypersensitivity in older adults can cause bradycardia and syncope from a tight collar or head turning. A drug that raises pressure by alpha-1 vasoconstriction likewise causes reflex bradycardia. Atrial and pulmonary low-pressure receptors sense volume rather than arterial pressure and, mainly through the vagus, adjust heart rate, renal sympathetic activity, and ADH release, linking this reflex to C06."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A drug causes pure arteriolar vasodilation and MAP falls. Predict heart rate, contractility, and plasma renin activity.","explain":"Lower MAP reduces baroreceptor firing, so vagal tone falls and sympathetic output rises. Heart rate and contractility increase (reflex tachycardia), and beta-1 stimulation of juxtaglomerular cells, together with lower renal perfusion pressure, raises renin release.","check":"Compare with the effectors and the reflex cycle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C06","obj":"CV-6.6","title":"Hormonal control of blood volume and pressure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Over hours to days, arterial pressure is set mainly by blood volume. Body Na+ content largely determines extracellular and plasma volume, while water balance determines osmolality. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and antidiuretic hormone (ADH) defend volume and pressure, and the natriuretic peptides oppose expansion."},{"type":"h","text":"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system"},{"type":"p","text":"Juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole release renin in response to low renal perfusion pressure (less arteriolar stretch), low macula densa NaCl delivery, and beta-1 stimulation; angiotensin II and ANP inhibit release. Renin cleaves hepatic angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, the rate-limiting step, and angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), most abundant on pulmonary capillary endothelium, forms angiotensin II. ACE also inactivates the vasodilator bradykinin. Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent, preserving filtration pressure when renal perfusion is low, and chronically promotes cardiac and vascular hypertrophy and fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone enters principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct and binds the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor, increasing transcription of apical epithelial Na+ channels (ENaC) and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. Na+ reabsorption rises, the lumen-negative potential drives K+ secretion, and intercalated cells secrete more H+. Because it acts through new protein synthesis, its effect takes hours."},{"type":"flow","title":"Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone response to low renal perfusion","steps":["Low perfusion pressure, low macula densa NaCl, or beta-1 input","Juxtaglomerular cells release renin","Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","Pulmonary ACE forms angiotensin II","Arteriolar constriction, proximal Na+ reabsorption, thirst, ADH","Aldosterone increases ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells","Na+ and water retention raise volume and pressure"],"caption":"Restored pressure and volume remove the stimulus for renin release."},{"type":"h","text":"ADH and the natriuretic peptides"},{"type":"p","text":"ADH (vasopressin) is released mainly in response to a rise in plasma osmolality (normal about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg), which anterior hypothalamic osmoreceptors detect at changes of about 1%. A fall in volume or pressure needs a larger change (on the order of 10% or more) but then produces levels high enough for V1 vasoconstriction. Angiotensin II, nausea, and pain increase release; atrial stretch inhibits it. Through V2 receptors and cAMP, ADH inserts collecting duct aquaporin-2 channels and raises inner medullary urea permeability. Because ADH retains water rather than Na+, its main job is correcting osmolality."},{"type":"p","text":"The natriuretic peptides oppose the RAAS and ADH. They dilate the afferent and constrict the efferent arteriole, raising GFR; inhibit collecting duct Na+ reabsorption; inhibit renin, aldosterone, and ADH; relax vascular smooth muscle; and increase capillary permeability. In heart failure their levels are high but the renal response is blunted, so they cannot overcome RAAS and sympathetic drive."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormonal regulators of volume and pressure","cols":["Hormone","Source","Main stimulus","Receptor and messenger","Main actions"],"rows":[["Angiotensin II","Formed from angiotensin I by ACE","Renin release","AT1, Gq","Arteriolar and efferent constriction, proximal Na+/H+ exchange, aldosterone, ADH, thirst, norepinephrine release"],["Aldosterone","Zona glomerulosa","Angiotensin II; high plasma K+","Intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor, gene transcription","ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase up; Na+ reabsorption, K+ and H+ secretion"],["ADH (vasopressin)","Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; posterior pituitary","High osmolality; large fall in volume or pressure","V2, Gs, cAMP (kidney); V1, Gq (vessels)","Aquaporin-2 insertion and water reabsorption; vasoconstriction at high levels"],["ANP","Atrial myocytes","Atrial stretch","NPR-A guanylyl cyclase, cGMP","Natriuresis, raised GFR, vasodilation; inhibits renin, aldosterone, ADH"],["BNP","Mainly ventricular myocytes","Ventricular stretch and wall stress","NPR-A guanylyl cyclase, cGMP","Same as ANP; BNP and inactive NT-proBNP mark ventricular strain"]],"caption":"Angiotensin II, aldosterone, and ADH conserve volume; the natriuretic peptides discard it."},{"type":"p","text":"The baroreceptor reflex acts in seconds, angiotensin II in minutes, and aldosterone over hours to days. Long-term control rests on renal pressure natriuresis, in which higher pressure increases Na+ and water excretion until volume and pressure return to the level the kidney defends. In primary hyperaldosteronism, Na+ retention raises pressure, but within days the higher pressure and ANP increase excretion enough to halt expansion (aldosterone escape), so hypertension and hypokalemia occur without edema."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-06-01-C07","obj":"CV-6.7","title":"Orthostatic hypotension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Orthostatic hypotension is a sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg, or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg, within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt to at least 60 degrees. It is a sign that the normal response to standing has failed."},{"type":"h","text":"Why standing threatens arterial pressure"},{"type":"p","text":"Standing raises foot venous pressure to about 90 mmHg in a motionless adult. Roughly half a liter of blood pools in the leg and pelvic veins within seconds and plasma filters into the leg interstitium over minutes, so venous return, SV, CO, and MAP fall. Normally the baroreceptor reflex (C05) raises heart rate by about 10 to 20 bpm and constricts splanchnic and muscle arterioles and veins, the skeletal muscle pump drives blood upward past venous valves, and renin and ADH add support over minutes. Symptoms of failure appear once MAP at head level falls below the lower limit of cerebral autoregulation (about 60 mmHg): lightheadedness, blurred vision, weakness, cognitive slowing, and syncope, with a coat-hanger ache of the neck and shoulders in neurogenic forms."},{"type":"h","text":"Mechanisms"},{"type":"p","text":"Volume depletion (hemorrhage, vomiting, diarrhea, diuresis, burns) lowers MSFP before the person stands. Adrenal insufficiency does the same by a hormonal route: aldosterone loss causes renal Na+ wasting, and cortisol loss reduces vascular responsiveness to catecholamines. With the reflex intact, heart rate rises vigorously, often 30 bpm or more."},{"type":"p","text":"In neurogenic orthostatic hypotension the efferent sympathetic pathway cannot release enough norepinephrine, so resistance and venous tone do not rise and heart rate rises little despite a large fall in pressure, the key distinction from volume depletion. Supine hypertension is common, partly because denervated vessels are hypersensitive to circulating pressors. In diabetic autonomic neuropathy, the classic cause, aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol and depletes NADPH (less glutathione regeneration, more oxidative stress), advanced glycation end products accumulate, and vasa nervorum disease adds ischemia. Long fibers fail first; vagal loss often precedes sympathetic loss, giving a fixed resting tachycardia before splanchnic vasoconstrictor loss causes orthostatic hypotension. Other causes include Parkinson disease, multiple system atrophy, pure autonomic failure, and amyloid neuropathy; alpha-1 blockade mimics it."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged bed rest shifts blood toward the thorax and stretches the atria, so ANP rises, ADH falls, and diuresis lowers plasma volume within days. The ventricle becomes smaller and stiffer, baroreflex sensitivity falls, and the muscle pump weakens; spaceflight has the same effect. POTS, by contrast, is a heart rate rise of at least 30 bpm in adults on standing with symptoms but no significant fall in pressure, and in vasovagal syncope sympathetic tone is suddenly withdrawn while vagal output surges, so pressure and heart rate fall together."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the main mechanisms","cols":["Feature","Volume depletion","Autonomic failure","Prolonged bed rest"],"rows":[["Primary defect","Low circulating volume and MSFP","Failed efferent sympathetic response","Low plasma volume, blunted reflexes, smaller ventricle"],["Reflex arc","Intact","Defective (efferent limb)","Intact but less sensitive"],["Heart rate on standing","Large rise","Little or no rise","Rise, often exaggerated"],["Supine blood pressure","Normal or low","Often high","Usually normal"],["Clues","Thirst, low urine output, raised BUN to creatinine ratio","Fixed heart rate, gastroparesis, bladder dysfunction","Recent immobility"]],"caption":"The heart rate response separates the two main mechanisms."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting two sets of orthostatic vital signs","text":"After three days of vomiting, patient A goes from 112/70 mmHg (HR 92 bpm) supine to 88/62 mmHg (HR 128 bpm) at 3 minutes standing: systolic pressure fell 24 mmHg and heart rate rose 36 bpm, so an intact reflex is working against too little volume. Patient B, with long-standing diabetes, goes from 162/88 mmHg (HR 96 bpm) to 118/70 mmHg (HR 100 bpm): a larger fall with a 4 bpm rise, so the sympathetic response is missing. Supine hypertension and a high, fixed resting rate support autonomic failure. The heart rate response separates the two mechanisms."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy places the controls. Carotid sinus afferents travel with CN IX and aortic arch afferents with CN X, so neck procedures or trauma at the carotid bifurcation can disturb pressure and heart rate. The juxtaglomerular apparatus sets renin cells beside the macula densa, letting one site sense both perfusion pressure and tubular NaCl, and thin-walled, compliant veins make the venous system the reservoir that pools on standing and that venoconstriction mobilizes. Second messengers tie the hormones together: Gq for angiotensin II and V1, Gs and cAMP for V2 and cardiac beta-1 receptors, cGMP for the natriuretic peptides, and gene transcription for aldosterone."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows when these controls are overloaded: pressure overload causes concentric hypertrophy and diastolic dysfunction, dilation raises wall stress, and chronic sympathetic, RAAS, and ADH activation drives fluid retention and remodeling in heart failure (CV-18-B). Inappropriate ADH secretion causes hyponatremia rather than hypertension because ADH retains water rather than Na+. The prediction tables assume one variable changes while the others stay fixed, but reflexes respond within seconds, so distinguish the immediate effect of a change from the net effect after compensation."}],"summary":["CO = SV x HR, SV = EDV - ESV, and EF = SV / EDV; at rest CO is about 5 L/min, SV about 70 mL, and EF about 55 to 70%.","MAP = CO x TPR (with RAP near 0), and at resting heart rates MAP is about diastolic pressure plus one third of the pulse pressure.","Fick: CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2), with CvO2 from the pulmonary artery and mL/dL multiplied by 10; at constant VO2 a widening arteriovenous O2 difference means CO has fallen.","Preload changes SV through EDV along one Frank-Starling curve; afterload raises ESV and contractility lowers it, and both shift the curve.","Venous return = (MSFP - RAP) / RVR; volume and venoconstriction shift the curve, resistance rotates it; CO and RAP moving together indicate a vascular cause, moving oppositely a cardiac cause.","Wall stress = P x r / 2h: concentric hypertrophy in pressure overload normalizes stress, while dilation raises stress and O2 demand.","Myocardial O2 demand is set by heart rate, contractility, and systolic wall stress; because extraction is already about 70 to 75%, more demand needs more coronary flow.","Baroreceptor afferents (carotid sinus in CN IX, aortic arch in CN X) reach the NTS; falling pressure, as on standing or after hemorrhage, lowers vagal and raises sympathetic output, and carotid sinus massage does the reverse.","Angiotensin II constricts arterioles and stimulates aldosterone, ADH, and proximal Na+ reabsorption; aldosterone increases ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase; ADH inserts aquaporin-2 through V2; ANP and BNP act through cGMP to oppose all three.","Orthostatic hypotension is a fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing; a large heart rate rise points to volume depletion and little or no rise to autonomic failure."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Output, Venous Return, and Their Regulation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Nervous Regulation of the Circulation and Rapid Control of Arterial Pressure"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the Kidneys in Long-Term Control of Arterial Pressure and in Hypertension"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The heart as a pump; regulation of arterial pressure and cardiac output"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on cardiac hypertrophy and heart failure"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease; Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex"}]},"CV-7":{"unit":"CV-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01","title":"Conduction System and the Electrocardiogram","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the heart generates its own rhythm and carries it through the atria and ventricles in a fixed order. It covers conduction anatomy and blood supply, pacemaker ion currents, and how the electrocardiogram (ECG) records these events, then applies them to blocks, arrhythmias, accessory pathways, and sinus node disease. Board questions usually give a strip, a lead group, or a clinical setting and ask you to work back to the structure, ion current, or artery responsible. If you can explain why the AV node is slow, why the Purkinje system is fast, and which way a wave of depolarization moves relative to a lead, you can reason through most ECG questions.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential and the Nernst equation for K+, Na+, and Ca2+","Phases of the ventricular myocyte action potential and refractory periods (CV-5)","Gross anatomy of the heart chambers, septa, and fibrous skeleton (CV-2, CV-3)","Coronary artery origins, branches, and dominance (CV-8)","Autonomic receptors and G protein signaling (Gs, Gi, cAMP, protein kinase A)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"The ventricular action potential that the conduction system triggers is the same one whose plateau and repolarization shape the ST segment and T wave."},{"to":"Coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"Which artery supplies the SA node, AV node, and bundle branches predicts the conduction problems that follow each infarct."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Ischemia slows conduction and alters refractoriness, which creates the substrate for reentrant ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation."},{"to":"Cardiovascular pathogens and immunology (CV-16, CV-17)","how":"Lyme carditis and maternal anti-Ro/SSA antibodies are classic causes of AV block, linking microbiology and immunology to conduction."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"Plasma K+, Ca2+, and Mg2+ set the membrane potential and repolarization of cardiac cells, so electrolyte disorders appear on the ECG."}],"terms":[{"term":"Automaticity","def":"The ability of a cell to depolarize spontaneously to threshold and fire an action potential without an external stimulus."},{"term":"Pacemaker potential","def":"The slow diastolic depolarization (phase 4) of nodal and Purkinje cells that carries the membrane from its most negative value up to threshold."},{"term":"Funny current (If)","def":"An inward current, mainly Na+, carried by HCN channels that open when the membrane hyperpolarizes and that begins phase 4 depolarization."},{"term":"Overdrive suppression","def":"Suppression of slower latent pacemakers by a faster pacemaker, so the fastest site normally controls the heart."},{"term":"Decremental conduction","def":"The property of AV nodal tissue whereby conduction slows progressively as impulses arrive more frequently."},{"term":"Escape rhythm","def":"A rhythm generated by a latent pacemaker below the site of failure when the higher pacemaker fails or its impulses are blocked."},{"term":"Mean QRS axis","def":"The average direction of ventricular depolarization in the frontal plane, expressed in degrees."},{"term":"QTc","def":"The QT interval corrected for heart rate, most often by dividing QT by the square root of the RR interval in seconds (Bazett formula)."},{"term":"AV dissociation","def":"Independent activity of atria and ventricles, each driven by its own pacemaker, as in complete heart block."},{"term":"Reentry","def":"Repeated reactivation of tissue by an impulse that travels around a circuit, requiring unidirectional block and sufficiently slow conduction."},{"term":"Triggered activity","def":"Extra depolarizations arising during or just after repolarization (early or delayed afterdepolarizations) that can reach threshold."},{"term":"Preexcitation","def":"Early activation of part of the ventricle through an accessory pathway, producing a short PR interval and delta wave."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C01","obj":"CV-7.1","title":"Anatomy of the conduction system and the purpose of AV nodal delay","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac myocytes are joined by gap junctions, so current passes directly from cell to cell. The conduction system imposes order on this syncytium: a fast pacemaker high in the right atrium, a deliberate delay at the only electrical gateway into the ventricles, and a high-speed distribution network inside them."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinoatrial (SA) node lies beneath the epicardium of the right atrium at its junction with the superior vena cava, at the upper end of the sulcus terminalis (crista terminalis inside). The impulse spreads through atrial muscle, reaching the left atrium through the anterior interatrial band. The atrioventricular (AV) node sits in the lower interatrial septum within the triangle of Koch (coronary sinus opening, septal tricuspid leaflet, tendon of Todaro). The bundle of His leaves it, pierces the fibrous skeleton, and runs along the lower border of the membranous septum. Because the fibrous skeleton is an insulator, the AV node and His bundle are the only normal electrical path from atria to ventricles."},{"type":"p","text":"The right bundle branch descends the right side of the septum, partly through the moderator band to the anterior papillary muscle, so that muscle tenses its chordae before the free wall contracts. The left bundle fans into anterior and posterior fascicles. Both end in subendocardial Purkinje fibers, which activate the apex and septum before the base and the wall from endocardium to epicardium."},{"type":"diagram","name":"conduction-pathway","caption":"SA node through the atria to the AV node, bundle of His, right and left bundle branches, and subendocardial Purkinje fibers.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-7-1.png"},{"type":"p","text":"Conduction velocity depends on fiber diameter, gap junction density, and the upstroke current. Purkinje fibers are large, well coupled, and use fast Na+ channels; AV nodal cells are small, poorly coupled, rest near -60 mV, and depolarize through slow L-type Ca2+ channels."},{"type":"table","title":"Conduction speed and intrinsic rate along the pathway","cols":["Site","Conduction velocity","Intrinsic rate","Upstroke"],"rows":[["SA node","About 0.05 m/s","About 100 bpm denervated; 60 to 100 at rest (vagal tone)","L-type Ca2+"],["Atrial muscle","About 0.3 m/s","None normally","Fast Na+"],["AV node","About 0.05 m/s, slowest","About 40 to 60 bpm (junctional escape)","L-type Ca2+"],["Purkinje fibers","About 1.5 to 4 m/s, fastest","About 20 to 40 bpm (ventricular escape)","Fast Na+"],["Ventricular muscle","About 0.3 to 0.5 m/s","None normally","Fast Na+"]],"caption":"Fastest to slowest: Purkinje, atrial muscle, ventricular muscle, AV node. Rate falls with distance from the SA node, setting the escape hierarchy."},{"type":"p","text":"The impulse reaches the AV node about 0.03 s after leaving the SA node, is held about 0.09 s in the node and 0.04 s in the penetrating bundle (total AV delay about 0.13 s), then crosses the whole ventricle through the His-Purkinje system in well under 0.1 s, which keeps the QRS narrow. The delay lets atrial systole, which adds up to about 20% of filling at rest and more when the ventricle is stiff or the rate fast, finish before the AV valves close, raising end-diastolic volume and stroke volume through the Frank-Starling mechanism. The node also filters: its refractory period outlasts its action potential, so when the atria fire 300 to 600 times per minute in flutter or fibrillation, most impulses are blocked. An accessory pathway removes both the delay and the filter (C07). When conduction must travel myocyte to myocyte instead, as in bundle branch block or a ventricular ectopic beat, contraction becomes less synchronous."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C02","obj":"CV-7.2","title":"The pacemaker action potential and its autonomic control","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Working myocytes rest near -85 to -90 mV because inward rectifier K+ channels (IK1) clamp the membrane near the K+ equilibrium potential. Nodal cells have very few IK1 channels, so after each beat their membrane drifts toward threshold. This instability is the basis of automaticity. Their most negative value, the maximum diastolic potential, is about -60 mV, where most fast Na+ channels are inactivated."},{"type":"bullets","items":["Phase 4: HCN channels open on repolarization and carry the funny current (If), mainly Na+ influx; delayed rectifier K+ current is switching off; late in phase 4, T-type Ca2+ current and Ca2+ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (extruded by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger) add inward current.","Phase 0: at about -40 mV, L-type Ca2+ channels open, giving a slow, small upstroke.","Phase 3: L-type channels inactivate and delayed rectifier K+ efflux returns the cell to about -60 mV.","There is no phase 1 notch or phase 2 plateau. Refractoriness outlasts repolarization because Ca2+ channel recovery is time dependent."]},{"type":"diagram","name":"pacemaker-ap","caption":"SA nodal action potential: phase 4 rise from If and T-type Ca2+ current, Ca2+-driven phase 0 at about -40 mV, and phase 3 K+ repolarization to about -60 mV.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-7-2.png"},{"type":"diagram","name":"ventricular-ap","caption":"Ventricular myocyte action potential for comparison: stable phase 4 near -90 mV, fast Na+ phase 0, phase 1 notch, Ca2+ plateau (phase 2), and K+ repolarization (phase 3).","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-7-3.png"},{"type":"p","text":"Rate depends on the phase 4 slope, the maximum diastolic potential, and the threshold. The SA node has the steepest slope, so it fires first and depolarizes latent pacemakers before they reach threshold. Driving them faster than their own rate also loads them with Na+, increasing electrogenic Na+/K+-ATPase activity and hyperpolarizing them (overdrive suppression), which is why a latent pacemaker often pauses before it escapes when the SA node stops."},{"type":"p","text":"A denervated SA node fires at about 100 bpm, so a resting rate of 60 to 80 bpm reflects tonic vagal restraint. Sympathetic stimulation acts through beta-1 receptors and Gs, as shown below, raising rate and speeding AV conduction (shorter PR)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sympathetic acceleration of the SA node","steps":["Norepinephrine binds beta-1 receptors on nodal cells","Gs activates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP rises","cAMP binds HCN channels directly; If increases","Protein kinase A phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels","Phase 4 steepens; threshold is reached sooner","Rate rises; AV conduction speeds"],"caption":"cAMP shifts HCN activation to less negative voltages, and protein kinase A increases Ca2+ current."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetylcholine acts on M2 receptors coupled to Gi. The alpha subunit lowers cAMP, reducing If; the beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine-activated K+ channels (IKACh), making the maximum diastolic potential more negative, and reduced Ca2+ current raises the effective threshold. The cell starts lower, climbs more slowly, and must climb further. In the AV node the same changes slow conduction and lengthen the PR; strong vagal discharge, as in the baroreceptor reflex or a vasovagal episode, can cause a sinus pause or transient AV block. Channel blockade confirms the physiology: blocking cardiac L-type Ca2+ channels slows sinus rate and AV conduction with little effect on the ventricular Na+ upstroke, and selective If blockade slows the rate without changing contractility."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a drug blocked HCN channels completely, would the SA node stop firing?","explain":"No. T-type Ca2+ current, falling delayed rectifier K+ current, and Ca2+ release with Na+/Ca2+ exchange still depolarize the cell, and it still lacks IK1. The slope flattens and rate falls, but automaticity persists.","check":"The phase 4 currents and the role of IK1."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C03","obj":"CV-7.3","title":"Blood supply of the nodes and conduction defects in inferior infarction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The conduction system is fed by small coronary branches, so the infarct location predicts the rhythm disturbance. The SA nodal artery arises from the proximal right coronary artery (RCA) in about 60% of hearts and the circumflex in about 40%. The AV nodal artery arises from the artery crossing the crux near the origin of the posterior descending artery, so it comes from the RCA in about 80% (right dominance). Below the node, septal perforators of the left anterior descending artery (LAD) supply the anterior two thirds of the septum, including the right bundle and left anterior fascicle. The His bundle has a dual supply (AV nodal artery and first septal perforator), and the broad left posterior fascicle receives LAD septal branches and the posterior descending artery, which is why isolated posterior fascicular block is uncommon."},{"type":"p","text":"Inferior MI (ST elevation in II, III, aVF) usually follows RCA occlusion and produces nodal bradyarrhythmias: sinus bradycardia, first degree block, Mobitz I, and sometimes complete block with a narrow junctional escape. Ischemia depresses the Ca2+-dependent nodal action potential, ischemic tissue releases adenosine, which acts on A1 receptors to open the same K+ channels as acetylcholine, and stimulation of vagal afferents in the inferoposterior wall triggers reflex bradycardia and hypotension (the Bezold-Jarisch reflex). Because nodal tissue usually survives and part of the cause is reflex and metabolic, the block is usually transient."},{"type":"flow","title":"From RCA occlusion to nodal block","steps":["Thrombus occludes the proximal RCA","SA and AV nodal arteries lose flow","Ischemia depresses Ca2+-dependent nodal conduction","Adenosine release and inferior wall vagal reflex","IKACh hyperpolarizes nodal cells","Bradycardia, AV block, narrow junctional escape"],"caption":"The block is in the AV node, so the escape pacemaker lies just below it and uses the normal bundle branches."},{"type":"p","text":"Anterior MI from LAD occlusion spares the node but necrotizes septal conduction tissue, giving infranodal block that is less likely to recover and signals a large infarct. In a left-dominant heart a circumflex occlusion can also cause AV nodal block, and because the circumflex supplies the SA node in about 40%, a lateral infarct can cause sinus node dysfunction."},{"type":"table","title":"Inferior versus anterior infarction","cols":["Feature","Inferior MI (RCA)","Anterior MI (LAD)"],"rows":[["Conduction tissue lost","SA node (about 60%), AV node (about 80%)","Right bundle, left anterior fascicle"],["Level of block","AV node","His bundle or bundle branches"],["Typical disturbance","Sinus bradycardia, first degree, Mobitz I","Bundle branch block, Mobitz II"],["Escape in complete block","Junctional, narrow, about 40 to 60 bpm","Ventricular, wide, about 20 to 40 bpm"],["Mechanism and course","Ischemia, adenosine, vagal reflex; usually transient","Necrosis; often persistent, worse prognosis"]],"caption":"The artery sets the level of block, and the level sets the escape rhythm and prognosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One patient with complete block has a narrow escape at 50 bpm after an inferior MI; another has a wide escape at 30 bpm after an anterior MI. Whose escape is less stable?","explain":"The anterior MI patient. Infranodal block leaves a distal Purkinje or ventricular pacemaker that is slow, conducts myocyte to myocyte, and may fail. Nodal block leaves a faster, more reliable junctional pacemaker that uses the normal bundle branches.","check":"The infarction table and the escape rates in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C04","obj":"CV-7.4","title":"Reading a normal ECG: waves, intervals, rate, and axis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ECG records the summed dipoles of moving depolarization and repolarization fronts. Depolarization toward a lead's positive electrode is upward, away is downward, and perpendicular is small and biphasic; repolarization gives the opposite sign. At 25 mm/s a small box is 0.04 s and a large box 0.20 s; 10 mm equals 1 mV."},{"type":"p","text":"Limb leads view the frontal plane: I (0 degrees), II (+60), III (+120), aVF (+90), aVL (-30), aVR (-150); II equals I plus III (Einthoven's law). V1 to V6 view the horizontal plane. Wall groups: septal V1 to V2 and anterior V3 to V4 (LAD), lateral I, aVL, V5 to V6 (circumflex or diagonals), inferior II, III, aVF (usually RCA)."},{"type":"p","text":"Atrial repolarization is hidden in the QRS, and the AV node and His bundle are too small to register, so their delay appears as the flat PR segment. The septum depolarizes left to right, giving small septal q waves in I, aVL, V5, V6 and a small r in V1; the thick left ventricle then dominates, giving tall lateral R waves and a deep S in V1 (transition V3 or V4). No gradient exists during the plateau, so ST is isoelectric. Shorter epicardial action potentials make repolarization run epicardium to endocardium, so the T wave is upright where the QRS is upright."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ecg-waveform","caption":"One normal beat with the P wave, PR interval, QRS complex, ST segment, T wave, and QT interval labeled.","img":"notes-web/fig/CV-7-6.png"},{"type":"table","title":"Normal waves and intervals","cols":["Component","Event","Normal value"],"rows":[["P wave","Atrial depolarization","Under 0.12 s; under 2.5 mm in II"],["PR interval (onset P to onset QRS)","Atrial and AV nodal plus His-Purkinje conduction","0.12 to 0.20 s"],["QRS","Ventricular depolarization","0.06 to 0.10 s; 0.12 s or more abnormal"],["ST segment","Plateau (phase 2)","Isoelectric"],["T wave","Repolarization (phase 3)","Same direction as QRS; inverted in aVR"],["QT interval","Depolarization plus repolarization","QTc under about 0.45 s (men), 0.46 s (women)"],["U wave","Late repolarization","Small; prominent in hypokalemia"]],"caption":"The QT interval approximates the ventricular action potential duration."},{"type":"p","text":"Sinus rhythm means a P wave upright in I and II before every QRS, a constant normal PR, and a rate of 60 to 100 bpm. Regular rate is 300 divided by large boxes between R waves (or 1500 divided by small boxes); for irregular rhythms, count QRS complexes in 10 s and multiply by 6. QT shortens as rate rises, so it is corrected (Bazett: QT divided by the square root of RR in seconds); at normal rates QT is under half the RR."},{"type":"p","text":"The normal mean QRS axis is about -30 to +90 degrees. If I and aVF are both positive, the axis is normal; if I is positive and aVF negative, a positive lead II means 0 to -30 (normal) and a negative II means left axis deviation; I negative with aVF positive is right axis deviation; both negative is extreme. Left axis deviation suggests left anterior fascicular block or inferior scar; right axis deviation suggests right ventricular hypertrophy, left posterior fascicular block, or a vertical heart."},{"type":"example","title":"Rate, intervals, and axis from a strip","text":"R waves are 4 large boxes apart, with an upright P in II before each QRS. PR is 4 small boxes, QRS 2, QT 9. Lead I is positive, aVF and II negative. Rate is 300 divided by 4, or 75 bpm, sinus rhythm; PR 0.16 s and QRS 0.08 s are normal. QT is 0.36 s; RR is 0.80 s, whose square root is about 0.89, so QTc is about 0.40 s. Axis: I positive and aVF negative place it between 0 and -90, and a negative II puts it beyond -30 (left axis deviation), most likely left anterior fascicular block if there is no inferior infarct."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C05","obj":"CV-7.5","title":"AV blocks and bundle branch blocks","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blocks are classified by site, and the two tissues fail differently. AV nodal cells use slow Ca2+ channels with time-dependent recovery, so the node conducts each closely spaced impulse more slowly (decremental conduction) and fails gradually. His-Purkinje cells use fast Na+ channels and conduct at nearly full speed or not at all, so infranodal block appears suddenly and leaves a slow, unreliable escape."},{"type":"p","text":"First degree block is a PR over 0.20 s with every P conducted, usually nodal (athletic vagal tone, nodal-slowing drugs, degeneration). In Mobitz I (Wenckebach) the PR lengthens until a P fails to conduct, then resets; the PR increment is largest after the pause, so RR intervals shorten before the drop and beats group. It is usually nodal, narrow, and benign. In Mobitz II the PR of conducted beats is constant and P waves drop suddenly, indicating His or bundle branch disease; the QRS is often wide and progression to complete block is a risk. In 2:1 block the type cannot be judged from the PR; a wide QRS favors an infranodal site. In third degree block P waves and QRS complexes march independently (AV dissociation), with a junctional or ventricular escape depending on the level. Causes include fibrosis, MI, Lyme carditis (Borrelia burgdorferi), sarcoidosis, and maternal anti-Ro/SSA antibodies injuring the fetal AV node."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the AV blocks","cols":["Block","PR interval","Site","QRS","Progression risk"],"rows":[["First degree","Constant, over 0.20 s","AV node","Narrow","Low"],["Mobitz I","Lengthens until a beat drops","AV node","Narrow","Low"],["Mobitz II","Constant; sudden drop","His or bundle branches","Often wide","High"],["Third degree","Varies (AV dissociation)","Node or infranodal","Narrow if junctional, wide if ventricular","Depends on escape"]],"caption":"Gradual PR change means AV node; all-or-none failure means His-Purkinje tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"When a bundle branch fails, its ventricle is activated late by myocyte-to-myocyte spread, the QRS reaches 0.12 s or more, the terminal QRS points toward the late ventricle, and ST-T changes point opposite to it. In RBBB, septal activation from the left bundle is preserved; causes include right ventricular strain (pulmonary embolism, atrial septal defect) and septal infarction, and it can be normal. LBBB reverses septal activation and almost always means structural heart disease (ischemia, hypertension, aortic stenosis, cardiomyopathy); it can mask a new infarct."},{"type":"table","title":"Right versus left bundle branch block","cols":["Feature","RBBB","LBBB"],"rows":[["Septal activation","Normal, left to right","Reversed, right to left"],["V1","rSR prime","Deep, wide QS or rS"],["I and V6","Wide slurred S; septal q kept","Broad notched R; no septal q"],["S2","Wide splitting","Paradoxical splitting"]],"caption":"Both have a QRS of 0.12 s or more; incomplete block shows the same pattern at about 0.10 to 0.12 s."},{"type":"p","text":"Left anterior fascicular block shifts the axis to about -45 degrees or beyond with little QRS widening; left posterior fascicular block gives right axis deviation. With bifascicular block (right bundle plus one fascicle), loss of the last fascicle produces complete infranodal block with a wide escape at about 20 to 40 bpm, enough to cause syncope."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C06","obj":"CV-7.6","title":"Premature beats and tachyarrhythmias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tachyarrhythmias arise from three mechanisms. Abnormal automaticity is a steep phase 4 outside the SA node (catecholamines, stretch, ischemia, electrolytes). Triggered activity is early afterdepolarizations (EADs) during a prolonged plateau or delayed afterdepolarizations in Ca2+-overloaded cells. Reentry, the commonest cause of sustained tachycardia, needs the conditions in the cycle below. It is sustained only when the circuit exceeds the wavelength (conduction velocity times refractory period); slow conduction (ischemia, scar) and short refractoriness (ischemia, atrial remodeling) shorten the wavelength."},{"type":"cycle","title":"A reentrant circuit","steps":["Premature impulse reaches a branch point","One limb is refractory (unidirectional block)","Impulse travels slowly down the other limb","Blocked limb recovers meanwhile","Impulse returns up the recovered limb","It re-enters the first limb and the loop repeats"],"caption":"Blocking a limb or lengthening refractoriness stops it."},{"type":"p","text":"A premature atrial contraction (PAC) is an early, abnormal P with a narrow QRS; it resets the SA node, so the pause is less than compensatory. A premature ventricular contraction (PVC) is an early wide QRS with no P and a discordant T; retrograde AV nodal block spares the sinus rhythm, so the pause is fully compensatory. A PVC on the vulnerable peak of the T wave (R on T) can start VF."},{"type":"p","text":"In atrial fibrillation, pulmonary vein triggers and a dilated, fibrotic atrium sustain wavelets at about 350 to 600 per minute; the node conducts only impulses that find it recovered, so the rhythm is irregularly irregular. Atrial kick is lost, and left atrial appendage stasis promotes embolic stroke. Atrial flutter is macroreentry around the tricuspid annulus through the cavotricuspid isthmus; with 2:1 conduction it gives a regular rate near 150 bpm. Multifocal atrial tachycardia is typical of COPD and low K+ or Mg2+. Paroxysmal SVT starts and stops abruptly and is usually AV nodal reentry, which vagal slowing of the node can terminate."},{"type":"example","title":"How a premature atrial beat starts AV nodal reentry","text":"The AV node has a fast pathway with a long refractory period and a slow pathway with a short one. A PAC finds the fast pathway refractory and conducts down the slow pathway, so its PR is long. By the time it reaches the lower junction, the fast pathway has recovered, so the impulse travels up it to the atria while also descending the His bundle. Atria and ventricles activate almost together, so the retrograde P is buried in or just after the narrow QRS, sometimes as a pseudo R prime in V1. The impulse then re-enters the slow pathway, and the circuit runs at 150 to 250 bpm."},{"type":"p","text":"Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is three or more consecutive ventricular beats over 100 bpm, usually scar reentry; AV dissociation, capture beats, and fusion beats confirm it. Torsades de pointes occurs with a long QT: inherited loss of IKs (KCNQ1) or IKr (KCNH2, hERG) or gain of SCN5A Na+ current; low K+ (which reduces IKr), Mg2+, or Ca2+; or IKr-blocking drugs such as some macrolides and antipsychotics. Ventricular fibrillation (VF), with no output, is the commonest initial rhythm of sudden cardiac death, usually triggered by ischemia."},{"type":"flow","title":"From long QT to torsades de pointes","steps":["Less repolarizing K+ current or persistent Na+ current","Action potential and QT lengthen","L-type Ca2+ channels recover and reopen","Early afterdepolarizations fire premature beats","Uneven repolarization supports reentry","Torsades, which may degenerate to VF"],"caption":"A long QTc carries risk even in an asymptomatic patient."},{"type":"table","title":"Tachyarrhythmias at a glance","cols":["Rhythm","Key ECG features","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Atrial fibrillation","No P, irregularly irregular narrow QRS","Multiple wavelets"],["Atrial flutter","Sawtooth waves about 300/min, often 2:1","Macroreentry"],["MAT","3 or more P shapes, irregular, over 100","Multiple atrial foci"],["AVNRT","Regular narrow 150 to 250, hidden P","Dual nodal pathways"],["VT","Regular wide, AV dissociation","Scar reentry"],["Torsades","Twisting polymorphic QRS, long QT","EADs plus reentry"],["VF","Chaotic, no QRS","Ventricular wavelets"]],"caption":"Narrow QRS means His-Purkinje activation; wide means ventricular origin or aberrant conduction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-07-01-C07","obj":"CV-7.7","title":"Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome and sick sinus syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Incomplete separation of atrial and ventricular muscle by the fibrous AV rings leaves an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) of fast Na+-dependent muscle that usually lacks decremental conduction. Preexcitation on the ECG is the Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pattern; with tachycardia episodes it is WPW syndrome. Ebstein anomaly is an association. Vagal slowing of the node enlarges the delta wave, and sympathetic drive can hide it; concealed pathways conduct only retrogradely and show no delta wave but still support reentry."},{"type":"flow","title":"How an accessory pathway produces the WPW pattern","steps":["Atria depolarize normally","Impulse crosses the pathway without nodal delay","Part of the ventricle activates early: PR under 0.12 s","Slow cell-to-cell spread forms the delta wave","Impulse via the AV node activates the rest rapidly","Wide fusion QRS with secondary ST-T changes"],"caption":"The pathway bypasses the AV node but enters outside the Purkinje network."},{"type":"p","text":"In orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia the impulse descends the node and returns up the pathway, so the QRS is narrow and the delta wave disappears; antidromic tachycardia descends the pathway and gives a wide, fully preexcited QRS. Atrial fibrillation is the danger. The pathway lacks the node's long, time-dependent refractoriness, so with a refractory period near 0.25 s it can pass about 240 impulses per minute or more, giving an irregular, very fast, wide-complex rhythm whose QRS shape varies with fusion. Short diastole and impulses landing in the vulnerable period allow degeneration to VF. Slowing the AV node does not slow this response and can shift more conduction onto the pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"Sick sinus syndrome is intrinsic failure of the SA node, usually from degenerative fibrosis in older adults, and less often from SA nodal artery ischemia, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, or inherited channel defects. It often coexists with AV conduction disease and atrial fibrillation. Fewer pacemaker cells give inappropriate sinus bradycardia and chronotropic incompetence (poor rate rise with exercise). In sinus arrest the node fails to fire, so the pause is not a multiple of the P to P interval; in SA exit block the impulse forms but cannot leave the node, so the pause is an exact multiple. Physiologic bradycardia from vagal tone and extrinsic causes such as hypothyroidism, hyperkalemia, or nodal-slowing drugs mimic it but resolve when the cause is removed."},{"type":"p","text":"In the tachy-brady form, the fibrotic atria produce paroxysms of fibrillation or flutter that overdrive suppress the diseased node. When the tachycardia stops, the node recovers slowly, and the pause of several seconds causes dizziness or syncope; fatigue reflects poor rate response."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 78-year-old has paroxysms of atrial fibrillation, each followed by a 5-second pause before a sinus P wave. Why does the pause follow the tachycardia?","explain":"The rapid atrial rhythm overdrive suppresses the SA node. A healthy node recovers within about 1.5 s, but a fibrotic node with few pacemaker cells recovers slowly, so nothing fires until it or an escape pacemaker reaches threshold.","check":"The tachy-brady paragraph and overdrive suppression in C02."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology explain the wiring. The fibrous skeleton that anchors the valves insulates the atria from the ventricles, and incomplete formation of the AV rings leaves accessory pathways. The conduction system is modified cardiac muscle, so it fails through the processes that damage muscle: ischemia, fibrosis, infiltration (sarcoidosis, amyloid), and inflammation, including Lyme carditis and maternal anti-Ro/SSA injury to the fetal AV node. Coronary anatomy decides which part is lost, with the RCA usually feeding the nodes and the LAD the bundle branches."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology explain the behavior. Each ECG finding traces to a current: If and Ca2+ currents set rate, Ca2+-dependent nodal conduction sets the PR, fast Na+ conduction keeps the QRS narrow, and K+ currents set the QT, so electrolyte disorders from the kidney or gut appear on the ECG and single-gene channelopathies cause sudden death in structurally normal hearts. The ECG shows electrical activity, not contraction, the nodes and His bundle are inferred from timing rather than seen, and reference values vary slightly with source, age, and sex, so borderline values are read with the whole tracing."}],"summary":["The SA node paces the heart because its phase 4 slope is steepest; overdrive suppression holds latent pacemakers in check.","The AV node and His bundle are the only normal link through the insulating fibrous skeleton; AV delay of about 0.13 s lets atrial systole finish filling and the node filters rapid atrial impulses.","Conduction is fastest in Purkinje fibers and slowest in the AV node, reflecting fiber size, gap junctions, and Na+ versus Ca2+ upstrokes.","Nodal cells lack IK1, so If and T-type Ca2+ current depolarize phase 4; sympathetic cAMP steepens it, and vagal IKACh and low cAMP flatten it.","Inferior MI (RCA) causes transient nodal block with a narrow escape; anterior MI (LAD) causes infranodal block with a wide, slow escape and worse prognosis.","Depolarization toward a positive electrode is positive; rate is 300 divided by large boxes, and leads I, aVF, and II place the axis.","Progressive PR lengthening (Mobitz I) means nodal block; constant PR with sudden drops (Mobitz II) means infranodal block with high risk.","RBBB gives rSR prime in V1 with septal q kept; LBBB gives broad notched lateral R waves with septal q lost.","Reentry needs two pathways, unidirectional block, and slow conduction; long QT causes EAD-triggered torsades.","WPW is short PR, delta wave, and wide QRS from an accessory pathway, dangerous in AF; sick sinus syndrome is fibrotic SA node failure with pauses and tachy-brady episodes."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Rhythmical Excitation of the Heart"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Fundamentals of Electrocardiography; Cardiac Arrhythmias and Their Electrocardiographic Interpretation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac electrophysiology and the electrocardiogram"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, conducting system of the heart and coronary arteries"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on arrhythmias"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-8":{"unit":"CV-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01","title":"Coronary Circulation","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers how the heart supplies its own muscle: the origins, courses, and territories of the coronary arteries, coronary dominance and the papillary muscles, venous drainage through the coronary sinus, and the physiology that sets the coronary bed apart. The left ventricle is perfused mainly in diastole, the myocardium extracts about 70% of delivered oxygen at rest, and a rise in demand can be met only by local metabolic vasodilation. The chapter closes with ALCAPA, a congenital lesion that shows these principles failing after birth. Board questions link structure to consequence: they ask which artery was occluded from the infarct leads or complication, or which layer of the wall becomes ischemic first.","prerequisites":["Surfaces, borders, grooves, and chambers of the heart, and the aortic sinuses (CV-3)","Phases of the cardiac cycle and the pressures in the aorta and left ventricle during each phase (CV-4, CV-6)","Oxygen content of blood, hemoglobin saturation, and the Fick principle","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships and the law of Laplace (CV-11)","Fetal circulation and the fall in pulmonary vascular resistance at birth (CV-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"The arteries that feed the SA node, AV node, and bundle branches predict which conduction problems follow an inferior or an anterior infarct."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Coronary autoregulation and metabolic vasodilation are the clearest example of local control overriding neural control of a vascular bed."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease (CV-18-C)","how":"Infarct location, ECG lead groups, and complications such as papillary muscle rupture all follow from the territories described here."},{"to":"Hypertensive and valvular heart disease (CV-18-A, CV-18-D)","how":"Ventricular hypertrophy, aortic stenosis, and aortic regurgitation cause subendocardial ischemia by raising wall stress or lowering diastolic perfusion pressure."},{"to":"Congenital heart conditions (CV-18-G)","how":"Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery depends on the same postnatal fall in pulmonary pressure that shapes shunt lesions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Crux of the heart","def":"The point on the posterior surface where the coronary sulcus meets the posterior interventricular groove."},{"term":"Coronary dominance","def":"A description of which artery gives rise to the posterior descending artery: the RCA in right dominance, the circumflex in left dominance, or both in codominance."},{"term":"Posterior descending artery (PDA)","def":"The artery in the posterior interventricular groove that supplies the inferior wall of the left ventricle and the posterior one-third of the septum."},{"term":"Coronary sinus","def":"The large venous channel in the posterior coronary sulcus that collects most of the venous blood of the left ventricle and empties into the right atrium."},{"term":"Smallest cardiac veins","def":"Tiny veins (venae cordis minimae) that drain myocardial capillary beds directly into the heart chambers."},{"term":"Coronary perfusion pressure","def":"The pressure difference driving left ventricular flow in diastole, about aortic diastolic pressure minus left ventricular end-diastolic pressure."},{"term":"Subendocardium","def":"The inner layer of the ventricular wall, which bears the highest compressive pressure in systole and is the first layer to become ischemic."},{"term":"Oxygen extraction","def":"The fraction of arterial oxygen content removed by a tissue, equal to the arteriovenous oxygen content difference divided by arterial oxygen content."},{"term":"Wall stress","def":"The force per unit area within the ventricular wall, proportional to cavity pressure times radius divided by wall thickness (law of Laplace)."},{"term":"Metabolic vasodilation","def":"Relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle caused by substances released from active or underperfused tissue, matching flow to metabolic demand."},{"term":"Coronary flow reserve","def":"The ratio of maximal coronary flow during full vasodilation to resting flow, normally about 3 to 5."},{"term":"Coronary steal","def":"Diversion of blood away from an ischemic bed toward a bed of lower resistance, either by vasodilation of normal vessels or by retrograde flow into a low-pressure vessel."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C01","obj":"CV-8.1","title":"Origins, courses, and territories of the coronary arteries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Apart from a layer about 0.1 mm thick next to the endocardium, the myocardium depends on two coronary arteries, the first branches of the aorta. They arise from the aortic sinuses, run in surface grooves, and send penetrating branches from epicardium toward endocardium. Each artery supplies the muscle on either side of the groove it occupies, so the grooves predict the territories."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterior surface is mostly right ventricle, the inferior (diaphragmatic) surface is mostly left ventricle, and the left surface is the lateral wall. The coronary sulcus (atrioventricular groove) circles the heart between atria and ventricles, the anterior and posterior interventricular grooves mark the septum, and the crux is where the coronary sulcus meets the posterior interventricular groove."},{"type":"h","text":"Right coronary artery"},{"type":"p","text":"The right coronary artery (RCA) arises from the right aortic sinus and runs in the right coronary sulcus. It gives a conus branch, the sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery in about 60% of hearts, and the right marginal artery at the acute margin, then reaches the crux, where in most hearts it gives the atrioventricular (AV) nodal artery (about 80%) and turns down the posterior interventricular groove as the posterior descending artery (PDA), which supplies the posterior one-third of the septum and the adjacent inferior wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Left main, LAD, and circumflex"},{"type":"p","text":"The left main coronary artery arises from the left aortic sinus and divides into the left anterior descending (LAD) and circumflex arteries, so its occlusion threatens most of the left ventricle."},{"type":"p","text":"The LAD descends in the anterior interventricular groove to the apex. Its septal perforators supply the anterior two-thirds of the septum, which carries the His bundle and both bundle branches, and its diagonal branches supply the anterolateral wall. Because it feeds the largest share of left ventricular muscle, it is the artery most often occluded in infarction, followed by the RCA and then the circumflex. The circumflex follows the left coronary sulcus, supplying the lateral wall through obtuse marginal branches and the left atrium. It usually ends before the crux; when it gives the PDA, the heart is left dominant (section C02)."},{"type":"table","title":"Coronary arteries, courses, and territories","cols":["Artery","Origin and course","Main branches","Territory","ECG leads"],"rows":[["Right coronary artery","Right aortic sinus; right coronary sulcus to the crux","Conus, SA nodal (about 60%), right marginal, AV nodal (about 80%), PDA if right dominant","Right atrium, most of the right ventricle, SA and AV nodes in most hearts","II, III, aVF if the PDA territory is involved; right-sided leads for the right ventricle"],["Left main","Left aortic sinus; between left auricle and pulmonary trunk","LAD, circumflex, sometimes ramus intermedius","Most of the left ventricle","Widespread anterior and lateral"],["LAD","Anterior interventricular groove to the apex","Septal perforators, diagonals","Anterior left ventricle, apex, anterior two-thirds of septum with the bundle branches","V1 to V4"],["Circumflex","Left coronary sulcus around the left border","Obtuse marginals, SA nodal (about 40%), PDA if left dominant","Left atrium, lateral and posterolateral left ventricle","I, aVL, V5, V6"],["PDA","Posterior interventricular groove from the crux","Septal branches","Inferior wall, posterior one-third of septum, posteromedial papillary muscle","II, III, aVF"]],"caption":"Nodal artery percentages are approximate. Lead groups are taught in CV-18-C."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the anatomy matters"},{"type":"p","text":"Because penetrating branches enter from the epicardium, the subendocardium lies at the far end of every branch (section C04). Territories also predict conduction problems. LAD occlusion can destroy the bundle branches and cause infranodal block, while RCA occlusion causes an inferior infarct that is often accompanied by sinus bradycardia or AV nodal block (CV-7)."},{"type":"p","text":"Coronary arteries are functional rather than absolute end arteries. Small anastomoses, especially near the apex and in the septum, can enlarge into useful collaterals when a stenosis develops over months, but they cannot protect against a sudden occlusion, which is why an acute thrombus infarcts the territory of the blocked artery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C02","obj":"CV-8.2","title":"Coronary dominance and the vulnerable posteromedial papillary muscle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Coronary dominance describes which artery gives rise to the PDA, not which artery is larger. The left coronary artery supplies most of the left ventricle in nearly every heart, yet most hearts are right dominant because the RCA gives the PDA. Dominance matters because the PDA supplies the inferior wall, the posterior one-third of the septum, and the posteromedial papillary muscle, and the artery that gives the PDA usually also gives the AV nodal artery."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of coronary dominance","cols":["Pattern","Source of the PDA","Approximate frequency","Consequence for occlusion"],"rows":[["Right dominant","RCA","Most hearts (about 70% to 85%)","RCA occlusion causes an inferior infarct, often with AV nodal block; circumflex occlusion stays lateral"],["Left dominant","Circumflex","About 5% to 15%","Circumflex occlusion causes an inferolateral infarct and can involve the AV node; left main occlusion is even more extensive"],["Codominant","Both the RCA and the circumflex reach the posterior interventricular groove","About 5% to 20%","Inferior wall split between two arteries, so either occlusion gives a smaller inferior infarct"]],"caption":"Frequencies vary between anatomical and angiographic series; the definition does not."},{"type":"h","text":"Blood supply of the papillary muscles"},{"type":"p","text":"The papillary muscles pull on the chordae tendineae in systole and keep the mitral leaflets from prolapsing into the left atrium. Their tips lie deep in the cavity, at the end of the coronary branches and within the subendocardium, which makes them highly sensitive to ischemia."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterolateral papillary muscle sits where the LAD diagonal and circumflex obtuse marginal territories overlap, so it has a dual supply and rarely ruptures. The posteromedial muscle on the inferior wall usually depends on the PDA alone, so it shares the fate of the PDA whatever its source: the RCA in right-dominant hearts and the circumflex in left-dominant hearts. In commonly quoted series it ruptures about 6 to 12 times as often as the anterolateral muscle, after an inferior infarct."},{"type":"p","text":"Rupture occurs most often about 2 to 7 days after infarction. The dead muscle keeps its tensile strength for the first day, but neutrophils then infiltrate and, from about day 3, macrophages remove necrotic myocytes before granulation tissue and collagen can replace them, so systolic traction tears softened tissue (CV-18-C)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From inferior infarct to papillary muscle rupture","steps":["Thrombus occludes the artery giving the PDA","Posteromedial papillary muscle loses its only supply","Coagulative necrosis of the papillary muscle","Neutrophils, then macrophages, soften it over days 2 to 7","Systolic traction tears the muscle or one head","Chordae lose their anchor; mitral leaflets flail","Acute severe mitral regurgitation"],"caption":"The occluded artery depends on dominance, but the chain is the same: single supply, necrosis, enzymatic softening, rupture."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Sudden mitral incompetence ejects much of each stroke volume into a normal-sized, relatively stiff left atrium. Left atrial pressure rises steeply and causes abrupt pulmonary edema, while forward stroke volume falls, causing hypotension and possibly cardiogenic shock. The murmur may be short and soft because the gradient across the valve collapses before systole ends. Post-infarct septal rupture also causes sudden decompensation with a new systolic murmur, but it produces an oxygen saturation step-up from right atrium to right ventricle. Ischemic papillary muscle dysfunction without rupture is more common and causes milder regurgitation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does an occluded LAD, which causes the largest infarcts, rarely cause papillary muscle rupture?","explain":"The anterolateral papillary muscle in the LAD region also receives circumflex branches, so losing the LAD alone leaves it perfused. The posteromedial muscle lies outside the LAD territory in most hearts. Rupture requires a muscle that has lost its only supply.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on dual and single supply under Blood supply of the papillary muscles."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C03","obj":"CV-8.3","title":"Cardiac venous drainage and the coronary sinus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Coronary veins follow the arteries back to the coronary sulcus, and most converge on the coronary sinus. About 75% of total coronary flow returns through the sinus, mainly the venous blood of the left ventricle, while most right ventricular venous blood returns through the anterior cardiac veins."},{"type":"h","text":"The coronary sinus"},{"type":"p","text":"The coronary sinus is a wide, thin-walled channel in the posterior coronary sulcus between the left atrium and left ventricle. It opens into the right atrium between the opening of the inferior vena cava and the tricuspid orifice, partly guarded by the valve of the coronary sinus, and its ostium forms one border of the triangle that locates the AV node (CV-7)."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinus begins where the great cardiac vein meets the oblique vein of the left atrium, a remnant of the embryonic left superior vena cava, and it develops from the left horn of the sinus venosus. A persistent left superior vena cava therefore drains into a dilated coronary sinus, usually harmlessly, because the blood still reaches the right atrium."},{"type":"h","text":"Tributaries and veins that bypass the sinus"},{"type":"p","text":"Each named vein shadows an artery: the great cardiac vein runs with the LAD and then the circumflex, the middle cardiac vein with the PDA, and the small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery. Two groups bypass the sinus. The anterior cardiac veins open directly into the right atrium, and the smallest cardiac veins (venae cordis minimae) drain capillary beds straight into the chambers, mostly the atria, although some open into the left ventricle."},{"type":"table","title":"Cardiac veins and their drainage","cols":["Vein","Course and companion artery","Region drained","Termination"],"rows":[["Great cardiac vein","Anterior interventricular groove with the LAD, then left coronary sulcus with the circumflex","Anterior and lateral left ventricle, anterior septum, left atrium","Becomes the coronary sinus"],["Middle cardiac vein","Posterior interventricular groove with the PDA","Inferior wall, posterior septum","Right end of the coronary sinus"],["Small cardiac vein","Inferior margin with the right marginal artery, then right coronary sulcus","Inferior right ventricle and right atrium","Right end of the coronary sinus"],["Anterior cardiac veins","Cross the right coronary sulcus from the anterior right ventricle","Anterior right ventricle","Directly into the right atrium"],["Smallest cardiac veins","Within the myocardium","Inner myocardium near each chamber","Directly into the chambers, mostly the atria"]],"caption":"The first three veins empty into the coronary sinus; the last two bypass it."},{"type":"h","text":"Physiological consequences"},{"type":"p","text":"Because the myocardium extracts about 70% of delivered oxygen (section C04), coronary sinus blood is only about 25% to 30% saturated, among the most deoxygenated blood in the body at rest, compared with about 75% in mixed venous blood. Coronary sinus blood therefore reflects left ventricular oxygen extraction and lactate release."},{"type":"p","text":"Smallest cardiac veins that open into the left heart, together with bronchial venous blood draining into the pulmonary veins, form the normal physiological shunt of about 2% of cardiac output. This is one reason arterial PO2 is slightly below alveolar PO2 and the normal alveolar to arterial PO2 difference is not zero."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C04","obj":"CV-8.4","title":"Diastolic perfusion, high oxygen extraction, and subendocardial vulnerability","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Coronary flow is about 225 mL/min at rest, about 4% to 5% of cardiac output, for an organ about 0.5% of body weight."},{"type":"h","text":"Diastolic perfusion of the left ventricle"},{"type":"p","text":"In systole, intramyocardial pressure in the inner wall rises to about cavity pressure and squeezes the vessels shut, so flow falls sharply (and can briefly reverse) as systole begins and surges in early diastole when compression is released. About 70% to 80% of left ventricular flow occurs in diastole. The right ventricle, at about 25 mmHg systolic, is perfused throughout the cycle unless pulmonary hypertension raises its pressure toward systemic levels."},{"type":"p","text":"Left ventricular coronary perfusion pressure is about aortic diastolic pressure minus left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP), because diastolic cavity pressure acts as the back pressure on the inner vessels. Hypotension and aortic regurgitation lower the first term, and heart failure and a stiff hypertrophied ventricle raise the second."},{"type":"h","text":"High oxygen extraction"},{"type":"p","text":"Arterial blood carries about 20 mL O2/dL and coronary sinus blood about 5 to 6 mL/dL, so the myocardium extracts about 70% to 75% at rest (about 25% for the whole body). Myocytes contract continuously and make almost all their ATP by oxidative phosphorylation, with mitochondria filling about one-third of cell volume, small creatine phosphate stores, and little anaerobic capacity. About one capillary per myocyte and cytoplasmic myoglobin ease diffusion, so the heart extracts most of the oxygen at rest and has little left to recruit (section C05)."},{"type":"h","text":"Subendocardial vulnerability"},{"type":"p","text":"The subendocardium lies at the end of the penetrating branches, receives almost no systolic flow, faces LVEDP in diastole, and has the highest wall stress and oxygen demand. Lower resting arteriolar tone normally gives it slightly more flow per gram than the subepicardium, but this spends part of its vasodilator reserve at rest, so it runs out first when perfusion pressure falls, diastole shortens, or demand rises."},{"type":"p","text":"By the law of Laplace, wall stress is proportional to cavity pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so high afterload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) and dilation (heart failure) raise oxygen demand. Hypertrophy lowers stress, but capillaries lag behind muscle growth and the stiff wall raises LVEDP."},{"type":"flow","title":"How tachycardia produces subendocardial ischemia","steps":["Heart rate rises (exercise, fever, anemia, arrhythmia)","Diastole shortens far more than systole","Diastolic perfusion time per minute falls","Each extra beat raises oxygen demand","Subendocardium exhausts its vasodilator reserve","Inner wall ischemia: angina and ST depression"],"caption":"Tachycardia lowers supply and raises demand at the same time."},{"type":"example","title":"Diastolic time per minute at two heart rates","text":"At 70 bpm a cycle lasts about 0.86 s. With systole about 0.30 s, diastole is about 0.56 s, or about 39 s per minute. At 150 bpm a cycle lasts 0.40 s, and systole shortens only to about 0.22 s, leaving about 0.18 s, or about 27 s of diastole per minute. Demand has risen, yet left ventricular perfusion time has fallen by about 30%. If a stenosis already limits arteriolar dilation, flow cannot compensate and the subendocardium becomes ischemic first, which is why exercise in coronary stenosis produces ST depression."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemia can therefore occur with normal coronary arteries. Aortic stenosis raises systolic pressure, muscle mass, and LVEDP, and aortic regurgitation lowers aortic diastolic pressure and dilates the ventricle. In severe anemia or hypoxemia, extraction is already near its limit, so lower arterial oxygen content cuts delivery directly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with severe aortic regurgitation has a blood pressure of 150/40 mmHg and an LVEDP of 25 mmHg. Estimate the coronary perfusion pressure and predict which layer of the left ventricle is most at risk.","explain":"About 40 minus 25, or 15 mmHg, far below the normal of about 70 mmHg (80 minus 8). Low diastolic perfusion pressure plus the high wall stress of a dilated ventricle threaten the subendocardium first.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on perfusion pressure and the subendocardium."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C05","obj":"CV-8.5","title":"Local metabolic control of coronary flow","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because extraction is nearly maximal at rest, the heart meets higher demand by raising flow in proportion to oxygen consumption. Myocyte metabolites dilate the arterioles that feed them, overriding most neural influences."},{"type":"p","text":"By the Fick principle, myocardial oxygen consumption equals coronary flow times the arteriovenous oxygen content difference. Skeletal muscle can widen that difference in exercise, but the myocardium already extracts about 70% to 75% at rest and can reach only about 80% to 90%, so flow is the only term left to change."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the Fick principle to show why the heart must raise flow","text":"Take resting flow as 2.25 dL/min, arterial O2 content 20 mL/dL, and coronary venous content 6 mL/dL. The arteriovenous difference is 14 mL/dL, so consumption is about 32 mL O2/min and extraction is 70%. Now double demand to about 63 mL/min. Raising extraction to its maximum of about 85% (17 mL/dL) at fixed flow gives only about 38 mL/min. Keeping extraction near 70% requires a flow of 63 divided by 14, about 4.5 dL/min, double the resting value. Only higher flow meets the demand, so any lesion that limits the rise in flow causes ischemia under stress."},{"type":"h","text":"Adenosine and other metabolic vasodilators"},{"type":"p","text":"When supply falls short, ATP breakdown outpaces regeneration and AMP accumulates. The enzyme 5 prime nucleotidase converts AMP to adenosine, which diffuses to arteriolar smooth muscle and binds A2A receptors. Gs raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A (PKA), which opens K+ channels, including ATP-sensitive (KATP) channels. Hyperpolarization closes L-type Ca2+ channels, cytosolic Ca2+ falls, myosin light chain kinase activity drops, and the arteriole relaxes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Adenosine-mediated coronary vasodilation","steps":["Myocardial oxygen demand exceeds supply","ATP breakdown outpaces regeneration; AMP accumulates","5 prime nucleotidase converts AMP to adenosine","Adenosine binds arteriolar A2A receptors (Gs, cAMP, PKA)","K+ channels open; hyperpolarization reduces L-type Ca2+ entry","Arteriolar smooth muscle relaxes; resistance falls","Flow rises until supply matches demand"],"caption":"A negative feedback loop: adenosine rises with the oxygen deficit and is cleared within seconds by cellular uptake and adenosine deaminase."},{"type":"p","text":"Blocking adenosine receptors blunts but does not abolish the rise in flow, so other mediators act in parallel: low PO2 and ATP opening KATP channels directly, CO2, H+, and lactate, K+ released by repeated depolarization, and endothelial nitric oxide released by shear stress. Sympathetic alpha-1 constriction is mild, while beta-1 stimulation raises cardiac work and metabolite release, so sympathetic activation raises coronary flow overall."},{"type":"h","text":"Autoregulation, flow reserve, and steal"},{"type":"p","text":"The same local mechanisms autoregulate flow, holding it nearly constant over a perfusion pressure range of about 60 to 140 mmHg, so at rest the arterioles keep spare dilating capacity. This coronary flow reserve, maximal flow divided by resting flow, is normally about 3 to 5. A fixed epicardial stenosis consumes the reserve as downstream arterioles dilate to offset it. Maximal flow starts to fall at about 50% diameter narrowing, while resting flow is preserved until about 80% or more. This is the mechanism of stable angina: flow is adequate at rest, but with exertion the arterioles are already dilated and the subendocardium becomes ischemic (CV-18-C)."},{"type":"p","text":"Coronary steal follows from the same physiology. A vasodilator such as adenosine, or dipyridamole, which blocks cellular adenosine uptake, dilates normal beds while the bed beyond a stenosis is already maximally dilated. Flow shifts toward normal myocardium and can fall in the ischemic bed, the basis of pharmacologic stress perfusion imaging."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a 75% diameter stenosis of the LAD. Predict coronary flow in that territory at rest and during exercise, and explain why the patient feels well while sitting.","explain":"At rest, downstream arterioles dilate enough to offset the stenosis, so flow is normal. Because part of the reserve is already spent at rest, exercise exhausts the rest, maximal flow falls short of demand, and the subendocardium becomes ischemic first.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on flow reserve and stenosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-08-01-C06","obj":"CV-8.6","title":"Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA), the left main coronary artery arises from the pulmonary trunk while the RCA arises normally from the aorta. It is rare but an important cause of myocardial infarction in infancy."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the lesion is tolerated before birth and fails after"},{"type":"p","text":"Before birth, high pulmonary vascular resistance and the ductus arteriosus keep pulmonary artery pressure about equal to aortic pressure, so the anomalous artery fills antegrade at systemic pressure. Fetal pulmonary artery blood is only modestly less saturated than ascending aortic blood, and the fetal myocardium is adapted to low oxygen tension, so the heart develops normally."},{"type":"p","text":"After birth, pulmonary vascular resistance falls, the ductus closes, and pulmonary artery pressure falls over days to weeks toward about 25/10 mmHg. The left coronary artery now carries low-pressure blood that is only about 70% to 75% saturated, which cannot supply a left ventricle that is perfused mainly in diastole against LVEDP and extracts about 70% of its oxygen. Collaterals from the RCA, still at aortic pressure, fill the left coronary branches, but because the pulmonary trunk is the lowest-pressure outlet, blood flows backward up the left coronary artery into it. The left coronary artery becomes a drain, a form of coronary steal, and the left ventricle stays ischemic despite growing collateral flow."},{"type":"flow","title":"From normal birth transition to infant myocardial ischemia","steps":["Before birth, pulmonary artery pressure equals aortic pressure; antegrade filling","After birth, pulmonary resistance falls and the ductus closes","Left coronary artery receives low-pressure, desaturated blood","RCA collaterals fill the left coronary branches","Flow reverses into the low-pressure pulmonary trunk (steal)","Anterolateral ischemia and infarction, often at 2 to 3 months"],"caption":"The lesion becomes symptomatic as the normal postnatal fall in pulmonary pressure removes the only pressure source the left coronary artery had."},{"type":"h","text":"Manifestations explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms usually begin around 2 to 3 months, once pulmonary pressure has reached its low postnatal level, and not in the first days, when it is still high. Feeding is an infant's main exertion, so ischemia appears with feeds or crying as pallor, sweating, irritability, and breathlessness, described as infant angina. The dilating, poorly contracting ventricle then causes heart failure with tachypnea, poor weight gain, and cardiomegaly. The ECG shows an anterolateral infarct pattern, with abnormal Q waves and ST and T changes in I, aVL, V5, and V6, which is almost unheard of in a normal infant. Anterolateral papillary muscle ischemia and annular dilation cause mitral regurgitation."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Self-reinforcing ischemia in ALCAPA","steps":["Low-pressure flow and steal leave the left ventricle ischemic","Ischemic ventricle contracts poorly and dilates","Larger radius raises wall stress and oxygen demand","Papillary ischemia and annular dilation cause mitral regurgitation","LVEDP rises and perfusion pressure falls further","Ischemia deepens and drives further dilation"],"caption":"Each consequence of ischemia either raises demand or lowers supply, so the infant left ventricle can deteriorate quickly."},{"type":"h","text":"Variability and distinctions"},{"type":"p","text":"Most infants have limited collaterals and develop severe ischemia and heart failure in the first year, with high mortality if the lesion is not recognized. A minority develop extensive RCA collaterals and survive into childhood or adulthood with a dilated RCA, a continuous murmur, mitral regurgitation, exercise-induced ischemia, or sudden arrhythmic death. In these patients pulmonary artery saturation may be slightly higher than right ventricular saturation because of the small left-to-right shunt from the coronary system."},{"type":"p","text":"A regional infarct in the left coronary territory in an infant whose symptoms began after the first weeks distinguishes ALCAPA from dilated cardiomyopathy and myocarditis. A coexisting lesion that keeps pulmonary pressure high, such as a large ventricular septal defect, can delay or mask the ischemia, because the critical variable is pulmonary artery pressure relative to the pressure needed to perfuse the left ventricle, not the anomalous origin itself."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy sets the territories: each coronary artery supplies the muscle beside its groove, the veins return along the same grooves, and the artery that reaches the crux decides which occlusion threatens the inferior wall, the AV node, and the posteromedial papillary muscle. Embryology explains the variants, from a persistent left superior vena cava draining into the coronary sinus to ALCAPA. Physiology and biochemistry explain the behavior of the bed: continuous oxidative metabolism forces near-maximal extraction at rest, adenosine and other metabolites match flow to demand, and systolic compression confines left ventricular perfusion to diastole, so heart rate, aortic diastolic pressure, LVEDP, and wall stress decide whether supply meets demand."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from these principles. A stenosis consumes flow reserve and causes exertional angina, an acute thrombus infarcts a territory and produces complications predicted by the structures in it, and aortic valve disease, hypertrophy, anemia, and sustained tachycardia can cause subendocardial ischemia without coronary disease. Territories are predictions to be confirmed by ECG or imaging, since dominance, nodal artery origin, and collateral development vary between individuals."}],"summary":["The RCA runs in the right coronary sulcus and supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, the SA node in about 60% and the AV node in about 80% of hearts.","The left main divides into the LAD (anterior wall, apex, anterior two-thirds of the septum with the bundle branches) and the circumflex (lateral wall and left atrium).","Dominance is defined only by which artery gives the PDA; most hearts are right dominant, so an inferior infarct usually implicates the RCA.","The posteromedial papillary muscle depends on the PDA alone and ruptures about 2 to 7 days after an inferior infarct, causing acute severe mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema.","The great, middle, and small cardiac veins follow the LAD and circumflex, the PDA, and the right marginal artery into the coronary sinus, which opens into the right atrium; anterior and smallest cardiac veins bypass it.","Systolic compression confines most left ventricular flow to diastole, and coronary perfusion pressure is about aortic diastolic pressure minus LVEDP.","The myocardium extracts about 70% to 75% of arterial oxygen at rest, so by the Fick principle a rise in demand must be met by a rise in flow.","Adenosine from ATP breakdown acts on A2A receptors (Gs, cAMP, K+ channel opening) to dilate arterioles, with low PO2, CO2, H+, K+, and nitric oxide contributing.","The subendocardium becomes ischemic first because of systolic compression, exposure to LVEDP, the highest wall stress, and the least remaining vasodilator reserve; tachycardia worsens all of these.","In ALCAPA, the postnatal fall in pulmonary pressure leaves the left coronary artery with low-pressure, desaturated blood, and RCA collateral flow drains into the pulmonary trunk, causing anterolateral infarction in early infancy."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, arterial supply and venous drainage of the heart"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Muscle Blood Flow and Cardiac Output During Exercise; the Coronary Circulation and Ischemic Heart Disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Special circulations, coronary circulation"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular system, development of the sinus venosus and fetal circulation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, sections on ischemic heart disease and congenital heart disease"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Cardiovascular Disorders: Heart Disease"}]},"CV-9":{"unit":"CV-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01","title":"Blood Distribution to Body Regions","subject":"Cardiovascular System (CARD)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter traces blood from the aorta to each body region and back to the heart: the aortic branches and their vertebral levels, the arterial supply of the brain and limbs, and venous return through the venae cavae and the hepatic portal system. It then turns to physiology, explaining why border zones between arterial territories infarct first when pressure falls and why each organ receives the share of cardiac output it does and extracts as much oxygen as it does. Board questions usually describe a weak pulse, a dilated vein, a stroke deficit, or an organ injured in shock and ask for the vessel, level, or mechanism.","prerequisites":["Embryonic pharyngeal arch arteries and the foregut, midgut, and hindgut (CV-1, GI-1)","Wall structure of elastic arteries, muscular arteries, arterioles, and veins (CV-2)","Vertebral levels and surface landmarks of the thorax and abdomen","Oxygen content of blood: about 20 mL O2 per 100 mL of arterial blood","Flow, pressure, and resistance in series and parallel circuits (CV-11)"],"connections":[{"to":"Coronary circulation (CV-8)","how":"The coronary arteries are the first aortic branches, and the heart's near-maximal resting extraction means coronary flow must rise with every increase in demand."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Organs are perfused in parallel, so each organ's arteriolar resistance sets its share of cardiac output."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal blood supply and hepatic disease (GI-5, GI-13-C)","how":"The gut arteries and portal circulation are developed organ by organ in GI-5, and cirrhosis produces the portal hypertension described here."},{"to":"CNS blood supply and neurological vascular disease (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"The circle of Willis and cerebral territories predict the deficits of territorial and watershed strokes."},{"to":"Vascular and hemodynamic conditions (CV-18-I, CV-18-H)","how":"Aneurysms, peripheral arterial disease, embolism, and shock all play out on this vascular map."}],"terms":[{"term":"Collateral circulation","def":"Flow through anastomotic channels that enlarge when the main route is narrowed or blocked."},{"term":"End artery","def":"An artery whose territory has no effective anastomosis with its neighbors, so occlusion causes infarction of the tissue it supplies."},{"term":"Circle of Willis (cerebral arterial circle)","def":"The ring of arteries at the base of the brain that links the two internal carotid systems with each other and with the vertebrobasilar system."},{"term":"Hepatic portal system","def":"The veins that carry blood from the gut, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder through a second capillary bed, the liver sinusoids, before it reaches the inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Portosystemic anastomosis","def":"A site where portal tributaries connect with systemic veins, providing a bypass around the liver when portal pressure rises."},{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"Abnormally high pressure in the portal venous system, usually from increased resistance to flow through or beyond the liver."},{"term":"Watershed (border zone)","def":"Tissue at the far edge of two adjacent arterial territories, supplied by the most distal branches of each and therefore perfused at the lowest pressure."},{"term":"Autoregulation","def":"The ability of an organ to keep its blood flow nearly constant across a range of perfusion pressures by adjusting its own arteriolar resistance."},{"term":"Arteriovenous O2 difference","def":"Arterial minus venous O2 content across an organ, in mL O2 per 100 mL of blood."},{"term":"Oxygen extraction ratio","def":"The fraction of delivered oxygen a tissue removes: the arteriovenous O2 difference divided by arterial O2 content."},{"term":"Fick principle","def":"An organ's oxygen consumption equals its blood flow multiplied by its arteriovenous O2 difference."},{"term":"Steal phenomenon","def":"Diversion of blood from one vascular bed into another through a collateral pathway when the second bed's pressure falls, as in subclavian steal."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C01","obj":"CV-9.1","title":"Branches of the aorta and their vertebral levels","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ascending aorta gives only the right and left coronary arteries (CV-8) and ends at the sternal angle (T4 to T5 disc). The arch passes back over the left lung root, returns to T4, and gives three branches from right to left: the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries. The brachiocephalic trunk divides behind the right sternoclavicular joint into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. The asymmetry is embryological: the left fourth arch artery forms part of the definitive arch, while the right forms only the proximal right subclavian (CV-1). The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, so a mass or aneurysm there causes hoarseness. Each common carotid divides at about C4, where the carotid sinus holds baroreceptors."},{"type":"p","text":"The descending thoracic aorta (T4 to T12) gives posterior intercostal arteries to spaces 3 to 11 (the first two arise from the costocervical trunk), subcostal arteries, and bronchial, esophageal, and superior phrenic branches. One large anterior segmental medullary artery, usually on the left, reinforces the anterior spinal artery over the lower cord. Dissection, aneurysm, or aortic clamping can interrupt it and infarct the anterior cord: paraplegia and loss of pain and temperature below the lesion, with vibration and position sense spared in the posterior columns."},{"type":"p","text":"The aorta passes the diaphragm at T12 and bifurcates at L4, near the umbilicus. Its unpaired anterior branches follow the embryonic gut (detail in GI-5). The pancreatic head and second part of the duodenum, at the foregut and midgut junction, receive both celiac and SMA blood, and the mesodermal spleen is supplied by the celiac trunk because it forms in the dorsal mesogastrium."},{"type":"table","title":"Branches of the abdominal aorta","cols":["Branch","Level","Supplies"],"rows":[["Inferior phrenic (paired)","About T12","Diaphragm"],["Celiac trunk","T12","Foregut to the major papilla, liver, spleen"],["Superior mesenteric (SMA)","L1","Midgut: distal duodenum to proximal two thirds of transverse colon"],["Renal and middle suprarenal (paired)","About L1","Kidneys and adrenals"],["Gonadal (paired)","About L2","Testes or ovaries"],["Inferior mesenteric (IMA)","L3","Hindgut: distal third of transverse colon to anal canal above the pectinate line"],["Lumbar (four pairs)","L1 to L4","Posterior abdominal wall"],["Bifurcation","L4","Common iliac arteries"],["Median sacral","Posterior aorta at the bifurcation","Sacrum and coccyx"]],"caption":"Levels follow Moore and vary by about half a vertebra. Gonadal arteries arise high because the gonads develop high and descend."},{"type":"p","text":"These levels predict findings. The left renal vein and third part of the duodenum pass between the SMA and aorta; loss of retroperitoneal fat narrows that angle and can obstruct the duodenum (SMA syndrome) or compress the vein (C04). A horseshoe kidney ascends until its isthmus catches under the IMA. Most abdominal aortic aneurysms are infrarenal (CV-18-I). Occlusion of the terminal aorta and common iliacs (Leriche syndrome) causes bilateral buttock and thigh claudication, weak femoral pulses, and erectile dysfunction. In adult coarctation, usually just distal to the left subclavian origin, arm pressure is high and femoral pulses are weak and delayed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collateral route around an adult coarctation","steps":["High pressure in the subclavian artery above the narrowing","Internal thoracic artery descends behind the costal cartilages","Anterior intercostal arteries carry blood around each space","Flow reverses in enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries","Blood enters the descending aorta below the coarctation","Enlarged intercostals notch inferior rib borders (about ribs 3 to 8)"],"caption":"Ribs 1 and 2 are spared because their intercostal arteries arise from the costocervical trunk above the narrowing."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C02","obj":"CV-9.2","title":"Arterial supply of the brain and the circle of Willis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The two internal carotid arteries (ICAs) form the anterior circulation, and the two vertebral arteries join as the basilar artery to form the posterior circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"The ICA has no neck branches. It crosses the cavernous sinus beside the abducens nerve and gives the ophthalmic artery, so a carotid embolus can cause transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax) as well as stroke. It then gives the posterior communicating artery and divides into the anterior (ACA) and middle cerebral arteries (MCA). The MCA continues the ICA and receives most emboli. Its lenticulostriate branches are thin end arteries leaving a high-pressure trunk at right angles, so chronic hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis, lacunar infarcts, and Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms. The external carotid supplies no brain, but the middle meningeal artery, from its maxillary branch, runs under the pterion (epidural hematoma)."},{"type":"p","text":"Each vertebral artery, the first subclavian branch, rises through the transverse foramina from C6 to C1, gives the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA), and joins its partner at the lower pons. The basilar artery gives the anterior inferior cerebellar (AICA) and superior cerebellar arteries, then the posterior cerebral arteries (PCAs); the oculomotor nerve emerges between the PCA and superior cerebellar artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The circle of Willis is formed by the anterior communicating artery, proximal ACAs, terminal ICAs, posterior communicating arteries, and proximal PCAs. It can refill the territory of a slowly occluded ICA, but a complete, symmetric circle exists in only a minority, and it acts only upstream: an embolus in an MCA branch still infarcts because cortical branches connect only through small leptomeningeal anastomoses. Berry aneurysms form at its branch points, about 90% anteriorly, most often at the anterior communicating junction; one at the posterior communicating origin compresses the oculomotor nerve and dilates the pupil early."},{"type":"table","title":"Cerebral and brainstem territories","cols":["Artery","Deficit (contralateral unless stated)"],"rows":[["ACA","Leg more than arm; incontinence, behavior change"],["MCA, cortical","Face and arm more than leg; aphasia (dominant) or neglect (nondominant)"],["Lenticulostriate","Pure motor hemiparesis (internal capsule lacune)"],["PCA","Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing"],["PICA","Lateral medulla: vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral Horner, ataxia, and face pain and temperature loss; contralateral body pain and temperature loss"],["AICA","Lateral pons: like PICA plus ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, without dysphagia"],["Anterior spinal (medulla)","Hemiparesis, lost position and vibration sense, ipsilateral tongue weakness"],["Basilar","Locked-in state with preserved vertical gaze"]],"caption":"Crossed findings (face on one side, body on the other) localize to the brainstem."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out a subclavian steal","text":"A 64-year-old smoker becomes dizzy while painting a ceiling with his left arm. His left radial pulse is weak and delayed, and left arm systolic pressure is 30 mmHg lower. Step 1: the interarm difference places a stenosis in the left subclavian artery. Step 2: if it lies proximal to the left vertebral origin, pressure there is below basilar pressure. Step 3: arm exercise dilates arm arterioles and lowers pressure further, so flow reverses down the left vertebral artery, taking blood from the posterior circulation. Step 4: vertigo and imbalance accompany arm claudication. A stenosis distal to the vertebral origin would cause arm claudication alone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C03","obj":"CV-9.3","title":"Arteries of the upper and lower limbs and the pulse points","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each limb has one trunk that changes name at fixed landmarks, then divides into arteries that rejoin in arches in the hand or foot. A pulse is palpable where an artery lies near the surface over something firm, and palpating in sequence places an obstruction between the last normal pulse and the first weak one."},{"type":"p","text":"The subclavian artery passes between the anterior and middle scalenes with the brachial plexus, so a cervical rib can compress both (thoracic outlet syndrome). Scapular anastomoses bypass an axillary block above the subscapular origin but not below it. The posterior circumflex humeral artery travels with the axillary nerve around the surgical neck, and the deep brachial artery with the radial nerve in the radial groove. A supracondylar fracture in a child can injure the brachial artery, and forearm flexor ischemia heals as Volkmann contracture. The connected palmar arches let either the radial or ulnar artery perfuse the hand (Allen test)."},{"type":"p","text":"In the femoral triangle the order from lateral to medial is nerve, artery, vein, canal. The medial circumflex femoral artery supplies the femoral head, so a displaced intracapsular neck fracture risks avascular necrosis. The popliteal artery is the deepest structure in its fossa and is tethered, so knee dislocation can tear it. At the lower border of popliteus it divides into the anterior tibial artery, which pierces the interosseous membrane, and the posterior tibial artery, which gives the fibular artery. The lateral compartment has no artery of its own; perforating anterior tibial and fibular branches supply it."},{"type":"table","title":"Limb landmarks and pulse points","cols":["Artery","Begins at","Pulse"],"rows":[["Axillary","Lateral border of first rib","Not routinely felt"],["Brachial","Lower border of teres major","Medial arm or cubital fossa, medial to biceps tendon"],["Radial and ulnar","Cubital fossa, at about the radial neck","Radial lateral to flexor carpi radialis; ulnar near pisiform, often faint"],["Femoral","Inguinal ligament, continuing the external iliac","Midinguinal point, just below the ligament"],["Popliteal","Adductor hiatus","Deep in fossa, knee flexed; often faint"],["Dorsalis pedis","Ankle, from the anterior tibial","Lateral to extensor hallucis longus; absent in a few normal adults"],["Posterior tibial","Lower border of popliteus","Behind the medial malleolus"]],"caption":"Emboli lodge most often at the common femoral bifurcation."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral arterial disease (CV-18-I) favors the superficial femoral artery in the adductor canal and the popliteal artery. Claudication appears one level below the narrowing: aortoiliac disease in the buttock and thigh, femoropopliteal disease in the calf. Ankle pressure normally equals or slightly exceeds arm pressure because the pulse wave amplifies distally, so an ankle-brachial index below about 0.9 means pressure is lost across a stenosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"The right femoral pulse is weak and the right popliteal and foot pulses are absent; left leg pulses are normal. Where is the obstruction, and which muscles ache first on walking?","explain":"The first abnormal pulse is the femoral, so the block lies upstream in the right common or external iliac artery. Thigh and calf muscles ache on walking, plus the buttock if the common iliac is involved.","check":"Compare with the pulse table and the peripheral arterial disease paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C04","obj":"CV-9.4","title":"Venous return: venae cavae, the left renal vein, and the portal system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Veins are richly interconnected, so a blocked vein reroutes blood through normally tiny channels. The brachiocephalic veins unite behind the right first costal cartilage as the superior vena cava (SVC), which receives the azygos vein at about T4. A mediastinal tumor obstructing the SVC swells the face, neck, and arms and distends chest wall veins (SVC syndrome); blood returns through the azygos vein if the block is above its entry, or reverses through the azygos and lumbar veins to the IVC if below."},{"type":"p","text":"The inferior vena cava (IVC) forms at L5, ascends right of the aorta, and passes the diaphragm at T8. The right gonadal vein enters the IVC; the left enters the left renal vein at a right angle, and that vein crosses in front of the aorta below the SMA. Compression there (nutcracker phenomenon) causes hematuria and gonadal back pressure, and with the longer left course explains why varicocele is mostly left-sided. A new isolated right varicocele suggests IVC obstruction or a retroperitoneal mass."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood from the gut, spleen, and pancreas passes through the liver sinusoids before the IVC, the basis of first-pass metabolism. The splenic and superior mesenteric veins form the portal vein behind the pancreatic neck. The liver receives about a quarter of cardiac output, three quarters of it portal, yet each source supplies about half its oxygen. Portal pressure is about 9 mmHg against near 0 in the hepatic veins, and the valveless portal system transmits any rise in resistance back to the gut and spleen."},{"type":"p","text":"Portal hypertension is classified by site: prehepatic (portal vein thrombosis), intrahepatic (cirrhosis; schistosomiasis, presinusoidal), or posthepatic (Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis). The hepatic venous pressure gradient is normally up to about 5 mmHg; varices form at about 10 mmHg."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Why portal pressure stays high in cirrhosis","steps":["Fibrosis, nodules, and stellate cell contraction raise resistance","Nitric oxide and escaped vasodilators dilate splanchnic arterioles","Portal inflow rises against the high resistance","Low effective arterial volume activates RAAS and sympathetic nerves","Kidneys retain Na+ and water, expanding volume","More splanchnic inflow keeps portal pressure high"],"caption":"Pressure is resistance times inflow; cirrhosis raises both. The same Na+ retention feeds ascites."},{"type":"table","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal vein","Systemic vein","Finding"],"rows":[["Lower esophagus","Left gastric","Esophageal to azygos","Varices that can bleed massively"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical","Superficial epigastric","Caput medusae"],["Anorectal junction","Superior rectal","Middle and inferior rectal","Anorectal varices"],["Retroperitoneum","Colic","Lumbar, renal","Silent varices"]],"caption":"Anorectal varices differ from hemorrhoids, which are not more frequent in portal hypertension. Congestive splenomegaly sequesters platelets."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does portal blood in cirrhosis reach the azygos vein through the esophagus rather than crossing the liver to the IVC?","explain":"The resistance lies within the liver, so blood takes the low-resistance portosystemic routes upstream; the left gastric vein connects with esophageal veins draining to the azygos, which dilate into varices.","check":"Compare with the causes of portal hypertension and the anastomosis table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C05","obj":"CV-9.5","title":"Watershed zones and why they infarct first in hypotension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A watershed is tissue fed by the last, smallest branches of two neighboring arterial trees. Pressure falls along every tree because each segment has resistance (CV-11), so the border has the lowest perfusion pressure, and its collaterals (leptomeningeal anastomoses, the marginal artery) are too small to rescue either side. Autoregulation normally hides this: cerebral flow stays nearly constant across a mean pressure of about 60 to 150 mmHg. Below that range, flow falls with pressure and the border zones fail first. Chronic hypertension shifts the range upward, so infarction occurs at pressures safe for others."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hypotension to watershed infarct","steps":["Systemic pressure falls (shock, cardiac arrest, surgery)","Arterioles maximally dilated; autoregulation exhausted","Flow falls in proportion to pressure","Distal border zones receive the lowest pressure","Border tissue infarcts while central territories survive"],"caption":"The same mechanism operates in brain and colon."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortical border zones lie between ACA and MCA, a strip over the upper convexity, and between MCA and PCA in the parieto-occipital region; the internal border zone lies in deep white matter. Bilateral ACA-MCA infarction weakens both proximal arms with face, hands, and legs spared (man in a barrel), and bilateral MCA-PCA infarction can cause Balint syndrome features. A severe carotid stenosis lets a modest systemic fall cause ipsilateral watershed infarcts."},{"type":"p","text":"In the colon, the splenic flexure (SMA and IMA) and rectosigmoid junction have weak marginal connections, so hypoperfusion causes ischemic colitis with left-sided pain and bloody diarrhea; the rectum has extra internal iliac supply. The mucosa fails first, because countercurrent exchange in the villi leaves the tips hypoxic. Hypoperfusion gives mucosal or mural infarction; transmural infarction follows occlusion."},{"type":"table","title":"Regions that fail first in low flow","cols":["Region","Why","Result"],"rows":[["Cerebral border zones","Distal ends of two territories","Bilateral proximal arm weakness"],["Splenic flexure, rectosigmoid","Weak marginal connections","Ischemic colitis"],["Subendocardium","Farthest from epicardial arteries; perfused mainly in diastole","Subendocardial infarction"],["Liver zone 3","Last on the sinusoid","Centrilobular necrosis"],["Renal outer medulla","Low PO2 from vasa recta countercurrent; high transport demand","Acute tubular necrosis"]],"caption":"Only brain and colon are true boundaries between territories; the others are distal ends of one supply path."},{"type":"p","text":"An embolus or thrombus fills one artery's territory, usually on one side. Strip-like infarcts along border zones, often bilateral and in several organs, point to global hypoperfusion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After an hour of hypotension from a ruptured aortic aneurysm, a man has bloody stools, bilateral shoulder weakness with normal grip, rising aminotransferases, and rising creatinine. What links these findings?","explain":"Every organ fell below its autoregulatory range at once, so the regions that fail first were injured: colonic border zones, ACA-MCA cortical border zones, centrilobular hepatocytes, and the outer medulla. An embolus would cause one territorial deficit.","check":"Compare with the border zone table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-CARD-09-01-C06","obj":"CV-9.6","title":"Distribution of resting cardiac output and organ O2 extraction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Systemic organs are perfused in parallel at the same aortic pressure, so each organ's share of cardiac output is set by its own arteriolar resistance (CV-11). Flow does not simply follow oxygen need: the kidneys and gut use blood for filtration and processing, while the heart strips most of its oxygen."},{"type":"p","text":"Arterial blood carries about 20 mL O2/dL. By the Fick principle, O2 consumption equals flow times arteriovenous O2 difference; extraction is that difference divided by arterial content. For the whole body, 5 L/min and 5 mL/dL give 250 mL/min, extraction about 25%, and mixed venous saturation about 75% (PO2 about 40 mmHg). Low extraction means reserve; high extraction means more oxygen can come only from more flow."},{"type":"table","title":"Resting flow and O2 extraction","cols":["Organ","Share of output","Extraction","Flow serves"],"rows":[["Gut and liver","About 25%","About 15 to 20%","Absorption and processing"],["Kidneys","About 20%","About 7 to 10%","Filtration"],["Resting muscle","About 15%","Body average; 75 to 90% in exercise","Metabolism"],["Brain","About 15%","About 30 to 35%","Constant demand"],["Heart","About 4 to 5%","About 70 to 75%","Metabolism"],["Skin","About 5 to 6%","Very low","Heat loss"]],"caption":"Approximate resting values; learn the ranking."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidneys (about 300 g) have the highest flow per gram of any large organ and filter about 20% of their plasma, so their venous blood stays highly saturated, and their O2 use tracks filtered Na+ load. The densely innervated splanchnic bed is the main blood reservoir. Resting muscle has low flow per gram; in exercise flow rises about 20-fold (CV-12). The brain (about 2% of body weight) uses about 20% of resting oxygen, has no oxygen store, and loses consciousness within about 5 to 10 seconds of no flow; its flow is held constant by autoregulation and raised by PCO2, while hyperventilation constricts cerebral arterioles. The heart's coronary sinus blood is only 25 to 30% saturated, so extra demand must be met by metabolic vasodilation (adenosine, nitric oxide), and a fixed stenosis becomes ischemia (CV-8)."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the Fick principle","text":"Arterial content is 20 mL/dL. Kidney: flow 11 dL/min, venous 18.5 mL/dL; consumption 11 times 1.5 is 16.5 mL/min, extraction 7.5%. Heart: flow 2.25 dL/min, coronary sinus 6 mL/dL; consumption 2.25 times 14 is 31.5 mL/min, extraction 70%. The kidneys get five times the heart's flow but use half its oxygen. If demand doubles, the kidney can double extraction to 15%, but the heart would need 140% extraction, so its flow must double."},{"type":"flow","title":"Redistribution of cardiac output in hemorrhage","steps":["Blood loss lowers arterial pressure","Baroreceptor firing falls; sympathetic output rises","Alpha-1 constriction of skin, splanchnic, renal, and muscle arterioles","Venoconstriction moves reservoir blood toward the heart","Cerebral and coronary arterioles stay dilated under local control","Brain and heart receive a larger share of output"],"caption":"These beds tolerate a brief fall in flow; prolonged low flow injures them (CV-18-H)."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains the layout: the asymmetric arch comes from selective persistence of pharyngeal arch arteries, the three gut arteries follow the foregut, midgut, and hindgut, and the right-sided IVC forces the left renal and gonadal veins into longer courses. Physiology explains the behavior: parallel perfusion lets each organ set its own flow, the Fick principle shows why low-extraction beds can be constricted in shock while the heart depends on flow, and falling pressure along each arterial tree makes border zones fail first. Atherosclerosis at branch points, cardiac emboli, cirrhosis, and vasculitis act on exactly these vessels, and collateral flow can either protect tissue (circle of Willis, coarctation) or steal from it (subclavian steal)."}],"summary":["The arch gives the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries; the carotids bifurcate at about C4.","Abdominal aortic levels: celiac T12, SMA L1, renal about L1, gonadal about L2, IMA L3, bifurcation L4; the gut arteries supply foregut, midgut, and hindgut.","The circle of Willis protects against slow occlusion of an inflow vessel but not against emboli beyond it.","Proximal subclavian stenosis can reverse vertebral flow during arm exercise (subclavian steal).","Limb arteries change names at fixed landmarks, and the first weak pulse in a sequence localizes an obstruction just upstream.","The long left renal vein crosses below the SMA and receives the left gonadal vein, explaining nutcracker compression and left varicocele.","Portal hypertension opens anastomoses at the lower esophagus, umbilicus, anorectal junction, and retroperitoneum.","Below the autoregulatory range, watershed zones (ACA-MCA, MCA-PCA, splenic flexure, rectosigmoid) infarct first.","The kidney extracts little O2 and the heart the most, so extra coronary supply must come from increased flow.","In shock, sympathetic constriction of low-extraction beds shifts output to the brain and heart."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax and abdomen: the aorta and its branches, the venae cavae, and the hepatic portal system"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, upper limb, and lower limb: arterial supply of the brain and the limb arteries"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular system: development of the aortic arches and the venous system"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Local and Humoral Control of Tissue Blood Flow; Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Coronary circulation, splanchnic and hepatic circulation, and circulatory shock"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels; the central nervous system (cerebrovascular disease); the gastrointestinal tract (ischemic bowel disease); the liver (portal hypertension)"}]}});
